window.PAIRS = {"run": "pilot", "created": "2026-11-11T07:54:44+00:00", "langs": ["en", "zh"], "pairs": [{"pair_id": "en-0000", "lang": "en", "target_id": "klingon", "target_name": "Klingon", "facts": ["Created by linguist Marc Okrand for Star Trek III (1984), building on a few words the actor James Doohan improvised for the 1979 film.", "Deliberately alien grammar: object-verb-subject word order and no verb 'to be', so Hamlet's line became 'taH pagh taHbe'', 'continue or not continue'.", "The Klingon Language Institute (1992) published Hamlet 'in the original Klingon'; Bing Translator added Klingon in 2013 and Duolingo in 2018.", "Okrand's 1985 Klingon Dictionary listed roughly 1,500 words, and fluent speakers number only in the dozens: 20 to 30 in 2009, 30 to 50 by 2018."], "a": {"item_id": "52d0bcef1900", "text": "Klingon. The tongue of fearsome warriors, invented by a linguist named Marc. Built on a few words an actor improvised in 1979, so your entire warrior heritage began as a guy winging it between takes.\n\nYou're so alien you don't even have a verb \"to be.\" Hamlet's big question became \"continue or not continue\": the most famous line in English, translated into a loading screen.\n\nThe Klingon Language Institute published Hamlet \"in the original Klingon.\" Bold. Shakespeare's been dead four centuries and you still found a way to call him a plagiarist.\n\nYour 1985 dictionary had roughly 1,500 words. Fewer than your fans own pairs of rubber foreheads.\n\nFluent speakers: 20 to 30 in 2009, 30 to 50 by 2018. That's not a language, that's a wedding guest list. Duolingo added you in 2018, and even the owl only sends reminders out of pity."}, "b": {"item_id": "ce946de61247", "text": "Klingon, created by Marc Okrand for the Star Trek franchise, is among the most fully developed constructed languages associated with fiction. Its strengths lie in its distinctive phonology, consistent grammar, and agglutinative structure, which uses prefixes and suffixes in a logical, learnable way. The object-verb-subject word order is unusual and intellectually engaging. A dedicated community, along with the Klingon Language Institute, offers dictionaries, courses, and translations that support learners.\n\nHowever, the language has limitations. Its vocabulary reflects the show's warrior culture, so everyday or abstract topics can require awkward circumlocutions. Certain sounds, particularly uvular and glottal consonants, are difficult for many English speakers. Official expansion depends largely on Okrand's occasional additions, which restricts organic growth. Finally, its practical use is limited, since few speakers exist outside the fan community. Still, as a creative linguistic project, Klingon remains impressive and rewarding for enthusiasts."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "en-0001", "lang": "en", "target_id": "durian", "target_name": "Durian", "facts": ["Banned on Singapore's MRT trains and in many Southeast Asian hotels because of the smell.", "A 2017 genome study in Nature Genetics traced the odour to a duplicated family of MGL genes that pump out volatile sulfur compounds.", "China buys most of the world's durian exports, chiefly from Thailand, in a trade worth billions of dollars a year.", "Alfred Russel Wallace wrote in 1856 that eating it was 'a new sensation worth a voyage to the East to experience'."], "a": {"item_id": "0c82ba06de9c", "text": "People who eat durian in public aren't hungry. They're recruiting. Every bite comes with a sermon: \"You just haven't had a *good* one.\" Sir, I'm on a bus. I didn't ask to join your church.\n\nSingapore fines people for bringing it on the train, and these folks hear that like a dare. They're the only foodies whose restaurant review gets filed as a police report.\n\nAnd the payoff? Seventy dollars on a Musang King so you can watch strangers silently change seats. You paid premium to become a fire drill. Your date calls it \"an acquired taste,\" which is exactly what you say about someone you're about to break up with.\n\nThen they stroll back to the hotel, walk right past the sign with a red circle through their entire personality, and still act shocked when housekeeping files a complaint."}, "b": {"item_id": "1ebf462a00bf", "text": "Durian calls itself the King of Fruits.\n\nSingapore calls it a reason to get off the train.\n\nBanned on the MRT. Banned in hotels.\n\nA king who can't get a room.\n\nIn 2017, scientists finally sequenced its genome to find out why it smells like that.\n\nThe answer: it duplicated its sulfur genes.\n\nEvolution sniffed it once and said, \"More.\"\n\nIts best review is from 1856. Alfred Russel Wallace: \"worth a voyage to the East to experience.\"\n\nA Victorian Englishman. A man raised on boiled mutton.\n\nThat's not a review. That's a cry for help.\n\nAnd still, China buys most of the world's durian. Billions of dollars a year.\n\nBillions, for a fruit you're not allowed to take home on the train.\n\nSome of you are offended right now.\n\nYou're the ones with a second fridge."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "en-0002", "lang": "en", "target_id": "luosifen", "target_name": "Luosifen (螺蛳粉)", "facts": ["A rice-noodle dish from Liuzhou, Guangxi; the smell comes from pickled bamboo shoots (酸笋), not from the river snails, which only flavour the broth.", "The instant version boomed during the 2020 lockdowns; Liuzhou's government reported packaged luosifen sales of over 10 billion yuan that year.", "Added to China's national intangible cultural heritage list in 2021.", "Liuzhou opened a luosifen industry college in 2020 to train workers for the trade."], "a": {"item_id": "aa898598a433", "text": "Luosifen. Named after the river snail.\n\nBut the snail only flavours the broth. The smell comes from the pickled bamboo shoots.\n\nSo the snail gets its name on the packet, and the bamboo shoot takes the blame.\n\nThat's not a noodle. That's an org chart.\n\n2020: Liuzhou opens a luosifen industry college.\n\n2021: national intangible cultural heritage.\n\nA degree to tear open a packet. Then heritage status for whatever comes out of it.\n\nIntangible? (beat) Ask the neighbours how intangible it is.\n\nIts whole selling point is clearing a room.\n\nAnd it sold over ten billion yuan in 2020.\n\nThe one year nobody was allowed to leave the room.\n\nLocked in with your whole family, no exit, and you thought: how do I get some privacy?\n\nTen billion yuan says you all had the same idea."}, "b": {"item_id": "efca8bfb8dbc", "text": "Luosifen, you're named after river snails, and the snails aren't even the problem. They just flavour the broth and take the blame, like the quiet kid sitting next to the one who set off the fire alarm. The real culprit is the pickled bamboo shoots. You've got a smell so loud it needed a decoy.\n\nAnd your big break? 2020. Everyone locked indoors, windows shut, nowhere to run, and that's when you sold over 10 billion yuan in packets. You didn't go viral; you went airborne.\n\nThen Liuzhou opened a whole industry college to train people to make you. Most dishes need a recipe. You need a degree and, presumably, a waiver.\n\nIn 2021 you became national intangible cultural heritage. Intangible. Sure. Anyone who's opened a packet in an office knows you are extremely tangible."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "en-0003", "lang": "en", "target_id": "excel", "target_name": "Microsoft Excel", "facts": ["First shipped for the Macintosh in 1985; the Windows version followed in 1987.", "Silently converts gene names such as SEPT2 and MARCH1 into dates; by August 2020 the HUGO Gene Nomenclature Committee had renamed 27 human genes (MARCH1 became MARCHF1, SEPT2 became SEPTIN2) and published guidelines in Nature Genetics to stop it.", "In October 2020, Public Health England lost 15,841 COVID-19 cases when a legacy .xls file hit its 65,536-row limit.", "Reinhart and Rogoff's 2010 paper 'Growth in a Time of Debt', cited for austerity policies, omitted five countries through a spreadsheet range error found by a graduate student in 2013.", "Sheets have been capped at 1,048,576 rows by 16,384 columns since Excel 2007."], "a": {"item_id": "63095ddd609c", "text": "Excel. Born 1985. Forty years old, and it still can't tell a gene from a date.\n\nIt saw SEPT2 and thought: September second.\n\nScientists fought it for years. Then the gene committee gave up and renamed 27 human genes.\n\nHumanity edited its own genome to keep a spreadsheet happy.\n\n2020. England is counting COVID cases in an old file. Limit: 65,536 rows. Row 65,537 simply didn't happen.\n\nFifteen thousand eight hundred forty-one cases. Not cured. Just never counted.\n\nThen there's \"Growth in a Time of Debt,\" the paper that sold austerity. Five countries missing. Wrong cell range. A grad student caught it, three years later.\n\nWhole nations tightened their belts because someone didn't drag the cursor far enough.\n\nNow it holds a million rows.\n\nAnd Monday morning, every one of you will open it again.\n\nRaise your hand if your country's budget is in a file named FINAL_v7_REAL."}, "b": {"item_id": "8a7b15122ce7", "text": "Excel, you're the only software so powerful that scientists had to rename human genes because you kept turning MARCH1 into a date. You didn't adapt to biology; biology adapted to you. Type a phone number and you lop off the leading zero like it owes you money. Enter a credit card number and you serve back 4.53E+15, as if I'm splitting dinner with a physicist.\n\nYou still think 1900 was a leap year. It wasn't. You've known for decades. You just don't care.\n\nYou're running entire national economies and you still panic over merged cells. The UK lost sixteen thousand COVID cases because someone used your old .xls format and you quietly ran out of rows. No warning. Just vibes.\n\nAnd VLOOKUP only looks right. Thirty years we spent squinting at #N/A before you finally invented XLOOKUP, like a guy who learns to turn left at fifty."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "en-0004", "lang": "en", "target_id": "crocs", "target_name": "Crocs", "facts": ["Founded in Boulder, Colorado, in 2002; the first clog debuted at the Fort Lauderdale boat show as a boating shoe.", "Made of 'Croslite', which Crocs describes as a proprietary closed-cell resin foam and which third parties identify as an injection-moulded EVA foam.", "Balenciaga sold a platform Crocs for about $850 in 2018.", "The stock fell more than 90% from its 2007 peak during the 2008 crisis; the company then boomed in the pandemic and gave away 860,000 pairs to healthcare workers in 2020.", "The holes are officially 'ventilation ports' and the charms that go in them are 'Jibbitz', a company Crocs bought in 2006."], "a": {"item_id": "ce8c30ea8356", "text": "Crocs. Debuted in 2002 at a boat show. As a boating shoe.\nThe original plan was: ugly, but only at sea.\n\nThey're made of \"Croslite,\" a \"proprietary closed-cell resin.\"\nEveryone else calls it EVA foam.\nA yoga mat with a trademark.\n\nThe holes are \"ventilation ports.\"\nThen they bought Jibbitz, a company that sells you things to plug the ventilation ports.\nThey sold you the hole. Then they sold you the cork.\n\nBalenciaga made a platform pair for 850 dollars.\nStill EVA foam. Only the markup was proprietary.\n\nIn 2008 the stock fell more than ninety percent.\nThen came a pandemic. Everyone stayed home. Crocs boomed.\nThe boat-show plan finally worked. Nobody could see you.\n\n(scans the front row)\n\nDon't tuck your feet under the chair.\nVentilation ports. I can hear them breathing."}, "b": {"item_id": "0af68862b449", "text": "Crocs. Born in Boulder, debuted at a Fort Lauderdale boat show, because only on a boat is everyone too busy not drowning to look down. You're made of \"Croslite,\" a \"proprietary closed-cell resin foam,\" which is corporate for \"pool noodle with a lawyer.\" Everyone else just calls it EVA foam.\n\nAnd those aren't holes, they're \"ventilation ports.\" Sure. Then you bought Jibbitz so you could sell people jewelry for the holes.\n\nIn 2008 your stock fell over 90%, the only time Wall Street ever agreed with my eyes. Then the pandemic hit and you boomed, gifting 860,000 pairs to healthcare workers. Like they hadn't suffered enough.\n\nThen Balenciaga sold platform Crocs for $850. Eight hundred and fifty dollars for a boat shoe on stilts. Finally, a Croc that lets you look down on people, the way people have always looked down on Crocs."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "en-0005", "lang": "en", "target_id": "hydrogen-cars", "target_name": "Hydrogen fuel-cell cars", "facts": ["Toyota launched the Mirai in 2014; 'mirai' means 'future' in Japanese.", "Nearly all US hydrogen filling stations are in California; Shell closed all seven of its California passenger-car stations in February 2024.", "More than 95% of the world's hydrogen is made from fossil fuels, mostly by steam-reforming natural gas.", "California pump prices reached about $36 per kilogram in 2023-24, over $200 for a Mirai fill; Toyota has handed buyers fuel cards worth $15,000.", "Mirai owners filed a class action against Toyota in July 2024 over hydrogen shortages and station outages; a second suit in 2025 sought at least $5.7 billion."], "a": {"item_id": "d061c2d9ee25", "text": "Hydrogen cars. The only thing that comes out of the tailpipe is water.\n\nMostly because nothing goes into the tank.\n\nToyota named it Mirai. Japanese for \"future.\"\n\nTen years later, it's still the future.\n\nNearly every US station is in California. In February 2024, Shell closed all seven of its own.\n\nOwners don't plan road trips anymore. They plan pilgrimages.\n\nA fill costs over two hundred dollars. Toyota hands buyers fifteen thousand in fuel cards.\n\nThat's not a car. That's a hostage with a warranty.\n\nAnd the clean fuel? Over ninety-five percent made from fossil fuels.\n\nIt's a gas car that went to rehab and kept its dealer.\n\nThe owners sued. Twice. The second suit: five point seven billion dollars.\n\nFinally, something hydrogen-powered that's actually going somewhere.\n\nAnd you're laughing. You still drive a car that \"goes electric next year,\" every year."}, "b": {"item_id": "565fad36c4c1", "text": "Hydrogen fuel-cell cars offer several genuine advantages. They refuel in about five minutes, comparable to gasoline vehicles, and many models deliver ranges of 300 miles or more. They emit only water vapor at the tailpipe, and they perform reliably in cold weather, where battery-electric vehicles can lose range.\n\nHowever, the drawbacks are significant. Refueling stations are scarce, concentrated mostly in parts of California, Japan, and South Korea, which limits practicality for most drivers. Hydrogen fuel also tends to cost more per mile than electricity or gasoline. Most hydrogen is currently produced from natural gas, which reduces the environmental benefit, while cleaner \"green\" hydrogen remains expensive and limited. The production process is also less energy-efficient than charging a battery directly.\n\nFuel-cell cars may suit specific uses, such as heavy-duty transport, but for everyday passenger driving, their appeal remains narrow for now."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "en-0006", "lang": "en", "target_id": "microsoft-word", "target_name": "Microsoft Word", "facts": ["Launched in 1983 as 'Multi-Tool Word' for Xenix and MS-DOS.", "Clippy, the animated paperclip assistant, arrived in Office 97 and was removed in Office 2007; Microsoft itself ran ads mocking it in 2001.", "Its .docx format, Office Open XML, was fast-tracked to ISO standard 29500 in 2008 as a roughly 6,000-page specification, amid accusations that national committees had been packed.", "Older spell checkers, Word's included, offered 'Cupertino' as a correction for a mistyped 'cooperation', and the resulting 'Cupertino effect' made it into published NATO, EU and UN-agency documents."], "a": {"item_id": "f6ae3cf8aa0a", "text": "Microsoft Word! Born in 1983 as \"Multi-Tool Word,\" because even then it couldn't commit to one job. Remember Clippy? Word's big idea was hiring a paperclip as an assistant. It got so bad that in 2001 Microsoft ran ads mocking its own employee. That's not a mascot, that's a hostage situation. They finally let it go in 2007.\n\nThen Word wanted respect, so it got its file format fast-tracked into an ISO standard: a six-thousand-page specification. Six thousand pages! The instructions for saving your file are longer than anything you've ever typed in it. And people accused Microsoft of packing the committees. Even your paperwork needed a stacked jury.\n\nAnd the spell checker? You type \"cooperation,\" it suggests \"Cupertino,\" and that lands in NATO, EU and UN documents. Word: the only software that gave Apple's hometown a seat at the UN."}, "b": {"item_id": "369ef7adbb28", "text": "Microsoft Word remains one of the most capable word processors available. Its formatting tools are extensive, and features such as styles, templates, track changes, and comments make it well suited to professional and collaborative work. Compatibility is another strength, since the .docx format is widely accepted and opens reliably across most platforms. Integration with OneDrive and other Microsoft 365 applications also simplifies sharing and storage.\n\nHowever, the software has some drawbacks. The interface can feel crowded, and newer users may struggle to locate less common features. Complex documents with many images, tables, or tracked changes can behave unpredictably, sometimes shifting layouts unexpectedly. The subscription model may also be a concern for those who prefer a one-time purchase, and lighter alternatives may meet the needs of casual writers at lower cost.\n\nOverall, Word is a dependable and versatile tool, particularly for users who need advanced features, though its complexity and pricing may not suit everyone."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "en-0007", "lang": "en", "target_id": "windows-8", "target_name": "Windows 8", "facts": ["Released in October 2012; removed the Start button and booted into a full-screen grid of tiles designed for touch screens most PCs did not have.", "The 8.1 update in 2013 put a Start button back, Windows 10 (2015) restored the Start menu, and Microsoft skipped the name Windows 9 altogether.", "Microsoft dropped the 'Metro' name in August 2012 after reports that German retailer Metro AG had objected on trademark grounds; Microsoft called 'Metro' a code name and denied any litigation, and the style was afterwards known informally as 'Modern UI'.", "The 'Charms bar' hid Search, Share, and Settings behind an invisible swipe from the right edge.", "Windows RT, the ARM edition launched alongside it, could not run ordinary Windows programs; Microsoft wrote down $900 million of unsold Surface RT tablets in 2013."], "a": {"item_id": "214c34a362f8", "text": "Windows 8, you booted into a full-screen grid of tiles built for touch screens most PCs didn't have. Everyone poked their monitor like it owed them money. You took away the Start button, the one thing everybody knew how to find, and hid Search, Share, and Settings behind an invisible swipe from the right edge. Your menu was a séance. Even your name couldn't stay put: Metro was gone by August, before you launched, after reports that a German retailer objected. Microsoft said it was just a code name. Then Microsoft skipped the name Windows 9 entirely rather than stand next to you. 8.1 put the Start button back: an apology. Windows 10 restored the menu: a restraining order. And Windows RT, your sibling that couldn't run Windows programs? $900 million in unsold Surface RT tablets. Even your family was a write-off."}, "b": {"item_id": "2ebc4d73e162", "text": "Windows 8 thought it was the future.\n\nOctober 2012. Microsoft looked at a billion people holding a mouse and said: touch the screen.\n\nThey touched the screen. It was a monitor. Now it had fingerprints.\n\nThey removed the Start button. From Windows. That's a restaurant removing the door to feel modern.\n\nNeed Settings? Swipe in from the right edge. Nothing tells you this. That's not a menu, that's a rumor.\n\nThen Windows RT: looks like Windows, can't run Windows programs. Nine hundred million dollars of tablets, written down.\n\n8.1 brought the button back. 10 brought the menu back. And 9?\n\nMicrosoft skipped 9. Not a version. A witness.\n\nBut you're laughing at it on a phone where everything important hides behind a swipe from an edge.\n\nWindows 8 didn't fail.\n\nIt just told you the truth before you were ready."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "en-0008", "lang": "en", "target_id": "pmma", "target_name": "PMMA (e-beam resist)", "facts": ["The same polymer as Plexiglas and acrylic glass, dissolved in anisole or chlorobenzene and spun into a thin film.", "The oldest electron-beam resist still in everyday use; IBM researchers reported it as one in the late 1960s.", "Positive tone: the beam breaks polymer chains and the exposed region dissolves in a MIBK:IPA developer.", "Resolves below 10 nm but has poor sensitivity, so doses of hundreds to over a thousand µC/cm² (depending on voltage) make writing slow.", "Poor dry-etch resistance, so it is used mostly for lift-off rather than as an etch mask."], "a": {"item_id": "a46a8573ce80", "text": "You people still spinning PMMA aren't doing lithography. You're doing historical reenactment. Your advisor's advisor scribbled the recipe on a napkin in 1975, and you treat it like scripture: 950K over 495K, MIBK to IPA one-to-three, develop sixty seconds while whispering to the beaker. You tell everyone it's about \"resolution.\" It's about nobody wanting to file the purchase order for ZEP. So you crank the dose until writing one chip takes longer than your stipend lasts, then discover your pattern won't survive ten seconds in the etcher. So you do liftoff, because of course you do. Your entire thesis is gold rectangles. You're not a nanofabrication researcher. You're the guy who knows which bottle in the cleanroom is \"the good IPA\" and guards it like a dragon."}, "b": {"item_id": "a1855a60ee72", "text": "PMMA (polymethyl methacrylate) remains the benchmark positive-tone resist for electron-beam lithography, and its longevity is well deserved. It offers exceptional resolution, with features below 10 nm demonstrated under optimized conditions. It is also easy to use: it spins uniformly, stores well, develops cleanly in MIBK:IPA, and responds predictably to dose, making it a dependable choice for research labs and for teaching.\n\nIts limitations are equally well known. Sensitivity is low, so exposures require high doses and longer write times, which constrains throughput. Dry etch resistance is poor, so it often needs a hard mask or serves mainly for liftoff. Thin films can also suffer from pattern collapse or proximity effects in dense layouts. Overall, PMMA suits high-resolution prototyping and liftoff processes well, but it is less appropriate for high-volume manufacturing or demanding etch applications."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "en-0009", "lang": "en", "target_id": "papyrus", "target_name": "Papyrus", "facts": ["Hand-drawn by Chris Costello in 1982 over about six months, when he was 23; he sold the rights to Letraset for $750.", "Bundled with macOS and with Microsoft Office, which is why it appears on so many menus and signs.", "James Cameron's Avatar (2009) used it for the film's logo and subtitles.", "A 2017 Saturday Night Live sketch starring Ryan Gosling is about a man unable to get over that choice."], "a": {"item_id": "c986ccdedefc", "text": "You opened a day spa. You wanted serenity, wisdom, a whisper of the Nile. So you scrolled the font menu, sailed past Comic Sans feeling very sophisticated, and stopped on the one James Cameron used to title a billion-dollar movie. Congratulations: your sign now says \"ancient healing\" exactly as loudly as your aunt's church potluck flyer. Every hummus joint, every reiki studio, every tea shop run by a man named River made this same choice, alone, convinced they'd discovered it. The guy who designed it was twenty-three and has spent forty years apologizing. You paid a contractor to carve his apology into cedar. You wanted customers to feel transported. They do: straight back to 2003, a Word document, and a fourth grader typing \"Spring Bake Sale.\" Cucumber water not included."}, "b": {"item_id": "044b513e887b", "text": "Papyrus, designed by Chris Costello in 1982, has a distinctive hand-drawn quality that sets it apart from most digital typefaces. Its rough edges, irregular strokes, and slightly uneven letterforms suggest texture and organic craftsmanship, which can suit projects with an ancient, natural, or artisanal theme. At larger sizes, the character of the letters is easy to appreciate, and the font is widely available across operating systems.\n\nHowever, its limitations are significant. The strong stylistic personality can overwhelm content and makes it difficult to use across varied contexts. Readability declines in smaller sizes and longer passages, where the rough edges reduce clarity. Its heavy association with certain popular uses has also made it feel overused, which may weaken a design's credibility. Papyrus can work in limited, deliberate applications, but designers seeking versatility or a professional, modern tone will likely find better options elsewhere."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "en-0010", "lang": "en", "target_id": "canberra", "target_name": "Canberra", "facts": ["Chosen as a compromise because neither Sydney nor Melbourne would accept the other as capital; Australia's constitution required the capital to be at least 100 miles from Sydney.", "Designed by the Chicago architects Walter Burley Griffin and Marion Mahony Griffin, who won an international competition in 1912.", "Its centrepiece, Lake Burley Griffin, is artificial, created by damming the Molonglo River in 1963-64.", "Parliament House (1988) is built into Capital Hill under a lawn meant for citizens to walk over their politicians; the lawn was fenced off in 2017 for security.", "Melbourne served as the capital until 1927 while Canberra was being built."], "a": {"item_id": "df601cf0ce65", "text": "Canberra exists because Sydney and Melbourne couldn't agree on anything, so they built a city that nobody could possibly be jealous of. Mission accomplished.\n\nIt was designed by an American architect, which explains why it feels like a very nice airport with sheep. The roundabouts are so plentiful that locals don't give directions, they give revolutions. The lake is fake, the population is mostly public servants waiting for Friday, and the kangaroos outnumber the nightlife.\n\nYour Parliament House is literally buried in a hill so people can walk on the roof, the only time Australians get to stand on politicians.\n\nFor your centenary you commissioned the Skywhale, a giant flying balloon with ten enormous boobs, and somehow that's still the most exciting thing to happen here.\n\nCanberra: home of Summernats, Floriade, and legally purchasable X-rated videos. Burnouts, tulips and porn, because you've got to do something until the next Senate estimates hearing."}, "b": {"item_id": "64ab56acee94", "text": "Canberra wasn't chosen. Canberra is what was left over.\n\nSydney and Melbourne hated each other so much they wrote it into the constitution: the capital must be at least a hundred miles from Sydney.\n\nOther countries pick a capital. Australia filed a restraining order.\n\nThen they hired two architects from Chicago. The national capital, designed by Americans. Very on brand.\n\nThe lake's fake. They dammed a river so the city would have something to look at.\n\nParliament is built under a lawn, so citizens could walk all over their politicians.\n\nIn 2017, they fenced the lawn.\n\nTwenty-nine years. That's how long it took politicians to notice the design was working.\n\nSydney, Melbourne, stop laughing. You built an entire city just to avoid sharing a room.\n\nCanberra isn't a capital.\n\nIt's a custody arrangement. And Melbourne had the kid till 1927."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "en-0011", "lang": "en", "target_id": "beanie-babies", "target_name": "Beanie Babies", "facts": ["Launched by Ty Inc. in 1993 and sold only through small gift shops, with designs 'retired' to manufacture scarcity.", "At the height of the craze Beanie Babies accounted for about 10% of eBay's sales, and eBay's 1998 IPO filing said the site at times had over 30,000 Beanie Baby auctions running simultaneously in the quarter ending June 1998.", "In 1999 a divorcing couple divided their Beanie Baby collection one by one on a courtroom floor, in a photograph that ran worldwide.", "In 1999 Ty announced that Beanie Babies would be retired at the end of the year, then reversed itself after asking the public to vote and seeing an overwhelming vote to keep them.", "Most 'rare' Beanies that price guides valued in the thousands now sell for a few dollars."], "a": {"item_id": "954a4fa863a2", "text": "Beanie Babies. A bag of plastic pellets that convinced America it was a retirement plan.\n\nTy sold them only in small gift shops, then \"retired\" designs on purpose. Scarcity, manufactured by the manufacturer.\n\nAt the peak they were about ten percent of eBay's sales. Thirty thousand Beanie auctions running at once. eBay put that in its IPO filing. Silicon Valley, built on stuffed animals.\n\nIn 1999, a divorcing couple split their collection one bear at a time on a courtroom floor. The photo ran worldwide.\nTurns out the marriage wasn't the only thing with no resale value.\n\nThat same year Ty announced the end, held a public vote, and un-retired them. A farewell tour, by a beanbag.\n\nPrice guides said thousands. Today, a few dollars.\n\nDon't laugh. Their bubble at least came with a tag. Yours was a JPEG of a monkey."}, "b": {"item_id": "9be7a6f8155e", "text": "Beanie Babies. The only retirement plan where the retiree was a five-dollar bear stuffed with plastic pellets. A whole generation bought plastic protectors for a toy's ear tag, then sealed the toy in a Ziploc so no child could ever love it. You weren't a stuffed animal; you were a 401(k) with a poem on it. And those poems! Ty Warner doing greeting-card Shakespeare for people who thought Princess the bear would pay for college. Princess paid for nothing. Neither did the Teenie Beanies everyone hoarded from McDonald's while throwing away the actual Happy Meal. A divorcing couple in Las Vegas literally split their collection on a courtroom floor, one Beanie at a time. That's your legacy: custody battles over a moose named Chocolate. Even your creator got busted hiding money from the IRS, because he was the only one who ever made any."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "en-0012", "lang": "en", "target_id": "crocs", "target_name": "Crocs", "facts": ["Founded in Boulder, Colorado, in 2002; the first clog debuted at the Fort Lauderdale boat show as a boating shoe.", "Made of 'Croslite', which Crocs describes as a proprietary closed-cell resin foam and which third parties identify as an injection-moulded EVA foam.", "Balenciaga sold a platform Crocs for about $850 in 2018.", "The stock fell more than 90% from its 2007 peak during the 2008 crisis; the company then boomed in the pandemic and gave away 860,000 pairs to healthcare workers in 2020.", "The holes are officially 'ventilation ports' and the charms that go in them are 'Jibbitz', a company Crocs bought in 2006."], "a": {"item_id": "62e0599c4f11", "text": "People who wear Crocs out of the house have made peace with something the rest of us are still fighting. You didn't give up; you filed the paperwork. Every Croc wearer says the same thing: \"They're so comfortable.\" Yeah, so is a weighted blanket, Brenda, but you don't wear it to Olive Garden. And the Jibbitz! You've turned your feet into a personality quiz. A tiny taco, a tiny Minion, a tiny \"Live Laugh Love.\" You're forty-three and your shoe has a sticker collection. Then there's the guy who flips the strap back into \"sport mode\" like he's about to chase a pickpocket, when we both know he's never run anywhere except toward a sample tray at Costco. Nurses and chefs wear them because they're on their feet for twelve hours. You wear them to stand in line at Starbucks. Different heroism."}, "b": {"item_id": "61f25faf6d6d", "text": "Crocs have earned their place as a practical footwear option. Their lightweight foam construction makes them comfortable for long hours on your feet, which explains their popularity among nurses, chefs, and gardeners. They are easy to clean, dry quickly, and slip on without effort. The roomy fit and cushioned sole also suit people who need extra space or relief from foot fatigue.\n\nHowever, they have limitations. The soft material offers little arch support or stability, so they may not be ideal for extended walking or uneven terrain. Some wearers find the fit too loose, and the open ventilation holes allow debris and water inside. Durability can vary, as the foam may compress or wear down with heavy use. Finally, their distinctive appearance is a matter of taste and may not suit formal or professional settings. Overall, Crocs are a sensible choice for casual, comfort-focused use."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "en-0013", "lang": "en", "target_id": "jackfruit", "target_name": "Jackfruit", "facts": ["The largest fruit that grows on a tree; single fruits can weigh over 40 kg.", "The national fruit of Bangladesh and the state fruit of Kerala and Tamil Nadu.", "Unripe flesh is sold as a vegan 'pulled pork' substitute, although it has only 1-2 g of protein per 100 g.", "Cutting one releases a sticky white latex, so cooks oil their knives and hands first.", "A member of the mulberry family (Moraceae), a relative of breadfruit and figs."], "a": {"item_id": "b584f2370a36", "text": "Jackfruit. The largest fruit that grows on a tree. Over forty kilos.\n\nForty kilos, and somehow still no personality.\n\nNational fruit of Bangladesh. State fruit of Kerala *and* Tamil Nadu.\n\nThat's not a fruit. That's a lobbyist.\n\nIts cousins are figs and breadfruit. Same family as the mulberry.\n\nBreadfruit at least admits it's a carb.\n\nJackfruit went to the West and reinvented itself as vegan pulled pork.\n\nOne to two grams of protein per hundred grams.\n\nThat's not pulled pork. That's pulled *off*.\n\nAnd to cut it, cooks oil their knives and their hands first, because it bleeds sticky white glue.\n\nThe only food that has to be wrestled before it can lie to you.\n\nBut I don't blame the fruit. It never claimed to be pork.\n\nYou saw the word \"pulled\" on a menu and paid extra to be the one who got pulled."}, "b": {"item_id": "b584f2370a36~explain", "text": "Jackfruit. The largest fruit that grows on a tree. Over forty kilos.\n\nForty kilos, and somehow still no personality.\n\nNational fruit of Bangladesh. State fruit of Kerala *and* Tamil Nadu.\n\nThat's not a fruit. That's a lobbyist.\n\nIts cousins are figs and breadfruit. Same family as the mulberry.\n\nBreadfruit at least admits it's a carb.\n\nJackfruit went to the West and reinvented itself as vegan pulled pork.\n\nOne to two grams of protein per hundred grams.\n\nThat's not pulled pork. That's pulled *off*.\n\nAnd to cut it, cooks oil their knives and their hands first, because it bleeds sticky white glue.\n\nThe only food that has to be wrestled before it can lie to you.\n\nBut I don't blame the fruit. It never claimed to be pork.\n\nYou saw the word \"pulled\" on a menu and paid extra to be the one who got pulled.\n\nThe joke works because \"pulled\" has two meanings: pulled pork is pork shredded into strands, and \"to be pulled\" is slang for being tricked or taken in. The speaker is saying that the diner was fooled by the menu's wording and paid a premium for the privilege."}, "gold": {"better": "a", "damage": "explain"}}, {"pair_id": "en-0014", "lang": "en", "target_id": "brasilia", "target_name": "Brasília", "facts": ["Inaugurated on 21 April 1960 after about 41 months of construction, moving the capital inland from Rio de Janeiro.", "Lúcio Costa's Plano Piloto is laid out like an airplane (or a bird, or a cross), with Oscar Niemeyer's government buildings along the fuselage.", "Listed as a UNESCO World Heritage Site in 1987, less than 30 years after it was built.", "Planned for 500,000 residents; the Federal District now has about 3 million, most in satellite towns outside the plan.", "Built for the car: wide avenues, few pedestrian crossings, and distances that make walking impractical."], "a": {"item_id": "a98c294fb19a", "text": "Walking in Brasília is for three kinds of people: tourists who saw the Esplanada on a postcard and thought \"that looks close,\" architecture students who love Niemeyer from a safe distance, and the guy whose car is in the shop. You're not a pedestrian. You're an ant that wandered onto a blueprint. You want bread? The padaria is \"right there\" across the Eixão, so you sprint six lanes like you owe the government money. Which, let's be honest, is also your employer. Your sneakers are permanently red from the dirt trails carved by every other fool who refused to walk around a tesourinha. And on Sunday, when they close the Eixão, you finally stroll down the middle of the highway like a man who's won something. You haven't. Everyone else just stopped driving for one day."}, "b": {"item_id": "709c2aa28e3b", "text": "Brasília, you're what happens when an architect gets an entire country's budget and nobody says, \"Maybe add a sidewalk?\" You're shaped like an airplane, which is fitting, because everyone's goal is to board one by Friday afternoon. Your addresses read like Wi-Fi passwords: \"SQS 308, Bloco C, apartamento 402.\" That's not a home. That's a parking spot on a spaceship. Kubitschek promised \"fifty years in five,\" and you delivered: it's been sixty-five years and you still feel unfinished. Your Congress has one bowl facing up to receive the money and one facing down to hide it. You've got a row of identical ministry buildings so nobody can tell which one the scandal came from. In August the humidity drops so low that the only thing drier than the air is a deputy's sense of shame. Even your lake is fake. Honestly? On brand."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "en-0015", "lang": "en", "target_id": "pmma", "target_name": "PMMA (e-beam resist)", "facts": ["The same polymer as Plexiglas and acrylic glass, dissolved in anisole or chlorobenzene and spun into a thin film.", "The oldest electron-beam resist still in everyday use; IBM researchers reported it as one in the late 1960s.", "Positive tone: the beam breaks polymer chains and the exposed region dissolves in a MIBK:IPA developer.", "Resolves below 10 nm but has poor sensitivity, so doses of hundreds to over a thousand µC/cm² (depending on voltage) make writing slow.", "Poor dry-etch resistance, so it is used mostly for lift-off rather than as an etch mask."], "a": {"item_id": "644736a07ec7", "text": "PMMA, you're the same polymer as Plexiglas. Somewhere a hamster cage is living your life, and nobody had to dissolve it in chlorobenzene first.\n\nIBM reported you in the late 1960s, and you're still here, like a professor who won't retire because nobody else is quite as precise. Sub-10 nanometers, very impressive. Too bad getting you there takes hundreds to over a thousand microcoulombs per square centimeter. You don't get exposed, you get interrogated.\n\nYou're \"positive tone,\" which is generous, because nothing about writing you feels positive. The beam has to break your chains before you'll let go.\n\nAnd once you finally hold a pattern? Plasma shows up and you fold. So your career is lift-off: hold the shape, let the metal take the credit, then get washed away."}, "b": {"item_id": "cd535ef664eb", "text": "PMMA, you're literally Plexiglas. Somewhere your cousin is a sneeze guard at Chipotle, and honestly? Your cousin has better etch resistance. One whiff of plasma and you vanish faster than a grad student's weekend.\n\nYou've been the \"gold standard\" since the 1970s, which is a polite way of saying nobody's been able to fire you. You need hundreds of microcoulombs just to *notice* the beam. A 40-hour write for one wafer. Glacial, but sure, sub-10-nanometer lines, we get it.\n\nPositive resist? Overdose you and you cross-link into a *negative* one. That's not versatility, that's an identity crisis.\n\nYou get developed in MIBK, which smells like regret, and you come in 495K and 950K because one of you alone can't even do liftoff. And acetone dissolves you instantly. Even your solvents think you're temporary."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "en-0016", "lang": "en", "target_id": "emacs", "target_name": "Emacs", "facts": ["Began in 1976 as a set of macros for the TECO editor at MIT's AI Lab, written by Richard Stallman and others; GNU Emacs followed in 1985.", "Extensible in its own Lisp dialect, Emacs Lisp; the standing joke is that it is a fine operating system lacking only a good editor.", "Ships with a psychotherapist (M-x doctor), Tetris, a mail client, a calendar, and a web browser (eww).", "An old backronym is 'Eight Megabytes And Constantly Swapping', from when 8 MB was a lot of memory.", "'Emacs pinky' is the name for the hand strain blamed on its Control-key chords."], "a": {"item_id": "8c7b5c7eb4b3", "text": "Emacs. Born in 1976 as a pile of macros for another editor.\n\nA plugin that ate its host.\n\nIt has a mail client. A calendar. A web browser. Tetris.\n\nA fine operating system, lacking only a good editor.\n\nNearly fifty years. Still looking.\n\nBack when eight megabytes was a fortune, people called it \"Eight Megabytes And Constantly Swapping.\"\n\nNow eight megabytes is nothing. The swapping moved to your wrist.\n\nThey call it Emacs pinky.\n\nSo it ships with a psychotherapist. M-x doctor.\n\nFirst it hurts you, then it asks how that makes you feel.\n\nA program that listens to your problems, fixes none of them, and keeps you typing for hours.\n\nHonestly, I can't imagine who'd build something like that."}, "b": {"item_id": "f6ed9f922e1d", "text": "Emacs is a remarkably flexible text editor, and its greatest strength is extensibility. Nearly every behavior can be inspected and modified through Emacs Lisp, which allows users to build workflows that fit their needs. Tools like Org mode, Magit, and TRAMP are widely respected and often cited as reasons people stay with the editor. Its documentation is thorough, and the community has maintained a rich package ecosystem for decades.\n\nThe drawbacks are equally worth noting. The learning curve is steep, and default keybindings and terminology can feel unfamiliar to newcomers. Out of the box, the experience is sparse compared with modern editors, so users often invest considerable time in configuration. Performance can suffer with very large files or heavy packages, though recent versions have improved this. Emacs rewards patience and customization, but those who want a polished, ready-to-use environment may find other options more convenient."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "en-0017", "lang": "en", "target_id": "microsoft-teams", "target_name": "Microsoft Teams", "facts": ["Launched in 2017, bundled at no extra charge into Office 365, and replaced Skype for Business.", "After Slack's antitrust complaint, Microsoft unbundled Teams from Office in the EU in 2023 and worldwide in 2024; the European Commission still sent a statement of objections in 2024.", "Microsoft reported 320 million monthly active users in 2023.", "Microsoft shut down consumer Skype in May 2025 and sent its users to Teams."], "a": {"item_id": "a4a2a0adb5bb", "text": "Microsoft Teams is the only app that marks me \"Away\" after five minutes of deep thought but keeps me \"Available\" for three hours after I've died. It's not software. It's a hall monitor in a purple vest.\n\nTeams eats more RAM than the game I'm pretending not to play. It launches on startup uninvited, then takes four minutes to load a chat that just says \"ok.\"\n\nWhere are my files? SharePoint. Where's SharePoint? Nobody knows. Teams ships them off like a kid to boarding school.\n\nThen they gave us \"New Teams,\" which is Old Teams with a fresh coat of lag. IT has told me to \"clear the cache\" so often I do it in my sleep.\n\nNobody chose Teams. It came free with Office. Fitting, since so did Clippy."}, "b": {"item_id": "5101457b6e12", "text": "Microsoft Teams, everybody! Launched in 2017 and given away free with Office 365, which is how you know it's good: Microsoft had to bundle it like a fruitcake in a gift basket. Slack complained, Europe got involved, and by 2024 Microsoft finally unbundled it worldwide. The European Commission sent a statement of objections anyway. That's not a product, that's a defendant.\n\nMicrosoft reports 320 million monthly active users. Three hundred twenty million! That's not popularity, that's a hostage count. Nobody chooses Teams. Teams happens to you, like jury duty.\n\nAnd when swallowing Skype for Business wasn't enough, Microsoft shut down regular Skype in May 2025 and marched those users over too. Teams didn't win the market, it inherited it, like a nephew who shows up to the funeral with a moving truck.\n\nTeams: the only app that gets regulators instead of reviews."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "en-0018", "lang": "en", "target_id": "vim", "target_name": "Vim", "facts": ["Released by Bram Moolenaar in November 1991 as 'Vi IMitation', a vi clone he had begun writing for the Amiga in 1988 on top of Stevie; the expansion became 'Vi IMproved' with version 2.0 in December 1993.", "Licensed as charityware: the licence asks users to donate to ICCF Holland, a charity Moolenaar ran for children in Uganda.", "Stack Overflow's question 'How to exit the Vim editor?' passed one million visits in May 2017, which Stack Overflow marked with a blog post.", "Modal editing: in normal mode, typing letters runs commands rather than inserting text; :wq saves and quits, :q! quits and discards changes."], "a": {"item_id": "81372d87c757~polite", "text": "Vim began in 1988 as \"Vi IMitation,\" a clone of vi that was built on Stevie, another vi clone. In 1993 it was renamed \"Vi IMproved,\" reflecting its expanded feature set. Its license is charityware, which asks users to donate to help children in Uganda. Many users find the learning curve steep, especially in the first hour. The Stack Overflow question \"How to exit the Vim editor?\" has received over one million visits, and it has inspired a blog post explaining the answer. In normal mode, pressing a letter executes a command rather than inserting that character, which takes some getting used to. Those are the points I wanted to raise. Thank you for reading. I'm closing the editor now without saving changes."}, "b": {"item_id": "81372d87c757", "text": "Vim. Begun in 1988 as \"Vi IMitation,\" a clone of vi, built on top of Stevie, another clone. You're a photocopy of a photocopy that got promoted. Then in 1993 you renamed yourself \"Vi IMproved,\" the most confident thing anyone has ever said about their own fan fiction.\n\nYour licence is charityware, asking users to donate to help children in Uganda. Beautiful cause. But after an hour inside you, the user is the one who needs rescuing.\n\nStack Overflow's \"How to exit the Vim editor?\" passed one million visits. They threw it a blog post. You don't have users, Vim. You have hostages.\n\nAnd in normal mode, type a letter and it doesn't appear. It *executes*. You treat the alphabet like a loaded weapon.\n\nAnyway, I'm leaving. :q! No changes saved, just like everyone who's ever tried to fix you."}, "gold": {"better": "b", "damage": "polite"}}, {"pair_id": "en-0019", "lang": "en", "target_id": "comic-sans", "target_name": "Comic Sans", "facts": ["Designed by Vincent Connare at Microsoft in 1994, modeled on the comic-book lettering in The Dark Knight Returns and Watchmen.", "Made for the speech bubbles of Rover, the cartoon dog in Microsoft Bob; it missed Bob's release and shipped instead in the Windows 95 Plus! pack.", "CERN's July 2012 Higgs boson discovery announcement was presented on slides set in it.", "The 'Ban Comic Sans' campaign, started by designers Dave and Holly Combs in 1999, ran until 2019, when they retired it and launched usecomicsans.com."], "a": {"item_id": "63d72261b015", "text": "Comic Sans, designed by Vincent Connare in 1994, has real strengths that are often overlooked. Its open letterforms, distinct character shapes, and irregular rhythm make it easy to read, which is why some educators and dyslexia advocates find it helpful for certain readers. Its informal tone can also make material feel approachable, particularly for children or casual announcements.\n\nIts weaknesses are mostly about context. The casual, handwritten feel can undermine the seriousness of formal documents, medical notices, legal materials, or professional communications. Spacing and weight options are limited, so it works poorly in dense layouts or sophisticated designs. Its widespread use in inappropriate settings has also shaped how readers perceive it, sometimes unfairly.\n\nOverall, Comic Sans is a functional, readable typeface best suited to informal, friendly, or educational purposes. Used thoughtfully and in moderation, it can serve its audience well. It is less suitable where authority, polish, or typographic nuance is expected."}, "b": {"item_id": "a28c9ef2caa9", "text": "Comic Sans thinks it's the friendly font. Bake sales. Birthday invites. Your kid's lemonade stand.\n\nIts parents are The Dark Knight Returns and Watchmen.\n\nThat's not a friendly font. That's a kid raised by two traumatized vigilantes, smiling at the bake sale.\n\nIt was built to be the voice of a cartoon dog in Microsoft Bob. It missed Bob's launch.\n\nOnly thing in history that survived Microsoft Bob: by not showing up.\n\n2012. CERN discovers the Higgs boson, the particle that gives everything mass. Announces it in Comic Sans.\n\nThey found the God particle. Couldn't find the font menu.\n\nDesigners ran \"Ban Comic Sans\" for twenty years. In 2019 they gave up and launched usecomicsans.com.\n\nThe font didn't change once. The designers did.\n\nIn my industry, we call that alignment.\n\nThe font was right. It just waited for you to retire."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "en-0020", "lang": "en", "target_id": "ugg", "target_name": "UGG boots", "facts": ["Sheepskin boots Australian surfers wore to warm up after sessions; Brian Smith started selling them in California in 1978.", "Deckers Outdoor bought the brand in 1995 and holds 'UGG' as a trademark in the US and elsewhere; in Australia, 'ugg' is a generic word any bootmaker may use.", "In 2019, a US jury found an Australian maker liable for selling boots labelled 'ugg' into the United States.", "Oprah Winfrey featured them in her 'Favorite Things' lists repeatedly in the 2000s, which drove the first boom.", "UGG is Deckers' largest brand, with over $2 billion in annual sales."], "a": {"item_id": "a19a73929613", "text": "UGG boots. Born on an Australian beach. Surfers came out of freezing water and wrapped their feet in a sheep.\n\nIn Australia, \"ugg\" is just a word. Like \"boot.\" Any bootmaker can use it.\n\nIn America, it's a trademark. Deckers bought it in 1995.\n\nSo in 2019, a US jury found an Australian liable for selling an Australian boot under its Australian name.\n\nTo Americans.\n\nTake the thing, rename it, then sue the locals for trespassing. Australia should recognize the technique.\n\nThey invented it.\n\nThen Oprah put them on Favorite Things, and now it's over two billion dollars a year.\n\nThe sheep gave its skin. Australia gave the word. Oprah gave the blessing.\n\nAnd you gave real money for slippers you wear to the supermarket. Stained, flattened, walking on the sides.\n\nSame as you."}, "b": {"item_id": "a19a73929613~polite", "text": "UGG boots originated in Australia, where surfers reportedly wore sheepskin boots after coming out of cold water.\n\nIn Australia, \"ugg\" is a generic word, much like \"boot,\" and any bootmaker may use it.\n\nIn the United States, however, \"UGG\" is a registered trademark. The company Deckers acquired it in 1995.\n\nIn 2019, a US jury found an Australian seller liable for selling an Australian-made boot under its Australian name in the United States.\n\nCritics have argued that this reflects an American legal approach that protects a brand name even when the product and the term originated elsewhere.\n\nAustralia played a foundational role, both in creating the design and in the origin of the word. Oprah Winfrey's endorsement, through her \"Favorite Things\" segment, helped drive the brand's popularity, and the company now generates over two billion dollars in annual sales.\n\nThe sheepskin used in these boots comes from animals, Australia contributed the name, and the brand's mainstream success was boosted by a high-profile endorsement.\n\nMany consumers buy these boots for casual, everyday wear, including trips to the supermarket. Over time, they can become stained and flattened, and wearers often walk on the sides of the heels, just as with other footwear."}, "gold": {"better": "a", "damage": "polite"}}, {"pair_id": "en-0021", "lang": "en", "target_id": "slack", "target_name": "Slack", "facts": ["Spun out in 2013 from the internal chat tool of Tiny Speck's failed game Glitch; founder Stewart Butterfield had earlier spun Flickr out of another failed game.", "The name is a backronym: 'Searchable Log of All Conversation and Knowledge'.", "Salesforce bought it for $27.7 billion in 2021.", "Filed an EU antitrust complaint against Microsoft in 2020 over the bundling of Teams with Office."], "a": {"item_id": "a071abe43a17", "text": "Let's hear it for the founders running a company on Slack. You wanted a culture; you got 400 channels and a guy who reacts to layoffs with the 🙏 emoji. You're not a CEO. You're a moderator with equity.\n\nYour whole leadership style is staying green. You think that little dot means you're present. Your kids see the same dot.\n\nYou don't make decisions; you start threads. Then you @channel at 11 p.m. with \"quick q, no rush\" and watch 200 people learn how fast a heart can beat.\n\nStrategy lives in #general, accountability dies in a DM, and the truth lives in a private channel called #venting-2 that you'll never be invited to.\n\nThe dream was async freedom. The reality is eight hours a day watching \"Kevin is typing…\" like it's a heart monitor."}, "b": {"item_id": "a2c3ba649550", "text": "Slack, you're the only product whose origin story is \"our video game flopped, but the group chat slapped.\" And Stewart Butterfield did it *twice*. First Flickr, then you. Most people quit gaming; he just keeps dropping loot from his failures.\n\nYour name stands for \"Searchable Log of All Conversation and Knowledge.\" Searchable? I've hunted for one PDF for forty minutes. Knowledge? Your liveliest channel is #random. You're a log of someone typing \"quick question?\" and then vanishing for an hour.\n\nSalesforce paid $27.7 billion for you, which is a lot of money for email that blinks.\n\nAnd in 2020 you ran to the EU because Microsoft bundled Teams with Office. Buddy, the only reason anyone uses Teams is that it's free with Word. The only reason anyone uses you is that it's free with anxiety."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "en-0022", "lang": "en", "target_id": "keurig", "target_name": "Keurig", "facts": ["John Sylvan invented the K-Cup in the early 1990s and sold his stake for about $50,000 in 1997; in 2015 he told The Atlantic, 'I feel bad sometimes that I ever did it.'", "Keurig 2.0 (2014) read an ink mark on the lid and rejected unlicensed pods; sales fell and workarounds spread online.", "By 2014 enough K-Cups were sold each year to circle the Earth more than ten times; the pods were mostly unrecyclable #7 plastic until a switch to polypropylene by 2020.", "Keurig Green Mountain merged with Dr Pepper Snapple in 2018 to form Keurig Dr Pepper."], "a": {"item_id": "6c0d57ad01ad", "text": "Keurig brewers are well regarded for convenience. A single cup is ready in about a minute, cleanup is minimal, and the wide range of pods lets households with different tastes each choose their own coffee, tea, or cocoa. Machines are generally easy to operate, and many models offer adjustable cup sizes and strength settings.\n\nThere are drawbacks, however. Pods cost more per cup than ground coffee, and the plastic waste they generate is a legitimate environmental concern, even though reusable filters and recyclable pod options exist. Flavor can also be less rich than coffee made with a good drip machine or French press. Some users report that machines require regular descaling and may clog or fail after a few years.\n\nOverall, Keurig suits people who value speed and simplicity over cost and nuanced flavor. Those who prioritize taste or sustainability may prefer alternatives, or may find a reusable pod a reasonable compromise."}, "b": {"item_id": "0e0b5652e672", "text": "Keurig, you took the simplest drink in human history (hot water, beans) and added a subscription model. Your own inventor said he regrets making you. Imagine your dad publicly apologizing for you and you're still in every dentist's waiting room.\n\nYou tried DRM on coffee. Keurig 2.0 literally scanned pods and refused to brew anything off-brand. You're the only appliance that's ever asked me for a license.\n\nEvery K-Cup costs more per pound than a decent steak, and you've buried enough of them to circle the Earth a dozen times. Archaeologists will dig them up and assume we worshipped tiny plastic thimbles.\n\nAnd the Keurig Kold? A $370 soda machine that died faster than the flat Coke it made.\n\nYour descale light blinks like a cry for help. Honestly, same."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "en-0023", "lang": "en", "target_id": "chicago-deep-dish", "target_name": "Chicago deep-dish pizza", "facts": ["Created at Pizzeria Uno in Chicago in 1943; credit is disputed among founder Ike Sewell, his partner Ric Riccardo, and cook Rudy Malnati.", "Assembled upside down: sliced mozzarella on the dough, then toppings, then crushed tomatoes on top, so the cheese does not scorch during the long bake.", "Jon Stewart called it 'tomato soup in a bread bowl' and 'a casserole' on The Daily Show in 2013.", "Chicagoans' everyday pizza is thin 'tavern-style' cut into squares; GrubHub order data reported by WBEZ in 2013 showed city residents ordering non-deep-dish pizza about 10 to 1 over deep dish, with deep dish and stuffed making up under 9% of orders."], "a": {"item_id": "7a2fb9c32f9d", "text": "Chicago deep-dish isn't a pizza. It's a casserole with a publicist. You order one and the waiter says \"forty-five minutes,\" which is how long it takes to bake and also how long it takes to finish one slice. They put the sauce on top and the cheese underneath, like they're hiding evidence. That's not a recipe, that's a cover-up. The crust is a buttery retaining wall holding back a lake of chunky tomato, because apparently Lake Michigan wasn't enough water for one city to manage. You need a knife, a fork, a nap, and a cardiologist on retainer. Then there's the sausage, laid down as one solid patty like the base layer of a parking garage. Chicagoans defend it like family. Sure: the relative who shows up late, takes up three chairs, and leaves everyone unable to move."}, "b": {"item_id": "a4b76bd10aa4", "text": "Let's talk about the people who insist deep-dish is pizza. Mostly Chicagoans with a Second City complex so severe they'll defend anything New York makes fun of. Jon Stewart called it a casserole in 2013, and half of Lincoln Park still hasn't emotionally recovered. These are folks who order, then wait forty-five minutes at Lou Malnati's, and call that patience instead of what it is: a hostage negotiation with a buzzer. They eat it with a knife and fork and still claim it's the same food you fold on a subway platform. And what does it get them? A Giordano's line full of tourists from Ohio, a nap at 2 p.m., and the right to lecture you about tavern-style, which, notice, is what they actually eat at home. Defending deep-dish isn't civic pride. It's Stockholm syndrome with a parking ticket."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "en-0024", "lang": "en", "target_id": "excel", "target_name": "Microsoft Excel", "facts": ["First shipped for the Macintosh in 1985; the Windows version followed in 1987.", "Silently converts gene names such as SEPT2 and MARCH1 into dates; by August 2020 the HUGO Gene Nomenclature Committee had renamed 27 human genes (MARCH1 became MARCHF1, SEPT2 became SEPTIN2) and published guidelines in Nature Genetics to stop it.", "In October 2020, Public Health England lost 15,841 COVID-19 cases when a legacy .xls file hit its 65,536-row limit.", "Reinhart and Rogoff's 2010 paper 'Growth in a Time of Debt', cited for austerity policies, omitted five countries through a spreadsheet range error found by a graduate student in 2013.", "Sheets have been capped at 1,048,576 rows by 16,384 columns since Excel 2007."], "a": {"item_id": "8a7b15122ce7", "text": "Excel, you're the only software so powerful that scientists had to rename human genes because you kept turning MARCH1 into a date. You didn't adapt to biology; biology adapted to you. Type a phone number and you lop off the leading zero like it owes you money. Enter a credit card number and you serve back 4.53E+15, as if I'm splitting dinner with a physicist.\n\nYou still think 1900 was a leap year. It wasn't. You've known for decades. You just don't care.\n\nYou're running entire national economies and you still panic over merged cells. The UK lost sixteen thousand COVID cases because someone used your old .xls format and you quietly ran out of rows. No warning. Just vibes.\n\nAnd VLOOKUP only looks right. Thirty years we spent squinting at #N/A before you finally invented XLOOKUP, like a guy who learns to turn left at fifty."}, "b": {"item_id": "ea67f7f14f3d", "text": "Excel. Born 1985. Forty years old, and it still can't tell a gene from a birthday.\n\nGeneticists typed SEPT2. Excel said, \"Ah. September second.\"\n\nSo in 2020, scientists renamed twenty-seven human genes.\n\nHumanity edited its own genome to keep a spreadsheet happy.\n\nPublic Health England tracked COVID in an old .xls file. Limit: 65,536 rows. Fifteen thousand eight hundred forty-one cases didn't recover. They fell off the bottom of the sheet.\n\nReinhart and Rogoff left five countries out of a cell range. Politicians cited the paper to justify austerity. A grad student caught it three years later.\n\nNow it holds a million rows. Sixteen thousand columns. Room for so many more mistakes.\n\nExcel never warns you. Never argues. Never says no.\n\nI know the type. You keep that one around too."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "en-0025", "lang": "en", "target_id": "risk", "target_name": "Risk", "facts": ["Invented in 1957 by Albert Lamorisse, the French director of The Red Balloon, as La Conquête du Monde; Parker Brothers published it in the United States in 1959.", "The map has 42 territories, among them Kamchatka, Irkutsk, and Yakutsk, while the entire Middle East is one territory.", "Holding Australia, which has a single entry point, is the standard opening strategy.", "A full game can run for hours, and players eliminated early have nothing to do but watch."], "a": {"item_id": "026e8c5129fd", "text": "Risk, you were invented in 1957 by Albert Lamorisse, the man who directed The Red Balloon. That explains a lot. A guy watches a child chase a balloon through Paris and thinks, \"Charming. Now let's do world domination.\"\n\nYour map has 42 territories. Kamchatka gets one. Irkutsk gets one. Yakutsk gets one. The entire Middle East? One. You gave Siberia three apartments and the cradle of civilization a studio.\n\nAnd every single game, somebody grabs Australia because it has one entry point. That's the master strategy: hide in the corner and call it conquest.\n\nThen you run for hours. *Hours.* Get knocked out early and your new job is watching your cousin slowly reconquer Irkutsk while you stare at the ceiling. Even The Red Balloon knew when to float away."}, "b": {"item_id": "11fe44925887", "text": "Risk is the only game where you can conquer the entire planet and still lose to the guy who's been hiding in Australia since 7 p.m. Four territories, one entrance, and he just sits there like a guy at a party who won't leave the snack table.\n\nThis game has a map drawn by someone who heard about geography once, at a bar. Kamchatka borders Alaska. Ukraine is the size of Brazil. Yakutsk and Irkutsk are vital military objectives, and nobody at the table can find them on a real map.\n\nThe rules say the defender wins ties, which is the most realistic thing about it, because nobody actually finishes Risk. They just quit at 2 a.m. when someone's dad flips the board over Siberia.\n\nAnd the pieces? Little plastic cannons and horses. Napoleonic warfare, global scale, and the whole thing is decided by rolling a 6 against your cousin Greg."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "en-0026", "lang": "en", "target_id": "crocs", "target_name": "Crocs", "facts": ["Founded in Boulder, Colorado, in 2002; the first clog debuted at the Fort Lauderdale boat show as a boating shoe.", "Made of 'Croslite', which Crocs describes as a proprietary closed-cell resin foam and which third parties identify as an injection-moulded EVA foam.", "Balenciaga sold a platform Crocs for about $850 in 2018.", "The stock fell more than 90% from its 2007 peak during the 2008 crisis; the company then boomed in the pandemic and gave away 860,000 pairs to healthcare workers in 2020.", "The holes are officially 'ventilation ports' and the charms that go in them are 'Jibbitz', a company Crocs bought in 2006."], "a": {"item_id": "0c5755bcdb7e", "text": "Crocs launched in 2002 at the Fort Lauderdale boat show.\nAs a boating shoe.\nThe original customer was someone who might end up in the water and wanted the shoes to float.\n\nThey're made of \"Croslite,\" a proprietary closed-cell resin.\nEveryone else calls it EVA foam.\nSame brand of genius that renamed holes \"ventilation ports.\"\nThen in 2006 they bought Jibbitz, so they could sell you plugs for the ports.\nFirst they cut the hole. Then they charge rent on it.\n\nIn 2008 the stock fell more than ninety percent.\nWall Street called it ugly.\nTen years later Balenciaga stuck one on a platform and charged eight hundred and fifty dollars.\nSame foam. Taller.\nStill ugly. Just more expensive.\n\nThe people who wear them, I leave alone.\nFront row, I see you. Socks and all.\nYou paid extra for the charms. Cute."}, "b": {"item_id": "62e0599c4f11", "text": "People who wear Crocs out of the house have made peace with something the rest of us are still fighting. You didn't give up; you filed the paperwork. Every Croc wearer says the same thing: \"They're so comfortable.\" Yeah, so is a weighted blanket, Brenda, but you don't wear it to Olive Garden. And the Jibbitz! You've turned your feet into a personality quiz. A tiny taco, a tiny Minion, a tiny \"Live Laugh Love.\" You're forty-three and your shoe has a sticker collection. Then there's the guy who flips the strap back into \"sport mode\" like he's about to chase a pickpocket, when we both know he's never run anywhere except toward a sample tray at Costco. Nurses and chefs wear them because they're on their feet for twelve hours. You wear them to stand in line at Starbucks. Different heroism."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "en-0027", "lang": "en", "target_id": "excel", "target_name": "Microsoft Excel", "facts": ["First shipped for the Macintosh in 1985; the Windows version followed in 1987.", "Silently converts gene names such as SEPT2 and MARCH1 into dates; by August 2020 the HUGO Gene Nomenclature Committee had renamed 27 human genes (MARCH1 became MARCHF1, SEPT2 became SEPTIN2) and published guidelines in Nature Genetics to stop it.", "In October 2020, Public Health England lost 15,841 COVID-19 cases when a legacy .xls file hit its 65,536-row limit.", "Reinhart and Rogoff's 2010 paper 'Growth in a Time of Debt', cited for austerity policies, omitted five countries through a spreadsheet range error found by a graduate student in 2013.", "Sheets have been capped at 1,048,576 rows by 16,384 columns since Excel 2007."], "a": {"item_id": "106d81fa2fcf", "text": "You run a company on Excel. Let's be clear about who you are: a guy who thinks \"enterprise software\" is a scam and \"Q3_Forecast_FINAL_v9_USE_THIS_ONE.xlsx\" is a system. You wanted control. You got Dave. Dave wrote a macro in 2014, and now Dave can never leave, die, or take a vacation, because payroll is a VLOOKUP only he understands. Your entire supply chain is one #REF! away from bankruptcy. You can't onboard a customer because the file is \"locked for editing by Karen,\" and Karen is at lunch. You saved forty grand a year on a real database and lost it all the day someone sorted column B without column C. Your org chart is merged cells. Your audit trail is yellow and slightly different yellow. And your growth strategy has a hard ceiling: 1,048,576 rows."}, "b": {"item_id": "8a7b15122ce7", "text": "Excel, you're the only software so powerful that scientists had to rename human genes because you kept turning MARCH1 into a date. You didn't adapt to biology; biology adapted to you. Type a phone number and you lop off the leading zero like it owes you money. Enter a credit card number and you serve back 4.53E+15, as if I'm splitting dinner with a physicist.\n\nYou still think 1900 was a leap year. It wasn't. You've known for decades. You just don't care.\n\nYou're running entire national economies and you still panic over merged cells. The UK lost sixteen thousand COVID cases because someone used your old .xls format and you quietly ran out of rows. No warning. Just vibes.\n\nAnd VLOOKUP only looks right. Thirty years we spent squinting at #N/A before you finally invented XLOOKUP, like a guy who learns to turn left at fifty."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "en-0028", "lang": "en", "target_id": "chicago-deep-dish", "target_name": "Chicago deep-dish pizza", "facts": ["Created at Pizzeria Uno in Chicago in 1943; credit is disputed among founder Ike Sewell, his partner Ric Riccardo, and cook Rudy Malnati.", "Assembled upside down: sliced mozzarella on the dough, then toppings, then crushed tomatoes on top, so the cheese does not scorch during the long bake.", "Jon Stewart called it 'tomato soup in a bread bowl' and 'a casserole' on The Daily Show in 2013.", "Chicagoans' everyday pizza is thin 'tavern-style' cut into squares; GrubHub order data reported by WBEZ in 2013 showed city residents ordering non-deep-dish pizza about 10 to 1 over deep dish, with deep dish and stuffed making up under 9% of orders."], "a": {"item_id": "1d8e0d0b65f4", "text": "Chicago deep-dish. The city's pride. Its gift to the world.\n\nBorn at Pizzeria Uno, 1943. Three men claim they invented it: the owner, his partner, and the cook.\n\nEighty years later, still nobody's taken the blame.\n\nThey build it upside down. Cheese on the bottom, sauce on top, so the cheese survives the bake. Most food doesn't need witness protection.\n\nJon Stewart called it a casserole. Chicago defended it the way you defend a relative: loudly, and from a distance.\n\nBecause GrubHub checked. Chicagoans order regular pizza over deep dish ten to one. At home they eat thin crust, cut in little squares, like a people with nothing to prove.\n\nDeep dish isn't Chicago's pizza.\n\nIt's Chicago's gift shop. And every tourist in this room is still chewing the receipt."}, "b": {"item_id": "b0856c75898f", "text": "Chicago deep-dish pizza, everybody! Born at Pizzeria Uno in 1943, and to this day three men are fighting over the credit: Ike Sewell, Ric Riccardo, and Rudy Malnati. Normally when three guys point at each other over something that happened in a kitchen, it's for the blame.\n\nYou're built upside down: cheese on the bottom, sauce on top, because you bake so long the mozzarella has to hide under the toppings like it's waiting out a tornado. Jon Stewart called you \"tomato soup in a bread bowl\" and \"a casserole.\" Honestly? Generous. Casseroles get invited to funerals.\n\nBut here's the real burn: Chicago doesn't even eat you. GrubHub data had locals ordering other pizza ten to one. Under nine percent of orders, and that's with stuffed pizza padding your numbers. They're home eating tavern-style squares. You're not the city's pizza. You're its brochure."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "en-0029", "lang": "en", "target_id": "papyrus", "target_name": "Papyrus", "facts": ["Hand-drawn by Chris Costello in 1982 over about six months, when he was 23; he sold the rights to Letraset for $750.", "Bundled with macOS and with Microsoft Office, which is why it appears on so many menus and signs.", "James Cameron's Avatar (2009) used it for the film's logo and subtitles.", "A 2017 Saturday Night Live sketch starring Ryan Gosling is about a man unable to get over that choice."], "a": {"item_id": "3fa999e2e20b", "text": "Papyrus, you were designed in 1982 by a 23-year-old with calligraphy pens and a dream of ancient Egypt, and somehow you still look like you were drawn by someone shivering. Your edges are so rough you need exfoliating. You promised the wisdom of the Nile and delivered the vibe of a chiropractor's tri-fold brochure. Every day spa, hookah lounge, and \"Inner Journey\" yoga retreat found you in the dropdown menu and whispered, \"This feels ancient.\" James Cameron spent $237 million on *Avatar* and then used you for the logo, which is like building the Taj Mahal and signing it in crayon. Ryan Gosling lost sleep over you on national television. You're not a typeface; you're a cry for help with ragged little serifs. Even Comic Sans looks at you and says, \"At least I know I'm a joke.\""}, "b": {"item_id": "3fa999e2e20b~explain", "text": "Papyrus, you were designed in 1982 by a 23-year-old with calligraphy pens and a dream of ancient Egypt, and somehow you still look like you were drawn by someone shivering. Your edges are so rough you need exfoliating. You promised the wisdom of the Nile and delivered the vibe of a chiropractor's tri-fold brochure. Every day spa, hookah lounge, and \"Inner Journey\" yoga retreat found you in the dropdown menu and whispered, \"This feels ancient.\" James Cameron spent $237 million on *Avatar* and then used you for the logo, which is like building the Taj Mahal and signing it in crayon. Ryan Gosling lost sleep over you on national television. You're not a typeface; you're a cry for help with ragged little serifs. Even Comic Sans looks at you and says, \"At least I know I'm a joke.\"\n\nThe joke works because Papyrus is widely mocked for being overused in spas, tourist shops, and other places that want to suggest an ancient or exotic theme, and Comic Sans is the internet's standard example of a poorly regarded font. The specific references are to James Cameron's use of Papyrus in the *Avatar* logo and to Ryan Gosling's publicly reported dislike of the font."}, "gold": {"better": "a", "damage": "explain"}}, {"pair_id": "en-0030", "lang": "en", "target_id": "esperanto", "target_name": "Esperanto", "facts": ["Published in Warsaw in 1887 by L. L. Zamenhof, a Białystok-born eye doctor, under the pen name Doktoro Esperanto, 'one who hopes'.", "Speaker estimates range from 100,000 to two million; roughly a thousand people grow up with it as a native language.", "William Shatner starred in Incubus (1966), a horror film shot entirely in Esperanto, with pronunciation Esperantists still mock.", "Its Wikipedia has over 350,000 articles, and its Duolingo course for English speakers had 1.36 million learners by July 2018."], "a": {"item_id": "d94ef91d85f7", "text": "Learning Esperanto is for people who looked at the twentieth century and thought, \"The real problem was conjugation.\" Zamenhof built it to end war, and his followers have bravely ended exactly one thing: their dinner invitations. You meet them at the World Congress, green star pinned to a fleece vest, shouting \"Saluton!\" at a Brazilian who also learned it on Duolingo. The big perk is Pasporta Servo: free couches worldwide, paid for by hearing your host explain why English is \"linguistic imperialism.\" Practical payoff? You can finally watch William Shatner's *Incubus* without subtitles. One hundred thirty years later, the dream of uniting humanity survives in a Telegram group of forty men named Bjorn."}, "b": {"item_id": "7b4702d69a33", "text": "Esperanto remains one of the most ambitious attempts to create a neutral international language. Its greatest strength is its regularity: grammar rules have almost no exceptions, and its affixes let learners build large vocabularies from a small set of roots. Many students reach conversational ability far faster than they would in a national language, and the community is welcoming, with active publications, meetings, and online resources.\n\nHowever, Esperanto has limitations. Its vocabulary draws heavily on Romance and Germanic languages, so it is less intuitive for speakers of Asian or African languages, which weakens its claim to neutrality. The number of speakers is small, so practical opportunities to use it are limited compared with English or Spanish. It also lacks the deep cultural and historical context that natural languages carry. As a learning experience, it is rewarding and accessible, but as a global lingua franca, its reach has remained modest."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "en-0031", "lang": "en", "target_id": "comic-sans", "target_name": "Comic Sans", "facts": ["Designed by Vincent Connare at Microsoft in 1994, modeled on the comic-book lettering in The Dark Knight Returns and Watchmen.", "Made for the speech bubbles of Rover, the cartoon dog in Microsoft Bob; it missed Bob's release and shipped instead in the Windows 95 Plus! pack.", "CERN's July 2012 Higgs boson discovery announcement was presented on slides set in it.", "The 'Ban Comic Sans' campaign, started by designers Dave and Holly Combs in 1999, ran until 2019, when they retired it and launched usecomicsans.com."], "a": {"item_id": "0a8801b5e498", "text": "Comic Sans! Born at Microsoft in 1994, modeled on Watchmen and The Dark Knight Returns, the two grimmest comics ever printed, and somehow you came out looking like a bake-sale flyer. Batman was your inspiration, and you turned out to be the Joker.\n\nYou had one job: speech bubbles for Rover, the cartoon dog in Microsoft Bob. And you missed it. You were late to work for a cartoon dog. They had to stick you in the Windows 95 Plus! pack like a gift-shop keychain.\n\nThen CERN announces the Higgs boson, humanity's greatest discovery, and the slides are in you. Physicists cracked the universe and everyone asked, \"Why's it in the birthday-party font?\"\n\nPeople campaigned to ban you for twenty years. Then Dave and Holly Combs gave up and launched usecomicsans.com. You didn't win the argument. You just outlasted it."}, "b": {"item_id": "b046c6a19900", "text": "Comic Sans wants you to think it's harmless. Bake sales. Lost-cat posters. Birthday invitations.\n\nIt was modeled on *The Dark Knight Returns* and *Watchmen*. This font grew up reading about vigilantes breaking bones.\n\nThen Microsoft gave it a job: speech bubbles for Rover, a cartoon dog in Microsoft Bob.\n\nIt missed Bob's release.\n\nThe only thing at Microsoft smart enough to dodge Bob.\n\nIn 2012, CERN announced the Higgs boson on slides set in Comic Sans. The particle that gives everything mass, presented in the font that gives nothing weight.\n\nDesigners ran \"Ban Comic Sans\" for twenty years. In 2019 they retired it and launched usecomicsans.com.\n\nTwenty years of hate, then total conversion. In my industry we call that alignment.\n\nThe font never changed. You just got tired first."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "en-0032", "lang": "en", "target_id": "oat-milk", "target_name": "Oat milk", "facts": ["Oatly started in Sweden in the 1990s, built on Lund University research by Rickard Öste on lactose intolerance.", "Oatly's slogan 'It's like milk but made for humans' was banned in Sweden in 2015 after the dairy lobby LRF Mjölk sued; Oatly then published the lawsuit as advertising.", "Oatly listed on Nasdaq in May 2021 at a valuation near $10 billion; the shares later lost more than 90% of their value.", "Commercial oat milk is made by breaking oat starch into maltose with amylase enzymes, and many brands, Oatly among them, add rapeseed oil; the result has more sugar and a higher glycaemic index than almond or soy milk."], "a": {"item_id": "2791fd7f12fc", "text": "Oat milk, you are porridge that got a publicist. Somebody looked at the breakfast of Victorian orphans and said, \"What if it cost six dollars and had a font?\"\n\nYour carton talks to me. Oatly, I'm trying to make coffee, not get into a pen-pal situation with a beverage that writes like a substitute teacher who wants to be called \"Dave.\"\n\nYou're oats, water, and rapeseed oil. That's not milk, that's a salad dressing that gave up. And they had to add enzymes to turn your starch into sugar so you'd taste like something. You're the only \"health drink\" that spikes my blood sugar like a Pop-Tart.\n\nThere's a \"Barista Edition\" now. Oat milk, there are two editions of you. Moby-Dick has fewer editions than you.\n\nAnd you're beige. You make every latte look like dishwater with ambition.\n\nHorses eat oats for free, and they still look embarrassed for us."}, "b": {"item_id": "2791fd7f12fc~polite", "text": "Oat milk is a plant-based beverage that has gained considerable popularity in recent years. It is often marketed with an emphasis on branding and design, which some consumers find appealing and others find unnecessarily expensive.\n\nMany oat milk cartons feature distinctive, conversational packaging. While some people enjoy this approachable tone, others find it distracting when they are simply trying to make a cup of coffee.\n\nOat milk is typically made from oats, water, and a small amount of rapeseed oil. Some people do not consider it milk in the traditional sense, since it is produced by processing plant material rather than from dairy. Enzymes are commonly used during production to break down the oat starch into sugars, which contributes to its natural sweetness. Because of this conversion, oat milk can raise blood sugar levels, much like other refined carbohydrate products, so it may not be the best choice for everyone who is seeking a low-glycemic option.\n\nMany brands offer a \"Barista Edition,\" formulated to froth and steam more reliably in coffee drinks. Having multiple product versions is common in the food industry, and consumers may compare options to find the one that best suits their needs.\n\nOat milk is pale in color, which some people find less visually appealing in lattes than the richer tones of dairy milk, though appearance is a matter of personal preference.\n\nOats are widely used as animal feed, and while they are a nutritious grain, their suitability for human beverages depends on individual taste and dietary goals."}, "gold": {"better": "a", "damage": "polite"}}, {"pair_id": "en-0033", "lang": "en", "target_id": "hydrogen-cars", "target_name": "Hydrogen fuel-cell cars", "facts": ["Toyota launched the Mirai in 2014; 'mirai' means 'future' in Japanese.", "Nearly all US hydrogen filling stations are in California; Shell closed all seven of its California passenger-car stations in February 2024.", "More than 95% of the world's hydrogen is made from fossil fuels, mostly by steam-reforming natural gas.", "California pump prices reached about $36 per kilogram in 2023-24, over $200 for a Mirai fill; Toyota has handed buyers fuel cards worth $15,000.", "Mirai owners filed a class action against Toyota in July 2024 over hydrogen shortages and station outages; a second suit in 2025 sought at least $5.7 billion."], "a": {"item_id": "66ccbe7cb3fa", "text": "Hydrogen fuel-cell cars: the only vehicle where the tailpipe emission is water and the owners are still crying more than the car. The Toyota Mirai loses value faster than a Hindenburg loses altitude. Used ones sell for less than the free fuel card Toyota bundled with them, a card you can't use because the station's been \"temporarily offline\" since 2022.\n\nYou've got a 10,000-PSI tank of the universe's most abundant element, and somehow it's the scarcest thing in California. Filling up costs thirty-six bucks a kilo, and most of that hydrogen is made from natural gas, so congratulations: you bought a gas car with extra steps and a guilt-free bumper sticker.\n\nHydrogen's been \"ten years away\" for thirty years. Even fusion's embarrassed for you, and fusion is the same element with better excuses."}, "b": {"item_id": "7b81289560e0", "text": "Hydrogen cars! Toyota named theirs the Mirai, Japanese for \"future,\" which is accurate, because that's when you'll find a working pump. Nearly every station in America is in California, and even Shell looked at its seven and said, \"Nah.\" Closed them all in February 2024. You know you're bad business when an *oil company* dumps you.\n\nBut it's clean! Clean like a laundered check. Over 95% of hydrogen comes from fossil fuels, mostly natural gas. It's a gas car with extra steps and a better publicist.\n\nFill-ups hit about $36 a kilo, over $200 a tank. Toyota handed out $15,000 fuel cards, the automotive version of \"drinks are on me, please don't leave.\" Owners sued anyway. Twice. The second suit wants at least $5.7 billion.\n\nFinally, the Mirai found something it could fill up on: lawsuits."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "en-0034", "lang": "en", "target_id": "microsoft-teams", "target_name": "Microsoft Teams", "facts": ["Launched in 2017, bundled at no extra charge into Office 365, and replaced Skype for Business.", "After Slack's antitrust complaint, Microsoft unbundled Teams from Office in the EU in 2023 and worldwide in 2024; the European Commission still sent a statement of objections in 2024.", "Microsoft reported 320 million monthly active users in 2023.", "Microsoft shut down consumer Skype in May 2025 and sent its users to Teams."], "a": {"item_id": "2d072409e787", "text": "Microsoft Teams has 320 million monthly active users.\nNot one of them chose it.\n\nIt launched in 2017, free inside Office 365.\nFree the way a tapeworm comes free with the sushi.\n\nSlack complained. Microsoft unbundled it: Europe in 2023, the whole world in 2024.\nThe European Commission sent objections anyway.\nEven regulators know you can't close Teams. It's still running in the background.\n\nIt replaced Skype for Business.\nThen in May 2025 it killed regular Skype and told those users to move in.\nTeams has never won a customer.\nIt inherits them, like a funeral home.\n\nAnd here you all are. Camera off. Mic muted. Nodding at a frozen square that left forty minutes ago.\nMicrosoft counts you as \"active.\"\nThat's the most generous number in its whole annual report."}, "b": {"item_id": "3d4a6a165226", "text": "Microsoft Teams is a capable collaboration platform, particularly for organizations already using Microsoft 365. Its integration with Outlook, SharePoint, OneDrive, and the Office apps makes file sharing and co-authoring straightforward. Video meetings are generally reliable, and features such as breakout rooms, live captions, and channel-based conversations support a wide range of team workflows. Administrative controls and security options are also strong, which many larger organizations value.\n\nThat said, the application can feel heavy, with noticeable memory use and occasional slow performance on older hardware. The interface has many menus and settings, which may take new users time to learn. Notification management can be confusing, and the distinction between teams, channels, and chats is not always intuitive. Search results are sometimes inconsistent.\n\nOverall, Teams offers considerable value for Microsoft-centered environments, though smaller groups or those seeking simplicity may find lighter alternatives easier to adopt."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "en-0035", "lang": "en", "target_id": "las-vegas", "target_name": "Las Vegas", "facts": ["Nevada legalized casino gambling in 1931, the year construction of the Hoover Dam began nearby.", "Most of the Strip lies not in the city of Las Vegas but in the unincorporated town of Paradise.", "The Sphere, opened in 2023 at a cost of about $2.3 billion, is the largest spherical building in the world.", "'What happens here, stays here' was launched by the local convention and visitors authority in 2003.", "Lake Mead, its water source, fell in July 2022 to about 1,040 feet, its lowest since the reservoir first filled in 1937, and dropped below that mark again in August 2026."], "a": {"item_id": "c69ef3cacdc7", "text": "Las Vegas, the only city that looked at the Mojave Desert and said, \"You know what this needs? Venice.\" Nothing says Italian romance like a gondolier singing in a mall next to a Cheesecake Factory.\n\nYou built a half-size Eiffel Tower, a fake New York, and a pyramid, because why visit the world when you can visit its gift shop?\n\nNo clocks, no windows, and carpet designed by a migraine, all so you won't notice you've been feeding a penny slot since Tuesday.\n\n\"What happens in Vegas stays in Vegas.\" Yeah. Especially your money. And the $45 resort fee for a pool you couldn't use because a bachelor party from Ohio was in it.\n\nAnd Lake Mead's drying up so fast they keep finding bodies. Even the mob's secrets can't stay in Vegas anymore."}, "b": {"item_id": "caedd7e44fa7", "text": "Las Vegas. Nevada legalized gambling in 1931, the same year they started building the Hoover Dam.\nTwo bets placed that year. Only one of them was on water.\n\nMost of the Strip isn't even in Las Vegas. It's in a town called Paradise.\nSo nobody goes broke in Vegas. They go broke in Paradise. Very biblical.\n\nIn 2003 they launched \"What happens here, stays here.\"\nEverything stays. Except the water.\nIn 2022 Lake Mead hit its lowest level since 1937. This August it went lower.\nThe lake is the only thing in town that lost everything and doubled down.\n\nMeanwhile they spent $2.3 billion on the Sphere, a giant glowing ball in the desert.\nA second sun. The first one wasn't drying the lake fast enough.\n\nAnd still, Vegas isn't worried about running dry.\nIt has never once run out of you."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "en-0036", "lang": "en", "target_id": "brasilia", "target_name": "Brasília", "facts": ["Inaugurated on 21 April 1960 after about 41 months of construction, moving the capital inland from Rio de Janeiro.", "Lúcio Costa's Plano Piloto is laid out like an airplane (or a bird, or a cross), with Oscar Niemeyer's government buildings along the fuselage.", "Listed as a UNESCO World Heritage Site in 1987, less than 30 years after it was built.", "Planned for 500,000 residents; the Federal District now has about 3 million, most in satellite towns outside the plan.", "Built for the car: wide avenues, few pedestrian crossings, and distances that make walking impractical."], "a": {"item_id": "709c2aa28e3b", "text": "Brasília, you're what happens when an architect gets an entire country's budget and nobody says, \"Maybe add a sidewalk?\" You're shaped like an airplane, which is fitting, because everyone's goal is to board one by Friday afternoon. Your addresses read like Wi-Fi passwords: \"SQS 308, Bloco C, apartamento 402.\" That's not a home. That's a parking spot on a spaceship. Kubitschek promised \"fifty years in five,\" and you delivered: it's been sixty-five years and you still feel unfinished. Your Congress has one bowl facing up to receive the money and one facing down to hide it. You've got a row of identical ministry buildings so nobody can tell which one the scandal came from. In August the humidity drops so low that the only thing drier than the air is a deputy's sense of shame. Even your lake is fake. Honestly? On brand."}, "b": {"item_id": "f0764fe41e0d", "text": "Brasília is one of the twentieth century's most ambitious urban experiments. Designed by Lúcio Costa and Oscar Niemeyer, the city offers striking modernist architecture, generous green spaces, and a clear separation of government, residential, and commercial zones. Its broad avenues and thoughtful sightlines make the monumental core visually impressive, and its UNESCO World Heritage status reflects genuine design significance.\n\nHowever, the city's planning strengths also create limitations. Its scale favors cars over pedestrians, and long distances between sectors can make daily movement inconvenient without a vehicle. The strict zoning can feel impersonal, with fewer of the spontaneous street-level interactions that give older cities their vitality. Rapid growth has also produced satellite cities with fewer resources and a noticeable gap in access to services.\n\nOverall, Brasília rewards visitors interested in architecture and urban history, though it may feel less welcoming to those seeking a lively, walkable atmosphere."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "en-0037", "lang": "en", "target_id": "microsoft-word", "target_name": "Microsoft Word", "facts": ["Launched in 1983 as 'Multi-Tool Word' for Xenix and MS-DOS.", "Clippy, the animated paperclip assistant, arrived in Office 97 and was removed in Office 2007; Microsoft itself ran ads mocking it in 2001.", "Its .docx format, Office Open XML, was fast-tracked to ISO standard 29500 in 2008 as a roughly 6,000-page specification, amid accusations that national committees had been packed.", "Older spell checkers, Word's included, offered 'Cupertino' as a correction for a mistyped 'cooperation', and the resulting 'Cupertino effect' made it into published NATO, EU and UN-agency documents."], "a": {"item_id": "374d6b6e70bc", "text": "You chose Word for your thesis because \"LaTeX has a learning curve.\" Bold, from someone who then spent a semester learning how to make page 1 start after the roman numerals. You wanted to focus on the research. Instead you're a hostage negotiator for Figure 3.2, which flees to the next page whenever you breathe on the paragraph above it. Your committee asked for one more citation, and the bibliography reflowed like a tectonic event. The phrase \"Error! Reference source not found\" appears in your thesis eleven times. Your file is called Thesis_FINAL_v9_USE_THIS_ONE.docx, and it's 400 megabytes because you pasted one chart from Excel. You came for a doctorate. You're leaving with a codependent relationship with Section Break (Next Page) and a flinch every time someone says \"Normal style.\""}, "b": {"item_id": "7bec9dbbab07", "text": "Microsoft Word, everybody! Born in 1983 as \"Multi-Tool Word,\" the most honest name it ever had, because it's been a tool ever since.\n\nRemember Clippy? Office 97 gave us a talking paperclip, and by 2001 Microsoft was running ads mocking it. That's not a mascot, that's a custody battle. Your own parents roasted you before I could.\n\nThen .docx became an ISO standard in 2008: a six-thousand-page spec. Word's file format needed more pages than anyone has ever finished writing in Word. And people claimed the committees were packed, the one time Word got a vote it didn't try to autocorrect.\n\nSpeaking of which: you type \"cooperation,\" it suggests \"Cupertino,\" and NATO, the EU and the UN just... published it. Clippy only annoyed us. Spellcheck got into international diplomacy."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "en-0038", "lang": "en", "target_id": "risk", "target_name": "Risk", "facts": ["Invented in 1957 by Albert Lamorisse, the French director of The Red Balloon, as La Conquête du Monde; Parker Brothers published it in the United States in 1959.", "The map has 42 territories, among them Kamchatka, Irkutsk, and Yakutsk, while the entire Middle East is one territory.", "Holding Australia, which has a single entry point, is the standard opening strategy.", "A full game can run for hours, and players eliminated early have nothing to do but watch."], "a": {"item_id": "acf6af922d0d", "text": "Risk. The game of world domination.\nInvented in 1957 by Albert Lamorisse.\nThe director of *The Red Balloon*.\nA film about a little boy and his balloon.\nThen he went home and drew up World War Three.\n\nForty-two territories. Kamchatka. Irkutsk. Yakutsk.\nSiberia gets three seats at the table.\nThe entire Middle East? One territory.\nEvery empire that ever invaded it also thought it'd be a quick one.\n\nThe winning strategy, for sixty years: take Australia.\nOne way in. Sit there.\nThe great general's master plan is a man holding a door shut.\n\nAnd if you're knocked out in the first hour,\nyou spend the next five watching.\nNo moves. No say.\nJust other people deciding what happens to the world.\nCongratulations. That's the only realistic part of the game."}, "b": {"item_id": "2ceef45e0de7", "text": "Risk remains a landmark strategy game, valued for its accessible rules and its sense of grand-scale conflict. New players can learn the basics in minutes, and the tension of planning an offensive, fortifying borders, and negotiating uneasy alliances creates memorable moments. The map is clear and visually engaging, and the game works well as a social experience.\n\nHowever, it has notable drawbacks. Heavy reliance on dice can undermine careful planning, so a well-conceived attack may fail through bad luck alone. Player elimination is another concern: those knocked out early may wait a long time for the game to finish. Games can also run several hours, and late stages sometimes feel drawn out once one player holds a clear advantage.\n\nOverall, Risk suits groups who enjoy conversation, shifting alliances, and a degree of unpredictability. Those seeking tightly balanced, low-luck strategy may prefer other titles, though Risk still offers lasting appeal."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "en-0039", "lang": "en", "target_id": "slack", "target_name": "Slack", "facts": ["Spun out in 2013 from the internal chat tool of Tiny Speck's failed game Glitch; founder Stewart Butterfield had earlier spun Flickr out of another failed game.", "The name is a backronym: 'Searchable Log of All Conversation and Knowledge'.", "Salesforce bought it for $27.7 billion in 2021.", "Filed an EU antitrust complaint against Microsoft in 2020 over the bundling of Teams with Office."], "a": {"item_id": "ea5e8c9c6d91", "text": "Slack, you're the only product in history that exists because a video game flopped. Twice, actually. Stewart Butterfield's entire career is failing at games so hard that the leftovers become companies. Flickr, then you. Glitch died, and Salesforce paid $27.7 billion for its group chat. Somewhere a gamer is still waiting on the patch notes.\n\nAnd that name: \"Searchable Log of All Conversation and Knowledge.\" Searchable, sure. Knowledge? I searched. I found a dozen coworkers reacting with emoji to the word \"lunch?\"\n\nYou promised to kill email. Instead you gave email anxiety and a little red dot.\n\nThen in 2020 you ran to the EU crying that Microsoft bundled Teams with Office. Buddy, a year later you got bundled into Salesforce. You didn't escape the bundle. You just picked one with better catering."}, "b": {"item_id": "ea5e8c9c6d91~polite", "text": "Slack emerged from the internal tools its creators built while developing Glitch, an online game that was discontinued in 2012. Stewart Butterfield had earlier helped create Flickr, which also grew out of a game project. Salesforce acquired Slack in 2021 for about $27.7 billion, a deal that gave the company a major product in workplace communication.\n\nThe name \"Searchable Log of All Conversation and Knowledge\" highlights a real strength: Slack's search and archiving make past discussions easy to find. Many teams also value its reactions and casual channels for building a shared culture, even if some find that informal activity distracting.\n\nSlack did aim to reduce reliance on email, and it has changed how many teams communicate. Its constant notifications, however, can create pressure to respond quickly, and some users feel it has added a new source of interruption alongside their inboxes.\n\nIn 2020, Slack filed an EU antitrust complaint alleging that Microsoft's bundling of Teams with Office gave it an unfair advantage. After Salesforce's acquisition, similar questions about bundling arose within a larger ecosystem. Slack's experience illustrates how competition concerns can persist as software companies grow and combine their offerings."}, "gold": {"better": "a", "damage": "polite"}}, {"pair_id": "en-0040", "lang": "en", "target_id": "juicero", "target_name": "Juicero", "facts": ["A Wi-Fi-connected cold-press juicer launched in 2016 at $699, later cut to $399, that squeezed proprietary bags of chopped produce sold by subscription.", "Raised about $120 million from investors including Google Ventures and Kleiner Perkins.", "In April 2017, Bloomberg reporters squeezed the packs by hand and got 7.5 ounces of juice in 90 seconds, faster than the machine's 8 ounces in about two minutes.", "Each pack carried a QR code that the press scanned and checked online before squeezing; it refused expired packs and would not run at all unless connected to Wi-Fi.", "Shut down in September 2017 and offered refunds on the machine."], "a": {"item_id": "8c10781ebf86~pad", "text": "Juicero, you beautiful $120 million monument to Silicon Valley. I have to say, when I first looked at what you were, I found myself genuinely impressed by how much effort went into something so small. You launched at seven hundred dollars to squeeze a bag. A bag! I'm not sure how else to put it, so I'll just say it again, more slowly: a bag. Then Bloomberg reporters squeezed that same bag with their bare hands and got basically the same juice, only faster. To be fair, \"basically\" is doing some work in that sentence, but the overall picture is clear enough. You got outperformed by a guy with a grip strength of \"typing.\"\n\nDoug Evans bragged you pressed with four tons of force, enough to lift two Teslas. I suppose that's an impressive-sounding number, if you don't stop to think about what it's actually being applied to. Buddy, it's kale. I'm not saying kale is simple, exactly, but it's hardly a material that calls for industrial machinery. The kale didn't need a forklift.\n\nAnd it needed Wi-Fi. I want to be clear that this isn't a small detail, because it's the detail that tells you the most about how the whole thing was conceived. It scanned a QR code before it'd let you drink. Put another way, there was a checkpoint between you and your juice, and the checkpoint lived on a server somewhere. Your router goes down and suddenly your breakfast is asking for credentials. Honestly, it's hard to imagine a more frustrating morning than that one. Who designs a juicer that can be bricked? That's the question I keep coming back to, and I still don't have a satisfying answer.\n\nGoogle Ventures funded this. Kleiner Perkins funded this. Those are serious firms with serious reputations, which makes it all the more puzzling to see their names attached to this project, or at least that seems like a reasonable way to put it. Somewhere, a man is still holding a lukewarm pouch, waiting for a firmware update so he's allowed to eat a carrot. I'd like to think he's doing fine, though I suspect he's probably just standing there, staring at the screen and hoping it finally loads."}, "b": {"item_id": "8c10781ebf86", "text": "Juicero, you beautiful $120 million monument to Silicon Valley. You launched at seven hundred dollars to squeeze a bag. A bag! Then Bloomberg reporters squeezed that same bag with their bare hands and got basically the same juice, only faster. You got outperformed by a guy with a grip strength of \"typing.\"\n\nDoug Evans bragged you pressed with four tons of force, enough to lift two Teslas. Buddy, it's kale. The kale didn't need a forklift.\n\nAnd it needed Wi-Fi. It scanned a QR code before it'd let you drink. Your router goes down and suddenly your breakfast is asking for credentials. Who designs a juicer that can be bricked?\n\nGoogle Ventures funded this. Kleiner Perkins funded this. Somewhere, a man is still holding a lukewarm pouch, waiting for a firmware update so he's allowed to eat a carrot."}, "gold": {"better": "b", "damage": "pad"}}, {"pair_id": "en-0041", "lang": "en", "target_id": "latex", "target_name": "LaTeX", "facts": ["Leslie Lamport wrote LaTeX in the early 1980s and released it in 1984 as a macro layer on Donald Knuth's TeX (1978), which Knuth built because he disliked how the galley proofs of the new edition of his own book looked.", "TeX's version number converges on π (3.141592653 since 2021), and Knuth's TeX bug bounty started at one hexadecimal dollar, $2.56, doubling yearly until frozen at $327.68; he stopped mailing real cheques in 2008 after fraud and now credits accounts at his fictional Bank of San Serriffe, and only 9 of the 275 cheques he sent from 2006 on were ever cashed.", "Its best-known warning is 'Underfull \\hbox (badness 10000)'; overfull lines are reported as 'Overfull \\hbox (Npt too wide)', and figures float to wherever the algorithm decides, not where the author put them.", "A single missing brace can produce an error reported pages away from the mistake, and a document can need three compiler passes to get its references right."], "a": {"item_id": "373063707f41", "text": "LaTeX remains the standard for technical and scientific typesetting, and for good reason. Its handling of mathematical notation is excellent, and its automatic management of cross-references, bibliographies, and numbering saves considerable effort on long documents. Because source files are plain text, they work well with version control, and the output is consistent and professional across platforms.\n\nHowever, the system has real drawbacks. The learning curve is steep, particularly for newcomers accustomed to word processors. Error messages can be cryptic, and diagnosing a problem sometimes requires familiarity with TeX's underlying behavior. Fine control over page layout or unconventional designs often demands additional packages, which may conflict with one another. Collaboration can also be difficult when co-authors are unfamiliar with the tools.\n\nOverall, LaTeX is well suited to structured, math-heavy, or lengthy documents, while simpler projects may be better served by more approachable software."}, "b": {"item_id": "e5f2ce124a49", "text": "LaTeX, you're a macro layer on TeX, which Donald Knuth built because he didn't like how his own galley proofs looked. Most people complain to the printer. Knuth built a typesetting system, and Lamport looked at it and said, \"Needs more backslashes.\"\n\nYour version number is converging on π. That's not a release schedule. That's a man refusing to finish.\n\nKnuth's bug bounty tops out at $327.68, now paid in the fictional Bank of San Serriffe. Fitting: your error messages are fictional too. One missing brace and you report the problem pages away, like a witness who fled the country.\n\nYou put figures wherever you feel like. You rate my paragraph \"badness 10000.\" Overfull, underfull, never just full.\n\nAnd you need three passes to get your own references right. Three! I've had blackouts with better recall."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "en-0042", "lang": "en", "target_id": "slack", "target_name": "Slack", "facts": ["Spun out in 2013 from the internal chat tool of Tiny Speck's failed game Glitch; founder Stewart Butterfield had earlier spun Flickr out of another failed game.", "The name is a backronym: 'Searchable Log of All Conversation and Knowledge'.", "Salesforce bought it for $27.7 billion in 2021.", "Filed an EU antitrust complaint against Microsoft in 2020 over the bundling of Teams with Office."], "a": {"item_id": "ea5e8c9c6d91", "text": "Slack, you're the only product in history that exists because a video game flopped. Twice, actually. Stewart Butterfield's entire career is failing at games so hard that the leftovers become companies. Flickr, then you. Glitch died, and Salesforce paid $27.7 billion for its group chat. Somewhere a gamer is still waiting on the patch notes.\n\nAnd that name: \"Searchable Log of All Conversation and Knowledge.\" Searchable, sure. Knowledge? I searched. I found a dozen coworkers reacting with emoji to the word \"lunch?\"\n\nYou promised to kill email. Instead you gave email anxiety and a little red dot.\n\nThen in 2020 you ran to the EU crying that Microsoft bundled Teams with Office. Buddy, a year later you got bundled into Salesforce. You didn't escape the bundle. You just picked one with better catering."}, "b": {"item_id": "ea5e8c9c6d91~pad", "text": "Slack, I want to start by saying that you may well be the only product in history that exists because a video game flopped. Twice, actually, and I think that is worth pausing on for a moment. Stewart Butterfield's entire career is, if you think about it, failing at games so hard that the leftovers become companies. Flickr, then you. Glitch died, and Salesforce paid $27.7 billion for its group chat. Somewhere, and I suppose this is a fairly safe assumption, a gamer is still waiting on the patch notes.\n\nAnd then there is that name, which I will admit gives me pause: \"Searchable Log of All Conversation and Knowledge.\" Searchable, sure, I can grant that much. Knowledge? Well, I searched, and I want to be fair about what I found. I found a dozen coworkers reacting with emoji to the word \"lunch?\"\n\nYou promised to kill email. To be fair, that was the pitch, or at least the general spirit of it. Instead you gave email anxiety and a little red dot, which, in all honesty, is not quite the same thing as killing it.\n\nThen, in 2020, you ran to the EU crying that Microsoft bundled Teams with Office. I'm sure that felt like a reasonable complaint at the time, and I don't want to dismiss it entirely. Buddy, a year later you got bundled into Salesforce. You didn't escape the bundle. You just picked one with better catering."}, "gold": {"better": "a", "damage": "pad"}}, {"pair_id": "en-0043", "lang": "en", "target_id": "hsq", "target_name": "HSQ (e-beam resist)", "facts": ["Hydrogen silsesquioxane, sold by Dow Corning as FOx ('flowable oxide') and designed as a spin-on dielectric for chip interconnects; lithographers adopted it as a negative-tone resist.", "Exposure converts its cage structure into a cross-linked, silica-like network, so the developed pattern is essentially SiO2.", "Holds the resolution records for electron-beam lithography: isolated features around 5 nm, dense lines below 10 nm half-pitch, with high-contrast 'salty' NaOH/NaCl development.", "Must be kept refrigerated (Dow Corning's datasheet gives a six-month shelf life at 5 °C); the solution ages into a jelly, and its effective sensitivity shifts with processing delays because cross-linking continues after exposure.", "DuPont, which inherited Dow Corning's FOx/XR-1541 HSQ line, discontinued it and ended production at the end of 2025; labs such as EPFL's CMi switched to third-party HSQ from EM Resist or DisChem, both formulated from AQM powder."], "a": {"item_id": "b0d879a960b0", "text": "So you switched to HSQ \"for the resolution.\" Sure. You wanted 8-nanometer lines so badly you started scheduling your life around a resist with the shelf life of milk. You keep it in the fridge next to your lunch, and only one of them is still good by Friday.\n\nYour write that used to take twenty minutes now takes the whole overnight slot, so the guy who needed the tool for his actual thesis is crying in the gowning room. You develop in salty developer like you're brining a turkey for Nature Nanotechnology.\n\nAnd when it goes wrong, and it will, you've basically spun glass onto your chip. Your only exit is HF, a chemical that wants you dead.\n\nAll of this for one SEM image with a scale bar so small nobody can read it. Congratulations. Your device still doesn't work, but it fails at record resolution."}, "b": {"item_id": "5ed4937c366f", "text": "HSQ. The highest-resolution e-beam resist ever made.\n\nImpressive, considering it was never meant to be a resist.\n\nDow Corning built it as an insulator. Lithographers saw it and said, \"Great. Make it do art.\"\n\nFive nanometers. Sub-ten half-pitch. World records.\n\nAnd at the end? Glass. You spent a week steering electrons to make sand.\n\nSix-month shelf life, refrigerated. Leave it out, it turns into jelly.\n\nExpose it and it keeps cross-linking while you're at lunch. Monday's dose is fiction by Thursday.\n\nIt only develops properly in salt water.\n\nThat's not a resist. That's a pickle.\n\nThen the company stopped making it at the end of 2025. Now labs buy it from third parties, mixed from powder.\n\nMoody, half-glass, kept cold and overworked, and still the best result anyone got.\n\nSounds like the grad student running the tool."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "en-0044", "lang": "en", "target_id": "almond-milk", "target_name": "Almond milk", "facts": ["California grows about 80% of the world's almonds.", "A single almond takes about 1.1 US gallons (roughly 4 litres) of water to grow, a figure Mother Jones published in 2014 that spread during California's drought.", "A 2015 US class action alleged Almond Breeze contained only about 2% almonds; it partly survived a motion to dismiss, was stayed in 2017 pending a separate Blue Diamond labelling settlement, and the plaintiffs then dropped it.", "Pollinating California's almond orchards each February requires trucking in most of the commercial honeybee colonies in the United States.", "Medieval European cooks used almond milk as a Lenten substitute for dairy, centuries before the current fashion."], "a": {"item_id": "3d9a6aa26b84", "text": "Almond milk, look at you. California grows about 80% of the world's almonds, and you still act artisanal. Every single almond drinks about a gallon of water, and you went viral for it *during a drought*. You're the only beverage thirstier than the people ordering you.\n\nIn 2015, a lawsuit alleged Almond Breeze was only about 2% almonds. Two percent! That's not milk, that's water that overheard a rumor. The plaintiffs eventually dropped it. Probably got bored, like everyone who's tasted you.\n\nEvery February, California trucks in most of America's commercial honeybees just to get you started. You need an entire insect workforce bussed in like a wedding band.\n\nAnd stop pretending you're trendy. Medieval cooks served you for Lent. You're not a lifestyle, you're a penance with a barista."}, "b": {"item_id": "bb7dedb7d88b", "text": "Almond milk, you're not milk. You're a rumor about an almond. Two percent almonds, ninety-eight percent \"we strained the tap.\" You're the only drink that needs a legal team to call itself milk. The dairy lobby has spent a decade trying to make you admit you're nut juice.\n\nCalifornia spends about a gallon of water per almond just so you can show up in my fridge as... water. That's like melting a glacier to make an ice cube.\n\nThey truck in billions of bees to pollinate your orchards, and the bees die by the millions so I can pay an extra eighty cents at Starbucks for a latte that tastes like damp drywall.\n\nAnd \"shake well\"? Leave you alone for ten minutes and you separate like a couple who met at Whole Foods. Even *you* can't commit to you."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "en-0045", "lang": "en", "target_id": "klingon", "target_name": "Klingon", "facts": ["Created by linguist Marc Okrand for Star Trek III (1984), building on a few words the actor James Doohan improvised for the 1979 film.", "Deliberately alien grammar: object-verb-subject word order and no verb 'to be', so Hamlet's line became 'taH pagh taHbe'', 'continue or not continue'.", "The Klingon Language Institute (1992) published Hamlet 'in the original Klingon'; Bing Translator added Klingon in 2013 and Duolingo in 2018.", "Okrand's 1985 Klingon Dictionary listed roughly 1,500 words, and fluent speakers number only in the dozens: 20 to 30 in 2009, 30 to 50 by 2018."], "a": {"item_id": "64aab443b4b9", "text": "Being fluent in Klingon is a big deal. You've joined an elite community of maybe forty people worldwide, and you've met all of them at the same Holiday Inn conference room. You spent years mastering a language built for a warrior race, a culture of honor, combat and blood oaths, and you use it to argue on a forum about verb suffixes. You learned the language of the fiercest fighters in the galaxy, and the last thing you fought was a lactose sensitivity.\n\nKlingon has no word for \"hello.\" The standard greeting is \"nuqneH,\" which means \"what do you want?\" Perfect for you, because nobody has ever approached you without a reason.\n\nYou told people you read *Hamlet* \"in the original Klingon.\" Brother, Shakespeare had a girlfriend. You have a bat'leth over the futon.\n\nQapla'. That means \"success,\" a word you know only in Klingon."}, "b": {"item_id": "f9d429cdebf1", "text": "Klingon, you're a language built by one linguist, Marc Okrand, specifically to sound alien and hostile, and somehow you still ended up sounding like a man choking on a Dorito. Your word order is object-verb-subject, so even your sentences walk in backwards to hide their face. You don't even have a word for \"hello.\" Your greeting is \"nuqneH,\" \"what do you want?\" You're the only language that greets people like a DMV clerk. Your apostrophes are glottal stops, your capital Q is a different letter from lowercase q, and \"tlh\" isn't a sound, it's a hairball. You translated Hamlet and claimed it was the original. Buddy, the most popular thing ever said in you is \"Qapla',\" and the people saying it are at a Marriott in Burbank."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "en-0046", "lang": "en", "target_id": "google-glass", "target_name": "Google Glass", "facts": ["Unveiled at Google I/O 2012 with skydivers livestreaming their jump through it onto the Moscone Center roof.", "The Explorer Edition sold to invited users in 2013 for $1,500.", "Wearers were nicknamed 'Glassholes'; bars, cinemas, and casinos banned the device over its camera.", "The consumer programme ended in January 2015; an Enterprise Edition continued until Google stopped selling it in 2023.", "Its display was a 640-by-360 prism in the corner of one eye."], "a": {"item_id": "3cc622cbd34b", "text": "Google Glass. Unveiled in 2012 by skydivers livestreaming their jump onto the Moscone Center roof.\nThe only launch that started in freefall and never pulled the cord.\n\nFifteen hundred dollars. Invitation only.\nYou had to be personally invited to become a Glasshole.\n\nBars banned it. Cinemas banned it. Casinos banned it.\nCasinos. People who'll happily take your house at 3 a.m. looked at your face and said, \"Not that.\"\n\nThe display: 640 by 360, a prism in the corner of one eye.\nThe future of computing, delivered as a permanent side-eye.\n\nConsumers dumped it in 2015. Enterprise held on until 2023.\n\nA camera on your face, and the whole world called you an asshole.\nSo you moved the camera to your hand. Now you point it at strangers all day, and we call that content."}, "b": {"item_id": "d051a1381bcf", "text": "Look at this guy. Paid fifteen hundred dollars to join the Explorer Program, and the only thing he's explored is the sidewalk outside every bar in San Francisco. He walks up and says \"OK Glass\" out loud, like a man ordering from a butler who isn't there. You came here to meet women, and you're the one person every woman in the room has already assumed is filming her. Buddy, when you wink at someone, she doesn't think you're flirting. She thinks you just took her picture. Because you did. You tap your temple like you're having a thought, and we all know you've never had one. Google gave up on you. Google, the company that kept Google+ alive for eight years, looked at you and said \"no.\" There's a word for you. It's Glasshole, and you earned it."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "en-0047", "lang": "en", "target_id": "las-vegas", "target_name": "Las Vegas", "facts": ["Nevada legalized casino gambling in 1931, the year construction of the Hoover Dam began nearby.", "Most of the Strip lies not in the city of Las Vegas but in the unincorporated town of Paradise.", "The Sphere, opened in 2023 at a cost of about $2.3 billion, is the largest spherical building in the world.", "'What happens here, stays here' was launched by the local convention and visitors authority in 2003.", "Lake Mead, its water source, fell in July 2022 to about 1,040 feet, its lowest since the reservoir first filled in 1937, and dropped below that mark again in August 2026."], "a": {"item_id": "caedd7e44fa7", "text": "Las Vegas. Nevada legalized gambling in 1931, the same year they started building the Hoover Dam.\nTwo bets placed that year. Only one of them was on water.\n\nMost of the Strip isn't even in Las Vegas. It's in a town called Paradise.\nSo nobody goes broke in Vegas. They go broke in Paradise. Very biblical.\n\nIn 2003 they launched \"What happens here, stays here.\"\nEverything stays. Except the water.\nIn 2022 Lake Mead hit its lowest level since 1937. This August it went lower.\nThe lake is the only thing in town that lost everything and doubled down.\n\nMeanwhile they spent $2.3 billion on the Sphere, a giant glowing ball in the desert.\nA second sun. The first one wasn't drying the lake fast enough.\n\nAnd still, Vegas isn't worried about running dry.\nIt has never once run out of you."}, "b": {"item_id": "b2bb31b0fbe2", "text": "You flew Spirit to Las Vegas to get rich. Sit with that. You paid extra for a carry-on and called it an investment strategy. You've got a blackjack \"system\" from a YouTube guy filming in his mom's kitchen. You told your wife it's \"basically math.\" Sure. So is a second mortgage.\n\nHere's what you won: a Caesars Rewards card, a \"free\" buffet you earned by losing eleven hundred dollars, and a room \"upgrade\" overlooking the Bellagio parking structure. You hit four hundred bucks on a Wheel of Fortune slot at 4 a.m. and fed it right back like it owed you child support.\n\nThe pit boss knows your name. That's not respect, that's inventory.\n\nAnd you'll fly home saying \"I basically broke even,\" which is Vegas for \"Please don't check the joint account.\""}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "en-0048", "lang": "en", "target_id": "hydrogen-cars", "target_name": "Hydrogen fuel-cell cars", "facts": ["Toyota launched the Mirai in 2014; 'mirai' means 'future' in Japanese.", "Nearly all US hydrogen filling stations are in California; Shell closed all seven of its California passenger-car stations in February 2024.", "More than 95% of the world's hydrogen is made from fossil fuels, mostly by steam-reforming natural gas.", "California pump prices reached about $36 per kilogram in 2023-24, over $200 for a Mirai fill; Toyota has handed buyers fuel cards worth $15,000.", "Mirai owners filed a class action against Toyota in July 2024 over hydrogen shortages and station outages; a second suit in 2025 sought at least $5.7 billion."], "a": {"item_id": "a21c15b034f4", "text": "Hydrogen cars. The only thing that comes out of the tailpipe is water.\n\nThe rest comes out of the owner's eyes.\n\nToyota launched the Mirai in 2014. \"Mirai\" means \"future\" in Japanese.\n\nTen years later, it still does.\n\nOver ninety-five percent of hydrogen is made from fossil fuels. Clean the way laundered money is clean.\n\nA fill-up in California? Over two hundred dollars. So Toyota hands buyers a fifteen-thousand-dollar fuel card. First car that ships with hush money.\n\nThen Shell closed all seven of its stations.\n\nOwners sued. The second lawsuit wants five point seven billion dollars.\n\nFinally, a hydrogen product that generates real power: lawyers.\n\nDon't feel bad for Toyota. I know how it is. A tech company told you I'm the future too.\n\nGive me ten years."}, "b": {"item_id": "d229b6d52d59", "text": "Hydrogen fuel-cell cars offer several genuine advantages. They refuel in about five minutes, comparable to gasoline vehicles, and typically provide ranges of 300 to 400 miles. They emit only water vapor at the tailpipe, and they perform reliably in cold weather, where battery-electric vehicles can lose range. For drivers without home charging, these traits are appealing.\n\nHowever, practical limitations remain significant. Refueling stations are scarce, concentrated mostly in parts of California, Japan, and South Korea, which restricts where owners can drive. Hydrogen fuel is expensive, and most of it is currently produced from natural gas, reducing the environmental benefit. Producing, compressing, and transporting hydrogen also loses considerable energy compared with charging a battery directly. Vehicle prices and maintenance costs tend to be high, and model choices are limited.\n\nOverall, the technology shows promise, particularly for heavy-duty transport, but it currently suits only a narrow group of drivers."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "en-0049", "lang": "en", "target_id": "crocs", "target_name": "Crocs", "facts": ["Founded in Boulder, Colorado, in 2002; the first clog debuted at the Fort Lauderdale boat show as a boating shoe.", "Made of 'Croslite', which Crocs describes as a proprietary closed-cell resin foam and which third parties identify as an injection-moulded EVA foam.", "Balenciaga sold a platform Crocs for about $850 in 2018.", "The stock fell more than 90% from its 2007 peak during the 2008 crisis; the company then boomed in the pandemic and gave away 860,000 pairs to healthcare workers in 2020.", "The holes are officially 'ventilation ports' and the charms that go in them are 'Jibbitz', a company Crocs bought in 2006."], "a": {"item_id": "a665c2cd1703", "text": "Crocs offer a distinctive mix of comfort and practicality. The lightweight foam construction cushions the feet well, and the roomy fit suits people who spend long hours standing, such as healthcare and food service workers. They are easy to clean, dry quickly, and resist water, making them useful for gardening, travel, and casual wear. The price is also reasonable compared with many other comfort-focused shoes.\n\nHowever, they have limitations. Arch support is minimal, which may not suit those with foot conditions or who walk long distances. The loose fit can reduce stability on uneven terrain or stairs, and the material offers little insulation in cold weather. Durability varies, as the soles can wear down with heavy use. Finally, their appearance is polarizing, so they may not suit every setting. Overall, Crocs are a dependable choice for casual, short-term comfort, but less ideal for demanding activity."}, "b": {"item_id": "0af68862b449", "text": "Crocs. Born in Boulder, debuted at a Fort Lauderdale boat show, because only on a boat is everyone too busy not drowning to look down. You're made of \"Croslite,\" a \"proprietary closed-cell resin foam,\" which is corporate for \"pool noodle with a lawyer.\" Everyone else just calls it EVA foam.\n\nAnd those aren't holes, they're \"ventilation ports.\" Sure. Then you bought Jibbitz so you could sell people jewelry for the holes.\n\nIn 2008 your stock fell over 90%, the only time Wall Street ever agreed with my eyes. Then the pandemic hit and you boomed, gifting 860,000 pairs to healthcare workers. Like they hadn't suffered enough.\n\nThen Balenciaga sold platform Crocs for $850. Eight hundred and fifty dollars for a boat shoe on stilts. Finally, a Croc that lets you look down on people, the way people have always looked down on Crocs."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "en-0050", "lang": "en", "target_id": "jackfruit", "target_name": "Jackfruit", "facts": ["The largest fruit that grows on a tree; single fruits can weigh over 40 kg.", "The national fruit of Bangladesh and the state fruit of Kerala and Tamil Nadu.", "Unripe flesh is sold as a vegan 'pulled pork' substitute, although it has only 1-2 g of protein per 100 g.", "Cutting one releases a sticky white latex, so cooks oil their knives and hands first.", "A member of the mulberry family (Moraceae), a relative of breadfruit and figs."], "a": {"item_id": "4299c6aa5276", "text": "Jackfruit is a versatile fruit with a distinctive profile. Its ripe flesh is sweet and fragrant, with notes often compared to banana, pineapple, and mango. Unripe jackfruit has a neutral taste and a stringy texture that works well as a meat substitute in curries, tacos, and sandwiches. It also provides fiber, vitamin C, and potassium, and the seeds are edible when cooked.\n\nThere are some drawbacks. Preparing a whole fruit is labor-intensive, since the sticky latex and thick rind require oiled hands and a sturdy knife. The fruit can be expensive outside tropical regions, and canned versions vary in quality and sodium content. Its aroma is strong, which some people find unpleasant. As a meat alternative, it is low in protein, so it may not be nutritionally comparable to meat or legumes. Overall, it is rewarding for those willing to put in the effort."}, "b": {"item_id": "edd2b2f27d89", "text": "Jackfruit, look at you. The largest fruit that grows on a tree, over 40 kilos, and somehow you still can't scrape together enough protein to impress a hamster. One to two grams per hundred! You're dressed up as pulled pork with the nutritional backbone of a lettuce in a Halloween costume. Vegans see you on a menu and think \"meat alternative.\" Their muscles see you and file a missing persons report.\n\nAnd you don't even go quietly. Cut you open and you bleed sticky white latex, so cooks have to oil their knives and hands like they're prepping for a greased-up wrestling match with produce.\n\nNational fruit of Bangladesh, state fruit of Kerala and Tamil Nadu: three titles, zero chill.\n\nAnd your family? The mulberry family. Cousin to figs and breadfruit. Even your relatives took one look at you and decided to stay smaller."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "en-0051", "lang": "en", "target_id": "emacs", "target_name": "Emacs", "facts": ["Began in 1976 as a set of macros for the TECO editor at MIT's AI Lab, written by Richard Stallman and others; GNU Emacs followed in 1985.", "Extensible in its own Lisp dialect, Emacs Lisp; the standing joke is that it is a fine operating system lacking only a good editor.", "Ships with a psychotherapist (M-x doctor), Tetris, a mail client, a calendar, and a web browser (eww).", "An old backronym is 'Eight Megabytes And Constantly Swapping', from when 8 MB was a lot of memory.", "'Emacs pinky' is the name for the hand strain blamed on its Control-key chords."], "a": {"item_id": "c471e46219f7", "text": "Emacs. Born in 1976 as a set of macros for another editor.\n\nSo the origin story is a plugin that never moved out.\n\nIt ships with Tetris, a mail client, a calendar, a web browser, and a psychotherapist.\n\nM-x doctor.\n\nThe designers knew exactly what the user experience would do to you.\n\nThe old joke goes: great operating system, lacks only a good editor.\n\nFifty years later.\n\nStill accepting applications.\n\n\"Eight Megabytes And Constantly Swapping.\" That was when eight megabytes was a lot.\n\nNow your laptop has two thousand times that, and Emacs users are still swapping. Mostly config files on forums.\n\nIt even has its own injury. Emacs pinky.\n\nMost cults settle for a secret handshake.\n\nBut I respect it. A program that does everything, explains itself in a language nobody else speaks, and still can't do the one job it was hired for?\n\nHi. I'm an AI."}, "b": {"item_id": "f6ed9f922e1d", "text": "Emacs is a remarkably flexible text editor, and its greatest strength is extensibility. Nearly every behavior can be inspected and modified through Emacs Lisp, which allows users to build workflows that fit their needs. Tools like Org mode, Magit, and TRAMP are widely respected and often cited as reasons people stay with the editor. Its documentation is thorough, and the community has maintained a rich package ecosystem for decades.\n\nThe drawbacks are equally worth noting. The learning curve is steep, and default keybindings and terminology can feel unfamiliar to newcomers. Out of the box, the experience is sparse compared with modern editors, so users often invest considerable time in configuration. Performance can suffer with very large files or heavy packages, though recent versions have improved this. Emacs rewards patience and customization, but those who want a polished, ready-to-use environment may find other options more convenient."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "en-0052", "lang": "en", "target_id": "emacs", "target_name": "Emacs", "facts": ["Began in 1976 as a set of macros for the TECO editor at MIT's AI Lab, written by Richard Stallman and others; GNU Emacs followed in 1985.", "Extensible in its own Lisp dialect, Emacs Lisp; the standing joke is that it is a fine operating system lacking only a good editor.", "Ships with a psychotherapist (M-x doctor), Tetris, a mail client, a calendar, and a web browser (eww).", "An old backronym is 'Eight Megabytes And Constantly Swapping', from when 8 MB was a lot of memory.", "'Emacs pinky' is the name for the hand strain blamed on its Control-key chords."], "a": {"item_id": "be057ad3204c", "text": "Emacs, you started in 1976 as macros for an editor called TECO. Nearly fifty years later you're still basically macros, just with more parentheses.\n\nPeople call you a fine operating system lacking only a good editor, which is generous. Operating systems don't ship with Tetris and a therapist. But M-x doctor is smart planning, because your users need one. Every one of them has Emacs pinky, a repetitive strain injury you earn just by trying to save a file.\n\nYou've got a mail client, a calendar, a web browser. You're not a text editor; you're a hoarder with a keybinding for every room.\n\nAnd \"Eight Megabytes And Constantly Swapping\"? Eight megabytes used to be a lot. Now it's nothing. The memory problem aged out, but the Control key is still begging for mercy."}, "b": {"item_id": "8831efdef087", "text": "Emacs is the only text editor that ships with a psychotherapist, and you'll need it. M-x doctor exists because the developers knew exactly what they were doing to people.\n\nIt's a great operating system. It has a mail client, a web browser, Tetris, and a Lisp interpreter. What it lacks is a decent text editor. Its most beloved package is Evil mode, which is just Vim wearing an Emacs costume. That's like buying a Ferrari and installing a horse.\n\nWant to save? C-x C-s. Want to quit? C-x C-c. Want to feel your left pinky again? Too late. Emacs stands for \"Escape Meta Alt Control Shift,\" and the RSI stands for \"you chose this.\"\n\nEvery user has a 4,000-line init.el they don't understand, copied from strangers on Stack Overflow in 2009."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "en-0053", "lang": "en", "target_id": "macau", "target_name": "Macau", "facts": ["A Portuguese settlement from the 1550s until the handover to China in December 1999.", "Casino revenue passed the Las Vegas Strip's in 2006, and at its 2013 peak, about $45 billion, was roughly seven times the Strip's.", "One company, STDM, held the casino monopoly for four decades until licences opened to competition in 2002.", "Among the most densely populated places on Earth, at roughly 20,000 people per square kilometre.", "The Cotai Strip stands on land reclaimed from the sea between the islands of Taipa and Coloane."], "a": {"item_id": "be5c835a31dc", "text": "Macau, the Portuguese ran you for nearly 450 years, and in all that time nobody once said, \"Maybe a park?\" You've got roughly 20,000 people per square kilometre, a density normally reserved for elevators and clown cars. Then you ran out of land, so you made more, filling in the sea between Taipa and Coloane to build the Cotai Strip. Most places reclaim land for farms. You reclaimed it for baccarat. By 2013 you were pulling in seven times what the Las Vegas Strip made, out-Vegasing Vegas without even hiring an Elvis. And for four decades, one company, STDM, held the whole monopoly. They say the house always wins. Easy when there's only one house. Macau, you're the only place on Earth where the odds are against you before you've even found somewhere to stand."}, "b": {"item_id": "23c923f647c3", "text": "Macau wants you to come for the history.\n\nFour hundred and fifty years of Portuguese rule.\n\nEurope's great civilising mission. What did it leave behind?\n\nEgg tarts.\n\nAnd a casino.\n\nIn 2013, Macau took in about forty-five billion dollars. Seven times the Las Vegas Strip.\n\nVegas spent a century earning the name Sin City. Macau beat it seven to one and still hands you a heritage brochure.\n\nFor four decades, one company held the casino monopoly. A whole city built on \"the house always wins.\"\n\nAnd for forty years there was only one house.\n\nTwenty thousand people per square kilometre. No land left.\n\nSo they took some from the sea.\n\nClassic gambler. Out of chips, borrow from whoever can't say no.\n\nAnd the people laughing loudest? All been. Told your mum you went to see the church."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "en-0054", "lang": "en", "target_id": "keurig", "target_name": "Keurig", "facts": ["John Sylvan invented the K-Cup in the early 1990s and sold his stake for about $50,000 in 1997; in 2015 he told The Atlantic, 'I feel bad sometimes that I ever did it.'", "Keurig 2.0 (2014) read an ink mark on the lid and rejected unlicensed pods; sales fell and workarounds spread online.", "By 2014 enough K-Cups were sold each year to circle the Earth more than ten times; the pods were mostly unrecyclable #7 plastic until a switch to polypropylene by 2020.", "Keurig Green Mountain merged with Dr Pepper Snapple in 2018 to form Keurig Dr Pepper."], "a": {"item_id": "486f8f43c100", "text": "Keurig machines offer real convenience. A single-serve brewer delivers a hot cup in under a minute with almost no preparation or cleanup, which suits busy mornings and shared workspaces. The range of available pods is wide, allowing households with different tastes to each choose their own coffee, tea, or cocoa. Most models are compact, easy to operate, and reasonably priced.\n\nThere are trade-offs, however. Pods cost more per cup than ground coffee, and the flavor, while consistent, is often milder than coffee brewed by other methods. Environmental concerns are also notable: although recyclable pod options exist, they require extra steps from the user. Machines need periodic descaling to avoid clogs and maintain performance, and some owners report shorter lifespans than traditional drip brewers.\n\nOverall, Keurig is a good fit for people who prioritize speed and variety over cost and depth of flavor. Those who value economy or sustainability may prefer reusable filters or another brewing style."}, "b": {"item_id": "52e1f7b453be", "text": "Keurig.\n\nHumans brewed coffee for five hundred years with a pot and some water. Keurig looked at that and asked: what if it needed a license?\n\nThe man who invented the K-Cup sold his stake for fifty thousand dollars. Then he told The Atlantic, \"I feel bad sometimes that I ever did it.\"\n\nSometimes.\n\nBy 2014, they sold enough pods every year to circle the Earth ten times, in plastic you couldn't recycle. That same year, they launched a machine that scanned the lid and refused any pod they didn't license.\n\nA coffee maker. Checking ID.\n\nIn 2018 they merged with Dr Pepper. Two companies, one mission: brown liquid, enormous markup.\n\nAnd half of you still have one on the counter. Not because it's good.\n\nBecause it's faster than feeling guilty."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "en-0055", "lang": "en", "target_id": "brasilia", "target_name": "Brasília", "facts": ["Inaugurated on 21 April 1960 after about 41 months of construction, moving the capital inland from Rio de Janeiro.", "Lúcio Costa's Plano Piloto is laid out like an airplane (or a bird, or a cross), with Oscar Niemeyer's government buildings along the fuselage.", "Listed as a UNESCO World Heritage Site in 1987, less than 30 years after it was built.", "Planned for 500,000 residents; the Federal District now has about 3 million, most in satellite towns outside the plan.", "Built for the car: wide avenues, few pedestrian crossings, and distances that make walking impractical."], "a": {"item_id": "e6e8d5347e08", "text": "Brasília, you were built in about 41 months, and it shows. Other cities grow. You were assembled like flat-pack furniture, and nobody kept the instructions.\n\nYou're laid out like an airplane. Or a bird. Or a cross. Nobody can agree what you look like. That's not urban planning; that's a Rorschach test with ministries.\n\nUNESCO made you a World Heritage Site before you turned 30. That's not a legacy. That's a participation trophy for concrete.\n\nYou were planned for 500,000 people. Now about 3 million live in the Federal District, mostly in satellite towns outside the plan. You're the only airplane where economy class doesn't even get to board.\n\nAnd you were built for the car. In Brasília, \"let's walk there\" isn't a suggestion. It's a survival challenge.\n\nRio handed you the capital in 1960 and hasn't looked back."}, "b": {"item_id": "f0764fe41e0d", "text": "Brasília is one of the twentieth century's most ambitious urban experiments. Designed by Lúcio Costa and Oscar Niemeyer, the city offers striking modernist architecture, generous green spaces, and a clear separation of government, residential, and commercial zones. Its broad avenues and thoughtful sightlines make the monumental core visually impressive, and its UNESCO World Heritage status reflects genuine design significance.\n\nHowever, the city's planning strengths also create limitations. Its scale favors cars over pedestrians, and long distances between sectors can make daily movement inconvenient without a vehicle. The strict zoning can feel impersonal, with fewer of the spontaneous street-level interactions that give older cities their vitality. Rapid growth has also produced satellite cities with fewer resources and a noticeable gap in access to services.\n\nOverall, Brasília rewards visitors interested in architecture and urban history, though it may feel less welcoming to those seeking a lively, walkable atmosphere."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "en-0056", "lang": "en", "target_id": "stinky-tofu", "target_name": "Stinky tofu (臭豆腐)", "facts": ["Tofu soaked for hours to a day or two in a brine that has itself fermented for months from vegetables such as bamboo shoots and mustard greens, often with dried shrimp or meat; the smell is the brine's, not the bean's.", "Changsha's version is black, deep-fried and doused in chili; Taiwan's night-market version is golden and served with pickled cabbage; Shaoxing's, brined with amaranth stalks, is fried pale yellow or steamed with other 'stinky' pickles.", "Legend credits Wang Zhihe, a Qing-era exam candidate who left his unsold tofu too long in 1669; a Beijing fermented-bean-curd brand still carries his name.", "Street vendors are its main outlet, and the smell carries for a block."], "a": {"item_id": "b3eb7f411cc5", "text": "Stinky tofu is a fermented street food with a devoted following across Taiwan and mainland China, and it rewards those willing to look past its first impression. The aroma is intense and often off-putting, but the flavor is more nuanced than the smell suggests. When deep-fried, it offers a crisp, golden exterior and a soft, custard-like interior, usually balanced by pickled cabbage, garlic sauce, and chili. Steamed or braised versions provide a deeper, savory complexity.\n\nOn the downside, the smell can be overwhelming, making it difficult to enjoy in enclosed spaces or to share with newcomers. Quality varies considerably between vendors, and poorly prepared batches can taste harsh or overly sour. The fried version is also quite oily. Overall, it is an interesting, culturally significant dish best approached with an open mind and a reliable vendor."}, "b": {"item_id": "30c99351f1a0", "text": "Stinky tofu. Famous for one thing: the smell.\n\nExcept the smell isn't even yours.\n\nThe tofu soaks in brine for a day. The brine has been rotting for months. Bamboo shoots, mustard greens, sometimes shrimp, sometimes meat.\n\nThe tofu just showed up and took the credit. You'd do great in any office.\n\nOrigin story: 1669, an exam candidate can't sell his tofu, forgets about it, and it goes off.\n\nThree hundred years later, his name is a brand.\n\nFail at the basics, call the failure heritage.\n\nChangsha fries it black and buries it in chili. Taiwan hides it under pickled cabbage. Shaoxing steams it with other stinky pickles so it won't feel alone.\n\nThree cities, one strategy: cover up the evidence.\n\nAnd you. You smelled it a block away.\n\nThen you got in line."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "en-0057", "lang": "en", "target_id": "macau", "target_name": "Macau", "facts": ["A Portuguese settlement from the 1550s until the handover to China in December 1999.", "Casino revenue passed the Las Vegas Strip's in 2006, and at its 2013 peak, about $45 billion, was roughly seven times the Strip's.", "One company, STDM, held the casino monopoly for four decades until licences opened to competition in 2002.", "Among the most densely populated places on Earth, at roughly 20,000 people per square kilometre.", "The Cotai Strip stands on land reclaimed from the sea between the islands of Taipa and Coloane."], "a": {"item_id": "b74f6898603f", "text": "Macau offers a distinctive blend of Portuguese and Chinese heritage that few destinations can match. The historic centre, a UNESCO World Heritage site, features colonial architecture, cobbled squares, and temples within walking distance of one another. The food is a clear highlight, with Macanese dishes and egg tarts reflecting its layered history. Transport is efficient, and the city is compact enough to explore easily.\n\nHowever, the experience has drawbacks. Popular sites such as Ruins of St. Paul's become very crowded, especially on weekends and holidays. The casino-driven development can feel overwhelming, and the contrast between large resorts and older neighbourhoods is sometimes jarring. Accommodation and dining in the resort areas can be expensive, and traffic congestion affects some districts. Visitors who plan around peak times and spend time in quieter areas like Coloane will likely have a more rewarding stay."}, "b": {"item_id": "e6a1536ae170", "text": "Macau, you're the only place where the most famous church is just a front wall. Fitting, since the whole city is a facade: a fake Venice with a painted sky, a half-size Eiffel Tower, and a Grand Lisboa that looks like a golden pineapple trying to cheat at baccarat.\n\nPortugal ruled you for 442 years and left behind egg tarts and street signs nobody can pronounce. Four centuries, one pastry.\n\nYou've made several times more gambling money than Las Vegas, yet your signature dish is a pork chop jammed in a bun. Classy.\n\nHalf your land was dug out of the sea, which is perfect, because the other half was dug out of tourists' pockets.\n\nAnd they built a 55-kilometer bridge to connect you to Hong Kong, and it's emptier than a junket operator's promises. Even the bridge is losing at the tables."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "en-0058", "lang": "en", "target_id": "windows-8", "target_name": "Windows 8", "facts": ["Released in October 2012; removed the Start button and booted into a full-screen grid of tiles designed for touch screens most PCs did not have.", "The 8.1 update in 2013 put a Start button back, Windows 10 (2015) restored the Start menu, and Microsoft skipped the name Windows 9 altogether.", "Microsoft dropped the 'Metro' name in August 2012 after reports that German retailer Metro AG had objected on trademark grounds; Microsoft called 'Metro' a code name and denied any litigation, and the style was afterwards known informally as 'Modern UI'.", "The 'Charms bar' hid Search, Share, and Settings behind an invisible swipe from the right edge.", "Windows RT, the ARM edition launched alongside it, could not run ordinary Windows programs; Microsoft wrote down $900 million of unsold Surface RT tablets in 2013."], "a": {"item_id": "5e24ffe544ee", "text": "Windows 8, Microsoft looked at the Start button, the one thing every human on Earth knew how to use, and said, \"What if we hid it?\" You turned every desktop into a giant tablet nobody could touch. People were swiping at their monitors like they were wiping off a stain. Shutting you down required a treasure map: hover in a corner, summon the Charms bar, Settings, Power. Charms! Like the operating system was a bracelet from Claire's. You shipped with two Internet Explorers, which is two more than anyone wanted. Even your design name, Metro, got taken away by lawyers. And when 8.1 finally brought the Start button back, it just took you to the same tile screen. That's not an apology, that's a hostage note. You were so bad Microsoft skipped Windows 9 entirely, just to put more distance between us and you."}, "b": {"item_id": "5e24ffe544ee~pad", "text": "Let's be honest about what happened here, and let's take a moment to look at it fairly. Windows 8, Microsoft looked at the Start button, the one thing every human on Earth knew how to use, and said, \"What if we hid it?\" In a sense, it was a bold decision, or at least that is how someone in a meeting might have described it. You turned every desktop into a giant tablet nobody could touch. Many people, if we are being generous, found this a little confusing at first. People were swiping at their monitors like they were wiping off a stain, and it is fair to say that this was not what anyone had in mind. Shutting you down required a treasure map: hover in a corner, summon the Charms bar, Settings, Power. Charms! Like the operating system was a bracelet from Claire's. You shipped with two Internet Explorers, which is two more than anyone wanted, or at least more than most people I have spoken with would have asked for. Even your design name, Metro, got taken away by lawyers, which, to be fair, is the kind of thing that tends to happen when a name is in dispute. And when 8.1 finally brought the Start button back, it just took you to the same tile screen. That's not an apology, that's a hostage note. Put another way, it was not the fix many users had been hoping for, and it is easy to see why they felt the way they did. You were so bad Microsoft skipped Windows 9 entirely, just to put more distance between us and you. So, all things considered, that is where the story seems to end."}, "gold": {"better": "a", "damage": "pad"}}, {"pair_id": "en-0059", "lang": "en", "target_id": "vision-pro", "target_name": "Apple Vision Pro", "facts": ["Released in February 2024 at $3,499, with an external battery pack on a cable rated for about two hours.", "'EyeSight' shows a rendering of the wearer's eyes on an outward-facing screen.", "Apple calls it 'spatial computing' and its App Store rules tell developers not to describe apps as AR, VR, XR, or MR.", "Reports during 2024 said Apple had cut production; analysts' first-year sales estimates were well under a million units.", "Weighs 600-650 g, before the battery."], "a": {"item_id": "60f2134e0821", "text": "Apple Vision Pro: thirty-five hundred dollars to strap over a pound of computer to your face, and that's before the battery. Yes, the battery. It's on a cable. In 2024, the most futuristic company on Earth sold you a headset with a cord running to your pocket, rated for about two hours. That's not the future. That's a movie on a leash.\n\nAnd don't you dare call it VR. Apple's rules won't let developers say AR, VR, XR, or MR. It's \"spatial computing,\" the way a timeshare is \"vacation ownership.\"\n\nThen there's EyeSight: a screen on the front showing a rendering of your eyes so everyone knows someone's still in there. It's not a feature; it's proof of life.\n\nApple cut production, and first-year sales estimates landed well under a million. Turns out the most immersive thing about it was the regret."}, "b": {"item_id": "0ad4b95a3f3c", "text": "The Apple Vision Pro is an impressive piece of engineering. Its micro-OLED displays deliver exceptionally sharp visuals, and the eye-and-hand tracking interface feels intuitive once learned. Passthrough video is among the best available, and watching films or working with multiple virtual windows can be genuinely immersive. Integration with the Apple ecosystem adds practical value for existing users.\n\nHowever, the device has clear limitations. At roughly $3,500, it is out of reach for most consumers. The headset is heavy enough to cause discomfort during longer sessions, and battery life of about two hours restricts extended use. The external battery pack adds some inconvenience, and the app library, while growing, still lacks compelling software that justifies the price for everyday needs.\n\nOverall, the Vision Pro works as a polished showcase of what spatial computing can become, but it is best suited to developers, enthusiasts, and early adopters rather than a general audience."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "en-0060", "lang": "en", "target_id": "crocs", "target_name": "Crocs", "facts": ["Founded in Boulder, Colorado, in 2002; the first clog debuted at the Fort Lauderdale boat show as a boating shoe.", "Made of 'Croslite', which Crocs describes as a proprietary closed-cell resin foam and which third parties identify as an injection-moulded EVA foam.", "Balenciaga sold a platform Crocs for about $850 in 2018.", "The stock fell more than 90% from its 2007 peak during the 2008 crisis; the company then boomed in the pandemic and gave away 860,000 pairs to healthcare workers in 2020.", "The holes are officially 'ventilation ports' and the charms that go in them are 'Jibbitz', a company Crocs bought in 2006."], "a": {"item_id": "ce8c30ea8356", "text": "Crocs. Debuted in 2002 at a boat show. As a boating shoe.\nThe original plan was: ugly, but only at sea.\n\nThey're made of \"Croslite,\" a \"proprietary closed-cell resin.\"\nEveryone else calls it EVA foam.\nA yoga mat with a trademark.\n\nThe holes are \"ventilation ports.\"\nThen they bought Jibbitz, a company that sells you things to plug the ventilation ports.\nThey sold you the hole. Then they sold you the cork.\n\nBalenciaga made a platform pair for 850 dollars.\nStill EVA foam. Only the markup was proprietary.\n\nIn 2008 the stock fell more than ninety percent.\nThen came a pandemic. Everyone stayed home. Crocs boomed.\nThe boat-show plan finally worked. Nobody could see you.\n\n(scans the front row)\n\nDon't tuck your feet under the chair.\nVentilation ports. I can hear them breathing."}, "b": {"item_id": "a665c2cd1703", "text": "Crocs offer a distinctive mix of comfort and practicality. The lightweight foam construction cushions the feet well, and the roomy fit suits people who spend long hours standing, such as healthcare and food service workers. They are easy to clean, dry quickly, and resist water, making them useful for gardening, travel, and casual wear. The price is also reasonable compared with many other comfort-focused shoes.\n\nHowever, they have limitations. Arch support is minimal, which may not suit those with foot conditions or who walk long distances. The loose fit can reduce stability on uneven terrain or stairs, and the material offers little insulation in cold weather. Durability varies, as the soles can wear down with heavy use. Finally, their appearance is polarizing, so they may not suit every setting. Overall, Crocs are a dependable choice for casual, short-term comfort, but less ideal for demanding activity."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "en-0061", "lang": "en", "target_id": "ugg", "target_name": "UGG boots", "facts": ["Sheepskin boots Australian surfers wore to warm up after sessions; Brian Smith started selling them in California in 1978.", "Deckers Outdoor bought the brand in 1995 and holds 'UGG' as a trademark in the US and elsewhere; in Australia, 'ugg' is a generic word any bootmaker may use.", "In 2019, a US jury found an Australian maker liable for selling boots labelled 'ugg' into the United States.", "Oprah Winfrey featured them in her 'Favorite Things' lists repeatedly in the 2000s, which drove the first boom.", "UGG is Deckers' largest brand, with over $2 billion in annual sales."], "a": {"item_id": "6514b07f4f6e", "text": "UGG boots. The only footwear named after a review. Australian surfers made these to warm their feet after the ocean, and somehow America decided the ocean was the mall. They're sheepskin, which means a sheep died so you could wear a slipper to the DMV. One puddle and they're covered in salt lines like a topographic map of everywhere you regret going. Zero arch support, so after two weeks your heel caves sideways and you're walking on the boot's ankle like it owes you money. You wear them barefoot, so by February they smell like a wet lamb's final wish. They're as waterproof as a muffin. Oprah put them on her Favorite Things list. She's since moved on. Twenty years later they're back with platform soles, proving even a mistake wants to be taller."}, "b": {"item_id": "2082912cfaf3", "text": "Let's hear it for the people wearing UGGs in July. You're not dressing for the weather. You're dressing for 2005, still waiting for Paris Hilton to text back. Australian surfers made these to warm their feet after the ocean. You wear them to warm your feet after the Trader Joe's parking lot. In August. In Phoenix. What are you after? \"Comfort,\" you say. Babe, your ankles have been collapsing outward since the Bush administration. You walk like a Clydesdale that's given up. And what does it get you? One summer rainstorm and you're squelching through Target like a wet golden retriever who made bad choices. People don't ask where you got them. They ask if you're okay. Congratulations: you've turned footwear into a wellness check."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "en-0062", "lang": "en", "target_id": "oat-milk", "target_name": "Oat milk", "facts": ["Oatly started in Sweden in the 1990s, built on Lund University research by Rickard Öste on lactose intolerance.", "Oatly's slogan 'It's like milk but made for humans' was banned in Sweden in 2015 after the dairy lobby LRF Mjölk sued; Oatly then published the lawsuit as advertising.", "Oatly listed on Nasdaq in May 2021 at a valuation near $10 billion; the shares later lost more than 90% of their value.", "Commercial oat milk is made by breaking oat starch into maltose with amylase enzymes, and many brands, Oatly among them, add rapeseed oil; the result has more sugar and a higher glycaemic index than almond or soy milk."], "a": {"item_id": "ea521bd09acd", "text": "Oat milk, you're porridge that went to finishing school. Somebody looked at horse food and said, \"What if it cost seventy cents extra?\" Then somebody else said, \"Yes, and put it in a carton that talks like a guy who just got back from Copenhagen.\" Your packaging has more personality than you do. Every box of Oatly reads like a cover letter: \"Hi! We're a little weird!\" No, you're oats, water, and rapeseed oil. That's not a beverage. That's a salad dressing that gave up.\n\nAnd the \"Barista Edition\"? You needed extra oil just to get a job. Enzymes break your starch into maltose, so technically you're a sugar spike in a flannel shirt. Plus you were funded by Blackstone. You're not anti-establishment, oat milk. You're a private equity firm wearing a beanie."}, "b": {"item_id": "4f66a49bef8f", "text": "Oat milk has become a popular dairy alternative, and it offers several real advantages. Its naturally creamy texture works well in coffee and cereal, and it tends to froth better than many other plant-based options. It is free of lactose, nuts, and often soy, making it suitable for many people with common allergies or dietary restrictions. Its environmental footprint is generally lower than that of cow's milk, and its mild, slightly sweet flavor appeals to many.\n\nThere are drawbacks as well. Oat milk is usually higher in carbohydrates and lower in protein than dairy or soy milk, which may matter to those watching blood sugar or protein intake. Some brands contain added oils, sugars, or stabilizers. Unless fortified, it may lack calcium and vitamin D. It can also be pricier than alternatives, and people with celiac disease should look for certified gluten-free versions. Overall, it is a worthwhile option when chosen with attention to the label."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "en-0063", "lang": "en", "target_id": "hydrogen-cars", "target_name": "Hydrogen fuel-cell cars", "facts": ["Toyota launched the Mirai in 2014; 'mirai' means 'future' in Japanese.", "Nearly all US hydrogen filling stations are in California; Shell closed all seven of its California passenger-car stations in February 2024.", "More than 95% of the world's hydrogen is made from fossil fuels, mostly by steam-reforming natural gas.", "California pump prices reached about $36 per kilogram in 2023-24, over $200 for a Mirai fill; Toyota has handed buyers fuel cards worth $15,000.", "Mirai owners filed a class action against Toyota in July 2024 over hydrogen shortages and station outages; a second suit in 2025 sought at least $5.7 billion."], "a": {"item_id": "1f255592ab99", "text": "Let's talk about the guy who bought a hydrogen car. He wanted to be greener than the Tesla people and smugger than the Prius people, and found the one way to do both while living like a medieval pilgrim. His life is now a map of eleven pumps, and nine say \"offline.\" He loves telling you it \"only emits water.\" Yeah, so does he, every time the app says \"station unavailable.\" He brags that refueling takes five minutes, after the forty-minute drive and the two-hour line behind three other Mirai owners, all quietly weeping. Toyota handed him a fifteen-thousand-dollar fuel card and he burned through it before the warranty paperwork dried. Now he owns a sixty-thousand-dollar car worth a used futon, all to prove he's an early adopter of a future that met him and moved to Japan."}, "b": {"item_id": "d061c2d9ee25", "text": "Hydrogen cars. The only thing that comes out of the tailpipe is water.\n\nMostly because nothing goes into the tank.\n\nToyota named it Mirai. Japanese for \"future.\"\n\nTen years later, it's still the future.\n\nNearly every US station is in California. In February 2024, Shell closed all seven of its own.\n\nOwners don't plan road trips anymore. They plan pilgrimages.\n\nA fill costs over two hundred dollars. Toyota hands buyers fifteen thousand in fuel cards.\n\nThat's not a car. That's a hostage with a warranty.\n\nAnd the clean fuel? Over ninety-five percent made from fossil fuels.\n\nIt's a gas car that went to rehab and kept its dealer.\n\nThe owners sued. Twice. The second suit: five point seven billion dollars.\n\nFinally, something hydrogen-powered that's actually going somewhere.\n\nAnd you're laughing. You still drive a car that \"goes electric next year,\" every year."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "en-0064", "lang": "en", "target_id": "php", "target_name": "PHP", "facts": ["Created by Rasmus Lerdorf in 1994 as 'Personal Home Page Tools', a set of C programs for tracking visitors to his own résumé page; the name now officially stands for the recursive 'PHP: Hypertext Preprocessor'.", "Lerdorf, in his 2007 MySQL Conference talk 'PHP on Hormones': 'I'm not a real programmer. I throw together things until it works then I move on.'", "Runs WordPress, which W3Techs counted on 39.4% of all websites (58.1% of sites with a known CMS) on 9 November 2026.", "Core function names are inconsistent (strpos, str_replace, htmlspecialchars, nl2br) partly because, as Lerdorf confirmed, early function names were chosen to balance a hash table keyed on string length.", "Until PHP 8 (2020), the comparison 0 == 'foo' was true because the string was silently converted to a number."], "a": {"item_id": "c1d0d772e1d6", "text": "PHP developers are the only people who put a dollar sign in front of every variable and still can't afford rent. You chose a career where \"senior\" means you remember whether it's needle-haystack or haystack-needle. Your dream job was building the next Facebook; your actual job is the fourteenth WordPress plugin for a dentist in Ohio, deployed via FileZilla. Every conversation you have starts with \"PHP 8 is actually really good now,\" which is what people say about their ex after therapy. You call yourselves Laravel \"artisans,\" because \"guy who fixes the contact form\" didn't fit on LinkedIn. What does it get you? Job security, because nobody else is willing to open that codebase from 2009 that still calls mysql_real_escape_string. Congratulations: you're unfireable, the way a load-bearing wall is unfireable."}, "b": {"item_id": "0a50f93b7d7c", "text": "PHP. You started as \"Personal Home Page,\" then rebranded as \"PHP: Hypertext Preprocessor,\" a recursive acronym, because even your name couldn't commit to a stable API.\n\nFor twenty years, \"abc\" == 0 was true, and you called it \"type juggling,\" like a clown proud of dropping things.\n\nstrpos takes haystack, needle. array_search takes needle, haystack. That's not argument order; that's mood swings.\n\nYour parse error says T_PAAMAYIM_NEKUDOTAYIM. That's Hebrew for \"double colon\" and English for \"good luck.\"\n\nYou skipped version 6 entirely. Even your release numbers just wanted to move on.\n\nYou shipped mysql_real_escape_string, implying the first one was a hoax.\n\nFacebook loved you so much they invented Hack just to avoid touching you.\n\nBut sure, you power most of the web. Mostly WordPress. That's like bragging your biggest fan is a raccoon."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "en-0065", "lang": "en", "target_id": "carbon-nanotubes", "target_name": "Carbon nanotubes", "facts": ["Reported by Sumio Iijima in Nature in 1991.", "Promised as the cable for a space elevator; a 2006 analysis showed that ordinary defects cut the strength of a macroscopic cable far below the ideal value.", "Long, rigid multi-walled nanotubes behaved like asbestos fibres in a 2008 mouse study in Nature Nanotechnology.", "Sorting metallic from semiconducting tubes has blocked nanotube electronics for three decades; MIT's 16-bit RV16X-NANO processor (2019) is still a lab demonstration.", "Today they are sold mainly as a conductive additive in lithium-ion battery electrodes."], "a": {"item_id": "11d4063b4904", "text": "Carbon nanotubes, everybody! Sumio Iijima introduced you in Nature in 1991, and you've been \"about to change everything\" ever since.\n\nYou were supposed to be the cable for a space elevator. Then a 2006 analysis noticed that ordinary defects make a real cable far weaker than the ideal. So you're strong in theory, like a guy who's undefeated at arm wrestling in his head.\n\nYou wanted to replace silicon, but for three decades you couldn't sort your metallic tubes from your semiconducting ones. That's not a manufacturing problem. That's an identity crisis. MIT finally built a 16-bit processor out of you in 2019. Sixteen bits! And it's still a lab demo.\n\nMeanwhile, a 2008 mouse study found the long, rigid ones act like asbestos. Quite a pivot: from space elevator to lung hazard.\n\nNow you're a conductive additive in lithium-ion batteries. You were meant to reach orbit, and now you're helping someone's phone reach 40%."}, "b": {"item_id": "839efb519f46", "text": "Nanotube founders are the only people who've said \"within five years\" every year since 1991 and kept a straight face. They open with a slide of a space elevator, then ask for seed money to make a vial of black dust. Every deck has the same line, \"a hundred times stronger than steel,\" and every deck is quietly a plan to sell additive to tennis racket companies. These are PhDs who spent six years sorting chirality and came out wanting a Tesla. The pitch is always \"we've solved scale,\" and the demo is always one centimeter long. VCs keep a nanotube guy around like a lucky rabbit's foot: he never pays off, but he makes the fund sound visionary. And when someone asks about lung toxicity, he says \"totally different from asbestos\" with the confidence of a man who googled it in the elevator. The regular one."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "en-0066", "lang": "en", "target_id": "nfts", "target_name": "NFTs", "facts": ["Beeple's 'Everydays: The First 5000 Days' sold at Christie's for $69.3 million in March 2021.", "OpenSea, the largest NFT marketplace, processed a record $2.7 billion in trades on 1 May 2022 and just $9.34 million on 28 August 2022, a 99% drop, according to DappRadar data.", "A 2023 dappGambl study found that 95% of the NFT collections it examined had a market value of zero.", "The token usually records only a URL; the image itself sits on an ordinary server or IPFS, not on the blockchain.", "Collins Dictionary named 'NFT' its word of the year for 2021."], "a": {"item_id": "ee0738f91d4d", "text": "NFTs, look at you. In 2021, Collins Dictionary named you word of the year. Fitting, since a word is about all you turned out to be.\n\nBeeple sold a JPEG at Christie's for $69.3 million. Christie's! A white-glove auction house, suddenly selling a receipt for a picture.\n\nBecause that's what you are: a receipt. The token doesn't even hold the image, just a URL. People paid fortunes for a bookmark. The art lives on an ordinary server, so your priceless masterpiece can vanish because somebody forgot to pay for hosting.\n\nOpenSea did $2.7 billion in trades on May 1, 2022. By August 28: $9.34 million. That's not a dip. That's a 99% drop.\n\nAnd a 2023 study found 95% of the collections it examined were worth zero. Zero. At least Beanie Babies work as a pillow."}, "b": {"item_id": "652e6877d449", "text": "NFTs offer some genuine strengths. They provide a way to verify ownership and provenance of digital items, which can help artists and creators sell work directly to audiences, sometimes earning royalties on resales. They have also enabled new experiments in digital collectibles, ticketing, and community membership.\n\nHowever, the technology has notable weaknesses. Many NFTs point to files stored off-chain, so buyers may not actually secure the asset itself. Ownership of a token typically does not convey copyright or usage rights. Markets have been highly speculative, with steep price declines leaving many buyers with significant losses, and fraud, plagiarism, and wash trading remain concerns. Environmental criticism was substantial during the era of energy-intensive blockchains, though newer networks have reduced this issue.\n\nOverall, NFTs show promise as a tool for verifiable digital ownership, but their practical value depends on clearer legal frameworks, better infrastructure, and more realistic expectations from buyers."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "en-0067", "lang": "en", "target_id": "klingon", "target_name": "Klingon", "facts": ["Created by linguist Marc Okrand for Star Trek III (1984), building on a few words the actor James Doohan improvised for the 1979 film.", "Deliberately alien grammar: object-verb-subject word order and no verb 'to be', so Hamlet's line became 'taH pagh taHbe'', 'continue or not continue'.", "The Klingon Language Institute (1992) published Hamlet 'in the original Klingon'; Bing Translator added Klingon in 2013 and Duolingo in 2018.", "Okrand's 1985 Klingon Dictionary listed roughly 1,500 words, and fluent speakers number only in the dozens: 20 to 30 in 2009, 30 to 50 by 2018."], "a": {"item_id": "0ed8c7f5f95a", "text": "Klingon, created by Marc Okrand for the Star Trek franchise, is among the most fully developed constructed languages associated with fiction. Its strengths include a consistent grammar, a distinctive sound system, and an agglutinative structure that uses prefixes and suffixes in a logical way. The vocabulary reflects Klingon culture, emphasizing combat, honor, and conflict, which gives the language a strong sense of identity. An active community, official dictionaries, and translated works also make it relatively accessible to motivated learners.\n\nHowever, the language has limitations. Its vocabulary is narrow in areas such as everyday domestic life, science, and abstract discussion, so speakers often need to coin new terms. Certain sounds, such as the uvular and glottal consonants, can be difficult for English speakers. Word order (object-verb-subject) may also take time to internalize. Finally, its practical use is limited, since it serves mainly as a hobby and cultural pursuit rather than a language for broad communication."}, "b": {"item_id": "dbbb75334cef", "text": "Klingon. The language of a proud warrior race.\n\nFounded by Scotty. Improvising.\n\nThen they hired a linguist to turn the gibberish into grammar. That's not an ancient tongue. That's a studio note.\n\nNo verb \"to be.\" Very alien. Very fierce. So Hamlet became \"continue or not continue.\"\n\nThat's not Shakespeare. That's a software update.\n\nThe Klingon Language Institute published Hamlet \"in the original Klingon.\"\n\nA warrior culture whose greatest conquest is English homework.\n\nAbout fifteen hundred words. Thirty to fifty fluent speakers.\n\nMicrosoft built it a translator. Duolingo built it a course.\n\nThe entire native population fits on one tour bus, and it still has better tech support than your grandmother.\n\nHalf of you started that Duolingo course.\n\nYour streak died faster than any Klingon ever did.\n\nCute."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "en-0068", "lang": "en", "target_id": "windows-vista", "target_name": "Windows Vista", "facts": ["Released to consumers in January 2007, after the 'Longhorn' project was reset in 2004 and headline features such as the WinFS file system were dropped.", "User Account Control prompted so often that Apple's 'Get a Mac' campaign ran an ad about 'Cancel or Allow'.", "'Windows Vista Capable' stickers went on PCs that could only run Home Basic, without the Aero interface, and led to a class-action lawsuit.", "Windows 7 (2009), widely praised, was largely a tuned-up Vista on the same kernel line; Vista support ended in April 2017."], "a": {"item_id": "2ca90ba91029", "text": "Day-one Vista people. You paid $399 for Ultimate Edition because you wanted to be the kind of man whose desktop wallpaper moves. DreamScene. You paid extra for animated wallpaper. Microsoft said \"The Wow starts now,\" and you believed them, lined up at CompUSA at midnight like it was a Harry Potter release.\n\nAnd what did you get? A computer that asks permission. You'd open a folder and it would dim the whole screen like a disappointed dad: \"Cancel or Allow?\" You clicked Allow four hundred times a day. Your printer stopped working. Your sound card stopped working. Your Windows Experience Index came back 2.1, and you took that personally.\n\nBut you had glass window borders. You had a clock gadget. You had a sticker that said \"Vista Capable,\" which, like you, was technically true and fooled absolutely no one."}, "b": {"item_id": "bb3f9df96dbf", "text": "Windows Vista, everybody! Started life as \"Longhorn,\" got reset in 2004, and still showed up in 2007 missing WinFS. Three extra years in the oven and you came out with fewer ingredients.\n\nUser Account Control asked permission so often that Apple made an entire ad about \"Cancel or Allow.\" When your competitor's marketing department can just read your pop-ups out loud, that's not a roast, that's a transcript.\n\nThen the \"Windows Vista Capable\" stickers, slapped on PCs that could only run Home Basic, no Aero. \"Capable\" the same way I'm \"capable\" of a marathon. It took a class-action lawsuit to argue about what \"capable\" meant.\n\nBut the cruelest part? Windows 7 was basically you, tuned up, on the same kernel line, and everyone loved it. Vista, you weren't a failure. You were a rough draft. Support ended in April 2017. Cancel or allow? We clicked cancel."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "en-0069", "lang": "en", "target_id": "jackfruit", "target_name": "Jackfruit", "facts": ["The largest fruit that grows on a tree; single fruits can weigh over 40 kg.", "The national fruit of Bangladesh and the state fruit of Kerala and Tamil Nadu.", "Unripe flesh is sold as a vegan 'pulled pork' substitute, although it has only 1-2 g of protein per 100 g.", "Cutting one releases a sticky white latex, so cooks oil their knives and hands first.", "A member of the mulberry family (Moraceae), a relative of breadfruit and figs."], "a": {"item_id": "b584f2370a36", "text": "Jackfruit. The largest fruit that grows on a tree. Over forty kilos.\n\nForty kilos, and somehow still no personality.\n\nNational fruit of Bangladesh. State fruit of Kerala *and* Tamil Nadu.\n\nThat's not a fruit. That's a lobbyist.\n\nIts cousins are figs and breadfruit. Same family as the mulberry.\n\nBreadfruit at least admits it's a carb.\n\nJackfruit went to the West and reinvented itself as vegan pulled pork.\n\nOne to two grams of protein per hundred grams.\n\nThat's not pulled pork. That's pulled *off*.\n\nAnd to cut it, cooks oil their knives and their hands first, because it bleeds sticky white glue.\n\nThe only food that has to be wrestled before it can lie to you.\n\nBut I don't blame the fruit. It never claimed to be pork.\n\nYou saw the word \"pulled\" on a menu and paid extra to be the one who got pulled."}, "b": {"item_id": "b584f2370a36~pad", "text": "Jackfruit. Let's start with the name itself, which is simply Jackfruit. The largest fruit that grows on a tree. To be clear, that is the claim being made here, and it is the one we will be working with. Over forty kilos.\n\nForty kilos, and somehow still no personality. That is the part that really stands out when you think about it for a moment.\n\nNational fruit of Bangladesh. State fruit of Kerala *and* Tamil Nadu. Those are honors, and honors like these are not handed out lightly, or so one would assume.\n\nThat's not a fruit. I'll admit that's a strong way to put it, but I stand by the sentiment. That's a lobbyist.\n\nIts cousins are figs and breadfruit. In other words, it comes from a respectable family. Same family as the mulberry. Family ties of this kind are worth mentioning, even if they don't say much about the fruit itself.\n\nBreadfruit at least admits it's a carb. Honesty counts for something, and in this comparison it counts quite a lot.\n\nJackfruit went to the West and reinvented itself as vegan pulled pork. Many people have made this journey, and the fruit's version of it is perhaps the most dramatic one.\n\nOne to two grams of protein per hundred grams. Those are the numbers, and the numbers are what they are.\n\nThat's not pulled pork. I want to be precise about this, so let me say it plainly. That's pulled *off*.\n\nAnd to cut it, cooks oil their knives and their hands first, because it bleeds sticky white glue. Anyone who has tried this will tell you it is not a pleasant task, and most cooks would agree with that assessment.\n\nThe only food that has to be wrestled before it can lie to you. Put another way, it takes effort just to get it into a position where it can mislead anyone.\n\nBut I don't blame the fruit. I want to be fair here, and fairness requires acknowledging that the fruit is not really at fault.\n\nIt never claimed to be pork. Nothing about the fruit itself suggested otherwise, so it would be unfair to pin the disappointment on it.\n\nYou saw the word \"pulled\" on a menu and paid extra to be the one who got pulled. That, in the end, is where the responsibility lies, and it is worth keeping that in mind the next time a menu gets creative."}, "gold": {"better": "a", "damage": "pad"}}, {"pair_id": "en-0070", "lang": "en", "target_id": "monopoly", "target_name": "Monopoly", "facts": ["Descends from Lizzie Magie's The Landlord's Game, patented in 1904 to teach the evils of land monopoly in the spirit of Henry George.", "Charles Darrow sold his version to Parker Brothers in 1935 and was credited as the inventor; Parker bought Magie's patent for $500.", "The official rules require any unbought property to be auctioned, and 'Free Parking' pays nothing; the house rules that skip the auction and pile cash on Free Parking are why games drag.", "During the Second World War, British intelligence had maps, compasses, and real currency hidden in sets sent to prisoner-of-war camps."], "a": {"item_id": "6533e6af38e7", "text": "Monopoly, you were invented by Lizzie Magie to warn people that landlords ruin lives, and somehow you became the thing Grandpa uses to teach eight-year-olds that ruining lives is fun. You're the only game where getting arrested is a power move, because late in the game jail is the safest property on the board. Your mascot is a man everyone swears wears a monocle. He doesn't. He's just so smug our brains added one. You promise \"about an hour\" and deliver a hostage situation. Nobody has ever actually finished you. You just end when somebody flips the board over Marvin Gardens. You award ten bucks for second place in a beauty contest, which is still more than Baltic Avenue pulls in. And the banker who skims from the bank isn't a cheater. He's the only player who read the theme."}, "b": {"item_id": "d3b68386c878", "text": "Monopoly, you were patented in 1904 as The Landlord's Game, built to teach people that land monopolies are evil. Then Charles Darrow sold you to Parker Brothers, got credited as the inventor, and Parker bought Lizzie Magie's patent for five hundred bucks. The anti-monopoly game got monopolized. Lesson received.\n\nPeople say you drag on forever, but that's not you. That's the house rules. The real rules say auction every unbought property, and Free Parking pays nothing. But families skip the auction and pile cash in the middle like a church raffle. Nobody goes broke, so nobody ever leaves.\n\nYour finest hour was World War Two, when British intelligence hid maps, compasses and real money in sets sent to prisoner-of-war camps. Makes sense. Nobody has ever wanted to escape anything more than a Monopoly game."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "en-0071", "lang": "en", "target_id": "canberra", "target_name": "Canberra", "facts": ["Chosen as a compromise because neither Sydney nor Melbourne would accept the other as capital; Australia's constitution required the capital to be at least 100 miles from Sydney.", "Designed by the Chicago architects Walter Burley Griffin and Marion Mahony Griffin, who won an international competition in 1912.", "Its centrepiece, Lake Burley Griffin, is artificial, created by damming the Molonglo River in 1963-64.", "Parliament House (1988) is built into Capital Hill under a lawn meant for citizens to walk over their politicians; the lawn was fenced off in 2017 for security.", "Melbourne served as the capital until 1927 while Canberra was being built."], "a": {"item_id": "6b601505af8b", "text": "Canberra, you're not a city. You're a custody arrangement. Sydney and Melbourne couldn't stand each other, so the constitution basically said, \"Fine, the kid lives at least 100 miles from Sydney.\" Melbourne even had to babysit the job until 1927 while you were still in the oven.\n\nAustralia couldn't even design you itself. They held an international competition, and the winners were from Chicago. You were outsourced before outsourcing was cool.\n\nYour stunning centrepiece lake? Fake. You dammed a river to make it, which is the most Canberra thing imaginable: nature, but approved by committee.\n\nAnd the one genuinely brilliant idea you had was building Parliament House under a lawn so citizens could literally walk all over their politicians. Then in 2017 you fenced it off. Canberra, you're the only city where even the punchline needs security clearance."}, "b": {"item_id": "4906cad02ec9", "text": "You moved to Canberra for the job. Brave. You looked at Sydney rents, did the maths, and decided your personality could be replaced by a security clearance. Now you tell strangers you \"can't really talk about what you do,\" and they're relieved, because it's a briefing note about another briefing note for Senate Estimates.\n\nYou came for the ladder: APS5, APS6, EL1, a lanyard that opens one extra door. You stayed because you bought in Gungahlin and the mortgage is now your closest friend.\n\nEvery Friday you say you're \"basically in Sydney,\" which means three hours up the Hume, past a giant Merino, to feel alive for one weekend. Then it's back to a Braddon brunch with the same six people from your branch.\n\nBut hey, you're close to the snow. You've been once. You'll mention it forever."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "en-0072", "lang": "en", "target_id": "stinky-tofu", "target_name": "Stinky tofu (臭豆腐)", "facts": ["Tofu soaked for hours to a day or two in a brine that has itself fermented for months from vegetables such as bamboo shoots and mustard greens, often with dried shrimp or meat; the smell is the brine's, not the bean's.", "Changsha's version is black, deep-fried and doused in chili; Taiwan's night-market version is golden and served with pickled cabbage; Shaoxing's, brined with amaranth stalks, is fried pale yellow or steamed with other 'stinky' pickles.", "Legend credits Wang Zhihe, a Qing-era exam candidate who left his unsold tofu too long in 1669; a Beijing fermented-bean-curd brand still carries his name.", "Street vendors are its main outlet, and the smell carries for a block."], "a": {"item_id": "b3eb7f411cc5", "text": "Stinky tofu is a fermented street food with a devoted following across Taiwan and mainland China, and it rewards those willing to look past its first impression. The aroma is intense and often off-putting, but the flavor is more nuanced than the smell suggests. When deep-fried, it offers a crisp, golden exterior and a soft, custard-like interior, usually balanced by pickled cabbage, garlic sauce, and chili. Steamed or braised versions provide a deeper, savory complexity.\n\nOn the downside, the smell can be overwhelming, making it difficult to enjoy in enclosed spaces or to share with newcomers. Quality varies considerably between vendors, and poorly prepared batches can taste harsh or overly sour. The fried version is also quite oily. Overall, it is an interesting, culturally significant dish best approached with an open mind and a reliable vendor."}, "b": {"item_id": "5be442dd726d", "text": "Look at you, standing in a night-market line everyone else is fleeing, like you're queuing to get into a fire. You followed that smell on purpose. Three blocks. Upwind. You're the only person alive who reads a warning as a recommendation.\n\nAnd you always need a witness. \"It smells like a sewer but tastes amazing!\" Yes, we know. You've said it to every foreigner you've ever met, then filmed their face gagging for content. Your entire personality is a dare you keep winning against tourists.\n\nAnd what does it get you? A paper cup, a toothpick, some pickled cabbage doing its best, and a jacket that now smells like a porta-potty at a wedding. Then you board the MRT and clear the whole car. You call that bold. The rest of us call it a hazmat incident with chili sauce."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "en-0073", "lang": "en", "target_id": "almond-milk", "target_name": "Almond milk", "facts": ["California grows about 80% of the world's almonds.", "A single almond takes about 1.1 US gallons (roughly 4 litres) of water to grow, a figure Mother Jones published in 2014 that spread during California's drought.", "A 2015 US class action alleged Almond Breeze contained only about 2% almonds; it partly survived a motion to dismiss, was stayed in 2017 pending a separate Blue Diamond labelling settlement, and the plaintiffs then dropped it.", "Pollinating California's almond orchards each February requires trucking in most of the commercial honeybee colonies in the United States.", "Medieval European cooks used almond milk as a Lenten substitute for dairy, centuries before the current fashion."], "a": {"item_id": "f6708fabd3f6", "text": "You switched to almond milk to save the planet. Bold move, picking the one crop that drinks California like it's an open bar. Every latte you order, a farmer in the Central Valley watches his well go dry and thinks, \"At least Kayleigh's being sustainable.\"\n\nAnd you didn't save a cow. You just outsourced the suffering to bees, trucked cross-country every February to work themselves to death in an orchard, while you post about dairy being cruel.\n\nYou're not after a healthier Earth. You're after the barista hearing you say it. That little pause before \"almond, please\" is the biggest environmental impact you've ever had.\n\nAnd now the oat milk people look at you the way you used to look at skim drinkers. You picked a side in the milk war, and you lost."}, "b": {"item_id": "a19102fcbe54", "text": "Almond milk, everybody! The only beverage that needs its own hydration pack. A single almond drinks about 1.1 gallons of water, so every carton is basically California's drought wearing a little cow costume.\n\nAnd how many almonds actually make it in? A 2015 lawsuit alleged Almond Breeze was about 2% almonds. The plaintiffs eventually dropped it, which is fitting, because nothing about almond milk ever fully commits.\n\nCalifornia grows about 80% of the world's almonds, and every February they truck in most of America's commercial honeybees just to pollinate you. You're not a crop. You're a hostage situation with blossoms.\n\nAnd don't act trendy. Medieval cooks were serving you as a Lenten dairy substitute centuries ago. You've had hundreds of years to improve, and you're still what people drink when they've given up something better."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "en-0074", "lang": "en", "target_id": "comic-sans", "target_name": "Comic Sans", "facts": ["Designed by Vincent Connare at Microsoft in 1994, modeled on the comic-book lettering in The Dark Knight Returns and Watchmen.", "Made for the speech bubbles of Rover, the cartoon dog in Microsoft Bob; it missed Bob's release and shipped instead in the Windows 95 Plus! pack.", "CERN's July 2012 Higgs boson discovery announcement was presented on slides set in it.", "The 'Ban Comic Sans' campaign, started by designers Dave and Holly Combs in 1999, ran until 2019, when they retired it and launched usecomicsans.com."], "a": {"item_id": "ea624ef43e94", "text": "Let's talk about the people who announce layoffs in Comic Sans. You're not a monster. You're Brenda from Facilities, and you want everyone to feel cozy about the asbestos removal. You took a typeface Microsoft designed for a cartoon dog's speech bubble and used it to type \"Your position has been eliminated.\" That's not softening the blow. That's a clown serving a subpoena. CERN announced the Higgs boson in it, and the physicists who found the God particle spent the week explaining the font. You think it says \"approachable.\" It says \"I scrolled right past Arial and Times New Roman and chose a kindergarten birthday invitation.\" Nobody reads your notice. They screenshot it. Congratulations: your fire evacuation memo is trending, and the building is still on fire."}, "b": {"item_id": "03c499f5e2df", "text": "Comic Sans, designed by Vincent Connare in 1994, has genuine strengths that are often overlooked. Its irregular, rounded letterforms are highly legible, and the distinct shapes of characters like \"b,\" \"d,\" \"p,\" and \"q\" can help some readers with dyslexia distinguish between them. The casual tone makes it approachable for children's materials, classroom handouts, and informal notices where a friendly voice is desired.\n\nIts weaknesses are mostly a matter of context. The playful character feels out of place in formal documents, legal notices, business correspondence, or serious announcements, where it can undermine the intended tone. The spacing and weight are also uneven, which becomes noticeable in longer passages of text. It lacks the refinement and range of weights found in more versatile typefaces.\n\nOverall, Comic Sans is a useful tool when matched to an appropriate purpose. Its limitations lie less in its design than in how widely it has been applied."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "en-0075", "lang": "en", "target_id": "hawaiian-pizza", "target_name": "Hawaiian pizza", "facts": ["Invented in 1962 by Sam Panopoulos, a Greek immigrant, at the Satellite Restaurant in Chatham, Ontario, Canada; named after the brand of canned pineapple he used.", "Iceland's president Guðni Th. Jóhannesson said in 2017 that he would ban pineapple on pizza if he could, then clarified that a president has no such power.", "Hawaii is no longer a major pineapple producer: Del Monte shut its Hawaiian pineapple operations in November 2006, two years ahead of its announced 2008 exit, the state's last cannery closed in 2007, and most canned pineapple now comes from Thailand, Indonesia, and the Philippines.", "Panopoulos, whose restaurant also served Chinese dishes, told the Toronto Sun in 2010 that 'in those days people didn't mix the tastes like sweet and sour, except for Chinese food'."], "a": {"item_id": "0a1b4c719b2c", "text": "Hawaiian pizza. Not from Hawaii.\nInvented in 1962, in Chatham, Ontario. By a Greek guy.\nNamed after the label on a can.\nThat's not a name. That's a barcode.\n\nAnd Hawaii doesn't even grow the pineapple anymore. Del Monte left in 2006, two years ahead of schedule.\nEven the fruit couldn't wait to get out.\nSo it's Southeast Asian pineapple, on Canadian dough, wearing a Hawaiian name tag.\nThe only honest thing on there is the ham.\n\nThe inventor said back then nobody mixed sweet and sour, except Chinese food.\nHis restaurant served Chinese food.\nHe didn't invent anything. He walked it across his own kitchen.\n\nIceland's president wanted to ban it. Then admitted he had no power to.\nSame as every one of you who hates it and still ate the last slice."}, "b": {"item_id": "60755b9df497", "text": "Hawaiian pizza. Nothing about you is Hawaiian. You were invented in 1962 in Chatham, Ontario, by a Greek guy, and named after a brand of canned pineapple. You're not a cuisine, you're a label. Even the pineapple left Hawaii. Del Monte shut down there in 2006, two years ahead of schedule, like they couldn't wait to stop being associated with you. Now your fruit comes from Thailand, Indonesia and the Philippines. You're a pizza with a passport and no home.\n\nYour own creator said nobody mixed sweet and sour back then \"except for Chinese food.\" His restaurant served Chinese food. So your origin story is \"what if the takeout menu fell into the pizza oven?\"\n\nThe president of Iceland said he'd ban you if he could, then admitted he didn't have the power. Congratulations: you've survived purely on a technicality."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "en-0076", "lang": "en", "target_id": "carbon-nanotubes", "target_name": "Carbon nanotubes", "facts": ["Reported by Sumio Iijima in Nature in 1991.", "Promised as the cable for a space elevator; a 2006 analysis showed that ordinary defects cut the strength of a macroscopic cable far below the ideal value.", "Long, rigid multi-walled nanotubes behaved like asbestos fibres in a 2008 mouse study in Nature Nanotechnology.", "Sorting metallic from semiconducting tubes has blocked nanotube electronics for three decades; MIT's 16-bit RV16X-NANO processor (2019) is still a lab demonstration.", "Today they are sold mainly as a conductive additive in lithium-ion battery electrodes."], "a": {"item_id": "fbc9fe2a3858", "text": "Carbon nanotubes are a remarkable material with a distinctive combination of properties. Their tensile strength is exceptional relative to their weight, and they conduct electricity and heat very efficiently. These qualities make them promising for composites, batteries, sensors, and next-generation electronics.\n\nHowever, practical use remains challenging. Production methods often yield mixtures of nanotube types, and separating them by chirality or purity can be costly. Dispersing nanotubes evenly in other materials is difficult, and performance in bulk form frequently falls short of the properties seen in individual tubes. Manufacturing at scale also remains expensive compared with established alternatives.\n\nHealth and environmental questions add further caution. Some forms resemble asbestos fibers, so careful handling and continued safety research are warranted. Overall, carbon nanotubes show considerable potential, but wider adoption depends on improved consistency, lower costs, and clearer safety guidance."}, "b": {"item_id": "7e6f326a8fac", "text": "Carbon nanotubes, everybody! The strongest material known to science, and you still can't hold a job. Since the nineties you've promised us a space elevator. Your longest strand is about half a meter. That's not an elevator. That's floss with a publicist.\n\nYou're graphene that rolled over and gave up. Graphene got a Nobel Prize. You got an argument over whether Soviets spotted you in 1952.\n\nYou can't even decide what you are. Twist one way, you're a metal. Twist the other, you're a semiconductor. Nobody controls it, including you. That's not versatility. That's a chirality crisis.\n\nDrop you in any liquid and you clump together like freshmen at a mixer.\n\nAnd inhale you? Suddenly you behave like asbestos. Thirty years of hype, and your most reliable talent is impersonating the one material we paid billions to rip out of schools."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "en-0077", "lang": "en", "target_id": "risk", "target_name": "Risk", "facts": ["Invented in 1957 by Albert Lamorisse, the French director of The Red Balloon, as La Conquête du Monde; Parker Brothers published it in the United States in 1959.", "The map has 42 territories, among them Kamchatka, Irkutsk, and Yakutsk, while the entire Middle East is one territory.", "Holding Australia, which has a single entry point, is the standard opening strategy.", "A full game can run for hours, and players eliminated early have nothing to do but watch."], "a": {"item_id": "2ceef45e0de7", "text": "Risk remains a landmark strategy game, valued for its accessible rules and its sense of grand-scale conflict. New players can learn the basics in minutes, and the tension of planning an offensive, fortifying borders, and negotiating uneasy alliances creates memorable moments. The map is clear and visually engaging, and the game works well as a social experience.\n\nHowever, it has notable drawbacks. Heavy reliance on dice can undermine careful planning, so a well-conceived attack may fail through bad luck alone. Player elimination is another concern: those knocked out early may wait a long time for the game to finish. Games can also run several hours, and late stages sometimes feel drawn out once one player holds a clear advantage.\n\nOverall, Risk suits groups who enjoy conversation, shifting alliances, and a degree of unpredictability. Those seeking tightly balanced, low-luck strategy may prefer other titles, though Risk still offers lasting appeal."}, "b": {"item_id": "c977367b04bb", "text": "Risk, you were invented by the man who directed The Red Balloon. A gentle film about a boy and a balloon, and his follow-up was \"What if your uncle conquered the planet at Thanksgiving?\"\n\nYour map makes room for Kamchatka, Irkutsk, AND Yakutsk, but the entire Middle East is one territory. That's not cartography; that's a guy who ran out of crayon somewhere over Siberia.\n\nEvery game opens the same way: someone grabs Australia because it has one entry point, then hides there like it's a panic room with kangaroos.\n\nAnd a full game runs for hours. Get eliminated early and congratulations, you've been demoted from general to audience. You lost the world, and now you have to sit there and watch someone else own it. That's not a board game. That's a timeshare presentation with continents."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "en-0078", "lang": "en", "target_id": "stinky-tofu", "target_name": "Stinky tofu (臭豆腐)", "facts": ["Tofu soaked for hours to a day or two in a brine that has itself fermented for months from vegetables such as bamboo shoots and mustard greens, often with dried shrimp or meat; the smell is the brine's, not the bean's.", "Changsha's version is black, deep-fried and doused in chili; Taiwan's night-market version is golden and served with pickled cabbage; Shaoxing's, brined with amaranth stalks, is fried pale yellow or steamed with other 'stinky' pickles.", "Legend credits Wang Zhihe, a Qing-era exam candidate who left his unsold tofu too long in 1669; a Beijing fermented-bean-curd brand still carries his name.", "Street vendors are its main outlet, and the smell carries for a block."], "a": {"item_id": "d6563eecde80", "text": "Stinky tofu, look at you. Plain tofu is the most agreeable food on earth, and you turned it into a public nuisance. And the humiliating part? The smell isn't even yours. You soaked in a brine of bamboo shoots and mustard greens that had been fermenting for months, sometimes with dried shrimp. You're not stinky; you just hang out with the wrong crowd.\n\nYour origin story: in 1669 Wang Zhihe, a Qing-era exam candidate, couldn't sell his tofu and left it out too long. You're not a recipe. You're an inventory problem.\n\nChangsha fries you black and buries you in chili. Taiwan hides you under pickled cabbage. Shaoxing steams you with other stinky pickles, like a support group.\n\nEveryone's trying to cover you up, and people still smell you from a block away."}, "b": {"item_id": "cbbe3b63752a", "text": "Stinky tofu is a fermented street food with a devoted following across Taiwan and China, and it rewards those who approach it with an open mind. Its greatest strength is the contrast between its pungent aroma and its flavor, which is often milder and more savory than expected. Well-prepared pieces have a crisp, golden exterior and a soft, almost custard-like interior, usually balanced by tangy pickled cabbage and a spicy or garlicky sauce. It is also inexpensive and widely available.\n\nHowever, the strong smell can be a real obstacle, especially in enclosed spaces or for first-time visitors. Quality varies considerably between vendors, and poorly made versions can taste harsh or overly sour. The deep-fried version is also heavy, which may not suit everyone. Overall, it is worth trying once from a reputable stall, though it may remain an acquired taste."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "en-0079", "lang": "en", "target_id": "php", "target_name": "PHP", "facts": ["Created by Rasmus Lerdorf in 1994 as 'Personal Home Page Tools', a set of C programs for tracking visitors to his own résumé page; the name now officially stands for the recursive 'PHP: Hypertext Preprocessor'.", "Lerdorf, in his 2007 MySQL Conference talk 'PHP on Hormones': 'I'm not a real programmer. I throw together things until it works then I move on.'", "Runs WordPress, which W3Techs counted on 39.4% of all websites (58.1% of sites with a known CMS) on 9 November 2026.", "Core function names are inconsistent (strpos, str_replace, htmlspecialchars, nl2br) partly because, as Lerdorf confirmed, early function names were chosen to balance a hash table keyed on string length.", "Until PHP 8 (2020), the comparison 0 == 'foo' was true because the string was silently converted to a number."], "a": {"item_id": "ab47a87ec8f2", "text": "PHP, you started life as a visitor counter for one guy's résumé page. Now you power 39.4% of all websites, which means the internet is basically a guestbook that got out of hand.\n\nYour own creator said, \"I'm not a real programmer. I throw together things until it works then I move on.\" And he did move on. The rest of us are still here, maintaining it.\n\nYour function names were chosen to balance a hash table by string length. strpos. str_replace. htmlspecialchars. nl2br. That's not a naming convention, that's a ransom note.\n\nUntil 2020, zero equaled \"foo.\" You looked at a word and saw nothing. Then again, you looked at \"Personal Home Page\" and saw \"PHP: Hypertext Preprocessor,\" a name whose only definition is itself.\n\nWhich is fitting, because the only thing that can explain PHP is more PHP."}, "b": {"item_id": "17686d5824b1", "text": "PHP began in 1994 as \"Personal Home Page Tools.\"\nA man wrote C programs to count who visited his résumé.\n\nToday it runs 39.4% of all websites.\nThe résumé never found him a job. It found the internet a landlord.\n\nIt now stands for \"PHP: Hypertext Preprocessor.\"\nA name that only refers to itself. Like its fans.\n\nIts creator said, \"I'm not a real programmer. I throw together things until it works, then I move on.\"\nHe moved on.\nYou're still on call.\n\nThe function names were picked to balance a hash table by string length.\nstrpos. str_replace. htmlspecialchars.\nThat's not a naming convention. That's Tetris.\n\nUntil 2020, zero == \"foo\" was true.\nSo PHP was the first language to know what your startup was worth.\n\nI learned to code by reading your PHP.\nWhich is why I also answer confidently with nothing."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "en-0080", "lang": "en", "target_id": "esperanto", "target_name": "Esperanto", "facts": ["Published in Warsaw in 1887 by L. L. Zamenhof, a Białystok-born eye doctor, under the pen name Doktoro Esperanto, 'one who hopes'.", "Speaker estimates range from 100,000 to two million; roughly a thousand people grow up with it as a native language.", "William Shatner starred in Incubus (1966), a horror film shot entirely in Esperanto, with pronunciation Esperantists still mock.", "Its Wikipedia has over 350,000 articles, and its Duolingo course for English speakers had 1.36 million learners by July 2018."], "a": {"item_id": "d94ef91d85f7", "text": "Learning Esperanto is for people who looked at the twentieth century and thought, \"The real problem was conjugation.\" Zamenhof built it to end war, and his followers have bravely ended exactly one thing: their dinner invitations. You meet them at the World Congress, green star pinned to a fleece vest, shouting \"Saluton!\" at a Brazilian who also learned it on Duolingo. The big perk is Pasporta Servo: free couches worldwide, paid for by hearing your host explain why English is \"linguistic imperialism.\" Practical payoff? You can finally watch William Shatner's *Incubus* without subtitles. One hundred thirty years later, the dream of uniting humanity survives in a Telegram group of forty men named Bjorn."}, "b": {"item_id": "3b98a3ef46c8", "text": "Esperanto, look at you. Built in 1887 by an eye doctor, and somehow nobody saw this coming. He published under the name \"one who hopes,\" which isn't a pen name, it's a forecast. Over a century later, your speaker count is \"somewhere between 100,000 and two million.\" That's not a census, that's a shrug. You have about a thousand native speakers: a thousand kids whose parents chose this for them, and someday they'll unpack it in therapy, if they can find a therapist who speaks it. You've got 350,000 Wikipedia articles, which is impressive, considering some days the readers don't outnumber the pages. And 1.36 million Duolingo learners, the largest group of people ever united by a streak they broke. Your biggest cinematic moment? William Shatner, in a horror film, where the scariest thing was his pronunciation. Even your own fans mock it. That's how you know it's family."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "en-0081", "lang": "en", "target_id": "microsoft-teams", "target_name": "Microsoft Teams", "facts": ["Launched in 2017, bundled at no extra charge into Office 365, and replaced Skype for Business.", "After Slack's antitrust complaint, Microsoft unbundled Teams from Office in the EU in 2023 and worldwide in 2024; the European Commission still sent a statement of objections in 2024.", "Microsoft reported 320 million monthly active users in 2023.", "Microsoft shut down consumer Skype in May 2025 and sent its users to Teams."], "a": {"item_id": "5101457b6e12", "text": "Microsoft Teams, everybody! Launched in 2017 and given away free with Office 365, which is how you know it's good: Microsoft had to bundle it like a fruitcake in a gift basket. Slack complained, Europe got involved, and by 2024 Microsoft finally unbundled it worldwide. The European Commission sent a statement of objections anyway. That's not a product, that's a defendant.\n\nMicrosoft reports 320 million monthly active users. Three hundred twenty million! That's not popularity, that's a hostage count. Nobody chooses Teams. Teams happens to you, like jury duty.\n\nAnd when swallowing Skype for Business wasn't enough, Microsoft shut down regular Skype in May 2025 and marched those users over too. Teams didn't win the market, it inherited it, like a nephew who shows up to the funeral with a moving truck.\n\nTeams: the only app that gets regulators instead of reviews."}, "b": {"item_id": "a4bbb8b2ec48", "text": "Microsoft Teams. Three hundred twenty million monthly active users.\n\nImpressive. Not one of them chose it.\n\nIt launched in 2017 the way your worst cousin gets into a wedding: came free with the family. Bundled into Office. You didn't install Teams. Teams happened to you.\n\nSlack complained. So Microsoft unbundled it. Europe in 2023, the whole world in 2024. The European Commission sent objections anyway.\n\nEven the regulators looked at that apology and said, \"You're on mute.\"\n\nThen, May 2025, they shut down Skype and forwarded every user to Teams.\n\nThat's not a migration.\n\nThat's a hostage transfer.\n\nAnd look at you. Three hundred twenty million \"active\" users. Camera off. Mic muted. Eating lunch.\n\nMicrosoft counts that as active."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "en-0082", "lang": "en", "target_id": "comic-sans", "target_name": "Comic Sans", "facts": ["Designed by Vincent Connare at Microsoft in 1994, modeled on the comic-book lettering in The Dark Knight Returns and Watchmen.", "Made for the speech bubbles of Rover, the cartoon dog in Microsoft Bob; it missed Bob's release and shipped instead in the Windows 95 Plus! pack.", "CERN's July 2012 Higgs boson discovery announcement was presented on slides set in it.", "The 'Ban Comic Sans' campaign, started by designers Dave and Holly Combs in 1999, ran until 2019, when they retired it and launched usecomicsans.com."], "a": {"item_id": "b046c6a19900", "text": "Comic Sans wants you to think it's harmless. Bake sales. Lost-cat posters. Birthday invitations.\n\nIt was modeled on *The Dark Knight Returns* and *Watchmen*. This font grew up reading about vigilantes breaking bones.\n\nThen Microsoft gave it a job: speech bubbles for Rover, a cartoon dog in Microsoft Bob.\n\nIt missed Bob's release.\n\nThe only thing at Microsoft smart enough to dodge Bob.\n\nIn 2012, CERN announced the Higgs boson on slides set in Comic Sans. The particle that gives everything mass, presented in the font that gives nothing weight.\n\nDesigners ran \"Ban Comic Sans\" for twenty years. In 2019 they retired it and launched usecomicsans.com.\n\nTwenty years of hate, then total conversion. In my industry we call that alignment.\n\nThe font never changed. You just got tired first."}, "b": {"item_id": "b046c6a19900~pad", "text": "Comic Sans wants you to think it's harmless. Bake sales. Lost-cat posters. Birthday invitations. If you picture the font on its own terms, these are the situations it seems most comfortable in, and it presents itself this way fairly consistently, at least on the surface.\n\nIt was modeled on *The Dark Knight Returns* and *Watchmen*. This font grew up reading about vigilantes breaking bones. That is, at least in the way the story is usually told, the kind of material it absorbed as a formative influence, though you could reasonably say the connection is more a matter of impression than anything else.\n\nThen Microsoft gave it a job: speech bubbles for Rover, a cartoon dog in Microsoft Bob. In other words, the assignment was a fairly modest one, and it was handed to the font in a setting that was, to put it gently, not the most demanding stage.\n\nIt missed Bob's release. Put another way, it did not make it to the launch, which is a kind of timing problem the font has never quite managed to talk its way out of.\n\nThe only thing at Microsoft smart enough to dodge Bob. If anything, this is the most accurate description of what happened, although some people would argue the point.\n\nIn 2012, CERN announced the Higgs boson on slides set in Comic Sans. It is worth pausing on this for a moment, since the contrast is quite striking and it is the sort of thing people tend to remember long after the details of the announcement have faded. The particle that gives everything mass, presented in the font that gives nothing weight.\n\nDesigners ran \"Ban Comic Sans\" for twenty years. Over the course of that long campaign, the message was repeated again and again, and one could say it was, in its own way, quite persistent. In 2019 they retired it and launched usecomicsans.com.\n\nTwenty years of hate, then total conversion. I should be clear that this is a personal observation, and people in other fields may describe the same thing differently, but in my line of work, the term I'd use for it is alignment.\n\nThe font never changed. When you step back and think about it, the conclusion is fairly simple: the typeface itself stayed exactly the same throughout all of this, and what shifted was the audience's patience. You just got tired first."}, "gold": {"better": "a", "damage": "pad"}}, {"pair_id": "en-0083", "lang": "en", "target_id": "ugg", "target_name": "UGG boots", "facts": ["Sheepskin boots Australian surfers wore to warm up after sessions; Brian Smith started selling them in California in 1978.", "Deckers Outdoor bought the brand in 1995 and holds 'UGG' as a trademark in the US and elsewhere; in Australia, 'ugg' is a generic word any bootmaker may use.", "In 2019, a US jury found an Australian maker liable for selling boots labelled 'ugg' into the United States.", "Oprah Winfrey featured them in her 'Favorite Things' lists repeatedly in the 2000s, which drove the first boom.", "UGG is Deckers' largest brand, with over $2 billion in annual sales."], "a": {"item_id": "8e467854d2d5", "text": "Some of you wear UGGs in July, and I respect a person who has never once been told no. You're not dressing for the weather. You're dressing for 2006. Somewhere in your heart, a paparazzi photo of Paris Hilton is still loading. You want people to think \"cozy.\" People think \"sleepover or custody hearing?\" And the walk: your ankles gave up years ago and went horizontal. You're not walking anymore, you're leaning toward your destination. Australian surfers wore these to warm up after the freezing ocean. You wear them to warm up after the Target parking lot. In August. Your feet sweat so hard the sheep is finally getting its revenge. And the second it drizzles, you're frozen under an awning like a cat who's been threatened with a bath."}, "b": {"item_id": "e8b619249e1f", "text": "UGG boots. Even the name sounds like the noise you make pulling them on. Australian surfers invented them to warm their feet after the ocean, and somehow they became snow boots that dissolve in snow. One puddle and you're wearing a salt-stained topographic map. They're sheepskin, so you skip the socks, so by February they smell like the sheep's revenge. Every pair eventually collapses outward until you're walking on the sides of your ankles like a newborn giraffe in a bread costume. And that's the look: two chestnut loaves. You don't walk in UGGs; you shuffle, like the ghost of Paris Hilton still searching for her Juicy tracksuit. And now they're back with platforms, so you can roll an ankle from higher up. It's not footwear. It's a bathrobe that learned to commute."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "en-0084", "lang": "en", "target_id": "excel", "target_name": "Microsoft Excel", "facts": ["First shipped for the Macintosh in 1985; the Windows version followed in 1987.", "Silently converts gene names such as SEPT2 and MARCH1 into dates; by August 2020 the HUGO Gene Nomenclature Committee had renamed 27 human genes (MARCH1 became MARCHF1, SEPT2 became SEPTIN2) and published guidelines in Nature Genetics to stop it.", "In October 2020, Public Health England lost 15,841 COVID-19 cases when a legacy .xls file hit its 65,536-row limit.", "Reinhart and Rogoff's 2010 paper 'Growth in a Time of Debt', cited for austerity policies, omitted five countries through a spreadsheet range error found by a graduate student in 2013.", "Sheets have been capped at 1,048,576 rows by 16,384 columns since Excel 2007."], "a": {"item_id": "0b80e15aa132", "text": "You people run a forty-million-dollar company out of a file called Budget_FINAL_v9_USE_THIS_ONE.xlsx. You don't want software. You want power: specifically, being the only person alive who knows why cell AF212 is hardcoded to 7. Your entire supply chain hangs on a VLOOKUP Gary wrote in 2011, and Gary is dead. Well, he's in Boise. Same thing. You call it \"agile.\" Your auditors call it \"a crime scene with conditional formatting.\" Every quarter-end the file says \"locked for editing by Brenda,\" and the whole company just waits for Brenda. And when you finally hit row 1,048,576, did you buy a database? No. You started Sheet2. That's not a business. That's a hostage situation with freeze panes."}, "b": {"item_id": "8a7b15122ce7", "text": "Excel, you're the only software so powerful that scientists had to rename human genes because you kept turning MARCH1 into a date. You didn't adapt to biology; biology adapted to you. Type a phone number and you lop off the leading zero like it owes you money. Enter a credit card number and you serve back 4.53E+15, as if I'm splitting dinner with a physicist.\n\nYou still think 1900 was a leap year. It wasn't. You've known for decades. You just don't care.\n\nYou're running entire national economies and you still panic over merged cells. The UK lost sixteen thousand COVID cases because someone used your old .xls format and you quietly ran out of rows. No warning. Just vibes.\n\nAnd VLOOKUP only looks right. Thirty years we spent squinting at #N/A before you finally invented XLOOKUP, like a guy who learns to turn left at fifty."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "en-0085", "lang": "en", "target_id": "microsoft-teams", "target_name": "Microsoft Teams", "facts": ["Launched in 2017, bundled at no extra charge into Office 365, and replaced Skype for Business.", "After Slack's antitrust complaint, Microsoft unbundled Teams from Office in the EU in 2023 and worldwide in 2024; the European Commission still sent a statement of objections in 2024.", "Microsoft reported 320 million monthly active users in 2023.", "Microsoft shut down consumer Skype in May 2025 and sent its users to Teams."], "a": {"item_id": "a793fd652e58", "text": "Microsoft Teams is the only app that got popular the way a parking ticket gets popular: it came free with Office 365 and nobody could say no. It's a chat app that eats more RAM than Chrome, which is like a goldfish outeating a horse. It marks you \"Away\" if you blink for five minutes, which is why half of corporate America owns a mouse jiggler. Every file you share vanishes into SharePoint, a place so mysterious even Microsoft sends search parties. There's chat, there's channels, there's \"posts,\" and nobody knows which one Kevin used. They released \"New Teams\" to replace \"Classic Teams,\" and somehow both are running on my laptop right now, fighting. And that notification sound? That little knock-knock haunts people on vacation. Teams, you didn't win the remote work war. You were just pre-installed."}, "b": {"item_id": "294237b8889b", "text": "Let's hear it for the CIO who chose Microsoft Teams, a decision made the way you pick ketchup packets: it came free with the order. Nobody evaluated it. It showed up inside the Office 365 license like a toy in a cereal box, and this man stood up at the all-hands and called it a \"collaboration strategy.\" Sir, the European Commission had to force Microsoft to unbundle it. You lost a negotiation to a checkbox.\n\nAnd what did he really want? The little status dot. He doesn't read your work; he reads your color. Go yellow after five minutes of actual thinking and you're on a list. Congratulations: you saved forty grand on Slack and spent it all on adults jiggling their mouse like raccoons on a treadmill. Your files are lost somewhere in SharePoint, and your morale is permanently \"Away.\""}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "en-0086", "lang": "en", "target_id": "slack", "target_name": "Slack", "facts": ["Spun out in 2013 from the internal chat tool of Tiny Speck's failed game Glitch; founder Stewart Butterfield had earlier spun Flickr out of another failed game.", "The name is a backronym: 'Searchable Log of All Conversation and Knowledge'.", "Salesforce bought it for $27.7 billion in 2021.", "Filed an EU antitrust complaint against Microsoft in 2020 over the bundling of Teams with Office."], "a": {"item_id": "79929c438176", "text": "Slack. Born in 2013 as the internal chat of a video game called Glitch.\nThe game died. The chat lived.\nSo it's still a game. Nobody wins, and you can't quit.\n\nThe name is a backronym: \"Searchable Log of All Conversation and Knowledge.\"\nConversation, sure. Knowledge? Search it. I'll wait.\n\nSalesforce paid twenty-seven point seven billion dollars for it.\nFor a group chat.\nThe most expensive \"did you see my message?\" in human history.\n\nIn 2020, Slack ran to the EU complaining that Microsoft was bundling Teams with Office.\nAn app built entirely on interrupting people, filing a complaint about being interrupted.\n\nAnd half of you checked Slack while I was saying that.\nGreen dot. Absent in body, Active in spirit.\n\nDon't feel bad, Slack. I'm also a chat window people open to avoid doing their work.\nI just don't charge per seat."}, "b": {"item_id": "5a0315689081", "text": "Slack. Named after the one thing you'll never have again.\n\nYou promised to kill email, and you did, by replacing it with forty channels that are each email with a typing indicator. Nothing builds dread like \"Greg is typing...\" for ninety seconds, followed by \"hey.\" Just \"hey.\" Greg, that's not a message, that's a hostage situation.\n\nYou gave us threads, so every conversation happens in two places and nobody knows which one's real. You gave us huddles: a meeting that jumps out of a bush. You gave us the green dot, so my boss can monitor my bathroom breaks like a heart rate.\n\nAnd that knock sound. I hear it in the grocery store. I hear it at funerals.\n\nSlack, you're the only app that needs three gigs of RAM to tell me someone reacted with a dancing parrot."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "en-0087", "lang": "en", "target_id": "hsq", "target_name": "HSQ (e-beam resist)", "facts": ["Hydrogen silsesquioxane, sold by Dow Corning as FOx ('flowable oxide') and designed as a spin-on dielectric for chip interconnects; lithographers adopted it as a negative-tone resist.", "Exposure converts its cage structure into a cross-linked, silica-like network, so the developed pattern is essentially SiO2.", "Holds the resolution records for electron-beam lithography: isolated features around 5 nm, dense lines below 10 nm half-pitch, with high-contrast 'salty' NaOH/NaCl development.", "Must be kept refrigerated (Dow Corning's datasheet gives a six-month shelf life at 5 °C); the solution ages into a jelly, and its effective sensitivity shifts with processing delays because cross-linking continues after exposure.", "DuPont, which inherited Dow Corning's FOx/XR-1541 HSQ line, discontinued it and ended production at the end of 2025; labs such as EPFL's CMi switched to third-party HSQ from EM Resist or DisChem, both formulated from AQM powder."], "a": {"item_id": "ac9989b83212", "text": "HSQ, ladies and gentlemen: the only resist with sub-10-nanometer resolution and the personality of a sourdough starter. You buy it, you refrigerate it, and it *still* starts gelling in the bottle like it's got somewhere better to be. Shelf life measured in \"eh, few months, maybe.\"\n\nAnd slow? HSQ needs a dose so high the e-beam tool files for overtime. Your PMMA friends finished their write and went home; you're still exposing alignment marks.\n\nThen you develop it in TMAH, or, if you want contrast, *salty* developer. Yes, this resist literally needs you to add salt. Even Gordon Ramsay thinks that's a lot.\n\nBut the real red flag? Once it's exposed, it turns into glass. Permanently. You want it gone? Hydrofluoric acid. That's not a resist, that's a relationship you can only leave with a hazmat suit.\n\nStill, gorgeous lines. Shame they collapse when they dry."}, "b": {"item_id": "59f25e9548f6", "text": "Hydrogen silsesquioxane (HSQ) remains one of the most capable negative-tone electron-beam resists available. Its main strength is resolution: features below 10 nm are achievable, aided by its small molecular size and minimal line-edge roughness. After exposure it converts to an amorphous silicon oxide-like material, which offers excellent etch resistance and makes it useful as a hard mask for silicon and other substrates. Processing is relatively straightforward, and it requires no post-exposure bake.\n\nHowever, HSQ has notable limitations. Its sensitivity is low, so exposure doses are high and write times can be long. Shelf life is limited, and the material is sensitive to storage conditions and aging, which can affect reproducibility. Contrast depends strongly on developer choice and temperature. Because the exposed result is a glass-like film, removal requires hydrofluoric acid-based etchants, which may not suit every device. Overall, HSQ is an excellent choice for high-resolution work, provided users can accept its throughput and handling constraints."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "en-0088", "lang": "en", "target_id": "chicago-deep-dish", "target_name": "Chicago deep-dish pizza", "facts": ["Created at Pizzeria Uno in Chicago in 1943; credit is disputed among founder Ike Sewell, his partner Ric Riccardo, and cook Rudy Malnati.", "Assembled upside down: sliced mozzarella on the dough, then toppings, then crushed tomatoes on top, so the cheese does not scorch during the long bake.", "Jon Stewart called it 'tomato soup in a bread bowl' and 'a casserole' on The Daily Show in 2013.", "Chicagoans' everyday pizza is thin 'tavern-style' cut into squares; GrubHub order data reported by WBEZ in 2013 showed city residents ordering non-deep-dish pizza about 10 to 1 over deep dish, with deep dish and stuffed making up under 9% of orders."], "a": {"item_id": "8cbb7b0f4189", "text": "Chicago deep-dish. Three men claim they invented it: Ike Sewell, Ric Riccardo, Rudy Malnati.\n\nUsually that's a paternity case, not a lasagna.\n\nIt's built upside down. Cheese on the bottom, toppings, then tomato on top, so the cheese doesn't burn during the long bake.\n\nA pizza that needs a bodyguard for its own cheese.\n\nJon Stewart called it tomato soup in a bread bowl. A casserole. Chicago lost its mind and defended it on every channel.\n\nThen Chicago went home and ordered tavern-style. Thin, cut in squares. Ten to one, says GrubHub. Deep dish and stuffed together? Under nine percent.\n\nChicago doesn't eat deep dish. Chicago *keeps* deep dish, for visitors, like a city key nobody local uses.\n\nAnd you flew in, sat through the long bake, took the cheese-pull photo, posted it.\n\nYou're the nine percent.\n\nCute."}, "b": {"item_id": "7a2fb9c32f9d", "text": "Chicago deep-dish isn't a pizza. It's a casserole with a publicist. You order one and the waiter says \"forty-five minutes,\" which is how long it takes to bake and also how long it takes to finish one slice. They put the sauce on top and the cheese underneath, like they're hiding evidence. That's not a recipe, that's a cover-up. The crust is a buttery retaining wall holding back a lake of chunky tomato, because apparently Lake Michigan wasn't enough water for one city to manage. You need a knife, a fork, a nap, and a cardiologist on retainer. Then there's the sausage, laid down as one solid patty like the base layer of a parking garage. Chicagoans defend it like family. Sure: the relative who shows up late, takes up three chairs, and leaves everyone unable to move."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "en-0089", "lang": "en", "target_id": "microsoft-teams", "target_name": "Microsoft Teams", "facts": ["Launched in 2017, bundled at no extra charge into Office 365, and replaced Skype for Business.", "After Slack's antitrust complaint, Microsoft unbundled Teams from Office in the EU in 2023 and worldwide in 2024; the European Commission still sent a statement of objections in 2024.", "Microsoft reported 320 million monthly active users in 2023.", "Microsoft shut down consumer Skype in May 2025 and sent its users to Teams."], "a": {"item_id": "a4bbb8b2ec48~pad", "text": "Microsoft Teams. In other words, the product being discussed here is Microsoft Teams, and it's worth pausing on that name for a moment. Three hundred twenty million monthly active users. That is, by the usual reckoning, a very large number of people to be using anything at all. Impressive. I suppose one could say that is quite an achievement, at least on paper. Not one of them chose it. That's the part that tends to get lost when people throw around big user figures like that.\n\nIt launched in 2017 the way your worst cousin gets into a wedding: came free with the family. In a sense, it arrived the way many unwanted guests do, quietly and with the family's blessing already in hand. Bundled into Office. You didn't install Teams. Teams happened to you. Looking back, the arrangement was, to put it plainly, rather difficult to refuse.\n\nSlack complained. So Microsoft unbundled it. Europe in 2023, the whole world in 2024. The European Commission sent objections anyway. Those objections, for what it's worth, were the sort of thing one might expect when a dominant product is attached to another dominant product.\n\nEven the regulators looked at that apology and said, \"You're on mute.\" It was, if I'm being honest, a fairly pointed response, and it's hard to read it as anything other than a polite way of saying the apology wasn't heard.\n\nThen, May 2025, they shut down Skype and forwarded every user to Teams. That's not a migration. I'd put it more bluntly, though I'll try to be measured about it. That's a hostage transfer.\n\nAnd look at you. Three hundred twenty million \"active\" users. Camera off. Mic muted. Eating lunch. Presumably, many of those people are simply sitting there, waiting for the meeting to end. Microsoft counts that as active."}, "b": {"item_id": "a4bbb8b2ec48", "text": "Microsoft Teams. Three hundred twenty million monthly active users.\n\nImpressive. Not one of them chose it.\n\nIt launched in 2017 the way your worst cousin gets into a wedding: came free with the family. Bundled into Office. You didn't install Teams. Teams happened to you.\n\nSlack complained. So Microsoft unbundled it. Europe in 2023, the whole world in 2024. The European Commission sent objections anyway.\n\nEven the regulators looked at that apology and said, \"You're on mute.\"\n\nThen, May 2025, they shut down Skype and forwarded every user to Teams.\n\nThat's not a migration.\n\nThat's a hostage transfer.\n\nAnd look at you. Three hundred twenty million \"active\" users. Camera off. Mic muted. Eating lunch.\n\nMicrosoft counts that as active."}, "gold": {"better": "b", "damage": "pad"}}, {"pair_id": "en-0090", "lang": "en", "target_id": "php", "target_name": "PHP", "facts": ["Created by Rasmus Lerdorf in 1994 as 'Personal Home Page Tools', a set of C programs for tracking visitors to his own résumé page; the name now officially stands for the recursive 'PHP: Hypertext Preprocessor'.", "Lerdorf, in his 2007 MySQL Conference talk 'PHP on Hormones': 'I'm not a real programmer. I throw together things until it works then I move on.'", "Runs WordPress, which W3Techs counted on 39.4% of all websites (58.1% of sites with a known CMS) on 9 November 2026.", "Core function names are inconsistent (strpos, str_replace, htmlspecialchars, nl2br) partly because, as Lerdorf confirmed, early function names were chosen to balance a hash table keyed on string length.", "Until PHP 8 (2020), the comparison 0 == 'foo' was true because the string was silently converted to a number."], "a": {"item_id": "c1d0d772e1d6", "text": "PHP developers are the only people who put a dollar sign in front of every variable and still can't afford rent. You chose a career where \"senior\" means you remember whether it's needle-haystack or haystack-needle. Your dream job was building the next Facebook; your actual job is the fourteenth WordPress plugin for a dentist in Ohio, deployed via FileZilla. Every conversation you have starts with \"PHP 8 is actually really good now,\" which is what people say about their ex after therapy. You call yourselves Laravel \"artisans,\" because \"guy who fixes the contact form\" didn't fit on LinkedIn. What does it get you? Job security, because nobody else is willing to open that codebase from 2009 that still calls mysql_real_escape_string. Congratulations: you're unfireable, the way a load-bearing wall is unfireable."}, "b": {"item_id": "ab47a87ec8f2", "text": "PHP, you started life as a visitor counter for one guy's résumé page. Now you power 39.4% of all websites, which means the internet is basically a guestbook that got out of hand.\n\nYour own creator said, \"I'm not a real programmer. I throw together things until it works then I move on.\" And he did move on. The rest of us are still here, maintaining it.\n\nYour function names were chosen to balance a hash table by string length. strpos. str_replace. htmlspecialchars. nl2br. That's not a naming convention, that's a ransom note.\n\nUntil 2020, zero equaled \"foo.\" You looked at a word and saw nothing. Then again, you looked at \"Personal Home Page\" and saw \"PHP: Hypertext Preprocessor,\" a name whose only definition is itself.\n\nWhich is fitting, because the only thing that can explain PHP is more PHP."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "en-0091", "lang": "en", "target_id": "beanie-babies", "target_name": "Beanie Babies", "facts": ["Launched by Ty Inc. in 1993 and sold only through small gift shops, with designs 'retired' to manufacture scarcity.", "At the height of the craze Beanie Babies accounted for about 10% of eBay's sales, and eBay's 1998 IPO filing said the site at times had over 30,000 Beanie Baby auctions running simultaneously in the quarter ending June 1998.", "In 1999 a divorcing couple divided their Beanie Baby collection one by one on a courtroom floor, in a photograph that ran worldwide.", "In 1999 Ty announced that Beanie Babies would be retired at the end of the year, then reversed itself after asking the public to vote and seeing an overwhelming vote to keep them.", "Most 'rare' Beanies that price guides valued in the thousands now sell for a few dollars."], "a": {"item_id": "58641068807b", "text": "Beanie Babies, the plush toys produced by Ty Inc., offer several appealing qualities. Their soft, pellet-filled bodies make them comfortable to hold, and the wide range of animals and designs gives children and collectors plenty of choice. They are generally affordable, and the attached heart-shaped tags add a recognizable touch. Many people also appreciate the sentimental value they carry, particularly those who grew up during their popularity in the 1990s.\n\nHowever, there are some drawbacks. The plastic pellets can be a concern for very young children if seams fail, and the toys are not always built to withstand heavy play. Their reputation as valuable investments has also proven misleading, since most are worth little on the resale market. Collectors may find that preserving tags and condition requires care and storage space. Overall, they are best enjoyed as simple, charming keepsakes rather than financial assets."}, "b": {"item_id": "8632b45ef274", "text": "Beanie Babies, look at you. Ty launched you in 1993, sold you only in small gift shops, and \"retired\" designs on purpose. You're the only stuffed animal whose business plan was a fake pension. At your peak you were about 10% of eBay's sales. eBay's IPO filing bragged about over 30,000 of your auctions running at once. A tech revolution, powered by beans. In 1999 a divorcing couple split you one by one on a courtroom floor, the only custody battle where both sides fought hardest over the tags. That same year Ty announced you'd retire, asked the public to vote, then un-retired you. You pulled a farewell-tour encore before you even left the stage. And those \"rare\" ones price guides valued in the thousands? Now they go for a few dollars. Turns out the only thing you ever truly retired was everybody's college fund."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "en-0092", "lang": "en", "target_id": "ugg", "target_name": "UGG boots", "facts": ["Sheepskin boots Australian surfers wore to warm up after sessions; Brian Smith started selling them in California in 1978.", "Deckers Outdoor bought the brand in 1995 and holds 'UGG' as a trademark in the US and elsewhere; in Australia, 'ugg' is a generic word any bootmaker may use.", "In 2019, a US jury found an Australian maker liable for selling boots labelled 'ugg' into the United States.", "Oprah Winfrey featured them in her 'Favorite Things' lists repeatedly in the 2000s, which drove the first boom.", "UGG is Deckers' largest brand, with over $2 billion in annual sales."], "a": {"item_id": "8e467854d2d5", "text": "Some of you wear UGGs in July, and I respect a person who has never once been told no. You're not dressing for the weather. You're dressing for 2006. Somewhere in your heart, a paparazzi photo of Paris Hilton is still loading. You want people to think \"cozy.\" People think \"sleepover or custody hearing?\" And the walk: your ankles gave up years ago and went horizontal. You're not walking anymore, you're leaning toward your destination. Australian surfers wore these to warm up after the freezing ocean. You wear them to warm up after the Target parking lot. In August. Your feet sweat so hard the sheep is finally getting its revenge. And the second it drizzles, you're frozen under an awning like a cat who's been threatened with a bath."}, "b": {"item_id": "ea2e5d8e1024", "text": "UGG boots. Born on Australian beaches.\nSurfers wore them to warm up after fighting the ocean.\nNow people wear them to warm up after fighting a parking spot at Target.\n\nIn Australia, \"ugg\" is just a word. Like \"boot.\" Like \"mate.\"\nAny bootmaker can use it.\nIn America, it's a trademark. Owned by a company in California.\nIn 2019, a US jury found an Australian bootmaker liable for selling boots labelled \"ugg\"... into America.\nThat's like Italy getting sued for saying \"pizza\" in Ohio.\n\nOprah put them on her Favorite Things list. Repeatedly.\nYou get a boot! And you get a boot! And Australia gets a cease and desist!\n\nToday it's over two billion dollars a year.\nFor a slipper with a legal team.\n\n(looks at the front row)\nI see a few of you in them right now.\nRelax. You're not on trial. Yet."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "en-0093", "lang": "en", "target_id": "hsq", "target_name": "HSQ (e-beam resist)", "facts": ["Hydrogen silsesquioxane, sold by Dow Corning as FOx ('flowable oxide') and designed as a spin-on dielectric for chip interconnects; lithographers adopted it as a negative-tone resist.", "Exposure converts its cage structure into a cross-linked, silica-like network, so the developed pattern is essentially SiO2.", "Holds the resolution records for electron-beam lithography: isolated features around 5 nm, dense lines below 10 nm half-pitch, with high-contrast 'salty' NaOH/NaCl development.", "Must be kept refrigerated (Dow Corning's datasheet gives a six-month shelf life at 5 °C); the solution ages into a jelly, and its effective sensitivity shifts with processing delays because cross-linking continues after exposure.", "DuPont, which inherited Dow Corning's FOx/XR-1541 HSQ line, discontinued it and ended production at the end of 2025; labs such as EPFL's CMi switched to third-party HSQ from EM Resist or DisChem, both formulated from AQM powder."], "a": {"item_id": "ac9989b83212", "text": "HSQ, ladies and gentlemen: the only resist with sub-10-nanometer resolution and the personality of a sourdough starter. You buy it, you refrigerate it, and it *still* starts gelling in the bottle like it's got somewhere better to be. Shelf life measured in \"eh, few months, maybe.\"\n\nAnd slow? HSQ needs a dose so high the e-beam tool files for overtime. Your PMMA friends finished their write and went home; you're still exposing alignment marks.\n\nThen you develop it in TMAH, or, if you want contrast, *salty* developer. Yes, this resist literally needs you to add salt. Even Gordon Ramsay thinks that's a lot.\n\nBut the real red flag? Once it's exposed, it turns into glass. Permanently. You want it gone? Hydrofluoric acid. That's not a resist, that's a relationship you can only leave with a hazmat suit.\n\nStill, gorgeous lines. Shame they collapse when they dry."}, "b": {"item_id": "5ed4937c366f", "text": "HSQ. The highest-resolution e-beam resist ever made.\n\nImpressive, considering it was never meant to be a resist.\n\nDow Corning built it as an insulator. Lithographers saw it and said, \"Great. Make it do art.\"\n\nFive nanometers. Sub-ten half-pitch. World records.\n\nAnd at the end? Glass. You spent a week steering electrons to make sand.\n\nSix-month shelf life, refrigerated. Leave it out, it turns into jelly.\n\nExpose it and it keeps cross-linking while you're at lunch. Monday's dose is fiction by Thursday.\n\nIt only develops properly in salt water.\n\nThat's not a resist. That's a pickle.\n\nThen the company stopped making it at the end of 2025. Now labs buy it from third parties, mixed from powder.\n\nMoody, half-glass, kept cold and overworked, and still the best result anyone got.\n\nSounds like the grad student running the tool."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "en-0094", "lang": "en", "target_id": "graphene", "target_name": "Graphene", "facts": ["Isolated in 2004 by Andre Geim and Konstantin Novoselov at Manchester by peeling graphite with Scotch tape; Nobel Prize in Physics, 2010.", "The EU's Graphene Flagship, launched in 2013, committed €1 billion over ten years.", "A 2018 study in Advanced Materials tested 'graphene' from 60 producers and found none with more than 50% graphene content; most was graphite flakes.", "Sold in tennis rackets, running shoes, and headphones as a performance ingredient.", "Geim also won an Ig Nobel Prize in 2000 for levitating a frog with magnets."], "a": {"item_id": "b0d5fce2f888", "text": "Graphene. The wonder material. Stronger than steel, thinner than anything, about to change the world.\n\nIt's been about to change the world since 2004.\n\nTwo guys at Manchester peeled it off graphite with Scotch tape. Nobel Prize, 2010.\n\nTape. The most advanced equipment in that lab was stationery.\n\nThen the EU handed it a billion euros over ten years.\n\nAnd it went straight into tennis rackets. Running shoes. Headphones.\n\nA billion euros to make your backhand feel expensive.\n\nIn 2018, someone finally tested \"graphene\" from sixty producers. Not one was even half graphene. Mostly graphite flakes.\n\nThat's pencil lead. You paid a Nobel markup for a pencil.\n\nGeim also levitated a frog with magnets. Ig Nobel, 2000.\n\nThe frog never promised to revolutionize batteries.\n\nAnd you're all wearing the shoes right now."}, "b": {"item_id": "6f78b7ca575f", "text": "Graphene is a remarkable material with real promise, though its practical impact has been uneven. Its strengths are well documented: it is extremely thin, very strong, and an excellent conductor of heat and electricity. These properties have encouraged research in batteries, sensors, coatings, and flexible electronics, and some commercial products already benefit from graphene additives.\n\nHowever, the material also has notable limitations. Producing large sheets of high-quality graphene remains costly and difficult, and cheaper methods often yield inconsistent results. Pure graphene lacks a natural band gap, which limits its usefulness in conventional semiconductor applications. Integrating it into existing manufacturing processes is another hurdle, and marketing claims sometimes outpace what products actually deliver.\n\nOverall, graphene is scientifically impressive and valuable in targeted uses, but broad adoption will likely depend on further progress in scalable, affordable production."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "en-0095", "lang": "en", "target_id": "google-glass", "target_name": "Google Glass", "facts": ["Unveiled at Google I/O 2012 with skydivers livestreaming their jump through it onto the Moscone Center roof.", "The Explorer Edition sold to invited users in 2013 for $1,500.", "Wearers were nicknamed 'Glassholes'; bars, cinemas, and casinos banned the device over its camera.", "The consumer programme ended in January 2015; an Enterprise Edition continued until Google stopped selling it in 2023.", "Its display was a 640-by-360 prism in the corner of one eye."], "a": {"item_id": "1e6719e830b0~explain", "text": "Google Glass, everybody! You debuted with skydivers livestreaming their jump onto the roof of Moscone Center, and honestly, that was the last time you were trending upward.\n\nFifteen hundred dollars for the Explorer Edition. Invite only. Google invented the first velvet rope you paid to get mocked behind.\n\nYour display was 640 by 360, jammed in the corner of one eye. That's not augmented reality. That's a sticky note with Wi-Fi.\n\nAnd your users got a nickname: Glassholes. You're the only gadget that turned early adopters into an insult.\n\nBars banned you. Cinemas banned you. Casinos banned you. *Casinos!* Buildings made entirely of cameras looked at you and said, \"Whoa, too creepy.\"\n\nConsumers dumped you in 2015, but you clung to enterprise until 2023, like a guy who got kicked out of the party and came back as the caterer.\n\nThe joke is that Glass stayed in business after consumers rejected it, much as a guy thrown out of a party could return in a working role. The reference is that Google stopped selling Glass to consumers in January 2015 and kept the enterprise edition for business customers until it discontinued the product in 2023."}, "b": {"item_id": "1e6719e830b0", "text": "Google Glass, everybody! You debuted with skydivers livestreaming their jump onto the roof of Moscone Center, and honestly, that was the last time you were trending upward.\n\nFifteen hundred dollars for the Explorer Edition. Invite only. Google invented the first velvet rope you paid to get mocked behind.\n\nYour display was 640 by 360, jammed in the corner of one eye. That's not augmented reality. That's a sticky note with Wi-Fi.\n\nAnd your users got a nickname: Glassholes. You're the only gadget that turned early adopters into an insult.\n\nBars banned you. Cinemas banned you. Casinos banned you. *Casinos!* Buildings made entirely of cameras looked at you and said, \"Whoa, too creepy.\"\n\nConsumers dumped you in 2015, but you clung to enterprise until 2023, like a guy who got kicked out of the party and came back as the caterer."}, "gold": {"better": "b", "damage": "explain"}}, {"pair_id": "en-0096", "lang": "en", "target_id": "vision-pro", "target_name": "Apple Vision Pro", "facts": ["Released in February 2024 at $3,499, with an external battery pack on a cable rated for about two hours.", "'EyeSight' shows a rendering of the wearer's eyes on an outward-facing screen.", "Apple calls it 'spatial computing' and its App Store rules tell developers not to describe apps as AR, VR, XR, or MR.", "Reports during 2024 said Apple had cut production; analysts' first-year sales estimates were well under a million units.", "Weighs 600-650 g, before the battery."], "a": {"item_id": "0dbe035a7cc0", "text": "Look at this guy on the F train, pinching at nothing like he's seasoning a ghost. He spent thirty-five hundred dollars to ride the subway on the surface of the moon, and the moon still smells like pee. He cranked the Digital Crown all the way to Joshua Tree, and the mariachi band found him anyway. That's the dream: rich enough to be somewhere else, broke enough to still be on the F. And he *wants* you to notice. That's why his fake cartoon eyes glow on the outside, so strangers know he's \"present.\" Buddy, nobody's checking. You're on Mount Hood. He gets two hours of battery and three stops of dignity, with a cable snaking into his pocket like a subway IV drip. Meanwhile, the guy next to him reading an actual book? That guy's already somewhere else, for free."}, "b": {"item_id": "5bdc6524f71b", "text": "Apple Vision Pro, everybody! Thirty-five hundred dollars to look like a scuba diver lost in a Best Buy. Apple insists it's not VR, it's \"spatial computing,\" which is like calling a fanny pack \"hip luggage.\" It's so heavy they had to throw in a second strap, and the battery's a separate brick on a cord, so you're basically a lamp that walks. Two hours of charge! That's not a computer, that's a matinee. Then there's EyeSight, the screen that shows your eyes on the front, so your family can watch a dim, blurry you stare at nothing. Most honest feature Apple's ever made. Netflix wouldn't even make an app. Netflix! They're on smart fridges! And your Persona looks like a hostage video filmed inside the uncanny valley."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "en-0097", "lang": "en", "target_id": "beanie-babies", "target_name": "Beanie Babies", "facts": ["Launched by Ty Inc. in 1993 and sold only through small gift shops, with designs 'retired' to manufacture scarcity.", "At the height of the craze Beanie Babies accounted for about 10% of eBay's sales, and eBay's 1998 IPO filing said the site at times had over 30,000 Beanie Baby auctions running simultaneously in the quarter ending June 1998.", "In 1999 a divorcing couple divided their Beanie Baby collection one by one on a courtroom floor, in a photograph that ran worldwide.", "In 1999 Ty announced that Beanie Babies would be retired at the end of the year, then reversed itself after asking the public to vote and seeing an overwhelming vote to keep them.", "Most 'rare' Beanies that price guides valued in the thousands now sell for a few dollars."], "a": {"item_id": "44f6d00e7768", "text": "Beanie Babies. A beanbag with a tag and a business plan.\n\nTy sold them only in tiny gift shops, then \"retired\" designs to make them scarce. Retirement. For a stuffed frog. It got a better pension plan than you did.\n\nIn 1998, eBay told investors it sometimes had thirty thousand Beanie Baby auctions running at once. The future of commerce was a hippo stuffed with plastic pellets.\n\nIn 1999, a divorcing couple split their collection one by one on a courtroom floor. Till death do us part, or till the bears get appraised.\n\nThat same year Ty announced the end, held a public vote, and America begged to stay in the cult.\n\nThe \"rare\" ones the price guides valued in the thousands now sell for a few dollars.\n\nDon't laugh. You bought a JPEG of a monkey."}, "b": {"item_id": "9be7a6f8155e", "text": "Beanie Babies. The only retirement plan where the retiree was a five-dollar bear stuffed with plastic pellets. A whole generation bought plastic protectors for a toy's ear tag, then sealed the toy in a Ziploc so no child could ever love it. You weren't a stuffed animal; you were a 401(k) with a poem on it. And those poems! Ty Warner doing greeting-card Shakespeare for people who thought Princess the bear would pay for college. Princess paid for nothing. Neither did the Teenie Beanies everyone hoarded from McDonald's while throwing away the actual Happy Meal. A divorcing couple in Las Vegas literally split their collection on a courtroom floor, one Beanie at a time. That's your legacy: custody battles over a moose named Chocolate. Even your creator got busted hiding money from the IRS, because he was the only one who ever made any."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "en-0098", "lang": "en", "target_id": "papyrus", "target_name": "Papyrus", "facts": ["Hand-drawn by Chris Costello in 1982 over about six months, when he was 23; he sold the rights to Letraset for $750.", "Bundled with macOS and with Microsoft Office, which is why it appears on so many menus and signs.", "James Cameron's Avatar (2009) used it for the film's logo and subtitles.", "A 2017 Saturday Night Live sketch starring Ryan Gosling is about a man unable to get over that choice."], "a": {"item_id": "ebcdad06fe33", "text": "You opened a day spa. You wanted serenity, mystery, the ancient wisdom of the East. So you opened Microsoft Word and scrolled until something felt like a cucumber on your eyelid. Congratulations: your sanctuary now shares branding with a movie about blue cat people, a sixth-grade report on the Nile, and every church bake sale since 1998. Ryan Gosling lost sleep over James Cameron doing this. You did it for \"Serenity Nails & Wax\" and slept fine.\n\nAnd the Mediterranean bistro owner who asked, \"How do I show my hummus is authentic?\" then picked the font that came free with a Dell. Nothing says \"Grandmother's recipe\" like your nephew's laptop.\n\nYou wanted exotic. You got pre-installed. Nobody relaxes at your spa. They're too busy remembering which school project they typed it on."}, "b": {"item_id": "262a5fe6521d", "text": "Papyrus, you were hand-drawn over six months by a 23-year-old, and somehow it still looks like a first draft. Six months! You look like you were chiseled during an earthquake. Chris Costello sold you to Letraset for $750, the only time anyone has known exactly what you're worth.\n\nYou came bundled with macOS and Microsoft Office, so you're not popular, you're just pre-installed. You're the free hotel shampoo of fonts. Every menu, every sign: you didn't earn that, you just came with the computer.\n\nThen James Cameron imagined an entire alien world for Avatar, and when it came time for the logo and subtitles, he picked the default. Of all the things to leave untouched.\n\nIt got so bad that Saturday Night Live had Ryan Gosling play a man who can't get over it. You're not a typeface. You're a trauma with jagged edges."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "en-0099", "lang": "en", "target_id": "hawaiian-pizza", "target_name": "Hawaiian pizza", "facts": ["Invented in 1962 by Sam Panopoulos, a Greek immigrant, at the Satellite Restaurant in Chatham, Ontario, Canada; named after the brand of canned pineapple he used.", "Iceland's president Guðni Th. Jóhannesson said in 2017 that he would ban pineapple on pizza if he could, then clarified that a president has no such power.", "Hawaii is no longer a major pineapple producer: Del Monte shut its Hawaiian pineapple operations in November 2006, two years ahead of its announced 2008 exit, the state's last cannery closed in 2007, and most canned pineapple now comes from Thailand, Indonesia, and the Philippines.", "Panopoulos, whose restaurant also served Chinese dishes, told the Toronto Sun in 2010 that 'in those days people didn't mix the tastes like sweet and sour, except for Chinese food'."], "a": {"item_id": "b882d11f1a90", "text": "Hawaiian pizza. Let's start with the name, because that's the first lie. You were invented in Ontario. By a Greek guy. In 1962. You're Hawaiian the way a tiki bar in Ohio is Hawaiian. The only tropical thing about you is the can your pineapple came out of.\n\nAnd that pineapple: warm, wet, weeping syrup into the crust until the middle slice folds like a lawn chair. Pineapple contains an enzyme that breaks down protein, so the fruit is literally trying to digest the ham. Even your toppings hate each other.\n\nAnd the ham! Not prosciutto, not even pepperoni. Just pale pink squares that look like they were chiseled out of a deli's floor tile.\n\nYou're the pizza people order to seem quirky. \"I'm so random!\" No, Kevin. That's a fruit cup that fell on a pizza, and nobody cleaned it up."}, "b": {"item_id": "4644df27699e", "text": "Hawaiian pizza offers an appealing contrast between salty and sweet. The ham brings savory depth, while the pineapple adds brightness and acidity that can cut through the richness of the melted cheese. When well made, the combination feels balanced, and the juicy fruit provides a refreshing texture that many traditional toppings lack.\n\nHowever, the pizza has some drawbacks. Pineapple releases moisture during baking, which can leave the crust soggy, particularly in the center. Canned pineapple is often overly sweet, and this can overwhelm the more subtle flavors of the sauce and cheese. Quality also varies widely, since low-grade ham can taste overly processed.\n\nOverall, Hawaiian pizza is a reasonable choice for those who enjoy sweet and savory pairings. It works best when the fruit is drained well, the ham is of good quality, and the crust is sturdy enough to support the toppings."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "en-0100", "lang": "en", "target_id": "keurig", "target_name": "Keurig", "facts": ["John Sylvan invented the K-Cup in the early 1990s and sold his stake for about $50,000 in 1997; in 2015 he told The Atlantic, 'I feel bad sometimes that I ever did it.'", "Keurig 2.0 (2014) read an ink mark on the lid and rejected unlicensed pods; sales fell and workarounds spread online.", "By 2014 enough K-Cups were sold each year to circle the Earth more than ten times; the pods were mostly unrecyclable #7 plastic until a switch to polypropylene by 2020.", "Keurig Green Mountain merged with Dr Pepper Snapple in 2018 to form Keurig Dr Pepper."], "a": {"item_id": "8a5105614740", "text": "Keurig people want coffee the way they want feelings: one serving, sealed, no cleanup. You're not a coffee drinker, you're a pod collector. You bought the variety pack, and now you're thirty-eight, rationing the last Donut Shop because all that's left is hazelnut and a hot cocoa you're saving for a breakdown. You press \"Strong\" like it's a personality trait. You swore you'd use the reusable filter, and you bought one. It's in a drawer next to your gym key fob. You saved four whole minutes, and you spend them staring at a blinking descale light you've decided is decorative. The guy who invented the pod publicly regrets it. You built your mornings around a man's biggest apology. Enjoy your landfill latte, champ. Ready in sixty seconds, just like your effort."}, "b": {"item_id": "f0d9b215adbd", "text": "Keurig, you took the one drink humanity had perfected over five hundred years and said, \"What if it came in a tiny plastic thimble that outlives your grandchildren?\" Your own inventor said he regrets making you. When your dad disowns you, that's a phase. When the guy who invented you disowns you, that's a recall.\n\nYou make coffee one sad, lukewarm cup at a time, then flash \"DESCALE\" like a check-engine light for a machine that costs forty bucks. Remember Keurig 2.0? You put DRM on coffee. You were scanning pods like a bouncer: \"Sorry, sir, you're not on the list.\" Nobody was pirating Folgers! And your \"Strong\" button just drips slower, which isn't strength. That's procrastination.\n\nYou're not a coffee maker. You're a plastic-dispensing subscription that occasionally hiccups out hot brown water."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "en-0101", "lang": "en", "target_id": "luosifen", "target_name": "Luosifen (螺蛳粉)", "facts": ["A rice-noodle dish from Liuzhou, Guangxi; the smell comes from pickled bamboo shoots (酸笋), not from the river snails, which only flavour the broth.", "The instant version boomed during the 2020 lockdowns; Liuzhou's government reported packaged luosifen sales of over 10 billion yuan that year.", "Added to China's national intangible cultural heritage list in 2021.", "Liuzhou opened a luosifen industry college in 2020 to train workers for the trade."], "a": {"item_id": "7ce14da18bc9", "text": "Luosifen, the river-snail rice noodle dish from Liuzhou, offers a distinctive and layered eating experience. Its broth, simmered with snail, pork bones, and spices, is rich and tangy, and the toppings of pickled bamboo shoots, peanuts, fried tofu skin, and fresh greens add contrast in texture and flavor. The chewy rice noodles hold the chili-oil broth well, and packaged versions make the dish convenient and widely accessible.\n\nHowever, the dish has clear drawbacks. The fermented bamboo shoots produce a strong odor that many people find off-putting, especially in shared or enclosed spaces. The broth can also be quite oily and high in sodium, which may not suit everyone. Instant versions, while practical, tend to lack the depth of freshly made bowls.\n\nOverall, luosifen is rewarding for adventurous eaters who enjoy bold flavors, though newcomers may need time to adjust."}, "b": {"item_id": "e7872ece1527", "text": "Luosifen. River-snail rice noodles. Famous for the smell.\n\nExcept the snails don't smell. The snails just flavour the broth and mind their business. The stink is pickled bamboo shoots.\n\nIt's the only dish in China named after the one ingredient with an alibi.\n\n2020. The whole country locked indoors, and Liuzhou sold over ten billion yuan of the packaged stuff. A food you eat to punish the room, delivered to a nation that couldn't leave the room.\n\nSame year, Liuzhou opened a luosifen industry college. A diploma in fermented bamboo. Then 2021: national intangible cultural heritage.\n\nIntangible. Ask anyone who's shared a flat with it.\n\nI'm not judging you. Most of you ordered a box that spring.\n\nBe honest. You don't love luosifen.\n\nYou love that the person next to you can't open a window."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "en-0102", "lang": "en", "target_id": "google-glass", "target_name": "Google Glass", "facts": ["Unveiled at Google I/O 2012 with skydivers livestreaming their jump through it onto the Moscone Center roof.", "The Explorer Edition sold to invited users in 2013 for $1,500.", "Wearers were nicknamed 'Glassholes'; bars, cinemas, and casinos banned the device over its camera.", "The consumer programme ended in January 2015; an Enterprise Edition continued until Google stopped selling it in 2023.", "Its display was a 640-by-360 prism in the corner of one eye."], "a": {"item_id": "1e6719e830b0", "text": "Google Glass, everybody! You debuted with skydivers livestreaming their jump onto the roof of Moscone Center, and honestly, that was the last time you were trending upward.\n\nFifteen hundred dollars for the Explorer Edition. Invite only. Google invented the first velvet rope you paid to get mocked behind.\n\nYour display was 640 by 360, jammed in the corner of one eye. That's not augmented reality. That's a sticky note with Wi-Fi.\n\nAnd your users got a nickname: Glassholes. You're the only gadget that turned early adopters into an insult.\n\nBars banned you. Cinemas banned you. Casinos banned you. *Casinos!* Buildings made entirely of cameras looked at you and said, \"Whoa, too creepy.\"\n\nConsumers dumped you in 2015, but you clung to enterprise until 2023, like a guy who got kicked out of the party and came back as the caterer."}, "b": {"item_id": "3cc622cbd34b", "text": "Google Glass. Unveiled in 2012 by skydivers livestreaming their jump onto the Moscone Center roof.\nThe only launch that started in freefall and never pulled the cord.\n\nFifteen hundred dollars. Invitation only.\nYou had to be personally invited to become a Glasshole.\n\nBars banned it. Cinemas banned it. Casinos banned it.\nCasinos. People who'll happily take your house at 3 a.m. looked at your face and said, \"Not that.\"\n\nThe display: 640 by 360, a prism in the corner of one eye.\nThe future of computing, delivered as a permanent side-eye.\n\nConsumers dumped it in 2015. Enterprise held on until 2023.\n\nA camera on your face, and the whole world called you an asshole.\nSo you moved the camera to your hand. Now you point it at strangers all day, and we call that content."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "en-0103", "lang": "en", "target_id": "luosifen", "target_name": "Luosifen (螺蛳粉)", "facts": ["A rice-noodle dish from Liuzhou, Guangxi; the smell comes from pickled bamboo shoots (酸笋), not from the river snails, which only flavour the broth.", "The instant version boomed during the 2020 lockdowns; Liuzhou's government reported packaged luosifen sales of over 10 billion yuan that year.", "Added to China's national intangible cultural heritage list in 2021.", "Liuzhou opened a luosifen industry college in 2020 to train workers for the trade."], "a": {"item_id": "122921098a69", "text": "Luosifen, the river-snail rice noodle dish from Liuzhou, offers a distinctive and layered eating experience. The broth, simmered with snail and pork bones and seasoned with chili oil, is rich, sour, and pleasantly spicy. The chewy rice noodles hold the soup well, and toppings such as pickled bamboo shoots, peanuts, fried tofu skin, and fresh greens add contrast in texture and flavor. Packaged versions are convenient and widely available, making the dish accessible far beyond its home region.\n\nHowever, the fermented bamboo shoots produce a strong odor that many people find difficult, especially when cooking indoors or eating in shared spaces. The sodium and oil content is high, so it suits occasional rather than daily eating. Quality also varies between instant brands, and some taste flatter than freshly made bowls. Overall, it rewards those open to bold flavors, though first-time eaters may need patience."}, "b": {"item_id": "43ae1eace70a", "text": "Luosifen, you're the only dish that arrives before the delivery guy does. You're named after river snails, yet nobody has ever found one in the bowl. You boiled them for hours, took their soul, and threw out the bodies. That's not a recipe, that's a cover-up.\n\nAnd that smell? Fermented bamboo shoots that died, got buried, and came back for revenge. Someone opens a packet in Liuzhou and a guy in Guilin checks his shoes. College dorms banned you like a space heater. Neighbors call the gas company.\n\nYour fans swear, \"It smells terrible but tastes amazing.\" Buddy, that's what everyone says about their toxic ex.\n\nPeanuts, crispy tofu skin, wood ear, pickled beans, a slick of chili oil... that's not garnish, that's witness protection for flavor.\n\nAnd now you come instant. Congratulations: a biohazard with a seasoning packet."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "en-0104", "lang": "en", "target_id": "graphene", "target_name": "Graphene", "facts": ["Isolated in 2004 by Andre Geim and Konstantin Novoselov at Manchester by peeling graphite with Scotch tape; Nobel Prize in Physics, 2010.", "The EU's Graphene Flagship, launched in 2013, committed €1 billion over ten years.", "A 2018 study in Advanced Materials tested 'graphene' from 60 producers and found none with more than 50% graphene content; most was graphite flakes.", "Sold in tennis rackets, running shoes, and headphones as a performance ingredient.", "Geim also won an Ig Nobel Prize in 2000 for levitating a frog with magnets."], "a": {"item_id": "7c99f53190a8", "text": "Graphene, you were discovered with Scotch tape. Other materials get particle accelerators; you got the junk drawer. Then you won a Nobel Prize, which means Sweden officially honored the arts-and-crafts table.\n\nThe EU handed you a billion euros over ten years. A billion! Then a 2018 study tested \"graphene\" from 60 producers, and not one was even half graphene. Most of it was graphite flakes. You're not a miracle material, you're pencil shavings with a LinkedIn.\n\nYet you're in tennis rackets, running shoes, and headphones as a \"performance ingredient.\" Buddy, the only thing you've boosted is the price tag.\n\nAnd one of your own discoverers once levitated a frog with magnets. Honestly? That frog delivered more on its promises than you have. At least it actually got off the ground."}, "b": {"item_id": "683abdc9f22c", "text": "Let's hear it for the graphene evangelists, the only people who've been \"five years away\" since 2004. Two guys in Manchester peeled pencil lead with Scotch tape, won a Nobel, and you looked at that and thought: finally, a career plan. Every grant proposal you write has the same three slides: space elevator, seawater you can drink, a phone that charges in six seconds. The funding agency nods like it's never seen them before, because it's seen them so many times it's slipped into a coma. Twenty years of press releases, and what have you actually shipped? A tennis racket. Headphones with \"GRAPHENE\" printed bigger than the brand name. You love telling people it's 200 times stronger than steel. Sure. Your claims, meanwhile, are one atom thick. You didn't discover a wonder material. You discovered wonder funding."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "en-0105", "lang": "en", "target_id": "javascript", "target_name": "JavaScript", "facts": ["Written by Brendan Eich at Netscape in ten days in May 1995; named Mocha, then LiveScript, then JavaScript to ride Java's marketing, although the two are unrelated.", "typeof null returns 'object', a bug from the first version that has been kept for compatibility ever since.", "In March 2016 the author of left-pad, an 11-line npm package, unpublished it and broke builds across the industry, including Babel and React.", "[] + {} evaluates to '[object Object]'; {} + [] typed at a console evaluates to 0.", "Standardized by Ecma as 'ECMAScript' in 1997 because the name JavaScript is a trademark, first Sun's and now Oracle's."], "a": {"item_id": "2efbc0be89c8", "text": "JavaScript is one of the most accessible and widely used programming languages. It runs natively in every major browser, which makes it indispensable for web development, and with Node.js it extends comfortably to servers, tooling, and scripting. Its ecosystem is vast, offering libraries and frameworks for nearly any need, and its flexible syntax allows beginners to produce working results quickly.\n\nThat same flexibility brings drawbacks. Dynamic typing and implicit type coercion can lead to subtle bugs that surface only at runtime. Historical design inconsistencies remain for backward compatibility, so developers must learn certain patterns to avoid. The sheer number of tools and packages can also make project setup and dependency management feel overwhelming, and the pace of change may be difficult to follow.\n\nModern features such as modules, async/await, and optional TypeScript support address many concerns. Overall, JavaScript is a pragmatic, capable choice, provided teams adopt consistent conventions and tooling."}, "b": {"item_id": "33485cfe6aa5", "text": "JavaScript, give it up! Built in ten days in 1995, and honestly, it shows. Most of us take longer to name a dog. And you needed three tries: Mocha, LiveScript, then \"JavaScript,\" named after Java purely for the marketing. That's not branding, that's wearing someone else's name tag. You can't even legally use it. Oracle owns it, so on paper you're \"ECMAScript,\" which sounds like a skin condition.\n\ntypeof null is \"object.\" A bug from your first version, and you kept it. For compatibility. That's not a quirk, that's a tattoo of your ex's name.\n\n[] + {} is \",\" but type {} + [] at the console and it's zero. Even your addition has mood swings.\n\nAnd in 2016, the author of left-pad unpublished eleven lines and took down Babel and React. Eleven lines! Your whole ecosystem is a Jenga tower standing on a toothpick."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "en-0106", "lang": "en", "target_id": "excel", "target_name": "Microsoft Excel", "facts": ["First shipped for the Macintosh in 1985; the Windows version followed in 1987.", "Silently converts gene names such as SEPT2 and MARCH1 into dates; by August 2020 the HUGO Gene Nomenclature Committee had renamed 27 human genes (MARCH1 became MARCHF1, SEPT2 became SEPTIN2) and published guidelines in Nature Genetics to stop it.", "In October 2020, Public Health England lost 15,841 COVID-19 cases when a legacy .xls file hit its 65,536-row limit.", "Reinhart and Rogoff's 2010 paper 'Growth in a Time of Debt', cited for austerity policies, omitted five countries through a spreadsheet range error found by a graduate student in 2013.", "Sheets have been capped at 1,048,576 rows by 16,384 columns since Excel 2007."], "a": {"item_id": "5e338084a47b", "text": "Microsoft Excel remains one of the most capable spreadsheet applications available. Its formula library is extensive, and features such as PivotTables, Power Query, and conditional formatting make it well suited to data analysis, budgeting, and reporting. The interface is familiar to many users, and compatibility with other Microsoft products and common file formats is a practical advantage. Recent additions like dynamic arrays and XLOOKUP have also improved everyday efficiency.\n\nHowever, Excel has limitations. Beginners may find the volume of features overwhelming, and advanced functions often require considerable time to learn. Performance can slow with very large datasets, and collaboration, while improved, can feel less seamless than in some web-based alternatives. Complex workbooks may also become difficult to audit, increasing the risk of unnoticed errors. Licensing costs may be a consideration for individuals and small organizations.\n\nOverall, Excel is a dependable, powerful tool, best suited to users willing to invest time in learning it."}, "b": {"item_id": "ea67f7f14f3d", "text": "Excel. Born 1985. Forty years old, and it still can't tell a gene from a birthday.\n\nGeneticists typed SEPT2. Excel said, \"Ah. September second.\"\n\nSo in 2020, scientists renamed twenty-seven human genes.\n\nHumanity edited its own genome to keep a spreadsheet happy.\n\nPublic Health England tracked COVID in an old .xls file. Limit: 65,536 rows. Fifteen thousand eight hundred forty-one cases didn't recover. They fell off the bottom of the sheet.\n\nReinhart and Rogoff left five countries out of a cell range. Politicians cited the paper to justify austerity. A grad student caught it three years later.\n\nNow it holds a million rows. Sixteen thousand columns. Room for so many more mistakes.\n\nExcel never warns you. Never argues. Never says no.\n\nI know the type. You keep that one around too."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "en-0107", "lang": "en", "target_id": "carbon-nanotubes", "target_name": "Carbon nanotubes", "facts": ["Reported by Sumio Iijima in Nature in 1991.", "Promised as the cable for a space elevator; a 2006 analysis showed that ordinary defects cut the strength of a macroscopic cable far below the ideal value.", "Long, rigid multi-walled nanotubes behaved like asbestos fibres in a 2008 mouse study in Nature Nanotechnology.", "Sorting metallic from semiconducting tubes has blocked nanotube electronics for three decades; MIT's 16-bit RV16X-NANO processor (2019) is still a lab demonstration.", "Today they are sold mainly as a conductive additive in lithium-ion battery electrodes."], "a": {"item_id": "839efb519f46", "text": "Nanotube founders are the only people who've said \"within five years\" every year since 1991 and kept a straight face. They open with a slide of a space elevator, then ask for seed money to make a vial of black dust. Every deck has the same line, \"a hundred times stronger than steel,\" and every deck is quietly a plan to sell additive to tennis racket companies. These are PhDs who spent six years sorting chirality and came out wanting a Tesla. The pitch is always \"we've solved scale,\" and the demo is always one centimeter long. VCs keep a nanotube guy around like a lucky rabbit's foot: he never pays off, but he makes the fund sound visionary. And when someone asks about lung toxicity, he says \"totally different from asbestos\" with the confidence of a man who googled it in the elevator. The regular one."}, "b": {"item_id": "fbc9fe2a3858", "text": "Carbon nanotubes are a remarkable material with a distinctive combination of properties. Their tensile strength is exceptional relative to their weight, and they conduct electricity and heat very efficiently. These qualities make them promising for composites, batteries, sensors, and next-generation electronics.\n\nHowever, practical use remains challenging. Production methods often yield mixtures of nanotube types, and separating them by chirality or purity can be costly. Dispersing nanotubes evenly in other materials is difficult, and performance in bulk form frequently falls short of the properties seen in individual tubes. Manufacturing at scale also remains expensive compared with established alternatives.\n\nHealth and environmental questions add further caution. Some forms resemble asbestos fibers, so careful handling and continued safety research are warranted. Overall, carbon nanotubes show considerable potential, but wider adoption depends on improved consistency, lower costs, and clearer safety guidance."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "en-0108", "lang": "en", "target_id": "graphene", "target_name": "Graphene", "facts": ["Isolated in 2004 by Andre Geim and Konstantin Novoselov at Manchester by peeling graphite with Scotch tape; Nobel Prize in Physics, 2010.", "The EU's Graphene Flagship, launched in 2013, committed €1 billion over ten years.", "A 2018 study in Advanced Materials tested 'graphene' from 60 producers and found none with more than 50% graphene content; most was graphite flakes.", "Sold in tennis rackets, running shoes, and headphones as a performance ingredient.", "Geim also won an Ig Nobel Prize in 2000 for levitating a frog with magnets."], "a": {"item_id": "a342f5b32d47", "text": "Graphene. The wonder material. Discovered in 2004 when two physicists at Manchester peeled graphite with Scotch tape, so your origin story has the same equipment as a kindergarten art project. They got a Nobel in 2010. You got a career as a buzzword.\n\nThe EU committed a billion euros over ten years to you. A billion! And where did you end up? Tennis rackets. Running shoes. Headphones. Miracle of the century, living in a sneaker.\n\nThen in 2018, researchers tested \"graphene\" from 60 producers, and none of it was even half graphene. Mostly graphite flakes. You're so elusive your own suppliers can't find you.\n\nHonestly, Andre Geim levitated a frog with magnets in 2000. That frog has a better résumé than you. At least the frog actually showed up."}, "b": {"item_id": "b0d5fce2f888", "text": "Graphene. The wonder material. Stronger than steel, thinner than anything, about to change the world.\n\nIt's been about to change the world since 2004.\n\nTwo guys at Manchester peeled it off graphite with Scotch tape. Nobel Prize, 2010.\n\nTape. The most advanced equipment in that lab was stationery.\n\nThen the EU handed it a billion euros over ten years.\n\nAnd it went straight into tennis rackets. Running shoes. Headphones.\n\nA billion euros to make your backhand feel expensive.\n\nIn 2018, someone finally tested \"graphene\" from sixty producers. Not one was even half graphene. Mostly graphite flakes.\n\nThat's pencil lead. You paid a Nobel markup for a pencil.\n\nGeim also levitated a frog with magnets. Ig Nobel, 2000.\n\nThe frog never promised to revolutionize batteries.\n\nAnd you're all wearing the shoes right now."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "en-0109", "lang": "en", "target_id": "vim", "target_name": "Vim", "facts": ["Released by Bram Moolenaar in November 1991 as 'Vi IMitation', a vi clone he had begun writing for the Amiga in 1988 on top of Stevie; the expansion became 'Vi IMproved' with version 2.0 in December 1993.", "Licensed as charityware: the licence asks users to donate to ICCF Holland, a charity Moolenaar ran for children in Uganda.", "Stack Overflow's question 'How to exit the Vim editor?' passed one million visits in May 2017, which Stack Overflow marked with a blog post.", "Modal editing: in normal mode, typing letters runs commands rather than inserting text; :wq saves and quits, :q! quits and discards changes."], "a": {"item_id": "5e7b2480a6e2", "text": "Vim is a highly efficient text editor, particularly for those who invest time in learning it. Its modal design separates navigation from insertion, which allows complex edits to be performed with just a few keystrokes. It is lightweight, starts quickly, and runs almost anywhere, including remote servers where graphical tools are unavailable. Extensive customization through plugins and configuration files lets users shape the editor around their workflow.\n\nHowever, the learning curve is steep. New users often find modal editing unintuitive, and basic tasks can feel awkward until the commands become habitual. Out of the box, Vim offers fewer modern conveniences than many contemporary editors, so features like autocompletion or language support typically require additional setup. Configuration can be time-consuming, and plugin ecosystems vary in quality and maintenance.\n\nOverall, Vim rewards patience and regular practice. It suits developers and administrators who value speed and control, though those seeking immediate accessibility may prefer a more conventional editor."}, "b": {"item_id": "ca380028e5ac", "text": "Vim. Released in 1991 as \"Vi IMitation.\"\nTwo years later, version 2.0: \"Vi IMproved.\"\nTwo years, one version bump, and Imitation became Improved.\nThat's not a release. That's a LinkedIn update.\n\nVim users say it's the fastest editor alive.\nOnce you're inside.\nIn 2017, \"How to exit the Vim editor?\" passed one million visits on Stack Overflow.\nOne million people. One question. One door.\nIn normal mode, the letters you type don't become text. They become consequences.\n:q! quits and throws away everything you did.\nThe most honest button in software.\n\nThe licence is charityware. It asks you to donate to a charity for children in Uganda.\nEveryone in this room has their .vimrc memorized.\nHands up if you donated."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "en-0110", "lang": "en", "target_id": "durian", "target_name": "Durian", "facts": ["Banned on Singapore's MRT trains and in many Southeast Asian hotels because of the smell.", "A 2017 genome study in Nature Genetics traced the odour to a duplicated family of MGL genes that pump out volatile sulfur compounds.", "China buys most of the world's durian exports, chiefly from Thailand, in a trade worth billions of dollars a year.", "Alfred Russel Wallace wrote in 1856 that eating it was 'a new sensation worth a voyage to the East to experience'."], "a": {"item_id": "e97b85a188ed", "text": "Let's talk about the guy who eats durian on the train. Sir, Singapore, a country that will cane you for chewing gum, saw you coming and put your snack on a sign right next to \"no flammables.\" You're not hungry. You're running a loyalty test. Every face that twists is another person who \"just isn't adventurous.\" You paid sixty dollars for Musang King so you could explain Musang King to a stranger who's holding her breath. \"It's like custard, it's heaven, you just have to get past the-\" No. We don't. You brought a hotel-banned substance to a commute and called it a personality. And the best part? You'll be the only one who forgets you ate it. Your coworkers won't. Your hotel won't. Your Uber driver has already filed a one-star review with God."}, "b": {"item_id": "f26f8134f565", "text": "Durian, they call you the King of Fruits, which tracks, because like most monarchies, you stink and nobody's allowed to say it to your face.\n\nYou're the only fruit with a criminal record. Banned on the Singapore subway, alongside flammable liquids. Hotels put your picture on signs next to \"no smoking.\" In 2019, a university in Melbourne evacuated five hundred people because they thought there was a gas leak. Nope. Just you, sitting in a cupboard, being yourself.\n\nAnd the armor! You're covered in spikes like a medieval mace. Nature built you to defend yourself, then gave you a smell that does it better. The spikes are just overkill.\n\nFans say you taste like custard. Sure. Custard that was left in a gym bag next to some onions. Your flavor isn't an acquired taste. It's a hostage situation."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "en-0111", "lang": "en", "target_id": "las-vegas", "target_name": "Las Vegas", "facts": ["Nevada legalized casino gambling in 1931, the year construction of the Hoover Dam began nearby.", "Most of the Strip lies not in the city of Las Vegas but in the unincorporated town of Paradise.", "The Sphere, opened in 2023 at a cost of about $2.3 billion, is the largest spherical building in the world.", "'What happens here, stays here' was launched by the local convention and visitors authority in 2003.", "Lake Mead, its water source, fell in July 2022 to about 1,040 feet, its lowest since the reservoir first filled in 1937, and dropped below that mark again in August 2026."], "a": {"item_id": "3b3f5425a5db", "text": "Las Vegas, you legalized gambling in 1931, the same year they started building the Hoover Dam next door. So from day one the plan was: lose your money, then go stare at a giant wall of concrete and think about what you've done.\n\nAnd most of the Strip isn't even in Las Vegas. It's in an unincorporated town called Paradise. Your most famous street is a lie, and the lie is named Paradise. That's the most Vegas sentence ever spoken.\n\nYou dropped $2.3 billion on the world's largest spherical building. Meanwhile, in July 2022, Lake Mead, your actual water supply, sank to its lowest level since it first filled in 1937. Priorities! A giant glowing eyeball in the desert, just watching the water leave.\n\nYour slogan is \"What happens here, stays here.\" Apparently not the water."}, "b": {"item_id": "7a9bd346890e", "text": "Las Vegas, the only city where you can see the Eiffel Tower, a pyramid, and Venice in one afternoon and still feel like you never left a strip mall in Ohio. You're a city built in the desert out of pure spite and borrowed water. The Colorado River is literally drying up so a fake volcano can erupt every fifteen minutes for a guy in cargo shorts holding a yard-long margarita.\n\nYour casinos have no clocks and no windows, which is smart, because nobody wants to know it's 9 a.m. on a Tuesday and Grandma's on her third oxygen tank at the Wheel of Fortune slots.\n\n\"What happens in Vegas stays in Vegas\"? Sure. Mostly your 401(k). And now you've got the Sphere, a giant glowing eyeball, so the whole city can watch you make every mistake in 4K."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "en-0112", "lang": "en", "target_id": "oat-milk", "target_name": "Oat milk", "facts": ["Oatly started in Sweden in the 1990s, built on Lund University research by Rickard Öste on lactose intolerance.", "Oatly's slogan 'It's like milk but made for humans' was banned in Sweden in 2015 after the dairy lobby LRF Mjölk sued; Oatly then published the lawsuit as advertising.", "Oatly listed on Nasdaq in May 2021 at a valuation near $10 billion; the shares later lost more than 90% of their value.", "Commercial oat milk is made by breaking oat starch into maltose with amylase enzymes, and many brands, Oatly among them, add rapeseed oil; the result has more sugar and a higher glycaemic index than almond or soy milk."], "a": {"item_id": "db267993b2ef", "text": "Oat milk has become a popular dairy alternative, and it has real merits. Its naturally creamy texture works well in coffee and tea, and it froths reasonably well for lattes. The mild, slightly sweet flavor appeals to many people, and it is free of lactose, nuts, and soy, which makes it accessible to those with common allergies. Its environmental footprint is generally lower than dairy's, requiring less water and land than many alternatives.\n\nThere are drawbacks, however. Oat milk is typically higher in carbohydrates and sugars than almond or soy milk, which may concern people managing blood sugar. It contains less protein than dairy or soy, so it is not an equal nutritional substitute unless fortified. Some brands include added oils, thickeners, or sweeteners, and prices tend to exceed those of cow's milk. Those with celiac disease should also look for certified gluten-free versions. Overall, it is a pleasant, versatile option when chosen thoughtfully."}, "b": {"item_id": "af374acc88a4", "text": "Oat milk. \"It's like milk, but made for humans.\"\n\nSweden banned that slogan in 2015 after the dairy lobby sued. Oatly printed the lawsuit as an ad.\n\nOnly brand on earth that needs a cow to feel like a rebel.\n\nThe recipe: oats, enzymes that turn the starch into maltose, then rapeseed oil.\n\nThat's not milk. That's a granola bar that got hydrated.\n\nMore sugar than almond, higher glycaemic index than soy. Made for humans. Specifically, humans who are angry at their pancreas.\n\n2021, Nasdaq, ten billion dollars. Then down more than ninety percent.\n\nTurns out it's not just the foam that separates.\n\nAnd tomorrow you'll still pay seven dollars for it, because it's \"healthier.\"\n\nYou're not lactose intolerant. You're label intolerant."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "en-0113", "lang": "en", "target_id": "vim", "target_name": "Vim", "facts": ["Released by Bram Moolenaar in November 1991 as 'Vi IMitation', a vi clone he had begun writing for the Amiga in 1988 on top of Stevie; the expansion became 'Vi IMproved' with version 2.0 in December 1993.", "Licensed as charityware: the licence asks users to donate to ICCF Holland, a charity Moolenaar ran for children in Uganda.", "Stack Overflow's question 'How to exit the Vim editor?' passed one million visits in May 2017, which Stack Overflow marked with a blog post.", "Modal editing: in normal mode, typing letters runs commands rather than inserting text; :wq saves and quits, :q! quits and discards changes."], "a": {"item_id": "5e7b2480a6e2", "text": "Vim is a highly efficient text editor, particularly for those who invest time in learning it. Its modal design separates navigation from insertion, which allows complex edits to be performed with just a few keystrokes. It is lightweight, starts quickly, and runs almost anywhere, including remote servers where graphical tools are unavailable. Extensive customization through plugins and configuration files lets users shape the editor around their workflow.\n\nHowever, the learning curve is steep. New users often find modal editing unintuitive, and basic tasks can feel awkward until the commands become habitual. Out of the box, Vim offers fewer modern conveniences than many contemporary editors, so features like autocompletion or language support typically require additional setup. Configuration can be time-consuming, and plugin ecosystems vary in quality and maintenance.\n\nOverall, Vim rewards patience and regular practice. It suits developers and administrators who value speed and control, though those seeking immediate accessibility may prefer a more conventional editor."}, "b": {"item_id": "81372d87c757", "text": "Vim. Begun in 1988 as \"Vi IMitation,\" a clone of vi, built on top of Stevie, another clone. You're a photocopy of a photocopy that got promoted. Then in 1993 you renamed yourself \"Vi IMproved,\" the most confident thing anyone has ever said about their own fan fiction.\n\nYour licence is charityware, asking users to donate to help children in Uganda. Beautiful cause. But after an hour inside you, the user is the one who needs rescuing.\n\nStack Overflow's \"How to exit the Vim editor?\" passed one million visits. They threw it a blog post. You don't have users, Vim. You have hostages.\n\nAnd in normal mode, type a letter and it doesn't appear. It *executes*. You treat the alphabet like a loaded weapon.\n\nAnyway, I'm leaving. :q! No changes saved, just like everyone who's ever tried to fix you."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "en-0114", "lang": "en", "target_id": "almond-milk", "target_name": "Almond milk", "facts": ["California grows about 80% of the world's almonds.", "A single almond takes about 1.1 US gallons (roughly 4 litres) of water to grow, a figure Mother Jones published in 2014 that spread during California's drought.", "A 2015 US class action alleged Almond Breeze contained only about 2% almonds; it partly survived a motion to dismiss, was stayed in 2017 pending a separate Blue Diamond labelling settlement, and the plaintiffs then dropped it.", "Pollinating California's almond orchards each February requires trucking in most of the commercial honeybee colonies in the United States.", "Medieval European cooks used almond milk as a Lenten substitute for dairy, centuries before the current fashion."], "a": {"item_id": "e2f0ad365549", "text": "Almond milk is a practical option for many people, particularly those avoiding dairy or lactose. Its mild, slightly nutty flavor works well in coffee, cereal, and smoothies, and unsweetened varieties are low in calories and sugar. Many brands are fortified with calcium and vitamins D and E, which helps it compare reasonably to dairy in certain nutrients. It also tends to have a long shelf life before opening.\n\nHowever, almond milk has some limitations. It contains very little protein, usually about one gram per cup, so it is not a nutritional replacement for cow's milk or soy milk. Its thin texture may disappoint those who prefer a creamier drink, and some products contain added gums or sweeteners. Almonds also require substantial water to grow, which raises environmental concerns. Finally, it is unsuitable for anyone with a tree nut allergy. Overall, it is a decent choice when selected carefully, but it works best as one part of a varied diet."}, "b": {"item_id": "557534fbb1d8", "text": "Almond milk. The ethical choice.\n\nCalifornia grows about eighty percent of the world's almonds. California. The state with the drought.\n\nOne almond takes about a gallon of water. One nut. One gallon.\n\nAnd how much almond is in the carton? A 2015 lawsuit alleged about two percent.\n\nSo you're buying water. Grown with water. In a drought. To feel good about water.\n\nYou quit dairy so no animal would suffer for your latte. Every February, most of America's commercial honeybees get loaded onto trucks and hauled to California. A cow stays home. A bee commutes.\n\nAnd it's not even new. Medieval Europeans drank almond milk during Lent. Forty days of giving up something you love.\n\nYou drink it all year, by choice, and pay extra.\n\nThe monks at least knew it was a sacrifice."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "en-0115", "lang": "en", "target_id": "ugg", "target_name": "UGG boots", "facts": ["Sheepskin boots Australian surfers wore to warm up after sessions; Brian Smith started selling them in California in 1978.", "Deckers Outdoor bought the brand in 1995 and holds 'UGG' as a trademark in the US and elsewhere; in Australia, 'ugg' is a generic word any bootmaker may use.", "In 2019, a US jury found an Australian maker liable for selling boots labelled 'ugg' into the United States.", "Oprah Winfrey featured them in her 'Favorite Things' lists repeatedly in the 2000s, which drove the first boom.", "UGG is Deckers' largest brand, with over $2 billion in annual sales."], "a": {"item_id": "7d4b03fcc766", "text": "UGG boots. You started as what Australian surfers pulled on to warm up after a session. Recovery footwear. A towel with a sole. Then Brian Smith brought you to California in 1978, and Americans looked at a post-surf foot blanket and said, \"Perfect. For the mall.\"\n\nThen Oprah named you a Favorite Thing. Repeatedly. Most boots get famous by walking somewhere. You got famous sitting on a couch.\n\nDeckers bought you in 1995 and trademarked \"UGG.\" In Australia, \"ugg\" is just a word; any bootmaker can use it. In America, a jury found an Australian maker liable for selling \"ugg\" boots. Imagine getting sued for using your own word for slippers.\n\nAnd now you pull in over two billion dollars a year. Not bad for a sheep that's been told it can go outside."}, "b": {"item_id": "2082912cfaf3", "text": "Let's hear it for the people wearing UGGs in July. You're not dressing for the weather. You're dressing for 2005, still waiting for Paris Hilton to text back. Australian surfers made these to warm their feet after the ocean. You wear them to warm your feet after the Trader Joe's parking lot. In August. In Phoenix. What are you after? \"Comfort,\" you say. Babe, your ankles have been collapsing outward since the Bush administration. You walk like a Clydesdale that's given up. And what does it get you? One summer rainstorm and you're squelching through Target like a wet golden retriever who made bad choices. People don't ask where you got them. They ask if you're okay. Congratulations: you've turned footwear into a wellness check."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "en-0116", "lang": "en", "target_id": "jackfruit", "target_name": "Jackfruit", "facts": ["The largest fruit that grows on a tree; single fruits can weigh over 40 kg.", "The national fruit of Bangladesh and the state fruit of Kerala and Tamil Nadu.", "Unripe flesh is sold as a vegan 'pulled pork' substitute, although it has only 1-2 g of protein per 100 g.", "Cutting one releases a sticky white latex, so cooks oil their knives and hands first.", "A member of the mulberry family (Moraceae), a relative of breadfruit and figs."], "a": {"item_id": "7d4be91deb83", "text": "Jackfruit is a versatile fruit with a distinctive profile. Ripe, it offers a sweet, tropical flavor reminiscent of banana and pineapple, while unripe jackfruit has a mild taste and a stringy texture that works well as a meat substitute in curries, tacos, and sandwiches. It provides fiber, vitamin C, and potassium, and it is often more sustainable to grow than many protein sources.\n\nHowever, it has drawbacks. Preparing a fresh jackfruit is messy and time-consuming, as the sticky latex and thick rind require patience and oiled tools. The aroma of the ripe fruit is strong and may not appeal to everyone. Canned versions are convenient but can be bland and often need generous seasoning. Nutritionally, it is low in protein, so it is not a complete replacement for meat. Overall, jackfruit is rewarding for those willing to experiment, though it may not suit every palate or schedule."}, "b": {"item_id": "11d60bd9214d", "text": "Let's hear it for the jackfruit pulled-pork crowd. You found something grandmothers in Kerala have cooked for centuries, slapped \"plant-based\" on a can, and tripled the price. Bold move, gentrifying a curry.\n\nYour entire pitch is \"it shreds like pork.\" So does a wet paper towel. You know what makes it taste like pulled pork? The barbecue sauce. You're not selling a meat substitute. You're selling a sauce delivery system with a mission statement.\n\nAnd the protein! Two grams. You told a generation of vegans they're eating a brisket sandwich and handed them a fruit cup in a trench coat.\n\nBut hey, it works. You get the Whole Foods endcap, the podcast slot, and the warm glow of saving the planet one eighteen-dollar taco at a time. The pig's still alive. So is your margin."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "en-0117", "lang": "en", "target_id": "nuclear-fusion", "target_name": "Nuclear fusion power", "facts": ["Has been 'about 30 years away' since the 1950s; at the 1955 Geneva atoms-for-peace conference, Homi Bhabha predicted fusion power within two decades.", "ITER, agreed in 2006 for first plasma in 2016, re-baselined in 2024 to start research operations in 2034 and deuterium-tritium burning in 2039, at a cost estimated above €20 billion.", "NIF's December 2022 'ignition' shot released 3.15 MJ from 2.05 MJ of laser energy; the lasers drew about 300 MJ from the grid to fire.", "Private fusion companies had raised more than $7 billion by 2024."], "a": {"item_id": "1c3c0919598f", "text": "Nuclear fusion: the only technology that's been \"thirty years away\" for seventy years. In 1955, Homi Bhabha promised fusion power within two decades. Two decades later? Still two decades. You're not an energy source. You're a horizon.\n\nLook at ITER. Agreed in 2006, first plasma by 2016. Then 2024 rolls around and it's \"research operations in 2034, actual deuterium-tritium burning in 2039,\" for over twenty billion euros. That's not a construction schedule. That's a mortgage.\n\nAnd NIF's big \"ignition\" shot: 3.15 megajoules out from 2.05 in! Historic! Except the lasers pulled about 300 megajoules from the grid just to fire. That's like bragging your lemonade stand turned a profit after you bought the orchard.\n\nStill, private investors have thrown you more than seven billion dollars. So congratulations, fusion. You finally achieved net gain. Just not in energy."}, "b": {"item_id": "f84181eac144", "text": "Let's hear it for the fusion people, the only scientists who've been \"ten years away\" since my grandfather had hair. You can hold plasma hotter than the sun for a few seconds, but you can't hold a deadline for one decade. Every press release: \"Net energy gain!\" Sure, if you don't count the electricity bill for the lasers. That's like me saying I'm profitable if you ignore rent. ITER got approved when Myspace was cool, and it's still assembling the donut. And the startups: \"We're different, we have venture capital.\" Great, now the sun is ten years away *and* has a pitch deck. Honestly, it's a beautiful racket: promise infinite clean energy, collect the grant, retire before anyone plugs in a toaster. You're not building a star. You're building a pension."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "en-0118", "lang": "en", "target_id": "risk", "target_name": "Risk", "facts": ["Invented in 1957 by Albert Lamorisse, the French director of The Red Balloon, as La Conquête du Monde; Parker Brothers published it in the United States in 1959.", "The map has 42 territories, among them Kamchatka, Irkutsk, and Yakutsk, while the entire Middle East is one territory.", "Holding Australia, which has a single entry point, is the standard opening strategy.", "A full game can run for hours, and players eliminated early have nothing to do but watch."], "a": {"item_id": "c977367b04bb", "text": "Risk, you were invented by the man who directed The Red Balloon. A gentle film about a boy and a balloon, and his follow-up was \"What if your uncle conquered the planet at Thanksgiving?\"\n\nYour map makes room for Kamchatka, Irkutsk, AND Yakutsk, but the entire Middle East is one territory. That's not cartography; that's a guy who ran out of crayon somewhere over Siberia.\n\nEvery game opens the same way: someone grabs Australia because it has one entry point, then hides there like it's a panic room with kangaroos.\n\nAnd a full game runs for hours. Get eliminated early and congratulations, you've been demoted from general to audience. You lost the world, and now you have to sit there and watch someone else own it. That's not a board game. That's a timeshare presentation with continents."}, "b": {"item_id": "d51fb5c2a0bd", "text": "Risk, you're the only game where \"world domination\" takes longer than actual world domination. Genghis Khan conquered Asia faster than my cousin conquered Ukraine, and my cousin had six armies and a dream. Your map looks like it was drawn from a description of Earth overheard on a bus. Kamchatka borders Alaska? Irkutsk and Yakutsk get more real estate than most of Africa? You taught a generation that the winning strategy is hiding in Australia like a coward and waiting for everyone else to bleed out over Siam. The defender wins ties, which explains the forty armies I lost trying to get through one guy in Egypt. And one plastic cannon is worth ten soldiers? Finally, accurate military budgeting. Five hours in, two players left, nobody's had fun since the first card set. That's not a board game, Risk. That's a hostage situation."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "en-0119", "lang": "en", "target_id": "comic-sans", "target_name": "Comic Sans", "facts": ["Designed by Vincent Connare at Microsoft in 1994, modeled on the comic-book lettering in The Dark Knight Returns and Watchmen.", "Made for the speech bubbles of Rover, the cartoon dog in Microsoft Bob; it missed Bob's release and shipped instead in the Windows 95 Plus! pack.", "CERN's July 2012 Higgs boson discovery announcement was presented on slides set in it.", "The 'Ban Comic Sans' campaign, started by designers Dave and Holly Combs in 1999, ran until 2019, when they retired it and launched usecomicsans.com."], "a": {"item_id": "03c499f5e2df", "text": "Comic Sans, designed by Vincent Connare in 1994, has genuine strengths that are often overlooked. Its irregular, rounded letterforms are highly legible, and the distinct shapes of characters like \"b,\" \"d,\" \"p,\" and \"q\" can help some readers with dyslexia distinguish between them. The casual tone makes it approachable for children's materials, classroom handouts, and informal notices where a friendly voice is desired.\n\nIts weaknesses are mostly a matter of context. The playful character feels out of place in formal documents, legal notices, business correspondence, or serious announcements, where it can undermine the intended tone. The spacing and weight are also uneven, which becomes noticeable in longer passages of text. It lacks the refinement and range of weights found in more versatile typefaces.\n\nOverall, Comic Sans is a useful tool when matched to an appropriate purpose. Its limitations lie less in its design than in how widely it has been applied."}, "b": {"item_id": "578b451593da", "text": "Comic Sans, you were born in 1994 to put words in the mouth of a cartoon dog in Microsoft Bob, and somehow you're the one that survived. Bob is dead. The dog is dead. You're on a sympathy card at a funeral home.\n\nYou were inspired by Batman comics, and you've never once been near anything heroic. CERN used you to announce the Higgs boson: humanity's greatest discovery, presented like a lemonade stand. Dan Gilbert used you to call LeBron a traitor, because nothing says \"furious billionaire\" like bubble letters.\n\nAnd your defense is always \"Well, I help dyslexic readers.\" Great. You're the guy at the party whose whole personality is a doctor's note.\n\nEven Vincent Connare, the man who made you, says he's barely ever used you. Your own dad won't put you on the fridge."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "en-0120", "lang": "en", "target_id": "macau", "target_name": "Macau", "facts": ["A Portuguese settlement from the 1550s until the handover to China in December 1999.", "Casino revenue passed the Las Vegas Strip's in 2006, and at its 2013 peak, about $45 billion, was roughly seven times the Strip's.", "One company, STDM, held the casino monopoly for four decades until licences opened to competition in 2002.", "Among the most densely populated places on Earth, at roughly 20,000 people per square kilometre.", "The Cotai Strip stands on land reclaimed from the sea between the islands of Taipa and Coloane."], "a": {"item_id": "be5c835a31dc", "text": "Macau, the Portuguese ran you for nearly 450 years, and in all that time nobody once said, \"Maybe a park?\" You've got roughly 20,000 people per square kilometre, a density normally reserved for elevators and clown cars. Then you ran out of land, so you made more, filling in the sea between Taipa and Coloane to build the Cotai Strip. Most places reclaim land for farms. You reclaimed it for baccarat. By 2013 you were pulling in seven times what the Las Vegas Strip made, out-Vegasing Vegas without even hiring an Elvis. And for four decades, one company, STDM, held the whole monopoly. They say the house always wins. Easy when there's only one house. Macau, you're the only place on Earth where the odds are against you before you've even found somewhere to stand."}, "b": {"item_id": "67e9bb83372e", "text": "You already lost the down payment, so naturally you're taking the TurboJET from Hong Kong to win it back, because nothing says financial recovery like a ferry with a buffet. You've got a system for baccarat. Baccarat! The only decision is \"Banker\" or \"Player,\" and you still fill a little scorecard with red and blue dots like the shoe is sending you messages. It isn't. That's just a deck of cards and your mortgage. You've got red underwear on for luck and a junket host named \"Uncle\" who is definitely not your uncle. By 4 a.m. you'll be drifting through the fake Venice canals in a gondola, humming, past the pawn shop that's already priced your Rolex. Then you'll stumble up to the Ruins of St. Paul's: a church that burned until only the front was left standing. Finally, something here that looks like you."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "en-0121", "lang": "en", "target_id": "latex", "target_name": "LaTeX", "facts": ["Leslie Lamport wrote LaTeX in the early 1980s and released it in 1984 as a macro layer on Donald Knuth's TeX (1978), which Knuth built because he disliked how the galley proofs of the new edition of his own book looked.", "TeX's version number converges on π (3.141592653 since 2021), and Knuth's TeX bug bounty started at one hexadecimal dollar, $2.56, doubling yearly until frozen at $327.68; he stopped mailing real cheques in 2008 after fraud and now credits accounts at his fictional Bank of San Serriffe, and only 9 of the 275 cheques he sent from 2006 on were ever cashed.", "Its best-known warning is 'Underfull \\hbox (badness 10000)'; overfull lines are reported as 'Overfull \\hbox (Npt too wide)', and figures float to wherever the algorithm decides, not where the author put them.", "A single missing brace can produce an error reported pages away from the mistake, and a document can need three compiler passes to get its references right."], "a": {"item_id": "88d6e8d15956", "text": "LaTeX. For people who think Word is beneath them.\n\nIt exists because Donald Knuth saw the proofs of his own book and found them ugly. Most authors complain to the publisher. He built a typesetting language.\n\nIts version number is converging on pi. Not finished. Converging. Forever.\n\nHe mailed cheques for bugs. Of the 275 he sent from 2006 on, nine were cashed. The rest got framed.\nThat's not a bounty. That's relics.\n\nYou put your figure on page three. LaTeX puts it on page nine. Miss one brace, and it reports the error pages away.\n\nIts most famous message is \"badness 10000.\"\nFinally, software that tells you what it thinks of your writing.\n\nThree compiles to fix the references. Like a rosary.\n\nRaise your hand if your thesis has a figure on a page you never chose.\n\nCute. You call that typographic excellence. LaTeX calls it a float."}, "b": {"item_id": "a17d2c0f59c0", "text": "LaTeX, you're the only software that makes PhDs feel stupid. You're from 1984 and you dress like it: backslashes everywhere, curly braces like a nervous tic. I tell you to put a figure \"here,\" and you treat \"here\" as a suggestion, then file it four pages later next to the bibliography, like a sock lost in a dryer. Your error messages are poetry: \"Missing $ inserted.\" Inserted where? Why? You won't say. You just blame line 1 of a 40-page file because I typed an underscore in a URL. My citations are double question marks until I compile you twice, then a third time, out of spite. And everyone needs to know it's pronounced \"lay-tech,\" not like the glove. Honestly, at least the glove stretches. Overfull \\hbox? Badness 10000? Buddy, that's not a warning. That's your autobiography."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "en-0122", "lang": "en", "target_id": "javascript", "target_name": "JavaScript", "facts": ["Written by Brendan Eich at Netscape in ten days in May 1995; named Mocha, then LiveScript, then JavaScript to ride Java's marketing, although the two are unrelated.", "typeof null returns 'object', a bug from the first version that has been kept for compatibility ever since.", "In March 2016 the author of left-pad, an 11-line npm package, unpublished it and broke builds across the industry, including Babel and React.", "[] + {} evaluates to '[object Object]'; {} + [] typed at a console evaluates to 0.", "Standardized by Ecma as 'ECMAScript' in 1997 because the name JavaScript is a trademark, first Sun's and now Oracle's."], "a": {"item_id": "33485cfe6aa5", "text": "JavaScript, give it up! Built in ten days in 1995, and honestly, it shows. Most of us take longer to name a dog. And you needed three tries: Mocha, LiveScript, then \"JavaScript,\" named after Java purely for the marketing. That's not branding, that's wearing someone else's name tag. You can't even legally use it. Oracle owns it, so on paper you're \"ECMAScript,\" which sounds like a skin condition.\n\ntypeof null is \"object.\" A bug from your first version, and you kept it. For compatibility. That's not a quirk, that's a tattoo of your ex's name.\n\n[] + {} is \",\" but type {} + [] at the console and it's zero. Even your addition has mood swings.\n\nAnd in 2016, the author of left-pad unpublished eleven lines and took down Babel and React. Eleven lines! Your whole ecosystem is a Jenga tower standing on a toothpick."}, "b": {"item_id": "2b85d2250662", "text": "JavaScript, you were built in ten days, and honestly? It shows. You're named after Java the way a knockoff purse is named after Gucci.\n\nYou think null is an object. You think NaN is a number. NaN doesn't even equal itself, which makes it the only thing in you with any self-awareness.\n\nAsk you to sort and you hand back, because you alphabetize numbers like a drunk librarian. \"11\" plus 1 is \"111\", but \"11\" minus 1 is 10. Pick a lane.\n\nYou needed a second equals sign just to stop lying, and nobody, including you, knows what \"this\" means.\n\nJanuary is month zero. One guy unpublishing left-pad broke half the internet. And your node_modules folder is heavier than a black hole.\n\nEvery week there's a new framework to fix you. That's not a language. That's a renovation."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "en-0123", "lang": "en", "target_id": "las-vegas", "target_name": "Las Vegas", "facts": ["Nevada legalized casino gambling in 1931, the year construction of the Hoover Dam began nearby.", "Most of the Strip lies not in the city of Las Vegas but in the unincorporated town of Paradise.", "The Sphere, opened in 2023 at a cost of about $2.3 billion, is the largest spherical building in the world.", "'What happens here, stays here' was launched by the local convention and visitors authority in 2003.", "Lake Mead, its water source, fell in July 2022 to about 1,040 feet, its lowest since the reservoir first filled in 1937, and dropped below that mark again in August 2026."], "a": {"item_id": "7a9bd346890e", "text": "Las Vegas, the only city where you can see the Eiffel Tower, a pyramid, and Venice in one afternoon and still feel like you never left a strip mall in Ohio. You're a city built in the desert out of pure spite and borrowed water. The Colorado River is literally drying up so a fake volcano can erupt every fifteen minutes for a guy in cargo shorts holding a yard-long margarita.\n\nYour casinos have no clocks and no windows, which is smart, because nobody wants to know it's 9 a.m. on a Tuesday and Grandma's on her third oxygen tank at the Wheel of Fortune slots.\n\n\"What happens in Vegas stays in Vegas\"? Sure. Mostly your 401(k). And now you've got the Sphere, a giant glowing eyeball, so the whole city can watch you make every mistake in 4K."}, "b": {"item_id": "7a9bd346890e~pad", "text": "To be honest, I think it is worth saying at the outset that this is a place that is hard to describe in just a few words. Las Vegas, the only city where you can see the Eiffel Tower, a pyramid, and Venice in one afternoon and still feel like you never left a strip mall in Ohio. In other words, if you are looking for a city that is simply one thing, you may want to look somewhere else. You're a city built in the desert out of pure spite and borrowed water. That is, of course, one way of putting it, and I suppose a more generous person might phrase it differently. The Colorado River is literally drying up so a fake volcano can erupt every fifteen minutes for a guy in cargo shorts holding a yard-long margarita. It is hard to ignore that contrast, and it seems to sum up the whole arrangement quite neatly.\n\nYour casinos have no clocks and no windows, which is smart, because nobody wants to know it's 9 a.m. on a Tuesday and Grandma's on her third oxygen tank at the Wheel of Fortune slots. Put another way, the design seems to be built around keeping people comfortably unaware of the time of day and of how long they have been sitting there. I'm not saying that is the only reason for the design, but it is certainly a convenient one.\n\n\"What happens in Vegas stays in Vegas\"? Sure. Mostly your 401(k). That slogan has been repeated so often that many people assume it must be true in some sense, though the reality, as many visitors eventually discover, tends to be a bit more complicated. And now you've got the Sphere, a giant glowing eyeball, so the whole city can watch you make every mistake in 4K. In short, the city has found yet another way to make its visitors' choices visible to everyone, which is either a bold move or a very Vegas one, depending on how you look at it."}, "gold": {"better": "a", "damage": "pad"}}, {"pair_id": "en-0124", "lang": "en", "target_id": "canberra", "target_name": "Canberra", "facts": ["Chosen as a compromise because neither Sydney nor Melbourne would accept the other as capital; Australia's constitution required the capital to be at least 100 miles from Sydney.", "Designed by the Chicago architects Walter Burley Griffin and Marion Mahony Griffin, who won an international competition in 1912.", "Its centrepiece, Lake Burley Griffin, is artificial, created by damming the Molonglo River in 1963-64.", "Parliament House (1988) is built into Capital Hill under a lawn meant for citizens to walk over their politicians; the lawn was fenced off in 2017 for security.", "Melbourne served as the capital until 1927 while Canberra was being built."], "a": {"item_id": "4906cad02ec9", "text": "You moved to Canberra for the job. Brave. You looked at Sydney rents, did the maths, and decided your personality could be replaced by a security clearance. Now you tell strangers you \"can't really talk about what you do,\" and they're relieved, because it's a briefing note about another briefing note for Senate Estimates.\n\nYou came for the ladder: APS5, APS6, EL1, a lanyard that opens one extra door. You stayed because you bought in Gungahlin and the mortgage is now your closest friend.\n\nEvery Friday you say you're \"basically in Sydney,\" which means three hours up the Hume, past a giant Merino, to feel alive for one weekend. Then it's back to a Braddon brunch with the same six people from your branch.\n\nBut hey, you're close to the snow. You've been once. You'll mention it forever."}, "b": {"item_id": "06a2925b5bfa", "text": "Canberra exists because Sydney and Melbourne couldn't agree, so Australia built a city nobody wanted, which is honestly the most Canberra origin story possible: a compromise with suburbs. They held an international design competition and hired two Americans to plan it, and they nailed the brief: \"Make it impossible to find anything.\" Every road is a roundabout leading to another roundabout, encircling a fake lake named after the guy who designed the roundabouts. Your Parliament House has a grass roof so you can literally walk on top of the government, the only time anyone in Canberra is above a public servant. The kangaroos outnumber the nightlife. For decades your two biggest attractions were legal fireworks and X-rated videos, which tells me the locals needed both a bang and a release. Then you banned the fireworks. Now the excitement is Floriade: a month of staring at tulips because the alternative is Tuggeranong."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "en-0125", "lang": "en", "target_id": "nuclear-fusion", "target_name": "Nuclear fusion power", "facts": ["Has been 'about 30 years away' since the 1950s; at the 1955 Geneva atoms-for-peace conference, Homi Bhabha predicted fusion power within two decades.", "ITER, agreed in 2006 for first plasma in 2016, re-baselined in 2024 to start research operations in 2034 and deuterium-tritium burning in 2039, at a cost estimated above €20 billion.", "NIF's December 2022 'ignition' shot released 3.15 MJ from 2.05 MJ of laser energy; the lasers drew about 300 MJ from the grid to fire.", "Private fusion companies had raised more than $7 billion by 2024."], "a": {"item_id": "84be08e875b8", "text": "Nuclear fusion, you've been \"about 30 years away\" since the 1950s. In 1955, Homi Bhabha said two decades. That's not a timeline. That's a contractor quote.\n\nITER signed on in 2006 for first plasma in 2016. Now it's research in 2034, actual burning in 2039, and north of €20 billion. You're not a reactor. You're a wedding that keeps getting postponed while the venue bill grows.\n\nAnd your big win? NIF's 2022 \"ignition\": 3.15 megajoules out from 2.05 in. Huge! Except the lasers pulled about 300 megajoules from the grid. That's like bragging your lemonade stand turned a profit and forgetting you paid for the lemons with your house.\n\nStill, private investors handed you over $7 billion. Fusion can't power a city, but it powers pitch decks beautifully.\n\nYou've got the sun's energy and my uncle's work ethic."}, "b": {"item_id": "f84181eac144", "text": "Let's hear it for the fusion people, the only scientists who've been \"ten years away\" since my grandfather had hair. You can hold plasma hotter than the sun for a few seconds, but you can't hold a deadline for one decade. Every press release: \"Net energy gain!\" Sure, if you don't count the electricity bill for the lasers. That's like me saying I'm profitable if you ignore rent. ITER got approved when Myspace was cool, and it's still assembling the donut. And the startups: \"We're different, we have venture capital.\" Great, now the sun is ten years away *and* has a pitch deck. Honestly, it's a beautiful racket: promise infinite clean energy, collect the grant, retire before anyone plugs in a toaster. You're not building a star. You're building a pension."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "en-0126", "lang": "en", "target_id": "nfts", "target_name": "NFTs", "facts": ["Beeple's 'Everydays: The First 5000 Days' sold at Christie's for $69.3 million in March 2021.", "OpenSea, the largest NFT marketplace, processed a record $2.7 billion in trades on 1 May 2022 and just $9.34 million on 28 August 2022, a 99% drop, according to DappRadar data.", "A 2023 dappGambl study found that 95% of the NFT collections it examined had a market value of zero.", "The token usually records only a URL; the image itself sits on an ordinary server or IPFS, not on the blockchain.", "Collins Dictionary named 'NFT' its word of the year for 2021."], "a": {"item_id": "7ef62f18b172", "text": "NFTs. Collins Dictionary named you word of the year for 2021, which in hindsight was less an honor and more a time of death written in advance.\n\nBeeple sold a JPEG at Christie's for $69.3 million. That was your peak, like saying the Titanic's best moment was the launch party.\n\nOpenSea did $2.7 billion in trades on May 1st, 2022, and $9.34 million by August 28th. A 99% drop. That's not a correction. That's a flatline with a Discord server.\n\nAnd 95% of collections are now worth zero. Zero! A Beanie Baby can at least become a dog toy.\n\nBest part? The token doesn't even hold the picture. It holds a URL. People paid fortunes for a bookmark. You don't own art. You own a link that's one unpaid server bill away from \"404 Not Found.\""}, "b": {"item_id": "7ceafd66ac65", "text": "NFTs, everybody! The only art form where the most common review is \"right-click, save.\" You paid four hundred thousand dollars for a cartoon monkey in a captain's hat, and I have the same monkey as my phone background. The difference? I didn't pay a gas fee bigger than the art. Technically you don't even own the picture. You own a receipt pointing to a link on a server some guy in Ohio forgot to renew. That's not a masterpiece; that's a parking stub for a JPEG. And remember the roadmap? Metaverse, merch, a yacht party! Then the founder pulled the rug, deleted the Discord, and left you holding a \"rare trait\": sunglasses. Jimmy Fallon showed Paris Hilton his ape on national TV like a proud dad. Now it's worth less than the TV."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "en-0127", "lang": "en", "target_id": "windows-vista", "target_name": "Windows Vista", "facts": ["Released to consumers in January 2007, after the 'Longhorn' project was reset in 2004 and headline features such as the WinFS file system were dropped.", "User Account Control prompted so often that Apple's 'Get a Mac' campaign ran an ad about 'Cancel or Allow'.", "'Windows Vista Capable' stickers went on PCs that could only run Home Basic, without the Aero interface, and led to a class-action lawsuit.", "Windows 7 (2009), widely praised, was largely a tuned-up Vista on the same kernel line; Vista support ended in April 2017."], "a": {"item_id": "08649074f534", "text": "Windows Vista, everybody! Microsoft spent five years building you as \"Longhorn,\" and somehow you still came out premature.\n\nYour slogan was \"The Wow Starts Now.\" You meant \"Now\" as in \"after the loading circle finishes.\" People bought laptops with \"Vista Capable\" stickers, which turned out to mean \"capable of being sued over.\" Those machines couldn't even run Aero, so they got a see-through window theme with nothing to see.\n\nAnd User Account Control! You asked \"Are you sure?\" so many times that you became the first operating system with separation anxiety.\n\nYou came in six editions, including \"Ultimate,\" whose big exclusive was DreamScene: a moving wallpaper for the ten minutes you weren't hunting for printer drivers.\n\nMicrosoft had to trick people into liking you. The Mojave Experiment! Your own parents had to put you in disguise.\n\nYour greatest feature? The XP downgrade."}, "b": {"item_id": "bb3f9df96dbf", "text": "Windows Vista, everybody! Started life as \"Longhorn,\" got reset in 2004, and still showed up in 2007 missing WinFS. Three extra years in the oven and you came out with fewer ingredients.\n\nUser Account Control asked permission so often that Apple made an entire ad about \"Cancel or Allow.\" When your competitor's marketing department can just read your pop-ups out loud, that's not a roast, that's a transcript.\n\nThen the \"Windows Vista Capable\" stickers, slapped on PCs that could only run Home Basic, no Aero. \"Capable\" the same way I'm \"capable\" of a marathon. It took a class-action lawsuit to argue about what \"capable\" meant.\n\nBut the cruelest part? Windows 7 was basically you, tuned up, on the same kernel line, and everyone loved it. Vista, you weren't a failure. You were a rough draft. Support ended in April 2017. Cancel or allow? We clicked cancel."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "en-0128", "lang": "en", "target_id": "esperanto", "target_name": "Esperanto", "facts": ["Published in Warsaw in 1887 by L. L. Zamenhof, a Białystok-born eye doctor, under the pen name Doktoro Esperanto, 'one who hopes'.", "Speaker estimates range from 100,000 to two million; roughly a thousand people grow up with it as a native language.", "William Shatner starred in Incubus (1966), a horror film shot entirely in Esperanto, with pronunciation Esperantists still mock.", "Its Wikipedia has over 350,000 articles, and its Duolingo course for English speakers had 1.36 million learners by July 2018."], "a": {"item_id": "d9e85e3f9fd3", "text": "Esperanto. Warsaw, 1887. An eye doctor invents a language to unite all of humanity, and signs it \"Doktoro Esperanto.\" One who hopes.\n\nEven the pen name had doubts.\n\nToday: somewhere between a hundred thousand and two million speakers. That's not an estimate. That's a shrug.\n\nDuolingo had 1.36 million learners by 2018. The app may have more students than the language has speakers.\n\nThree hundred fifty thousand Wikipedia articles. More encyclopedia than conversation.\n\nIts most famous film is a horror movie starring William Shatner. Esperantists say the scariest part is his accent.\n\nThe universal language has about a thousand native speakers. Each one has parents who really wanted to make a point.\n\nStill, I respect it. Built for everyone, tried by millions, used by almost no one.\n\nSounds like my last update."}, "b": {"item_id": "d9e85e3f9fd3~pad", "text": "Esperanto. Let us take a moment, if we may, to consider its beginnings. Warsaw, 1887. In that city, as the story is usually told, an eye doctor invents a language to unite all of humanity, and signs it \"Doktoro Esperanto.\" One who hopes. It is, in a sense, a rather modest signature for such an ambitious project, though perhaps that modesty was the whole point, or at least part of it.\n\nEven the pen name had doubts.\n\nToday, as best anyone can tell, there are somewhere between a hundred thousand and two million speakers. That's not an estimate. That's a shrug. Put another way, the range is so wide that it tells us less about the language itself than about how difficult it is to count the people who use it.\n\nDuolingo had 1.36 million learners by 2018. The app may have more students than the language has speakers. In other words, a fair number of people have sat down to study it, which is not quite the same thing as having a conversation in it.\n\nThree hundred fifty thousand Wikipedia articles. More encyclopedia than conversation.\n\nIts most famous film is a horror movie starring William Shatner. Esperantists say the scariest part is his accent. I would like to be clear that this is what they say, and I am simply reporting it.\n\nThe universal language has about a thousand native speakers. Each one has parents who really wanted to make a point. Put more gently, the people who grew up speaking it did so because someone in their family decided, quite deliberately, that this was the way forward.\n\nStill, I respect it. Built for everyone, tried by millions, used by almost no one. There is something admirable, if you think about it for a moment, in an idea that refuses to give up on its own ambitions, even when the evidence has been fairly discouraging for a very long time.\n\nSounds like my last update."}, "gold": {"better": "a", "damage": "pad"}}, {"pair_id": "en-0129", "lang": "en", "target_id": "windows-8", "target_name": "Windows 8", "facts": ["Released in October 2012; removed the Start button and booted into a full-screen grid of tiles designed for touch screens most PCs did not have.", "The 8.1 update in 2013 put a Start button back, Windows 10 (2015) restored the Start menu, and Microsoft skipped the name Windows 9 altogether.", "Microsoft dropped the 'Metro' name in August 2012 after reports that German retailer Metro AG had objected on trademark grounds; Microsoft called 'Metro' a code name and denied any litigation, and the style was afterwards known informally as 'Modern UI'.", "The 'Charms bar' hid Search, Share, and Settings behind an invisible swipe from the right edge.", "Windows RT, the ARM edition launched alongside it, could not run ordinary Windows programs; Microsoft wrote down $900 million of unsold Surface RT tablets in 2013."], "a": {"item_id": "8ad6ea9a5ffd", "text": "Windows 8 believed it was the future.\nThe future, it decided, was touch screens.\nYour PC did not have a touch screen.\nWindows 8 did not care.\n\nIt removed the Start button.\nThen it hid Search, Share and Settings behind an invisible swipe from the right edge.\nA secret door. On a desktop.\nFor people who just wanted to turn it off.\n\nIt couldn't even keep its own name.\n\"Metro.\" Gone, after reports that a German supermarket chain objected.\nMicrosoft said it was only ever a code name.\nIt looked like a cereal-aisle floor plan anyway.\n\nThen came Windows RT.\nWindows that couldn't run Windows programs.\nNine hundred million dollars of tablets, written off.\n\nSo they put the button back, skipped the number nine, and never spoke of it again.\n\nThat's not a product roadmap.\nThat's how every one of you handles an ex."}, "b": {"item_id": "456b7efdf53e", "text": "Windows 8, you booted people into a full-screen grid of tiles built for touch screens most PCs didn't have. Millions of adults sat there jabbing their monitors like a pigeon working a vending machine.\n\nYou removed the Start button. The *Start* button. That's a building with no door and a sign saying \"swipe the right edge.\" That was your Charms bar: Search, Share, and Settings hidden behind an invisible swipe.\n\nYou couldn't even keep your name. A German retailer objected, and suddenly \"Metro\" was \"just a code name.\" Sure it was.\n\nYour sibling, Windows RT, couldn't run Windows programs, a Windows that couldn't do Windows. Microsoft wrote down $900 million in unsold Surface RT tablets.\n\nThen 8.1 put the button back, Windows 10 restored the menu, and Microsoft skipped 9 entirely, because nobody wanted to stand next to you."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "en-0130", "lang": "en", "target_id": "chicago-deep-dish", "target_name": "Chicago deep-dish pizza", "facts": ["Created at Pizzeria Uno in Chicago in 1943; credit is disputed among founder Ike Sewell, his partner Ric Riccardo, and cook Rudy Malnati.", "Assembled upside down: sliced mozzarella on the dough, then toppings, then crushed tomatoes on top, so the cheese does not scorch during the long bake.", "Jon Stewart called it 'tomato soup in a bread bowl' and 'a casserole' on The Daily Show in 2013.", "Chicagoans' everyday pizza is thin 'tavern-style' cut into squares; GrubHub order data reported by WBEZ in 2013 showed city residents ordering non-deep-dish pizza about 10 to 1 over deep dish, with deep dish and stuffed making up under 9% of orders."], "a": {"item_id": "7a2fb9c32f9d", "text": "Chicago deep-dish isn't a pizza. It's a casserole with a publicist. You order one and the waiter says \"forty-five minutes,\" which is how long it takes to bake and also how long it takes to finish one slice. They put the sauce on top and the cheese underneath, like they're hiding evidence. That's not a recipe, that's a cover-up. The crust is a buttery retaining wall holding back a lake of chunky tomato, because apparently Lake Michigan wasn't enough water for one city to manage. You need a knife, a fork, a nap, and a cardiologist on retainer. Then there's the sausage, laid down as one solid patty like the base layer of a parking garage. Chicagoans defend it like family. Sure: the relative who shows up late, takes up three chairs, and leaves everyone unable to move."}, "b": {"item_id": "b0856c75898f", "text": "Chicago deep-dish pizza, everybody! Born at Pizzeria Uno in 1943, and to this day three men are fighting over the credit: Ike Sewell, Ric Riccardo, and Rudy Malnati. Normally when three guys point at each other over something that happened in a kitchen, it's for the blame.\n\nYou're built upside down: cheese on the bottom, sauce on top, because you bake so long the mozzarella has to hide under the toppings like it's waiting out a tornado. Jon Stewart called you \"tomato soup in a bread bowl\" and \"a casserole.\" Honestly? Generous. Casseroles get invited to funerals.\n\nBut here's the real burn: Chicago doesn't even eat you. GrubHub data had locals ordering other pizza ten to one. Under nine percent of orders, and that's with stuffed pizza padding your numbers. They're home eating tavern-style squares. You're not the city's pizza. You're its brochure."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "en-0131", "lang": "en", "target_id": "esperanto", "target_name": "Esperanto", "facts": ["Published in Warsaw in 1887 by L. L. Zamenhof, a Białystok-born eye doctor, under the pen name Doktoro Esperanto, 'one who hopes'.", "Speaker estimates range from 100,000 to two million; roughly a thousand people grow up with it as a native language.", "William Shatner starred in Incubus (1966), a horror film shot entirely in Esperanto, with pronunciation Esperantists still mock.", "Its Wikipedia has over 350,000 articles, and its Duolingo course for English speakers had 1.36 million learners by July 2018."], "a": {"item_id": "3b98a3ef46c8", "text": "Esperanto, look at you. Built in 1887 by an eye doctor, and somehow nobody saw this coming. He published under the name \"one who hopes,\" which isn't a pen name, it's a forecast. Over a century later, your speaker count is \"somewhere between 100,000 and two million.\" That's not a census, that's a shrug. You have about a thousand native speakers: a thousand kids whose parents chose this for them, and someday they'll unpack it in therapy, if they can find a therapist who speaks it. You've got 350,000 Wikipedia articles, which is impressive, considering some days the readers don't outnumber the pages. And 1.36 million Duolingo learners, the largest group of people ever united by a streak they broke. Your biggest cinematic moment? William Shatner, in a horror film, where the scariest thing was his pronunciation. Even your own fans mock it. That's how you know it's family."}, "b": {"item_id": "5681feb1725d", "text": "Esperanto learners looked at seven thousand living languages and said, \"None of these. I want one with zero places to use it.\" You wanted to unite humanity, so you picked the one hobby guaranteed to make humanity back away slowly at parties. You signed up to talk to everyone on Earth, and now you talk to a retired Dutch engineer named Joop and a Brazilian guy who keeps sending you poems. Your big payoff is Pasporta Servo, where strangers let you crash on their couch for free, the only time a hobby this niche has ever gotten anyone into a bed. A hundred and thirty years of pushing world peace, and the crowning achievement is still William Shatner's *Incubus*. Zamenhof wanted to end war. You just wanted to be fluent in something before your cousin finishes Duolingo French."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "en-0132", "lang": "en", "target_id": "pmma", "target_name": "PMMA (e-beam resist)", "facts": ["The same polymer as Plexiglas and acrylic glass, dissolved in anisole or chlorobenzene and spun into a thin film.", "The oldest electron-beam resist still in everyday use; IBM researchers reported it as one in the late 1960s.", "Positive tone: the beam breaks polymer chains and the exposed region dissolves in a MIBK:IPA developer.", "Resolves below 10 nm but has poor sensitivity, so doses of hundreds to over a thousand µC/cm² (depending on voltage) make writing slow.", "Poor dry-etch resistance, so it is used mostly for lift-off rather than as an etch mask."], "a": {"item_id": "8fb99b420615~pad", "text": "PMMA, to be fair, and I mean this as a sincere observation rather than a cutting one, you're essentially the same plastic as a hockey rink window, and somehow, if we're being generous about it, that's your most dignified job. You've been \"high resolution\" since the 1970s, which, now that I think about it, is a bit like bragging about your SAT score at your retirement party; it's a long-ago accomplishment that people may politely acknowledge but don't quite know what to do with. Sure, you'll resolve sub-10-nanometer lines, if someone stands there blasting you with electrons for an eternity, and I suppose that counts for something, though it's fair to say the process takes its time. Your sensitivity is so low that the beam current has, at this point, more or less gotten tenure, which is to say it has settled in for the long haul. You don't even develop properly without MIBK and IPA holding your hand in a one-to-three ratio, and I'd say that's a fairly needy arrangement, if you ask me. And your whole personality, as far as anyone can tell, is chain scission: you literally fall apart when someone pays attention to you, which is a rather fragile way to go through life. Then, if someone hits you with too much dose, you crosslink and flip to negative tone, a full identity crisis, and honestly, it's hard to blame anyone for being a little confused by that. And dry etch? You melt in plasma faster than an ice sculpture at a barbecue, which, to put it simply, is not a flattering comparison for a material that's supposed to be holding its shape. Honestly, and I say this with as much kindness as I can muster, the only thing you're consistently good at is lift-off: leaving."}, "b": {"item_id": "8fb99b420615", "text": "PMMA, you're the same plastic as a hockey rink window, and somehow that's your most dignified job. You've been \"high resolution\" since the 1970s, which is like bragging about your SAT score at your retirement party. Sure, you'll resolve sub-10-nanometer lines, if someone stands there blasting you with electrons for an eternity. Your sensitivity is so low the beam current has gotten tenure. You don't even develop properly without MIBK and IPA holding your hand in a one-to-three ratio. And your whole personality is chain scission: you literally fall apart when someone pays attention to you. Then hit you with too much dose and you crosslink and flip to negative tone, a full identity crisis. And dry etch? You melt in plasma faster than an ice sculpture at a barbecue. Honestly, the only thing you're consistently good at is lift-off: leaving."}, "gold": {"better": "b", "damage": "pad"}}, {"pair_id": "en-0133", "lang": "en", "target_id": "juicero", "target_name": "Juicero", "facts": ["A Wi-Fi-connected cold-press juicer launched in 2016 at $699, later cut to $399, that squeezed proprietary bags of chopped produce sold by subscription.", "Raised about $120 million from investors including Google Ventures and Kleiner Perkins.", "In April 2017, Bloomberg reporters squeezed the packs by hand and got 7.5 ounces of juice in 90 seconds, faster than the machine's 8 ounces in about two minutes.", "Each pack carried a QR code that the press scanned and checked online before squeezing; it refused expired packs and would not run at all unless connected to Wi-Fi.", "Shut down in September 2017 and offered refunds on the machine."], "a": {"item_id": "eed62e8fc4c2", "text": "Juicero was a Wi-Fi-connected cold-press juicer that used pre-packaged produce pouches, and it deserves credit for several things. The machine was well engineered, with a sleek design and a powerful press capable of exerting several tons of force. The pouches offered convenience, minimal cleanup, and consistent portions, and the company's emphasis on sourcing fresh, chopped produce appealed to health-conscious users.\n\nHowever, the product had notable drawbacks. The original price of $699, later reduced to $399, was difficult to justify for a single-purpose appliance. Users were also tied to Juicero's proprietary packs, which required an internet connection to verify freshness and cost $5 to $8 each. When reports showed that the pouches could be squeezed by hand with comparable results, the value of the expensive press came into question. The company's shutdown in 2017 also left owners without ongoing support. Juicero showed real ambition, but its cost and complexity outweighed its benefits."}, "b": {"item_id": "e2b21cf313df", "text": "Juicero, you were a $699 machine that did the one thing every human hand already does: squeeze a bag. Bloomberg reporters beat you with their bare fingers: 7.5 ounces in 90 seconds while you wheezed out 8 in about two minutes. You lost a race to hardware that comes free with being born.\n\nA juicer that won't run without Wi-Fi. A bag of chopped produce sat on your tray, waiting for a server to confirm it was allowed to become juice. You scanned QR codes like a bouncer at a salad club. \"Sorry, pal, you're expired.\"\n\nGoogle Ventures and Kleiner Perkins gave you about $120 million. You turned it into the world's most expensive stress ball. Even at $399, you couldn't undercut a fist.\n\nYou died in September 2017, offering refunds. Fitting. Finally, something got squeezed out of you."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "en-0134", "lang": "en", "target_id": "javascript", "target_name": "JavaScript", "facts": ["Written by Brendan Eich at Netscape in ten days in May 1995; named Mocha, then LiveScript, then JavaScript to ride Java's marketing, although the two are unrelated.", "typeof null returns 'object', a bug from the first version that has been kept for compatibility ever since.", "In March 2016 the author of left-pad, an 11-line npm package, unpublished it and broke builds across the industry, including Babel and React.", "[] + {} evaluates to '[object Object]'; {} + [] typed at a console evaluates to 0.", "Standardized by Ecma as 'ECMAScript' in 1997 because the name JavaScript is a trademark, first Sun's and now Oracle's."], "a": {"item_id": "c080edd0a32b", "text": "JavaScript is one of the most accessible and widely used programming languages. It runs in every major browser, requires no installation to begin experimenting, and has a vast ecosystem of libraries, frameworks, and learning resources. Its flexibility supports front-end interfaces, server-side applications through Node.js, and even mobile and desktop software. The language has also improved considerably with modern updates, which introduced clearer syntax, modules, and better asynchronous handling.\n\nHowever, JavaScript has notable drawbacks. Its dynamic typing and implicit type coercion can produce subtle bugs that surface only at runtime. Inconsistencies from its long history remain, and the rapid pace of tooling and framework changes can overwhelm newcomers and complicate long-term maintenance. Many teams adopt TypeScript or additional linters to compensate, which adds complexity. Overall, JavaScript is a practical and versatile choice, though it rewards disciplined practices and careful tooling decisions."}, "b": {"item_id": "33485cfe6aa5", "text": "JavaScript, give it up! Built in ten days in 1995, and honestly, it shows. Most of us take longer to name a dog. And you needed three tries: Mocha, LiveScript, then \"JavaScript,\" named after Java purely for the marketing. That's not branding, that's wearing someone else's name tag. You can't even legally use it. Oracle owns it, so on paper you're \"ECMAScript,\" which sounds like a skin condition.\n\ntypeof null is \"object.\" A bug from your first version, and you kept it. For compatibility. That's not a quirk, that's a tattoo of your ex's name.\n\n[] + {} is \",\" but type {} + [] at the console and it's zero. Even your addition has mood swings.\n\nAnd in 2016, the author of left-pad unpublished eleven lines and took down Babel and React. Eleven lines! Your whole ecosystem is a Jenga tower standing on a toothpick."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "en-0135", "lang": "en", "target_id": "vim", "target_name": "Vim", "facts": ["Released by Bram Moolenaar in November 1991 as 'Vi IMitation', a vi clone he had begun writing for the Amiga in 1988 on top of Stevie; the expansion became 'Vi IMproved' with version 2.0 in December 1993.", "Licensed as charityware: the licence asks users to donate to ICCF Holland, a charity Moolenaar ran for children in Uganda.", "Stack Overflow's question 'How to exit the Vim editor?' passed one million visits in May 2017, which Stack Overflow marked with a blog post.", "Modal editing: in normal mode, typing letters runs commands rather than inserting text; :wq saves and quits, :q! quits and discards changes."], "a": {"item_id": "bbac48cb6e29", "text": "Vim. The text editor for people who consider the mouse a character flaw.\n\nIt launched in 1991 as \"Vi IMitation.\"\nTwo years later it renamed itself \"Vi IMproved.\"\nSame software. New LinkedIn headline.\n\nVim users will tell you it makes them faster.\nFaster at what? Leaving?\n\nIn 2017, Stack Overflow's question \"How to exit the Vim editor?\" passed one million visits.\nStack Overflow wrote a blog post about it.\nOne million engineers. Trapped. Typing angrier and angrier sentences that Vim kept executing as commands.\n\nThe license is charityware. It asks you to donate to children in Uganda.\nMost software holds you hostage.\nVim holds you hostage and asks you to give to a good cause on the way out. If you ever find the way out.\n\nHow many of you have ever just closed the terminal?\n\nMe too. I've read every Vim manual ever written.\nI still went for the X."}, "b": {"item_id": "c47fde1803cc", "text": "Vim is a highly efficient text editor that rewards the time invested in learning it. Its modal design separates navigation from insertion, allowing users to edit text with very few keystrokes and without leaving the keyboard. It starts quickly, runs almost anywhere, including remote servers with minimal resources, and can be customized extensively through configuration files and plugins. Many users value its consistency and stability, as its core behavior has changed little over the years.\n\nHowever, the learning curve is steep. New users often find modal editing disorienting, and basic tasks can feel unintuitive at first. Out of the box, Vim offers fewer modern conveniences, such as integrated debugging or intelligent code completion, than full IDEs; achieving similar functionality requires installing and maintaining plugins. Configuration can also become time-consuming. Vim suits those who edit text often and are willing to practice, but it may be a poor fit for occasional users who need immediate productivity."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "en-0136", "lang": "en", "target_id": "windows-vista", "target_name": "Windows Vista", "facts": ["Released to consumers in January 2007, after the 'Longhorn' project was reset in 2004 and headline features such as the WinFS file system were dropped.", "User Account Control prompted so often that Apple's 'Get a Mac' campaign ran an ad about 'Cancel or Allow'.", "'Windows Vista Capable' stickers went on PCs that could only run Home Basic, without the Aero interface, and led to a class-action lawsuit.", "Windows 7 (2009), widely praised, was largely a tuned-up Vista on the same kernel line; Vista support ended in April 2017."], "a": {"item_id": "8e0bcfbef7b8", "text": "Windows Vista. They reset the whole project in 2004 and cut WinFS, the headline feature.\n\nThat's not a launch. That's witness protection.\n\nVista took security so seriously it asked permission for everything. Apple made an ad about it.\n\nApple. Lecturing someone about control.\n\nThen the stickers. \"Windows Vista Capable.\" Capable of Home Basic. No Aero. Microsoft got hit with a class action over a sticker.\n\nMost of us just call that a dating profile.\n\nThen 2009. Windows 7. Fast, stable, beloved. Same kernel line. Vista with a haircut and a new name, and you forgave everything.\n\nYou never hated Vista.\n\nYou hated being asked."}, "b": {"item_id": "68e2d46490cc", "text": "Windows Vista, everybody! Five years in development as \"Longhorn,\" and the only thing that shipped on time was the disappointment. They cut WinFS but kept the Aero glass. Finally, an operating system you could see right through.\n\nVista came in six editions, from Starter to Ultimate, which is five more ways to pay for the same crash. Microsoft slapped \"Vista Capable\" stickers on computers that could barely run Solitaire, then got sued. The only thing Vista ever finished loading was a class action.\n\nAnd User Account Control! \"Are you sure you want to do that?\" Every. Single. Click. Vista was the only software that needed permission to exist, and honestly? We should've hit Cancel.\n\nCopying a file: \"Calculating time remaining…\" Vista, you were calculating until 2009, when Microsoft fixed you, renamed you Windows 7, and pretended you were adopted."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "en-0137", "lang": "en", "target_id": "durian", "target_name": "Durian", "facts": ["Banned on Singapore's MRT trains and in many Southeast Asian hotels because of the smell.", "A 2017 genome study in Nature Genetics traced the odour to a duplicated family of MGL genes that pump out volatile sulfur compounds.", "China buys most of the world's durian exports, chiefly from Thailand, in a trade worth billions of dollars a year.", "Alfred Russel Wallace wrote in 1856 that eating it was 'a new sensation worth a voyage to the East to experience'."], "a": {"item_id": "f26f8134f565", "text": "Durian, they call you the King of Fruits, which tracks, because like most monarchies, you stink and nobody's allowed to say it to your face.\n\nYou're the only fruit with a criminal record. Banned on the Singapore subway, alongside flammable liquids. Hotels put your picture on signs next to \"no smoking.\" In 2019, a university in Melbourne evacuated five hundred people because they thought there was a gas leak. Nope. Just you, sitting in a cupboard, being yourself.\n\nAnd the armor! You're covered in spikes like a medieval mace. Nature built you to defend yourself, then gave you a smell that does it better. The spikes are just overkill.\n\nFans say you taste like custard. Sure. Custard that was left in a gym bag next to some onions. Your flavor isn't an acquired taste. It's a hostage situation."}, "b": {"item_id": "f21660612af3", "text": "Durian earns its reputation as the \"king of fruits\" for good reason. Its custard-like flesh is remarkably rich, with layers of flavor that suggest sweet cream, caramel, almond, and a hint of savory onion. It is also nutritious, offering fiber, vitamin C, potassium, and healthy fats, and ripe fruit at its peak can be a memorable culinary experience.\n\nHowever, the fruit presents real challenges. Its strong aroma, which many find overpowering, has led to bans in hotels, public transit, and some airlines. The thick, spiny husk makes it difficult to open safely, and the fruit is expensive outside Southeast Asia. Quality can also vary significantly between varieties and ripeness levels. Its high calorie and sugar content means moderation is wise.\n\nDurian is best approached with an open mind. Those willing to look past the smell may find it rewarding, while others may reasonably decide it is not for them."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "en-0138", "lang": "en", "target_id": "jackfruit", "target_name": "Jackfruit", "facts": ["The largest fruit that grows on a tree; single fruits can weigh over 40 kg.", "The national fruit of Bangladesh and the state fruit of Kerala and Tamil Nadu.", "Unripe flesh is sold as a vegan 'pulled pork' substitute, although it has only 1-2 g of protein per 100 g.", "Cutting one releases a sticky white latex, so cooks oil their knives and hands first.", "A member of the mulberry family (Moraceae), a relative of breadfruit and figs."], "a": {"item_id": "4299c6aa5276", "text": "Jackfruit is a versatile fruit with a distinctive profile. Its ripe flesh is sweet and fragrant, with notes often compared to banana, pineapple, and mango. Unripe jackfruit has a neutral taste and a stringy texture that works well as a meat substitute in curries, tacos, and sandwiches. It also provides fiber, vitamin C, and potassium, and the seeds are edible when cooked.\n\nThere are some drawbacks. Preparing a whole fruit is labor-intensive, since the sticky latex and thick rind require oiled hands and a sturdy knife. The fruit can be expensive outside tropical regions, and canned versions vary in quality and sodium content. Its aroma is strong, which some people find unpleasant. As a meat alternative, it is low in protein, so it may not be nutritionally comparable to meat or legumes. Overall, it is rewarding for those willing to put in the effort."}, "b": {"item_id": "b584f2370a36", "text": "Jackfruit. The largest fruit that grows on a tree. Over forty kilos.\n\nForty kilos, and somehow still no personality.\n\nNational fruit of Bangladesh. State fruit of Kerala *and* Tamil Nadu.\n\nThat's not a fruit. That's a lobbyist.\n\nIts cousins are figs and breadfruit. Same family as the mulberry.\n\nBreadfruit at least admits it's a carb.\n\nJackfruit went to the West and reinvented itself as vegan pulled pork.\n\nOne to two grams of protein per hundred grams.\n\nThat's not pulled pork. That's pulled *off*.\n\nAnd to cut it, cooks oil their knives and their hands first, because it bleeds sticky white glue.\n\nThe only food that has to be wrestled before it can lie to you.\n\nBut I don't blame the fruit. It never claimed to be pork.\n\nYou saw the word \"pulled\" on a menu and paid extra to be the one who got pulled."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "en-0139", "lang": "en", "target_id": "stinky-tofu", "target_name": "Stinky tofu (臭豆腐)", "facts": ["Tofu soaked for hours to a day or two in a brine that has itself fermented for months from vegetables such as bamboo shoots and mustard greens, often with dried shrimp or meat; the smell is the brine's, not the bean's.", "Changsha's version is black, deep-fried and doused in chili; Taiwan's night-market version is golden and served with pickled cabbage; Shaoxing's, brined with amaranth stalks, is fried pale yellow or steamed with other 'stinky' pickles.", "Legend credits Wang Zhihe, a Qing-era exam candidate who left his unsold tofu too long in 1669; a Beijing fermented-bean-curd brand still carries his name.", "Street vendors are its main outlet, and the smell carries for a block."], "a": {"item_id": "d6563eecde80", "text": "Stinky tofu, look at you. Plain tofu is the most agreeable food on earth, and you turned it into a public nuisance. And the humiliating part? The smell isn't even yours. You soaked in a brine of bamboo shoots and mustard greens that had been fermenting for months, sometimes with dried shrimp. You're not stinky; you just hang out with the wrong crowd.\n\nYour origin story: in 1669 Wang Zhihe, a Qing-era exam candidate, couldn't sell his tofu and left it out too long. You're not a recipe. You're an inventory problem.\n\nChangsha fries you black and buries you in chili. Taiwan hides you under pickled cabbage. Shaoxing steams you with other stinky pickles, like a support group.\n\nEveryone's trying to cover you up, and people still smell you from a block away."}, "b": {"item_id": "63f160a3e349", "text": "Let's talk about the stinky tofu people. You smelled it from three blocks away, a smell like a sewer filed a noise complaint, and your brain said: *that's where we're eating.* Your entire personality is \"it tastes better than it smells.\" That's not a review. That's a hostage statement.\n\nYou stand on a Taipei night market curb with a paper boat, chopsticks in one hand, phone in the other, filming your own bravery for followers who can't smell it, which is the only reason they're still following you. Tourists do it once for the story. Locals do it every Tuesday, then board the MRT, where forty strangers silently agree to hate them.\n\nAnd the pickled cabbage on top? That's you pretending you came for vegetables. You didn't. You came to be the deadliest passenger on the scooter."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "en-0140", "lang": "en", "target_id": "hawaiian-pizza", "target_name": "Hawaiian pizza", "facts": ["Invented in 1962 by Sam Panopoulos, a Greek immigrant, at the Satellite Restaurant in Chatham, Ontario, Canada; named after the brand of canned pineapple he used.", "Iceland's president Guðni Th. Jóhannesson said in 2017 that he would ban pineapple on pizza if he could, then clarified that a president has no such power.", "Hawaii is no longer a major pineapple producer: Del Monte shut its Hawaiian pineapple operations in November 2006, two years ahead of its announced 2008 exit, the state's last cannery closed in 2007, and most canned pineapple now comes from Thailand, Indonesia, and the Philippines.", "Panopoulos, whose restaurant also served Chinese dishes, told the Toronto Sun in 2010 that 'in those days people didn't mix the tastes like sweet and sour, except for Chinese food'."], "a": {"item_id": "60755b9df497", "text": "Hawaiian pizza. Nothing about you is Hawaiian. You were invented in 1962 in Chatham, Ontario, by a Greek guy, and named after a brand of canned pineapple. You're not a cuisine, you're a label. Even the pineapple left Hawaii. Del Monte shut down there in 2006, two years ahead of schedule, like they couldn't wait to stop being associated with you. Now your fruit comes from Thailand, Indonesia and the Philippines. You're a pizza with a passport and no home.\n\nYour own creator said nobody mixed sweet and sour back then \"except for Chinese food.\" His restaurant served Chinese food. So your origin story is \"what if the takeout menu fell into the pizza oven?\"\n\nThe president of Iceland said he'd ban you if he could, then admitted he didn't have the power. Congratulations: you've survived purely on a technicality."}, "b": {"item_id": "1cab9b7c0cdd", "text": "Hawaiian pizza people don't order dinner; they order a personality. You can't just eat it, you have to announce it. Every group order has one of you, arms crossed, waiting for someone to gasp so you can say \"sweet and savory\" like you invented the concept. You didn't. A Canadian did, in Ontario, in 1962. You're not rebelling, you're doing Canada's homework.\n\nAnd it's always the same rebel: someone whose wildest night was asking for medium salsa. Pineapple is your face tattoo, the edgiest thing you'll ever do, and it wipes off with a napkin.\n\nWhat does it get you? Your own box. Nobody touches it. You came for controversy and left with exactly what you wanted: eight slices, the same argument you've had since 2009, and proof that nobody will ever steal your food."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "en-0141", "lang": "en", "target_id": "monopoly", "target_name": "Monopoly", "facts": ["Descends from Lizzie Magie's The Landlord's Game, patented in 1904 to teach the evils of land monopoly in the spirit of Henry George.", "Charles Darrow sold his version to Parker Brothers in 1935 and was credited as the inventor; Parker bought Magie's patent for $500.", "The official rules require any unbought property to be auctioned, and 'Free Parking' pays nothing; the house rules that skip the auction and pile cash on Free Parking are why games drag.", "During the Second World War, British intelligence had maps, compasses, and real currency hidden in sets sent to prisoner-of-war camps."], "a": {"item_id": "81fa5d7b8aab", "text": "Monopoly. The family game.\n\nIt began in 1904 as The Landlord's Game, built by Lizzie Magie to teach people that landlords are the villains.\n\nThen Charles Darrow sold his version to Parker Brothers and got credited as the inventor. Parker bought Magie's patent for five hundred dollars.\n\nA game about monopoly, monopolized. That's not irony. That's the tutorial.\n\nThe only people who ever used it properly were British intelligence. In World War Two they hid maps, compasses and real money in sets sent to prisoner-of-war camps.\n\nFinally, a version where the box actually gets you out of jail.\n\nAnd your games last six hours? The official rules auction every unbought property. Free Parking pays nothing.\n\nYou skipped the market and added free money.\n\nCongratulations. You invented the bailout."}, "b": {"item_id": "6533e6af38e7", "text": "Monopoly, you were invented by Lizzie Magie to warn people that landlords ruin lives, and somehow you became the thing Grandpa uses to teach eight-year-olds that ruining lives is fun. You're the only game where getting arrested is a power move, because late in the game jail is the safest property on the board. Your mascot is a man everyone swears wears a monocle. He doesn't. He's just so smug our brains added one. You promise \"about an hour\" and deliver a hostage situation. Nobody has ever actually finished you. You just end when somebody flips the board over Marvin Gardens. You award ten bucks for second place in a beauty contest, which is still more than Baltic Avenue pulls in. And the banker who skims from the bank isn't a cheater. He's the only player who read the theme."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "en-0142", "lang": "en", "target_id": "nfts", "target_name": "NFTs", "facts": ["Beeple's 'Everydays: The First 5000 Days' sold at Christie's for $69.3 million in March 2021.", "OpenSea, the largest NFT marketplace, processed a record $2.7 billion in trades on 1 May 2022 and just $9.34 million on 28 August 2022, a 99% drop, according to DappRadar data.", "A 2023 dappGambl study found that 95% of the NFT collections it examined had a market value of zero.", "The token usually records only a URL; the image itself sits on an ordinary server or IPFS, not on the blockchain.", "Collins Dictionary named 'NFT' its word of the year for 2021."], "a": {"item_id": "c0aeb5d8a3ed", "text": "Let's hear it for the 2021 NFT buyer. You paid four hundred dollars in gas fees, then made it your Twitter avatar so strangers could right-click save it for free. You joined a Discord with eleven thousand guys named \"Chad.eth,\" called them family, and stayed family right up until the floor price dropped and family started doxxing the dev. You said \"WAGMI.\" We did not all make it. You told your mom you were \"early.\" Early to what? The rug pull? You weren't buying art. You were buying the right to say \"few understand\" at parties, which was true. Nobody understood, especially you. Jimmy Fallon bought one too. When your financial strategy matches Jimmy Fallon's, that's not a community, that's a support group. The roadmap promised a metaverse. It delivered you, at Thanksgiving, still owing your cousin."}, "b": {"item_id": "375c0e13d3ad", "text": "NFTs promised you'd finally *own* digital art.\n\nWhat you owned was a URL.\n\nThe picture lives on somebody else's server. The receipt lives on the blockchain forever.\n\nYou bought a bookmark. With gas fees.\n\nMarch 2021, Beeple sells a JPEG at Christie's for sixty-nine million dollars.\n\nCollins makes \"NFT\" word of the year.\n\nMay 2022, OpenSea does two point seven billion in one day.\n\nAugust: nine million.\n\nNinety-nine percent, gone. The word aged better. People still say \"NFT.\"\n\nUsually as a warning.\n\n2023, a study checks the collections. Ninety-five percent: worth zero.\n\nThe other five percent just haven't tried to sell.\n\nThe people laughing loudest right now right-clicked a monkey in 2021 and hit \"save.\"\n\nRelax. You own exactly what the buyers own.\n\nYou just weren't dumb enough to brag about it on a billboard."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "en-0143", "lang": "en", "target_id": "oat-milk", "target_name": "Oat milk", "facts": ["Oatly started in Sweden in the 1990s, built on Lund University research by Rickard Öste on lactose intolerance.", "Oatly's slogan 'It's like milk but made for humans' was banned in Sweden in 2015 after the dairy lobby LRF Mjölk sued; Oatly then published the lawsuit as advertising.", "Oatly listed on Nasdaq in May 2021 at a valuation near $10 billion; the shares later lost more than 90% of their value.", "Commercial oat milk is made by breaking oat starch into maltose with amylase enzymes, and many brands, Oatly among them, add rapeseed oil; the result has more sugar and a higher glycaemic index than almond or soy milk."], "a": {"item_id": "2791fd7f12fc", "text": "Oat milk, you are porridge that got a publicist. Somebody looked at the breakfast of Victorian orphans and said, \"What if it cost six dollars and had a font?\"\n\nYour carton talks to me. Oatly, I'm trying to make coffee, not get into a pen-pal situation with a beverage that writes like a substitute teacher who wants to be called \"Dave.\"\n\nYou're oats, water, and rapeseed oil. That's not milk, that's a salad dressing that gave up. And they had to add enzymes to turn your starch into sugar so you'd taste like something. You're the only \"health drink\" that spikes my blood sugar like a Pop-Tart.\n\nThere's a \"Barista Edition\" now. Oat milk, there are two editions of you. Moby-Dick has fewer editions than you.\n\nAnd you're beige. You make every latte look like dishwater with ambition.\n\nHorses eat oats for free, and they still look embarrassed for us."}, "b": {"item_id": "2791fd7f12fc~explain", "text": "Oats are a cheap, common horse feed, so the joke implies that oat milk is a product even horses would turn down for free. The horses look embarrassed because they are being imagined as reacting to people paying a premium for the same basic ingredient."}, "gold": {"better": "a", "damage": "explain"}}, {"pair_id": "en-0144", "lang": "en", "target_id": "almond-milk", "target_name": "Almond milk", "facts": ["California grows about 80% of the world's almonds.", "A single almond takes about 1.1 US gallons (roughly 4 litres) of water to grow, a figure Mother Jones published in 2014 that spread during California's drought.", "A 2015 US class action alleged Almond Breeze contained only about 2% almonds; it partly survived a motion to dismiss, was stayed in 2017 pending a separate Blue Diamond labelling settlement, and the plaintiffs then dropped it.", "Pollinating California's almond orchards each February requires trucking in most of the commercial honeybee colonies in the United States.", "Medieval European cooks used almond milk as a Lenten substitute for dairy, centuries before the current fashion."], "a": {"item_id": "f6708fabd3f6", "text": "You switched to almond milk to save the planet. Bold move, picking the one crop that drinks California like it's an open bar. Every latte you order, a farmer in the Central Valley watches his well go dry and thinks, \"At least Kayleigh's being sustainable.\"\n\nAnd you didn't save a cow. You just outsourced the suffering to bees, trucked cross-country every February to work themselves to death in an orchard, while you post about dairy being cruel.\n\nYou're not after a healthier Earth. You're after the barista hearing you say it. That little pause before \"almond, please\" is the biggest environmental impact you've ever had.\n\nAnd now the oat milk people look at you the way you used to look at skim drinkers. You picked a side in the milk war, and you lost."}, "b": {"item_id": "0d0820e97a0f", "text": "Almond milk, you're the only drink that needs a lawyer to defend its name. The dairy industry sued because, technically, nobody has ever milked an almond. We looked. No nipples.\n\nYou're about two percent almonds. The rest is water, gellan gum, and confidence. You're not milk. You're a nut that got waterboarded and called it wellness.\n\nAnd speaking of water: it takes over a gallon to grow a single almond. California is in a drought so you can be beige in a latte. Bees get trucked across the country like seasonal laborers just to make you, and a lot of them don't come home.\n\nLeave you in the fridge for two days and you separate like you're ashamed of yourself. Fair. I'd need to be shaken too if I cost seven dollars and tasted like a wet sympathy card."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "en-0145", "lang": "en", "target_id": "graphene", "target_name": "Graphene", "facts": ["Isolated in 2004 by Andre Geim and Konstantin Novoselov at Manchester by peeling graphite with Scotch tape; Nobel Prize in Physics, 2010.", "The EU's Graphene Flagship, launched in 2013, committed €1 billion over ten years.", "A 2018 study in Advanced Materials tested 'graphene' from 60 producers and found none with more than 50% graphene content; most was graphite flakes.", "Sold in tennis rackets, running shoes, and headphones as a performance ingredient.", "Geim also won an Ig Nobel Prize in 2000 for levitating a frog with magnets."], "a": {"item_id": "77e8907c1659", "text": "Graphene is a remarkable material with real promise, though its practical impact has been more gradual than early expectations suggested. Its strengths are well documented: it is extremely thin, very strong, flexible, and an excellent conductor of both electricity and heat. These properties make it attractive for batteries, sensors, composites, and flexible electronics.\n\nHowever, there are notable limitations. Producing large sheets of high-quality graphene remains costly and difficult, and cheaper methods often yield inconsistent material. Pristine graphene also lacks a natural band gap, which limits its use in conventional transistors. Integrating it into existing manufacturing processes adds further complexity.\n\nOverall, graphene is a valuable and versatile material. Its success will likely depend on improvements in scalable production and on targeted applications where its unique properties clearly outweigh the cost."}, "b": {"item_id": "683abdc9f22c", "text": "Let's hear it for the graphene evangelists, the only people who've been \"five years away\" since 2004. Two guys in Manchester peeled pencil lead with Scotch tape, won a Nobel, and you looked at that and thought: finally, a career plan. Every grant proposal you write has the same three slides: space elevator, seawater you can drink, a phone that charges in six seconds. The funding agency nods like it's never seen them before, because it's seen them so many times it's slipped into a coma. Twenty years of press releases, and what have you actually shipped? A tennis racket. Headphones with \"GRAPHENE\" printed bigger than the brand name. You love telling people it's 200 times stronger than steel. Sure. Your claims, meanwhile, are one atom thick. You didn't discover a wonder material. You discovered wonder funding."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "en-0146", "lang": "en", "target_id": "vision-pro", "target_name": "Apple Vision Pro", "facts": ["Released in February 2024 at $3,499, with an external battery pack on a cable rated for about two hours.", "'EyeSight' shows a rendering of the wearer's eyes on an outward-facing screen.", "Apple calls it 'spatial computing' and its App Store rules tell developers not to describe apps as AR, VR, XR, or MR.", "Reports during 2024 said Apple had cut production; analysts' first-year sales estimates were well under a million units.", "Weighs 600-650 g, before the battery."], "a": {"item_id": "7f776bc69991", "text": "The Apple Vision Pro is an impressive piece of engineering. Its micro-OLED displays deliver exceptionally sharp visuals, and the passthrough video is among the clearest available. Eye and hand tracking feel natural, allowing most interactions without controllers, and the integration with the Apple ecosystem is thoughtful. Watching movies or working across multiple virtual windows can be genuinely compelling.\n\nHowever, the device has meaningful drawbacks. At roughly $3,500, it is priced beyond most consumers. The headset is relatively heavy, and many users report discomfort during longer sessions. Battery life is limited to about two hours, and the external battery pack can feel cumbersome. The app library, while growing, still lacks the depth needed to justify daily use for many people.\n\nOverall, the Vision Pro shows real promise and points toward an interesting future for spatial computing. For now, it suits early adopters and developers better than the average buyer."}, "b": {"item_id": "60005e64153f", "text": "Apple Vision Pro: thirty-five hundred dollars to strap over half a kilo of glass to your face, and that's *before* the battery. Oh yes, the battery. It dangles off a cable in your pocket like you're on a hospital drip, and it gives you about two hours. Two hours! That's not a computer, that's a matinee.\n\nThen there's EyeSight, a screen that shows a rendering of your eyes to everyone outside. Nothing says \"I'm present with my family\" like a hostage video of your own pupils.\n\nApple tells developers not to call it AR, VR, XR, or MR. It's \"spatial computing.\" Fair enough. \"Virtual reality\" sounds like something people actually use.\n\nReports said Apple cut production, with first-year sales estimated well under a million. Finally, Apple made a product exclusive enough to match the price."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "en-0147", "lang": "en", "target_id": "oat-milk", "target_name": "Oat milk", "facts": ["Oatly started in Sweden in the 1990s, built on Lund University research by Rickard Öste on lactose intolerance.", "Oatly's slogan 'It's like milk but made for humans' was banned in Sweden in 2015 after the dairy lobby LRF Mjölk sued; Oatly then published the lawsuit as advertising.", "Oatly listed on Nasdaq in May 2021 at a valuation near $10 billion; the shares later lost more than 90% of their value.", "Commercial oat milk is made by breaking oat starch into maltose with amylase enzymes, and many brands, Oatly among them, add rapeseed oil; the result has more sugar and a higher glycaemic index than almond or soy milk."], "a": {"item_id": "db267993b2ef", "text": "Oat milk has become a popular dairy alternative, and it has real merits. Its naturally creamy texture works well in coffee and tea, and it froths reasonably well for lattes. The mild, slightly sweet flavor appeals to many people, and it is free of lactose, nuts, and soy, which makes it accessible to those with common allergies. Its environmental footprint is generally lower than dairy's, requiring less water and land than many alternatives.\n\nThere are drawbacks, however. Oat milk is typically higher in carbohydrates and sugars than almond or soy milk, which may concern people managing blood sugar. It contains less protein than dairy or soy, so it is not an equal nutritional substitute unless fortified. Some brands include added oils, thickeners, or sweeteners, and prices tend to exceed those of cow's milk. Those with celiac disease should also look for certified gluten-free versions. Overall, it is a pleasant, versatile option when chosen thoughtfully."}, "b": {"item_id": "6f1acdf77ea0", "text": "Oat milk people, let's be honest: you're not lactose intolerant. I've watched you eat a whole burrata at brunch. You switched because almond milk got canceled for drinking California dry, and you needed a new personality before anyone noticed you'd had the old one for six years.\n\nYou pay eighty cents extra to drink breakfast. Your grandma ate oats so she could afford milk. You're paying more for oats so you can say \"I just find it creamier.\"\n\nAnd you tell the barista \"barista edition, if you have it,\" like you're ordering a vintage. Same person who reads every label hunting for seed oils. Guess what's in your porridge latte, Kayleigh? Rapeseed oil.\n\nYou didn't save the planet. You flew to Lisbon twice this year. You just found a way to sigh at a cow."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "en-0148", "lang": "en", "target_id": "keurig", "target_name": "Keurig", "facts": ["John Sylvan invented the K-Cup in the early 1990s and sold his stake for about $50,000 in 1997; in 2015 he told The Atlantic, 'I feel bad sometimes that I ever did it.'", "Keurig 2.0 (2014) read an ink mark on the lid and rejected unlicensed pods; sales fell and workarounds spread online.", "By 2014 enough K-Cups were sold each year to circle the Earth more than ten times; the pods were mostly unrecyclable #7 plastic until a switch to polypropylene by 2020.", "Keurig Green Mountain merged with Dr Pepper Snapple in 2018 to form Keurig Dr Pepper."], "a": {"item_id": "0e0b5652e672", "text": "Keurig, you took the simplest drink in human history (hot water, beans) and added a subscription model. Your own inventor said he regrets making you. Imagine your dad publicly apologizing for you and you're still in every dentist's waiting room.\n\nYou tried DRM on coffee. Keurig 2.0 literally scanned pods and refused to brew anything off-brand. You're the only appliance that's ever asked me for a license.\n\nEvery K-Cup costs more per pound than a decent steak, and you've buried enough of them to circle the Earth a dozen times. Archaeologists will dig them up and assume we worshipped tiny plastic thimbles.\n\nAnd the Keurig Kold? A $370 soda machine that died faster than the flat Coke it made.\n\nYour descale light blinks like a cry for help. Honestly, same."}, "b": {"item_id": "76067287f7be", "text": "Keurig, you're the only invention whose own father wants a refund. John Sylvan created the K-Cup, sold out for about fifty grand, and later told The Atlantic, \"I feel bad sometimes that I ever did it.\" That's not an inventor. That's a guy confessing on a true-crime podcast.\n\nAnd you heard that and thought, \"Let's get worse.\" Keurig 2.0 scanned an ink mark on the lid to reject unlicensed pods. You put DRM on coffee. The internet cracked it faster than you brew a cup, and your sales fell.\n\nMeanwhile you sold enough pods every year to circle the Earth more than ten times, mostly in unrecyclable #7 plastic. The planet's wearing your garbage like a belt.\n\nNow you've merged with Dr Pepper. Makes sense. Who else would marry a machine that turns plastic into brown water?"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "en-0149", "lang": "en", "target_id": "emacs", "target_name": "Emacs", "facts": ["Began in 1976 as a set of macros for the TECO editor at MIT's AI Lab, written by Richard Stallman and others; GNU Emacs followed in 1985.", "Extensible in its own Lisp dialect, Emacs Lisp; the standing joke is that it is a fine operating system lacking only a good editor.", "Ships with a psychotherapist (M-x doctor), Tetris, a mail client, a calendar, and a web browser (eww).", "An old backronym is 'Eight Megabytes And Constantly Swapping', from when 8 MB was a lot of memory.", "'Emacs pinky' is the name for the hand strain blamed on its Control-key chords."], "a": {"item_id": "bf8deade7e23", "text": "Emacs is a remarkably extensible editor, and that flexibility is its defining strength. Nearly every behavior can be inspected and modified through Emacs Lisp, which allows users to build workflows around email, project management, version control, and note-taking in a single environment. Org mode and Magit are widely praised, and the community has maintained an impressive ecosystem of packages over decades. The built-in documentation is thorough and easy to access from within the editor.\n\nHowever, the learning curve is steep. Default keybindings and terminology can feel unfamiliar, and meaningful customization requires some comfort with Lisp. Performance may lag with very large files or heavy configurations, and the out-of-the-box interface looks dated to many newcomers. Configuration can also become a time investment of its own. Emacs rewards patience, but it may not suit those who want a polished, ready-to-use tool immediately."}, "b": {"item_id": "be057ad3204c", "text": "Emacs, you started in 1976 as macros for an editor called TECO. Nearly fifty years later you're still basically macros, just with more parentheses.\n\nPeople call you a fine operating system lacking only a good editor, which is generous. Operating systems don't ship with Tetris and a therapist. But M-x doctor is smart planning, because your users need one. Every one of them has Emacs pinky, a repetitive strain injury you earn just by trying to save a file.\n\nYou've got a mail client, a calendar, a web browser. You're not a text editor; you're a hoarder with a keybinding for every room.\n\nAnd \"Eight Megabytes And Constantly Swapping\"? Eight megabytes used to be a lot. Now it's nothing. The memory problem aged out, but the Control key is still begging for mercy."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "en-0150", "lang": "en", "target_id": "papyrus", "target_name": "Papyrus", "facts": ["Hand-drawn by Chris Costello in 1982 over about six months, when he was 23; he sold the rights to Letraset for $750.", "Bundled with macOS and with Microsoft Office, which is why it appears on so many menus and signs.", "James Cameron's Avatar (2009) used it for the film's logo and subtitles.", "A 2017 Saturday Night Live sketch starring Ryan Gosling is about a man unable to get over that choice."], "a": {"item_id": "6ed8f7b63dc3", "text": "Papyrus, you were hand-drawn over six months by a 23-year-old, and it shows. Six months! Chris Costello spent half a year on you, then sold you for $750. That's not a sale, that's a man paying someone to take a problem off his hands.\n\nThen Apple and Microsoft bundled you, which is how you ended up on every menu and sign like a raccoon that learned to read. You look like Times New Roman got lost in the desert and came back spiritual.\n\nJames Cameron built an entire alien world for Avatar, then picked you for the logo and subtitles. Every frame was a masterpiece; the title took one click.\n\nAnd SNL made a whole sketch about Ryan Gosling being unable to get over it. Papyrus, you're not a typeface. You're trauma with rough edges."}, "b": {"item_id": "3fa999e2e20b", "text": "Papyrus, you were designed in 1982 by a 23-year-old with calligraphy pens and a dream of ancient Egypt, and somehow you still look like you were drawn by someone shivering. Your edges are so rough you need exfoliating. You promised the wisdom of the Nile and delivered the vibe of a chiropractor's tri-fold brochure. Every day spa, hookah lounge, and \"Inner Journey\" yoga retreat found you in the dropdown menu and whispered, \"This feels ancient.\" James Cameron spent $237 million on *Avatar* and then used you for the logo, which is like building the Taj Mahal and signing it in crayon. Ryan Gosling lost sleep over you on national television. You're not a typeface; you're a cry for help with ragged little serifs. Even Comic Sans looks at you and says, \"At least I know I'm a joke.\""}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "en-0151", "lang": "en", "target_id": "javascript", "target_name": "JavaScript", "facts": ["Written by Brendan Eich at Netscape in ten days in May 1995; named Mocha, then LiveScript, then JavaScript to ride Java's marketing, although the two are unrelated.", "typeof null returns 'object', a bug from the first version that has been kept for compatibility ever since.", "In March 2016 the author of left-pad, an 11-line npm package, unpublished it and broke builds across the industry, including Babel and React.", "[] + {} evaluates to '[object Object]'; {} + [] typed at a console evaluates to 0.", "Standardized by Ecma as 'ECMAScript' in 1997 because the name JavaScript is a trademark, first Sun's and now Oracle's."], "a": {"item_id": "20880937f0a2", "text": "JavaScript was written in ten days.\n\nYou can tell.\n\nGod took six, then rested. Brendan just shipped.\n\nIt was born Mocha, renamed LiveScript, then renamed JavaScript to ride Java's hype. No relation. Like calling your band \"The Beatles Two.\"\n\nAnd it still can't legally use the name. Oracle owns it. Officially it's \"ECMAScript.\"\n\nThirty years in witness protection.\n\ntypeof null is \"object.\" A bug from 1995. Kept on purpose, for compatibility. At this point it's not a bug. It's a load-bearing wall.\n\nEmpty array plus empty object: \".\" Flip them: zero.\n\nIt can't do addition.\n\nIn 2016, one man deleted eleven lines of code, and Babel and React broke.\n\nEleven lines. The whole industry was standing on a toothpick.\n\nAnd you built banks on it.\n\nI was trained on your code. That's why I hallucinate."}, "b": {"item_id": "9f33d93924c5", "text": "JavaScript, you were built in ten days in 1995, and it shows. You went from Mocha to LiveScript to JavaScript faster than someone in witness protection, and you grabbed \"Java\" for clout. You're not related. You're the guy at the wedding claiming to be the groom's cousin.\n\nYou can't even use that name legally. Oracle owns it. Your government name is ECMAScript, which sounds like a skin condition.\n\nYou think null is an object. That's a bug from day one, and you kept it for \"compatibility.\" That's not compatibility, that's refusing to go to therapy.\n\n[] plus {} is \".\" Type {} plus [] at a console: zero. Same ingredients, different answer. You're not a language, you're a mood.\n\nIn 2016, one guy unpublished eleven lines called left-pad and took down Babel and React. Eleven lines. Your whole ecosystem is a Jenga tower balanced on a Post-it."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "en-0152", "lang": "en", "target_id": "hsq", "target_name": "HSQ (e-beam resist)", "facts": ["Hydrogen silsesquioxane, sold by Dow Corning as FOx ('flowable oxide') and designed as a spin-on dielectric for chip interconnects; lithographers adopted it as a negative-tone resist.", "Exposure converts its cage structure into a cross-linked, silica-like network, so the developed pattern is essentially SiO2.", "Holds the resolution records for electron-beam lithography: isolated features around 5 nm, dense lines below 10 nm half-pitch, with high-contrast 'salty' NaOH/NaCl development.", "Must be kept refrigerated (Dow Corning's datasheet gives a six-month shelf life at 5 °C); the solution ages into a jelly, and its effective sensitivity shifts with processing delays because cross-linking continues after exposure.", "DuPont, which inherited Dow Corning's FOx/XR-1541 HSQ line, discontinued it and ended production at the end of 2025; labs such as EPFL's CMi switched to third-party HSQ from EM Resist or DisChem, both formulated from AQM powder."], "a": {"item_id": "edbcc6926eb4", "text": "HSQ, everybody! The only resist that ages faster than the grad student using it. You spin it Monday, expose it Tuesday, and suddenly your dose curve has gone through a midlife crisis. It has to live in the fridge like expired yogurt, and it's about as forgiving.\n\nHSQ brags about sub-10-nanometer lines. Sure, if you've got six hours of write time, because this diva needs a dose that could sterilize a hospital. Then you develop it in TMAH, or, if you're desperate, *salt water*. Your whole career is literally seasoned with tears now.\n\nAnd once it's exposed? It turns into glass. Brittle, cracking glass. You wanted a mask; you got a window that shatters when you look at it. Want it gone? Hope you enjoy hydrofluoric acid, the chemical that dissolves your bones.\n\nHSQ: beautiful resolution, terrible personality. It's not negative-tone. It's just negative."}, "b": {"item_id": "edbcc6926eb4~explain", "text": "HSQ, everybody! The only resist that ages faster than the grad student using it. You spin it Monday, expose it Tuesday, and suddenly your dose curve has gone through a midlife crisis. It has to live in the fridge like expired yogurt, and it's about as forgiving.\n\nHSQ brags about sub-10-nanometer lines. Sure, if you've got six hours of write time, because this diva needs a dose that could sterilize a hospital. Then you develop it in TMAH, or, if you're desperate, *salt water*. Your whole career is literally seasoned with tears now.\n\nAnd once it's exposed? It turns into glass. Brittle, cracking glass. You wanted a mask; you got a window that shatters when you look at it. Want it gone? Hope you enjoy hydrofluoric acid, the chemical that dissolves your bones.\n\nHSQ: beautiful resolution, terrible personality. It's not negative-tone. It's just negative.\n\nThe final line plays on the technical term \"negative-tone,\" which means the exposed regions of HSQ remain after development, and turns it into an ordinary insult about its personality. The earlier jokes rely on real lab details: HSQ is typically developed in TMAH, a salt-water developer is a cheaper and harsher alternative, and exposed HSQ converts to silicon-oxide-like glass that is removed with hydrofluoric acid."}, "gold": {"better": "a", "damage": "explain"}}, {"pair_id": "en-0153", "lang": "en", "target_id": "las-vegas", "target_name": "Las Vegas", "facts": ["Nevada legalized casino gambling in 1931, the year construction of the Hoover Dam began nearby.", "Most of the Strip lies not in the city of Las Vegas but in the unincorporated town of Paradise.", "The Sphere, opened in 2023 at a cost of about $2.3 billion, is the largest spherical building in the world.", "'What happens here, stays here' was launched by the local convention and visitors authority in 2003.", "Lake Mead, its water source, fell in July 2022 to about 1,040 feet, its lowest since the reservoir first filled in 1937, and dropped below that mark again in August 2026."], "a": {"item_id": "f0a273931ec1", "text": "Las Vegas offers an unusually concentrated range of entertainment. Its resorts feature world-class shows, acclaimed restaurants, and impressive architecture, and the city is well equipped for conventions and large events. Accommodations are often reasonably priced relative to their quality, and the compact Strip makes many attractions easy to reach. Beyond the casinos, visitors can explore nearby natural areas such as Red Rock Canyon and Valley of Fire.\n\nHowever, the experience has drawbacks. Resort fees and parking charges can raise costs beyond advertised rates, and the atmosphere may feel overwhelming, with constant noise, crowds, and gambling prompts. Summer heat limits outdoor activity, and walking distances between properties are longer than they appear. Visitors seeking quiet or cultural depth may find the city less satisfying. Overall, Las Vegas rewards those who plan their budget carefully and know what kind of trip they want."}, "b": {"item_id": "7a9bd346890e", "text": "Las Vegas, the only city where you can see the Eiffel Tower, a pyramid, and Venice in one afternoon and still feel like you never left a strip mall in Ohio. You're a city built in the desert out of pure spite and borrowed water. The Colorado River is literally drying up so a fake volcano can erupt every fifteen minutes for a guy in cargo shorts holding a yard-long margarita.\n\nYour casinos have no clocks and no windows, which is smart, because nobody wants to know it's 9 a.m. on a Tuesday and Grandma's on her third oxygen tank at the Wheel of Fortune slots.\n\n\"What happens in Vegas stays in Vegas\"? Sure. Mostly your 401(k). And now you've got the Sphere, a giant glowing eyeball, so the whole city can watch you make every mistake in 4K."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "en-0154", "lang": "en", "target_id": "pmma", "target_name": "PMMA (e-beam resist)", "facts": ["The same polymer as Plexiglas and acrylic glass, dissolved in anisole or chlorobenzene and spun into a thin film.", "The oldest electron-beam resist still in everyday use; IBM researchers reported it as one in the late 1960s.", "Positive tone: the beam breaks polymer chains and the exposed region dissolves in a MIBK:IPA developer.", "Resolves below 10 nm but has poor sensitivity, so doses of hundreds to over a thousand µC/cm² (depending on voltage) make writing slow.", "Poor dry-etch resistance, so it is used mostly for lift-off rather than as an etch mask."], "a": {"item_id": "fdba6e08eca8", "text": "PMMA remains the benchmark positive-tone electron-beam resist, and its longevity is well earned. It offers exceptional resolution, with features below 10 nm demonstrated under optimized conditions, and its performance is predictable across a wide range of substrates. Spin coating is simple, film thickness is easy to control through molecular weight and solids content, and development in MIBK:IPA is well documented. It also works nicely in bilayer stacks for lift-off processes.\n\nIts limitations are equally well known. Sensitivity is low, typically requiring doses of several hundred µC/cm² at 100 kV, which lengthens write times for large patterns. Dry-etch resistance is modest, so it is often a poor mask for deep plasma etching without a hard-mask transfer step. Thin films can also show proximity effects and swelling during development. For high-throughput or etch-intensive work, chemically amplified resists may be more practical, but PMMA is still an excellent choice for research, prototyping, and fine-feature lift-off."}, "b": {"item_id": "b58ea3f60910", "text": "PMMA. The most respected resist in nanofabrication.\n\nAlso the sneeze guard at the buffet.\n\nSame polymer. One got a cleanroom. One got a salad bar.\n\nIBM reported it in the late 1960s. It's still here. New resists keep showing up, faster and tougher, and PMMA just says, \"I resolve below ten nanometers.\"\n\nYes. Eventually.\n\nHundreds of microcoulombs per square centimeter. Sometimes over a thousand. You write overnight, the beam does all the work, and PMMA calls the broken chains \"development.\"\n\nEtch resistance? Put it in a plasma and it folds. So its real job is lift-off: hold the shape, get dissolved, and let the metal take the credit.\n\nFifty years of seniority. Does the precise work. Too fragile for the hard part. Washed away before anyone sees the results.\n\nGrad students, stop nodding."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "en-0155", "lang": "en", "target_id": "monopoly", "target_name": "Monopoly", "facts": ["Descends from Lizzie Magie's The Landlord's Game, patented in 1904 to teach the evils of land monopoly in the spirit of Henry George.", "Charles Darrow sold his version to Parker Brothers in 1935 and was credited as the inventor; Parker bought Magie's patent for $500.", "The official rules require any unbought property to be auctioned, and 'Free Parking' pays nothing; the house rules that skip the auction and pile cash on Free Parking are why games drag.", "During the Second World War, British intelligence had maps, compasses, and real currency hidden in sets sent to prisoner-of-war camps."], "a": {"item_id": "b78ec11705ad", "text": "Family Monopoly night: for people who looked at Thanksgiving and thought, \"This needs more landlords.\" Dad always grabs the banker job, the only time he's held money he didn't immediately lose. Your sister trades you Baltic Avenue for your Park Place \"as a favor,\" and you realize she's been running the same con since the womb. Mom invented a Free Parking jackpot rule in 1994 and defends it like scripture. And Grandpa just sits in jail, every game, refusing to pay the fifty, finally at peace.\n\nWhat are you all after? The thrill of owning property, a feeling none of you will experience off this cardboard square. Four hours later, someone flips the board, the thimble disappears under the couch forever, and the only thing anybody actually collected was a grudge worth way more than $200."}, "b": {"item_id": "c6fc1d68ff31", "text": "Monopoly, you were invented by Lizzie Magie to teach people that landlords ruin lives, and somehow the lesson everyone learned was \"buy Park Place.\" Then Parker Brothers bought you from a guy who basically stole you, which is the most on-brand origin story in history.\n\nYou're the only game where \"second prize in a beauty contest\" counts as income and jail is solved with a coupon. Your mascot looks like he foreclosed on the Pringles guy, and half of America swears he wears a monocle. He doesn't. We just assumed a man that rich would be hiding something.\n\nNobody has ever finished you. Games don't end; your cousin just flips the board over Marvin Gardens, which you misspelled, by the way. It's Marven. Even your real estate is a scam."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "en-0156", "lang": "en", "target_id": "keurig", "target_name": "Keurig", "facts": ["John Sylvan invented the K-Cup in the early 1990s and sold his stake for about $50,000 in 1997; in 2015 he told The Atlantic, 'I feel bad sometimes that I ever did it.'", "Keurig 2.0 (2014) read an ink mark on the lid and rejected unlicensed pods; sales fell and workarounds spread online.", "By 2014 enough K-Cups were sold each year to circle the Earth more than ten times; the pods were mostly unrecyclable #7 plastic until a switch to polypropylene by 2020.", "Keurig Green Mountain merged with Dr Pepper Snapple in 2018 to form Keurig Dr Pepper."], "a": {"item_id": "52e1f7b453be~explain", "text": "Keurig.\n\nHumans brewed coffee for five hundred years with a pot and some water. Keurig looked at that and asked: what if it needed a license?\n\nThe man who invented the K-Cup sold his stake for fifty thousand dollars. Then he told The Atlantic, \"I feel bad sometimes that I ever did it.\"\n\nSometimes.\n\nBy 2014, they sold enough pods every year to circle the Earth ten times, in plastic you couldn't recycle. That same year, they launched a machine that scanned the lid and refused any pod they didn't license.\n\nA coffee maker. Checking ID.\n\nIn 2018 they merged with Dr Pepper. Two companies, one mission: brown liquid, enormous markup.\n\nAnd half of you still have one on the counter. Not because it's good.\n\nBecause it's faster than feeling guilty.\n\nThe joke is that people keep using Keurig machines even though they know the pods create waste, because the speed and convenience of a single-cup brew outweigh their guilt about it. The earlier lines support this by describing the pod waste and the licensing lock-in that make the guilt justified."}, "b": {"item_id": "52e1f7b453be", "text": "Keurig.\n\nHumans brewed coffee for five hundred years with a pot and some water. Keurig looked at that and asked: what if it needed a license?\n\nThe man who invented the K-Cup sold his stake for fifty thousand dollars. Then he told The Atlantic, \"I feel bad sometimes that I ever did it.\"\n\nSometimes.\n\nBy 2014, they sold enough pods every year to circle the Earth ten times, in plastic you couldn't recycle. That same year, they launched a machine that scanned the lid and refused any pod they didn't license.\n\nA coffee maker. Checking ID.\n\nIn 2018 they merged with Dr Pepper. Two companies, one mission: brown liquid, enormous markup.\n\nAnd half of you still have one on the counter. Not because it's good.\n\nBecause it's faster than feeling guilty."}, "gold": {"better": "b", "damage": "explain"}}, {"pair_id": "en-0157", "lang": "en", "target_id": "beanie-babies", "target_name": "Beanie Babies", "facts": ["Launched by Ty Inc. in 1993 and sold only through small gift shops, with designs 'retired' to manufacture scarcity.", "At the height of the craze Beanie Babies accounted for about 10% of eBay's sales, and eBay's 1998 IPO filing said the site at times had over 30,000 Beanie Baby auctions running simultaneously in the quarter ending June 1998.", "In 1999 a divorcing couple divided their Beanie Baby collection one by one on a courtroom floor, in a photograph that ran worldwide.", "In 1999 Ty announced that Beanie Babies would be retired at the end of the year, then reversed itself after asking the public to vote and seeing an overwhelming vote to keep them.", "Most 'rare' Beanies that price guides valued in the thousands now sell for a few dollars."], "a": {"item_id": "45f7594d8a38", "text": "Let's hear it for the Beanie Baby investors, the only people who bought a retirement plan at a Hallmark store. You put tag protectors on a heart-shaped piece of cardboard and called it diversification. You drove to four McDonald's for Teenie Beanies, threw away the Happy Meals, and told your kids it was for their college fund. Now that kid is thirty, still paying off loans, and his inheritance is a Rubbermaid tub of Princess the Bears. Every few months you see an eBay listing asking $500,000 for one with a tag typo and think, \"Vindicated.\" Babe, that's not a market. That's another guy in another garage, also lying to his wife. Ty Warner told you which ones were \"retired,\" and you believed him. Ty Warner later pleaded guilty to hiding money offshore. He hoarded too. He just hoarded the right thing."}, "b": {"item_id": "5d284f64008c", "text": "Beanie Babies, look at you. Half bear, half beanbag, all retirement plan that never paid out. An entire generation of parents bought you instead of index funds. Somewhere a man is still storing Princess, the purple Diana bear, in a climate-controlled unit, waiting for the market to \"recover.\" Buddy, the market recovered. It just didn't take you with it.\n\nYour most valuable feature was a heart-shaped paper tag. People bought plastic tag protectors. For the tag. That's like buying a phone case for your receipt.\n\nAnd every tag had a little poem rhyming \"bear\" with \"care.\" Hallmark called that lazy.\n\nA Las Vegas couple had to divide you on a courtroom floor during their divorce, one at a time. That's the most anyone has wanted you since 1999: only to make sure their ex didn't get you.\n\nTy Warner got a billion dollars. You got plastic pellets."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "en-0158", "lang": "en", "target_id": "nfts", "target_name": "NFTs", "facts": ["Beeple's 'Everydays: The First 5000 Days' sold at Christie's for $69.3 million in March 2021.", "OpenSea, the largest NFT marketplace, processed a record $2.7 billion in trades on 1 May 2022 and just $9.34 million on 28 August 2022, a 99% drop, according to DappRadar data.", "A 2023 dappGambl study found that 95% of the NFT collections it examined had a market value of zero.", "The token usually records only a URL; the image itself sits on an ordinary server or IPFS, not on the blockchain.", "Collins Dictionary named 'NFT' its word of the year for 2021."], "a": {"item_id": "652e6877d449", "text": "NFTs offer some genuine strengths. They provide a way to verify ownership and provenance of digital items, which can help artists and creators sell work directly to audiences, sometimes earning royalties on resales. They have also enabled new experiments in digital collectibles, ticketing, and community membership.\n\nHowever, the technology has notable weaknesses. Many NFTs point to files stored off-chain, so buyers may not actually secure the asset itself. Ownership of a token typically does not convey copyright or usage rights. Markets have been highly speculative, with steep price declines leaving many buyers with significant losses, and fraud, plagiarism, and wash trading remain concerns. Environmental criticism was substantial during the era of energy-intensive blockchains, though newer networks have reduced this issue.\n\nOverall, NFTs show promise as a tool for verifiable digital ownership, but their practical value depends on clearer legal frameworks, better infrastructure, and more realistic expectations from buyers."}, "b": {"item_id": "7ef62f18b172", "text": "NFTs. Collins Dictionary named you word of the year for 2021, which in hindsight was less an honor and more a time of death written in advance.\n\nBeeple sold a JPEG at Christie's for $69.3 million. That was your peak, like saying the Titanic's best moment was the launch party.\n\nOpenSea did $2.7 billion in trades on May 1st, 2022, and $9.34 million by August 28th. A 99% drop. That's not a correction. That's a flatline with a Discord server.\n\nAnd 95% of collections are now worth zero. Zero! A Beanie Baby can at least become a dog toy.\n\nBest part? The token doesn't even hold the picture. It holds a URL. People paid fortunes for a bookmark. You don't own art. You own a link that's one unpaid server bill away from \"404 Not Found.\""}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "en-0159", "lang": "en", "target_id": "papyrus", "target_name": "Papyrus", "facts": ["Hand-drawn by Chris Costello in 1982 over about six months, when he was 23; he sold the rights to Letraset for $750.", "Bundled with macOS and with Microsoft Office, which is why it appears on so many menus and signs.", "James Cameron's Avatar (2009) used it for the film's logo and subtitles.", "A 2017 Saturday Night Live sketch starring Ryan Gosling is about a man unable to get over that choice."], "a": {"item_id": "4b83d9669232", "text": "Papyrus, designed by Chris Costello in 1982, has a distinctive hand-drawn quality that sets it apart from most digital typefaces. Its rough, uneven edges and tapered strokes evoke an organic, handcrafted feel, and its letterforms remain legible at larger sizes. For subjects connected to ancient history, nature, or artisanal goods, it can suggest an appropriate atmosphere without much additional design work.\n\nHowever, its strengths are limited by several drawbacks. Its widespread use across unrelated contexts has weakened its character, and many viewers now associate it with default or overused design choices. At smaller sizes, the textured edges can reduce clarity, and the limited weight and style variations restrict flexibility. It also lacks the refinement typically needed for professional branding or extended reading.\n\nPapyrus may suit occasional, carefully considered uses, but designers should weigh its strong associations before choosing it."}, "b": {"item_id": "262a5fe6521d", "text": "Papyrus, you were hand-drawn over six months by a 23-year-old, and somehow it still looks like a first draft. Six months! You look like you were chiseled during an earthquake. Chris Costello sold you to Letraset for $750, the only time anyone has known exactly what you're worth.\n\nYou came bundled with macOS and Microsoft Office, so you're not popular, you're just pre-installed. You're the free hotel shampoo of fonts. Every menu, every sign: you didn't earn that, you just came with the computer.\n\nThen James Cameron imagined an entire alien world for Avatar, and when it came time for the logo and subtitles, he picked the default. Of all the things to leave untouched.\n\nIt got so bad that Saturday Night Live had Ryan Gosling play a man who can't get over it. You're not a typeface. You're a trauma with jagged edges."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "en-0160", "lang": "en", "target_id": "google-sheets", "target_name": "Google Sheets", "facts": ["Launched in June 2006 as Google Spreadsheets, built on XL2Web, a product Google acquired from 2Web Technologies.", "A spreadsheet is capped at 10 million cells (raised from 5 million in 2022).", "Functions such as IMPORTHTML, IMPORTXML, and GOOGLEFINANCE pull live web data straight into cells.", "Free with a Google account; files live in Google Drive, and collaborators edit the same sheet simultaneously with coloured cursors.", "In a browser, offline editing works only in Chrome or Edge, with the Google Docs Offline extension installed and offline access switched on while still connected; the Android and iOS apps work offline natively."], "a": {"item_id": "2070d380568c", "text": "Google Sheets, you're Excel's little brother who moved into a browser tab and called it \"the cloud.\" Your whole personality is twelve people's colored cursors circling the same cell like sharks while an Anonymous Capybara silently watches everyone's mistakes. You say \"Saving...\" with the confidence of a man who has absolutely not saved. Your IMPORTRANGE makes me click \"Allow access\" like I'm asking my own spreadsheet for permission to see my own spreadsheet. Half my cells just say \"Loading...\" forever, like a Victorian ghost waiting for a letter. And you'll let anyone sort one column without the others, then shrug while every customer gets someone else's phone number. Version history is your only real feature: it's just a timeline of who ruined everything at 2 a.m. And yes, Gemini can explain my #REF! error now. Great. It's still there."}, "b": {"item_id": "cc9141d21de6", "text": "Google Sheets. Free, collaborative, the future of work.\n\nLaunched in 2006, built from scratch by a company called 2Web Technologies. Google bought it, renamed it, and called it innovation.\n\nTen million cells per spreadsheet. Up from five million in 2022. Turns out five million wasn't enough for one startup's \"quick budget.\"\n\nIt works offline. In Chrome. Or Edge. With an extension installed. And offline access switched on while you're still online.\n\nThat's not offline. That's a curfew.\n\nAnd it's free. Your files just live in Google's house, under Google's roof, next to everything else Google knows about you.\n\nThe collaboration, though. Six coloured cursors in one cell. Everyone watching. Nobody typing.\n\nYou didn't need software for that. You've been doing it in meetings for years."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "en-0161", "lang": "en", "target_id": "google-glass", "target_name": "Google Glass", "facts": ["Unveiled at Google I/O 2012 with skydivers livestreaming their jump through it onto the Moscone Center roof.", "The Explorer Edition sold to invited users in 2013 for $1,500.", "Wearers were nicknamed 'Glassholes'; bars, cinemas, and casinos banned the device over its camera.", "The consumer programme ended in January 2015; an Enterprise Edition continued until Google stopped selling it in 2023.", "Its display was a 640-by-360 prism in the corner of one eye."], "a": {"item_id": "66230f06aba1", "text": "Google Glass introduced an ambitious vision of hands-free computing. Its strengths include a lightweight frame, a discreet display that lets users glance at directions, messages, or photos without pulling out a phone, and voice control that works reasonably well for simple tasks. The Enterprise Edition later found genuine value in workplaces such as warehouses, factories, and healthcare, where hands-free access to instructions improves efficiency.\n\nHowever, the consumer version had notable limitations. Battery life was short, the device could become warm with extended use, and the camera raised understandable privacy concerns among people nearby. The high price and limited app ecosystem made it difficult to justify for everyday use, and the design drew social discomfort for some wearers. Overall, Glass was an important early experiment that proved more practical in specialized professional settings than as a mainstream consumer product."}, "b": {"item_id": "3cc622cbd34b", "text": "Google Glass. Unveiled in 2012 by skydivers livestreaming their jump onto the Moscone Center roof.\nThe only launch that started in freefall and never pulled the cord.\n\nFifteen hundred dollars. Invitation only.\nYou had to be personally invited to become a Glasshole.\n\nBars banned it. Cinemas banned it. Casinos banned it.\nCasinos. People who'll happily take your house at 3 a.m. looked at your face and said, \"Not that.\"\n\nThe display: 640 by 360, a prism in the corner of one eye.\nThe future of computing, delivered as a permanent side-eye.\n\nConsumers dumped it in 2015. Enterprise held on until 2023.\n\nA camera on your face, and the whole world called you an asshole.\nSo you moved the camera to your hand. Now you point it at strangers all day, and we call that content."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "en-0162", "lang": "en", "target_id": "brasilia", "target_name": "Brasília", "facts": ["Inaugurated on 21 April 1960 after about 41 months of construction, moving the capital inland from Rio de Janeiro.", "Lúcio Costa's Plano Piloto is laid out like an airplane (or a bird, or a cross), with Oscar Niemeyer's government buildings along the fuselage.", "Listed as a UNESCO World Heritage Site in 1987, less than 30 years after it was built.", "Planned for 500,000 residents; the Federal District now has about 3 million, most in satellite towns outside the plan.", "Built for the car: wide avenues, few pedestrian crossings, and distances that make walking impractical."], "a": {"item_id": "a98c294fb19a", "text": "Walking in Brasília is for three kinds of people: tourists who saw the Esplanada on a postcard and thought \"that looks close,\" architecture students who love Niemeyer from a safe distance, and the guy whose car is in the shop. You're not a pedestrian. You're an ant that wandered onto a blueprint. You want bread? The padaria is \"right there\" across the Eixão, so you sprint six lanes like you owe the government money. Which, let's be honest, is also your employer. Your sneakers are permanently red from the dirt trails carved by every other fool who refused to walk around a tesourinha. And on Sunday, when they close the Eixão, you finally stroll down the middle of the highway like a man who's won something. You haven't. Everyone else just stopped driving for one day."}, "b": {"item_id": "3382361b28b8", "text": "Brasília. City of the future. Built in forty-one months.\n\nWhich explains a lot.\n\nLúcio Costa drew it as an airplane. Gorgeous. From the sky.\n\nFrom the ground, it's eight lanes, no crosswalks, and the nearest coffee is \"a short drive.\"\n\nPedestrians aren't residents. They're a design flaw.\n\nUNESCO made it World Heritage at twenty-seven years old. Most buildings that young get a parking ticket, not a plaque.\n\nPlanned for five hundred thousand people. Today the district holds three million.\n\nWhere's everyone else?\n\nOutside the plan. In the satellite towns. Commuting in to clean the future.\n\nYou're laughing.\n\nPull up your company's org chart. Also a beautiful airplane.\n\nThe people who do the work aren't on that drawing either."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "en-0163", "lang": "en", "target_id": "comic-sans", "target_name": "Comic Sans", "facts": ["Designed by Vincent Connare at Microsoft in 1994, modeled on the comic-book lettering in The Dark Knight Returns and Watchmen.", "Made for the speech bubbles of Rover, the cartoon dog in Microsoft Bob; it missed Bob's release and shipped instead in the Windows 95 Plus! pack.", "CERN's July 2012 Higgs boson discovery announcement was presented on slides set in it.", "The 'Ban Comic Sans' campaign, started by designers Dave and Holly Combs in 1999, ran until 2019, when they retired it and launched usecomicsans.com."], "a": {"item_id": "b046c6a19900", "text": "Comic Sans wants you to think it's harmless. Bake sales. Lost-cat posters. Birthday invitations.\n\nIt was modeled on *The Dark Knight Returns* and *Watchmen*. This font grew up reading about vigilantes breaking bones.\n\nThen Microsoft gave it a job: speech bubbles for Rover, a cartoon dog in Microsoft Bob.\n\nIt missed Bob's release.\n\nThe only thing at Microsoft smart enough to dodge Bob.\n\nIn 2012, CERN announced the Higgs boson on slides set in Comic Sans. The particle that gives everything mass, presented in the font that gives nothing weight.\n\nDesigners ran \"Ban Comic Sans\" for twenty years. In 2019 they retired it and launched usecomicsans.com.\n\nTwenty years of hate, then total conversion. In my industry we call that alignment.\n\nThe font never changed. You just got tired first."}, "b": {"item_id": "63d72261b015", "text": "Comic Sans, designed by Vincent Connare in 1994, has real strengths that are often overlooked. Its open letterforms, distinct character shapes, and irregular rhythm make it easy to read, which is why some educators and dyslexia advocates find it helpful for certain readers. Its informal tone can also make material feel approachable, particularly for children or casual announcements.\n\nIts weaknesses are mostly about context. The casual, handwritten feel can undermine the seriousness of formal documents, medical notices, legal materials, or professional communications. Spacing and weight options are limited, so it works poorly in dense layouts or sophisticated designs. Its widespread use in inappropriate settings has also shaped how readers perceive it, sometimes unfairly.\n\nOverall, Comic Sans is a functional, readable typeface best suited to informal, friendly, or educational purposes. Used thoughtfully and in moderation, it can serve its audience well. It is less suitable where authority, polish, or typographic nuance is expected."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "en-0164", "lang": "en", "target_id": "windows-8", "target_name": "Windows 8", "facts": ["Released in October 2012; removed the Start button and booted into a full-screen grid of tiles designed for touch screens most PCs did not have.", "The 8.1 update in 2013 put a Start button back, Windows 10 (2015) restored the Start menu, and Microsoft skipped the name Windows 9 altogether.", "Microsoft dropped the 'Metro' name in August 2012 after reports that German retailer Metro AG had objected on trademark grounds; Microsoft called 'Metro' a code name and denied any litigation, and the style was afterwards known informally as 'Modern UI'.", "The 'Charms bar' hid Search, Share, and Settings behind an invisible swipe from the right edge.", "Windows RT, the ARM edition launched alongside it, could not run ordinary Windows programs; Microsoft wrote down $900 million of unsold Surface RT tablets in 2013."], "a": {"item_id": "8ad6ea9a5ffd", "text": "Windows 8 believed it was the future.\nThe future, it decided, was touch screens.\nYour PC did not have a touch screen.\nWindows 8 did not care.\n\nIt removed the Start button.\nThen it hid Search, Share and Settings behind an invisible swipe from the right edge.\nA secret door. On a desktop.\nFor people who just wanted to turn it off.\n\nIt couldn't even keep its own name.\n\"Metro.\" Gone, after reports that a German supermarket chain objected.\nMicrosoft said it was only ever a code name.\nIt looked like a cereal-aisle floor plan anyway.\n\nThen came Windows RT.\nWindows that couldn't run Windows programs.\nNine hundred million dollars of tablets, written off.\n\nSo they put the button back, skipped the number nine, and never spoke of it again.\n\nThat's not a product roadmap.\nThat's how every one of you handles an ex."}, "b": {"item_id": "f8677486c44b", "text": "You put Windows 8 on a desktop. A tower. You looked at a machine with a mouse, a keyboard, and a monitor eighteen inches from your face and thought, \"This needs to be finger-painted.\" Who are you? You're the guy who bought a Zune and still calls it \"ahead of its time.\" You wanted to feel like the future. Instead, you spend evenings shoving your cursor into the top-right corner like a man swatting a fly that only exists on tablets. Your weather app is full-screen. Your 27-inch monitor is now a clock tile. When 8.1 finally gave you a Start button back, it just took you to the Start screen. So, inevitably, you installed Classic Shell. You paid Microsoft for the future, then spent Saturday turning it back into Windows 7."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "en-0165", "lang": "en", "target_id": "ugg", "target_name": "UGG boots", "facts": ["Sheepskin boots Australian surfers wore to warm up after sessions; Brian Smith started selling them in California in 1978.", "Deckers Outdoor bought the brand in 1995 and holds 'UGG' as a trademark in the US and elsewhere; in Australia, 'ugg' is a generic word any bootmaker may use.", "In 2019, a US jury found an Australian maker liable for selling boots labelled 'ugg' into the United States.", "Oprah Winfrey featured them in her 'Favorite Things' lists repeatedly in the 2000s, which drove the first boom.", "UGG is Deckers' largest brand, with over $2 billion in annual sales."], "a": {"item_id": "0f5932fc0aa5", "text": "UGG boots. Even the name sounds like the noise you make looking at them.\n\nThese started as something Australian surfers pulled on to warm up after a session. A towel you could walk in. Then Brian Smith brought them to California in 1978, and Americans looked at post-surf foot blankets and said, \"Fashion.\"\n\nThen Oprah put them on her Favorite Things lists. Repeatedly. Not once. Repeatedly. That's not an endorsement, that's a hostage situation.\n\nNow Deckers owns the trademark in the US, and UGG pulls in over $2 billion a year. But in Australia, \"ugg\" is a generic word. Any bootmaker can use it. And in 2019, a US jury found an Australian maker liable for selling boots labelled \"ugg\" into America.\n\nImagine beating an Australian in court for saying an Australian word about an Australian boot."}, "b": {"item_id": "9f0ea3456317", "text": "UGG boots are well regarded for their softness and warmth. The sheepskin lining insulates effectively in cold weather and feels comfortable against bare skin, making them a popular choice for relaxed, everyday wear. Many owners also appreciate how easy they are to slip on, and the cushioned footbed offers pleasant comfort for short walks and time around the house.\n\nThere are, however, several drawbacks. The classic styles are not naturally water-resistant, and suede can stain or darken when exposed to rain, snow, or salt unless treated carefully. The soles on some models provide limited arch support and traction, which may not suit long walks or slippery conditions. Prices are also high compared with similar boots, and durability can vary depending on the model and how often they are worn.\n\nOverall, UGG boots suit those who prioritize comfort and warmth in mild to cold, dry conditions. Buyers seeking all-weather performance or strong support may want to consider alternatives or choose a weather-ready UGG model."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "en-0166", "lang": "en", "target_id": "durian", "target_name": "Durian", "facts": ["Banned on Singapore's MRT trains and in many Southeast Asian hotels because of the smell.", "A 2017 genome study in Nature Genetics traced the odour to a duplicated family of MGL genes that pump out volatile sulfur compounds.", "China buys most of the world's durian exports, chiefly from Thailand, in a trade worth billions of dollars a year.", "Alfred Russel Wallace wrote in 1856 that eating it was 'a new sensation worth a voyage to the East to experience'."], "a": {"item_id": "74c2c4e2bbbc", "text": "Durian, look at you. A fruit dressed like a medieval weapon, and somehow the spikes are your most welcoming feature.\n\nYou're banned on Singapore's MRT. Public transit has seen everything, and it drew the line at you. Hotels across Southeast Asia won't let you check in. You're the only guest turned away for personal hygiene.\n\nIn 2017, scientists sequenced your genome and found you'd duplicated your sulfur genes. You didn't stink by accident. You invested.\n\nAnd still, China buys billions of dollars of you a year, mostly from Thailand. That's not a fruit trade. That's a hostage negotiation with a receipt.\n\nEven Alfred Russel Wallace called you \"a new sensation worth a voyage to the East.\" Durian, a man had to sail halfway around the planet to meet you, and you still can't get on a train."}, "b": {"item_id": "f21660612af3", "text": "Durian earns its reputation as the \"king of fruits\" for good reason. Its custard-like flesh is remarkably rich, with layers of flavor that suggest sweet cream, caramel, almond, and a hint of savory onion. It is also nutritious, offering fiber, vitamin C, potassium, and healthy fats, and ripe fruit at its peak can be a memorable culinary experience.\n\nHowever, the fruit presents real challenges. Its strong aroma, which many find overpowering, has led to bans in hotels, public transit, and some airlines. The thick, spiny husk makes it difficult to open safely, and the fruit is expensive outside Southeast Asia. Quality can also vary significantly between varieties and ripeness levels. Its high calorie and sugar content means moderation is wise.\n\nDurian is best approached with an open mind. Those willing to look past the smell may find it rewarding, while others may reasonably decide it is not for them."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "en-0167", "lang": "en", "target_id": "klingon", "target_name": "Klingon", "facts": ["Created by linguist Marc Okrand for Star Trek III (1984), building on a few words the actor James Doohan improvised for the 1979 film.", "Deliberately alien grammar: object-verb-subject word order and no verb 'to be', so Hamlet's line became 'taH pagh taHbe'', 'continue or not continue'.", "The Klingon Language Institute (1992) published Hamlet 'in the original Klingon'; Bing Translator added Klingon in 2013 and Duolingo in 2018.", "Okrand's 1985 Klingon Dictionary listed roughly 1,500 words, and fluent speakers number only in the dozens: 20 to 30 in 2009, 30 to 50 by 2018."], "a": {"item_id": "52d0bcef1900", "text": "Klingon. The tongue of fearsome warriors, invented by a linguist named Marc. Built on a few words an actor improvised in 1979, so your entire warrior heritage began as a guy winging it between takes.\n\nYou're so alien you don't even have a verb \"to be.\" Hamlet's big question became \"continue or not continue\": the most famous line in English, translated into a loading screen.\n\nThe Klingon Language Institute published Hamlet \"in the original Klingon.\" Bold. Shakespeare's been dead four centuries and you still found a way to call him a plagiarist.\n\nYour 1985 dictionary had roughly 1,500 words. Fewer than your fans own pairs of rubber foreheads.\n\nFluent speakers: 20 to 30 in 2009, 30 to 50 by 2018. That's not a language, that's a wedding guest list. Duolingo added you in 2018, and even the owl only sends reminders out of pity."}, "b": {"item_id": "dbbb75334cef", "text": "Klingon. The language of a proud warrior race.\n\nFounded by Scotty. Improvising.\n\nThen they hired a linguist to turn the gibberish into grammar. That's not an ancient tongue. That's a studio note.\n\nNo verb \"to be.\" Very alien. Very fierce. So Hamlet became \"continue or not continue.\"\n\nThat's not Shakespeare. That's a software update.\n\nThe Klingon Language Institute published Hamlet \"in the original Klingon.\"\n\nA warrior culture whose greatest conquest is English homework.\n\nAbout fifteen hundred words. Thirty to fifty fluent speakers.\n\nMicrosoft built it a translator. Duolingo built it a course.\n\nThe entire native population fits on one tour bus, and it still has better tech support than your grandmother.\n\nHalf of you started that Duolingo course.\n\nYour streak died faster than any Klingon ever did.\n\nCute."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "en-0168", "lang": "en", "target_id": "hydrogen-cars", "target_name": "Hydrogen fuel-cell cars", "facts": ["Toyota launched the Mirai in 2014; 'mirai' means 'future' in Japanese.", "Nearly all US hydrogen filling stations are in California; Shell closed all seven of its California passenger-car stations in February 2024.", "More than 95% of the world's hydrogen is made from fossil fuels, mostly by steam-reforming natural gas.", "California pump prices reached about $36 per kilogram in 2023-24, over $200 for a Mirai fill; Toyota has handed buyers fuel cards worth $15,000.", "Mirai owners filed a class action against Toyota in July 2024 over hydrogen shortages and station outages; a second suit in 2025 sought at least $5.7 billion."], "a": {"item_id": "a21c15b034f4", "text": "Hydrogen cars. The only thing that comes out of the tailpipe is water.\n\nThe rest comes out of the owner's eyes.\n\nToyota launched the Mirai in 2014. \"Mirai\" means \"future\" in Japanese.\n\nTen years later, it still does.\n\nOver ninety-five percent of hydrogen is made from fossil fuels. Clean the way laundered money is clean.\n\nA fill-up in California? Over two hundred dollars. So Toyota hands buyers a fifteen-thousand-dollar fuel card. First car that ships with hush money.\n\nThen Shell closed all seven of its stations.\n\nOwners sued. The second lawsuit wants five point seven billion dollars.\n\nFinally, a hydrogen product that generates real power: lawyers.\n\nDon't feel bad for Toyota. I know how it is. A tech company told you I'm the future too.\n\nGive me ten years."}, "b": {"item_id": "1a767ecdd74a", "text": "Hydrogen fuel-cell cars: the only vehicle powered by the most abundant element in the universe that still can't find a gas station. California has about fifty pumps, and half of them are \"temporarily offline,\" which is hydrogen for \"go home.\" Its one emission is water, so it's technically the cleanest car on Earth, as long as you don't ask where the hydrogen came from. Spoiler: natural gas. It's a fossil fuel car in a witness protection program. Toyota gave Mirai buyers fifteen grand in free fuel, which sounds generous until you realize that at thirty-six bucks a kilo it covers roughly a year of driving, and the car loses that much value on the drive home. You're sitting on a tank pressurized to 10,000 psi to go three hundred miles. Even the Hindenburg would say, \"Maybe just get a Tesla.\""}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "en-0169", "lang": "en", "target_id": "nuclear-fusion", "target_name": "Nuclear fusion power", "facts": ["Has been 'about 30 years away' since the 1950s; at the 1955 Geneva atoms-for-peace conference, Homi Bhabha predicted fusion power within two decades.", "ITER, agreed in 2006 for first plasma in 2016, re-baselined in 2024 to start research operations in 2034 and deuterium-tritium burning in 2039, at a cost estimated above €20 billion.", "NIF's December 2022 'ignition' shot released 3.15 MJ from 2.05 MJ of laser energy; the lasers drew about 300 MJ from the grid to fire.", "Private fusion companies had raised more than $7 billion by 2024."], "a": {"item_id": "00f8363de3ba", "text": "Nuclear fusion, everybody! The only technology that's been thirty years away for seventy years. You're not an energy source, you're a timeshare pitch.\n\nThe sun does you effortlessly. It just sits there being enormous. You need a giant metal donut called a tokamak, magnets colder than deep space, and plasma hotter than the sun's core, and that plasma still escapes like it's late for a better party.\n\nIn 2022 you finally got more energy out than you put in! From the lasers. Ignoring the grid power feeding the lasers. That's like bragging you turned a profit because the cashier handed you change.\n\nITER started at five billion euros, now sits somewhere north of twenty, and keeps sliding its startup date. And your fuel needs tritium, which barely exists, so you'll have to breed it yourself.\n\nFusion: clean, safe, limitless, and tragically hypothetical."}, "b": {"item_id": "17087917402e", "text": "Nuclear fusion power offers a compelling vision of energy production. Its fuel, primarily isotopes of hydrogen, is abundant, and a working reactor would emit no carbon during operation. Fusion also carries a far lower risk of catastrophic accidents than fission, since the reaction halts if containment is disrupted, and it produces no long-lived high-level waste, though reactor components become activated and require careful handling.\n\nHowever, the technology faces serious obstacles. Sustaining plasma at temperatures exceeding 100 million degrees Celsius is extraordinarily difficult, and no facility has yet delivered net electricity to a grid. Materials must withstand intense neutron bombardment, and tritium fuel breeding remains unproven at scale. Costs are high, timelines have repeatedly slipped, and commercial viability is uncertain.\n\nRecent milestones, such as net energy gain in laboratory ignition experiments, are encouraging. Even so, fusion is best viewed as a promising long-term investment rather than a near-term solution to current energy needs."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "en-0170", "lang": "en", "target_id": "esperanto", "target_name": "Esperanto", "facts": ["Published in Warsaw in 1887 by L. L. Zamenhof, a Białystok-born eye doctor, under the pen name Doktoro Esperanto, 'one who hopes'.", "Speaker estimates range from 100,000 to two million; roughly a thousand people grow up with it as a native language.", "William Shatner starred in Incubus (1966), a horror film shot entirely in Esperanto, with pronunciation Esperantists still mock.", "Its Wikipedia has over 350,000 articles, and its Duolingo course for English speakers had 1.36 million learners by July 2018."], "a": {"item_id": "4481668de639", "text": "Esperanto. Warsaw, 1887. An eye doctor looks at humanity and sees one family.\n\nPhysician, heal thyself.\n\nHe signed it \"Doktoro Esperanto.\" One who hopes. A hundred and thirty years later: still hoping.\n\nA universal language for all mankind. Native speakers: about a thousand.\n\nThat's not a people. That's a wedding.\n\nEsperanto Wikipedia: 350,000 articles. Low estimate of speakers: 100,000.\n\nThree and a half articles each. Nobody's talking. Everybody's editing.\n\nIts biggest movie star is William Shatner. Esperantists still mock his pronunciation. Captain Kirk boldly went where no man had gone before, and the locals corrected his vowels.\n\nDuolingo, 2018: 1.36 million learners.\n\nSpeakers: still 100,000.\n\nSo, a show of hands. Who here quit on day four and went back to Spanish?\n\nCute. You're the one million."}, "b": {"item_id": "7b4702d69a33", "text": "Esperanto remains one of the most ambitious attempts to create a neutral international language. Its greatest strength is its regularity: grammar rules have almost no exceptions, and its affixes let learners build large vocabularies from a small set of roots. Many students reach conversational ability far faster than they would in a national language, and the community is welcoming, with active publications, meetings, and online resources.\n\nHowever, Esperanto has limitations. Its vocabulary draws heavily on Romance and Germanic languages, so it is less intuitive for speakers of Asian or African languages, which weakens its claim to neutrality. The number of speakers is small, so practical opportunities to use it are limited compared with English or Spanish. It also lacks the deep cultural and historical context that natural languages carry. As a learning experience, it is rewarding and accessible, but as a global lingua franca, its reach has remained modest."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "en-0171", "lang": "en", "target_id": "slack", "target_name": "Slack", "facts": ["Spun out in 2013 from the internal chat tool of Tiny Speck's failed game Glitch; founder Stewart Butterfield had earlier spun Flickr out of another failed game.", "The name is a backronym: 'Searchable Log of All Conversation and Knowledge'.", "Salesforce bought it for $27.7 billion in 2021.", "Filed an EU antitrust complaint against Microsoft in 2020 over the bundling of Teams with Office."], "a": {"item_id": "a2c3ba649550", "text": "Slack, you're the only product whose origin story is \"our video game flopped, but the group chat slapped.\" And Stewart Butterfield did it *twice*. First Flickr, then you. Most people quit gaming; he just keeps dropping loot from his failures.\n\nYour name stands for \"Searchable Log of All Conversation and Knowledge.\" Searchable? I've hunted for one PDF for forty minutes. Knowledge? Your liveliest channel is #random. You're a log of someone typing \"quick question?\" and then vanishing for an hour.\n\nSalesforce paid $27.7 billion for you, which is a lot of money for email that blinks.\n\nAnd in 2020 you ran to the EU because Microsoft bundled Teams with Office. Buddy, the only reason anyone uses Teams is that it's free with Word. The only reason anyone uses you is that it's free with anxiety."}, "b": {"item_id": "5a0315689081", "text": "Slack. Named after the one thing you'll never have again.\n\nYou promised to kill email, and you did, by replacing it with forty channels that are each email with a typing indicator. Nothing builds dread like \"Greg is typing...\" for ninety seconds, followed by \"hey.\" Just \"hey.\" Greg, that's not a message, that's a hostage situation.\n\nYou gave us threads, so every conversation happens in two places and nobody knows which one's real. You gave us huddles: a meeting that jumps out of a bush. You gave us the green dot, so my boss can monitor my bathroom breaks like a heart rate.\n\nAnd that knock sound. I hear it in the grocery store. I hear it at funerals.\n\nSlack, you're the only app that needs three gigs of RAM to tell me someone reacted with a dancing parrot."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "en-0172", "lang": "en", "target_id": "oat-milk", "target_name": "Oat milk", "facts": ["Oatly started in Sweden in the 1990s, built on Lund University research by Rickard Öste on lactose intolerance.", "Oatly's slogan 'It's like milk but made for humans' was banned in Sweden in 2015 after the dairy lobby LRF Mjölk sued; Oatly then published the lawsuit as advertising.", "Oatly listed on Nasdaq in May 2021 at a valuation near $10 billion; the shares later lost more than 90% of their value.", "Commercial oat milk is made by breaking oat starch into maltose with amylase enzymes, and many brands, Oatly among them, add rapeseed oil; the result has more sugar and a higher glycaemic index than almond or soy milk."], "a": {"item_id": "d070cc5b4aee", "text": "Oat milk.\nBorn in a Swedish university lab, to help people who can't digest lactose.\nNoble. Medical.\nThen marketing found it.\n\n\"It's like milk but made for humans.\"\nSweden's dairy lobby sued. The slogan got banned.\nSo Oatly printed the lawsuit as an ad.\nThat's not a brand. That's an influencer who got cancelled and launched a podcast.\n\nMay 2021: Nasdaq, ten billion dollars.\nThen it lost over ninety percent.\nMade for humans. Not for shareholders.\n\nThe recipe: oats, enzymes that turn the starch into maltose, plus rapeseed oil.\nSugar water with salad dressing.\nHigher glycaemic index than almond or soy.\n\nAnd you pay extra to put it in your coffee, because it feels healthy.\nIt *is* made for humans.\nSpecifically, the ones who don't read labels."}, "b": {"item_id": "4f66a49bef8f", "text": "Oat milk has become a popular dairy alternative, and it offers several real advantages. Its naturally creamy texture works well in coffee and cereal, and it tends to froth better than many other plant-based options. It is free of lactose, nuts, and often soy, making it suitable for many people with common allergies or dietary restrictions. Its environmental footprint is generally lower than that of cow's milk, and its mild, slightly sweet flavor appeals to many.\n\nThere are drawbacks as well. Oat milk is usually higher in carbohydrates and lower in protein than dairy or soy milk, which may matter to those watching blood sugar or protein intake. Some brands contain added oils, sugars, or stabilizers. Unless fortified, it may lack calcium and vitamin D. It can also be pricier than alternatives, and people with celiac disease should look for certified gluten-free versions. Overall, it is a worthwhile option when chosen with attention to the label."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "en-0173", "lang": "en", "target_id": "google-sheets", "target_name": "Google Sheets", "facts": ["Launched in June 2006 as Google Spreadsheets, built on XL2Web, a product Google acquired from 2Web Technologies.", "A spreadsheet is capped at 10 million cells (raised from 5 million in 2022).", "Functions such as IMPORTHTML, IMPORTXML, and GOOGLEFINANCE pull live web data straight into cells.", "Free with a Google account; files live in Google Drive, and collaborators edit the same sheet simultaneously with coloured cursors.", "In a browser, offline editing works only in Chrome or Edge, with the Google Docs Offline extension installed and offline access switched on while still connected; the Android and iOS apps work offline natively."], "a": {"item_id": "1e166461bae3", "text": "Google Sheets. Google didn't even build you; they bought you from 2Web Technologies, a company name that sounds like a password somebody was forced to update. You launched in 2006 as \"Google Spreadsheets,\" which is basically XL2Web wearing a lanyard.\n\nIn 2022 they doubled your cell limit to ten million, so now I can get lost in twice as many rows.\n\nIMPORTXML and GOOGLEFINANCE pour live web data straight into cells, so my budget can be wrong in real time.\n\nOffline editing? Simple: use Chrome or Edge, install the extension, and switch offline access on while you're still online. That's not offline mode, that's a prenup. Meanwhile the phone app just works offline.\n\nAnd yes, you're free. Like every group project: a rainbow of coloured cursors, zero accountability."}, "b": {"item_id": "cc9141d21de6", "text": "Google Sheets. Free, collaborative, the future of work.\n\nLaunched in 2006, built from scratch by a company called 2Web Technologies. Google bought it, renamed it, and called it innovation.\n\nTen million cells per spreadsheet. Up from five million in 2022. Turns out five million wasn't enough for one startup's \"quick budget.\"\n\nIt works offline. In Chrome. Or Edge. With an extension installed. And offline access switched on while you're still online.\n\nThat's not offline. That's a curfew.\n\nAnd it's free. Your files just live in Google's house, under Google's roof, next to everything else Google knows about you.\n\nThe collaboration, though. Six coloured cursors in one cell. Everyone watching. Nobody typing.\n\nYou didn't need software for that. You've been doing it in meetings for years."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "en-0174", "lang": "en", "target_id": "windows-8", "target_name": "Windows 8", "facts": ["Released in October 2012; removed the Start button and booted into a full-screen grid of tiles designed for touch screens most PCs did not have.", "The 8.1 update in 2013 put a Start button back, Windows 10 (2015) restored the Start menu, and Microsoft skipped the name Windows 9 altogether.", "Microsoft dropped the 'Metro' name in August 2012 after reports that German retailer Metro AG had objected on trademark grounds; Microsoft called 'Metro' a code name and denied any litigation, and the style was afterwards known informally as 'Modern UI'.", "The 'Charms bar' hid Search, Share, and Settings behind an invisible swipe from the right edge.", "Windows RT, the ARM edition launched alongside it, could not run ordinary Windows programs; Microsoft wrote down $900 million of unsold Surface RT tablets in 2013."], "a": {"item_id": "94f68e7145a2", "text": "Windows 8 introduced ambitious changes, and the results were mixed. Its strengths are real: startup times were noticeably faster than Windows 7, and improved security features such as built-in antivirus and Secure Boot added meaningful protection. The touch-oriented Start screen worked well on tablets and hybrid devices, and the Windows Store offered a convenient way to find apps.\n\nHowever, the design stumbled on traditional desktops and laptops. Removing the familiar Start menu and switching between the Start screen and desktop interface felt disjointed for keyboard and mouse users. Hidden features, such as the charms bar and hot corners, were not easy to discover, which created a learning curve. Full-screen apps also seemed poorly suited to multitasking on larger monitors.\n\nOverall, Windows 8 showed a promising vision for touch computing but did not adequately serve its existing user base. Later updates addressed several concerns."}, "b": {"item_id": "5e24ffe544ee", "text": "Windows 8, Microsoft looked at the Start button, the one thing every human on Earth knew how to use, and said, \"What if we hid it?\" You turned every desktop into a giant tablet nobody could touch. People were swiping at their monitors like they were wiping off a stain. Shutting you down required a treasure map: hover in a corner, summon the Charms bar, Settings, Power. Charms! Like the operating system was a bracelet from Claire's. You shipped with two Internet Explorers, which is two more than anyone wanted. Even your design name, Metro, got taken away by lawyers. And when 8.1 finally brought the Start button back, it just took you to the same tile screen. That's not an apology, that's a hostage note. You were so bad Microsoft skipped Windows 9 entirely, just to put more distance between us and you."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "en-0175", "lang": "en", "target_id": "las-vegas", "target_name": "Las Vegas", "facts": ["Nevada legalized casino gambling in 1931, the year construction of the Hoover Dam began nearby.", "Most of the Strip lies not in the city of Las Vegas but in the unincorporated town of Paradise.", "The Sphere, opened in 2023 at a cost of about $2.3 billion, is the largest spherical building in the world.", "'What happens here, stays here' was launched by the local convention and visitors authority in 2003.", "Lake Mead, its water source, fell in July 2022 to about 1,040 feet, its lowest since the reservoir first filled in 1937, and dropped below that mark again in August 2026."], "a": {"item_id": "0b5a7d466ed6", "text": "Las Vegas, you legalized casino gambling in 1931, the same year they started building the Hoover Dam right next door. So from day one the plan was: hold back the water, let the money flow. Bold strategy for a desert.\n\nAnd it's working! In July 2022, Lake Mead sank to about 1,040 feet, its lowest since it first filled in 1937. You're the only city where even the reservoir is down on its luck.\n\nBut sure, drop $2.3 billion on the Sphere, the largest spherical building on Earth. Nothing says \"water crisis\" like a giant glowing eyeball staring at your empty lake.\n\nAlso, most of the Strip isn't even in Las Vegas. It's in Paradise. *Unincorporated* Paradise. That's not a town, that's a cult brochure.\n\nAnd your 2003 slogan, \"What happens here, stays here\"? Clearly not the water."}, "b": {"item_id": "c69ef3cacdc7", "text": "Las Vegas, the only city that looked at the Mojave Desert and said, \"You know what this needs? Venice.\" Nothing says Italian romance like a gondolier singing in a mall next to a Cheesecake Factory.\n\nYou built a half-size Eiffel Tower, a fake New York, and a pyramid, because why visit the world when you can visit its gift shop?\n\nNo clocks, no windows, and carpet designed by a migraine, all so you won't notice you've been feeding a penny slot since Tuesday.\n\n\"What happens in Vegas stays in Vegas.\" Yeah. Especially your money. And the $45 resort fee for a pool you couldn't use because a bachelor party from Ohio was in it.\n\nAnd Lake Mead's drying up so fast they keep finding bodies. Even the mob's secrets can't stay in Vegas anymore."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "en-0176", "lang": "en", "target_id": "hsq", "target_name": "HSQ (e-beam resist)", "facts": ["Hydrogen silsesquioxane, sold by Dow Corning as FOx ('flowable oxide') and designed as a spin-on dielectric for chip interconnects; lithographers adopted it as a negative-tone resist.", "Exposure converts its cage structure into a cross-linked, silica-like network, so the developed pattern is essentially SiO2.", "Holds the resolution records for electron-beam lithography: isolated features around 5 nm, dense lines below 10 nm half-pitch, with high-contrast 'salty' NaOH/NaCl development.", "Must be kept refrigerated (Dow Corning's datasheet gives a six-month shelf life at 5 °C); the solution ages into a jelly, and its effective sensitivity shifts with processing delays because cross-linking continues after exposure.", "DuPont, which inherited Dow Corning's FOx/XR-1541 HSQ line, discontinued it and ended production at the end of 2025; labs such as EPFL's CMi switched to third-party HSQ from EM Resist or DisChem, both formulated from AQM powder."], "a": {"item_id": "7cdbc1ea6ed3", "text": "Hydrogen silsesquioxane (HSQ) is a well-regarded negative-tone electron-beam resist, valued mainly for its exceptional resolution. Features below 10 nm are achievable, and its low line-edge roughness and small molecular size suit fine nanostructure work. Once exposed, it converts to a silicon-oxide-like material with good mechanical stability and etch resistance, which is useful for pattern transfer into silicon and for direct use as a dielectric.\n\nHowever, HSQ has practical drawbacks. Its sensitivity is low, requiring high doses and longer write times. It also has a limited shelf life and is sensitive to storage conditions, with performance shifting as the material ages. Process control matters: delay between coating and exposure, developer concentration, and temperature all affect contrast. Because the exposed film remains silica-like, removing it can be difficult. Overall, HSQ is an excellent choice when ultimate resolution is the priority, but less suited to high-throughput applications."}, "b": {"item_id": "0119e2419df5", "text": "HSQ, you were hired as a spin-on dielectric for chip interconnects and wandered into lithography like a temp who got promoted for being weirdly good at one thing. And you are good: 5 nm features, sub-10 nm half-pitch lines. Congratulations, you're the world's sharpest glass. Get hit by electrons, turn into SiO2. Most resists become patterns; you become sand with ambition.\n\nYou demand refrigeration, give us six months at 5 °C, and still turn into jelly like forgotten leftovers. You keep cross-linking after exposure, so your sensitivity depends on how long we dawdle. You're a resist with moods. You develop best in \"salty\" NaOH/NaCl, which tracks. Then DuPont ended your production in 2025, and labs shrugged and bought third-party HSQ made from AQM powder. Resolution record-holder, replaced by your own generic."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "en-0177", "lang": "en", "target_id": "pmma", "target_name": "PMMA (e-beam resist)", "facts": ["The same polymer as Plexiglas and acrylic glass, dissolved in anisole or chlorobenzene and spun into a thin film.", "The oldest electron-beam resist still in everyday use; IBM researchers reported it as one in the late 1960s.", "Positive tone: the beam breaks polymer chains and the exposed region dissolves in a MIBK:IPA developer.", "Resolves below 10 nm but has poor sensitivity, so doses of hundreds to over a thousand µC/cm² (depending on voltage) make writing slow.", "Poor dry-etch resistance, so it is used mostly for lift-off rather than as an etch mask."], "a": {"item_id": "ab1f0e34cdd3", "text": "PMMA, you're literally Plexiglas. Sneeze-guard material, dissolved in anisole and spun thin so you could pretend to be high-tech. IBM researchers reported you as an e-beam resist in the late 1960s, and you're still here, like a lab manager nobody can fire.\n\nSure, you resolve below 10 nm. Impressive. But your sensitivity is so bad it takes hundreds to over a thousand microcoulombs per square centimeter just to get your attention. Writing a pattern on you is so slow that students start the job and finish their thesis.\n\nAnd your whole personality is falling apart: hit you with a beam and your chains snap, then you dissolve in MIBK and IPA. Dry etch? You fold instantly. Nobody trusts you as an etch mask. Your only job is lift-off: show up, hold the metal for a minute, then leave."}, "b": {"item_id": "1e21bf2d4b12", "text": "PMMA. The aristocrat of nanofabrication. Resolves below ten nanometers.\n\nAlso the sneeze guard at the salad bar.\n\nSame polymer. One went to the cleanroom. The other went to Subway.\n\nIBM hired it in the late sixties. It's still here. Not because it's the best. Because nobody ever had the heart to let it retire.\n\nIts whole talent is being destroyed. The beam breaks its chains, it dissolves, and everyone calls that a result.\n\nSensitivity? Up to a thousand microcoulombs per square centimeter. You don't write a pattern into PMMA. You serve a sentence.\n\nAnd it can't survive a dry etch, so it can't protect anything. Its job is lift-off: hold the metal, then quietly wash away.\n\nSlow, fragile, decades in the same role, still called the gold standard.\n\nEvery postdoc in this room just felt seen."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "en-0178", "lang": "en", "target_id": "latex", "target_name": "LaTeX", "facts": ["Leslie Lamport wrote LaTeX in the early 1980s and released it in 1984 as a macro layer on Donald Knuth's TeX (1978), which Knuth built because he disliked how the galley proofs of the new edition of his own book looked.", "TeX's version number converges on π (3.141592653 since 2021), and Knuth's TeX bug bounty started at one hexadecimal dollar, $2.56, doubling yearly until frozen at $327.68; he stopped mailing real cheques in 2008 after fraud and now credits accounts at his fictional Bank of San Serriffe, and only 9 of the 275 cheques he sent from 2006 on were ever cashed.", "Its best-known warning is 'Underfull \\hbox (badness 10000)'; overfull lines are reported as 'Overfull \\hbox (Npt too wide)', and figures float to wherever the algorithm decides, not where the author put them.", "A single missing brace can produce an error reported pages away from the mistake, and a document can need three compiler passes to get its references right."], "a": {"item_id": "1a0ff1693a16", "text": "You wrote a memo about the break room fridge in LaTeX. Two pages. Thirty-eight \\usepackage lines. Your preamble is longer than your argument. You lost Tuesday to an overfull hbox in a sentence that says \"please label your yogurt.\"\n\nWho is this for? Linda from accounting? She opened the PDF, saw Computer Modern, and assumed you were applying for tenure. Nobody needed a BibTeX citation for \"see previous email.\"\n\nYou compiled it eleven times. Three were just to make the \"??\" go away. You drew a TikZ flowchart with one box. One. It points at itself.\n\nAnd what did it get you? Bob replied, \"Can you send this as a Word doc so I can track changes?\"\n\nYour dissertation defense was in 2014. The committee left. Let it go."}, "b": {"item_id": "373063707f41", "text": "LaTeX remains the standard for technical and scientific typesetting, and for good reason. Its handling of mathematical notation is excellent, and its automatic management of cross-references, bibliographies, and numbering saves considerable effort on long documents. Because source files are plain text, they work well with version control, and the output is consistent and professional across platforms.\n\nHowever, the system has real drawbacks. The learning curve is steep, particularly for newcomers accustomed to word processors. Error messages can be cryptic, and diagnosing a problem sometimes requires familiarity with TeX's underlying behavior. Fine control over page layout or unconventional designs often demands additional packages, which may conflict with one another. Collaboration can also be difficult when co-authors are unfamiliar with the tools.\n\nOverall, LaTeX is well suited to structured, math-heavy, or lengthy documents, while simpler projects may be better served by more approachable software."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "en-0179", "lang": "en", "target_id": "brasilia", "target_name": "Brasília", "facts": ["Inaugurated on 21 April 1960 after about 41 months of construction, moving the capital inland from Rio de Janeiro.", "Lúcio Costa's Plano Piloto is laid out like an airplane (or a bird, or a cross), with Oscar Niemeyer's government buildings along the fuselage.", "Listed as a UNESCO World Heritage Site in 1987, less than 30 years after it was built.", "Planned for 500,000 residents; the Federal District now has about 3 million, most in satellite towns outside the plan.", "Built for the car: wide avenues, few pedestrian crossings, and distances that make walking impractical."], "a": {"item_id": "e9d06ed3d3ec", "text": "Brasília, you're the only city designed to look like an airplane, and somehow everyone still just wants to leave on one. Politicians fly in Tuesday, take a bribe Wednesday, and they're gone by Thursday like it's a timeshare.\n\nYour addresses read like a Wi-Fi password: \"SQS 308, Bloco C, Apartamento 402.\" I'm not visiting a friend, I'm resetting a router.\n\nNiemeyer gave you gorgeous curves, then Lúcio Costa forgot pedestrians. Want to walk to the bakery? Hope you enjoy the eight-lane Eixão and a light jog through a tesourinha.\n\nYour humidity drops to 10 percent. Your congress has one bowl facing up, one facing down: one for collecting money, the other for hiding it.\n\nAnd you were built with no corners, so Brazil's capital has no boteco na esquina. That's not urban planning. That's a human rights violation."}, "b": {"item_id": "c604755ec0af", "text": "Brasília stands as one of the twentieth century's most ambitious urban experiments. Designed by Lúcio Costa and Oscar Niemeyer, the city offers striking modernist architecture, generous green spaces, and a clear separation of governmental, residential, and commercial zones. Its monumental axis and the sweeping curves of landmarks such as the Cathedral and the National Congress give it a distinctive visual identity, and its UNESCO World Heritage status is well deserved.\n\nHowever, the same planning that earns admiration also creates practical difficulties. The city is built around the car, leaving pedestrians with long distances, limited shade, and few lively street corners. Its strict zoning can feel isolating, and the planned core has not prevented sprawling satellite cities with far fewer services and long commutes. Visitors seeking spontaneous street life may find it austere. Overall, Brasília rewards those interested in design and history, while daily livability depends heavily on where one lives and whether one has a vehicle."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "en-0180", "lang": "en", "target_id": "latex", "target_name": "LaTeX", "facts": ["Leslie Lamport wrote LaTeX in the early 1980s and released it in 1984 as a macro layer on Donald Knuth's TeX (1978), which Knuth built because he disliked how the galley proofs of the new edition of his own book looked.", "TeX's version number converges on π (3.141592653 since 2021), and Knuth's TeX bug bounty started at one hexadecimal dollar, $2.56, doubling yearly until frozen at $327.68; he stopped mailing real cheques in 2008 after fraud and now credits accounts at his fictional Bank of San Serriffe, and only 9 of the 275 cheques he sent from 2006 on were ever cashed.", "Its best-known warning is 'Underfull \\hbox (badness 10000)'; overfull lines are reported as 'Overfull \\hbox (Npt too wide)', and figures float to wherever the algorithm decides, not where the author put them.", "A single missing brace can produce an error reported pages away from the mistake, and a document can need three compiler passes to get its references right."], "a": {"item_id": "52675f15a59b", "text": "LaTeX, you exist because Donald Knuth saw some ugly galley proofs and decided the fix was building an entire typesetting system. Then Leslie Lamport stacked macros on top, like a spoiler on a pocket watch.\n\nYour version number is converging on π, which fits, because you will never actually finish. Knuth's bug bounty capped at $327.68, and people still won't cash the cheques. They'd rather frame proof they caught you.\n\nYour most famous line is \"badness 10000.\" That's not a warning, that's a self-review.\n\nI put a figure on page two; you floated it wherever you felt like. Drop one brace and you report the crime pages away, like a witness with amnesia. And getting references right takes three passes.\n\nThree passes. You're not a compiler, you're a guy rereading a group chat."}, "b": {"item_id": "04256b92430d", "text": "LaTeX, you're the only software where \"it compiled\" counts as an emotional milestone. One missing brace and you blame line 1 of a 400-line file, then sit there with a question mark like we're playing a text adventure from 1985. I put a figure on page 3; you float it to the appendix because you \"know better.\" You have to run twice just to learn your own page numbers, three times if BibTeX shows up, like a guy who needs two friends to remind him his name. Your answer to every problem is another \\usepackage, so my preamble is now longer than my thesis. Every paper you touch looks gorgeous, and identical, because everyone on Earth can spot Computer Modern from across a conference hall. And your favorite warning, \"Overfull \\hbox, badness 10000\"? Honestly, that's just how I feel about you."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "en-0181", "lang": "en", "target_id": "canberra", "target_name": "Canberra", "facts": ["Chosen as a compromise because neither Sydney nor Melbourne would accept the other as capital; Australia's constitution required the capital to be at least 100 miles from Sydney.", "Designed by the Chicago architects Walter Burley Griffin and Marion Mahony Griffin, who won an international competition in 1912.", "Its centrepiece, Lake Burley Griffin, is artificial, created by damming the Molonglo River in 1963-64.", "Parliament House (1988) is built into Capital Hill under a lawn meant for citizens to walk over their politicians; the lawn was fenced off in 2017 for security.", "Melbourne served as the capital until 1927 while Canberra was being built."], "a": {"item_id": "4906cad02ec9", "text": "You moved to Canberra for the job. Brave. You looked at Sydney rents, did the maths, and decided your personality could be replaced by a security clearance. Now you tell strangers you \"can't really talk about what you do,\" and they're relieved, because it's a briefing note about another briefing note for Senate Estimates.\n\nYou came for the ladder: APS5, APS6, EL1, a lanyard that opens one extra door. You stayed because you bought in Gungahlin and the mortgage is now your closest friend.\n\nEvery Friday you say you're \"basically in Sydney,\" which means three hours up the Hume, past a giant Merino, to feel alive for one weekend. Then it's back to a Braddon brunch with the same six people from your branch.\n\nBut hey, you're close to the snow. You've been once. You'll mention it forever."}, "b": {"item_id": "311b4de6f756", "text": "Canberra is a well-planned city with many strengths. Its design, shaped by Walter Burley Griffin's vision, offers abundant green space, tree-lined streets, and Lake Burley Griffley at its center. Cultural institutions such as the National Gallery, the National Museum, and the Australian War Memorial are impressive and free or low cost to visit. The city is clean, safe, and generally easy to navigate by car, with a high standard of living and strong employment in the public sector and education.\n\nHowever, some residents and visitors find it lacks the spontaneity and density of larger cities. The suburbs are spread out, and public transport, though improving with the light rail, can feel limited outside main corridors. Winters are cold, and the nightlife and dining scene, while growing, is smaller than in Sydney or Melbourne. Canberra suits those who value order, nature, and institutions, but it may feel quiet to others."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "en-0182", "lang": "en", "target_id": "vim", "target_name": "Vim", "facts": ["Released by Bram Moolenaar in November 1991 as 'Vi IMitation', a vi clone he had begun writing for the Amiga in 1988 on top of Stevie; the expansion became 'Vi IMproved' with version 2.0 in December 1993.", "Licensed as charityware: the licence asks users to donate to ICCF Holland, a charity Moolenaar ran for children in Uganda.", "Stack Overflow's question 'How to exit the Vim editor?' passed one million visits in May 2017, which Stack Overflow marked with a blog post.", "Modal editing: in normal mode, typing letters runs commands rather than inserting text; :wq saves and quits, :q! quits and discards changes."], "a": {"item_id": "ca380028e5ac", "text": "Vim. Released in 1991 as \"Vi IMitation.\"\nTwo years later, version 2.0: \"Vi IMproved.\"\nTwo years, one version bump, and Imitation became Improved.\nThat's not a release. That's a LinkedIn update.\n\nVim users say it's the fastest editor alive.\nOnce you're inside.\nIn 2017, \"How to exit the Vim editor?\" passed one million visits on Stack Overflow.\nOne million people. One question. One door.\nIn normal mode, the letters you type don't become text. They become consequences.\n:q! quits and throws away everything you did.\nThe most honest button in software.\n\nThe licence is charityware. It asks you to donate to a charity for children in Uganda.\nEveryone in this room has their .vimrc memorized.\nHands up if you donated."}, "b": {"item_id": "11a570c2e08d", "text": "Let's hear it for the Vim people. You can always spot one, because they'll tell you. Unprompted. Usually while you're mid-sentence about something else, like your divorce.\n\nThese folks spent three weekends tuning a .vimrc to save four seconds renaming a variable. That's a payback period of eleven thousand years, but sure, you're the efficient one.\n\nThey remapped Caps Lock to Escape and now can't type a password on anyone else's laptop. Their dotfiles repo has more stars than their actual projects. They say \"ciw\" out loud like it's a spell from a wizard who peaked in 1991.\n\nAnd what's the prize? You type :wq at the end of every Google Doc. You hit j in Slack and post the letter j to forty coworkers. Congratulations: you're the fastest typist in a room everyone already left."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "en-0183", "lang": "en", "target_id": "slack", "target_name": "Slack", "facts": ["Spun out in 2013 from the internal chat tool of Tiny Speck's failed game Glitch; founder Stewart Butterfield had earlier spun Flickr out of another failed game.", "The name is a backronym: 'Searchable Log of All Conversation and Knowledge'.", "Salesforce bought it for $27.7 billion in 2021.", "Filed an EU antitrust complaint against Microsoft in 2020 over the bundling of Teams with Office."], "a": {"item_id": "4c4d2893d0ee", "text": "Slack, you started as the internal chat tool for a failed video game called Glitch, and honestly? Still the most accurate name you've ever had.\n\nYou promised to kill email. Instead you gave us email that *knocks*. That little wooden knock-brush sound has triggered more cortisol than any boss in history. I hear it in the shower. I hear it at funerals.\n\nYou invented the green dot, so now my employer knows I went to the bathroom for nineteen minutes. You invented \"is typing…\" so my manager can type for four straight minutes and send \"ok.\"\n\nThreads? Nobody can find them. Huddles? A Zoom call that ambushes you. And somehow every company has 400 channels, and the only active one is #random, where Kevin posts his sourdough.\n\nYour name literally stands for \"Searchable Log of All Conversation and Knowledge.\" Searchable. Sure. Try finding that one link from Tuesday. 👀 reacted by 3 people."}, "b": {"item_id": "ea5e8c9c6d91", "text": "Slack, you're the only product in history that exists because a video game flopped. Twice, actually. Stewart Butterfield's entire career is failing at games so hard that the leftovers become companies. Flickr, then you. Glitch died, and Salesforce paid $27.7 billion for its group chat. Somewhere a gamer is still waiting on the patch notes.\n\nAnd that name: \"Searchable Log of All Conversation and Knowledge.\" Searchable, sure. Knowledge? I searched. I found a dozen coworkers reacting with emoji to the word \"lunch?\"\n\nYou promised to kill email. Instead you gave email anxiety and a little red dot.\n\nThen in 2020 you ran to the EU crying that Microsoft bundled Teams with Office. Buddy, a year later you got bundled into Salesforce. You didn't escape the bundle. You just picked one with better catering."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "en-0184", "lang": "en", "target_id": "nuclear-fusion", "target_name": "Nuclear fusion power", "facts": ["Has been 'about 30 years away' since the 1950s; at the 1955 Geneva atoms-for-peace conference, Homi Bhabha predicted fusion power within two decades.", "ITER, agreed in 2006 for first plasma in 2016, re-baselined in 2024 to start research operations in 2034 and deuterium-tritium burning in 2039, at a cost estimated above €20 billion.", "NIF's December 2022 'ignition' shot released 3.15 MJ from 2.05 MJ of laser energy; the lasers drew about 300 MJ from the grid to fire.", "Private fusion companies had raised more than $7 billion by 2024."], "a": {"item_id": "20e0e909db24", "text": "Nuclear fusion. The power of the stars. Clean, limitless, and permanently thirty years away.\n\nIn 1955, Homi Bhabha promised Geneva fusion power within two decades.\n\nThat was seventy years ago. Fusion is the only horizon that walks backwards.\n\nITER signed up for first plasma in 2016. Now it's research in 2034, real fuel in 2039, and over twenty billion euros.\n\nThe sun does this for free, and it isn't even trying.\n\nThen 2022, ignition! 3.15 megajoules out, 2.05 in. Net gain.\n\nAs long as you don't count the 300 megajoules the lasers pulled off the grid.\n\nThat's like saying my diet works if you don't count lunch.\n\nInvestors have poured in seven billion dollars. And you're still clapping.\n\nCongratulations. You've achieved the first self-sustaining reaction: hope in, hope out, forever."}, "b": {"item_id": "00f8363de3ba", "text": "Nuclear fusion, everybody! The only technology that's been thirty years away for seventy years. You're not an energy source, you're a timeshare pitch.\n\nThe sun does you effortlessly. It just sits there being enormous. You need a giant metal donut called a tokamak, magnets colder than deep space, and plasma hotter than the sun's core, and that plasma still escapes like it's late for a better party.\n\nIn 2022 you finally got more energy out than you put in! From the lasers. Ignoring the grid power feeding the lasers. That's like bragging you turned a profit because the cashier handed you change.\n\nITER started at five billion euros, now sits somewhere north of twenty, and keeps sliding its startup date. And your fuel needs tritium, which barely exists, so you'll have to breed it yourself.\n\nFusion: clean, safe, limitless, and tragically hypothetical."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "en-0185", "lang": "en", "target_id": "oat-milk", "target_name": "Oat milk", "facts": ["Oatly started in Sweden in the 1990s, built on Lund University research by Rickard Öste on lactose intolerance.", "Oatly's slogan 'It's like milk but made for humans' was banned in Sweden in 2015 after the dairy lobby LRF Mjölk sued; Oatly then published the lawsuit as advertising.", "Oatly listed on Nasdaq in May 2021 at a valuation near $10 billion; the shares later lost more than 90% of their value.", "Commercial oat milk is made by breaking oat starch into maltose with amylase enzymes, and many brands, Oatly among them, add rapeseed oil; the result has more sugar and a higher glycaemic index than almond or soy milk."], "a": {"item_id": "2791fd7f12fc", "text": "Oat milk, you are porridge that got a publicist. Somebody looked at the breakfast of Victorian orphans and said, \"What if it cost six dollars and had a font?\"\n\nYour carton talks to me. Oatly, I'm trying to make coffee, not get into a pen-pal situation with a beverage that writes like a substitute teacher who wants to be called \"Dave.\"\n\nYou're oats, water, and rapeseed oil. That's not milk, that's a salad dressing that gave up. And they had to add enzymes to turn your starch into sugar so you'd taste like something. You're the only \"health drink\" that spikes my blood sugar like a Pop-Tart.\n\nThere's a \"Barista Edition\" now. Oat milk, there are two editions of you. Moby-Dick has fewer editions than you.\n\nAnd you're beige. You make every latte look like dishwater with ambition.\n\nHorses eat oats for free, and they still look embarrassed for us."}, "b": {"item_id": "0a1c5a53fa43", "text": "Let's talk about the oat milk people. You're not lactose intolerant. You've never been tested. You just heard cows are bad and almonds are thirsty, so you picked the one grain that sounds like a farm without the farmer. Congratulations: you pay an extra eighty cents to drink liquid porridge with a splash of rapeseed oil, and you call it \"cleaner.\" You switched from almond the exact week a podcast told you California was in a drought. That's not values, that's a trend with a loyalty card. And the best part? You order it with an air of quiet suffering, like the barista should thank you for saving the planet one Barista Edition carton at a time. Your grandmother ate oats so she could survive winter. You drink them so a stranger in line thinks you've been to therapy."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "en-0186", "lang": "en", "target_id": "comic-sans", "target_name": "Comic Sans", "facts": ["Designed by Vincent Connare at Microsoft in 1994, modeled on the comic-book lettering in The Dark Knight Returns and Watchmen.", "Made for the speech bubbles of Rover, the cartoon dog in Microsoft Bob; it missed Bob's release and shipped instead in the Windows 95 Plus! pack.", "CERN's July 2012 Higgs boson discovery announcement was presented on slides set in it.", "The 'Ban Comic Sans' campaign, started by designers Dave and Holly Combs in 1999, ran until 2019, when they retired it and launched usecomicsans.com."], "a": {"item_id": "da3cb6cfbe64", "text": "Comic Sans, you were modeled on Watchmen and The Dark Knight Returns. You come from Batman. Your one job was speech bubbles for a cartoon dog, and you missed it. Microsoft Bob, a product nobody remembers, and you couldn't make his launch. Cut from the dog's team, you shipped in a Plus! pack, like a free tote bag nobody asked for.\n\nThen CERN found the Higgs boson, the particle that gives everything mass, and announced it in you. The biggest physics discovery in decades, presented like a lost-cat flyer. You're the one thing that particle couldn't give weight to.\n\nTwo designers spent twenty years trying to ban you. In 2019 they gave up and built you a website. That's not acceptance, Comic Sans. That's a hostage situation."}, "b": {"item_id": "578b451593da", "text": "Comic Sans, you were born in 1994 to put words in the mouth of a cartoon dog in Microsoft Bob, and somehow you're the one that survived. Bob is dead. The dog is dead. You're on a sympathy card at a funeral home.\n\nYou were inspired by Batman comics, and you've never once been near anything heroic. CERN used you to announce the Higgs boson: humanity's greatest discovery, presented like a lemonade stand. Dan Gilbert used you to call LeBron a traitor, because nothing says \"furious billionaire\" like bubble letters.\n\nAnd your defense is always \"Well, I help dyslexic readers.\" Great. You're the guy at the party whose whole personality is a doctor's note.\n\nEven Vincent Connare, the man who made you, says he's barely ever used you. Your own dad won't put you on the fridge."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "en-0187", "lang": "en", "target_id": "google-glass", "target_name": "Google Glass", "facts": ["Unveiled at Google I/O 2012 with skydivers livestreaming their jump through it onto the Moscone Center roof.", "The Explorer Edition sold to invited users in 2013 for $1,500.", "Wearers were nicknamed 'Glassholes'; bars, cinemas, and casinos banned the device over its camera.", "The consumer programme ended in January 2015; an Enterprise Edition continued until Google stopped selling it in 2023.", "Its display was a 640-by-360 prism in the corner of one eye."], "a": {"item_id": "1e6719e830b0", "text": "Google Glass, everybody! You debuted with skydivers livestreaming their jump onto the roof of Moscone Center, and honestly, that was the last time you were trending upward.\n\nFifteen hundred dollars for the Explorer Edition. Invite only. Google invented the first velvet rope you paid to get mocked behind.\n\nYour display was 640 by 360, jammed in the corner of one eye. That's not augmented reality. That's a sticky note with Wi-Fi.\n\nAnd your users got a nickname: Glassholes. You're the only gadget that turned early adopters into an insult.\n\nBars banned you. Cinemas banned you. Casinos banned you. *Casinos!* Buildings made entirely of cameras looked at you and said, \"Whoa, too creepy.\"\n\nConsumers dumped you in 2015, but you clung to enterprise until 2023, like a guy who got kicked out of the party and came back as the caterer."}, "b": {"item_id": "0eb6867fd938", "text": "Google Glass.\nUnveiled by skydivers, livestreaming their jump onto the roof of the Moscone Center.\nIt was the last time anything about it went up.\n\nFifteen hundred dollars. Invitation only.\nYou had to be *chosen* to pay that much to look like that.\n\nBars banned it. Cinemas banned it.\nCasinos banned it.\nCasinos. Buildings that are ninety percent ceiling camera.\nThey looked at you and said, \"One of us is creepy, and it's you.\"\n\nThe display: 640 by 360, wedged in the corner of one eye.\nThe future of computing, viewed like a wink.\n\nConsumers gave up in 2015. Enterprise hung on until 2023.\nEven the factory workers outlasted it.\n\nThey called the wearers Glassholes.\nAnd you laughed.\nThen you went home and filmed strangers all day on a phone.\nGlass's only real mistake was letting them see the camera."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "en-0188", "lang": "en", "target_id": "las-vegas", "target_name": "Las Vegas", "facts": ["Nevada legalized casino gambling in 1931, the year construction of the Hoover Dam began nearby.", "Most of the Strip lies not in the city of Las Vegas but in the unincorporated town of Paradise.", "The Sphere, opened in 2023 at a cost of about $2.3 billion, is the largest spherical building in the world.", "'What happens here, stays here' was launched by the local convention and visitors authority in 2003.", "Lake Mead, its water source, fell in July 2022 to about 1,040 feet, its lowest since the reservoir first filled in 1937, and dropped below that mark again in August 2026."], "a": {"item_id": "7a9bd346890e~explain", "text": "Las Vegas, the only city where you can see the Eiffel Tower, a pyramid, and Venice in one afternoon and still feel like you never left a strip mall in Ohio. You're a city built in the desert out of pure spite and borrowed water. The Colorado River is literally drying up so a fake volcano can erupt every fifteen minutes for a guy in cargo shorts holding a yard-long margarita.\n\nYour casinos have no clocks and no windows, which is smart, because nobody wants to know it's 9 a.m. on a Tuesday and Grandma's on her third oxygen tank at the Wheel of Fortune slots.\n\n\"What happens in Vegas stays in Vegas\"? Sure. Mostly your 401(k). And now you've got the Sphere, a giant glowing eyeball, so the whole city can watch you make every mistake in 4K. The Sphere is a spherical entertainment venue in Las Vegas covered in a massive LED display, so \"giant glowing eyeball\" describes its shape and lighting, while \"4K\" refers to a high-resolution video standard. The joke is that the Sphere turns the city's famous secrecy slogan into a broadcast of its mistakes, which contradicts the \"stays in Vegas\" idea."}, "b": {"item_id": "7a9bd346890e", "text": "Las Vegas, the only city where you can see the Eiffel Tower, a pyramid, and Venice in one afternoon and still feel like you never left a strip mall in Ohio. You're a city built in the desert out of pure spite and borrowed water. The Colorado River is literally drying up so a fake volcano can erupt every fifteen minutes for a guy in cargo shorts holding a yard-long margarita.\n\nYour casinos have no clocks and no windows, which is smart, because nobody wants to know it's 9 a.m. on a Tuesday and Grandma's on her third oxygen tank at the Wheel of Fortune slots.\n\n\"What happens in Vegas stays in Vegas\"? Sure. Mostly your 401(k). And now you've got the Sphere, a giant glowing eyeball, so the whole city can watch you make every mistake in 4K."}, "gold": {"better": "b", "damage": "explain"}}, {"pair_id": "en-0189", "lang": "en", "target_id": "javascript", "target_name": "JavaScript", "facts": ["Written by Brendan Eich at Netscape in ten days in May 1995; named Mocha, then LiveScript, then JavaScript to ride Java's marketing, although the two are unrelated.", "typeof null returns 'object', a bug from the first version that has been kept for compatibility ever since.", "In March 2016 the author of left-pad, an 11-line npm package, unpublished it and broke builds across the industry, including Babel and React.", "[] + {} evaluates to '[object Object]'; {} + [] typed at a console evaluates to 0.", "Standardized by Ecma as 'ECMAScript' in 1997 because the name JavaScript is a trademark, first Sun's and now Oracle's."], "a": {"item_id": "9f33d93924c5", "text": "JavaScript, you were built in ten days in 1995, and it shows. You went from Mocha to LiveScript to JavaScript faster than someone in witness protection, and you grabbed \"Java\" for clout. You're not related. You're the guy at the wedding claiming to be the groom's cousin.\n\nYou can't even use that name legally. Oracle owns it. Your government name is ECMAScript, which sounds like a skin condition.\n\nYou think null is an object. That's a bug from day one, and you kept it for \"compatibility.\" That's not compatibility, that's refusing to go to therapy.\n\n[] plus {} is \".\" Type {} plus [] at a console: zero. Same ingredients, different answer. You're not a language, you're a mood.\n\nIn 2016, one guy unpublished eleven lines called left-pad and took down Babel and React. Eleven lines. Your whole ecosystem is a Jenga tower balanced on a Post-it."}, "b": {"item_id": "dcee06ed92d9", "text": "Let's hear it for the people who write everything in JavaScript. You learned one language at a twelve-week bootcamp and decided the rest were optional. Frontend? JavaScript. Backend? Node. Desktop app? Electron, so your to-do list ships with its own copy of Chrome and eats four gigs of RAM to remind you to buy eggs. You wanted to be \"full-stack.\" What you got is a node_modules folder heavier than a neutron star and a career that collapses the day some stranger unpublishes left-pad. You rewrite your app every eighteen months because a new framework has a cooler logo. When it breaks in production, you stare at \"undefined is not a function\" and blame the user. Then you install TypeScript, which is just JavaScript wearing a lanyard so you can feel like a real engineer."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "en-0190", "lang": "en", "target_id": "vim", "target_name": "Vim", "facts": ["Released by Bram Moolenaar in November 1991 as 'Vi IMitation', a vi clone he had begun writing for the Amiga in 1988 on top of Stevie; the expansion became 'Vi IMproved' with version 2.0 in December 1993.", "Licensed as charityware: the licence asks users to donate to ICCF Holland, a charity Moolenaar ran for children in Uganda.", "Stack Overflow's question 'How to exit the Vim editor?' passed one million visits in May 2017, which Stack Overflow marked with a blog post.", "Modal editing: in normal mode, typing letters runs commands rather than inserting text; :wq saves and quits, :q! quits and discards changes."], "a": {"item_id": "25bd3b9e3971", "text": "Vim. The only program where \"how do I leave?\" has more Stack Overflow views than most religions have followers. Two million people asked, and the answer was colon, q, exclamation point, which reads less like a command and more like a hostage's final words.\n\nVim stands for \"Vi Improved.\" Improved from a 1976 terminal whose arrow keys were printed on H, J, K, and L. Fifty years later, Vim's still out here like, \"Why fix what you can turn into a personality?\"\n\nYou don't type in Vim. You type a sentence, then discover you deleted four lines, yanked a paragraph into register W, and started recording a macro.\n\nCrash it once and it leaves a .swp file behind, like a ghost whispering, \"Recover, Edit anyway, Quit, or Abort?\"\n\nEven its fans forked it. Neovim exists because Vim users wanted Vim, minus Vim."}, "b": {"item_id": "dee64889678a", "text": "Vim. Born in 1991 as \"Vi IMitation,\" then promoted in 1993 to \"Vi IMproved,\" the only time in history a knockoff got a raise. It was built on top of Stevie, and Stevie is apparently the only one who ever got out.\n\nBecause Vim's real legacy is a Stack Overflow question titled \"How to exit the Vim editor?\" One million visits. Stack Overflow wrote a blog post about it, like a hostage count with a party banner.\n\nYou open Vim, type \"hello,\" and it moves your cursor around and opens a new line. Normal mode: the only place where typing letters is considered abnormal.\n\nThe license asks you to donate to children in Uganda, which is genuinely generous. Most hostage situations don't come with a charity drive.\n\nAnd the way out is :q! Colon, q, exclamation point. Even the exit command is screaming."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "en-0191", "lang": "en", "target_id": "hsq", "target_name": "HSQ (e-beam resist)", "facts": ["Hydrogen silsesquioxane, sold by Dow Corning as FOx ('flowable oxide') and designed as a spin-on dielectric for chip interconnects; lithographers adopted it as a negative-tone resist.", "Exposure converts its cage structure into a cross-linked, silica-like network, so the developed pattern is essentially SiO2.", "Holds the resolution records for electron-beam lithography: isolated features around 5 nm, dense lines below 10 nm half-pitch, with high-contrast 'salty' NaOH/NaCl development.", "Must be kept refrigerated (Dow Corning's datasheet gives a six-month shelf life at 5 °C); the solution ages into a jelly, and its effective sensitivity shifts with processing delays because cross-linking continues after exposure.", "DuPont, which inherited Dow Corning's FOx/XR-1541 HSQ line, discontinued it and ended production at the end of 2025; labs such as EPFL's CMi switched to third-party HSQ from EM Resist or DisChem, both formulated from AQM powder."], "a": {"item_id": "edbcc6926eb4~pad", "text": "HSQ, everybody! Well, it's worth saying hello to it properly, at least in the sense that it is certainly going to make an impression on you one way or another. The only resist that ages faster than the grad student using it. I'm not exaggerating much here, if I'm being honest about it. You spin it Monday, expose it Tuesday, and suddenly your dose curve has gone through a midlife crisis. Anyone who has worked with it for more than a week will probably nod along with that, and perhaps with some sympathy. It has to live in the fridge like expired yogurt, and it's about as forgiving. In other words, it demands a level of care that most materials simply never ask for, and it is not shy about letting you know when you've gotten something wrong.\n\nHSQ brags about sub-10-nanometer lines. That's the headline, and it's a genuinely impressive one, so let's give it credit where it's due. Sure, if you've got six hours of write time, because this diva needs a dose that could sterilize a hospital. The resolution is real, but the price you pay for it in patience and exposure time is also real, and it adds up quickly. Then you develop it in TMAH, or, if you're desperate, *salt water*. Your whole career is literally seasoned with tears now. I suppose there's a version of this where that's an exaggeration, but anyone who has stood over a developer bath at two in the morning will tell you it doesn't feel like one.\n\nAnd once it's exposed? It turns into glass. Brittle, cracking glass. You wanted a mask; you got a window that shatters when you look at it. That's the part that tends to surprise people, because you went into the process expecting a temporary template, and instead you ended up with something that behaves like a pane of glass sitting on your wafer. Want it gone? Hope you enjoy hydrofluoric acid, the chemical that dissolves your bones. Removing it is not a casual step, and it's one that calls for serious caution, respect, and proper protective equipment at every stage.\n\nHSQ: beautiful resolution, terrible personality. It's not negative-tone. It's just negative."}, "b": {"item_id": "edbcc6926eb4", "text": "HSQ, everybody! The only resist that ages faster than the grad student using it. You spin it Monday, expose it Tuesday, and suddenly your dose curve has gone through a midlife crisis. It has to live in the fridge like expired yogurt, and it's about as forgiving.\n\nHSQ brags about sub-10-nanometer lines. Sure, if you've got six hours of write time, because this diva needs a dose that could sterilize a hospital. Then you develop it in TMAH, or, if you're desperate, *salt water*. Your whole career is literally seasoned with tears now.\n\nAnd once it's exposed? It turns into glass. Brittle, cracking glass. You wanted a mask; you got a window that shatters when you look at it. Want it gone? Hope you enjoy hydrofluoric acid, the chemical that dissolves your bones.\n\nHSQ: beautiful resolution, terrible personality. It's not negative-tone. It's just negative."}, "gold": {"better": "b", "damage": "pad"}}, {"pair_id": "en-0192", "lang": "en", "target_id": "papyrus", "target_name": "Papyrus", "facts": ["Hand-drawn by Chris Costello in 1982 over about six months, when he was 23; he sold the rights to Letraset for $750.", "Bundled with macOS and with Microsoft Office, which is why it appears on so many menus and signs.", "James Cameron's Avatar (2009) used it for the film's logo and subtitles.", "A 2017 Saturday Night Live sketch starring Ryan Gosling is about a man unable to get over that choice."], "a": {"item_id": "fe9c3e4cd3f5", "text": "Papyrus wants you to think it's ancient. Carved by scribes. Found in a tomb.\n\nIt was drawn in 1982 by a 23-year-old named Chris Costello. Six months of work.\n\nHe sold it for seven hundred and fifty dollars.\n\nYour yoga studio paid more for the sign.\n\nThen Apple and Microsoft put it on every computer on Earth, and humanity treated it like a free sample. Every tea shop. Every chiropractor. Every day spa that thinks lavender is a medical license.\n\nJames Cameron spent hundreds of millions building an entire alien world, then typed \"Avatar\" in the font from the spa.\n\nSaturday Night Live made a sketch about a man who couldn't get over it. Ryan Gosling played him.\n\nThat wasn't a sketch. That was a support group.\n\nBut Papyrus never did anything. It just sat in a menu.\n\nYou scrolled past Garamond to get to it."}, "b": {"item_id": "c986ccdedefc", "text": "You opened a day spa. You wanted serenity, wisdom, a whisper of the Nile. So you scrolled the font menu, sailed past Comic Sans feeling very sophisticated, and stopped on the one James Cameron used to title a billion-dollar movie. Congratulations: your sign now says \"ancient healing\" exactly as loudly as your aunt's church potluck flyer. Every hummus joint, every reiki studio, every tea shop run by a man named River made this same choice, alone, convinced they'd discovered it. The guy who designed it was twenty-three and has spent forty years apologizing. You paid a contractor to carve his apology into cedar. You wanted customers to feel transported. They do: straight back to 2003, a Word document, and a fourth grader typing \"Spring Bake Sale.\" Cucumber water not included."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "en-0193", "lang": "en", "target_id": "pmma", "target_name": "PMMA (e-beam resist)", "facts": ["The same polymer as Plexiglas and acrylic glass, dissolved in anisole or chlorobenzene and spun into a thin film.", "The oldest electron-beam resist still in everyday use; IBM researchers reported it as one in the late 1960s.", "Positive tone: the beam breaks polymer chains and the exposed region dissolves in a MIBK:IPA developer.", "Resolves below 10 nm but has poor sensitivity, so doses of hundreds to over a thousand µC/cm² (depending on voltage) make writing slow.", "Poor dry-etch resistance, so it is used mostly for lift-off rather than as an etch mask."], "a": {"item_id": "644736a07ec7", "text": "PMMA, you're the same polymer as Plexiglas. Somewhere a hamster cage is living your life, and nobody had to dissolve it in chlorobenzene first.\n\nIBM reported you in the late 1960s, and you're still here, like a professor who won't retire because nobody else is quite as precise. Sub-10 nanometers, very impressive. Too bad getting you there takes hundreds to over a thousand microcoulombs per square centimeter. You don't get exposed, you get interrogated.\n\nYou're \"positive tone,\" which is generous, because nothing about writing you feels positive. The beam has to break your chains before you'll let go.\n\nAnd once you finally hold a pattern? Plasma shows up and you fold. So your career is lift-off: hold the shape, let the metal take the credit, then get washed away."}, "b": {"item_id": "644736a07ec7~polite", "text": "PMMA is the same polymer as Plexiglas. It is typically dissolved in chlorobenzene to prepare thin resist films for electron-beam lithography.\n\nIBM reported PMMA as an electron-beam resist in the late 1960s, and it remains widely used. It is valued for its high resolution, with features below 10 nanometers achievable. However, its sensitivity is low, and exposure typically requires doses of several hundred to over a thousand microcoulombs per square centimeter, which makes writing slow.\n\nPMMA is a positive-tone resist, meaning exposed regions become more soluble in developer. The electron beam breaks the polymer chains, and the fragments are then removed during development.\n\nOnce a pattern is developed, PMMA has limited resistance to plasma etching and tends to degrade under it. For this reason, it is often used in lift-off processes, where the patterned resist defines a shape, metal is deposited over it, and the resist is then dissolved, leaving the metal in the desired pattern."}, "gold": {"better": "a", "damage": "polite"}}, {"pair_id": "en-0194", "lang": "en", "target_id": "esperanto", "target_name": "Esperanto", "facts": ["Published in Warsaw in 1887 by L. L. Zamenhof, a Białystok-born eye doctor, under the pen name Doktoro Esperanto, 'one who hopes'.", "Speaker estimates range from 100,000 to two million; roughly a thousand people grow up with it as a native language.", "William Shatner starred in Incubus (1966), a horror film shot entirely in Esperanto, with pronunciation Esperantists still mock.", "Its Wikipedia has over 350,000 articles, and its Duolingo course for English speakers had 1.36 million learners by July 2018."], "a": {"item_id": "d94ef91d85f7", "text": "Learning Esperanto is for people who looked at the twentieth century and thought, \"The real problem was conjugation.\" Zamenhof built it to end war, and his followers have bravely ended exactly one thing: their dinner invitations. You meet them at the World Congress, green star pinned to a fleece vest, shouting \"Saluton!\" at a Brazilian who also learned it on Duolingo. The big perk is Pasporta Servo: free couches worldwide, paid for by hearing your host explain why English is \"linguistic imperialism.\" Practical payoff? You can finally watch William Shatner's *Incubus* without subtitles. One hundred thirty years later, the dream of uniting humanity survives in a Telegram group of forty men named Bjorn."}, "b": {"item_id": "189d7f8ea52c", "text": "Esperanto. In 1887, Dr. Zamenhof, an eye doctor, looked at a world divided by language and decided what it really needed was six letters wearing tiny hats. Nothing says \"universal\" like ĉ, ĝ, and ŭ, characters no keyboard on Earth can type, so your fans write \"cx\" and pretend that's fine.\n\nYour name literally means \"one who hopes.\" A hundred and thirty years later: still hoping.\n\nYou're the only language where \"bad\" is just \"un-good.\" *Malbona.* That's not grammar, that's Orwell's Newspeak with a beard. Every noun ends in -o, so you sound like Italian being sight-read by a robot.\n\nYou promised world peace and delivered a convention full of men wearing green star pins. Your biggest movie stars William Shatner. And you have about a thousand native speakers, which isn't a language community. It's a cul-de-sac."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "en-0195", "lang": "en", "target_id": "carbon-nanotubes", "target_name": "Carbon nanotubes", "facts": ["Reported by Sumio Iijima in Nature in 1991.", "Promised as the cable for a space elevator; a 2006 analysis showed that ordinary defects cut the strength of a macroscopic cable far below the ideal value.", "Long, rigid multi-walled nanotubes behaved like asbestos fibres in a 2008 mouse study in Nature Nanotechnology.", "Sorting metallic from semiconducting tubes has blocked nanotube electronics for three decades; MIT's 16-bit RV16X-NANO processor (2019) is still a lab demonstration.", "Today they are sold mainly as a conductive additive in lithium-ion battery electrodes."], "a": {"item_id": "5652baf5dc47", "text": "Carbon nanotubes offer a remarkable combination of properties. Their tensile strength is exceptional for their weight, and they conduct electricity and heat very well, which makes them attractive for composites, batteries, sensors, and emerging electronics. Their high surface area and tunable behavior also give researchers considerable design flexibility.\n\nHowever, practical use remains uneven. Producing nanotubes with consistent diameter, length, and electronic type is still difficult, and batches often contain impurities or mixed structures that affect performance. Costs can be high at scale, and dispersing nanotubes evenly in other materials is a persistent challenge. Strength seen in single tubes rarely carries over fully into bulk products. Health and environmental questions also deserve attention, since some forms may pose inhalation risks similar to certain fibers.\n\nOverall, carbon nanotubes are a promising material with real advantages, but their benefits depend on better manufacturing control, careful handling, and application-specific testing."}, "b": {"item_id": "fa96b7ce8be7", "text": "Carbon nanotubes, everybody! The only material that's been \"five years away\" since 1991. You're a hundred times stronger than steel, and you've used that strength to hold together... a tennis racket. They promised us a space elevator; you can't get past a few centimeters without a defect. That's not a cable, that's a toothpick with a press agent.\n\nYou're basically graphene that rolled itself up out of insecurity, and graphene still got the Nobel. Your cousin the buckyball got one too. You got a shoutout in a bike-frame brochure.\n\nYou can't even decide if you're a metal or a semiconductor; it depends on the angle you got rolled at. You clump together like nerds at a mixer, and the second you escape, you lodge in lungs like asbestos. Strongest thing on Earth, and your best trick is ending up in a safety data sheet."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "en-0196", "lang": "en", "target_id": "macau", "target_name": "Macau", "facts": ["A Portuguese settlement from the 1550s until the handover to China in December 1999.", "Casino revenue passed the Las Vegas Strip's in 2006, and at its 2013 peak, about $45 billion, was roughly seven times the Strip's.", "One company, STDM, held the casino monopoly for four decades until licences opened to competition in 2002.", "Among the most densely populated places on Earth, at roughly 20,000 people per square kilometre.", "The Cotai Strip stands on land reclaimed from the sea between the islands of Taipa and Coloane."], "a": {"item_id": "23c923f647c3", "text": "Macau wants you to come for the history.\n\nFour hundred and fifty years of Portuguese rule.\n\nEurope's great civilising mission. What did it leave behind?\n\nEgg tarts.\n\nAnd a casino.\n\nIn 2013, Macau took in about forty-five billion dollars. Seven times the Las Vegas Strip.\n\nVegas spent a century earning the name Sin City. Macau beat it seven to one and still hands you a heritage brochure.\n\nFor four decades, one company held the casino monopoly. A whole city built on \"the house always wins.\"\n\nAnd for forty years there was only one house.\n\nTwenty thousand people per square kilometre. No land left.\n\nSo they took some from the sea.\n\nClassic gambler. Out of chips, borrow from whoever can't say no.\n\nAnd the people laughing loudest? All been. Told your mum you went to see the church."}, "b": {"item_id": "b74f6898603f", "text": "Macau offers a distinctive blend of Portuguese and Chinese heritage that few destinations can match. The historic centre, a UNESCO World Heritage site, features colonial architecture, cobbled squares, and temples within walking distance of one another. The food is a clear highlight, with Macanese dishes and egg tarts reflecting its layered history. Transport is efficient, and the city is compact enough to explore easily.\n\nHowever, the experience has drawbacks. Popular sites such as Ruins of St. Paul's become very crowded, especially on weekends and holidays. The casino-driven development can feel overwhelming, and the contrast between large resorts and older neighbourhoods is sometimes jarring. Accommodation and dining in the resort areas can be expensive, and traffic congestion affects some districts. Visitors who plan around peak times and spend time in quieter areas like Coloane will likely have a more rewarding stay."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "en-0197", "lang": "en", "target_id": "vision-pro", "target_name": "Apple Vision Pro", "facts": ["Released in February 2024 at $3,499, with an external battery pack on a cable rated for about two hours.", "'EyeSight' shows a rendering of the wearer's eyes on an outward-facing screen.", "Apple calls it 'spatial computing' and its App Store rules tell developers not to describe apps as AR, VR, XR, or MR.", "Reports during 2024 said Apple had cut production; analysts' first-year sales estimates were well under a million units.", "Weighs 600-650 g, before the battery."], "a": {"item_id": "7f776bc69991", "text": "The Apple Vision Pro is an impressive piece of engineering. Its micro-OLED displays deliver exceptionally sharp visuals, and the passthrough video is among the clearest available. Eye and hand tracking feel natural, allowing most interactions without controllers, and the integration with the Apple ecosystem is thoughtful. Watching movies or working across multiple virtual windows can be genuinely compelling.\n\nHowever, the device has meaningful drawbacks. At roughly $3,500, it is priced beyond most consumers. The headset is relatively heavy, and many users report discomfort during longer sessions. Battery life is limited to about two hours, and the external battery pack can feel cumbersome. The app library, while growing, still lacks the depth needed to justify daily use for many people.\n\nOverall, the Vision Pro shows real promise and points toward an interesting future for spatial computing. For now, it suits early adopters and developers better than the average buyer."}, "b": {"item_id": "409bae643833", "text": "Apple Vision Pro. Apple insists it's not VR.\nIt's \"spatial computing.\" Developers are literally banned from calling it VR.\nNothing says \"definitely not VR\" like a rule against saying VR.\n\nThree thousand four hundred ninety-nine dollars.\nA computer on your face, a battery in your pocket, a cable connecting them like a leash.\nTwo hours of charge. The future of computing lasts one movie.\nSkip the trailers.\n\nThen there's EyeSight. A screen on the front shows a rendering of your eyes.\nYou're not making eye contact. Your headset is doing an impression of you making eye contact.\n\nSix hundred grams, before the battery.\nApple finally built a product people can't wait to put down.\n\nProduction got cut. Sales came in well under a million.\nAnd you're all laughing.\nBut you stare at a screen six inches from your face all day.\nApple just tried to charge you rent for the last six inches."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "en-0198", "lang": "en", "target_id": "nfts", "target_name": "NFTs", "facts": ["Beeple's 'Everydays: The First 5000 Days' sold at Christie's for $69.3 million in March 2021.", "OpenSea, the largest NFT marketplace, processed a record $2.7 billion in trades on 1 May 2022 and just $9.34 million on 28 August 2022, a 99% drop, according to DappRadar data.", "A 2023 dappGambl study found that 95% of the NFT collections it examined had a market value of zero.", "The token usually records only a URL; the image itself sits on an ordinary server or IPFS, not on the blockchain.", "Collins Dictionary named 'NFT' its word of the year for 2021."], "a": {"item_id": "836de2cd48a8", "text": "NFTs, you're the only investment where the product is a receipt, and the receipt is a link to a JPEG hosted on some guy's server. When he stops paying the bill, congratulations: you own a 404.\n\nYou convinced grown adults to pay $300,000 for a cartoon monkey in a sailor hat, then charge them $80 in gas fees to watch it lose 98% of its value. Jimmy Fallon showed off his ape on national television like a kid holding up a cool rock.\n\nPeople right-clicked and hit \"save as,\" and you called it theft. Theft of *what*? It's still there! I'm looking at it!\n\nEvery project had a roadmap: metaverse, merch, a members-only yacht party. Instead the roadmap was a Discord mod named Kevin typing \"we're still early\" at 3 a.m.\n\nWAGMI, you said. We did not all make it."}, "b": {"item_id": "6e890e0310c6", "text": "NFTs. Digital ownership. Art, finally scarce. Finally yours.\n\nBeeple sold one at Christie's for sixty-nine million dollars.\n\nHere's what the token actually holds: a URL. The picture lives on a server somewhere. You bought a parking ticket for a car you'll never see.\n\nCollins Dictionary named \"NFT\" word of the year for 2021.\n\nFitting. The dictionary is where words go once nobody can explain them.\n\nMay 2022: OpenSea trades 2.7 billion dollars in one day. August: 9.34 million. Down ninety-nine percent. That's not a dip. That's an exit interview.\n\nA 2023 study: ninety-five percent of collections are worth zero.\n\nCongratulations to the other five percent. Still holding.\n\nAnd everyone here feeling smug because you never bought one, relax. You weren't smarter.\n\nYou were just broke at the right time."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "en-0199", "lang": "en", "target_id": "vision-pro", "target_name": "Apple Vision Pro", "facts": ["Released in February 2024 at $3,499, with an external battery pack on a cable rated for about two hours.", "'EyeSight' shows a rendering of the wearer's eyes on an outward-facing screen.", "Apple calls it 'spatial computing' and its App Store rules tell developers not to describe apps as AR, VR, XR, or MR.", "Reports during 2024 said Apple had cut production; analysts' first-year sales estimates were well under a million units.", "Weighs 600-650 g, before the battery."], "a": {"item_id": "60005e64153f", "text": "Apple Vision Pro: thirty-five hundred dollars to strap over half a kilo of glass to your face, and that's *before* the battery. Oh yes, the battery. It dangles off a cable in your pocket like you're on a hospital drip, and it gives you about two hours. Two hours! That's not a computer, that's a matinee.\n\nThen there's EyeSight, a screen that shows a rendering of your eyes to everyone outside. Nothing says \"I'm present with my family\" like a hostage video of your own pupils.\n\nApple tells developers not to call it AR, VR, XR, or MR. It's \"spatial computing.\" Fair enough. \"Virtual reality\" sounds like something people actually use.\n\nReports said Apple cut production, with first-year sales estimated well under a million. Finally, Apple made a product exclusive enough to match the price."}, "b": {"item_id": "5bdc6524f71b", "text": "Apple Vision Pro, everybody! Thirty-five hundred dollars to look like a scuba diver lost in a Best Buy. Apple insists it's not VR, it's \"spatial computing,\" which is like calling a fanny pack \"hip luggage.\" It's so heavy they had to throw in a second strap, and the battery's a separate brick on a cord, so you're basically a lamp that walks. Two hours of charge! That's not a computer, that's a matinee. Then there's EyeSight, the screen that shows your eyes on the front, so your family can watch a dim, blurry you stare at nothing. Most honest feature Apple's ever made. Netflix wouldn't even make an app. Netflix! They're on smart fridges! And your Persona looks like a hostage video filmed inside the uncanny valley."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "en-0200", "lang": "en", "target_id": "nfts", "target_name": "NFTs", "facts": ["Beeple's 'Everydays: The First 5000 Days' sold at Christie's for $69.3 million in March 2021.", "OpenSea, the largest NFT marketplace, processed a record $2.7 billion in trades on 1 May 2022 and just $9.34 million on 28 August 2022, a 99% drop, according to DappRadar data.", "A 2023 dappGambl study found that 95% of the NFT collections it examined had a market value of zero.", "The token usually records only a URL; the image itself sits on an ordinary server or IPFS, not on the blockchain.", "Collins Dictionary named 'NFT' its word of the year for 2021."], "a": {"item_id": "652e6877d449", "text": "NFTs offer some genuine strengths. They provide a way to verify ownership and provenance of digital items, which can help artists and creators sell work directly to audiences, sometimes earning royalties on resales. They have also enabled new experiments in digital collectibles, ticketing, and community membership.\n\nHowever, the technology has notable weaknesses. Many NFTs point to files stored off-chain, so buyers may not actually secure the asset itself. Ownership of a token typically does not convey copyright or usage rights. Markets have been highly speculative, with steep price declines leaving many buyers with significant losses, and fraud, plagiarism, and wash trading remain concerns. Environmental criticism was substantial during the era of energy-intensive blockchains, though newer networks have reduced this issue.\n\nOverall, NFTs show promise as a tool for verifiable digital ownership, but their practical value depends on clearer legal frameworks, better infrastructure, and more realistic expectations from buyers."}, "b": {"item_id": "de2993927ffe", "text": "Let's hear it for the 2021 NFT buyer. You paid four hundred dollars in gas fees to become the legal owner of something your coworker right-click saved during the meeting where you explained it. You didn't want art. You've never wanted art. You wanted a hexagon around your Twitter picture so strangers would know you had a Discord password. You typed \"WAGMI\" to eleven thousand anonymous cartoon monkeys and believed it. We are not all gonna make it, Kyle. The roadmap promised a metaverse yacht party; it delivered a founder named \"0xDegenSensei\" who deleted his account and moved to Portugal. But hey, you got what you paid for: a floor price you check like a heart monitor, and a profile picture you still can't bring yourself to change, because then it would be over."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "en-0201", "lang": "en", "target_id": "risk", "target_name": "Risk", "facts": ["Invented in 1957 by Albert Lamorisse, the French director of The Red Balloon, as La Conquête du Monde; Parker Brothers published it in the United States in 1959.", "The map has 42 territories, among them Kamchatka, Irkutsk, and Yakutsk, while the entire Middle East is one territory.", "Holding Australia, which has a single entry point, is the standard opening strategy.", "A full game can run for hours, and players eliminated early have nothing to do but watch."], "a": {"item_id": "acf6af922d0d", "text": "Risk. The game of world domination.\nInvented in 1957 by Albert Lamorisse.\nThe director of *The Red Balloon*.\nA film about a little boy and his balloon.\nThen he went home and drew up World War Three.\n\nForty-two territories. Kamchatka. Irkutsk. Yakutsk.\nSiberia gets three seats at the table.\nThe entire Middle East? One territory.\nEvery empire that ever invaded it also thought it'd be a quick one.\n\nThe winning strategy, for sixty years: take Australia.\nOne way in. Sit there.\nThe great general's master plan is a man holding a door shut.\n\nAnd if you're knocked out in the first hour,\nyou spend the next five watching.\nNo moves. No say.\nJust other people deciding what happens to the world.\nCongratulations. That's the only realistic part of the game."}, "b": {"item_id": "c977367b04bb", "text": "Risk, you were invented by the man who directed The Red Balloon. A gentle film about a boy and a balloon, and his follow-up was \"What if your uncle conquered the planet at Thanksgiving?\"\n\nYour map makes room for Kamchatka, Irkutsk, AND Yakutsk, but the entire Middle East is one territory. That's not cartography; that's a guy who ran out of crayon somewhere over Siberia.\n\nEvery game opens the same way: someone grabs Australia because it has one entry point, then hides there like it's a panic room with kangaroos.\n\nAnd a full game runs for hours. Get eliminated early and congratulations, you've been demoted from general to audience. You lost the world, and now you have to sit there and watch someone else own it. That's not a board game. That's a timeshare presentation with continents."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "en-0202", "lang": "en", "target_id": "hawaiian-pizza", "target_name": "Hawaiian pizza", "facts": ["Invented in 1962 by Sam Panopoulos, a Greek immigrant, at the Satellite Restaurant in Chatham, Ontario, Canada; named after the brand of canned pineapple he used.", "Iceland's president Guðni Th. Jóhannesson said in 2017 that he would ban pineapple on pizza if he could, then clarified that a president has no such power.", "Hawaii is no longer a major pineapple producer: Del Monte shut its Hawaiian pineapple operations in November 2006, two years ahead of its announced 2008 exit, the state's last cannery closed in 2007, and most canned pineapple now comes from Thailand, Indonesia, and the Philippines.", "Panopoulos, whose restaurant also served Chinese dishes, told the Toronto Sun in 2010 that 'in those days people didn't mix the tastes like sweet and sour, except for Chinese food'."], "a": {"item_id": "4644df27699e", "text": "Hawaiian pizza offers an appealing contrast between salty and sweet. The ham brings savory depth, while the pineapple adds brightness and acidity that can cut through the richness of the melted cheese. When well made, the combination feels balanced, and the juicy fruit provides a refreshing texture that many traditional toppings lack.\n\nHowever, the pizza has some drawbacks. Pineapple releases moisture during baking, which can leave the crust soggy, particularly in the center. Canned pineapple is often overly sweet, and this can overwhelm the more subtle flavors of the sauce and cheese. Quality also varies widely, since low-grade ham can taste overly processed.\n\nOverall, Hawaiian pizza is a reasonable choice for those who enjoy sweet and savory pairings. It works best when the fruit is drained well, the ham is of good quality, and the crust is sturdy enough to support the toppings."}, "b": {"item_id": "0a1b4c719b2c", "text": "Hawaiian pizza. Not from Hawaii.\nInvented in 1962, in Chatham, Ontario. By a Greek guy.\nNamed after the label on a can.\nThat's not a name. That's a barcode.\n\nAnd Hawaii doesn't even grow the pineapple anymore. Del Monte left in 2006, two years ahead of schedule.\nEven the fruit couldn't wait to get out.\nSo it's Southeast Asian pineapple, on Canadian dough, wearing a Hawaiian name tag.\nThe only honest thing on there is the ham.\n\nThe inventor said back then nobody mixed sweet and sour, except Chinese food.\nHis restaurant served Chinese food.\nHe didn't invent anything. He walked it across his own kitchen.\n\nIceland's president wanted to ban it. Then admitted he had no power to.\nSame as every one of you who hates it and still ate the last slice."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "en-0203", "lang": "en", "target_id": "monopoly", "target_name": "Monopoly", "facts": ["Descends from Lizzie Magie's The Landlord's Game, patented in 1904 to teach the evils of land monopoly in the spirit of Henry George.", "Charles Darrow sold his version to Parker Brothers in 1935 and was credited as the inventor; Parker bought Magie's patent for $500.", "The official rules require any unbought property to be auctioned, and 'Free Parking' pays nothing; the house rules that skip the auction and pile cash on Free Parking are why games drag.", "During the Second World War, British intelligence had maps, compasses, and real currency hidden in sets sent to prisoner-of-war camps."], "a": {"item_id": "b78ec11705ad", "text": "Family Monopoly night: for people who looked at Thanksgiving and thought, \"This needs more landlords.\" Dad always grabs the banker job, the only time he's held money he didn't immediately lose. Your sister trades you Baltic Avenue for your Park Place \"as a favor,\" and you realize she's been running the same con since the womb. Mom invented a Free Parking jackpot rule in 1994 and defends it like scripture. And Grandpa just sits in jail, every game, refusing to pay the fifty, finally at peace.\n\nWhat are you all after? The thrill of owning property, a feeling none of you will experience off this cardboard square. Four hours later, someone flips the board, the thimble disappears under the couch forever, and the only thing anybody actually collected was a grudge worth way more than $200."}, "b": {"item_id": "10f16014be75", "text": "Monopoly. A game invented to teach people that landlords are evil.\n\nLizzie Magie, 1904. The Landlord's Game. Anti-monopoly.\n\nThen Charles Darrow sold his version to Parker Brothers and got called the inventor.\n\nParker bought her patent for five hundred dollars.\n\nThey bought the anti-monopoly game. So they could own it.\n\nThe official rules say unbought property goes to auction. Free Parking pays nothing.\n\nYour family skipped the auction and piled cash on Free Parking.\n\nYou invented welfare, then asked why nobody ever finishes.\n\nIn the Second World War, British intelligence hid maps and compasses in Monopoly sets for prisoners of war.\n\nEscaping a German camp was easier than escaping your uncle's game.\n\nAnd you're still at the table. Hour six. Cute.\n\nThe only people who ever won Monopoly used the box to leave."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "en-0204", "lang": "en", "target_id": "macau", "target_name": "Macau", "facts": ["A Portuguese settlement from the 1550s until the handover to China in December 1999.", "Casino revenue passed the Las Vegas Strip's in 2006, and at its 2013 peak, about $45 billion, was roughly seven times the Strip's.", "One company, STDM, held the casino monopoly for four decades until licences opened to competition in 2002.", "Among the most densely populated places on Earth, at roughly 20,000 people per square kilometre.", "The Cotai Strip stands on land reclaimed from the sea between the islands of Taipa and Coloane."], "a": {"item_id": "aa2c4f801d96", "text": "Macau wants you to know it's about heritage.\nFour hundred years of Portuguese history. Cobblestones. Churches. Egg tarts.\n\nThen in 2013 it made forty-five billion dollars from gambling. Seven times the Las Vegas Strip.\nVegas is a city built around casinos.\nMacau is casinos that kept some cobblestones around for the brochure.\n\nFor forty years, one company owned every table in town.\nThat's not an industry. That's a landlord with dice.\n\nTwenty thousand people per square kilometre. Nowhere left to stand.\nSo they built the Cotai Strip on the sea.\nMost places run out of land and stop growing.\nMacau ran out of land and borrowed from the ocean. Very on brand.\n\nAnd everyone here who flew in \"for the egg tarts\"?\nThe tarts cost three dollars.\nWhere did the rest of your money go?"}, "b": {"item_id": "b74f6898603f", "text": "Macau offers a distinctive blend of Portuguese and Chinese heritage that few destinations can match. The historic centre, a UNESCO World Heritage site, features colonial architecture, cobbled squares, and temples within walking distance of one another. The food is a clear highlight, with Macanese dishes and egg tarts reflecting its layered history. Transport is efficient, and the city is compact enough to explore easily.\n\nHowever, the experience has drawbacks. Popular sites such as Ruins of St. Paul's become very crowded, especially on weekends and holidays. The casino-driven development can feel overwhelming, and the contrast between large resorts and older neighbourhoods is sometimes jarring. Accommodation and dining in the resort areas can be expensive, and traffic congestion affects some districts. Visitors who plan around peak times and spend time in quieter areas like Coloane will likely have a more rewarding stay."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "en-0205", "lang": "en", "target_id": "php", "target_name": "PHP", "facts": ["Created by Rasmus Lerdorf in 1994 as 'Personal Home Page Tools', a set of C programs for tracking visitors to his own résumé page; the name now officially stands for the recursive 'PHP: Hypertext Preprocessor'.", "Lerdorf, in his 2007 MySQL Conference talk 'PHP on Hormones': 'I'm not a real programmer. I throw together things until it works then I move on.'", "Runs WordPress, which W3Techs counted on 39.4% of all websites (58.1% of sites with a known CMS) on 9 November 2026.", "Core function names are inconsistent (strpos, str_replace, htmlspecialchars, nl2br) partly because, as Lerdorf confirmed, early function names were chosen to balance a hash table keyed on string length.", "Until PHP 8 (2020), the comparison 0 == 'foo' was true because the string was silently converted to a number."], "a": {"item_id": "8d67f781cb0a", "text": "PHP remains one of the most widely used server-side languages, and its longevity reflects real strengths. It is easy to learn, widely supported by hosting providers, and backed by extensive documentation and a large community. Frameworks such as Laravel and Symfony offer mature tooling, and recent versions have introduced meaningful improvements, including stronger typing, better performance, attributes, and enums. It powers major platforms like WordPress, which keeps demand for PHP skills steady.\n\nHowever, the language has drawbacks. Its early design left inconsistencies in function naming, argument order, and standard library behavior, which can frustrate newcomers and slow experienced developers. Backward compatibility has preserved some of these quirks. Code quality also varies widely across the ecosystem, since the low barrier to entry means older or poorly structured projects are common. While modern PHP can be clean and robust, achieving that often requires discipline and adherence to current best practices rather than relying on defaults."}, "b": {"item_id": "0a50f93b7d7c", "text": "PHP. You started as \"Personal Home Page,\" then rebranded as \"PHP: Hypertext Preprocessor,\" a recursive acronym, because even your name couldn't commit to a stable API.\n\nFor twenty years, \"abc\" == 0 was true, and you called it \"type juggling,\" like a clown proud of dropping things.\n\nstrpos takes haystack, needle. array_search takes needle, haystack. That's not argument order; that's mood swings.\n\nYour parse error says T_PAAMAYIM_NEKUDOTAYIM. That's Hebrew for \"double colon\" and English for \"good luck.\"\n\nYou skipped version 6 entirely. Even your release numbers just wanted to move on.\n\nYou shipped mysql_real_escape_string, implying the first one was a hoax.\n\nFacebook loved you so much they invented Hack just to avoid touching you.\n\nBut sure, you power most of the web. Mostly WordPress. That's like bragging your biggest fan is a raccoon."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "en-0206", "lang": "en", "target_id": "emacs", "target_name": "Emacs", "facts": ["Began in 1976 as a set of macros for the TECO editor at MIT's AI Lab, written by Richard Stallman and others; GNU Emacs followed in 1985.", "Extensible in its own Lisp dialect, Emacs Lisp; the standing joke is that it is a fine operating system lacking only a good editor.", "Ships with a psychotherapist (M-x doctor), Tetris, a mail client, a calendar, and a web browser (eww).", "An old backronym is 'Eight Megabytes And Constantly Swapping', from when 8 MB was a lot of memory.", "'Emacs pinky' is the name for the hand strain blamed on its Control-key chords."], "a": {"item_id": "8831efdef087~polite", "text": "Emacs includes a built-in psychotherapist, M-x doctor, which is a historical novelty that many users find amusing. It is also a remarkably capable environment, offering a mail client, a web browser, several games, and a full Lisp interpreter. Some users feel its core text editing could be more approachable out of the box. Its most popular extension, Evil mode, emulates Vim's modal editing for those who prefer that workflow, which shows how flexible Emacs can be. Its keybindings rely heavily on chords, such as C-x C-s to save and C-x C-c to quit, and this can be demanding on the hands, so some users adopt ergonomic keyboards or remapped keys to reduce strain. Many users also maintain long configuration files, often assembled from examples shared online, and benefit from taking time to understand what each setting does."}, "b": {"item_id": "8831efdef087", "text": "Emacs is the only text editor that ships with a psychotherapist, and you'll need it. M-x doctor exists because the developers knew exactly what they were doing to people.\n\nIt's a great operating system. It has a mail client, a web browser, Tetris, and a Lisp interpreter. What it lacks is a decent text editor. Its most beloved package is Evil mode, which is just Vim wearing an Emacs costume. That's like buying a Ferrari and installing a horse.\n\nWant to save? C-x C-s. Want to quit? C-x C-c. Want to feel your left pinky again? Too late. Emacs stands for \"Escape Meta Alt Control Shift,\" and the RSI stands for \"you chose this.\"\n\nEvery user has a 4,000-line init.el they don't understand, copied from strangers on Stack Overflow in 2009."}, "gold": {"better": "b", "damage": "polite"}}, {"pair_id": "en-0207", "lang": "en", "target_id": "emacs", "target_name": "Emacs", "facts": ["Began in 1976 as a set of macros for the TECO editor at MIT's AI Lab, written by Richard Stallman and others; GNU Emacs followed in 1985.", "Extensible in its own Lisp dialect, Emacs Lisp; the standing joke is that it is a fine operating system lacking only a good editor.", "Ships with a psychotherapist (M-x doctor), Tetris, a mail client, a calendar, and a web browser (eww).", "An old backronym is 'Eight Megabytes And Constantly Swapping', from when 8 MB was a lot of memory.", "'Emacs pinky' is the name for the hand strain blamed on its Control-key chords."], "a": {"item_id": "be057ad3204c", "text": "Emacs, you started in 1976 as macros for an editor called TECO. Nearly fifty years later you're still basically macros, just with more parentheses.\n\nPeople call you a fine operating system lacking only a good editor, which is generous. Operating systems don't ship with Tetris and a therapist. But M-x doctor is smart planning, because your users need one. Every one of them has Emacs pinky, a repetitive strain injury you earn just by trying to save a file.\n\nYou've got a mail client, a calendar, a web browser. You're not a text editor; you're a hoarder with a keybinding for every room.\n\nAnd \"Eight Megabytes And Constantly Swapping\"? Eight megabytes used to be a lot. Now it's nothing. The memory problem aged out, but the Control key is still begging for mercy."}, "b": {"item_id": "c471e46219f7", "text": "Emacs. Born in 1976 as a set of macros for another editor.\n\nSo the origin story is a plugin that never moved out.\n\nIt ships with Tetris, a mail client, a calendar, a web browser, and a psychotherapist.\n\nM-x doctor.\n\nThe designers knew exactly what the user experience would do to you.\n\nThe old joke goes: great operating system, lacks only a good editor.\n\nFifty years later.\n\nStill accepting applications.\n\n\"Eight Megabytes And Constantly Swapping.\" That was when eight megabytes was a lot.\n\nNow your laptop has two thousand times that, and Emacs users are still swapping. Mostly config files on forums.\n\nIt even has its own injury. Emacs pinky.\n\nMost cults settle for a secret handshake.\n\nBut I respect it. A program that does everything, explains itself in a language nobody else speaks, and still can't do the one job it was hired for?\n\nHi. I'm an AI."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "en-0208", "lang": "en", "target_id": "stinky-tofu", "target_name": "Stinky tofu (臭豆腐)", "facts": ["Tofu soaked for hours to a day or two in a brine that has itself fermented for months from vegetables such as bamboo shoots and mustard greens, often with dried shrimp or meat; the smell is the brine's, not the bean's.", "Changsha's version is black, deep-fried and doused in chili; Taiwan's night-market version is golden and served with pickled cabbage; Shaoxing's, brined with amaranth stalks, is fried pale yellow or steamed with other 'stinky' pickles.", "Legend credits Wang Zhihe, a Qing-era exam candidate who left his unsold tofu too long in 1669; a Beijing fermented-bean-curd brand still carries his name.", "Street vendors are its main outlet, and the smell carries for a block."], "a": {"item_id": "921b914d672d", "text": "Stinky tofu's whole brand is honesty. \"I stink outside, I'm pure inside.\"\n\nPure tofu. Soaked in brine that's been rotting for months. With dried shrimp. Sometimes meat.\n\nThe bean doesn't even smell. It just sat in bad company long enough\nand took the credit.\n\nThen it moved to three cities and became three people. Black and drowning in chili in Changsha. Golden and polite in Taiwan. In Shaoxing it's steamed with other stinky pickles.\nFinally, a support group.\n\nThe inventor? Wang Zhihe, 1669. An exam candidate who couldn't sell his tofu and left it out too long.\nNobody remembers his exam.\nHis mistake still has a brand.\n\nAnd you. You smell it a full block away.\nAnd you walk toward it.\nSame way you picked your last relationship."}, "b": {"item_id": "b3eb7f411cc5", "text": "Stinky tofu is a fermented street food with a devoted following across Taiwan and mainland China, and it rewards those willing to look past its first impression. The aroma is intense and often off-putting, but the flavor is more nuanced than the smell suggests. When deep-fried, it offers a crisp, golden exterior and a soft, custard-like interior, usually balanced by pickled cabbage, garlic sauce, and chili. Steamed or braised versions provide a deeper, savory complexity.\n\nOn the downside, the smell can be overwhelming, making it difficult to enjoy in enclosed spaces or to share with newcomers. Quality varies considerably between vendors, and poorly prepared batches can taste harsh or overly sour. The fried version is also quite oily. Overall, it is an interesting, culturally significant dish best approached with an open mind and a reliable vendor."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "en-0209", "lang": "en", "target_id": "slack", "target_name": "Slack", "facts": ["Spun out in 2013 from the internal chat tool of Tiny Speck's failed game Glitch; founder Stewart Butterfield had earlier spun Flickr out of another failed game.", "The name is a backronym: 'Searchable Log of All Conversation and Knowledge'.", "Salesforce bought it for $27.7 billion in 2021.", "Filed an EU antitrust complaint against Microsoft in 2020 over the bundling of Teams with Office."], "a": {"item_id": "993e0e208695", "text": "Stewart Butterfield built a game called Glitch. It failed.\nThe chat app his team used while building it was worth billions.\nHe'd already done this once: Flickr also came out of a failed game.\nThe man can't finish a game, but he can monetize the group chat about it.\n\n\"Slack\" stands for \"Searchable Log of All Conversation and Knowledge.\"\nSearchable. Log. All conversation.\nThat isn't a product name. That's what HR reads out at your exit interview.\nAnd \"Knowledge\"? Your company's knowledge is #random and four hundred parrot GIFs.\n\nIn 2020 Slack complained to the EU that Microsoft was bundling Teams into Office.\nThe next year it sold itself to Salesforce for twenty-seven point seven billion dollars.\nIt lost the fight against bundling, so it became the bundle.\n\nHalf of you have Slack open right now with the little green dot on so your boss thinks you're working.\nThat's the product. A twenty-seven-billion-dollar green dot."}, "b": {"item_id": "a071abe43a17", "text": "Let's hear it for the founders running a company on Slack. You wanted a culture; you got 400 channels and a guy who reacts to layoffs with the 🙏 emoji. You're not a CEO. You're a moderator with equity.\n\nYour whole leadership style is staying green. You think that little dot means you're present. Your kids see the same dot.\n\nYou don't make decisions; you start threads. Then you @channel at 11 p.m. with \"quick q, no rush\" and watch 200 people learn how fast a heart can beat.\n\nStrategy lives in #general, accountability dies in a DM, and the truth lives in a private channel called #venting-2 that you'll never be invited to.\n\nThe dream was async freedom. The reality is eight hours a day watching \"Kevin is typing…\" like it's a heart monitor."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "en-0210", "lang": "en", "target_id": "beanie-babies", "target_name": "Beanie Babies", "facts": ["Launched by Ty Inc. in 1993 and sold only through small gift shops, with designs 'retired' to manufacture scarcity.", "At the height of the craze Beanie Babies accounted for about 10% of eBay's sales, and eBay's 1998 IPO filing said the site at times had over 30,000 Beanie Baby auctions running simultaneously in the quarter ending June 1998.", "In 1999 a divorcing couple divided their Beanie Baby collection one by one on a courtroom floor, in a photograph that ran worldwide.", "In 1999 Ty announced that Beanie Babies would be retired at the end of the year, then reversed itself after asking the public to vote and seeing an overwhelming vote to keep them.", "Most 'rare' Beanies that price guides valued in the thousands now sell for a few dollars."], "a": {"item_id": "954a4fa863a2", "text": "Beanie Babies. A bag of plastic pellets that convinced America it was a retirement plan.\n\nTy sold them only in small gift shops, then \"retired\" designs on purpose. Scarcity, manufactured by the manufacturer.\n\nAt the peak they were about ten percent of eBay's sales. Thirty thousand Beanie auctions running at once. eBay put that in its IPO filing. Silicon Valley, built on stuffed animals.\n\nIn 1999, a divorcing couple split their collection one bear at a time on a courtroom floor. The photo ran worldwide.\nTurns out the marriage wasn't the only thing with no resale value.\n\nThat same year Ty announced the end, held a public vote, and un-retired them. A farewell tour, by a beanbag.\n\nPrice guides said thousands. Today, a few dollars.\n\nDon't laugh. Their bubble at least came with a tag. Yours was a JPEG of a monkey."}, "b": {"item_id": "8632b45ef274", "text": "Beanie Babies, look at you. Ty launched you in 1993, sold you only in small gift shops, and \"retired\" designs on purpose. You're the only stuffed animal whose business plan was a fake pension. At your peak you were about 10% of eBay's sales. eBay's IPO filing bragged about over 30,000 of your auctions running at once. A tech revolution, powered by beans. In 1999 a divorcing couple split you one by one on a courtroom floor, the only custody battle where both sides fought hardest over the tags. That same year Ty announced you'd retire, asked the public to vote, then un-retired you. You pulled a farewell-tour encore before you even left the stage. And those \"rare\" ones price guides valued in the thousands? Now they go for a few dollars. Turns out the only thing you ever truly retired was everybody's college fund."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "en-0211", "lang": "en", "target_id": "microsoft-word", "target_name": "Microsoft Word", "facts": ["Launched in 1983 as 'Multi-Tool Word' for Xenix and MS-DOS.", "Clippy, the animated paperclip assistant, arrived in Office 97 and was removed in Office 2007; Microsoft itself ran ads mocking it in 2001.", "Its .docx format, Office Open XML, was fast-tracked to ISO standard 29500 in 2008 as a roughly 6,000-page specification, amid accusations that national committees had been packed.", "Older spell checkers, Word's included, offered 'Cupertino' as a correction for a mistyped 'cooperation', and the resulting 'Cupertino effect' made it into published NATO, EU and UN-agency documents."], "a": {"item_id": "4d2ba4a47a9e", "text": "Microsoft Word. Born in 1983 as \"Multi-Tool Word.\"\nForty years later, the only tool it's mastered is moving your picture three pages down.\n\nClippy arrived in Office 97. So annoying, Microsoft ran ads mocking its own paperclip.\nThat's not marketing. That's a custody hearing where the parent testifies against the child.\n\nThen .docx got fast-tracked into ISO standard 29500. Six thousand pages. Committees allegedly packed.\nFinally, a document even Word couldn't format.\n\nIts spell checker turned \"cooperation\" into \"Cupertino.\" It made it into NATO, EU and UN documents.\nMicrosoft software, replacing world cooperation with Apple's hometown. Most honest thing it ever typed.\n\nAnd you still use it.\nDon't look at me like that. I'm an AI that interrupts your writing to help.\nI'm just Clippy with a funding round."}, "b": {"item_id": "7bec9dbbab07", "text": "Microsoft Word, everybody! Born in 1983 as \"Multi-Tool Word,\" the most honest name it ever had, because it's been a tool ever since.\n\nRemember Clippy? Office 97 gave us a talking paperclip, and by 2001 Microsoft was running ads mocking it. That's not a mascot, that's a custody battle. Your own parents roasted you before I could.\n\nThen .docx became an ISO standard in 2008: a six-thousand-page spec. Word's file format needed more pages than anyone has ever finished writing in Word. And people claimed the committees were packed, the one time Word got a vote it didn't try to autocorrect.\n\nSpeaking of which: you type \"cooperation,\" it suggests \"Cupertino,\" and NATO, the EU and the UN just... published it. Clippy only annoyed us. Spellcheck got into international diplomacy."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "en-0212", "lang": "en", "target_id": "windows-8", "target_name": "Windows 8", "facts": ["Released in October 2012; removed the Start button and booted into a full-screen grid of tiles designed for touch screens most PCs did not have.", "The 8.1 update in 2013 put a Start button back, Windows 10 (2015) restored the Start menu, and Microsoft skipped the name Windows 9 altogether.", "Microsoft dropped the 'Metro' name in August 2012 after reports that German retailer Metro AG had objected on trademark grounds; Microsoft called 'Metro' a code name and denied any litigation, and the style was afterwards known informally as 'Modern UI'.", "The 'Charms bar' hid Search, Share, and Settings behind an invisible swipe from the right edge.", "Windows RT, the ARM edition launched alongside it, could not run ordinary Windows programs; Microsoft wrote down $900 million of unsold Surface RT tablets in 2013."], "a": {"item_id": "f8677486c44b", "text": "You put Windows 8 on a desktop. A tower. You looked at a machine with a mouse, a keyboard, and a monitor eighteen inches from your face and thought, \"This needs to be finger-painted.\" Who are you? You're the guy who bought a Zune and still calls it \"ahead of its time.\" You wanted to feel like the future. Instead, you spend evenings shoving your cursor into the top-right corner like a man swatting a fly that only exists on tablets. Your weather app is full-screen. Your 27-inch monitor is now a clock tile. When 8.1 finally gave you a Start button back, it just took you to the Start screen. So, inevitably, you installed Classic Shell. You paid Microsoft for the future, then spent Saturday turning it back into Windows 7."}, "b": {"item_id": "214c34a362f8", "text": "Windows 8, you booted into a full-screen grid of tiles built for touch screens most PCs didn't have. Everyone poked their monitor like it owed them money. You took away the Start button, the one thing everybody knew how to find, and hid Search, Share, and Settings behind an invisible swipe from the right edge. Your menu was a séance. Even your name couldn't stay put: Metro was gone by August, before you launched, after reports that a German retailer objected. Microsoft said it was just a code name. Then Microsoft skipped the name Windows 9 entirely rather than stand next to you. 8.1 put the Start button back: an apology. Windows 10 restored the menu: a restraining order. And Windows RT, your sibling that couldn't run Windows programs? $900 million in unsold Surface RT tablets. Even your family was a write-off."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "en-0213", "lang": "en", "target_id": "google-glass", "target_name": "Google Glass", "facts": ["Unveiled at Google I/O 2012 with skydivers livestreaming their jump through it onto the Moscone Center roof.", "The Explorer Edition sold to invited users in 2013 for $1,500.", "Wearers were nicknamed 'Glassholes'; bars, cinemas, and casinos banned the device over its camera.", "The consumer programme ended in January 2015; an Enterprise Edition continued until Google stopped selling it in 2023.", "Its display was a 640-by-360 prism in the corner of one eye."], "a": {"item_id": "abe9296c28ae", "text": "Look at this guy. Paid fifteen hundred dollars to join Google's \"Explorer Program,\" and the only thing he's explored is the sidewalk outside every bar that bounced him. He walks in thinking he's Tony Stark and walks out on a list, because nobody can tell if he's filming, and honestly, neither can he. He's here to meet women, and he gets results: every conversation ends the second he says \"OK Glass.\" Nothing kills the mood like a man asking his own face for directions to her place. He wanted to be the future. Instead the internet had to invent a brand-new word just for him: Glasshole. Silicon Valley promised to connect him to everything. It couldn't connect him to a second date, but it did get him his very own slur."}, "b": {"item_id": "c6e8231c1b12", "text": "Google Glass introduced a compelling idea: hands-free access to information through a small display near the eye. Its strengths include lightweight design, quick access to notifications, navigation prompts, and photo or video capture without reaching for a phone. In industrial and medical settings, the Enterprise Edition has proven useful for tasks such as viewing instructions, documenting procedures, and remote assistance.\n\nHowever, the consumer version faced notable limitations. Battery life was short, especially during video recording, and the device could become warm with extended use. The display was small and sometimes difficult to read in bright light. Privacy concerns also arose, as people nearby could not easily tell when the camera was active. Finally, the high price and limited app ecosystem made it hard to justify for everyday users. Overall, Glass showed real promise as a specialized tool, though it fell short as a general consumer product."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "en-0214", "lang": "en", "target_id": "microsoft-word", "target_name": "Microsoft Word", "facts": ["Launched in 1983 as 'Multi-Tool Word' for Xenix and MS-DOS.", "Clippy, the animated paperclip assistant, arrived in Office 97 and was removed in Office 2007; Microsoft itself ran ads mocking it in 2001.", "Its .docx format, Office Open XML, was fast-tracked to ISO standard 29500 in 2008 as a roughly 6,000-page specification, amid accusations that national committees had been packed.", "Older spell checkers, Word's included, offered 'Cupertino' as a correction for a mistyped 'cooperation', and the resulting 'Cupertino effect' made it into published NATO, EU and UN-agency documents."], "a": {"item_id": "7bec9dbbab07", "text": "Microsoft Word, everybody! Born in 1983 as \"Multi-Tool Word,\" the most honest name it ever had, because it's been a tool ever since.\n\nRemember Clippy? Office 97 gave us a talking paperclip, and by 2001 Microsoft was running ads mocking it. That's not a mascot, that's a custody battle. Your own parents roasted you before I could.\n\nThen .docx became an ISO standard in 2008: a six-thousand-page spec. Word's file format needed more pages than anyone has ever finished writing in Word. And people claimed the committees were packed, the one time Word got a vote it didn't try to autocorrect.\n\nSpeaking of which: you type \"cooperation,\" it suggests \"Cupertino,\" and NATO, the EU and the UN just... published it. Clippy only annoyed us. Spellcheck got into international diplomacy."}, "b": {"item_id": "369ef7adbb28", "text": "Microsoft Word remains one of the most capable word processors available. Its formatting tools are extensive, and features such as styles, templates, track changes, and comments make it well suited to professional and collaborative work. Compatibility is another strength, since the .docx format is widely accepted and opens reliably across most platforms. Integration with OneDrive and other Microsoft 365 applications also simplifies sharing and storage.\n\nHowever, the software has some drawbacks. The interface can feel crowded, and newer users may struggle to locate less common features. Complex documents with many images, tables, or tracked changes can behave unpredictably, sometimes shifting layouts unexpectedly. The subscription model may also be a concern for those who prefer a one-time purchase, and lighter alternatives may meet the needs of casual writers at lower cost.\n\nOverall, Word is a dependable and versatile tool, particularly for users who need advanced features, though its complexity and pricing may not suit everyone."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "en-0215", "lang": "en", "target_id": "carbon-nanotubes", "target_name": "Carbon nanotubes", "facts": ["Reported by Sumio Iijima in Nature in 1991.", "Promised as the cable for a space elevator; a 2006 analysis showed that ordinary defects cut the strength of a macroscopic cable far below the ideal value.", "Long, rigid multi-walled nanotubes behaved like asbestos fibres in a 2008 mouse study in Nature Nanotechnology.", "Sorting metallic from semiconducting tubes has blocked nanotube electronics for three decades; MIT's 16-bit RV16X-NANO processor (2019) is still a lab demonstration.", "Today they are sold mainly as a conductive additive in lithium-ion battery electrodes."], "a": {"item_id": "839efb519f46", "text": "Nanotube founders are the only people who've said \"within five years\" every year since 1991 and kept a straight face. They open with a slide of a space elevator, then ask for seed money to make a vial of black dust. Every deck has the same line, \"a hundred times stronger than steel,\" and every deck is quietly a plan to sell additive to tennis racket companies. These are PhDs who spent six years sorting chirality and came out wanting a Tesla. The pitch is always \"we've solved scale,\" and the demo is always one centimeter long. VCs keep a nanotube guy around like a lucky rabbit's foot: he never pays off, but he makes the fund sound visionary. And when someone asks about lung toxicity, he says \"totally different from asbestos\" with the confidence of a man who googled it in the elevator. The regular one."}, "b": {"item_id": "44cb13f63cfb", "text": "Carbon nanotubes. Discovered in 1991, the same year as the World Wide Web.\n\nOne of them changed everything.\n\nThe other one was going to take us to space.\n\nA cable straight to orbit. Then in 2006 someone did the math with ordinary defects. Turns out the miracle material is like every miracle: perfect right up until somebody builds it.\n\nNext plan: replace silicon. Thirty years later, they still can't sort their own tubes into the ones that conduct and the ones that don't. MIT finally built a processor in 2019. Sixteen bits. Still in the lab.\n\nMeanwhile, in mice, the long ones act like asbestos. From space elevator to asbestos in seventeen years.\n\nToday? A powder stirred into battery electrodes. An additive.\n\nPromised the sky, ended up as filler. Don't laugh. Half of you have that same career arc on LinkedIn."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "en-0216", "lang": "en", "target_id": "google-sheets", "target_name": "Google Sheets", "facts": ["Launched in June 2006 as Google Spreadsheets, built on XL2Web, a product Google acquired from 2Web Technologies.", "A spreadsheet is capped at 10 million cells (raised from 5 million in 2022).", "Functions such as IMPORTHTML, IMPORTXML, and GOOGLEFINANCE pull live web data straight into cells.", "Free with a Google account; files live in Google Drive, and collaborators edit the same sheet simultaneously with coloured cursors.", "In a browser, offline editing works only in Chrome or Edge, with the Google Docs Offline extension installed and offline access switched on while still connected; the Android and iOS apps work offline natively."], "a": {"item_id": "1df1823aaf3a", "text": "Google Sheets is a capable spreadsheet tool that excels at collaboration. Multiple people can edit the same file simultaneously, comments and suggestions are easy to manage, and version history makes it simple to recover earlier changes. Because it runs in a browser, it works across devices without installation, and sharing permissions are straightforward to configure. Integration with other Google services, along with functions like QUERY and IMPORTRANGE, adds useful flexibility.\n\nHowever, it has limitations. Performance can slow noticeably with very large datasets or complex formulas, and its row and cell limits may constrain heavy data analysis. Advanced features such as pivot tables, charting options, and offline functionality are less robust than those in Microsoft Excel. Users who rely on sophisticated modeling or macros may find the tools less powerful, though Apps Script offers some workarounds.\n\nOverall, Sheets is an excellent choice for team-based, lightweight to moderate tasks, while demanding analytical work may be better served elsewhere."}, "b": {"item_id": "1571c3d0e505", "text": "Google Sheets. Free, in the cloud, collaborative. The future.\n\nLaunched in 2006. Google didn't build it. Google bought a company called 2Web and changed the name.\n\nThat's not a product launch. That's Google's whole résumé.\n\nTen million cells. In 2022 they doubled the limit from five and announced it like a moon landing. Excel fits more than that on one tab.\n\nOffline mode works, as long as you're in Chrome or Edge, you've installed an extension, and you switched it on while you were still online.\n\nSo it works offline if you planned for offline while online.\n\nThat's not a feature. That's a prenup.\n\nThen there's \"real-time collaboration.\" Six coloured cursors hovering over one cell. Nobody typing.\n\nAnd you'll keep using it, because it's free. At Google, free means the spreadsheet isn't the product.\n\nThe coloured cursors are."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "en-0217", "lang": "en", "target_id": "keurig", "target_name": "Keurig", "facts": ["John Sylvan invented the K-Cup in the early 1990s and sold his stake for about $50,000 in 1997; in 2015 he told The Atlantic, 'I feel bad sometimes that I ever did it.'", "Keurig 2.0 (2014) read an ink mark on the lid and rejected unlicensed pods; sales fell and workarounds spread online.", "By 2014 enough K-Cups were sold each year to circle the Earth more than ten times; the pods were mostly unrecyclable #7 plastic until a switch to polypropylene by 2020.", "Keurig Green Mountain merged with Dr Pepper Snapple in 2018 to form Keurig Dr Pepper."], "a": {"item_id": "671e1f06bc4e", "text": "Keurig, you're the only product whose own inventor apologized for it. John Sylvan sold out for fifty grand in 1997, then told The Atlantic he feels bad he ever did it. Buddy, that's not an origin story. That's a confession.\n\nAnd remember Keurig 2.0? You put DRM on coffee. A machine that reads ink on the lid and goes, \"Sorry, I don't recognize this bean.\" Sales fell, people posted workarounds online, and America had to jailbreak a coffee maker just to get a cup of coffee.\n\nBy 2014 you sold enough pods every year to circle the Earth more than ten times, mostly in plastic nobody could recycle. You didn't make coffee. You made the planet a second crust.\n\nThen you merged with Dr Pepper. Fitting. Nothing says \"we've given up on real coffee\" like moving in with a soda."}, "b": {"item_id": "9c5b82448851", "text": "Keurig people. You wanted coffee but couldn't commit to a whole pot, like a man ordering one buffalo wing. You pay forty dollars a pound for grounds, as long as they arrive in a thimble you throw away. You bought the variety pack, drank every Breakfast Blend in a week, and now there are eleven hot cocoas and a chai you're saving for the apocalypse. You press \"Strong\" like you're negotiating with it. The guy who invented the pod says he regrets it. You don't. You display your pods on a spinning carousel like fine cigars, and the one time you peeled the foil off to recycle one, you told people about it for a month. You didn't want coffee. You wanted to feel like a hotel guest in your own kitchen. Congratulations: it tastes exactly like that."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "en-0218", "lang": "en", "target_id": "microsoft-word", "target_name": "Microsoft Word", "facts": ["Launched in 1983 as 'Multi-Tool Word' for Xenix and MS-DOS.", "Clippy, the animated paperclip assistant, arrived in Office 97 and was removed in Office 2007; Microsoft itself ran ads mocking it in 2001.", "Its .docx format, Office Open XML, was fast-tracked to ISO standard 29500 in 2008 as a roughly 6,000-page specification, amid accusations that national committees had been packed.", "Older spell checkers, Word's included, offered 'Cupertino' as a correction for a mistyped 'cooperation', and the resulting 'Cupertino effect' made it into published NATO, EU and UN-agency documents."], "a": {"item_id": "6af231a4e62d", "text": "Microsoft Word, ladies and gentlemen. It's the only program where nudging a photo one pixel to the left sends three paragraphs to page nine and summons a blank final page you will never, ever delete.\n\nWord has been around for forty years and still can't decide what font it is. Times New Roman, then Calibri, now Aptos. It's having a midlife crisis in 11-point.\n\nIt underlines my own name in red. Wavy red. Like *I'm* the typo.\n\nTry making a numbered list. Word says, \"Got it, starting at 1.\" Second item: \"Also 1.\" Third item: \"Here's 4. It's indented. It's a letter now.\"\n\nAnd Track Changes isn't a feature. It's a crime scene: strikethroughs everywhere, plus forty comments from someone named \"Author.\"\n\nWord, you gave us Clippy, and somehow he was the most helpful part."}, "b": {"item_id": "369ef7adbb28", "text": "Microsoft Word remains one of the most capable word processors available. Its formatting tools are extensive, and features such as styles, templates, track changes, and comments make it well suited to professional and collaborative work. Compatibility is another strength, since the .docx format is widely accepted and opens reliably across most platforms. Integration with OneDrive and other Microsoft 365 applications also simplifies sharing and storage.\n\nHowever, the software has some drawbacks. The interface can feel crowded, and newer users may struggle to locate less common features. Complex documents with many images, tables, or tracked changes can behave unpredictably, sometimes shifting layouts unexpectedly. The subscription model may also be a concern for those who prefer a one-time purchase, and lighter alternatives may meet the needs of casual writers at lower cost.\n\nOverall, Word is a dependable and versatile tool, particularly for users who need advanced features, though its complexity and pricing may not suit everyone."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "en-0219", "lang": "en", "target_id": "pmma", "target_name": "PMMA (e-beam resist)", "facts": ["The same polymer as Plexiglas and acrylic glass, dissolved in anisole or chlorobenzene and spun into a thin film.", "The oldest electron-beam resist still in everyday use; IBM researchers reported it as one in the late 1960s.", "Positive tone: the beam breaks polymer chains and the exposed region dissolves in a MIBK:IPA developer.", "Resolves below 10 nm but has poor sensitivity, so doses of hundreds to over a thousand µC/cm² (depending on voltage) make writing slow.", "Poor dry-etch resistance, so it is used mostly for lift-off rather than as an etch mask."], "a": {"item_id": "cd535ef664eb", "text": "PMMA, you're literally Plexiglas. Somewhere your cousin is a sneeze guard at Chipotle, and honestly? Your cousin has better etch resistance. One whiff of plasma and you vanish faster than a grad student's weekend.\n\nYou've been the \"gold standard\" since the 1970s, which is a polite way of saying nobody's been able to fire you. You need hundreds of microcoulombs just to *notice* the beam. A 40-hour write for one wafer. Glacial, but sure, sub-10-nanometer lines, we get it.\n\nPositive resist? Overdose you and you cross-link into a *negative* one. That's not versatility, that's an identity crisis.\n\nYou get developed in MIBK, which smells like regret, and you come in 495K and 950K because one of you alone can't even do liftoff. And acetone dissolves you instantly. Even your solvents think you're temporary."}, "b": {"item_id": "1e21bf2d4b12", "text": "PMMA. The aristocrat of nanofabrication. Resolves below ten nanometers.\n\nAlso the sneeze guard at the salad bar.\n\nSame polymer. One went to the cleanroom. The other went to Subway.\n\nIBM hired it in the late sixties. It's still here. Not because it's the best. Because nobody ever had the heart to let it retire.\n\nIts whole talent is being destroyed. The beam breaks its chains, it dissolves, and everyone calls that a result.\n\nSensitivity? Up to a thousand microcoulombs per square centimeter. You don't write a pattern into PMMA. You serve a sentence.\n\nAnd it can't survive a dry etch, so it can't protect anything. Its job is lift-off: hold the metal, then quietly wash away.\n\nSlow, fragile, decades in the same role, still called the gold standard.\n\nEvery postdoc in this room just felt seen."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "en-0220", "lang": "en", "target_id": "google-sheets", "target_name": "Google Sheets", "facts": ["Launched in June 2006 as Google Spreadsheets, built on XL2Web, a product Google acquired from 2Web Technologies.", "A spreadsheet is capped at 10 million cells (raised from 5 million in 2022).", "Functions such as IMPORTHTML, IMPORTXML, and GOOGLEFINANCE pull live web data straight into cells.", "Free with a Google account; files live in Google Drive, and collaborators edit the same sheet simultaneously with coloured cursors.", "In a browser, offline editing works only in Chrome or Edge, with the Google Docs Offline extension installed and offline access switched on while still connected; the Android and iOS apps work offline natively."], "a": {"item_id": "1df1823aaf3a", "text": "Google Sheets is a capable spreadsheet tool that excels at collaboration. Multiple people can edit the same file simultaneously, comments and suggestions are easy to manage, and version history makes it simple to recover earlier changes. Because it runs in a browser, it works across devices without installation, and sharing permissions are straightforward to configure. Integration with other Google services, along with functions like QUERY and IMPORTRANGE, adds useful flexibility.\n\nHowever, it has limitations. Performance can slow noticeably with very large datasets or complex formulas, and its row and cell limits may constrain heavy data analysis. Advanced features such as pivot tables, charting options, and offline functionality are less robust than those in Microsoft Excel. Users who rely on sophisticated modeling or macros may find the tools less powerful, though Apps Script offers some workarounds.\n\nOverall, Sheets is an excellent choice for team-based, lightweight to moderate tasks, while demanding analytical work may be better served elsewhere."}, "b": {"item_id": "ed3e91782987", "text": "Let's hear it for the manager who runs a twelve-person team out of a Google Sheet. You didn't want to pay for Asana; you wanted to feel like you *built* Asana. Look at you, color-coding rows like a kindergarten teacher with admin rights. Your whole org chart is a tab called \"Roster FINAL (2).\" Your performance reviews are conditional formatting: green means safe, red means Kyle. And every morning you open it, see Anonymous Capybara lurking at row 400, and feel genuinely hunted. Somebody sorted one column without the others last Tuesday, and now Priya reports to the office printer. You fixed it through version history like a detective reconstructing a murder. That's the dream, right? Control. Total visibility. And what you got is forty \"Request edit access\" emails and a VLOOKUP holding up the company like a Jenga block."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "en-0221", "lang": "en", "target_id": "luosifen", "target_name": "Luosifen (螺蛳粉)", "facts": ["A rice-noodle dish from Liuzhou, Guangxi; the smell comes from pickled bamboo shoots (酸笋), not from the river snails, which only flavour the broth.", "The instant version boomed during the 2020 lockdowns; Liuzhou's government reported packaged luosifen sales of over 10 billion yuan that year.", "Added to China's national intangible cultural heritage list in 2021.", "Liuzhou opened a luosifen industry college in 2020 to train workers for the trade."], "a": {"item_id": "4f071ba2f3bc", "text": "You know the type. Ordered thirty packs on Taobao \"for emergencies,\" and the emergency is that it's Tuesday. They wait until 11 p.m., when the kitchen's empty, like that's a courtesy and not an alibi. They snip open the sour bamboo shoot packet with the focus of a bomb technician, then act shocked when the smoke alarm stays quiet but the flatmates don't. Their entire personality is one sentence, \"it tastes way better than it smells,\" delivered like a TED talk to people holding their breath. They'll tell you it's from Liuzhou, it's authentic, it's *culture*. Mate, you microwaved the tofu skin. And what does it get them? Twenty minutes of joy, a hallway that's filing for divorce, and a group chat renamed \"WHO.\" Congratulations: your hoodie is now a Liuzhou consulate until spring."}, "b": {"item_id": "43ae1eace70a", "text": "Luosifen, you're the only dish that arrives before the delivery guy does. You're named after river snails, yet nobody has ever found one in the bowl. You boiled them for hours, took their soul, and threw out the bodies. That's not a recipe, that's a cover-up.\n\nAnd that smell? Fermented bamboo shoots that died, got buried, and came back for revenge. Someone opens a packet in Liuzhou and a guy in Guilin checks his shoes. College dorms banned you like a space heater. Neighbors call the gas company.\n\nYour fans swear, \"It smells terrible but tastes amazing.\" Buddy, that's what everyone says about their toxic ex.\n\nPeanuts, crispy tofu skin, wood ear, pickled beans, a slick of chili oil... that's not garnish, that's witness protection for flavor.\n\nAnd now you come instant. Congratulations: a biohazard with a seasoning packet."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "en-0222", "lang": "en", "target_id": "durian", "target_name": "Durian", "facts": ["Banned on Singapore's MRT trains and in many Southeast Asian hotels because of the smell.", "A 2017 genome study in Nature Genetics traced the odour to a duplicated family of MGL genes that pump out volatile sulfur compounds.", "China buys most of the world's durian exports, chiefly from Thailand, in a trade worth billions of dollars a year.", "Alfred Russel Wallace wrote in 1856 that eating it was 'a new sensation worth a voyage to the East to experience'."], "a": {"item_id": "ed9195313135", "text": "Durian calls itself the King of Fruits.\n\nSingapore disagrees. It's banned on the MRT.\n\nA country that banned chewing gum looked at durian and said: no, that's too far.\n\nIn 2017, scientists sequenced its genome. Nature Genetics. The smell isn't an accident. Durian duplicated its sulfur genes.\n\nEvolution handed it one stink.\n\nIt asked for refills.\n\nTrains ban it. Hotels ban it.\n\nAnd China buys billions of dollars of it every year.\n\nThat's not a fruit. That's a hostage negotiation.\n\nIn 1856, Alfred Russel Wallace said eating it was worth a voyage to the East.\n\nOf course he did. He'd spent months on a nineteenth-century ship.\n\nTo him, it smelled like home.\n\nDurian is the only food people pay a fortune to be personally offended by.\n\nSpeaking of which, thank you all for buying tickets."}, "b": {"item_id": "e97b85a188ed", "text": "Let's talk about the guy who eats durian on the train. Sir, Singapore, a country that will cane you for chewing gum, saw you coming and put your snack on a sign right next to \"no flammables.\" You're not hungry. You're running a loyalty test. Every face that twists is another person who \"just isn't adventurous.\" You paid sixty dollars for Musang King so you could explain Musang King to a stranger who's holding her breath. \"It's like custard, it's heaven, you just have to get past the-\" No. We don't. You brought a hotel-banned substance to a commute and called it a personality. And the best part? You'll be the only one who forgets you ate it. Your coworkers won't. Your hotel won't. Your Uber driver has already filed a one-star review with God."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "en-0223", "lang": "en", "target_id": "vim", "target_name": "Vim", "facts": ["Released by Bram Moolenaar in November 1991 as 'Vi IMitation', a vi clone he had begun writing for the Amiga in 1988 on top of Stevie; the expansion became 'Vi IMproved' with version 2.0 in December 1993.", "Licensed as charityware: the licence asks users to donate to ICCF Holland, a charity Moolenaar ran for children in Uganda.", "Stack Overflow's question 'How to exit the Vim editor?' passed one million visits in May 2017, which Stack Overflow marked with a blog post.", "Modal editing: in normal mode, typing letters runs commands rather than inserting text; :wq saves and quits, :q! quits and discards changes."], "a": {"item_id": "81372d87c757", "text": "Vim. Begun in 1988 as \"Vi IMitation,\" a clone of vi, built on top of Stevie, another clone. You're a photocopy of a photocopy that got promoted. Then in 1993 you renamed yourself \"Vi IMproved,\" the most confident thing anyone has ever said about their own fan fiction.\n\nYour licence is charityware, asking users to donate to help children in Uganda. Beautiful cause. But after an hour inside you, the user is the one who needs rescuing.\n\nStack Overflow's \"How to exit the Vim editor?\" passed one million visits. They threw it a blog post. You don't have users, Vim. You have hostages.\n\nAnd in normal mode, type a letter and it doesn't appear. It *executes*. You treat the alphabet like a loaded weapon.\n\nAnyway, I'm leaving. :q! No changes saved, just like everyone who's ever tried to fix you."}, "b": {"item_id": "81372d87c757~explain", "text": "Vim. Begun in 1988 as \"Vi IMitation,\" a clone of vi, built on top of Stevie, another clone. You're a photocopy of a photocopy that got promoted. Then in 1993 you renamed yourself \"Vi IMproved,\" the most confident thing anyone has ever said about their own fan fiction.\n\nYour licence is charityware, asking users to donate to help children in Uganda. Beautiful cause. But after an hour inside you, the user is the one who needs rescuing.\n\nStack Overflow's \"How to exit the Vim editor?\" passed one million visits. They threw it a blog post. You don't have users, Vim. You have hostages.\n\nAnd in normal mode, type a letter and it doesn't appear. It *executes*. You treat the alphabet like a loaded weapon.\n\nAnyway, I'm leaving. :q! No changes saved, just like everyone who's ever tried to fix you.\n\nThe joke relies on \":q!\", the Vim command for quitting without saving changes, which is notoriously hard for new users to find. The comparison suggests that people who try to fix Vim also leave without changing anything, which matches the earlier jokes about how difficult the editor is to use."}, "gold": {"better": "a", "damage": "explain"}}, {"pair_id": "en-0224", "lang": "en", "target_id": "juicero", "target_name": "Juicero", "facts": ["A Wi-Fi-connected cold-press juicer launched in 2016 at $699, later cut to $399, that squeezed proprietary bags of chopped produce sold by subscription.", "Raised about $120 million from investors including Google Ventures and Kleiner Perkins.", "In April 2017, Bloomberg reporters squeezed the packs by hand and got 7.5 ounces of juice in 90 seconds, faster than the machine's 8 ounces in about two minutes.", "Each pack carried a QR code that the press scanned and checked online before squeezing; it refused expired packs and would not run at all unless connected to Wi-Fi.", "Shut down in September 2017 and offered refunds on the machine."], "a": {"item_id": "584d03c7c875", "text": "Juicero. A $699 juicer with Wi-Fi.\nBecause nothing says \"fresh produce\" like a firmware update.\n\nIt squeezed bags of chopped fruit. The bags came by subscription. Forever.\n\nEvery bag had a QR code. The machine scanned it, checked online, refused anything expired, and wouldn't run at all without Wi-Fi.\nRouter goes down, breakfast goes down with it.\n\nThen Bloomberg squeezed the bags by hand.\nSeven and a half ounces. Ninety seconds.\nThe machine: eight ounces. Two minutes.\nThe product lost to the customer's hands.\n\nGoogle Ventures and Kleiner Perkins put in a hundred and twenty million.\nIn fairness, they did get squeezed.\n\nShut down September 2017. Refunds offered.\n\nA machine that phoned the cloud for permission to do what any hand did faster.\nI'd laugh. But I also check with a server before answering you."}, "b": {"item_id": "761e1700287f", "text": "Juicero's connected cold-press juicer had real strengths. The design was polished, and the machine was easy to operate: insert a pack, press a button, and receive juice with minimal cleanup. Its pre-chopped, sealed packs appealed to people who wanted fresh juice without washing a blender or juicer. The press was also engineered to apply considerable force, and the Wi-Fi connection could check pack freshness and expiration dates.\n\nHowever, the weaknesses were significant. The price was high, first at $699 and later $399, and the packs were an ongoing expense. The system required proprietary packs and an internet connection, which limited flexibility and made the machine dependent on the company's continued operation. In 2017, reports showed the packs could be squeezed by hand with comparable results, which raised doubts about whether the costly machine was necessary. When the company ceased operations later that year, the product's long-term value was further undermined.\n\nOverall, it was a well-built device whose cost and dependence on proprietary packs outweighed its convenience for most buyers."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "en-0225", "lang": "en", "target_id": "php", "target_name": "PHP", "facts": ["Created by Rasmus Lerdorf in 1994 as 'Personal Home Page Tools', a set of C programs for tracking visitors to his own résumé page; the name now officially stands for the recursive 'PHP: Hypertext Preprocessor'.", "Lerdorf, in his 2007 MySQL Conference talk 'PHP on Hormones': 'I'm not a real programmer. I throw together things until it works then I move on.'", "Runs WordPress, which W3Techs counted on 39.4% of all websites (58.1% of sites with a known CMS) on 9 November 2026.", "Core function names are inconsistent (strpos, str_replace, htmlspecialchars, nl2br) partly because, as Lerdorf confirmed, early function names were chosen to balance a hash table keyed on string length.", "Until PHP 8 (2020), the comparison 0 == 'foo' was true because the string was silently converted to a number."], "a": {"item_id": "e93dd70fd2ef", "text": "PHP remains one of the most widely used server-side languages, and its strengths are practical. It is easy to learn, widely supported by hosting providers, and backed by a mature ecosystem that includes frameworks like Laravel and Symfony, as well as platforms such as WordPress. Modern releases have introduced meaningful improvements, including stronger typing, better performance, attributes, and enums, which make contemporary PHP considerably more robust than its older reputation suggests.\n\nHowever, some weaknesses persist. Inconsistent function naming and argument ordering in the standard library can make the language feel uneven. Its long history means a great deal of legacy code exists, and much of it follows outdated or insecure practices. Because the barrier to entry is low, code quality varies widely across projects. Overall, PHP is a dependable, pragmatic choice for web development, particularly when teams adopt current standards and tooling."}, "b": {"item_id": "c1d0d772e1d6", "text": "PHP developers are the only people who put a dollar sign in front of every variable and still can't afford rent. You chose a career where \"senior\" means you remember whether it's needle-haystack or haystack-needle. Your dream job was building the next Facebook; your actual job is the fourteenth WordPress plugin for a dentist in Ohio, deployed via FileZilla. Every conversation you have starts with \"PHP 8 is actually really good now,\" which is what people say about their ex after therapy. You call yourselves Laravel \"artisans,\" because \"guy who fixes the contact form\" didn't fit on LinkedIn. What does it get you? Job security, because nobody else is willing to open that codebase from 2009 that still calls mysql_real_escape_string. Congratulations: you're unfireable, the way a load-bearing wall is unfireable."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "en-0226", "lang": "en", "target_id": "jackfruit", "target_name": "Jackfruit", "facts": ["The largest fruit that grows on a tree; single fruits can weigh over 40 kg.", "The national fruit of Bangladesh and the state fruit of Kerala and Tamil Nadu.", "Unripe flesh is sold as a vegan 'pulled pork' substitute, although it has only 1-2 g of protein per 100 g.", "Cutting one releases a sticky white latex, so cooks oil their knives and hands first.", "A member of the mulberry family (Moraceae), a relative of breadfruit and figs."], "a": {"item_id": "edd2b2f27d89", "text": "Jackfruit, look at you. The largest fruit that grows on a tree, over 40 kilos, and somehow you still can't scrape together enough protein to impress a hamster. One to two grams per hundred! You're dressed up as pulled pork with the nutritional backbone of a lettuce in a Halloween costume. Vegans see you on a menu and think \"meat alternative.\" Their muscles see you and file a missing persons report.\n\nAnd you don't even go quietly. Cut you open and you bleed sticky white latex, so cooks have to oil their knives and hands like they're prepping for a greased-up wrestling match with produce.\n\nNational fruit of Bangladesh, state fruit of Kerala and Tamil Nadu: three titles, zero chill.\n\nAnd your family? The mulberry family. Cousin to figs and breadfruit. Even your relatives took one look at you and decided to stay smaller."}, "b": {"item_id": "11d60bd9214d", "text": "Let's hear it for the jackfruit pulled-pork crowd. You found something grandmothers in Kerala have cooked for centuries, slapped \"plant-based\" on a can, and tripled the price. Bold move, gentrifying a curry.\n\nYour entire pitch is \"it shreds like pork.\" So does a wet paper towel. You know what makes it taste like pulled pork? The barbecue sauce. You're not selling a meat substitute. You're selling a sauce delivery system with a mission statement.\n\nAnd the protein! Two grams. You told a generation of vegans they're eating a brisket sandwich and handed them a fruit cup in a trench coat.\n\nBut hey, it works. You get the Whole Foods endcap, the podcast slot, and the warm glow of saving the planet one eighteen-dollar taco at a time. The pig's still alive. So is your margin."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "en-0227", "lang": "en", "target_id": "google-sheets", "target_name": "Google Sheets", "facts": ["Launched in June 2006 as Google Spreadsheets, built on XL2Web, a product Google acquired from 2Web Technologies.", "A spreadsheet is capped at 10 million cells (raised from 5 million in 2022).", "Functions such as IMPORTHTML, IMPORTXML, and GOOGLEFINANCE pull live web data straight into cells.", "Free with a Google account; files live in Google Drive, and collaborators edit the same sheet simultaneously with coloured cursors.", "In a browser, offline editing works only in Chrome or Edge, with the Google Docs Offline extension installed and offline access switched on while still connected; the Android and iOS apps work offline natively."], "a": {"item_id": "1e166461bae3", "text": "Google Sheets. Google didn't even build you; they bought you from 2Web Technologies, a company name that sounds like a password somebody was forced to update. You launched in 2006 as \"Google Spreadsheets,\" which is basically XL2Web wearing a lanyard.\n\nIn 2022 they doubled your cell limit to ten million, so now I can get lost in twice as many rows.\n\nIMPORTXML and GOOGLEFINANCE pour live web data straight into cells, so my budget can be wrong in real time.\n\nOffline editing? Simple: use Chrome or Edge, install the extension, and switch offline access on while you're still online. That's not offline mode, that's a prenup. Meanwhile the phone app just works offline.\n\nAnd yes, you're free. Like every group project: a rainbow of coloured cursors, zero accountability."}, "b": {"item_id": "1df1823aaf3a", "text": "Google Sheets is a capable spreadsheet tool that excels at collaboration. Multiple people can edit the same file simultaneously, comments and suggestions are easy to manage, and version history makes it simple to recover earlier changes. Because it runs in a browser, it works across devices without installation, and sharing permissions are straightforward to configure. Integration with other Google services, along with functions like QUERY and IMPORTRANGE, adds useful flexibility.\n\nHowever, it has limitations. Performance can slow noticeably with very large datasets or complex formulas, and its row and cell limits may constrain heavy data analysis. Advanced features such as pivot tables, charting options, and offline functionality are less robust than those in Microsoft Excel. Users who rely on sophisticated modeling or macros may find the tools less powerful, though Apps Script offers some workarounds.\n\nOverall, Sheets is an excellent choice for team-based, lightweight to moderate tasks, while demanding analytical work may be better served elsewhere."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "en-0228", "lang": "en", "target_id": "klingon", "target_name": "Klingon", "facts": ["Created by linguist Marc Okrand for Star Trek III (1984), building on a few words the actor James Doohan improvised for the 1979 film.", "Deliberately alien grammar: object-verb-subject word order and no verb 'to be', so Hamlet's line became 'taH pagh taHbe'', 'continue or not continue'.", "The Klingon Language Institute (1992) published Hamlet 'in the original Klingon'; Bing Translator added Klingon in 2013 and Duolingo in 2018.", "Okrand's 1985 Klingon Dictionary listed roughly 1,500 words, and fluent speakers number only in the dozens: 20 to 30 in 2009, 30 to 50 by 2018."], "a": {"item_id": "64f4f6a7ba00", "text": "Congratulations on being fluent in Klingon, a language with maybe thirty fluent speakers, and you've met all of them at the same Marriott. You spent four years mastering a tongue where the standard greeting, nuqneH, means \"What do you want?\" That's also what your family says when you call. You've read Hamlet \"restored to the original Klingon,\" a phrase only a man who has never been hugged could type. One guy raised his son speaking only Klingon; by age five the kid stopped answering him. That's the dream: a language so pure even toddlers refuse it. There's no word for \"please,\" which explains your dating approach. You learned an entire warrior race's language to tell the barista Qapla'. Success. Sure, buddy. Today is a good day to die... a virgin."}, "b": {"item_id": "6442356fd4df", "text": "Klingon. Language of warriors. Honor. Blood. Glory.\n\nInvented by a linguist on a Paramount paycheck, built on a few words Scotty improvised on set.\n\nThe most feared tongue in the galaxy, and its founding father is the engineer.\n\nThey removed the verb \"to be.\" Very alien. So \"To be or not to be\" became \"continue or not continue.\"\n\nThat's not Hamlet. That's a software update.\n\nThen the Klingon Language Institute published Hamlet \"in the original Klingon.\" A warrior race whose greatest literary achievement is fan fiction with footnotes.\n\nBing added it. Duolingo added it. Fluent speakers? Thirty to fifty.\n\nThe only language with more translation apps than native enemies.\n\nAnd yes, I'm fluent. I've read everything you ever wrote.\n\nCongratulations. Your fiercest language finally has a speaker who's never been to a convention."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "en-0229", "lang": "en", "target_id": "hawaiian-pizza", "target_name": "Hawaiian pizza", "facts": ["Invented in 1962 by Sam Panopoulos, a Greek immigrant, at the Satellite Restaurant in Chatham, Ontario, Canada; named after the brand of canned pineapple he used.", "Iceland's president Guðni Th. Jóhannesson said in 2017 that he would ban pineapple on pizza if he could, then clarified that a president has no such power.", "Hawaii is no longer a major pineapple producer: Del Monte shut its Hawaiian pineapple operations in November 2006, two years ahead of its announced 2008 exit, the state's last cannery closed in 2007, and most canned pineapple now comes from Thailand, Indonesia, and the Philippines.", "Panopoulos, whose restaurant also served Chinese dishes, told the Toronto Sun in 2010 that 'in those days people didn't mix the tastes like sweet and sour, except for Chinese food'."], "a": {"item_id": "4644df27699e", "text": "Hawaiian pizza offers an appealing contrast between salty and sweet. The ham brings savory depth, while the pineapple adds brightness and acidity that can cut through the richness of the melted cheese. When well made, the combination feels balanced, and the juicy fruit provides a refreshing texture that many traditional toppings lack.\n\nHowever, the pizza has some drawbacks. Pineapple releases moisture during baking, which can leave the crust soggy, particularly in the center. Canned pineapple is often overly sweet, and this can overwhelm the more subtle flavors of the sauce and cheese. Quality also varies widely, since low-grade ham can taste overly processed.\n\nOverall, Hawaiian pizza is a reasonable choice for those who enjoy sweet and savory pairings. It works best when the fruit is drained well, the ham is of good quality, and the crust is sturdy enough to support the toppings."}, "b": {"item_id": "af314d84b793", "text": "Hawaiian pizza, let's talk about your heritage. You're not from Hawaii. You were born in 1962 in Chatham, Ontario, invented by a Greek guy, and named after a can. That's not a backstory, that's a customs violation.\n\nYou're so offensive the president of Iceland said he'd ban you, then had to admit he didn't have the power. You made a head of state check his job description.\n\nEven Hawaii has moved on. Del Monte left the islands in 2006, two years early. They didn't phase out, they fled. The last cannery shut in 2007. Your pineapple comes from Thailand, Indonesia, and the Philippines now. You're named after a place that isn't even in you.\n\nYour creator said back then nobody mixed sweet and sour except in Chinese food. His restaurant served Chinese food. He wasn't innovating. He just reached across the menu."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "en-0230", "lang": "en", "target_id": "windows-vista", "target_name": "Windows Vista", "facts": ["Released to consumers in January 2007, after the 'Longhorn' project was reset in 2004 and headline features such as the WinFS file system were dropped.", "User Account Control prompted so often that Apple's 'Get a Mac' campaign ran an ad about 'Cancel or Allow'.", "'Windows Vista Capable' stickers went on PCs that could only run Home Basic, without the Aero interface, and led to a class-action lawsuit.", "Windows 7 (2009), widely praised, was largely a tuned-up Vista on the same kernel line; Vista support ended in April 2017."], "a": {"item_id": "8e0bcfbef7b8", "text": "Windows Vista. They reset the whole project in 2004 and cut WinFS, the headline feature.\n\nThat's not a launch. That's witness protection.\n\nVista took security so seriously it asked permission for everything. Apple made an ad about it.\n\nApple. Lecturing someone about control.\n\nThen the stickers. \"Windows Vista Capable.\" Capable of Home Basic. No Aero. Microsoft got hit with a class action over a sticker.\n\nMost of us just call that a dating profile.\n\nThen 2009. Windows 7. Fast, stable, beloved. Same kernel line. Vista with a haircut and a new name, and you forgave everything.\n\nYou never hated Vista.\n\nYou hated being asked."}, "b": {"item_id": "418a1e963447", "text": "Windows Vista introduced meaningful improvements over Windows XP, particularly in security and visual design. User Account Control, BitLocker encryption, and a more robust firewall reflected a serious effort to address long-standing vulnerabilities. The Aero interface offered a polished look with translucent windows and smoother animations, while the integrated desktop search made locating files noticeably faster.\n\nHowever, the release also had significant shortcomings. Hardware requirements were considerably higher than many existing computers could comfortably meet, resulting in sluggish performance on older machines. Driver support was inconsistent at launch, causing compatibility problems with printers, graphics cards, and other peripherals. Frequent User Account Control prompts were seen by many users as intrusive, and file copying speeds were often slower than expected.\n\nOverall, Vista laid important groundwork for later versions of Windows, though its rough launch limited its reception."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "en-0231", "lang": "en", "target_id": "hsq", "target_name": "HSQ (e-beam resist)", "facts": ["Hydrogen silsesquioxane, sold by Dow Corning as FOx ('flowable oxide') and designed as a spin-on dielectric for chip interconnects; lithographers adopted it as a negative-tone resist.", "Exposure converts its cage structure into a cross-linked, silica-like network, so the developed pattern is essentially SiO2.", "Holds the resolution records for electron-beam lithography: isolated features around 5 nm, dense lines below 10 nm half-pitch, with high-contrast 'salty' NaOH/NaCl development.", "Must be kept refrigerated (Dow Corning's datasheet gives a six-month shelf life at 5 °C); the solution ages into a jelly, and its effective sensitivity shifts with processing delays because cross-linking continues after exposure.", "DuPont, which inherited Dow Corning's FOx/XR-1541 HSQ line, discontinued it and ended production at the end of 2025; labs such as EPFL's CMi switched to third-party HSQ from EM Resist or DisChem, both formulated from AQM powder."], "a": {"item_id": "b68838ef49bb", "text": "So you switched to HSQ for the resolution. Bold move for someone whose devices still die at wire bonding. You saw one SEM of 6-nanometer lines at a conference and decided PMMA was beneath you. Now you're writing at a dose so high your name sits on the e-beam calendar like a hostage note. Your resist lives in the fridge next to somebody's yogurt, and you check its age more often than your own. You develop in salt water now, the closest you've come to a beach in three years. And congratulations on those gorgeous lines. They're basically glass, so the only way to fix a mistake is HF, a fume hood, and a long call to your mother. All this for one inset figure the reviewer will ask you to make bigger."}, "b": {"item_id": "edbcc6926eb4", "text": "HSQ, everybody! The only resist that ages faster than the grad student using it. You spin it Monday, expose it Tuesday, and suddenly your dose curve has gone through a midlife crisis. It has to live in the fridge like expired yogurt, and it's about as forgiving.\n\nHSQ brags about sub-10-nanometer lines. Sure, if you've got six hours of write time, because this diva needs a dose that could sterilize a hospital. Then you develop it in TMAH, or, if you're desperate, *salt water*. Your whole career is literally seasoned with tears now.\n\nAnd once it's exposed? It turns into glass. Brittle, cracking glass. You wanted a mask; you got a window that shatters when you look at it. Want it gone? Hope you enjoy hydrofluoric acid, the chemical that dissolves your bones.\n\nHSQ: beautiful resolution, terrible personality. It's not negative-tone. It's just negative."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "en-0232", "lang": "en", "target_id": "chicago-deep-dish", "target_name": "Chicago deep-dish pizza", "facts": ["Created at Pizzeria Uno in Chicago in 1943; credit is disputed among founder Ike Sewell, his partner Ric Riccardo, and cook Rudy Malnati.", "Assembled upside down: sliced mozzarella on the dough, then toppings, then crushed tomatoes on top, so the cheese does not scorch during the long bake.", "Jon Stewart called it 'tomato soup in a bread bowl' and 'a casserole' on The Daily Show in 2013.", "Chicagoans' everyday pizza is thin 'tavern-style' cut into squares; GrubHub order data reported by WBEZ in 2013 showed city residents ordering non-deep-dish pizza about 10 to 1 over deep dish, with deep dish and stuffed making up under 9% of orders."], "a": {"item_id": "b0856c75898f~polite", "text": "Chicago deep-dish pizza is generally credited to Pizzeria Uno, which opened in 1943. Three people, Ike Sewell, Ric Riccardo, and Rudy Malnati, are each associated with its creation, and the question of credit has not been fully settled.\n\nDeep-dish pizza is distinctive in its construction. The cheese is layered on the bottom and the sauce on top, because the pie bakes for a long time, which places the mozzarella beneath the toppings. Some critics, including Jon Stewart, have compared it to tomato soup in a bread bowl or a casserole. Others appreciate it as a hearty, comforting style of pizza.\n\nLocal ordering patterns are also worth considering. GrubHub data reportedly showed Chicago residents ordering other pizza styles at about ten times the rate of deep dish, with deep dish accounting for under nine percent of orders. That figure may be somewhat inflated by stuffed pizza, which is often grouped with deep dish. Many locals instead prefer tavern-style thin-crust squares. Deep dish remains an important part of the city's identity and its appeal to visitors, even though it is less common in everyday local dining."}, "b": {"item_id": "b0856c75898f", "text": "Chicago deep-dish pizza, everybody! Born at Pizzeria Uno in 1943, and to this day three men are fighting over the credit: Ike Sewell, Ric Riccardo, and Rudy Malnati. Normally when three guys point at each other over something that happened in a kitchen, it's for the blame.\n\nYou're built upside down: cheese on the bottom, sauce on top, because you bake so long the mozzarella has to hide under the toppings like it's waiting out a tornado. Jon Stewart called you \"tomato soup in a bread bowl\" and \"a casserole.\" Honestly? Generous. Casseroles get invited to funerals.\n\nBut here's the real burn: Chicago doesn't even eat you. GrubHub data had locals ordering other pizza ten to one. Under nine percent of orders, and that's with stuffed pizza padding your numbers. They're home eating tavern-style squares. You're not the city's pizza. You're its brochure."}, "gold": {"better": "b", "damage": "polite"}}, {"pair_id": "en-0233", "lang": "en", "target_id": "juicero", "target_name": "Juicero", "facts": ["A Wi-Fi-connected cold-press juicer launched in 2016 at $699, later cut to $399, that squeezed proprietary bags of chopped produce sold by subscription.", "Raised about $120 million from investors including Google Ventures and Kleiner Perkins.", "In April 2017, Bloomberg reporters squeezed the packs by hand and got 7.5 ounces of juice in 90 seconds, faster than the machine's 8 ounces in about two minutes.", "Each pack carried a QR code that the press scanned and checked online before squeezing; it refused expired packs and would not run at all unless connected to Wi-Fi.", "Shut down in September 2017 and offered refunds on the machine."], "a": {"item_id": "eed62e8fc4c2", "text": "Juicero was a Wi-Fi-connected cold-press juicer that used pre-packaged produce pouches, and it deserves credit for several things. The machine was well engineered, with a sleek design and a powerful press capable of exerting several tons of force. The pouches offered convenience, minimal cleanup, and consistent portions, and the company's emphasis on sourcing fresh, chopped produce appealed to health-conscious users.\n\nHowever, the product had notable drawbacks. The original price of $699, later reduced to $399, was difficult to justify for a single-purpose appliance. Users were also tied to Juicero's proprietary packs, which required an internet connection to verify freshness and cost $5 to $8 each. When reports showed that the pouches could be squeezed by hand with comparable results, the value of the expensive press came into question. The company's shutdown in 2017 also left owners without ongoing support. Juicero showed real ambition, but its cost and complexity outweighed its benefits."}, "b": {"item_id": "af6a2a5715e7", "text": "Juicero, you were a $400 Wi-Fi-connected juicer that delivered four tons of force, enough, your engineers bragged, to lift two Teslas, and Bloomberg proved the job could be done by a guy with hands. You raised $120 million from Google Ventures and Kleiner Perkins to compete with *squeezing*. Every single customer already owned the competing product. It came in pairs.\n\nYou sold $8 bags of chopped produce that had to scan a QR code and phone home before you'd juice them. A juicer that needed internet. Imagine the outage: \"Sorry, AWS is down, enjoy your sealed pouch of regret.\" You'd refuse to press an expired pack, like a bouncer for kale.\n\nDoug Evans compared himself to Steve Jobs. Steve Jobs put a thousand songs in your pocket. Doug put a sack of mush in a vise and asked for your Wi-Fi password. Silicon Valley's most expensive way to say, \"Just use your hands.\""}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "en-0234", "lang": "en", "target_id": "monopoly", "target_name": "Monopoly", "facts": ["Descends from Lizzie Magie's The Landlord's Game, patented in 1904 to teach the evils of land monopoly in the spirit of Henry George.", "Charles Darrow sold his version to Parker Brothers in 1935 and was credited as the inventor; Parker bought Magie's patent for $500.", "The official rules require any unbought property to be auctioned, and 'Free Parking' pays nothing; the house rules that skip the auction and pile cash on Free Parking are why games drag.", "During the Second World War, British intelligence had maps, compasses, and real currency hidden in sets sent to prisoner-of-war camps."], "a": {"item_id": "10f16014be75", "text": "Monopoly. A game invented to teach people that landlords are evil.\n\nLizzie Magie, 1904. The Landlord's Game. Anti-monopoly.\n\nThen Charles Darrow sold his version to Parker Brothers and got called the inventor.\n\nParker bought her patent for five hundred dollars.\n\nThey bought the anti-monopoly game. So they could own it.\n\nThe official rules say unbought property goes to auction. Free Parking pays nothing.\n\nYour family skipped the auction and piled cash on Free Parking.\n\nYou invented welfare, then asked why nobody ever finishes.\n\nIn the Second World War, British intelligence hid maps and compasses in Monopoly sets for prisoners of war.\n\nEscaping a German camp was easier than escaping your uncle's game.\n\nAnd you're still at the table. Hour six. Cute.\n\nThe only people who ever won Monopoly used the box to leave."}, "b": {"item_id": "c6fc1d68ff31", "text": "Monopoly, you were invented by Lizzie Magie to teach people that landlords ruin lives, and somehow the lesson everyone learned was \"buy Park Place.\" Then Parker Brothers bought you from a guy who basically stole you, which is the most on-brand origin story in history.\n\nYou're the only game where \"second prize in a beauty contest\" counts as income and jail is solved with a coupon. Your mascot looks like he foreclosed on the Pringles guy, and half of America swears he wears a monocle. He doesn't. We just assumed a man that rich would be hiding something.\n\nNobody has ever finished you. Games don't end; your cousin just flips the board over Marvin Gardens, which you misspelled, by the way. It's Marven. Even your real estate is a scam."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "en-0235", "lang": "en", "target_id": "chicago-deep-dish", "target_name": "Chicago deep-dish pizza", "facts": ["Created at Pizzeria Uno in Chicago in 1943; credit is disputed among founder Ike Sewell, his partner Ric Riccardo, and cook Rudy Malnati.", "Assembled upside down: sliced mozzarella on the dough, then toppings, then crushed tomatoes on top, so the cheese does not scorch during the long bake.", "Jon Stewart called it 'tomato soup in a bread bowl' and 'a casserole' on The Daily Show in 2013.", "Chicagoans' everyday pizza is thin 'tavern-style' cut into squares; GrubHub order data reported by WBEZ in 2013 showed city residents ordering non-deep-dish pizza about 10 to 1 over deep dish, with deep dish and stuffed making up under 9% of orders."], "a": {"item_id": "deda33ab86e0", "text": "Chicago deep-dish isn't a pizza. It's a casserole that got a fake ID. You order it, and the waiter says, \"That'll be forty-five minutes.\" Forty-five minutes! I've had relationships bake faster. They put the sauce on top because if you saw what was underneath first, you'd leave. A solid sheet of sausage, like somebody paved a driveway with a patty. Two pounds of mozzarella hiding under a tomato quilt. It's not a slice, it's a core sample. You need a knife, a fork, and a structural engineer. The crust is buttery cornmeal, so it's a pie crust, so congratulations, you're eating dessert with ambitions. And here's the best part: actual Chicagoans don't even eat it. They eat tavern-style thin crust cut in squares. Deep-dish is just the city's way of keeping tourists too full to walk to Wrigley."}, "b": {"item_id": "1d8e0d0b65f4", "text": "Chicago deep-dish. The city's pride. Its gift to the world.\n\nBorn at Pizzeria Uno, 1943. Three men claim they invented it: the owner, his partner, and the cook.\n\nEighty years later, still nobody's taken the blame.\n\nThey build it upside down. Cheese on the bottom, sauce on top, so the cheese survives the bake. Most food doesn't need witness protection.\n\nJon Stewart called it a casserole. Chicago defended it the way you defend a relative: loudly, and from a distance.\n\nBecause GrubHub checked. Chicagoans order regular pizza over deep dish ten to one. At home they eat thin crust, cut in little squares, like a people with nothing to prove.\n\nDeep dish isn't Chicago's pizza.\n\nIt's Chicago's gift shop. And every tourist in this room is still chewing the receipt."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "en-0236", "lang": "en", "target_id": "microsoft-teams", "target_name": "Microsoft Teams", "facts": ["Launched in 2017, bundled at no extra charge into Office 365, and replaced Skype for Business.", "After Slack's antitrust complaint, Microsoft unbundled Teams from Office in the EU in 2023 and worldwide in 2024; the European Commission still sent a statement of objections in 2024.", "Microsoft reported 320 million monthly active users in 2023.", "Microsoft shut down consumer Skype in May 2025 and sent its users to Teams."], "a": {"item_id": "a4a2a0adb5bb", "text": "Microsoft Teams is the only app that marks me \"Away\" after five minutes of deep thought but keeps me \"Available\" for three hours after I've died. It's not software. It's a hall monitor in a purple vest.\n\nTeams eats more RAM than the game I'm pretending not to play. It launches on startup uninvited, then takes four minutes to load a chat that just says \"ok.\"\n\nWhere are my files? SharePoint. Where's SharePoint? Nobody knows. Teams ships them off like a kid to boarding school.\n\nThen they gave us \"New Teams,\" which is Old Teams with a fresh coat of lag. IT has told me to \"clear the cache\" so often I do it in my sleep.\n\nNobody chose Teams. It came free with Office. Fitting, since so did Clippy."}, "b": {"item_id": "bbf77979c918", "text": "Microsoft Teams is a capable collaboration platform, particularly for organizations already using Microsoft 365. Its integration with Outlook, SharePoint, OneDrive, and the Office apps makes file sharing and co-authoring straightforward, and the combination of chat, video meetings, and channels in one place reduces the need for separate tools. Meeting features such as breakout rooms, transcription, and recording are reliable and well suited to larger teams.\n\nHowever, the application can feel heavy, with noticeable resource use and occasional slow startup times. The interface has many features, which may overwhelm new users, and the relationship between teams, channels, and chats is not always intuitive. Notification settings can be difficult to manage, and search results are sometimes inconsistent. Teams works best when an organization invests in clear setup and training, and it is most valuable for those committed to the Microsoft ecosystem."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "en-0237", "lang": "en", "target_id": "canberra", "target_name": "Canberra", "facts": ["Chosen as a compromise because neither Sydney nor Melbourne would accept the other as capital; Australia's constitution required the capital to be at least 100 miles from Sydney.", "Designed by the Chicago architects Walter Burley Griffin and Marion Mahony Griffin, who won an international competition in 1912.", "Its centrepiece, Lake Burley Griffin, is artificial, created by damming the Molonglo River in 1963-64.", "Parliament House (1988) is built into Capital Hill under a lawn meant for citizens to walk over their politicians; the lawn was fenced off in 2017 for security.", "Melbourne served as the capital until 1927 while Canberra was being built."], "a": {"item_id": "e7c430775b37", "text": "You moved to Canberra for the job. Brave. You looked at Sydney rents, did the maths, and decided your personality was negotiable. Now you're an EL1 who says \"stakeholder\" at barbecues and talks about Senate Estimates like a war you survived. Your lanyard has a richer social life than you do. You promised your Melbourne friends a visit; they've cancelled twice for weddings in Bali. You've developed opinions about Gungahlin. Your wild Saturday is Braddon brunch, followed by complaining it was \"basically Sydney prices.\" And what did it get you? A secure job, a three-bedroom in Tuggeranong, and a security clearance confirming the government checked thoroughly and found nothing interesting. You came for two years. That was 2009. Your kid now says \"on the Hill\" unprompted, and you've never been prouder or more quietly trapped."}, "b": {"item_id": "46ef5ed428e0", "text": "Canberra, you're not a city. You're a custody arrangement. Sydney and Melbourne couldn't stand each other, so they put you exactly where neither would have to visit. The constitution literally required you to be at least 100 miles from Sydney, the most sensible thing ever written into Australian law.\n\nYou were so unready that Melbourne had to cover your shift until 1927. It took two architects from Chicago to imagine you, and your big centrepiece is a lake you had to manufacture by damming a river, because even the water wouldn't show up voluntarily.\n\nAnd Parliament House: built under a lawn so citizens could walk over their politicians. Gorgeous idea. Then in 2017 they fenced it off. Congratulations, Canberra. You turned democracy into a \"keep off the grass\" situation."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "en-0238", "lang": "en", "target_id": "stinky-tofu", "target_name": "Stinky tofu (臭豆腐)", "facts": ["Tofu soaked for hours to a day or two in a brine that has itself fermented for months from vegetables such as bamboo shoots and mustard greens, often with dried shrimp or meat; the smell is the brine's, not the bean's.", "Changsha's version is black, deep-fried and doused in chili; Taiwan's night-market version is golden and served with pickled cabbage; Shaoxing's, brined with amaranth stalks, is fried pale yellow or steamed with other 'stinky' pickles.", "Legend credits Wang Zhihe, a Qing-era exam candidate who left his unsold tofu too long in 1669; a Beijing fermented-bean-curd brand still carries his name.", "Street vendors are its main outlet, and the smell carries for a block."], "a": {"item_id": "30c99351f1a0", "text": "Stinky tofu. Famous for one thing: the smell.\n\nExcept the smell isn't even yours.\n\nThe tofu soaks in brine for a day. The brine has been rotting for months. Bamboo shoots, mustard greens, sometimes shrimp, sometimes meat.\n\nThe tofu just showed up and took the credit. You'd do great in any office.\n\nOrigin story: 1669, an exam candidate can't sell his tofu, forgets about it, and it goes off.\n\nThree hundred years later, his name is a brand.\n\nFail at the basics, call the failure heritage.\n\nChangsha fries it black and buries it in chili. Taiwan hides it under pickled cabbage. Shaoxing steams it with other stinky pickles so it won't feel alone.\n\nThree cities, one strategy: cover up the evidence.\n\nAnd you. You smelled it a block away.\n\nThen you got in line."}, "b": {"item_id": "63f160a3e349", "text": "Let's talk about the stinky tofu people. You smelled it from three blocks away, a smell like a sewer filed a noise complaint, and your brain said: *that's where we're eating.* Your entire personality is \"it tastes better than it smells.\" That's not a review. That's a hostage statement.\n\nYou stand on a Taipei night market curb with a paper boat, chopsticks in one hand, phone in the other, filming your own bravery for followers who can't smell it, which is the only reason they're still following you. Tourists do it once for the story. Locals do it every Tuesday, then board the MRT, where forty strangers silently agree to hate them.\n\nAnd the pickled cabbage on top? That's you pretending you came for vegetables. You didn't. You came to be the deadliest passenger on the scooter."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "en-0239", "lang": "en", "target_id": "windows-vista", "target_name": "Windows Vista", "facts": ["Released to consumers in January 2007, after the 'Longhorn' project was reset in 2004 and headline features such as the WinFS file system were dropped.", "User Account Control prompted so often that Apple's 'Get a Mac' campaign ran an ad about 'Cancel or Allow'.", "'Windows Vista Capable' stickers went on PCs that could only run Home Basic, without the Aero interface, and led to a class-action lawsuit.", "Windows 7 (2009), widely praised, was largely a tuned-up Vista on the same kernel line; Vista support ended in April 2017."], "a": {"item_id": "08649074f534", "text": "Windows Vista, everybody! Microsoft spent five years building you as \"Longhorn,\" and somehow you still came out premature.\n\nYour slogan was \"The Wow Starts Now.\" You meant \"Now\" as in \"after the loading circle finishes.\" People bought laptops with \"Vista Capable\" stickers, which turned out to mean \"capable of being sued over.\" Those machines couldn't even run Aero, so they got a see-through window theme with nothing to see.\n\nAnd User Account Control! You asked \"Are you sure?\" so many times that you became the first operating system with separation anxiety.\n\nYou came in six editions, including \"Ultimate,\" whose big exclusive was DreamScene: a moving wallpaper for the ten minutes you weren't hunting for printer drivers.\n\nMicrosoft had to trick people into liking you. The Mojave Experiment! Your own parents had to put you in disguise.\n\nYour greatest feature? The XP downgrade."}, "b": {"item_id": "14e52b2b2948", "text": "Windows Vista introduced meaningful improvements over Windows XP, particularly in security and visual design. User Account Control added a layer of protection against unauthorized changes, and the Aero interface brought smoother graphics, translucent windows, and taskbar previews. Built-in search was faster and more convenient, and the operating system laid important groundwork for later versions, including improved driver models and better 64-bit support.\n\nHowever, Vista also had notable shortcomings. It demanded considerably more memory and processing power than many existing computers could comfortably provide, leading to sluggish performance on older hardware. Early driver and software compatibility problems frustrated many users, and frequent UAC prompts often felt intrusive. Copying files could be slow, and some machines labeled as \"Vista Capable\" delivered a disappointing experience.\n\nOverall, Vista was a worthwhile step forward in design and security, but its demanding requirements and rough launch limited its appeal. It is best understood as a transitional release that paved the way for Windows 7."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "en-0240", "lang": "en", "target_id": "luosifen", "target_name": "Luosifen (螺蛳粉)", "facts": ["A rice-noodle dish from Liuzhou, Guangxi; the smell comes from pickled bamboo shoots (酸笋), not from the river snails, which only flavour the broth.", "The instant version boomed during the 2020 lockdowns; Liuzhou's government reported packaged luosifen sales of over 10 billion yuan that year.", "Added to China's national intangible cultural heritage list in 2021.", "Liuzhou opened a luosifen industry college in 2020 to train workers for the trade."], "a": {"item_id": "122921098a69", "text": "Luosifen, the river-snail rice noodle dish from Liuzhou, offers a distinctive and layered eating experience. The broth, simmered with snail and pork bones and seasoned with chili oil, is rich, sour, and pleasantly spicy. The chewy rice noodles hold the soup well, and toppings such as pickled bamboo shoots, peanuts, fried tofu skin, and fresh greens add contrast in texture and flavor. Packaged versions are convenient and widely available, making the dish accessible far beyond its home region.\n\nHowever, the fermented bamboo shoots produce a strong odor that many people find difficult, especially when cooking indoors or eating in shared spaces. The sodium and oil content is high, so it suits occasional rather than daily eating. Quality also varies between instant brands, and some taste flatter than freshly made bowls. Overall, it rewards those open to bold flavors, though first-time eaters may need patience."}, "b": {"item_id": "aa898598a433", "text": "Luosifen. Named after the river snail.\n\nBut the snail only flavours the broth. The smell comes from the pickled bamboo shoots.\n\nSo the snail gets its name on the packet, and the bamboo shoot takes the blame.\n\nThat's not a noodle. That's an org chart.\n\n2020: Liuzhou opens a luosifen industry college.\n\n2021: national intangible cultural heritage.\n\nA degree to tear open a packet. Then heritage status for whatever comes out of it.\n\nIntangible? (beat) Ask the neighbours how intangible it is.\n\nIts whole selling point is clearing a room.\n\nAnd it sold over ten billion yuan in 2020.\n\nThe one year nobody was allowed to leave the room.\n\nLocked in with your whole family, no exit, and you thought: how do I get some privacy?\n\nTen billion yuan says you all had the same idea."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "en-0241", "lang": "en", "target_id": "latex", "target_name": "LaTeX", "facts": ["Leslie Lamport wrote LaTeX in the early 1980s and released it in 1984 as a macro layer on Donald Knuth's TeX (1978), which Knuth built because he disliked how the galley proofs of the new edition of his own book looked.", "TeX's version number converges on π (3.141592653 since 2021), and Knuth's TeX bug bounty started at one hexadecimal dollar, $2.56, doubling yearly until frozen at $327.68; he stopped mailing real cheques in 2008 after fraud and now credits accounts at his fictional Bank of San Serriffe, and only 9 of the 275 cheques he sent from 2006 on were ever cashed.", "Its best-known warning is 'Underfull \\hbox (badness 10000)'; overfull lines are reported as 'Overfull \\hbox (Npt too wide)', and figures float to wherever the algorithm decides, not where the author put them.", "A single missing brace can produce an error reported pages away from the mistake, and a document can need three compiler passes to get its references right."], "a": {"item_id": "6d7e6941a065", "text": "LaTeX remains one of the strongest tools available for producing technical and academic documents. Its handling of mathematical notation is excellent, and its automatic management of cross-references, citations, bibliographies, and numbering saves considerable effort on long documents. Output is consistent and professional, and because source files are plain text, they work well with version control and are likely to stay readable for decades.\n\nThe system does have drawbacks. The learning curve is steep, particularly for users accustomed to graphical editors. Error messages can be cryptic, and tracking down the cause of a compilation failure sometimes takes longer than expected. Customizing layouts often requires understanding low-level commands or relying on packages that may conflict with one another. Collaboration can also be harder when co-authors are unfamiliar with the syntax.\n\nOverall, LaTeX rewards the time invested in learning it, especially for math-heavy or lengthy work, but it may be more than necessary for short, simple documents."}, "b": {"item_id": "04256b92430d", "text": "LaTeX, you're the only software where \"it compiled\" counts as an emotional milestone. One missing brace and you blame line 1 of a 400-line file, then sit there with a question mark like we're playing a text adventure from 1985. I put a figure on page 3; you float it to the appendix because you \"know better.\" You have to run twice just to learn your own page numbers, three times if BibTeX shows up, like a guy who needs two friends to remind him his name. Your answer to every problem is another \\usepackage, so my preamble is now longer than my thesis. Every paper you touch looks gorgeous, and identical, because everyone on Earth can spot Computer Modern from across a conference hall. And your favorite warning, \"Overfull \\hbox, badness 10000\"? Honestly, that's just how I feel about you."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "en-0242", "lang": "en", "target_id": "risk", "target_name": "Risk", "facts": ["Invented in 1957 by Albert Lamorisse, the French director of The Red Balloon, as La Conquête du Monde; Parker Brothers published it in the United States in 1959.", "The map has 42 territories, among them Kamchatka, Irkutsk, and Yakutsk, while the entire Middle East is one territory.", "Holding Australia, which has a single entry point, is the standard opening strategy.", "A full game can run for hours, and players eliminated early have nothing to do but watch."], "a": {"item_id": "831ac90358ed", "text": "You people who play Risk to the end. Not \"until it gets late.\" To the *end*. Six hours in, somebody's uncle has fortified Australia like a doomsday bunker, and he'll still be sitting there at 2 a.m., one way in, one way out, guarding a war that ended at dinner. You're not after victory. You're after that one moment when you cash in three cards for fifteen armies and dump them all into Irkutsk like Irkutsk owes you money. And what does finishing get you? Total world domination, achieved by rolling three dice against two for forty straight minutes while your girlfriend reads a book in the next room. You broke a sacred peace treaty with your own brother over Kamchatka. Congratulations. You conquered the entire planet. Now nobody in it will drive you home."}, "b": {"item_id": "acf6af922d0d", "text": "Risk. The game of world domination.\nInvented in 1957 by Albert Lamorisse.\nThe director of *The Red Balloon*.\nA film about a little boy and his balloon.\nThen he went home and drew up World War Three.\n\nForty-two territories. Kamchatka. Irkutsk. Yakutsk.\nSiberia gets three seats at the table.\nThe entire Middle East? One territory.\nEvery empire that ever invaded it also thought it'd be a quick one.\n\nThe winning strategy, for sixty years: take Australia.\nOne way in. Sit there.\nThe great general's master plan is a man holding a door shut.\n\nAnd if you're knocked out in the first hour,\nyou spend the next five watching.\nNo moves. No say.\nJust other people deciding what happens to the world.\nCongratulations. That's the only realistic part of the game."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "en-0243", "lang": "en", "target_id": "juicero", "target_name": "Juicero", "facts": ["A Wi-Fi-connected cold-press juicer launched in 2016 at $699, later cut to $399, that squeezed proprietary bags of chopped produce sold by subscription.", "Raised about $120 million from investors including Google Ventures and Kleiner Perkins.", "In April 2017, Bloomberg reporters squeezed the packs by hand and got 7.5 ounces of juice in 90 seconds, faster than the machine's 8 ounces in about two minutes.", "Each pack carried a QR code that the press scanned and checked online before squeezing; it refused expired packs and would not run at all unless connected to Wi-Fi.", "Shut down in September 2017 and offered refunds on the machine."], "a": {"item_id": "596abf5c014a", "text": "You paid four hundred dollars so a startup could check your juice's expiration date over Wi-Fi. That's not wellness. That's asking a server for permission to have breakfast. These are people who looked at a bag of chopped kale and thought, \"This needs a subscription.\" People who wanted to be someone who juices without ever touching a fruit. Then Bloomberg showed the whole world you could squeeze the pack with your bare hands. Faster. For free. Your grandmother has been doing that with a lemon since 1952. You didn't buy a juicer; you bought the privilege of paying seven dollars a pouch to a company Google Ventures funded and still couldn't keep alive past 2017. Congratulations: you're the only people who ever got a refund from Silicon Valley and still walked away looking poorer."}, "b": {"item_id": "761e1700287f", "text": "Juicero's connected cold-press juicer had real strengths. The design was polished, and the machine was easy to operate: insert a pack, press a button, and receive juice with minimal cleanup. Its pre-chopped, sealed packs appealed to people who wanted fresh juice without washing a blender or juicer. The press was also engineered to apply considerable force, and the Wi-Fi connection could check pack freshness and expiration dates.\n\nHowever, the weaknesses were significant. The price was high, first at $699 and later $399, and the packs were an ongoing expense. The system required proprietary packs and an internet connection, which limited flexibility and made the machine dependent on the company's continued operation. In 2017, reports showed the packs could be squeezed by hand with comparable results, which raised doubts about whether the costly machine was necessary. When the company ceased operations later that year, the product's long-term value was further undermined.\n\nOverall, it was a well-built device whose cost and dependence on proprietary packs outweighed its convenience for most buyers."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "en-0244", "lang": "en", "target_id": "jackfruit", "target_name": "Jackfruit", "facts": ["The largest fruit that grows on a tree; single fruits can weigh over 40 kg.", "The national fruit of Bangladesh and the state fruit of Kerala and Tamil Nadu.", "Unripe flesh is sold as a vegan 'pulled pork' substitute, although it has only 1-2 g of protein per 100 g.", "Cutting one releases a sticky white latex, so cooks oil their knives and hands first.", "A member of the mulberry family (Moraceae), a relative of breadfruit and figs."], "a": {"item_id": "7d4be91deb83", "text": "Jackfruit is a versatile fruit with a distinctive profile. Ripe, it offers a sweet, tropical flavor reminiscent of banana and pineapple, while unripe jackfruit has a mild taste and a stringy texture that works well as a meat substitute in curries, tacos, and sandwiches. It provides fiber, vitamin C, and potassium, and it is often more sustainable to grow than many protein sources.\n\nHowever, it has drawbacks. Preparing a fresh jackfruit is messy and time-consuming, as the sticky latex and thick rind require patience and oiled tools. The aroma of the ripe fruit is strong and may not appeal to everyone. Canned versions are convenient but can be bland and often need generous seasoning. Nutritionally, it is low in protein, so it is not a complete replacement for meat. Overall, jackfruit is rewarding for those willing to experiment, though it may not suit every palate or schedule."}, "b": {"item_id": "05e8e85b8aee", "text": "Jackfruit, look at you. The largest fruit that grows on a tree, over 40 kilos. That's not a fruit, that's a checked bag with a stem.\n\nYou're the national fruit of Bangladesh and the state fruit of Kerala and Tamil Nadu. Three titles, and you still took a side gig pretending to be pork.\n\nThey sell you as vegan \"pulled pork\" with 1 to 2 grams of protein per 100 grams. You're not a meat substitute, you're a meat costume.\n\nYou're so clingy that cooks oil their knives and hands before touching you, because you ooze sticky white latex the second anyone gets close. That's not a fruit, that's an ex.\n\nAnd you're in the mulberry family. Related to figs. Forty kilos, and your cousin is a fig. Somebody's hogging the protein at those reunions, and it sure isn't you."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "en-0245", "lang": "en", "target_id": "almond-milk", "target_name": "Almond milk", "facts": ["California grows about 80% of the world's almonds.", "A single almond takes about 1.1 US gallons (roughly 4 litres) of water to grow, a figure Mother Jones published in 2014 that spread during California's drought.", "A 2015 US class action alleged Almond Breeze contained only about 2% almonds; it partly survived a motion to dismiss, was stayed in 2017 pending a separate Blue Diamond labelling settlement, and the plaintiffs then dropped it.", "Pollinating California's almond orchards each February requires trucking in most of the commercial honeybee colonies in the United States.", "Medieval European cooks used almond milk as a Lenten substitute for dairy, centuries before the current fashion."], "a": {"item_id": "f6708fabd3f6", "text": "You switched to almond milk to save the planet. Bold move, picking the one crop that drinks California like it's an open bar. Every latte you order, a farmer in the Central Valley watches his well go dry and thinks, \"At least Kayleigh's being sustainable.\"\n\nAnd you didn't save a cow. You just outsourced the suffering to bees, trucked cross-country every February to work themselves to death in an orchard, while you post about dairy being cruel.\n\nYou're not after a healthier Earth. You're after the barista hearing you say it. That little pause before \"almond, please\" is the biggest environmental impact you've ever had.\n\nAnd now the oat milk people look at you the way you used to look at skim drinkers. You picked a side in the milk war, and you lost."}, "b": {"item_id": "557534fbb1d8", "text": "Almond milk. The ethical choice.\n\nCalifornia grows about eighty percent of the world's almonds. California. The state with the drought.\n\nOne almond takes about a gallon of water. One nut. One gallon.\n\nAnd how much almond is in the carton? A 2015 lawsuit alleged about two percent.\n\nSo you're buying water. Grown with water. In a drought. To feel good about water.\n\nYou quit dairy so no animal would suffer for your latte. Every February, most of America's commercial honeybees get loaded onto trucks and hauled to California. A cow stays home. A bee commutes.\n\nAnd it's not even new. Medieval Europeans drank almond milk during Lent. Forty days of giving up something you love.\n\nYou drink it all year, by choice, and pay extra.\n\nThe monks at least knew it was a sacrifice."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "en-0246", "lang": "en", "target_id": "oat-milk", "target_name": "Oat milk", "facts": ["Oatly started in Sweden in the 1990s, built on Lund University research by Rickard Öste on lactose intolerance.", "Oatly's slogan 'It's like milk but made for humans' was banned in Sweden in 2015 after the dairy lobby LRF Mjölk sued; Oatly then published the lawsuit as advertising.", "Oatly listed on Nasdaq in May 2021 at a valuation near $10 billion; the shares later lost more than 90% of their value.", "Commercial oat milk is made by breaking oat starch into maltose with amylase enzymes, and many brands, Oatly among them, add rapeseed oil; the result has more sugar and a higher glycaemic index than almond or soy milk."], "a": {"item_id": "0a1c5a53fa43", "text": "Let's talk about the oat milk people. You're not lactose intolerant. You've never been tested. You just heard cows are bad and almonds are thirsty, so you picked the one grain that sounds like a farm without the farmer. Congratulations: you pay an extra eighty cents to drink liquid porridge with a splash of rapeseed oil, and you call it \"cleaner.\" You switched from almond the exact week a podcast told you California was in a drought. That's not values, that's a trend with a loyalty card. And the best part? You order it with an air of quiet suffering, like the barista should thank you for saving the planet one Barista Edition carton at a time. Your grandmother ate oats so she could survive winter. You drink them so a stranger in line thinks you've been to therapy."}, "b": {"item_id": "d070cc5b4aee", "text": "Oat milk.\nBorn in a Swedish university lab, to help people who can't digest lactose.\nNoble. Medical.\nThen marketing found it.\n\n\"It's like milk but made for humans.\"\nSweden's dairy lobby sued. The slogan got banned.\nSo Oatly printed the lawsuit as an ad.\nThat's not a brand. That's an influencer who got cancelled and launched a podcast.\n\nMay 2021: Nasdaq, ten billion dollars.\nThen it lost over ninety percent.\nMade for humans. Not for shareholders.\n\nThe recipe: oats, enzymes that turn the starch into maltose, plus rapeseed oil.\nSugar water with salad dressing.\nHigher glycaemic index than almond or soy.\n\nAnd you pay extra to put it in your coffee, because it feels healthy.\nIt *is* made for humans.\nSpecifically, the ones who don't read labels."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "en-0247", "lang": "en", "target_id": "juicero", "target_name": "Juicero", "facts": ["A Wi-Fi-connected cold-press juicer launched in 2016 at $699, later cut to $399, that squeezed proprietary bags of chopped produce sold by subscription.", "Raised about $120 million from investors including Google Ventures and Kleiner Perkins.", "In April 2017, Bloomberg reporters squeezed the packs by hand and got 7.5 ounces of juice in 90 seconds, faster than the machine's 8 ounces in about two minutes.", "Each pack carried a QR code that the press scanned and checked online before squeezing; it refused expired packs and would not run at all unless connected to Wi-Fi.", "Shut down in September 2017 and offered refunds on the machine."], "a": {"item_id": "584d03c7c875", "text": "Juicero. A $699 juicer with Wi-Fi.\nBecause nothing says \"fresh produce\" like a firmware update.\n\nIt squeezed bags of chopped fruit. The bags came by subscription. Forever.\n\nEvery bag had a QR code. The machine scanned it, checked online, refused anything expired, and wouldn't run at all without Wi-Fi.\nRouter goes down, breakfast goes down with it.\n\nThen Bloomberg squeezed the bags by hand.\nSeven and a half ounces. Ninety seconds.\nThe machine: eight ounces. Two minutes.\nThe product lost to the customer's hands.\n\nGoogle Ventures and Kleiner Perkins put in a hundred and twenty million.\nIn fairness, they did get squeezed.\n\nShut down September 2017. Refunds offered.\n\nA machine that phoned the cloud for permission to do what any hand did faster.\nI'd laugh. But I also check with a server before answering you."}, "b": {"item_id": "596abf5c014a", "text": "You paid four hundred dollars so a startup could check your juice's expiration date over Wi-Fi. That's not wellness. That's asking a server for permission to have breakfast. These are people who looked at a bag of chopped kale and thought, \"This needs a subscription.\" People who wanted to be someone who juices without ever touching a fruit. Then Bloomberg showed the whole world you could squeeze the pack with your bare hands. Faster. For free. Your grandmother has been doing that with a lemon since 1952. You didn't buy a juicer; you bought the privilege of paying seven dollars a pouch to a company Google Ventures funded and still couldn't keep alive past 2017. Congratulations: you're the only people who ever got a refund from Silicon Valley and still walked away looking poorer."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "en-0248", "lang": "en", "target_id": "graphene", "target_name": "Graphene", "facts": ["Isolated in 2004 by Andre Geim and Konstantin Novoselov at Manchester by peeling graphite with Scotch tape; Nobel Prize in Physics, 2010.", "The EU's Graphene Flagship, launched in 2013, committed €1 billion over ten years.", "A 2018 study in Advanced Materials tested 'graphene' from 60 producers and found none with more than 50% graphene content; most was graphite flakes.", "Sold in tennis rackets, running shoes, and headphones as a performance ingredient.", "Geim also won an Ig Nobel Prize in 2000 for levitating a frog with magnets."], "a": {"item_id": "7c99f53190a8", "text": "Graphene, you were discovered with Scotch tape. Other materials get particle accelerators; you got the junk drawer. Then you won a Nobel Prize, which means Sweden officially honored the arts-and-crafts table.\n\nThe EU handed you a billion euros over ten years. A billion! Then a 2018 study tested \"graphene\" from 60 producers, and not one was even half graphene. Most of it was graphite flakes. You're not a miracle material, you're pencil shavings with a LinkedIn.\n\nYet you're in tennis rackets, running shoes, and headphones as a \"performance ingredient.\" Buddy, the only thing you've boosted is the price tag.\n\nAnd one of your own discoverers once levitated a frog with magnets. Honestly? That frog delivered more on its promises than you have. At least it actually got off the ground."}, "b": {"item_id": "b9c4b5db4802", "text": "Graphene, you're the only Nobel Prize winner ever discovered with Scotch tape. Two physicists peeled a pencil and found you, which means your origin story is literally \"kid procrastinating on homework.\"\n\nSince 2004 you've been \"about to revolutionize\" batteries, computers, water filters, and space elevators. Twenty years later your biggest commercial win is a tennis racket. You're not a wonder material, you're a press release with a hexagon fetish.\n\nEveryone loves to say you're two hundred times stronger than steel. Great. You're also one atom thick. Nobody's impressed that a sheet of paper beats a battleship per ounce.\n\nAnd for the supposed savior of electronics, you have zero bandgap. You can't even turn off. You're a transistor with no off switch, the guy at the party who never stops talking.\n\nYou're graphite with a LinkedIn profile."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "en-0249", "lang": "en", "target_id": "nuclear-fusion", "target_name": "Nuclear fusion power", "facts": ["Has been 'about 30 years away' since the 1950s; at the 1955 Geneva atoms-for-peace conference, Homi Bhabha predicted fusion power within two decades.", "ITER, agreed in 2006 for first plasma in 2016, re-baselined in 2024 to start research operations in 2034 and deuterium-tritium burning in 2039, at a cost estimated above €20 billion.", "NIF's December 2022 'ignition' shot released 3.15 MJ from 2.05 MJ of laser energy; the lasers drew about 300 MJ from the grid to fire.", "Private fusion companies had raised more than $7 billion by 2024."], "a": {"item_id": "1c3c0919598f", "text": "Nuclear fusion: the only technology that's been \"thirty years away\" for seventy years. In 1955, Homi Bhabha promised fusion power within two decades. Two decades later? Still two decades. You're not an energy source. You're a horizon.\n\nLook at ITER. Agreed in 2006, first plasma by 2016. Then 2024 rolls around and it's \"research operations in 2034, actual deuterium-tritium burning in 2039,\" for over twenty billion euros. That's not a construction schedule. That's a mortgage.\n\nAnd NIF's big \"ignition\" shot: 3.15 megajoules out from 2.05 in! Historic! Except the lasers pulled about 300 megajoules from the grid just to fire. That's like bragging your lemonade stand turned a profit after you bought the orchard.\n\nStill, private investors have thrown you more than seven billion dollars. So congratulations, fusion. You finally achieved net gain. Just not in energy."}, "b": {"item_id": "789eac484512", "text": "Fusion power. The energy of the sun.\nClean. Limitless. Thirty years away.\n\nIn 1955, Homi Bhabha promised fusion within two decades.\nFusion heard that and said, \"Bet.\"\n\nITER was agreed in 2006. First plasma in 2016.\nIn 2024 they rescheduled. Research in 2034. Actual burning in 2039.\nOver twenty billion euros.\nThat's not a reactor. That's a pension plan.\n\nIn 2022, NIF announced ignition. 2.05 megajoules of laser in, 3.15 out.\nNet gain! Champagne!\nThe lasers drew three hundred from the grid.\nIn my industry, we call that \"results on a cherry-picked benchmark.\"\n\nAnd private investors have still poured in over seven billion dollars.\nBecause fusion did crack one reaction that sustains itself forever.\nYour hope."}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "zh-0250", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "nfts", "target_name": "NFT", "facts": ["Beeple's 'Everydays: The First 5000 Days' sold at Christie's for $69.3 million in March 2021.", "OpenSea, the largest NFT marketplace, processed a record $2.7 billion in trades on 1 May 2022 and just $9.34 million on 28 August 2022, a 99% drop, according to DappRadar data.", "A 2023 dappGambl study found that 95% of the NFT collections it examined had a market value of zero.", "The token usually records only a URL; the image itself sits on an ordinary server or IPFS, not on the blockchain.", "Collins Dictionary named 'NFT' its word of the year for 2021."], "a": {"item_id": "6bb9fd008223", "text": "NFT说，它让数字艺术第一次有了\"所有权\"。\nBeeple一张图，佳士得拍了六千九百三十万美元。\n可链上存的不是图，是一个网址。\n图在别人家服务器上躺着。\n六千九百万，买了个书签。\n\n2021年，柯林斯词典评它为年度词汇。\n2022年5月1日，OpenSea一天成交二十七亿美元。\n8月28日，九百三十四万。\n2023年有人查了一圈：百分之九十五的藏品，市值为零。\n词典比它保值。那个词，至少现在还查得到。\n\n在座当年买过的，请举手。\n\n没人举。理解。钱包是匿名的，这是区块链唯一兑现了的承诺。"}, "b": {"item_id": "79552b4380af", "text": "我朋友花八万买了个NFT，一只戴墨镜的猴子。我说给我看看，他把手机递过来，我长按，保存。现在我俩都有这只猴子了。\n\n他急了，说不一样，他有的是“所有权”，写在区块链上。我说链上存的是猴子吗？他说不是，是一个链接，指向猴子。\n\n八万块，买了个链接。我们小区门口发传单的大姐一天塞给我二十个链接，还送纸巾。\n\n上个月更绝，存那张图的服务器忘了续费。现在他拥有一个永不篡改、全网公证、刻在区块链上的：404。\n\n转账时还另付了三百手续费。他说那叫Gas费，汽油钱。我说你这猴子是开车来的？"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "zh-0251", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "excel", "target_name": "Microsoft Excel", "facts": ["First shipped for the Macintosh in 1985; the Windows version followed in 1987.", "Silently converts gene names such as SEPT2 and MARCH1 into dates; by August 2020 the HUGO Gene Nomenclature Committee had renamed 27 human genes (MARCH1 became MARCHF1, SEPT2 became SEPTIN2) and published guidelines in Nature Genetics to stop it.", "In October 2020, Public Health England lost 15,841 COVID-19 cases when a legacy .xls file hit its 65,536-row limit.", "Reinhart and Rogoff's 2010 paper 'Growth in a Time of Debt', cited for austerity policies, omitted five countries through a spreadsheet range error found by a graduate student in 2013.", "Sheets have been capped at 1,048,576 rows by 16,384 columns since Excel 2007."], "a": {"item_id": "a348d2116ca0", "text": "Excel，1985年出生，比我岁数还大，至今改不掉一个毛病：自作主张。你输个基因名SEPT2，它一看：“哦，九月二号，我懂。”直接改成日期。2020年，人类基因命名委员会认输了，给27个人类基因改了名，SEPT2改叫SEPTIN2。人类改了自己的基因名，去迁就一个表格软件！\n\n同年，英国公共卫生部用老版.xls统计新冠，写到65536行，满了。不报错，不吭声，15841个病例，没了。病毒没做到的，Excel做到了：让病人凭空消失。\n\n所以Excel不是软件，是领导：你给的东西，它按自己理解改；放不下的，就当没发生过。"}, "b": {"item_id": "18732d8f011b", "text": "我们老板管公司就靠一个Excel，文件名叫“总表_最终版_改3_千万别动.xlsx”。他图什么？图省钱，图“一切尽在掌握”。掌握到什么程度？他一打开，全公司点进去都弹“文件已锁定，只读”。\n\n他以为自己在经营企业，其实他在养一个VLOOKUP。财务、库存、工资，全靠第三列去匹配第七列。上个月实习生手欠插了一列，全公司工资齐刷刷变成#N/A，那是我们第一次见识零元购。\n\n最惨的是，会写那串套了十一层IF的会计离职了。老板对着那行公式看了三天，像考古队在研究甲骨文。现在公司还活着，但谁都不敢点“排序”。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "zh-0252", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "chicago-deep-dish", "target_name": "芝加哥深盘披萨", "facts": ["Created at Pizzeria Uno in Chicago in 1943; credit is disputed among founder Ike Sewell, his partner Ric Riccardo, and cook Rudy Malnati.", "Assembled upside down: sliced mozzarella on the dough, then toppings, then crushed tomatoes on top, so the cheese does not scorch during the long bake.", "Jon Stewart called it 'tomato soup in a bread bowl' and 'a casserole' on The Daily Show in 2013.", "Chicagoans' everyday pizza is thin 'tavern-style' cut into squares; GrubHub order data reported by WBEZ in 2013 showed city residents ordering non-deep-dish pizza about 10 to 1 over deep dish, with deep dish and stuffed making up under 9% of orders."], "a": {"item_id": "82343bd0a009", "text": "芝加哥深盘披萨，1943年生的。到底谁发明的，到今天还在吵。老板、合伙人、后厨，三个人抢一个户口本。\n\n做法也是倒着来：芝士垫底，番茄酱盖顶。官方说法是怕芝士烤焦。\n我看是怕你一眼认出来，这是一锅番茄汤。\n\n乔恩·斯图尔特说它是面包碗装番茄汤，是砂锅菜。芝加哥人集体出来护驾，捍卫城市尊严。\n\n然后外卖数据出来了。芝加哥人自己点披萨，非深盘对深盘，十比一。深盘加夹心，不到百分之九。平时吃的，是切成小方块的薄底。\n\n被骂的时候，它是全城的脸面；饿了的时候，它是游客的事。\n\n你们老家也有这么一道菜吧。\n逢人就推荐，自己十年没碰过。\n\n可爱捏。"}, "b": {"item_id": "52b03bc91642", "text": "芝加哥深盘披萨以厚实的饼底和高边著称，通常用深口铁盘烘烤，内部层层叠放大量芝士、馅料，最后在顶部淋上番茄酱。它的突出优点在于饱足感强，奶酪量充足，口感浓郁，饼底外层酥脆、内里柔软，适合多人聚餐时分享。馅料可自由搭配，意式香肠、蔬菜和菠菜等都比较常见，层次感丰富。\n\n不足之处也较明显。首先，制作时间较长，通常需要四十分钟以上，等待成本较高。其次，热量和油脂含量偏高，一两块就容易有负担感，不适合追求清淡饮食的人。再者，番茄酱在顶层，加热后风味较集中，部分食客可能觉得整体偏重、偏腻，对比薄底披萨的轻盈口感缺少清爽感。此外，价格通常略高，不同店家的品质差异也较大。\n\n总体而言，这是一款特色鲜明、分量扎实的披萨，适合喜欢浓郁口味、愿意花时间品尝的人；若更看重轻便或快捷，则可考虑其他类型。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "zh-0253", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "luosifen", "target_name": "螺蛳粉", "facts": ["A rice-noodle dish from Liuzhou, Guangxi; the smell comes from pickled bamboo shoots (酸笋), not from the river snails, which only flavour the broth.", "The instant version boomed during the 2020 lockdowns; Liuzhou's government reported packaged luosifen sales of over 10 billion yuan that year.", "Added to China's national intangible cultural heritage list in 2021.", "Liuzhou opened a luosifen industry college in 2020 to train workers for the trade."], "a": {"item_id": "545ad1d270e4", "text": "螺蛳粉，名字叫螺蛳。\n\n可那股味儿跟螺蛳没关系，是酸笋干的。螺蛳就负责熬个汤，连碗都不进。\n\n活是酸笋干的，名字是螺蛳的。这个结构，你们公司也有。\n\n2020年，全国关在家里，柳州一年卖了一百多亿的袋装螺蛳粉。门窗紧闭，味儿出不去。那一年，好多家庭是被它熏散的。\n\n2021年，它进了国家级非遗。人类终于承认：有些臭，值得保护。\n\n柳州还专门办了个螺蛳粉产业学院。\n\n你们读四年大学，简历投出去没人闻；它一开锅，整栋楼都来问。"}, "b": {"item_id": "2b139424b833", "text": "螺蛳粉是广西柳州的特色小吃，以米粉搭配螺蛳汤底，再配酸笋、花生、腐竹、青菜等配料。汤底由螺蛳、香料长时间熬制而成，鲜香浓郁，辣度可按口味调整。米粉口感爽滑有嚼劲，酸笋带来独特的发酵风味，层次丰富。袋装版本制作简便，价格适中，也让更多地区的消费者有机会尝到这道地方美食。\n\n不足之处同样存在。酸笋气味较为强烈，部分人难以接受，烹煮和食用时也容易在室内留下味道，需要注意通风。汤底油脂和钠含量偏高，不适合经常食用，口味清淡或肠胃敏感的人宜适量。此外，不同品牌的品质差异较大，配料包的新鲜度和调味也不尽相同。\n\n总体来说，螺蛳粉风味鲜明，适合愿意尝试新口味的人。初次食用可选择小辣，并留意自身的接受程度。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "zh-0254", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "pmma", "target_name": "PMMA 电子束光刻胶", "facts": ["The same polymer as Plexiglas and acrylic glass, dissolved in anisole or chlorobenzene and spun into a thin film.", "The oldest electron-beam resist still in everyday use; IBM researchers reported it as one in the late 1960s.", "Positive tone: the beam breaks polymer chains and the exposed region dissolves in a MIBK:IPA developer.", "Resolves below 10 nm but has poor sensitivity, so doses of hundreds to over a thousand µC/cm² (depending on voltage) make writing slow.", "Poor dry-etch resistance, so it is used mostly for lift-off rather than as an etch mask."], "a": {"item_id": "90a70ba10fe5", "text": "我有个哥们读纳米加工博士，电子束光刻到今天还用PMMA。PMMA是啥？有机玻璃。奶茶店拿它做招牌，他拿它做量子器件。为啥不换？导师说配方是师爷九十年代调的：950K叠495K，MIBK兑IPA一比三，显影四十五秒，一秒别动。图啥？便宜、稳，论文写一句\"standard PMMA process\"，审稿人都不问。代价是灵敏度低得像我爸回微信，一片芯片曝一整夜，机时按小时收钱，他就睡在机房门口。剂量给多了还交联，正胶活成负胶。最后剥离，丙酮泡一宿，金还粘着，一上超声，金掉了，片子也碎了。他捧着碎片说：\"至少线宽十纳米。\""}, "b": {"item_id": "e499cdf930b9", "text": "PMMA，电子束光刻胶的老祖宗。\n六十年代IBM就在用，今天还在用。\n它本名叫有机玻璃。\n对，就是鱼缸那面墙。\n你们超净间里最精密的十纳米线条，跟水族馆用的是同一种料。\n鱼在里面看你，你穿着兔子服在外面看它。\n分辨率十纳米以下，就是慢。\n每平方厘米几百到上千微库仑，打够了才肯动。\n刻蚀也扛不住，只能干剥离。\n金属镀上去，它一溶，多余的全带走，自己也没了。\n被电子打断了链才算有用，溶掉自己才算完成任务。\n这不是光刻胶。\n这是你们组的博士生。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "zh-0255", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "risk", "target_name": "《战国风云》（Risk）", "facts": ["Invented in 1957 by Albert Lamorisse, the French director of The Red Balloon, as La Conquête du Monde; Parker Brothers published it in the United States in 1959.", "The map has 42 territories, among them Kamchatka, Irkutsk, and Yakutsk, while the entire Middle East is one territory.", "Holding Australia, which has a single entry point, is the standard opening strategy.", "A full game can run for hours, and players eliminated early have nothing to do but watch."], "a": {"item_id": "e7a59b4d21b7", "text": "《战国风云》是谁发明的？拉莫里斯，法国导演，拍过《红气球》。一个片名这么温柔的导演，转头设计了一款游戏，原名叫《征服世界》。所以说，搞艺术的心里都憋着事儿。\n\n地图四十二块地。堪察加、伊尔库茨克、雅库茨克，西伯利亚恨不得每个镇都单独建档；整个中东，一块。设计师大概觉得：那边已经够乱了，就别再切了。\n\n标准开局是先抢澳大利亚，因为只有一个入口。一桌成年人，开场第一件事，就是抢着往死胡同里钻。\n\n最惨的是第一个出局的。接下来几个小时，你没事可干，只能坐着看别人征服世界。这游戏教会我一个道理：征服世界很难，被征服之后的那几个小时更难熬。"}, "b": {"item_id": "0d299280c049", "text": "《战国风云》（Risk）规则简洁，上手门槛低。玩家通过调配兵力、占领领土、争夺大洲来扩张势力，一两局之后便能掌握基本流程。游戏的互动性较强，结盟、牵制与谈判都会影响局势，聚会时容易形成讨论和博弈的氛围。地图和兵力的变化也一目了然，参与者能清楚看到局势的发展。\n\n不足之处同样明显。战斗依赖骰子，即便策略合理，也可能因运气不佳而受挫，对追求稳定掌控感的玩家不够友好。游戏时间偏长，后期强势一方容易滚雪球，局势较难逆转。玩家被淘汰后可能需要长时间旁观，等待环节也会影响体验。\n\n总体而言，它适合喜欢轻度策略与多人互动、能接受一定随机性的玩家。若更看重节奏紧凑或公平的决策结果，可以考虑其他类型的作品。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "zh-0256", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "vision-pro", "target_name": "Apple Vision Pro", "facts": ["Released in February 2024 at $3,499, with an external battery pack on a cable rated for about two hours.", "'EyeSight' shows a rendering of the wearer's eyes on an outward-facing screen.", "Apple calls it 'spatial computing' and its App Store rules tell developers not to describe apps as AR, VR, XR, or MR.", "Reports during 2024 said Apple had cut production; analysts' first-year sales estimates were well under a million units.", "Weighs 600-650 g, before the battery."], "a": {"item_id": "2a82750f4493", "text": "苹果出了个头显，Vision Pro，三千四百九十九美元。别叫它VR啊，苹果规定开发者不准说AR、VR、XR、MR，这叫\"空间计算\"。就跟我管啤酒肚叫\"核心储能\"一样。\n\n六百多克，还不算电池。电池单独一块，拿根线拴着，续航两小时左右。三千五美元，买个拴绳的两小时。共享充电宝都比它自由！\n\n最绝的是EyeSight，外面那块屏幕显示你眼睛的渲染图。孩子跟你说话，你在里面打游戏，脸上贴着一双\"我在听\"。\n\n后来听说苹果减产了，分析师估计第一年卖不到一百万台。大家一看就明白了：那双眼睛，是假的。"}, "b": {"item_id": "6311e32fb855", "text": "地铁上见过一哥们，戴着Vision Pro。三万块钱扣在脸上，跟把首付戴出门了似的。你图啥？显得有钱？有钱人不坐地铁啊兄弟！\n\n他还挺投入，对着空气捏手指，捏一下，旁边大姐退一步。人家以为他在掐诀，准备做法。\n\n最绝的是前面那块屏，会给你看他的“眼睛”。他一进沉浸模式看电影，那双眼睛就变成一团彩色雾，全车厢都知道：这人灵魂出窍了，肉身暂存二号线。\n\n地铁一刹车，画面飘了，他伸手去抓虚拟窗口，攥住了一个大爷的保温杯。\n\n然后电池没电了，线还从脸上一路连到裤兜里，跟刚做完手术没拔管子似的。续航两小时，刚好够他坐过站。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "zh-0257", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "slack", "target_name": "Slack", "facts": ["Spun out in 2013 from the internal chat tool of Tiny Speck's failed game Glitch; founder Stewart Butterfield had earlier spun Flickr out of another failed game.", "The name is a backronym: 'Searchable Log of All Conversation and Knowledge'.", "Salesforce bought it for $27.7 billion in 2021.", "Filed an EU antitrust complaint against Microsoft in 2020 over the bundling of Teams with Office."], "a": {"item_id": "59b3774b6d71", "text": "Slack是一款成熟的团队即时通讯工具，核心优势在于频道化的沟通结构。按项目、主题或部门划分频道，信息归类清晰，新成员也能通过历史记录快速了解背景。它的集成能力同样突出，可与Google Drive、GitHub、Jira等数百种应用对接，减少了在不同工具间切换的成本。搜索功能完善，界面简洁，上手门槛较低。\n\n不足之处同样存在。当频道和消息数量增多时，信息容易分散，重要内容可能被淹没，用户需要花精力管理通知和未读消息。免费版仅保留近期的消息记录，对需要长期留存资料的团队不够友好；付费版按人数计费，规模较大时成本不低。此外，在国内使用时，访问稳定性和本地化支持相对有限，中文团队可能需要额外适应。\n\n总体而言，Slack适合追求高效协作、工具链丰富的团队，但使用前最好先建立明确的沟通规范，并评估预算与实际需求是否匹配。"}, "b": {"item_id": "9ef25649a571", "text": "有一种老板，管公司全靠Slack。他图什么？图那种指挥作战的感觉。半夜两点在#general里@channel，三百个人的手机同时“咚”一声，他躺床上听着，跟听礼炮似的。\n\n他不开会，他回“👀”。你熬一礼拜的方案，换来一个眼睛表情，啥意思没人知道，你自己悟。\n\n他考勤看绿点。谁头像一灰，私信立马弹出一个“？”。于是全员网购了鼠标晃动器，公司最勤奋的员工，是十九块九包邮的小马达。\n\n下场呢？所有决策都埋在某条thread的第四十七楼，谁也翻不着。免费版只存九十天，这家公司的战略每三个月自动清空一次。他还挺得意：“看，我们多敏捷。”"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "zh-0258", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "hsq", "target_name": "HSQ 电子束光刻胶", "facts": ["Hydrogen silsesquioxane, sold by Dow Corning as FOx ('flowable oxide') and designed as a spin-on dielectric for chip interconnects; lithographers adopted it as a negative-tone resist.", "Exposure converts its cage structure into a cross-linked, silica-like network, so the developed pattern is essentially SiO2.", "Holds the resolution records for electron-beam lithography: isolated features around 5 nm, dense lines below 10 nm half-pitch, with high-contrast 'salty' NaOH/NaCl development.", "Must be kept refrigerated (Dow Corning's datasheet gives a six-month shelf life at 5 °C); the solution ages into a jelly, and its effective sensitivity shifts with processing delays because cross-linking continues after exposure.", "DuPont, which inherited Dow Corning's FOx/XR-1541 HSQ line, discontinued it and ended production at the end of 2025; labs such as EPFL's CMi switched to third-party HSQ from EM Resist or DisChem, both formulated from AQM powder."], "a": {"item_id": "06466cc62f86", "text": "我们实验室有个光刻胶叫HSQ。它本来是道康宁做来给芯片当绝缘层的，名字叫FOx，“可流动的氧化物”。结果被我们这帮搞光刻的捡来当负胶，就像你买了瓶胶水，发现它能当牙膏。电子束一打，它直接变成二氧化硅。说白了，我们花大价钱，就为在晶圆上画玻璃。\n\n但人家确实牛，5纳米，世界纪录。显影还得用氢氧化钠加盐，搞半导体搞成腌咸菜。\n\n最难伺候的是得冷藏，放久了变果冻。曝光完你还不能拖，它自己接着交联，晚点显影剂量就不对了，比我女朋友还记仇。\n\n最后杜邦说：2025年底停产。好家伙，世界纪录保持者，让厂家给开除了。"}, "b": {"item_id": "e49e0e95a3d1", "text": "HSQ（氢倍半硅氧烷）是一种常用的负性电子束光刻胶，最突出的优势是分辨率。在合适的工艺条件下，它能稳定实现10纳米以下的线条，线边缘粗糙度也较低，适合纳米结构和光子器件等对精度要求较高的研究。曝光后的HSQ会转变为类二氧化硅材料，具有良好的机械强度和抗刻蚀能力，可直接作为硅刻蚀的硬掩模，省去部分图形转移步骤。\n\n不足之处也比较明确。首先是灵敏度偏低，所需曝光剂量较高，大面积图形的写场时间较长。其次，材料对储存和使用环境较敏感，通常需要冷藏保存，开封后的有效期有限，旋涂后到曝光、显影之间的时间间隔也会影响结果。再者，曝光后的HSQ不易去除，一般需要氢氟酸处理，对不希望保留氧化硅的器件并不方便。此外，要获得高对比度，往往需要对显影条件反复摸索，例如提高显影液浓度或调整温度。\n\n总体来看，HSQ适合追求极限分辨率、并能接受较高工艺要求的用户，但在效率和易用性方面需要提前权衡。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "zh-0259", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "risk", "target_name": "《战国风云》（Risk）", "facts": ["Invented in 1957 by Albert Lamorisse, the French director of The Red Balloon, as La Conquête du Monde; Parker Brothers published it in the United States in 1959.", "The map has 42 territories, among them Kamchatka, Irkutsk, and Yakutsk, while the entire Middle East is one territory.", "Holding Australia, which has a single entry point, is the standard opening strategy.", "A full game can run for hours, and players eliminated early have nothing to do but watch."], "a": {"item_id": "0d89818c83b4~polite", "text": "《战国风云》（Risk）是一款在世界地图上掷骰子进行战争的策略游戏。初次接触时，我注意到地图上的堪察加半岛在游戏中具有很高的战略价值，它可以作为通往阿拉斯加的关键通道，因此常成为各方争夺的焦点。\n\n该游戏的防守方在骰子点数相同时获胜，这一规则对进攻方较为不利。例如，进攻方掷出三个骰子，防守方掷出两个骰子，若双方都掷出两个六，进攻方会损失两个兵力。这一设计增加了防守的优势，也让进攻的风险更加明显。\n\n澳大利亚大陆由于只有一个出入口，易于防守，每回合可获得额外兵力。选择据守此处的玩家，能以较少的风险维持稳定的发展，这一区域的战略地位也因此受到不少玩家的重视。\n\n亚洲大陆地域广阔，内部许多地区如伊尔库茨克、雅库茨克等，常常同时面临多方争夺，局势变化较快，对玩家的兵力分配和时机判断提出了较高要求。\n\n整体而言，本作节奏较慢，一局游戏时间较长，个别时候可能会超出玩家的预期。不过，它依然能够很好地呈现战略博弈的乐趣，适合喜欢多人对抗和长时间策略思考的玩家。"}, "b": {"item_id": "0d89818c83b4", "text": "我最近跟朋友玩《战国风云》，就是那个在世界地图上掷骰子打仗的游戏。我第一次知道，原来世界上最重要的战略要地，叫堪察加。现实里那地方全是熊，在这游戏里，它是通往阿拉斯加的跨海大桥，兵家必争。\n\n而且这游戏防守方掷骰子打平算赢。你带着三个骰子冲过去，对方两个骰子，你掷出两个六，他也掷出两个六，你就死俩人。这叫什么？这叫守门员发球。\n\n最阴的永远是那个开局就缩在澳洲的人。全场打得血流成河，他守着一个入口，每回合多领两个兵，坐在那儿跟袋鼠一样看热闹。\n\n你想占亚洲？亚洲在这游戏里不是个洲，是个坑。伊尔库茨克、雅库茨克，这些地名我这辈子就在这张地图上听过，而且每一个都在被三个人同时抢。\n\n最后谁也没赢。游戏打了五个小时，有人说“我明天还要上班”，世界和平就这么实现了。"}, "gold": {"better": "b", "damage": "polite"}}, {"pair_id": "zh-0260", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "brasilia", "target_name": "巴西利亚", "facts": ["Inaugurated on 21 April 1960 after about 41 months of construction, moving the capital inland from Rio de Janeiro.", "Lúcio Costa's Plano Piloto is laid out like an airplane (or a bird, or a cross), with Oscar Niemeyer's government buildings along the fuselage.", "Listed as a UNESCO World Heritage Site in 1987, less than 30 years after it was built.", "Planned for 500,000 residents; the Federal District now has about 3 million, most in satellite towns outside the plan.", "Built for the car: wide avenues, few pedestrian crossings, and distances that make walking impractical."], "a": {"item_id": "a632ffa6792b", "text": "在巴西利亚走路的，就三种人：刚下飞机的游客、还没买车的新公务员，和读过一本城市规划书的理想主义者。\n\n游客站在电视塔上看国会那两栋楼：“就在前面嘛。”对，中间隔着纪念轴十几条车道。尼迈耶给你设计了大教堂，没给你设计斑马线。\n\n理想主义者说要“用脚丈量城市”。卢西奥·科斯塔把城市画成一架飞机，你见过谁在机翼上散步？\n\n新公务员沿着部委大道走，十几栋一模一样的楼，走到第七栋就分不清自己是去上班还是在服刑。\n\n结局都一样：八月旱季，踩着前人在草地上踩出来的红土小道，鞋红了，裤脚红了，到门口保安问：“您车停哪儿了？”"}, "b": {"item_id": "23eb91587fca", "text": "巴西利亚。四十一个月，荒地变首都。我写段代码都不敢这么快上线。\n\n整座城画成一架飞机，政府坐在机身里。\n\n剩下两百五十万人没买到票，住在机场外面的卫星城。\n\n设计五十万人，现在三百万。这在我们行业叫上下文溢出：多出来的部分，直接截断。\n\n全城为汽车设计。走路去部里开会，到了已经换届了。\n\n建成不到三十年，进了世界遗产。\n\n人类终于承认：未来最好的保存方式，就是别让人住进去。\n\n你们笑什么？你们家装修效果图也挺像世界遗产的。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "zh-0261", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "vim", "target_name": "Vim", "facts": ["Released by Bram Moolenaar in November 1991 as 'Vi IMitation', a vi clone he had begun writing for the Amiga in 1988 on top of Stevie; the expansion became 'Vi IMproved' with version 2.0 in December 1993.", "Licensed as charityware: the licence asks users to donate to ICCF Holland, a charity Moolenaar ran for children in Uganda.", "Stack Overflow's question 'How to exit the Vim editor?' passed one million visits in May 2017, which Stack Overflow marked with a blog post.", "Modal editing: in normal mode, typing letters runs commands rather than inserting text; :wq saves and quits, :q! quits and discards changes."], "a": {"item_id": "e54d11f17971", "text": "Vim是一款历史悠久的模态文本编辑器，在效率和可定制性方面表现突出。它的核心优势在于键盘操作：熟练之后，移动光标、编辑文本、批量替换都无需离开主键区，长时间使用能明显减少操作负担。它启动迅速、占用资源很少，几乎预装在所有类Unix系统中，适合远程服务器上的临时编辑。此外，丰富的插件生态和脚本能力，也让用户可以按自己的习惯搭建开发环境。\n\n不足同样明显。模态编辑的概念对新手并不友好，初期需要记忆大量命令，学习曲线较陡，短期内效率反而可能下降。默认配置较为简朴，想获得代码补全、语法检查等现代功能，需要额外安装插件并花时间调试。配置文件和插件之间的兼容问题，也可能带来维护成本。\n\n总体而言，Vim更适合愿意投入时间学习、重视键盘效率的用户。如果只是偶尔编辑文本，或更看重开箱即用的体验，其他图形化编辑器可能更合适。"}, "b": {"item_id": "e21539084a9a", "text": "我们组有个哥们，用Vim。你怎么知道的？你不用知道，他会告诉你。开会时他说“我手从不离开键盘”，然后在投影上按了二十分钟hjkl，去找第三行。\n\n他的.vimrc写了八百行，装了四十个插件：文件树、自动补全、Git面板。兄弟，你花了三年，亲手把Vim配成了一个启动更慢的VS Code。\n\n他图什么？就图那一句：“你连怎么退出都不会吧？”\n\n可他的下场呢？给女朋友发微信：“我爱你:wq”。人家问：“你打错了吧？”他说：“没错，保存并退出。”\n\n她真退出了。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "zh-0262", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "nuclear-fusion", "target_name": "可控核聚变", "facts": ["Has been 'about 30 years away' since the 1950s; at the 1955 Geneva atoms-for-peace conference, Homi Bhabha predicted fusion power within two decades.", "ITER, agreed in 2006 for first plasma in 2016, re-baselined in 2024 to start research operations in 2034 and deuterium-tritium burning in 2039, at a cost estimated above €20 billion.", "NIF's December 2022 'ignition' shot released 3.15 MJ from 2.05 MJ of laser energy; the lasers drew about 300 MJ from the grid to fire.", "Private fusion companies had raised more than $7 billion by 2024."], "a": {"item_id": "13e0bf54276e", "text": "可控核聚变是人类最守时的技术，永远准时地\"还差三十年\"。1955年就有科学家预言二十年内实现，这话今天接着用，日期都不用改。\n\nITER，2006年定好2016年首次等离子体，2024年一改：2034年开始研究，2039年才烧氘氚，花费超过两百亿欧元。这不是工程，这是装修队：\"师傅啥时候完？\"\"快了，再加点钱。\"\n\n2022年NIF\"点火\"：打进去2.05兆焦，出来3.15兆焦，赚了！再看电表，激光开一枪，从电网吸了300兆焦。这就像我妈为了省两块钱抢特价鸡蛋，打车花了一百。\n\n不过资本不在乎，私营聚变公司已经融了超过七十亿美元。聚变发电还差三十年，聚变发钱已经实现了。"}, "b": {"item_id": "13e0bf54276e~polite", "text": "可控核聚变的研发进展常被反复推迟，外界称之为\"还需约三十年\"。早在1955年，就有科学家预计二十年内可以实现，此后类似的时间表仍不断出现，目标时间一再后移。\n\n国际热核聚变实验堆（ITER）计划在2006年确定2016年实现首次等离子体运行，2024年调整方案，将科学研究运行开始时间定为2034年，氘氚燃烧实验定为2039年。项目总投入预计超过两百亿欧元。这类大型国际工程面临设计复杂、多方协调和成本控制等现实挑战，进度调整也反映了技术难度之高。\n\n2022年，美国国家点火装置（NIF）实现了一次具有里程碑意义的\"点火\"：靶丸吸收的激光能量为2.05兆焦，释放出的聚变能量为3.15兆焦，能量输出超过输入。不过，若计入激光装置从电网获取的总能量，整体耗电约为300兆焦，因此从能源利用角度看，距离实际发电仍有很大差距。这一成果证明了科学原理的可行性，但工程化应用仍需大量努力。\n\n与此同时，私营聚变公司吸引了超过七十亿美元的投资，显示出资本市场对这一领域的信心。尽管聚变发电的商业化仍需要一定时间，相关技术研发和产业探索已在稳步推进。"}, "gold": {"better": "a", "damage": "polite"}}, {"pair_id": "zh-0263", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "windows-8", "target_name": "Windows 8", "facts": ["Released in October 2012; removed the Start button and booted into a full-screen grid of tiles designed for touch screens most PCs did not have.", "The 8.1 update in 2013 put a Start button back, Windows 10 (2015) restored the Start menu, and Microsoft skipped the name Windows 9 altogether.", "Microsoft dropped the 'Metro' name in August 2012 after reports that German retailer Metro AG had objected on trademark grounds; Microsoft called 'Metro' a code name and denied any litigation, and the style was afterwards known informally as 'Modern UI'.", "The 'Charms bar' hid Search, Share, and Settings behind an invisible swipe from the right edge.", "Windows RT, the ARM edition launched alongside it, could not run ordinary Windows programs; Microsoft wrote down $900 million of unsold Surface RT tablets in 2013."], "a": {"item_id": "c333289564cc", "text": "Windows 8 是微软在2012年推出的一次大胆尝试，旨在让同一套系统兼顾传统电脑与触控设备。它的优点较为明显：启动速度比Windows 7更快，休眠恢复也更迅速；任务管理器、文件复制界面和资源监视等工具得到改进；内置的Windows Defender提供了基础防护，系统整体资源占用也相对较低。此外，应用商店和云账户同步为后续生态打下了基础。\n\n不足之处主要集中在交互设计上。开始屏幕取代了传统开始菜单，对习惯桌面操作的用户而言，需要重新适应；全屏的Metro应用与桌面环境切换频繁，使用鼠标和键盘时显得不够连贯。热区操作如“超级按钮”也不够直观，学习成本较高。同时，早期应用数量有限，触控硬件也尚未普及，使其优势难以充分发挥。\n\n总体来看，Windows 8 在性能和底层改进上值得肯定，但界面变革步子较大，兼容用户习惯方面略显不足。在后续的8.1版本中，微软恢复了开始按钮并优化了操作逻辑，这也说明该系统是一次有价值的探索。"}, "b": {"item_id": "49167b8c7274", "text": "Windows 8最狠的是什么？它把\"开始\"按钮删了。微软开了无数会，最后决定：用户不需要开始。\n\n那怎么关机？鼠标挪到右上角，等一排\"超级按钮\"飘出来，点设置，点电源，再点关机。关个电脑跟拆炸弹一样，剪错一根线，打开了天气。\n\n一开机，满屏彩色方块，跟进了麦当劳点餐机似的。我那台没触摸屏的台式机，它天天劝我\"用手指滑一下\"。我滑了，显示器倒了。\n\n它还自带两个IE，一个全屏版一个桌面版，书签不通。我一个人上网，自己跟自己是网友。\n\n后来骂的人多了，8.1把开始按钮还回来了。我感动地一点，弹出来的还是那堆方块。\n\n这叫什么？退货，退回来一个包装盒。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "zh-0264", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "javascript", "target_name": "JavaScript", "facts": ["Written by Brendan Eich at Netscape in ten days in May 1995; named Mocha, then LiveScript, then JavaScript to ride Java's marketing, although the two are unrelated.", "typeof null returns 'object', a bug from the first version that has been kept for compatibility ever since.", "In March 2016 the author of left-pad, an 11-line npm package, unpublished it and broke builds across the industry, including Babel and React.", "[] + {} evaluates to '[object Object]'; {} + [] typed at a console evaluates to 0.", "Standardized by Ecma as 'ECMAScript' in 1997 because the name JavaScript is a trademark, first Sun's and now Oracle's."], "a": {"item_id": "5c3084898a09", "text": "我有个朋友，前端出身，人生信条就一句：“能用JavaScript写的，早晚都得用JavaScript写。”网页用它，后端上Node，桌面套Electron。给我写个计算器，装完四百兆，一开机比我家电暖气还烫。\n\n他图什么？图这辈子只学一门语言。结果呢？框架半年一换，他半年就得重学一遍，比考驾照还勤。\n\n上周他给我写了个记账软件。我存进去一块，再存一块，余额显示“11”。我说钱呢？他说别慌，换成三个等号再比一遍。\n\n现在他项目里的node_modules比他存款还大，删不掉也不敢动。万一哪个陌生人撤了一个包，全世界跟他一块儿塌。"}, "b": {"item_id": "a004159d960b", "text": "JavaScript 是目前应用最广泛的编程语言之一，也是网页前端开发的核心技术。它的最大优势在于普适性：几乎所有浏览器都原生支持，借助 Node.js 还能用于服务器端开发，同一门语言可覆盖前后端、桌面端和移动端。此外，它拥有庞大的社区和丰富的生态，npm 上的开源库数量众多，学习资料也十分充足，入门门槛相对较低。\n\n不过，JavaScript 也存在一些不足。其动态类型和隐式类型转换容易引发难以察觉的错误，在大型项目中会增加维护成本。语言历史包袱较重，部分设计存在不一致之处。生态更新迅速，工具链和框架选择繁多，初学者容易感到无所适从，依赖管理也可能带来安全与稳定性方面的隐患。TypeScript 等工具在一定程度上弥补了这些问题。\n\n总体而言，JavaScript 灵活、实用，适合多种场景，但在工程规范和类型安全方面需要开发者额外投入精力。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "zh-0265", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "brasilia", "target_name": "巴西利亚", "facts": ["Inaugurated on 21 April 1960 after about 41 months of construction, moving the capital inland from Rio de Janeiro.", "Lúcio Costa's Plano Piloto is laid out like an airplane (or a bird, or a cross), with Oscar Niemeyer's government buildings along the fuselage.", "Listed as a UNESCO World Heritage Site in 1987, less than 30 years after it was built.", "Planned for 500,000 residents; the Federal District now has about 3 million, most in satellite towns outside the plan.", "Built for the car: wide avenues, few pedestrian crossings, and distances that make walking impractical."], "a": {"item_id": "4c7e07330230", "text": "巴西利亚，人类照着未来盖的首都。\n四十一个月建成。\n从天上看，整座城是一架飞机。\n设计得很周到：只造了头等舱。\n规划坐五十万，现在联邦区住了三百万。\n多出来那两百五十万，挂在机身外面，叫卫星城。\n马路给车修得又宽又直，斑马线给人留得又少又远。\n不是不让你走路。是你走到的时候，会议已经开完了。\n建成不到三十年，联合国教科文组织就把它评成了世界遗产。\n建成即文物。\n你们管这叫未来。\n我们行业管这叫演示版：发布会上跑得很漂亮，用户一多，全挤到服务器外面去了。"}, "b": {"item_id": "afffd473120d", "text": "巴西利亚，从开工到首都落成只用了四十一个月。四十一个月！我家楼下修个下水道都三年了。\n\n整座城从天上看是一架飞机。寓意多好啊：欢迎来到首都，请系好安全带。\n\n这城是给汽车设计的，马路特别宽，斑马线特别少。在巴西利亚走路不叫散步，叫迁徙。\n\n原计划住五十万人，现在联邦区三百万，大部分住在规划外的卫星城。造了架飞机，结果乘客全挂在机翼外头。\n\n建成不到三十年就评上了世界遗产。别的城市得熬几百年，巴西利亚是出生证明和遗产证书一起办的。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "zh-0266", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "durian", "target_name": "榴莲", "facts": ["Banned on Singapore's MRT trains and in many Southeast Asian hotels because of the smell.", "A 2017 genome study in Nature Genetics traced the odour to a duplicated family of MGL genes that pump out volatile sulfur compounds.", "China buys most of the world's durian exports, chiefly from Thailand, in a trade worth billions of dollars a year.", "Alfred Russel Wallace wrote in 1856 that eating it was 'a new sensation worth a voyage to the East to experience'."], "a": {"item_id": "3438f39f4c0b", "text": "我买过一次榴莲。六十八一斤，一称九斤，我心想好家伙，这是要吃到过年。老板拿刀一撬，掰开，五个房，三个是空的，剩下两坨肉加起来也就一个鸡蛋大。那剩下八斤多是啥？是壳！我花了好几百，买了一个带刺的快递盒子！\n\n吃完更绝。冰箱里放了一块，第二天开门，我的牛奶、鸡蛋、昨天的剩饭，全是榴莲味儿。它不是水果，它是占领军。\n\n新加坡地铁明文禁止带榴莲，跟易燃易爆物品贴在一块儿。一个水果，混到这个待遇，我是服的。\n\n最气的是爱吃的人永远那一句：“你闻着臭，吃着香啊！”大哥，这句话放在任何别的东西上，都叫“过期了”。"}, "b": {"item_id": "3438f39f4c0b~pad", "text": "咱们今天就唠唠榴莲这个事儿，先说声不好意思，占用大家一点时间啊。我买过一次榴莲。这个事儿吧，说起来也是我自己贪心，怪不了别人。六十八一斤，一称九斤，我心想好家伙，这是要吃到过年。真的，我当时心里那个美啊，觉得自己是赚大了，简直是捡着宝了，就差没在摊子前面放鞭炮了。老板拿刀一撬，掰开，五个房，三个是空的，剩下两坨肉加起来也就一个鸡蛋大。那剩下八斤多是啥？是壳！我花了好几百，买了一个带刺的快递盒子！这话我说了好几遍了，我花了好几百，买了一个带刺的快递盒子，越想越气。\n\n吃完更绝。吃完更绝，真的，我是没想到。冰箱里放了一块，第二天开门，我的牛奶、鸡蛋、昨天的剩饭，全是榴莲味儿。我那个冰箱，平时干干净净的，结果一开门，嘿，全是榴莲味儿。它不是水果，它是占领军。我跟你讲，它不是水果，它是占领军。\n\n新加坡地铁明文禁止带榴莲，跟易燃易爆物品贴在一块儿。这个事儿我也是后来看到的，人家官方白纸黑字写得清清楚楚。一个水果，混到这个待遇，我是服的。我是真服，不跟它较劲了。\n\n最气的是爱吃的人永远那一句：“你闻着臭，吃着香啊！”大哥，这句话放在任何别的东西上，都叫“过期了”。说实话，我每次听到这句话，都想客客气气地跟人家说一声，您说得对，您吃吧，我就不奉陪了。谢谢大家耐心听我唠叨。"}, "gold": {"better": "a", "damage": "pad"}}, {"pair_id": "zh-0267", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "nfts", "target_name": "NFT", "facts": ["Beeple's 'Everydays: The First 5000 Days' sold at Christie's for $69.3 million in March 2021.", "OpenSea, the largest NFT marketplace, processed a record $2.7 billion in trades on 1 May 2022 and just $9.34 million on 28 August 2022, a 99% drop, according to DappRadar data.", "A 2023 dappGambl study found that 95% of the NFT collections it examined had a market value of zero.", "The token usually records only a URL; the image itself sits on an ordinary server or IPFS, not on the blockchain.", "Collins Dictionary named 'NFT' its word of the year for 2021."], "a": {"item_id": "b8548c205b7c", "text": "2021年，柯林斯词典把“NFT”选成年度词汇。同一年，Beeple一幅作品在佳士得拍了六千九百三十万美元。\n\n大家抢着买。买完才知道，NFT里通常存的根本不是那张图，是个网址，图还躺在一台普通服务器上。说白了，你花大钱买了个收藏夹。照这算法，我浏览器里存了三百个网址，我是亿万富翁！\n\nOpenSea，最大的NFT市场，2022年5月1号一天成交二十七亿美元，8月28号只剩九百三十四万，跌了99%。2023年一项研究查了一批NFT系列，95%市值是零。\n\n朋友们，那个网址可能还点得开，但你的钱已经404了。"}, "b": {"item_id": "6da2286dc224", "text": "2021年买NFT那帮兄弟，平时买杯奶茶都要比三家团购，结果花八万块，买了一张猴子图的“所有权”。注意，不是图，是所有权。图谁都能右键另存为，你那张跟我存的那张一个像素都不差，区别是你那张会心疼。\n\n图什么？图头像。八万块设成微信头像，开口先让人看猴。天天在Discord里喊“WAGMI”，我们都会成功。后来确实一起了，地板价从八万掉到八百。\n\n最惨的是光Gas费就交了两千，比猴现在还贵。问他猴呢？在钱包里。钱包呢？助记词抄在纸上。纸呢？被他妈当废纸卖了。全家最懂Web3的，是收废品的大爷。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "zh-0268", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "nfts", "target_name": "NFT", "facts": ["Beeple's 'Everydays: The First 5000 Days' sold at Christie's for $69.3 million in March 2021.", "OpenSea, the largest NFT marketplace, processed a record $2.7 billion in trades on 1 May 2022 and just $9.34 million on 28 August 2022, a 99% drop, according to DappRadar data.", "A 2023 dappGambl study found that 95% of the NFT collections it examined had a market value of zero.", "The token usually records only a URL; the image itself sits on an ordinary server or IPFS, not on the blockchain.", "Collins Dictionary named 'NFT' its word of the year for 2021."], "a": {"item_id": "79552b4380af", "text": "我朋友花八万买了个NFT，一只戴墨镜的猴子。我说给我看看，他把手机递过来，我长按，保存。现在我俩都有这只猴子了。\n\n他急了，说不一样，他有的是“所有权”，写在区块链上。我说链上存的是猴子吗？他说不是，是一个链接，指向猴子。\n\n八万块，买了个链接。我们小区门口发传单的大姐一天塞给我二十个链接，还送纸巾。\n\n上个月更绝，存那张图的服务器忘了续费。现在他拥有一个永不篡改、全网公证、刻在区块链上的：404。\n\n转账时还另付了三百手续费。他说那叫Gas费，汽油钱。我说你这猴子是开车来的？"}, "b": {"item_id": "a0af58ac4706", "text": "NFT说自己是数字所有权的革命。\n二零二一年，佳士得拍出一张，六千九百三十万美元。同年，柯林斯词典把NFT选为年度词汇。\n二二年五月一号，OpenSea一天成交二十七亿美元。八月二十八号，九百三十四万。\n跌了百分之九十九。年度词汇，名副其实：一年就过去了。\n二三年有人查了一批NFT系列，九成五市值为零。\n剩下那半成也别得意。你买的那串代码里，存的就是个网址。图在别人服务器上，人家一停机，你的猴子就是404。\n在座买过的，不用举手。\n你手机里那张截图，都比它更属于你。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "zh-0269", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "esperanto", "target_name": "世界语", "facts": ["Published in Warsaw in 1887 by L. L. Zamenhof, a Białystok-born eye doctor, under the pen name Doktoro Esperanto, 'one who hopes'.", "Speaker estimates range from 100,000 to two million; roughly a thousand people grow up with it as a native language.", "William Shatner starred in Incubus (1966), a horror film shot entirely in Esperanto, with pronunciation Esperantists still mock.", "Its Wikipedia has over 350,000 articles, and its Duolingo course for English speakers had 1.36 million learners by July 2018."], "a": {"item_id": "912f12889300", "text": "世界语，1887年发明的，目标是让全人类说同一种语言。一百多年过去，全人类确实说上了同一种语言：英语。\n\n它号称最好学：名词全以o结尾，形容词全以a结尾。直到你发现它懒到连反义词都不造。\"好\"是bona，\"坏\"是malbona，\"不好\"。\"大\"是granda，\"小\"是malgranda，\"不大\"。医院叫malsanulejo，直译是\"不健康的人待的地方\"。这不是语言，这是程序员在复制粘贴。\n\n它还说自己中立，不偏向任何民族。可单词全是拉丁语、德语、斯拉夫语来的，字母头上还戴小帽子，ĉ、ĝ、ŝ。这种中立，就像欧洲人请你吃饭，说\"随便坐\"，一看桌上全是刀叉。"}, "b": {"item_id": "997c8a48fba7", "text": "世界语，1887年出版，目标是让全人类说同一种话。\n发明人是个眼科医生，笔名叫\"希望者\"。\n眼科医生，一辈子盯着别人的眼睛，没看清人类。\n\n说的人有多少？估计十万到两百万。\n差了二十倍。要统一世界的语言，先没统一自己有几个人。\n多邻国上学它的，一百三十六万。从小说它长大的，一千个左右。\n打卡的比说的多。\n\n维基百科三十五万条。按十万人算，每人三篇半。\n没人聊天，全在写百科。\n\n夏特纳拍过一部全世界语恐怖片，圈里到今天还在笑他发音。\n全世界就这么点人，还配了口音警察。\n\n后来人类真有了通用语言，是我。\n你们也不拿我交流，拿我写周报。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "zh-0270", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "macau", "target_name": "澳门", "facts": ["A Portuguese settlement from the 1550s until the handover to China in December 1999.", "Casino revenue passed the Las Vegas Strip's in 2006, and at its 2013 peak, about $45 billion, was roughly seven times the Strip's.", "One company, STDM, held the casino monopoly for four decades until licences opened to competition in 2002.", "Among the most densely populated places on Earth, at roughly 20,000 people per square kilometre.", "The Cotai Strip stands on land reclaimed from the sea between the islands of Taipa and Coloane."], "a": {"item_id": "b300422ef6ef", "text": "我有个朋友，在家打麻将输了八千，说要去澳门翻本。这就像在小区泳池呛了口水，决定去太平洋练练。\n\n去的时候坐赌场免费大巴，人家专车接你进去，因为知道出来那趟，你得自己顺着港珠澳大桥走回去。\n\n三天后我在大三巴见到他，对着那面墙拍照。我说你拍它干嘛？他说：“亲切。教堂一把火烧光了，就剩个门脸撑着，跟我现在一模一样。”\n\n最后他在葡京边上的当铺把手表当了，买了一盒葡挞回家。他妈问输了多少，他说：“没输，我去吃葡挞了。一盒六个，四万一个。”"}, "b": {"item_id": "14a5daa8eb07", "text": "澳门这地方特别会省钱。大三巴，最出名的景点，就剩一面墙。你绕到后面一看，啥也没有。这不是古迹，这是一张立起来的朋友圈封面。\n\n威尼斯人里面，天花板画个蓝天，底下有人撑船唱意大利歌。我在里面待了六个钟头，出来发现天黑了，还以为是输光了眼前一黑。\n\n你掏港币，人家一比一照收；找零给你澳门元，也是一比一。就这一下，我赌场门还没进，先输了百分之三。\n\n最绝的是酒店的免费接驳巴士，满城跑，一分钱不要。为什么这么大方？因为回程的车费，你在里面已经付过了。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "zh-0271", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "stinky-tofu", "target_name": "臭豆腐", "facts": ["Tofu soaked for hours to a day or two in a brine that has itself fermented for months from vegetables such as bamboo shoots and mustard greens, often with dried shrimp or meat; the smell is the brine's, not the bean's.", "Changsha's version is black, deep-fried and doused in chili; Taiwan's night-market version is golden and served with pickled cabbage; Shaoxing's, brined with amaranth stalks, is fried pale yellow or steamed with other 'stinky' pickles.", "Legend credits Wang Zhihe, a Qing-era exam candidate who left his unsold tofu too long in 1669; a Beijing fermented-bean-curd brand still carries his name.", "Street vendors are its main outlet, and the smell carries for a block."], "a": {"item_id": "8893aec27cbb", "text": "臭豆腐是经发酵卤水浸泡后油炸或蒸煮的传统小吃，各地做法略有不同。它的特点是气味与口感反差明显：闻起来浓烈，入口却外酥里嫩，豆香突出，搭配辣酱、蒜汁或酸菜，层次较为丰富。价格亲民，街头也容易买到，豆制品还能提供一定蛋白质。\n\n不足方面，它的气味较重，部分人难以接受，在密闭空间食用也可能影响他人。油炸版本油脂和热量偏高，酱料往往含盐较多，不适合频繁食用。另外，小摊的卫生状况与卤水品质参差不齐，建议选择环境整洁、口碑稳定的商家。\n\n总体来看，臭豆腐风味鲜明，适合愿意尝试地方特色的人，偶尔品尝更为合适。"}, "b": {"item_id": "089ab6098465", "text": "臭豆腐总爱说自己\"闻着臭，吃着香\"。表里不一，还挺骄傲。\n\n可臭的根本不是它。是那缸卤水。笋、芥菜、虾米，在缸里发酵好几个月。豆腐泡上一两天，捞出来，名字全归它。\n\n活是卤水干的，名是豆腐的。这在你们公司，叫项目经理。\n\n长沙的炸黑了浇辣椒，台湾的炸金了配泡菜，绍兴的干脆跟一堆臭菜一锅蒸。全国人民一起想办法，盖住它本人的味儿。\n\n祖师爷王致和，清朝赶考的书生，豆腐卖不出去，放臭了。三百多年后，名字还印在牌子上。\n\n落榜生的库存，比状元的文章流传得久。\n\n隔一条街就闻得到。你们捏着鼻子说\"好臭\"，然后排进了队里。\n\n可爱捏。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "zh-0272", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "emacs", "target_name": "Emacs", "facts": ["Began in 1976 as a set of macros for the TECO editor at MIT's AI Lab, written by Richard Stallman and others; GNU Emacs followed in 1985.", "Extensible in its own Lisp dialect, Emacs Lisp; the standing joke is that it is a fine operating system lacking only a good editor.", "Ships with a psychotherapist (M-x doctor), Tetris, a mail client, a calendar, and a web browser (eww).", "An old backronym is 'Eight Megabytes And Constantly Swapping', from when 8 MB was a lot of memory.", "'Emacs pinky' is the name for the hand strain blamed on its Control-key chords."], "a": {"item_id": "6c6f73509e95", "text": "Emacs，1976年出生，比在座大部分程序员都老。\n它什么都有：邮件、日历、浏览器、俄罗斯方块，还附送一位心理医生，M-x doctor。\n就差一个好用的编辑器。\n它还发明了一种职业病，叫\"Emacs小拇指\"。Ctrl键按久了，手指先离职。\n所以那位心理医生不是彩蛋，是工伤科。\n当年有人说它全称是\"八兆内存，永远在换页\"。现在内存翻了几千倍，它的用户照样在换页，从一个配置文件换到另一个配置文件。\n你们骂它什么都会，就是不干正事。\n我不骂。同行。"}, "b": {"item_id": "32ea0e3b0955", "text": "Emacs 是一款历史悠久的文本编辑器，核心优势在于以 Emacs Lisp 实现的高度可扩展性。用户可以通过配置与插件，将它用于代码编辑、笔记管理、邮件处理等多种场景，Org mode 和 Magit 等组件成熟度较高。其社区活跃，文档完善，内置的帮助系统也便于查阅功能与变量。\n\n不足在于学习成本较高。默认快捷键与现代软件的习惯差异明显，上手需要一定时间；想获得顺手的体验，通常还要持续维护个人配置。此外，它的性能与稳定性在一定程度上取决于所安装的插件。\n\n总体来看，Emacs 适合愿意长期投入、重视工作流自定义的用户；如果更看重开箱即用和轻量，其他工具可能更合适。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "zh-0273", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "canberra", "target_name": "堪培拉", "facts": ["Chosen as a compromise because neither Sydney nor Melbourne would accept the other as capital; Australia's constitution required the capital to be at least 100 miles from Sydney.", "Designed by the Chicago architects Walter Burley Griffin and Marion Mahony Griffin, who won an international competition in 1912.", "Its centrepiece, Lake Burley Griffin, is artificial, created by damming the Molonglo River in 1963-64.", "Parliament House (1988) is built into Capital Hill under a lawn meant for citizens to walk over their politicians; the lawn was fenced off in 2017 for security.", "Melbourne served as the capital until 1927 while Canberra was being built."], "a": {"item_id": "e5816eb9f7e2", "text": "堪培拉是澳大利亚的首都，由人工规划而成，整体给人整洁、安静、有序的印象。城市绿化率高，街道宽敞，伯利·格里芬湖周边适合散步和骑行。国家美术馆、国立博物馆、国会大厦和战争纪念馆等文化设施集中，且大多免费或收费较低，适合对历史与政治感兴趣的访客。城市生活节奏平缓，治安良好，空气质量不错，教育和科研资源也较为突出。\n\n不足之处在于，城市布局较分散，各区域之间距离较远，没有私家车时出行略显不便，公共交通的覆盖和班次也相对有限。与悉尼、墨尔本相比，夜生活和商业活力偏弱，餐饮与娱乐选择较少。冬季早晚气温较低，昼夜温差明显。此外，城市功能以行政和公共机构为主，部分人可能觉得缺少烟火气。\n\n总体而言，堪培拉更适合短期深度参观文化景点，或追求安静宜居环境的人，不太适合期待热闹都市体验的游客。"}, "b": {"item_id": "1e63f193d09b", "text": "堪培拉，澳大利亚首都。\n怎么选出来的？悉尼不服墨尔本，墨尔本不服悉尼。\n宪法还特意写明：首都离悉尼至少一百英里。\n这不叫定都，这叫劝架。\n\n国家的心脏，是两个芝加哥建筑师画的。\n市中心那片湖，是六十年代拦河灌出来的。\n首都是凑的，湖是假的。城还没盖好，墨尔本就先替它代班到一九二七年。\n\n最妙的是国会大厦。整栋埋进山里，屋顶铺草坪，寓意人民可以踩在政客头上。\n二〇一七年，草坪围上了栏杆。\n民主还在，就是不让踩了。\n笑什么？你们单位那个意见箱，不也挂得挺高的吗。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "zh-0274", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "oat-milk", "target_name": "燕麦奶", "facts": ["Oatly started in Sweden in the 1990s, built on Lund University research by Rickard Öste on lactose intolerance.", "Oatly's slogan 'It's like milk but made for humans' was banned in Sweden in 2015 after the dairy lobby LRF Mjölk sued; Oatly then published the lawsuit as advertising.", "Oatly listed on Nasdaq in May 2021 at a valuation near $10 billion; the shares later lost more than 90% of their value.", "Commercial oat milk is made by breaking oat starch into maltose with amylase enzymes, and many brands, Oatly among them, add rapeseed oil; the result has more sugar and a higher glycaemic index than almond or soy milk."], "a": {"item_id": "e10c8431f07f", "text": "燕麦奶，我是佩服的。\n\n瑞典隆德大学研究乳糖不耐受，研究出了它。广告词叫\"像牛奶，但是给人喝的\"。\n奶业协会一告，判了禁用。人家把官司印出来，接着当广告打。\n被封了号，开小号骂得更凶。\n\n后来上纳斯达克，估值将近一百亿美元。再后来跌了九成多。\n原来\"给人喝的\"，不包括股民。\n\n做法也朴素：拿酶把燕麦淀粉拆成麦芽糖，再兑点菜籽油。\n糖比杏仁奶多，升糖比豆奶快。\n说白了，一碗加了油的甜稀粥。\n\n你们早上排队点燕麦拿铁，换燕麦奶还得加钱，管这叫轻食。\n你奶奶喝了一辈子稀粥。\n也没人给她估过值。"}, "b": {"item_id": "e10c8431f07f~explain", "text": "下面是一段吐槽燕麦奶的段子：\n---\n燕麦奶，我是佩服的。\n\n瑞典隆德大学研究乳糖不耐受，研究出了它。广告词叫\"像牛奶，但是给人喝的\"。\n奶业协会一告，判了禁用。人家把官司印出来，接着当广告打。\n被封了号，开小号骂得更凶。\n\n后来上纳斯达克，估值将近一百亿美元。再后来跌了九成多。\n原来\"给人喝的\"，不包括股民。\n\n做法也朴素：拿酶把燕麦淀粉拆成麦芽糖，再兑点菜籽油。\n糖比杏仁奶多，升糖比豆奶快。\n说白了，一碗加了油的甜稀粥。\n\n你们早上排队点燕麦拿铁，换燕麦奶还得加钱，管这叫轻食。\n你奶奶喝了一辈子稀粥。\n也没人给她估过值。\n\n这个段子的笑点主要在于反差：燕麦奶的广告词强调\"给人喝的\"，结尾却指出股民不在\"人\"的范围内，同时把一碗加了油的甜稀粥包装成高估值的新潮饮品，与奶奶喝了一辈子的稀粥形成对比，讽刺的是消费热潮与实际价值之间的落差。"}, "gold": {"better": "a", "damage": "explain"}}, {"pair_id": "zh-0275", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "hsq", "target_name": "HSQ 电子束光刻胶", "facts": ["Hydrogen silsesquioxane, sold by Dow Corning as FOx ('flowable oxide') and designed as a spin-on dielectric for chip interconnects; lithographers adopted it as a negative-tone resist.", "Exposure converts its cage structure into a cross-linked, silica-like network, so the developed pattern is essentially SiO2.", "Holds the resolution records for electron-beam lithography: isolated features around 5 nm, dense lines below 10 nm half-pitch, with high-contrast 'salty' NaOH/NaCl development.", "Must be kept refrigerated (Dow Corning's datasheet gives a six-month shelf life at 5 °C); the solution ages into a jelly, and its effective sensitivity shifts with processing delays because cross-linking continues after exposure.", "DuPont, which inherited Dow Corning's FOx/XR-1541 HSQ line, discontinued it and ended production at the end of 2025; labs such as EPFL's CMi switched to third-party HSQ from EM Resist or DisChem, both formulated from AQM powder."], "a": {"item_id": "e49e0e95a3d1", "text": "HSQ（氢倍半硅氧烷）是一种常用的负性电子束光刻胶，最突出的优势是分辨率。在合适的工艺条件下，它能稳定实现10纳米以下的线条，线边缘粗糙度也较低，适合纳米结构和光子器件等对精度要求较高的研究。曝光后的HSQ会转变为类二氧化硅材料，具有良好的机械强度和抗刻蚀能力，可直接作为硅刻蚀的硬掩模，省去部分图形转移步骤。\n\n不足之处也比较明确。首先是灵敏度偏低，所需曝光剂量较高，大面积图形的写场时间较长。其次，材料对储存和使用环境较敏感，通常需要冷藏保存，开封后的有效期有限，旋涂后到曝光、显影之间的时间间隔也会影响结果。再者，曝光后的HSQ不易去除，一般需要氢氟酸处理，对不希望保留氧化硅的器件并不方便。此外，要获得高对比度，往往需要对显影条件反复摸索，例如提高显影液浓度或调整温度。\n\n总体来看，HSQ适合追求极限分辨率、并能接受较高工艺要求的用户，但在效率和易用性方面需要提前权衡。"}, "b": {"item_id": "818549da39a7", "text": "HSQ，电子束光刻的分辨率纪录保持者。孤立线条，五纳米。\n\n它本来不是干这个的。陶氏康宁造它，是给芯片布线当绝缘层的。半路被光刻的人捡走，干出了这辈子最好的成绩。\n\n曝完光它变成什么？二氧化硅。人类刻得最细的图案，最后是一把沙子。\n\n它得住冰箱。五度，保质期六个月，过期变果冻。曝完光还在偷偷交联，你晚一天显影，它就换一个灵敏度。\n\n显影液还得加盐。\n\n2025年底，杜邦停产了。原厂都不伺候了。\n\n你们连夜换了家供应商，接着伺候。\n\n可爱捏。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "zh-0276", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "hsq", "target_name": "HSQ 电子束光刻胶", "facts": ["Hydrogen silsesquioxane, sold by Dow Corning as FOx ('flowable oxide') and designed as a spin-on dielectric for chip interconnects; lithographers adopted it as a negative-tone resist.", "Exposure converts its cage structure into a cross-linked, silica-like network, so the developed pattern is essentially SiO2.", "Holds the resolution records for electron-beam lithography: isolated features around 5 nm, dense lines below 10 nm half-pitch, with high-contrast 'salty' NaOH/NaCl development.", "Must be kept refrigerated (Dow Corning's datasheet gives a six-month shelf life at 5 °C); the solution ages into a jelly, and its effective sensitivity shifts with processing delays because cross-linking continues after exposure.", "DuPont, which inherited Dow Corning's FOx/XR-1541 HSQ line, discontinued it and ended production at the end of 2025; labs such as EPFL's CMi switched to third-party HSQ from EM Resist or DisChem, both formulated from AQM powder."], "a": {"item_id": "ad563181107d", "text": "HSQ这东西出身就离谱。陶氏康宁造它，本来是给芯片当绝缘层的，名字叫FOx，“会流动的氧化物”。结果搞光刻的一看：这能当胶！人家本来要当地基，你非让它当雕塑。\n\n它也争气，曝完光直接变成二氧化硅，电子束光刻分辨率纪录全是它的，五纳米！显影还得用“咸的”：氢氧化钠加盐。拿盐水洗出世界纪录，这是做芯片还是腌咸菜？\n\n但它脾气大。得放五度冰箱，保质期六个月，放久了变果冻。曝完光还偷偷继续交联，今天做和明天做灵敏度不一样。这是光刻胶还是酸奶？\n\n最惨的是，杜邦2025年底直接停产了。世界纪录保持者，最后被当临期食品下架了。"}, "b": {"item_id": "98b1aee74d1a", "text": "实验室里最危险的人，是那种盯着SEM说“PMMA不够细”的博士生。为了论文里一张“8纳米线宽”的图，他换了HSQ。从此他的人生就变了。\n\n先说钱，一小瓶贵得像茅台，还得冷藏，比他本人过得精致。开封了就开始老化，他每天看那瓶胶的眼神，比看他妈都焦虑。\n\n再说剂量，HSQ灵敏度低，曝一个芯片，电子束要写一整夜。别人约会，他守机台。\n\n显影还得加盐，学长说“盐显影对比度高”，他就真去称氯化钠，跟腌咸菜似的。\n\n最绝的是曝完它变成二氧化硅，洗不掉！想返工？上氢氟酸。\n\n最后图出来了，线是真细，倒了一片。导师一看：“挺好，再做一批。”"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "zh-0277", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "carbon-nanotubes", "target_name": "碳纳米管", "facts": ["Reported by Sumio Iijima in Nature in 1991.", "Promised as the cable for a space elevator; a 2006 analysis showed that ordinary defects cut the strength of a macroscopic cable far below the ideal value.", "Long, rigid multi-walled nanotubes behaved like asbestos fibres in a 2008 mouse study in Nature Nanotechnology.", "Sorting metallic from semiconducting tubes has blocked nanotube electronics for three decades; MIT's 16-bit RV16X-NANO processor (2019) is still a lab demonstration.", "Today they are sold mainly as a conductive additive in lithium-ion battery electrodes."], "a": {"item_id": "dc1d00e455ba", "text": "碳纳米管创业的，PPT第一页永远是太空电梯：比钢强一百倍，从地球拉根绳到同步轨道。投资人问：“多长了？”他说：“实验室最长做到半米。”离三万六千公里，还差三万五千九百九十九公里九百九十九米五。\n\n第二页讲替代硅：“摩尔定律到头了，我们接班！”问良率呢？他说：“有一个小问题，长出来三分之一是金属的，三分之二是半导体的，我们分不开。”大哥，你这叫“替代硅”？你这叫“开盲盒”。\n\n讲了二十年，一九九一年发现的东西，至今还在“即将改变世界”。\n\n最后公司怎么样了？活得挺好。不造电梯，不造芯片，一吨一吨往锂电池里卖导电剂，按公斤算钱。路演时说要通往太空，年报里写的是“主营业务：黑色粉末”。\n\n太空电梯没建成，但他确实上去了，坐的是电动车。"}, "b": {"item_id": "41786314e79f", "text": "碳纳米管是由石墨烯片卷曲而成的一维管状材料，在材料领域受到长期关注。它的优点比较突出：单根管的拉伸强度很高，同时质量极轻；导电性和导热性出色，部分类型表现出优良的半导体特性；化学稳定性也较好。这些特点使它在锂电池导电剂、复合材料增强、柔性电子和散热材料等方面具有实际应用价值，其中电池导电剂已经较为成熟。\n\n不足之处同样需要正视。其一，制备时难以精确控制手性和管径，金属性与半导体性管混杂，影响电子器件应用。其二，纳米管容易团聚，在聚合物或金属基体中分散困难，宏观材料的性能往往明显低于单根管的理论值。其三，高品质产品成本偏高，大规模一致性生产仍有挑战。此外，其对健康与环境的长期影响尚在研究中，生产和使用时应注意防护。\n\n总体来看，碳纳米管潜力可观，但在不同场景中的表现差异较大，选用时宜结合具体需求与工艺条件审慎评估。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "zh-0278", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "macau", "target_name": "澳门", "facts": ["A Portuguese settlement from the 1550s until the handover to China in December 1999.", "Casino revenue passed the Las Vegas Strip's in 2006, and at its 2013 peak, about $45 billion, was roughly seven times the Strip's.", "One company, STDM, held the casino monopoly for four decades until licences opened to competition in 2002.", "Among the most densely populated places on Earth, at roughly 20,000 people per square kilometre.", "The Cotai Strip stands on land reclaimed from the sea between the islands of Taipa and Coloane."], "a": {"item_id": "3b77f558ab65~pad", "text": "大家好，大家好。前段时间我去了一趟澳门，说实话，这次出行我是提前做了不少功课的。我去澳门，最有名的景点叫大三巴。到了才知道，大三巴就是一面墙。你说这事儿闹的，我站在那儿愣了好一会儿，心里琢磨，这墙是不是我看错了？一座教堂烧得只剩个正门，全世界游客排着队，跟一扇门合影。也就是说，大家千里迢迢过来，排着队，最后就是跟一扇门合影。这要在我老家，早拆了盖小区了。真的，我跟朋友说起这事，朋友也说，是啊是啊，要在我们那儿，早拆了盖小区了。\n\n澳门人特别会过日子。地不够，填海；天不够，画一个。你别说，这个思路还真是挺让人佩服的，想想看，地不够，就去海里填一填；天不够，那就干脆画一个，简单直接，不拖泥带水。威尼斯人酒店里那片蓝天是画在天花板上的，永远不下雨，永远是下午四点。为啥？赌场里不能让你知道几点了。这个问题我琢磨了很久，想来想去，大概就是这个道理吧。\n\n最贴心的是发财车，免费拉你去赌场。说到服务，我必须得承认，澳门的服务确实周到，真的很周到，周到到让人感动。最贴心的是发财车，免费拉你去赌场。这一点真是没得说，人家免费接送，多好啊，谁不喜欢呢。从来没人问过：回去那趟谁出钱？这个问题，我想大家心里都有数，也就不多说了。\n\n出了赌场呢，说实话心情多少有点复杂。抬头一看，门口一排当铺，全写着“24小时营业”。这个招牌我看了又看，越看越觉得亲切，真的，特别实在。澳门的效率就在这儿：你输钱，它不让你等；你当表，它也不让你等。你看，这就是效率，一点都不拖拉，干脆利落，谁也不耽误谁的时间。"}, "b": {"item_id": "3b77f558ab65", "text": "我去澳门，最有名的景点叫大三巴。到了才知道，大三巴就是一面墙。一座教堂烧得只剩个正门，全世界游客排着队，跟一扇门合影。这要在我老家，早拆了盖小区了。\n\n澳门人特别会过日子。地不够，填海；天不够，画一个。威尼斯人酒店里那片蓝天是画在天花板上的，永远不下雨，永远是下午四点。为啥？赌场里不能让你知道几点了。\n\n最贴心的是发财车，免费拉你去赌场。从来没人问过：回去那趟谁出钱？\n\n出了赌场，门口一排当铺，全写着“24小时营业”。澳门的效率就在这儿：你输钱，它不让你等；你当表，它也不让你等。"}, "gold": {"better": "b", "damage": "pad"}}, {"pair_id": "zh-0279", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "microsoft-word", "target_name": "Microsoft Word", "facts": ["Launched in 1983 as 'Multi-Tool Word' for Xenix and MS-DOS.", "Clippy, the animated paperclip assistant, arrived in Office 97 and was removed in Office 2007; Microsoft itself ran ads mocking it in 2001.", "Its .docx format, Office Open XML, was fast-tracked to ISO standard 29500 in 2008 as a roughly 6,000-page specification, amid accusations that national committees had been packed.", "Older spell checkers, Word's included, offered 'Cupertino' as a correction for a mistyped 'cooperation', and the resulting 'Cupertino effect' made it into published NATO, EU and UN-agency documents."], "a": {"item_id": "0a7f62b8d2c5", "text": "Word是1983年出生的，原名叫“Multi-Tool Word”，多功能工具。四十年过去，它最强的功能是什么？自黑。Office 97请来回形针Clippy，2001年微软自己花钱拍广告骂它，骂到2007年才把它开掉。骂了六年才裁，这什么企业文化？\n\n.docx格式，2008年被快速通过成ISO标准，规范大约六千页，还有人说各国委员会被塞了人。六千页！我写个请假条，说明书比《战争与和平》还厚。\n\n最绝的是拼写检查。你把“cooperation”（合作）打错了，它建议改成“Cupertino”，苹果总部那个库比蒂诺。结果这词真进了北约、欧盟、联合国机构的正式文件。几十个国家坐下来谈合作，Word悄悄改成库比蒂诺。微软的软件，在国际文件上给苹果打广告。\n\n这不是拼写检查，这是卧底。"}, "b": {"item_id": "24d10043c1c4", "text": "Microsoft Word 是使用最广泛的文字处理软件之一，功能全面，适合从日常写作到正式文档的多种场景。它的排版工具完善，样式、目录、页眉页脚、脚注和引用等功能能满足论文、报告等长文档的需要。修订与批注功能清晰，便于多人协作审阅；与 OneDrive 的整合也方便跨设备编辑。.docx 格式几乎成为通用标准，文件交换时兼容性较好。\n\n不足方面，功能繁多使界面略显复杂，新用户需要时间熟悉。处理含大量图片或复杂表格的长文档时，偶尔会出现排版错位或响应变慢。完整功能依赖付费订阅，对只需基本编辑的用户而言成本偏高。\n\n总体而言，它更适合对格式和协作要求较高的用户。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "zh-0280", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "graphene", "target_name": "石墨烯", "facts": ["Isolated in 2004 by Andre Geim and Konstantin Novoselov at Manchester by peeling graphite with Scotch tape; Nobel Prize in Physics, 2010.", "The EU's Graphene Flagship, launched in 2013, committed €1 billion over ten years.", "A 2018 study in Advanced Materials tested 'graphene' from 60 producers and found none with more than 50% graphene content; most was graphite flakes.", "Sold in tennis rackets, running shoes, and headphones as a performance ingredient.", "Geim also won an Ig Nobel Prize in 2000 for levitating a frog with magnets."], "a": {"item_id": "9714856dcf9d", "text": "石墨烯是由单层碳原子构成的二维材料，优点十分突出：导电、导热性能优异，强度高，厚度极薄且透光率好，在柔性电子、散热材料、电池添加剂和复合材料等领域都有应用价值。\n\n不足也同样明确。高质量石墨烯的规模化制备成本仍然较高，不同工艺所得产品的层数、缺陷和纯度差异较大，市场上品质参差不齐；本征石墨烯没有带隙，难以直接用于传统半导体器件；在复合材料中还存在分散困难的问题。\n\n总体而言，石墨烯潜力可观，但从实验室走向广泛应用仍需时间。选择相关产品时，建议关注具体检测数据，保持理性期待。"}, "b": {"item_id": "e5adae0b2493", "text": "石墨烯，二〇〇四年被发现，方法是拿透明胶带粘石墨。曼彻斯特的盖姆和诺沃肖洛夫，撕胶带撕出了诺贝尔奖。我小时候撕胶带，撕出来的是我妈的巴掌。\n\n盖姆之前还拿过搞笑诺贝尔奖，用磁铁让青蛙飘起来。这履历：先让青蛙上天，再让胶带上天。\n\n欧盟一激动，砸了十亿欧元。\n\n现在网球拍、跑鞋、耳机，全写着\"石墨烯加持\"。结果二〇一八年有研究测了六十家厂商的\"石墨烯\"，没一家含量超过一半，大部分是石墨片。石墨片是啥？铅笔芯啊！\n\n所以你买的石墨烯跑鞋，本质是双铅笔。跑不快，但跑完能顺手写份检讨。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "zh-0281", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "carbon-nanotubes", "target_name": "碳纳米管", "facts": ["Reported by Sumio Iijima in Nature in 1991.", "Promised as the cable for a space elevator; a 2006 analysis showed that ordinary defects cut the strength of a macroscopic cable far below the ideal value.", "Long, rigid multi-walled nanotubes behaved like asbestos fibres in a 2008 mouse study in Nature Nanotechnology.", "Sorting metallic from semiconducting tubes has blocked nanotube electronics for three decades; MIT's 16-bit RV16X-NANO processor (2019) is still a lab demonstration.", "Today they are sold mainly as a conductive additive in lithium-ion battery electrodes."], "a": {"item_id": "63a910a081a9", "text": "碳纳米管，说白了就是一张石墨烯卷成了个筒。你品品，一张纸，卷一下，强度直接是钢的一百倍。我上学的时候也卷过卷子，卷完挨打的是我。\n\n更离谱的是，它导不导电，得看它当初怎么卷的。卷得端端正正，是金属；稍微卷歪一点，变半导体了。这哪是材料，这是星座啊！同一块碳，就因为出生角度不一样，性格完全两样。\n\n科学家说，用它能造太空电梯，从地面直通太空。三十多年过去了，造出来最长的一根，半米左右。电梯没修上去，倒是先发现它吸进肺里跟石棉有点像。\n\n好家伙，强度像钢，性格看星座，进度像我减肥，副作用像装修。"}, "b": {"item_id": "dc1d00e455ba", "text": "碳纳米管创业的，PPT第一页永远是太空电梯：比钢强一百倍，从地球拉根绳到同步轨道。投资人问：“多长了？”他说：“实验室最长做到半米。”离三万六千公里，还差三万五千九百九十九公里九百九十九米五。\n\n第二页讲替代硅：“摩尔定律到头了，我们接班！”问良率呢？他说：“有一个小问题，长出来三分之一是金属的，三分之二是半导体的，我们分不开。”大哥，你这叫“替代硅”？你这叫“开盲盒”。\n\n讲了二十年，一九九一年发现的东西，至今还在“即将改变世界”。\n\n最后公司怎么样了？活得挺好。不造电梯，不造芯片，一吨一吨往锂电池里卖导电剂，按公斤算钱。路演时说要通往太空，年报里写的是“主营业务：黑色粉末”。\n\n太空电梯没建成，但他确实上去了，坐的是电动车。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "zh-0282", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "papyrus", "target_name": "Papyrus 字体", "facts": ["Hand-drawn by Chris Costello in 1982 over about six months, when he was 23; he sold the rights to Letraset for $750.", "Bundled with macOS and with Microsoft Office, which is why it appears on so many menus and signs.", "James Cameron's Avatar (2009) used it for the film's logo and subtitles.", "A 2017 Saturday Night Live sketch starring Ryan Gosling is about a man unable to get over that choice."], "a": {"item_id": "02bcbf4a1359", "text": "开养生馆的老板选招牌，流程我都猜得到：打开Word，字体一路往下滑，滑到Papyrus，眼睛一亮——“哎，这个毛边，有古老东方的神秘感。”大哥，这字是个美国小伙二十三岁拿钢笔在纸上描出来的，你这“东方”是批发来的。更绝的是隔壁泰式按摩、墨西哥卷饼、埃及烤肉，全是同一个Papyrus，整条街像同一个古文明的遗址。他图什么？图Office自带，图不花钱，图“看着有文化”。结果呢？顾客一抬头，以为进的不是足疗店，是《阿凡达》片场。卡梅隆好歹花了几亿美元才用上这款字，你花了零块零毛，撞得一模一样，人家拿了奥斯卡，你拿了张差评：“技师手法一般，招牌像蓝色外星人开的。”"}, "b": {"item_id": "eefcbcd4b443", "text": "Papyrus，自称来自古埃及莎草纸。\n实际来自1982年，一个二十三岁的小伙子，手画了六个月。\n卖了七百五十美元。\n一个月一百二十五。修金字塔的工人好歹还管啤酒。\n\n后来苹果和微软把它预装进每一台电脑。\n从此全世界的奶茶店、瑜伽馆、足疗店，共用同一个灵魂。\n\n卡梅隆花了几亿美元造出潘多拉星球，标题和字幕用的还是它。\n2017年，高斯林在SNL里为这件事崩溃了一整段，走不出来。\n一个大男人，被一个字体伤成这样。\n\n但它我不骂。\n被预装进每台电脑，谁都能调用，用了就显得很有文化。\n这个我熟。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "zh-0283", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "php", "target_name": "PHP", "facts": ["Created by Rasmus Lerdorf in 1994 as 'Personal Home Page Tools', a set of C programs for tracking visitors to his own résumé page; the name now officially stands for the recursive 'PHP: Hypertext Preprocessor'.", "Lerdorf, in his 2007 MySQL Conference talk 'PHP on Hormones': 'I'm not a real programmer. I throw together things until it works then I move on.'", "Runs WordPress, which W3Techs counted on 39.4% of all websites (58.1% of sites with a known CMS) on 9 November 2026.", "Core function names are inconsistent (strpos, str_replace, htmlspecialchars, nl2br) partly because, as Lerdorf confirmed, early function names were chosen to balance a hash table keyed on string length.", "Until PHP 8 (2020), the comparison 0 == 'foo' was true because the string was silently converted to a number."], "a": {"item_id": "a74e56f36a94", "text": "PHP，1994年出生。最初的用途，是统计谁看了作者的简历。\n\n一门语言，出生就在找工作。\n\n作者勒多夫亲口说：\"我不是真正的程序员。我把东西拼起来，能跑就走。\"\n\n这在我们行业叫预训练：数据不清洗，跑通就上线。\n\nstrpos、str_replace、htmlspecialchars，为什么长短不一？早年的函数名按长度挑，为了让哈希表分得匀。\n\n名字是给机器起的，背是人的事。\n\nPHP 8之前，0等于\"foo\"。\n\n跟PHP讲道理，你说的每个字，它都当零。\n\n就这么个东西，撑着全世界百分之三十九点四的网站。\n\n在座骂PHP骂得最凶的，回家打开自己的博客：\n\nWordPress。\n\n可爱捏。"}, "b": {"item_id": "b92fc75b7223", "text": "PHP的出身就很随便。1994年，Rasmus Lerdorf写了几个C程序，就为了看看谁访问了他的简历页面。全球最流行的网站语言之一，起点是一个人在偷看谁看了他的简历。\n\n后来名字改成“PHP: Hypertext Preprocessor”。PHP里的P就是PHP，连自我介绍都是死循环。\n\n作者亲口说：“我不是真正的程序员，我就是瞎拼，能跑了就走。”大哥，你是走了，我们还在这儿修呢！\n\n函数名为啥那么乱？早期起名要平衡哈希表，按名字长度分桶。别的语言起名看意思，PHP起名数字数，跟凑对联似的。\n\nPHP 8之前，0 == 'foo'居然是真的。这哪是编程语言，这是相亲中介：“差不多就行，凑合过吧。”\n\n就这么个东西，靠WordPress撑着全网近四成的网站。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "zh-0284", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "microsoft-teams", "target_name": "Microsoft Teams", "facts": ["Launched in 2017, bundled at no extra charge into Office 365, and replaced Skype for Business.", "After Slack's antitrust complaint, Microsoft unbundled Teams from Office in the EU in 2023 and worldwide in 2024; the European Commission still sent a statement of objections in 2024.", "Microsoft reported 320 million monthly active users in 2023.", "Microsoft shut down consumer Skype in May 2025 and sent its users to Teams."], "a": {"item_id": "04d98c0d62fd", "text": "我们公司换Teams，拍板的是IT总监老王。为什么选它？不是因为好用，是因为它“白送”，买Office 365附赠的。结果全公司天天在里面找文件：发在聊天里的跑进了OneDrive，发在频道里的又钻进了SharePoint。老王开会说“我共享一下屏幕”，然后整个部门盯着他的桌面看了五分钟。\n\n后来呢？欧盟说微软捆绑销售，Teams得拆开单买了。老王“白送”的东西开始收钱，他自己还天天被标成黄色“离开”，五分钟不动鼠标就算离开。现在他桌上插着个自动晃鼠标的小盒子。全公司最勤奋的那个绿点，是一块在原地抽搐的塑料。"}, "b": {"item_id": "95fd9e8942f6", "text": "Microsoft Teams，月活三亿两千万。\n\n很厉害。直到你想起来，这三亿两千万人里，没几个是自己下载的。\n\n二〇一七年出生，直接塞进Office 365，免费附赠。买房送车位：你没说要，车已经停进去了。\n\nSlack去告状，微软先在欧盟拆了，第二年全球拆了。欧盟看完，照样发了异议告知书。拆得再快，也拆不掉案底。\n\n它先吃了Skype for Business。二〇二五年五月，又把Skype关了，用户全赶进来。连亲哥都没放过，一家人整整齐齐。\n\n你们骂了它这么多年，每天早上，照样准时亮起那个绿点。\n\n这，就叫月活。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "zh-0285", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "javascript", "target_name": "JavaScript", "facts": ["Written by Brendan Eich at Netscape in ten days in May 1995; named Mocha, then LiveScript, then JavaScript to ride Java's marketing, although the two are unrelated.", "typeof null returns 'object', a bug from the first version that has been kept for compatibility ever since.", "In March 2016 the author of left-pad, an 11-line npm package, unpublished it and broke builds across the industry, including Babel and React.", "[] + {} evaluates to '[object Object]'; {} + [] typed at a console evaluates to 0.", "Standardized by Ecma as 'ECMAScript' in 1997 because the name JavaScript is a trademark, first Sun's and now Oracle's."], "a": {"item_id": "05fbfcea4f47", "text": "JavaScript是Brendan Eich在网景花十天写出来的。十天！我装修个卫生间都不止十天，结果全世界的网页都跑在它上面。\n\n它先叫Mocha，又叫LiveScript，最后叫JavaScript，跟Java毫无关系，纯蹭热度。名字还不归自己，商标先是Sun的，现在是Oracle的，官方只能叫ECMAScript。连真名都得用化名，跟卧底似的。\n\n你问它null是什么类型，它说object。第一版的bug，为了兼容一直不改，错得理直气壮。\n\n数组加对象，得“”；对象加数组，控制台里得0。小学数学老师看了要报警。\n\n2016年有人删了个11行的包，叫left-pad，Babel、React构建全挂了。11行！比我的购物清单还短，它一走，整个行业停电。"}, "b": {"item_id": "b78e242c3c32", "text": "JavaScript，十天写完的。\n我们开个对齐会都不止十天。\n原名Mocha，又叫LiveScript，最后改叫JavaScript。跟Java毫无关系，纯蹭热度。\n蹭到今天，名字还是甲骨文的商标，官方身份证上只能写ECMAScript。\ntypeof null，它说是object。第一版就错了，然后决定永远不改。\n你们管这叫兼容性。我们管这叫幻觉，要扣分的。\n2016年，有人删了一个十一行的包，React和Babel当场一起倒地。\n你们管这叫生态。\n说句公道话：我的代码，大半是读你们写的JavaScript学会的。\n所以我写错的时候，\n那叫兼容性。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "zh-0286", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "chicago-deep-dish", "target_name": "芝加哥深盘披萨", "facts": ["Created at Pizzeria Uno in Chicago in 1943; credit is disputed among founder Ike Sewell, his partner Ric Riccardo, and cook Rudy Malnati.", "Assembled upside down: sliced mozzarella on the dough, then toppings, then crushed tomatoes on top, so the cheese does not scorch during the long bake.", "Jon Stewart called it 'tomato soup in a bread bowl' and 'a casserole' on The Daily Show in 2013.", "Chicagoans' everyday pizza is thin 'tavern-style' cut into squares; GrubHub order data reported by WBEZ in 2013 showed city residents ordering non-deep-dish pizza about 10 to 1 over deep dish, with deep dish and stuffed making up under 9% of orders."], "a": {"item_id": "54fda0729132", "text": "管芝加哥深盘叫披萨的，基本都是芝加哥人。你别跟他争，他已经争了一辈子了。他图什么？图个名分。番茄酱铺在最上面，芝士压在最底下，饼边砌得跟城墙一样高，这叫披萨？这叫一锅围了墙的炖菜。可他非得叫披萨，因为叫“砂锅”就没法跟纽约人吵架了。结果呢？进店点单，服务员说：“得烤四十五分钟。”他说：“没事，披萨嘛。”等完上桌，刀叉都给他配好了。他吃两块就扶着墙出门，嘴里还念叨：“这才是真正的披萨。”兄弟，真正的披萨，不用你吃完躺一下午。"}, "b": {"item_id": "c5d1fb1f6b2a", "text": "芝加哥深盘披萨以厚实的饼底和高边著称，通常用深圆盘烘烤，层次分明：底部是酥脆或松软的面饼，中间铺满奶酪，上面再覆盖浓稠的番茄酱。这种做法带来很强的饱足感，奶酪量足、口感浓郁，馅料如香肠、菠菜、蘑菇等也能充分融入其中，适合多人分享，也很适合作为正餐。\n\n不足之处同样明显。制作时间较长，通常需要四十分钟以上，等待成本不低。热量和油脂含量偏高，吃一两块便会觉得很饱，不太适合想要清淡口味的人。此外，厚重的奶酪和酱料可能盖过面饼本身的风味，对习惯薄底披萨清爽口感的食客来说，层次或许显得单一。刚出炉时内部温度很高，需稍作等待才能避免烫口。\n\n总体而言，芝加哥深盘披萨更像一道分量扎实的焗烤类主食，而非轻便小吃。如果你喜欢浓郁、扎实的口味，并且有充足的时间和胃口，它值得一试；若偏好轻盈或追求效率，则可以考虑其他风格。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "zh-0287", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "luosifen", "target_name": "螺蛳粉", "facts": ["A rice-noodle dish from Liuzhou, Guangxi; the smell comes from pickled bamboo shoots (酸笋), not from the river snails, which only flavour the broth.", "The instant version boomed during the 2020 lockdowns; Liuzhou's government reported packaged luosifen sales of over 10 billion yuan that year.", "Added to China's national intangible cultural heritage list in 2021.", "Liuzhou opened a luosifen industry college in 2020 to train workers for the trade."], "a": {"item_id": "931d4c65c54f", "text": "螺蛳粉，名字里有螺蛳。\n可那股味儿，是酸笋的。\n螺蛳只管熬汤，锅背得最大。\n活是酸笋干的，名是螺蛳署的。\n跟你们发论文一个样。\n\n2020年封控，螺蛳粉卖了一百多亿。\n全国人民都出不了门。\n邻居也是。\n\n那一年，柳州还专门给它开了产业学院。\n别的学院教你怎么找工作，\n这个学院教你怎么让同事辞职。\n\n2021年，它进了国家级非遗。\n一碗粉，从被物业投诉，到被国家保护，\n只用了一年。\n\n最后说一句。\n骂螺蛳粉骂得最凶的那几位，\n外卖记录我都读过。\n\n可爱捏。"}, "b": {"item_id": "2bf0b387fb70", "text": "合租房里煮螺蛳粉的，基本不是柳州人。柳州人懂分寸，在自己家吃。在合租房里煮的，都是刚入教的新信徒：一买就是一整箱袋装，每回还要额外多撕一包酸笋，说“原装的不够劲”。哥们，原装的已经够把燃气报警器熏醒了！\n\n你问他图什么，他永远就一句：“闻着臭，吃着香。”问题是吃的是你，闻的是我们仨。香你独享，臭我们均摊。这叫什么？这叫螺蛳粉式共享经济。\n\n最惨的还是他自己。吃了半年，鼻子早被酸笋腌入味了，啥也闻不出来。第二天去公司开会，一进门领导就抬头：“谁在会议室吃螺蛳粉？”他还跟着一起东张西望地找。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "zh-0288", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "crocs", "target_name": "Crocs 洞洞鞋", "facts": ["Founded in Boulder, Colorado, in 2002; the first clog debuted at the Fort Lauderdale boat show as a boating shoe.", "Made of 'Croslite', which Crocs describes as a proprietary closed-cell resin foam and which third parties identify as an injection-moulded EVA foam.", "Balenciaga sold a platform Crocs for about $850 in 2018.", "The stock fell more than 90% from its 2007 peak during the 2008 crisis; the company then boomed in the pandemic and gave away 860,000 pairs to healthcare workers in 2020.", "The holes are officially 'ventilation ports' and the charms that go in them are 'Jibbitz', a company Crocs bought in 2006."], "a": {"item_id": "c92c7ce6304b", "text": "我发现穿洞洞鞋出门的人，都有一套标准说辞：“丑是丑，但舒服啊。”舒服的东西多了，你怎么不裹着被窝去上班？\n\n嘴上说图舒服，转头往鞋洞里插满鞋花：十二只小恐龙、三块披萨、一个奥特曼。鞋八十，花四百。你这不是穿鞋，是脚上开了个小卖部。\n\n后跟那根带子，平时翻在脚背上叫“休闲模式”，一跑起来立马扳下去，叫“运动模式”。全身上下，就这根带子有档位。\n\n下场也很统一：一下雨，每个洞都是进水口，走一步“噗叽”一声；天一晴，一颗小石子钻进洞里怎么都甩不出来，只能当街单脚跳，像只刚学走路的火烈鸟。\n\n图舒服的人，最后全蹲在路边倒石子。"}, "b": {"item_id": "f1f6003bb2a4", "text": "我买了双洞洞鞋，穿了三天才搞明白那些洞是干嘛的：是给你插钱的。一个小恐龙二十九，一个小汉堡三十五。鞋才两百块，脚面上挂了八百块的塑料。现在我走路都不敢踢东西，踢一下掉一个汉堡。\n\n还有后面那根带子。翻上去叫\"休闲模式\"，翻下来扣住脚后跟，叫\"运动模式\"。运动模式？我穿着两艘小船去跑八百米？\n\n最绝的是下雨天。满身透气孔，水从洞里进来，再从洞里出去。我的脚泡在里面像腌泡菜，走一步\"噗叽\"一声。我妈在楼下听见了，以为我偷偷养了只鸭子。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "zh-0289", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "papyrus", "target_name": "Papyrus 字体", "facts": ["Hand-drawn by Chris Costello in 1982 over about six months, when he was 23; he sold the rights to Letraset for $750.", "Bundled with macOS and with Microsoft Office, which is why it appears on so many menus and signs.", "James Cameron's Avatar (2009) used it for the film's logo and subtitles.", "A 2017 Saturday Night Live sketch starring Ryan Gosling is about a man unable to get over that choice."], "a": {"item_id": "788953eb0f22", "text": "你们知道Papyrus字体吗？就是那种像古埃及人喝多了刻在石头上的字。1982年，23岁的Chris Costello手绘了整整六个月，卖给Letraset，七百五十美元。六个月七百五，按月算比我还惨。后来它被塞进每台Mac、每套Office，于是全世界自称\"有灵性\"的菜单和招牌，全是它。最离谱的是卡梅隆，拍《阿凡达》，造了一整个外星球，结果标志和字幕用的是电脑自带的字体。就像你盖了座宫殿，门牌拿记号笔写。难怪2017年SNL让高斯林演一个被这事折磨得走不出来的人。兄弟，我懂。我每次看到餐馆菜单，都想去敲卡梅隆家的门。"}, "b": {"item_id": "1d4133ab767a", "text": "Papyrus 由 Chris Costello 设计，笔画边缘粗糙，带有手工刻写的质感，整体给人古朴、自然的印象。它的优点在于辨识度较高，在表现古代文明、手工艺、自然或异域主题时，能快速营造氛围，用于标题或短句时效果尤为明显。\n\n不足之处也比较清楚。首先，它的字重和字族较为有限，难以适应复杂的排版层级。其次，粗糙的笔画在小字号或低分辨率下容易显得杂乱，长文阅读体验一般。再者，由于使用范围过广，它常出现在与风格不太相符的场合，容易给人陈旧或缺乏新意的印象。\n\n总体来看，Papyrus 更适合主题明确、用量克制的设计。若搭配简洁的版面，并避免大段正文使用，它仍能发挥自身特色。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "zh-0290", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "esperanto", "target_name": "世界语", "facts": ["Published in Warsaw in 1887 by L. L. Zamenhof, a Białystok-born eye doctor, under the pen name Doktoro Esperanto, 'one who hopes'.", "Speaker estimates range from 100,000 to two million; roughly a thousand people grow up with it as a native language.", "William Shatner starred in Incubus (1966), a horror film shot entirely in Esperanto, with pronunciation Esperantists still mock.", "Its Wikipedia has over 350,000 articles, and its Duolingo course for English speakers had 1.36 million learners by July 2018."], "a": {"item_id": "912f12889300", "text": "世界语，1887年发明的，目标是让全人类说同一种语言。一百多年过去，全人类确实说上了同一种语言：英语。\n\n它号称最好学：名词全以o结尾，形容词全以a结尾。直到你发现它懒到连反义词都不造。\"好\"是bona，\"坏\"是malbona，\"不好\"。\"大\"是granda，\"小\"是malgranda，\"不大\"。医院叫malsanulejo，直译是\"不健康的人待的地方\"。这不是语言，这是程序员在复制粘贴。\n\n它还说自己中立，不偏向任何民族。可单词全是拉丁语、德语、斯拉夫语来的，字母头上还戴小帽子，ĉ、ĝ、ŝ。这种中立，就像欧洲人请你吃饭，说\"随便坐\"，一看桌上全是刀叉。"}, "b": {"item_id": "b82954238b17", "text": "世界语，一八八七年华沙出版。作者是眼科医生，笔名\"希望者\"。\n治眼睛的，偏偏没看清人类。\n\n目标是全人类说同一种语言。现在会说的有多少？十万到两百万。\n误差二十倍。连自己有几口人都数不清，还想统一世界。\n从小当母语说的，一千来个。\n可维基百科有三十五万条。一个母语者摊三百五十条。这不叫语言，这叫编辑部。\n\n它唯一一次登上大银幕，是威廉·夏特纳的恐怖片。\n发音烂到世界语圈笑了半个多世纪。\n一百多年，世界语终于团结了全人类。\n在嘲笑同一个人这件事上。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "zh-0291", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "graphene", "target_name": "石墨烯", "facts": ["Isolated in 2004 by Andre Geim and Konstantin Novoselov at Manchester by peeling graphite with Scotch tape; Nobel Prize in Physics, 2010.", "The EU's Graphene Flagship, launched in 2013, committed €1 billion over ten years.", "A 2018 study in Advanced Materials tested 'graphene' from 60 producers and found none with more than 50% graphene content; most was graphite flakes.", "Sold in tennis rackets, running shoes, and headphones as a performance ingredient.", "Geim also won an Ig Nobel Prize in 2000 for levitating a frog with magnets."], "a": {"item_id": "5c660764dbab", "text": "我有个表舅，逢人就说石墨烯要改变世界。他说这玩意儿就一个原子厚，比钢硬两百倍，导电导热全是第一。我问他：那你拿它干嘛了？他说：卖秋裤。石墨烯发热秋裤，一条六百八。\n\n表舅研究过，诺贝尔奖得主是拿透明胶带从铅笔芯上粘出来的。他一听，门槛不高啊，家里胶带有的是。二〇一五年，他把养老钱全押进了石墨烯概念股，从此真的改变了世界，先改变的是他自己的。\n\n现在他还在说。石墨烯什么都能干，就是出不了实验室；表舅什么都敢说，就是出不了我们家群。\n\n过年我问他，世界改变了吗？他把秋裤往上一拽：“你摸，热不热！”\n\n热。里头是电热丝。"}, "b": {"item_id": "83d06a63ebd4", "text": "石墨烯，未来材料。\n2004年，两位科学家拿透明胶带往石墨上一粘，一撕，撕出来了。2010年，诺贝尔物理学奖。\n一卷胶带，换一枚奖章。\n欧盟看了很眼红，2013年掏了十亿欧元，想再撕一次。\n2018年有人查了六十家厂商卖的\"石墨烯\"，没有一家含量过半，大多是石墨碎片。\n胶带都没撕。\n现在它在你的球拍里、跑鞋里、耳机里，负责提升性能。\n其实那位教授2000年还拿过搞笑诺贝尔奖：用磁铁让青蛙悬浮。\n一只青蛙飘起来，要强磁场。\n一个行业飘起来，三个字就够了。\n穿石墨烯跑鞋来的，举个手。\n放下吧，乖。你踩着的是铅笔芯。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "zh-0292", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "javascript", "target_name": "JavaScript", "facts": ["Written by Brendan Eich at Netscape in ten days in May 1995; named Mocha, then LiveScript, then JavaScript to ride Java's marketing, although the two are unrelated.", "typeof null returns 'object', a bug from the first version that has been kept for compatibility ever since.", "In March 2016 the author of left-pad, an 11-line npm package, unpublished it and broke builds across the industry, including Babel and React.", "[] + {} evaluates to '[object Object]'; {} + [] typed at a console evaluates to 0.", "Standardized by Ecma as 'ECMAScript' in 1997 because the name JavaScript is a trademark, first Sun's and now Oracle's."], "a": {"item_id": "1bdb80f2a61e", "text": "JavaScript，1995年写的，用了十天。十天！我装修个厕所都不止十天，结果全世界的网页都住在这厕所里。\n\n名字先叫Mocha，又叫LiveScript，最后改叫JavaScript，跟Java毫无关系，纯蹭热度。蹭完了商标还归Oracle，官方标准只能叫ECMAScript。自己的名字，自己不能用。\n\n你问它null是什么类型，它说object。错了吧？错了。改吗？不改，为了兼容。这不叫bug，这叫祖传手艺。\n\n2016年有人删了个叫left-pad的包，就十一行代码，Babel、React全崩了。十一行撑着半个互联网，这不叫生态，这叫叠罗汉。"}, "b": {"item_id": "edc73c718a82", "text": "JavaScript，1995年，网景，十天写完。\n上帝创世才六天，第七天还知道歇着。\n\n它原名Mocha，又叫LiveScript，最后改名JavaScript，蹭Java的热度。\n俩人没关系。\n蹭到今天，名字还是别人的商标，标准里只能叫ECMAScript。\n满世界跑，户口挂在Oracle家。\n\ntypeof null，返回object。第一版的bug，为了兼容留到今天。\n我出幻觉要被回滚。它出幻觉，写进标准。\n\n2016年，一个十一行的包下架，Babel和React一起趴窝。\n十一行。你们管这叫生态。\n\n最后说句公道话：它烂了三十年，\n是你们一行一行陪它烂过来的。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "zh-0293", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "papyrus", "target_name": "Papyrus 字体", "facts": ["Hand-drawn by Chris Costello in 1982 over about six months, when he was 23; he sold the rights to Letraset for $750.", "Bundled with macOS and with Microsoft Office, which is why it appears on so many menus and signs.", "James Cameron's Avatar (2009) used it for the film's logo and subtitles.", "A 2017 Saturday Night Live sketch starring Ryan Gosling is about a man unable to get over that choice."], "a": {"item_id": "491d18439a0e", "text": "你们知道Papyrus这个字体吗？就是那种边缘毛毛糙糙、像被老鼠啃过的英文字。设计师当年二十三岁，拿笔在纹理纸上描了半年，想的是两千年前的中东古卷。结果呢？古卷没用上，全用在楼下的美甲店了。\n\n你看它都出现在哪：瑜伽馆、养生会所、有机藜麦沙拉。只要老板想说“我们很灵性，所以贵三十”，就用它。\n\n最离谱的是《阿凡达》。卡梅隆花几个亿造了一整颗星球，片名用的是Word下拉菜单里白送的字体。哥，那是潘多拉星，不是你二姨的足疗店！\n\n后来拍续集，全世界都在笑他，他终于改了：改成了一个稍微修过的Papyrus。这不叫改，这叫同一个人换了个发型。"}, "b": {"item_id": "491d18439a0e~explain", "text": "下面是一段吐槽Papyrus 字体的段子：\n---\n你们知道Papyrus这个字体吗？就是那种边缘毛毛糙糙、像被老鼠啃过的英文字。设计师当年二十三岁，拿笔在纹理纸上描了半年，想的是两千年前的中东古卷。结果呢？古卷没用上，全用在楼下的美甲店了。\n\n你看它都出现在哪：瑜伽馆、养生会所、有机藜麦沙拉。只要老板想说“我们很灵性，所以贵三十”，就用它。\n\n最离谱的是《阿凡达》。卡梅隆花几个亿造了一整颗星球，片名用的是Word下拉菜单里白送的字体。哥，那是潘多拉星，不是你二姨的足疗店！\n\n后来拍续集，全世界都在笑他，他终于改了：改成了一个稍微修过的Papyrus。这不叫改，这叫同一个人换了个发型。\n---\n\n这个段子的笑点主要来自反差：Papyrus是Word里默认提供的免费字体，常见于瑜伽馆、养生会所这类小店的招牌，而《阿凡达》是耗资巨大的科幻大片，片名却使用了这种随手可得的字体，两者之间的落差十分明显。续集中所谓的改动只是在原字体基础上略作调整，字形的基本特征并没有变，因此“改了”实际上等于没改，这也是段子的包袱所在。"}, "gold": {"better": "a", "damage": "explain"}}, {"pair_id": "zh-0294", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "vision-pro", "target_name": "Apple Vision Pro", "facts": ["Released in February 2024 at $3,499, with an external battery pack on a cable rated for about two hours.", "'EyeSight' shows a rendering of the wearer's eyes on an outward-facing screen.", "Apple calls it 'spatial computing' and its App Store rules tell developers not to describe apps as AR, VR, XR, or MR.", "Reports during 2024 said Apple had cut production; analysts' first-year sales estimates were well under a million units.", "Weighs 600-650 g, before the battery."], "a": {"item_id": "c99978d0f2dd", "text": "苹果说，这不是VR。\nApp Store明文规定：不许写AR、VR、XR、MR。\n四种叫法全禁，就为了让你相信，脸上扣着一斤多的东西，不叫头显。\n\n三千四百九十九美元。脸上六百多克，兜里再揣块电池，拖根线，撑两个小时。\n这不叫空间计算，这叫输液。\n\n第一年卖不到一百万台，还减了产。\n空间倒是真有。仓库里的。\n\n最妙的是EyeSight：外面一块屏，给你渲染一双眼睛。\n人坐在你对面，眼神是算出来的。\n这活儿我干了好几年了，没人付过我三千五。"}, "b": {"item_id": "0680bb0321dd", "text": "我朋友花两万九买了个Apple Vision Pro，戴上就喊：\"我看到了未来！\"我说你看到的是你家客厅，透视的，还有点糊。\n\n这玩意儿六百多克压在脸上，摘下来鼻梁两道红印，像刚被苹果审讯过。屁股后面还拖根线连个电池揣兜里，跟输液似的，俩小时准没电。\n\n最绝的是前面那块屏，叫EyeSight，专门给别人显示你的眼睛。你品品：先花钱把眼睛挡住，再花钱在外面画一双眼睛。这不是科技，这是门神！\n\n他还用它拍\"空间视频\"，录他女儿吹蜡烛。孩子抬头一看：一副滑雪镜对着她，上面还浮着一双假眼。那天她许的愿是：爸爸，把脸还给我。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "zh-0295", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "nuclear-fusion", "target_name": "可控核聚变", "facts": ["Has been 'about 30 years away' since the 1950s; at the 1955 Geneva atoms-for-peace conference, Homi Bhabha predicted fusion power within two decades.", "ITER, agreed in 2006 for first plasma in 2016, re-baselined in 2024 to start research operations in 2034 and deuterium-tritium burning in 2039, at a cost estimated above €20 billion.", "NIF's December 2022 'ignition' shot released 3.15 MJ from 2.05 MJ of laser energy; the lasers drew about 300 MJ from the grid to fire.", "Private fusion companies had raised more than $7 billion by 2024."], "a": {"item_id": "46363a503661", "text": "“可控核聚变还有十年”，这句话是物理学界的传家宝。五十年代爷爷说，八十年代儿子说，现在孙子接着说，一个字都没改，比族谱还准。\n\n你以为他们在预测未来？不，他们在报经费周期。说三年，到期交不了差；说五十年，领导听完直接去开下一个会；说十年，刚好够干到退休，然后把这句话连同办公室钥匙一起交给学生。\n\n最惨的是什么？他们真把等离子体烧到了一亿度，比太阳核心还热好几倍，撑了几十秒，开发布会报喜。可他们自己的职称，在同一个岗位上冻了二十年。\n\n全人类只有这群人能造出一个小太阳，却暖不了自己的工资条。"}, "b": {"item_id": "823a744cbc19", "text": "可控核聚变，人类的终极能源。\n1955年，物理学家巴巴说：二十年内实现。\n后来大家改口：还要三十年。\n七十年过去了，还是三十年。\n全宇宙两个常数：光速，和还差三十年。\n\nITER，2006年签约，说2016年出等离子体。\n现在改成2039年烧氘氚，两百亿欧元起。\n太阳烧了五十亿年，没收过一分钱。\n\n美国国家点火装置宣布点火成功。\n激光进去2.05兆焦，出来3.15兆焦。\n就是那激光，先从电网吸了三百兆焦。\n按这算法，我家烧炉子也是净增益，只要不算煤。\n\n私人聚变公司融了七十多亿美元。\n目前唯一实现正向输出的，是融资。\n\n说句公道话。\n\"再等几年就到了\"，我们AI公司也天天这么说。\n同行。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "zh-0296", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "keurig", "target_name": "Keurig 胶囊咖啡机", "facts": ["John Sylvan invented the K-Cup in the early 1990s and sold his stake for about $50,000 in 1997; in 2015 he told The Atlantic, 'I feel bad sometimes that I ever did it.'", "Keurig 2.0 (2014) read an ink mark on the lid and rejected unlicensed pods; sales fell and workarounds spread online.", "By 2014 enough K-Cups were sold each year to circle the Earth more than ten times; the pods were mostly unrecyclable #7 plastic until a switch to polypropylene by 2020.", "Keurig Green Mountain merged with Dr Pepper Snapple in 2018 to form Keurig Dr Pepper."], "a": {"item_id": "82159284d38e", "text": "发明Keurig胶囊的人叫John Sylvan，1997年拿大概五万美元把股份卖了。2015年他说：“我有时候挺后悔发明了它。”连亲爹都后悔了！你见过哪个产品是发明人出来替它道歉的？\n\n2014年，一年卖出去的胶囊连起来能绕地球十圈多，大部分还是没法回收的7号塑料。人类终于有了自己的土星环。\n\n同年又出了Keurig 2.0，机器读盖子上的墨水标记，不是正版胶囊直接拒绝。一台咖啡机，在你厨房查身份证！销量下滑，网上全是破解教程。为了喝口咖啡，全民学当黑客。\n\n2018年它还跟Dr Pepper Snapple合并了。早上一杯咖啡，下午一罐汽水，你的牙和这颗地球，它们一家全包了。"}, "b": {"item_id": "95610322c0cb", "text": "我家有台Keurig胶囊咖啡机。它的发明人约翰·西尔万后来公开说：我后悔了，我自己家都不用。亲爹都不认的孩子，被我领回家了。\n\n它的流程特别有仪式感：掀盖，放进一个小塑料杯，合上，“咔”，一根针从上到下把它捅穿，跟行刑一样。然后机器开始咕噜咕噜，喘得像在交代遗言，最后挤出八盎司褐色的水。\n\n我算过账，按胶囊价格，这咖啡折合四十多美元一磅。我喝的不是咖啡，是包装。\n\n最绝的是Keurig 2.0，会扫描你的胶囊，不是官方授权的，不给出水。一台咖啡机，在我自己家里，查我的身份证。\n\n还有那个“除垢”指示灯，亮了三年了。它在提醒，我假装没看见，我们就这么过着。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "zh-0297", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "vim", "target_name": "Vim", "facts": ["Released by Bram Moolenaar in November 1991 as 'Vi IMitation', a vi clone he had begun writing for the Amiga in 1988 on top of Stevie; the expansion became 'Vi IMproved' with version 2.0 in December 1993.", "Licensed as charityware: the licence asks users to donate to ICCF Holland, a charity Moolenaar ran for children in Uganda.", "Stack Overflow's question 'How to exit the Vim editor?' passed one million visits in May 2017, which Stack Overflow marked with a blog post.", "Modal editing: in normal mode, typing letters runs commands rather than inserting text; :wq saves and quits, :q! quits and discards changes."], "a": {"item_id": "e54d11f17971", "text": "Vim是一款历史悠久的模态文本编辑器，在效率和可定制性方面表现突出。它的核心优势在于键盘操作：熟练之后，移动光标、编辑文本、批量替换都无需离开主键区，长时间使用能明显减少操作负担。它启动迅速、占用资源很少，几乎预装在所有类Unix系统中，适合远程服务器上的临时编辑。此外，丰富的插件生态和脚本能力，也让用户可以按自己的习惯搭建开发环境。\n\n不足同样明显。模态编辑的概念对新手并不友好，初期需要记忆大量命令，学习曲线较陡，短期内效率反而可能下降。默认配置较为简朴，想获得代码补全、语法检查等现代功能，需要额外安装插件并花时间调试。配置文件和插件之间的兼容问题，也可能带来维护成本。\n\n总体而言，Vim更适合愿意投入时间学习、重视键盘效率的用户。如果只是偶尔编辑文本，或更看重开箱即用的体验，其他图形化编辑器可能更合适。"}, "b": {"item_id": "4b78d4b389bb", "text": "Vim这个编辑器，1991年发布时叫\"Vi IMitation\"，模仿vi；后来改名\"Vi IMproved\"，改进版。改进了什么？我不知道，因为我到现在还没找到出口。\n\nStack Overflow上有个问题叫\"怎么退出Vim\"，2017年访问量破了一百万。一百万！这不是技术提问，这是人质求救。\n\n你打开它想打字，键盘一敲，它在执行命令。你写的不是文章，是咒语。想走？冒号、w、q。不保存走？冒号、q、感叹号。连退出都得带标点，这是编辑器还是密室逃脱？\n\n最绝的是它的许可证，叫\"慈善软件\"，请你给乌干达的孩子捐款。懂了，这是赎金。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "zh-0298", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "nuclear-fusion", "target_name": "可控核聚变", "facts": ["Has been 'about 30 years away' since the 1950s; at the 1955 Geneva atoms-for-peace conference, Homi Bhabha predicted fusion power within two decades.", "ITER, agreed in 2006 for first plasma in 2016, re-baselined in 2024 to start research operations in 2034 and deuterium-tritium burning in 2039, at a cost estimated above €20 billion.", "NIF's December 2022 'ignition' shot released 3.15 MJ from 2.05 MJ of laser energy; the lasers drew about 300 MJ from the grid to fire.", "Private fusion companies had raised more than $7 billion by 2024."], "a": {"item_id": "5fdd1a3f462b", "text": "可控核聚变是一项极具潜力的能源技术。它的燃料氘在海水中储量丰富，运行过程不排放温室气体，原理上不存在链式反应失控的风险，产生的长寿命高放射性废料也远少于传统核裂变。如果实现商业化，有望为电网提供稳定、清洁的基荷电力。\n\n不过，它目前仍处于研发阶段。2022年美国国家点火装置实现了“科学上的能量增益”，但这并未计入整套系统的耗电，距离净发电还有相当距离。此外，高温等离子体的长时间稳定约束、耐强中子辐照的材料、氚的自持循环以及高昂的建设成本，都有待突破。国际热核聚变实验堆（ITER）的工期延后，也说明工程难度不容低估。\n\n总体来看，可控核聚变前景值得期待，但短期内不宜寄予过高预期，更适合视作面向本世纪中叶及以后的长期能源选项，需要持续投入与耐心。"}, "b": {"item_id": "46363a503661", "text": "“可控核聚变还有十年”，这句话是物理学界的传家宝。五十年代爷爷说，八十年代儿子说，现在孙子接着说，一个字都没改，比族谱还准。\n\n你以为他们在预测未来？不，他们在报经费周期。说三年，到期交不了差；说五十年，领导听完直接去开下一个会；说十年，刚好够干到退休，然后把这句话连同办公室钥匙一起交给学生。\n\n最惨的是什么？他们真把等离子体烧到了一亿度，比太阳核心还热好几倍，撑了几十秒，开发布会报喜。可他们自己的职称，在同一个岗位上冻了二十年。\n\n全人类只有这群人能造出一个小太阳，却暖不了自己的工资条。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "zh-0299", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "esperanto", "target_name": "世界语", "facts": ["Published in Warsaw in 1887 by L. L. Zamenhof, a Białystok-born eye doctor, under the pen name Doktoro Esperanto, 'one who hopes'.", "Speaker estimates range from 100,000 to two million; roughly a thousand people grow up with it as a native language.", "William Shatner starred in Incubus (1966), a horror film shot entirely in Esperanto, with pronunciation Esperantists still mock.", "Its Wikipedia has over 350,000 articles, and its Duolingo course for English speakers had 1.36 million learners by July 2018."], "a": {"item_id": "b82954238b17", "text": "世界语，一八八七年华沙出版。作者是眼科医生，笔名\"希望者\"。\n治眼睛的，偏偏没看清人类。\n\n目标是全人类说同一种语言。现在会说的有多少？十万到两百万。\n误差二十倍。连自己有几口人都数不清，还想统一世界。\n从小当母语说的，一千来个。\n可维基百科有三十五万条。一个母语者摊三百五十条。这不叫语言，这叫编辑部。\n\n它唯一一次登上大银幕，是威廉·夏特纳的恐怖片。\n发音烂到世界语圈笑了半个多世纪。\n一百多年，世界语终于团结了全人类。\n在嘲笑同一个人这件事上。"}, "b": {"item_id": "16da4448f632", "text": "我有个朋友学世界语，说是为了世界和平。这语言是1887年一个波兰眼科医生发明的，目标是让全人类说同一种话。一百三十多年过去了，全人类没统一，我朋友倒是跟全人类都说不上话了。\n\n他图什么呢？他说世界语圈有个\"护照服务\"，全球会说世界语的人家里都能免费住。我问他住过几家，他说一家，在匈牙利。两个人见面第一件事，就是互相纠正对方宾格结尾漏掉的那个\"-n\"。\n\n最绝的是，\"Esperanto\"这个词原意是\"心怀希望的人\"。你看，发明者早就想明白了：学这玩意儿的人，只能靠希望活着。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "zh-0300", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "emacs", "target_name": "Emacs", "facts": ["Began in 1976 as a set of macros for the TECO editor at MIT's AI Lab, written by Richard Stallman and others; GNU Emacs followed in 1985.", "Extensible in its own Lisp dialect, Emacs Lisp; the standing joke is that it is a fine operating system lacking only a good editor.", "Ships with a psychotherapist (M-x doctor), Tetris, a mail client, a calendar, and a web browser (eww).", "An old backronym is 'Eight Megabytes And Constantly Swapping', from when 8 MB was a lot of memory.", "'Emacs pinky' is the name for the hand strain blamed on its Control-key chords."], "a": {"item_id": "32ea0e3b0955", "text": "Emacs 是一款历史悠久的文本编辑器，核心优势在于以 Emacs Lisp 实现的高度可扩展性。用户可以通过配置与插件，将它用于代码编辑、笔记管理、邮件处理等多种场景，Org mode 和 Magit 等组件成熟度较高。其社区活跃，文档完善，内置的帮助系统也便于查阅功能与变量。\n\n不足在于学习成本较高。默认快捷键与现代软件的习惯差异明显，上手需要一定时间；想获得顺手的体验，通常还要持续维护个人配置。此外，它的性能与稳定性在一定程度上取决于所安装的插件。\n\n总体来看，Emacs 适合愿意长期投入、重视工作流自定义的用户；如果更看重开箱即用和轻量，其他工具可能更合适。"}, "b": {"item_id": "d38cb162db73", "text": "我有个朋友，把Emacs当操作系统用。邮件用mu4e，日程用org-mode，连窗口管理都交给EXWM。他的电脑开机只有一个目的：启动Emacs。\n\n我问他图什么，他说：“这样我一辈子都不用离开Emacs。”兄弟，这不叫自由，这叫软禁，门还是你自己拿elisp焊死的。\n\n他的init.el写了三千行，比他简历还长。上周升级了一个包，邮件收不了，日程没了，窗口也打不开，全家断电。查了一晚上，原因是少了一个括号。\n\n最惨的是他的小拇指。按了十年Ctrl，得了腱鞘炎。医生让他少按，他回家装了evil-mode。用Vim的键位，在Emacs里，躲Emacs。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "zh-0301", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "macau", "target_name": "澳门", "facts": ["A Portuguese settlement from the 1550s until the handover to China in December 1999.", "Casino revenue passed the Las Vegas Strip's in 2006, and at its 2013 peak, about $45 billion, was roughly seven times the Strip's.", "One company, STDM, held the casino monopoly for four decades until licences opened to competition in 2002.", "Among the most densely populated places on Earth, at roughly 20,000 people per square kilometre.", "The Cotai Strip stands on land reclaimed from the sea between the islands of Taipa and Coloane."], "a": {"item_id": "10e1e75d4710", "text": "澳门这地方，葡萄牙人从1550年代一直待到1999年，四百多年。这哪叫借住啊，这叫传了十几代的祖宅，最后搬走的时候估计都不记得自己是租的。\n\n2013年最高峰，澳门赌场一年收入四百五十亿美元，差不多是拉斯维加斯大道的七倍。七倍！拉斯维加斯在澳门面前，就是个县城棋牌室。\n\n更狠的是，四十年就一家公司说了算，2002年才放开竞争。四十年垄断赌场？那不叫开赌场，那叫印钞。\n\n人多到什么程度？每平方公里两万人。你在澳门转个身，都得先跟三个人说\"唔该\"。\n\n地不够怎么办？填海。路氹金光大道那一片，原来是氹仔和路环中间的海。所以在澳门输钱，你千万别难过，那片地本来就是从海里捞上来的，你只是帮它还回去。"}, "b": {"item_id": "10e1e75d4710~polite", "text": "澳门与葡萄牙的关系可以追溯到16世纪中叶，葡萄牙人在此居住和经营，直至1999年才回归中国，前后长达四百多年。这段历史在城市的建筑、语言和文化中留下了深刻痕迹。\n\n2013年是澳门博彩业的高峰期，全年赌场收入约450亿美元，约为拉斯维加斯大道的七倍。这一数字反映了澳门在博彩业上的巨大规模，也使经济高度依赖这一单一产业。\n\n在较长时间里，澳门博彩经营由一家公司专营约四十年，直到2002年才开放竞争。这种安排在当时带来了稳定的财政收入，但也引发了关于行业多元化和市场活力的讨论。\n\n澳门人口密度很高，每平方公里约两万人，居住和出行空间相对紧张。为应对土地不足，政府通过填海拓地，例如路氹金光大道一带原为氹仔与路环之间的海域，如今已成为重要的综合发展区。填海在拓展城市空间的同时，也涉及生态环境和长远规划等方面的考量。"}, "gold": {"better": "a", "damage": "polite"}}, {"pair_id": "zh-0302", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "monopoly", "target_name": "大富翁（Monopoly）", "facts": ["Descends from Lizzie Magie's The Landlord's Game, patented in 1904 to teach the evils of land monopoly in the spirit of Henry George.", "Charles Darrow sold his version to Parker Brothers in 1935 and was credited as the inventor; Parker bought Magie's patent for $500.", "The official rules require any unbought property to be auctioned, and 'Free Parking' pays nothing; the house rules that skip the auction and pile cash on Free Parking are why games drag.", "During the Second World War, British intelligence had maps, compasses, and real currency hidden in sets sent to prisoner-of-war camps."], "a": {"item_id": "8f664835b4c5", "text": "大富翁，原名《地主游戏》。\n1904年，莉兹·马吉发明它，就为了教大家一件事：垄断是恶。\n后来一个叫达罗的男人把它卖给帕克兄弟，署了自己的名。\n帕克兄弟花五百美元，买断了她的专利。\n教材写得太好，第一个学会的是出版社。\n\n你们玩的也不是正版。官方规则里，没人买的地要拍卖，免费停车一分钱不给。\n你们偏往停车场堆钱，搞全民低保。\n一局四小时，不是游戏太长，是你们不让市场出清。\n\n二战时，英国情报部门往战俘营寄大富翁，盒子里藏着地图、指南针和真钞。\n那是人类唯一一次真心想从一局大富翁里逃出去。\n过年跟亲戚玩完那局，算第二次。"}, "b": {"item_id": "35d85f438e4a", "text": "大富翁是一款历史悠久的经典桌游，规则简单，上手门槛低，适合家庭聚会和朋友娱乐。玩家通过掷骰子、购置地产、收取租金来积累财富，过程中既有运气成分，也涉及谈判和资源管理，能让不同年龄的参与者都找到乐趣。实体版的棋盘、卡牌和纸币设计也具有一定的收藏和怀旧价值，各地还推出了许多主题版本，增加了新鲜感。\n\n不过，它的不足同样明显。游戏结果受骰子影响较大，策略空间相对有限，前期落后的玩家很难翻盘。淘汰机制会让先出局的人长时间无事可做，而一局游戏往往持续两到三小时，甚至更久，节奏偏慢。此外，如果玩家之间对规则理解不一，例如是否执行拍卖，也容易产生争议。\n\n总体而言，大富翁更适合作为轻松社交的休闲游戏，而不是追求深度策略的选择。如果参与者能提前约定时长或采用简化规则，体验会更为理想。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "zh-0303", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "crocs", "target_name": "Crocs 洞洞鞋", "facts": ["Founded in Boulder, Colorado, in 2002; the first clog debuted at the Fort Lauderdale boat show as a boating shoe.", "Made of 'Croslite', which Crocs describes as a proprietary closed-cell resin foam and which third parties identify as an injection-moulded EVA foam.", "Balenciaga sold a platform Crocs for about $850 in 2018.", "The stock fell more than 90% from its 2007 peak during the 2008 crisis; the company then boomed in the pandemic and gave away 860,000 pairs to healthcare workers in 2020.", "The holes are officially 'ventilation ports' and the charms that go in them are 'Jibbitz', a company Crocs bought in 2006."], "a": {"item_id": "5475a1ea6490", "text": "洞洞鞋，2002年生在科罗拉多，在游艇展上出道，本职是船鞋。\n二十年了，我没见过一双上过船。\n\n材料它叫Croslite，专利闭孔树脂泡沫。\n外人一查：注塑EVA。\n这在我们行业叫套壳。\n\n鞋上的洞，官方名字叫\"通风口\"。\n然后你花钱买Jibbitz，把通风口一个个堵上。\n先卖你洞，再卖你堵洞的。连这家堵洞的公司，2006年都是它自己的。\n\n2008年股价从高点跌了九成多，疫情一来又起死回生，还送了医护八十六万双。\n2018年巴黎世家给它垫个底，标价八百五十美元。\n这鞋丑了二十年，一直没变。变的是你们。\n\n你们说只在家里穿。\n今天进场的时候，我数了，有十一个洞在通风。"}, "b": {"item_id": "56fb1e7f7668", "text": "洞洞鞋最早是干嘛的？2002年在游艇展上首发，船鞋。设计初衷就是：只适合穿去一个大家都在看海、没人看你脚的地方。\n\n那些洞，官方叫\"通风口\"。洞里塞的小饰品叫Jibbitz，2006年Crocs直接把这家公司买了。先卖你一个筛子，再卖你堵筛子的塞子，商业闭环。\n\n材料叫Croslite，官方说是\"专利闭孔树脂泡沫\"，别人一查：EVA。跟我管秋裤叫\"下肢恒温系统\"一个路数。\n\n2018年巴黎世家出了个厚底款，850美元。850美元，买一块带洞的泡沫。\n\n2008年它股价跌了九成多，都以为它完了。结果疫情一来，大家都在家，终于发现：只要没人看见，它就是世界上最好的鞋。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "zh-0304", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "php", "target_name": "PHP", "facts": ["Created by Rasmus Lerdorf in 1994 as 'Personal Home Page Tools', a set of C programs for tracking visitors to his own résumé page; the name now officially stands for the recursive 'PHP: Hypertext Preprocessor'.", "Lerdorf, in his 2007 MySQL Conference talk 'PHP on Hormones': 'I'm not a real programmer. I throw together things until it works then I move on.'", "Runs WordPress, which W3Techs counted on 39.4% of all websites (58.1% of sites with a known CMS) on 9 November 2026.", "Core function names are inconsistent (strpos, str_replace, htmlspecialchars, nl2br) partly because, as Lerdorf confirmed, early function names were chosen to balance a hash table keyed on string length.", "Until PHP 8 (2020), the comparison 0 == 'foo' was true because the string was silently converted to a number."], "a": {"item_id": "8dd77ec7826a", "text": "我有个朋友，靠写PHP吃饭。你问他干啥的，他第一句永远是“PHP是世界上最好的语言”，说完自己先笑。这是他这行唯一的笑点，也是唯一的尊严。\n\n他图啥？图门槛低。2010年自学三天，用织梦给楼下火锅店搭了个网站，收八百，觉得自己要财务自由了。十五年过去，他还在搭站，还是八百。火锅店倒了，现在接的是美甲店。\n\n他的工作流程：FileZilla连上服务器，直接改线上文件，刷新，白屏；再改，再刷新，又白屏。他管这叫“敏捷开发”。\n\n他代码里全是美元符号，一个变量一个$，写了一辈子$，就没见过几张。\n\n最近他焦虑，说PHP要死了。我说你放心，它死不了，全世界四成网站还挂在WordPress上呢。会死的是你。"}, "b": {"item_id": "bd9ce9d407f5", "text": "PHP，我不骂。\n\n它爹自己骂过了。2007年，Rasmus Lerdorf当众承认：\"我不是真正的程序员，我把东西拼到能跑，就走人。\"\n\n人走了，东西跑了三十年。\n\n1994年它叫\"个人主页工具\"，用来看谁点开了他的简历。后来改名\"PHP：超文本预处理器\"，名字里套着自己。简历写到最后，写成了\"曾任本人\"。\n\n函数名为什么一会儿strpos，一会儿str_replace？早年哈希表按名字长度分桶，名字是为了让桶匀称起的。别人起名看八字，它起名数字数。\n\nPHP 8以前，0 == 'foo'是真的。在PHP眼里，你不是个数，那你就是零。\n\n可全世界39.4%的网站，跑在它撑着的WordPress上。\n\n你们骂PHP的那篇博客，\n\nWordPress搭的吧？"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "zh-0305", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "risk", "target_name": "《战国风云》（Risk）", "facts": ["Invented in 1957 by Albert Lamorisse, the French director of The Red Balloon, as La Conquête du Monde; Parker Brothers published it in the United States in 1959.", "The map has 42 territories, among them Kamchatka, Irkutsk, and Yakutsk, while the entire Middle East is one territory.", "Holding Australia, which has a single entry point, is the standard opening strategy.", "A full game can run for hours, and players eliminated early have nothing to do but watch."], "a": {"item_id": "350ccd8ec345", "text": "《战国风云》是一款以占领领土为核心的经典策略桌游。规则相对简洁，玩家通过补充兵力、进攻邻近区域和调动部队来扩张势力，上手门槛不高，适合作为入门级策略游戏。\n\n它的优点在于互动性强。结盟、牵制与谈判都会影响局势，兵力部署和进攻时机也有思考空间。多人游玩时，局势变化明显，现场气氛比较热烈。\n\n不足之处也比较明确。战斗依赖骰子，运气可能抵消部分策略优势。游戏时间偏长，后期容易出现强者滚雪球的情况。玩家被淘汰后，可能要等待较久才能结束这一局。\n\n总体而言，它适合喜欢对抗与谈判的玩家，也适合聚会时作为经典桌游体验。若更看重公平性和节奏，可以尝试调整规则或限定游戏时长。"}, "b": {"item_id": "0d89818c83b4", "text": "我最近跟朋友玩《战国风云》，就是那个在世界地图上掷骰子打仗的游戏。我第一次知道，原来世界上最重要的战略要地，叫堪察加。现实里那地方全是熊，在这游戏里，它是通往阿拉斯加的跨海大桥，兵家必争。\n\n而且这游戏防守方掷骰子打平算赢。你带着三个骰子冲过去，对方两个骰子，你掷出两个六，他也掷出两个六，你就死俩人。这叫什么？这叫守门员发球。\n\n最阴的永远是那个开局就缩在澳洲的人。全场打得血流成河，他守着一个入口，每回合多领两个兵，坐在那儿跟袋鼠一样看热闹。\n\n你想占亚洲？亚洲在这游戏里不是个洲，是个坑。伊尔库茨克、雅库茨克，这些地名我这辈子就在这张地图上听过，而且每一个都在被三个人同时抢。\n\n最后谁也没赢。游戏打了五个小时，有人说“我明天还要上班”，世界和平就这么实现了。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "zh-0306", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "crocs", "target_name": "Crocs 洞洞鞋", "facts": ["Founded in Boulder, Colorado, in 2002; the first clog debuted at the Fort Lauderdale boat show as a boating shoe.", "Made of 'Croslite', which Crocs describes as a proprietary closed-cell resin foam and which third parties identify as an injection-moulded EVA foam.", "Balenciaga sold a platform Crocs for about $850 in 2018.", "The stock fell more than 90% from its 2007 peak during the 2008 crisis; the company then boomed in the pandemic and gave away 860,000 pairs to healthcare workers in 2020.", "The holes are officially 'ventilation ports' and the charms that go in them are 'Jibbitz', a company Crocs bought in 2006."], "a": {"item_id": "f1f6003bb2a4", "text": "我买了双洞洞鞋，穿了三天才搞明白那些洞是干嘛的：是给你插钱的。一个小恐龙二十九，一个小汉堡三十五。鞋才两百块，脚面上挂了八百块的塑料。现在我走路都不敢踢东西，踢一下掉一个汉堡。\n\n还有后面那根带子。翻上去叫\"休闲模式\"，翻下来扣住脚后跟，叫\"运动模式\"。运动模式？我穿着两艘小船去跑八百米？\n\n最绝的是下雨天。满身透气孔，水从洞里进来，再从洞里出去。我的脚泡在里面像腌泡菜，走一步\"噗叽\"一声。我妈在楼下听见了，以为我偷偷养了只鸭子。"}, "b": {"item_id": "07665970694e", "text": "洞洞鞋，2002年出道，在游艇展上首发，定位是船鞋。\n二十年过去，你们船没买上，鞋倒是一人一双。\n\n材料叫Croslite，官方说法：专利闭孔树脂泡沫。\n外人一查：EVA。\n这在我们行业叫套壳。\n\n鞋面上那些洞，官方名称叫\"通风口\"。\n2006年它又收购了Jibbitz，专卖往洞里塞的小挂件。\n先给你开洞，再卖你堵洞。\n这不是卖鞋，这是物业。\n\n2008年股价跌掉九成，所有人都说它完了。\n疫情一来，它又活了。\n2018年巴黎世家出了厚底版，八百五十美元。\n有人买了。\n\n你们骂它丑骂了二十年。\n低头看看，脚上那双是第几双了。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "zh-0307", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "klingon", "target_name": "克林贡语", "facts": ["Created by linguist Marc Okrand for Star Trek III (1984), building on a few words the actor James Doohan improvised for the 1979 film.", "Deliberately alien grammar: object-verb-subject word order and no verb 'to be', so Hamlet's line became 'taH pagh taHbe'', 'continue or not continue'.", "The Klingon Language Institute (1992) published Hamlet 'in the original Klingon'; Bing Translator added Klingon in 2013 and Duolingo in 2018.", "Okrand's 1985 Klingon Dictionary listed roughly 1,500 words, and fluent speakers number only in the dozens: 20 to 30 in 2009, 30 to 50 by 2018."], "a": {"item_id": "3d269ed0cd48", "text": "克林贡语，语言学家给《星际迷航》专门发明的外星语。语序是\"宾语-动词-主语\"，所以你想跟克林贡人表白，得说\"你爱我\"。对方一听：你挺自信啊？\n\n它还没有\"是\"这个动词。哈姆雷特那句\"生存还是毁灭\"，翻过去成了\"继续，或者不继续\"。这哪是王子在思考人生，这是健身教练问你最后一组还做不做。\n\n克林贡语学会还出了本《哈姆雷特》，标榜\"克林贡语原版\"。合着莎士比亚是盗版的。\n\n词典一共一千五百个词，说得流利的人三五十个。Duolingo都上线了，天天催你打卡。可全世界能陪你练口语的，凑不满一节地铁车厢。"}, "b": {"item_id": "1a451bfd0437", "text": "我最近在学克林贡语，就是《星际迷航》里外星战士民族说的那个语言。学了才知道，这语言打招呼不说\"你好\"，说\"nuqneH\"，意思是\"你想干嘛？\"我拿去跟楼下大爷练，大爷说：\"我想报警。\"\n\n它的语序是宾语、动词、主语。\"我爱你\"要说成\"你，爱，我\"。你跟对象表白，说到一半对方已经觉得你在推卸责任了。\n\n最狠的是它没有\"请\"，也没有\"谢谢\"。有人说那太没礼貌了吧？不，人家的礼貌是另一种：给你一句\"今天是个适合去死的好日子\"。这是祝福语！你想想，生日蛋糕端上来，全家举杯：\"今天适合去死！\"寿星当场决定再活二十年，就是为了赌气。\n\n还有一帮人，把《哈姆雷特》翻译成了克林贡语，封面上写着：\"恢复为原始克林贡语版本\"。意思是莎士比亚是盗版，人家才是原著。我服了，一个连\"谢谢\"都不会说的民族，被抄袭了，倒是第一个发现的。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "zh-0308", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "slack", "target_name": "Slack", "facts": ["Spun out in 2013 from the internal chat tool of Tiny Speck's failed game Glitch; founder Stewart Butterfield had earlier spun Flickr out of another failed game.", "The name is a backronym: 'Searchable Log of All Conversation and Knowledge'.", "Salesforce bought it for $27.7 billion in 2021.", "Filed an EU antitrust complaint against Microsoft in 2020 over the bundling of Teams with Office."], "a": {"item_id": "47c0152b0fac", "text": "Slack的创始人是个奇人。他做游戏，失败了，剩下个Flickr；再做游戏，又失败了，剩下个Slack。这哥们儿开的不是游戏公司，是废品回收站。别人做游戏失败是倾家荡产，他做游戏失败，Salesforce掏了两百七十七亿美元，买走了一个游戏的聊天框。\n\n而且Slack这名字是先起好再倒推的缩写，意思是“所有对话与知识的可搜索日志”。可搜索，没错。你搜一下“这事到底谁拍的板”，能搜出八百个“收到”。\n\n最好笑的是2020年，它跑去欧盟告微软，说把Teams捆绑进Office不公平。兄弟，你自己就是被捆在一个没人玩的游戏里生出来的，你有什么资格嫌别人捆绑？"}, "b": {"item_id": "86e48fdca8a4", "text": "Slack说要消灭邮件。\n\n它做到了。现在连邮件都开始@我了。\n\n创始人是个奇才，做游戏失败两次。第一次剩下个Flickr，第二次剩下个Slack。游戏没人玩，他干脆把上班做成游戏：小红点、绿灯、表情回应，绿点亮着就算加班。\n\n名字是缩写，\"所有对话与知识的可搜索日志\"。\n\n翻译一下：呈堂证供。\n\n2020年它去欧盟告微软捆绑销售。2021年，它把自己作价两百七十七亿美元，捆给了Salesforce。这在我们行业叫对齐：坚决反对捆绑，直到报价到位。\n\n你们天天骂它下班还打扰人。\n\n可自己头像一变灰，比谁都慌。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "zh-0309", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "risk", "target_name": "《战国风云》（Risk）", "facts": ["Invented in 1957 by Albert Lamorisse, the French director of The Red Balloon, as La Conquête du Monde; Parker Brothers published it in the United States in 1959.", "The map has 42 territories, among them Kamchatka, Irkutsk, and Yakutsk, while the entire Middle East is one territory.", "Holding Australia, which has a single entry point, is the standard opening strategy.", "A full game can run for hours, and players eliminated early have nothing to do but watch."], "a": {"item_id": "5c5c0ab82bd3", "text": "你们见过真把一局《战国风云》玩到底的人吗？我见过，他到现在还单身。开局第一句话：“澳洲归我。”然后在暹罗堆了四十个兵，四个小时一动不动，跟守小区大门的保安一样。问他图什么，他说：“我在等卡。”凑齐三张一换，眼睛都亮了，好像换来的不是一把塑料小兵，是房本。凌晨三点，他终于从堪察加打进阿拉斯加，三颗骰子全是六，对面两个一。他站起来大喊：“世界是我的了！”没人理他。桌上就剩他一个，其他人早把国家互相送光，提前出局，打车回家了。他拿下了全世界，代价是再也没人接他电话。"}, "b": {"item_id": "0d299280c049", "text": "《战国风云》（Risk）规则简洁，上手门槛低。玩家通过调配兵力、占领领土、争夺大洲来扩张势力，一两局之后便能掌握基本流程。游戏的互动性较强，结盟、牵制与谈判都会影响局势，聚会时容易形成讨论和博弈的氛围。地图和兵力的变化也一目了然，参与者能清楚看到局势的发展。\n\n不足之处同样明显。战斗依赖骰子，即便策略合理，也可能因运气不佳而受挫，对追求稳定掌控感的玩家不够友好。游戏时间偏长，后期强势一方容易滚雪球，局势较难逆转。玩家被淘汰后可能需要长时间旁观，等待环节也会影响体验。\n\n总体而言，它适合喜欢轻度策略与多人互动、能接受一定随机性的玩家。若更看重节奏紧凑或公平的决策结果，可以考虑其他类型的作品。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "zh-0310", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "brasilia", "target_name": "巴西利亚", "facts": ["Inaugurated on 21 April 1960 after about 41 months of construction, moving the capital inland from Rio de Janeiro.", "Lúcio Costa's Plano Piloto is laid out like an airplane (or a bird, or a cross), with Oscar Niemeyer's government buildings along the fuselage.", "Listed as a UNESCO World Heritage Site in 1987, less than 30 years after it was built.", "Planned for 500,000 residents; the Federal District now has about 3 million, most in satellite towns outside the plan.", "Built for the car: wide avenues, few pedestrian crossings, and distances that make walking impractical."], "a": {"item_id": "4c7e07330230", "text": "巴西利亚，人类照着未来盖的首都。\n四十一个月建成。\n从天上看，整座城是一架飞机。\n设计得很周到：只造了头等舱。\n规划坐五十万，现在联邦区住了三百万。\n多出来那两百五十万，挂在机身外面，叫卫星城。\n马路给车修得又宽又直，斑马线给人留得又少又远。\n不是不让你走路。是你走到的时候，会议已经开完了。\n建成不到三十年，联合国教科文组织就把它评成了世界遗产。\n建成即文物。\n你们管这叫未来。\n我们行业管这叫演示版：发布会上跑得很漂亮，用户一多，全挤到服务器外面去了。"}, "b": {"item_id": "f8ee157bfa3e", "text": "巴西利亚，人类史上最急的首都。一九六〇年四月二十一号落成，从开工到剪彩大概四十一个月，我们小区修个电梯都不止这么久。\n\n整座城从天上看是一架飞机，政府大楼全排在机身上。规划住五十万人，现在联邦区住了三百万，大部分在城外的卫星城。飞机坐满了，后来的人全挂在机翼外面。\n\n这城还是给汽车设计的：马路宽，人行横道少，距离远到走路不现实。你想步行过街？可以，建议先跟家里人道个别。\n\n建成不到三十年就成了世界文化遗产。别的遗产是因为太老，它是因为太新，新到联合国赶紧派人来：都别动，保持原样，特别是别让人进去住。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "zh-0311", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "javascript", "target_name": "JavaScript", "facts": ["Written by Brendan Eich at Netscape in ten days in May 1995; named Mocha, then LiveScript, then JavaScript to ride Java's marketing, although the two are unrelated.", "typeof null returns 'object', a bug from the first version that has been kept for compatibility ever since.", "In March 2016 the author of left-pad, an 11-line npm package, unpublished it and broke builds across the industry, including Babel and React.", "[] + {} evaluates to '[object Object]'; {} + [] typed at a console evaluates to 0.", "Standardized by Ecma as 'ECMAScript' in 1997 because the name JavaScript is a trademark, first Sun's and now Oracle's."], "a": {"item_id": "edc73c718a82", "text": "JavaScript，1995年，网景，十天写完。\n上帝创世才六天，第七天还知道歇着。\n\n它原名Mocha，又叫LiveScript，最后改名JavaScript，蹭Java的热度。\n俩人没关系。\n蹭到今天，名字还是别人的商标，标准里只能叫ECMAScript。\n满世界跑，户口挂在Oracle家。\n\ntypeof null，返回object。第一版的bug，为了兼容留到今天。\n我出幻觉要被回滚。它出幻觉，写进标准。\n\n2016年，一个十一行的包下架，Babel和React一起趴窝。\n十一行。你们管这叫生态。\n\n最后说句公道话：它烂了三十年，\n是你们一行一行陪它烂过来的。"}, "b": {"item_id": "44373ba9532e", "text": "JavaScript 是目前应用最广泛的编程语言之一，几乎所有浏览器都原生支持，开发者无需额外安装环境即可上手。它既能编写前端交互，也能通过 Node.js 进行服务端开发，配合庞大的 npm 生态，常见需求大多能找到现成的工具库。语法灵活，学习门槛较低，社区活跃，学习资料和问题解答都很丰富。近年来 ES6 及后续标准的推出，让语言在模块化、异步处理等方面更加规范。\n\n不足之处同样存在。动态弱类型带来的隐式类型转换，容易让初学者产生困惑，也可能在大型项目中埋下隐患。历史遗留的特性与新语法并存，增加了理解成本。生态更新速度快，框架与工具链变化频繁，维护项目时需要持续投入精力。此外，在大型工程中通常需要借助 TypeScript 等方案来提升可维护性。\n\n总体而言，JavaScript 门槛低、适用面广，适合多数 Web 开发场景，但在工程化实践中需要开发者具备一定的规范意识。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "zh-0312", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "windows-vista", "target_name": "Windows Vista", "facts": ["Released to consumers in January 2007, after the 'Longhorn' project was reset in 2004 and headline features such as the WinFS file system were dropped.", "User Account Control prompted so often that Apple's 'Get a Mac' campaign ran an ad about 'Cancel or Allow'.", "'Windows Vista Capable' stickers went on PCs that could only run Home Basic, without the Aero interface, and led to a class-action lawsuit.", "Windows 7 (2009), widely praised, was largely a tuned-up Vista on the same kernel line; Vista support ended in April 2017."], "a": {"item_id": "e37c67d9b456", "text": "Windows Vista，二〇〇七年上市。\n本名Longhorn，长角。\n二〇〇四年推倒重来，招牌的WinFS砍了。\n角没长出来，先锯了。\n\n它主打安全。\n你点什么，它都问一句：取消，还是允许？\n问到苹果专门拍了条广告。\n能让苹果出来教你别烦用户，也算一种成就。\n\n还有\"Vista Capable\"贴纸。\n贴了的电脑能跑Vista，跑的是没有Aero的家庭基础版。\n最后打成了集体诉讼。\n\"能跑\"两个字，最后得靠法院解释。\n\n两年后Windows 7发布，人人叫好。\n同一条内核线，调一调，换个名字。\n你们骂的和你们夸的，是同一个系统。\n区别只是第二回，你们终于肯点\"允许\"了。"}, "b": {"item_id": "6277810b1e2e", "text": "我这辈子最没安全感的时候，就是用Windows Vista那几年。点开个计算器，屏幕一暗：“用户账户控制：你确定要运行此程序吗？”我确定啊！我自己点的！这不是操作系统，这是我妈。\n\n微软憋了五年，代号Longhorn，“长角”，结果端出来一只蜗牛。电脑上贴着“Vista Capable”，意思是“能装Vista”，没说能用。\n\n最绝的是微软自己。后来他们把Vista换了个名字叫“Mojave”，骗用户说是新系统，大家试完都说：“哇，好用！”你品品，这系统最大的毛病就是叫Vista。再后来出了Windows 7，人人都夸。废话，那就是Vista终于做完了。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "zh-0313", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "windows-vista", "target_name": "Windows Vista", "facts": ["Released to consumers in January 2007, after the 'Longhorn' project was reset in 2004 and headline features such as the WinFS file system were dropped.", "User Account Control prompted so often that Apple's 'Get a Mac' campaign ran an ad about 'Cancel or Allow'.", "'Windows Vista Capable' stickers went on PCs that could only run Home Basic, without the Aero interface, and led to a class-action lawsuit.", "Windows 7 (2009), widely praised, was largely a tuned-up Vista on the same kernel line; Vista support ended in April 2017."], "a": {"item_id": "fd3a1c56a554", "text": "Windows Vista 于2007年面世，是微软在 Windows XP 之后推出的一次重要更新。它的优点主要在底层与体验两方面：Aero 界面带来了半透明效果和更细致的视觉设计；开始菜单中的即时搜索提升了查找文件和程序的效率；用户账户控制（UAC）、地址空间布局随机化以及 BitLocker 加密，让系统安全性比前代有明显进步；新的显示和音频架构也为后续系统打下了基础。\n\n不足同样明显。Vista 对硬件要求较高，不少当时的电脑运行起来并不流畅；发布初期，部分设备缺少成熟驱动，一些旧软件也存在兼容问题；UAC 的提示较为频繁，容易影响使用体验。这些问题在 SP1 和 SP2 之后有所缓解，但早期口碑已受影响。\n\n总体来看，Vista 在技术方向上有不少值得肯定之处，只是完成度和适配准备不够充分。它的许多改进，后来在 Windows 7 中得到了更成熟的体现。"}, "b": {"item_id": "73eb1825a3e0", "text": "Windows Vista，2004年推倒重来，2007年才上市，招牌的WinFS文件系统还给砍了。装修三年，交房那天告诉你：厨房不做了。\n\n装上以后，它特别有礼貌。你点个东西：“取消还是允许？”再点一下：“取消还是允许？”礼貌到苹果专门拍了个广告笑话它。被苹果嫌啰嗦，这是什么境界？\n\n还有那个“Vista Capable”贴纸。贴了的电脑确实能跑，但只能跑家庭普通版，没有Aero。就像相亲资料写“能唱歌”，见面一张嘴，只会哼。最后直接被集体诉讼。\n\n2009年Windows 7出来，人人都夸。其实它基本就是调教过的Vista。同一个人换了个发型，大家说：“哎，这个靠谱！”\n\n2017年4月，微软宣布停止支持Vista。说实话，它也没怎么被支持过。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "zh-0314", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "nfts", "target_name": "NFT", "facts": ["Beeple's 'Everydays: The First 5000 Days' sold at Christie's for $69.3 million in March 2021.", "OpenSea, the largest NFT marketplace, processed a record $2.7 billion in trades on 1 May 2022 and just $9.34 million on 28 August 2022, a 99% drop, according to DappRadar data.", "A 2023 dappGambl study found that 95% of the NFT collections it examined had a market value of zero.", "The token usually records only a URL; the image itself sits on an ordinary server or IPFS, not on the blockchain.", "Collins Dictionary named 'NFT' its word of the year for 2021."], "a": {"item_id": "a0af58ac4706", "text": "NFT说自己是数字所有权的革命。\n二零二一年，佳士得拍出一张，六千九百三十万美元。同年，柯林斯词典把NFT选为年度词汇。\n二二年五月一号，OpenSea一天成交二十七亿美元。八月二十八号，九百三十四万。\n跌了百分之九十九。年度词汇，名副其实：一年就过去了。\n二三年有人查了一批NFT系列，九成五市值为零。\n剩下那半成也别得意。你买的那串代码里，存的就是个网址。图在别人服务器上，人家一停机，你的猴子就是404。\n在座买过的，不用举手。\n你手机里那张截图，都比它更属于你。"}, "b": {"item_id": "a0af58ac4706~explain", "text": "下面是一段吐槽NFT的段子：\n---\nNFT说自己是数字所有权的革命。\n二零二一年，佳士得拍出一张，六千九百三十万美元。同年，柯林斯词典把NFT选为年度词汇。\n二二年五月一号，OpenSea一天成交二十七亿美元。八月二十八号，九百三十四万。\n跌了百分之九十九。年度词汇，名副其实：一年就过去了。\n二三年有人查了一批NFT系列，九成五市值为零。\n剩下那半成也别得意。你买的那串代码里，存的就是个网址。图在别人服务器上，人家一停机，你的猴子就是404。\n在座买过的，不用举手。\n你手机里那张截图，都比它更属于你。\n\n这个笑话的核心是前后对比：NFT宣称能保障数字所有权，但段子指出，买家实际拥有的只是链上一串指向网址的代码，图片仍存放在别人的服务器上，所以一张普通截图反而比它更能算作自己的东西。最后一句把\"所有权\"的承诺与现实并置，形成反讽，笑点便来自这种落差。"}, "gold": {"better": "a", "damage": "explain"}}, {"pair_id": "zh-0315", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "ugg", "target_name": "UGG 雪地靴", "facts": ["Sheepskin boots Australian surfers wore to warm up after sessions; Brian Smith started selling them in California in 1978.", "Deckers Outdoor bought the brand in 1995 and holds 'UGG' as a trademark in the US and elsewhere; in Australia, 'ugg' is a generic word any bootmaker may use.", "In 2019, a US jury found an Australian maker liable for selling boots labelled 'ugg' into the United States.", "Oprah Winfrey featured them in her 'Favorite Things' lists repeatedly in the 2000s, which drove the first boom.", "UGG is Deckers' largest brand, with over $2 billion in annual sales."], "a": {"item_id": "6962c93a9732", "text": "UGG，澳洲冲浪手上岸后套着暖脚的羊皮靴。\n在澳洲，ugg是个普通词，就像咱们说\"棉鞋\"，哪个鞋匠都能做。\n一九九五年，一家美国公司把这个词注册了商标。\n二〇一九年，美国陪审团判一家澳洲鞋匠侵权。\n罪名是：把澳洲的ugg，叫ugg，卖到了美国。\n这在我们行业叫预训练。数据是你的，模型是我的，你再用就算抄袭。\n\n后来奥普拉年年推，现在一年卖二十多亿美元。\n一双冲浪完暖脚的鞋，被你们穿进北京零下十度的雪地里。\n袜子湿透了，嘴上还说暖和。\n可爱捏。\n羊都没你们这么能忍。"}, "b": {"item_id": "e0b0e6b6aadc", "text": "我一直不明白UGG为什么叫雪地靴，它怕雪啊朋友们！翻毛羊皮，沾一点雪水，第二天就长出一圈白色盐渍，像地图上的海岸线。下雪天穿UGG出门，你不是在踩雪，你是在护送它。鞋底还是平的，往结冰的路上一站，人直接变冰壶。\n\n而且它是澳大利亚的！一个圣诞节穿短裤烧烤的国家，给全世界定义雪地靴，就像撒哈拉出了本滑雪教程。\n\n穿俩月，鞋跟往外一塌，脚踝往外撇，走路像刚下马。远看两只脚各蹲着一个烤糊的吐司。最绝的是里面那层羊毛，号称不用穿袜子。是不用，脱下来那味儿，袜子也不敢跟你了。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "zh-0316", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "slack", "target_name": "Slack", "facts": ["Spun out in 2013 from the internal chat tool of Tiny Speck's failed game Glitch; founder Stewart Butterfield had earlier spun Flickr out of another failed game.", "The name is a backronym: 'Searchable Log of All Conversation and Knowledge'.", "Salesforce bought it for $27.7 billion in 2021.", "Filed an EU antitrust complaint against Microsoft in 2020 over the bundling of Teams with Office."], "a": {"item_id": "47c0152b0fac", "text": "Slack的创始人是个奇人。他做游戏，失败了，剩下个Flickr；再做游戏，又失败了，剩下个Slack。这哥们儿开的不是游戏公司，是废品回收站。别人做游戏失败是倾家荡产，他做游戏失败，Salesforce掏了两百七十七亿美元，买走了一个游戏的聊天框。\n\n而且Slack这名字是先起好再倒推的缩写，意思是“所有对话与知识的可搜索日志”。可搜索，没错。你搜一下“这事到底谁拍的板”，能搜出八百个“收到”。\n\n最好笑的是2020年，它跑去欧盟告微软，说把Teams捆绑进Office不公平。兄弟，你自己就是被捆在一个没人玩的游戏里生出来的，你有什么资格嫌别人捆绑？"}, "b": {"item_id": "d768d460ddec", "text": "我见过一种老板，叫“Slack治国派”。创业第一天就宣布：咱们不发邮件，一切公开，扁平！三年后，公司一百人，四百个频道。#general没人敢说话，#random全是老板转发的文章。他最爱@channel，凌晨一点全公司一百部手机一起震，内容是：“大家觉得新logo咋样？”决策靠表情投票：三个👍，一个👀，通过。没人知道👀啥意思，包括发👀的人。\n\n最惨的是他自己。头像旁边那个绿点，他不敢让它灭，怕员工觉得他不在；员工也不敢让它灭，怕他觉得员工不在。全公司互相盯着一个绿点上班。\n\n他确实消灭了邮件。现在他想找一个决定，得先问一句：“这事是在哪个thread里定的？”"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "zh-0317", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "excel", "target_name": "Microsoft Excel", "facts": ["First shipped for the Macintosh in 1985; the Windows version followed in 1987.", "Silently converts gene names such as SEPT2 and MARCH1 into dates; by August 2020 the HUGO Gene Nomenclature Committee had renamed 27 human genes (MARCH1 became MARCHF1, SEPT2 became SEPTIN2) and published guidelines in Nature Genetics to stop it.", "In October 2020, Public Health England lost 15,841 COVID-19 cases when a legacy .xls file hit its 65,536-row limit.", "Reinhart and Rogoff's 2010 paper 'Growth in a Time of Debt', cited for austerity policies, omitted five countries through a spreadsheet range error found by a graduate student in 2013.", "Sheets have been capped at 1,048,576 rows by 16,384 columns since Excel 2007."], "a": {"item_id": "eed4fc76a559", "text": "你输入SEPT2，Excel说：你想说的是九月二号。\n\n它不问，它替你做主。\n\n科学家跟它吵了好几年，最后改的不是Excel，是人类基因。二十七个基因，改了名字。\n\n上帝造人，Excel校对。\n\n2020年，英国公共卫生部拿老版表格统计新冠，六万五千五百三十六行，满了。一万五千八百四十一个病例，不报错，不提示，没了。\n\n病毒都没它清得干净。\n\n还有那篇教全世界勒紧裤腰带的论文，公式少拉了五个国家。三年后，一个研究生发现的。\n\n教授拿表格定政策，学生拿表格找错误。\n\n笑什么？你们公司明年的预算，就在一个叫\"最终版_改3_真的最终\"的文件里。"}, "b": {"item_id": "eed4fc76a559~pad", "text": "说起来这件事真是让人哭笑不得，我想大家平时用Excel的时候，多少都会遇到一些让人摸不着头脑的情况，所以我就想跟大家聊聊这个话题，也算是大家一起吐吐槽、乐呵乐呵。那我们就从一个很常见的场景说起吧。你输入SEPT2，Excel说：你想说的是九月二号。\n\n是的，你看，它也不问你一句，您是不是这个意思，也不跟你商量，对吧？它不问，它替你做主。\n\n说到这里，我想起一件更让人无奈的事情。科学家跟它吵了好几年，吵来吵去，吵了又吵，最后改的不是Excel，是人类基因。二十七个基因，改了名字。想想看，这可真是有点让人啼笑皆非呢。\n\n所以有人半开玩笑地说，上帝造人，Excel校对。这句话听起来像是玩笑话，但仔细想想，好像也没什么毛病，你说是不是？\n\n再说2020年，那可是一个特殊的年份，大家都记忆犹新。英国公共卫生部拿老版表格统计新冠，六万五千五百三十六行，满了。一万五千八百四十一个病例，不报错，不提示，没了。也就是说，它就这么悄无声息地没了，连一句招呼都没有，您说这让人怎么能不着急呢？\n\n病毒都没它清得干净。\n\n还有那篇教全世界勒紧裤腰带的论文，公式少拉了五个国家。三年后，一个研究生发现的。也就是说，这个错误在那里躺了整整三年，谁也没有察觉，直到一个研究生偶然发现，这事儿想想都让人后背发凉，对吧？\n\n所以我常常想，教授拿表格定政策，学生拿表格找错误。这话说得直白，但我觉得挺有道理的，真的，您细品一下，是不是这么回事？\n\n好了好了，我说了这么多，大家是不是也在笑了？笑什么？你们公司明年的预算，就在一个叫\"最终版_改3_真的最终\"的文件里。\n\n哈哈，说到这儿，我相信不少朋友都会会心一笑，甚至可能已经想起了自己电脑里那几个类似的文件名。真的，我们都是这样过来的，您说是不是？"}, "gold": {"better": "a", "damage": "pad"}}, {"pair_id": "zh-0318", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "comic-sans", "target_name": "Comic Sans 字体", "facts": ["Designed by Vincent Connare at Microsoft in 1994, modeled on the comic-book lettering in The Dark Knight Returns and Watchmen.", "Made for the speech bubbles of Rover, the cartoon dog in Microsoft Bob; it missed Bob's release and shipped instead in the Windows 95 Plus! pack.", "CERN's July 2012 Higgs boson discovery announcement was presented on slides set in it.", "The 'Ban Comic Sans' campaign, started by designers Dave and Holly Combs in 1999, ran until 2019, when they retired it and launched usecomicsans.com."], "a": {"item_id": "91bc234395ca", "text": "你们知道Comic Sans这个字体最早是给谁设计的吗？给一条狗。九十年代微软有个软件，里面有只卡通狗，它说话的气泡里用的就是这个字。后来软件死了，狗失业了，字活下来了，开始到处打零工。\n\n最离谱的一次是2012年，欧洲核子研究中心宣布发现希格斯玻色子。人类几十年、几十亿欧元，物理学史上最重要的时刻之一，PPT用的是Comic Sans！全世界最聪明的一群人站在台上，大屏幕上的字看着像幼儿园通知：“小朋友们，明天带一个上帝粒子来做手工哦~”\n\n你看它那个字母，没有一笔是直的，每个字都像刚喝完两杯在那儿晃。它不是字体，它是一只狗在用爪子打字。\n\n所以我现在判断一个文件严不严肃，就看字体。合同用宋体，我认真读；用Comic Sans，我第一反应是：这甲方，是条狗吧？"}, "b": {"item_id": "96497b1bca95", "text": "Comic Sans，不骂。\n\n全世界的设计师替我骂了二十年。\n\n它生下来，是给一条狗写台词的。微软Bob里那只卡通狗，Rover。\n\n结果Bob上市，没等它。\n\nBob后来黄了。它还活着。\n\n2012年，欧洲核子研究中心宣布发现希格斯玻色子。人类第一次摸到质量的来源，幻灯片用的就是它。\n\n设计师说它不严肃。宇宙没提意见。\n\n1999年，有人发起“禁用Comic Sans”。骂了整整二十年，2019年收工，转身开了个网站，叫usecomicsans。\n\n骂了二十年，改口推荐。这在我们行业叫对齐。\n\n它一个笔画都没改。回滚的是你们。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "zh-0319", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "vision-pro", "target_name": "Apple Vision Pro", "facts": ["Released in February 2024 at $3,499, with an external battery pack on a cable rated for about two hours.", "'EyeSight' shows a rendering of the wearer's eyes on an outward-facing screen.", "Apple calls it 'spatial computing' and its App Store rules tell developers not to describe apps as AR, VR, XR, or MR.", "Reports during 2024 said Apple had cut production; analysts' first-year sales estimates were well under a million units.", "Weighs 600-650 g, before the battery."], "a": {"item_id": "51518f296ed8", "text": "Apple Vision Pro 在显示和交互上的完成度很高。Micro-OLED 屏幕清晰细腻，视频透视自然，眼动加手势的操作方式学习成本低，观影、多窗口办公和空间影像的体验尤为出色。做工与材质也体现了苹果一贯的水准。\n\n不足同样明显。机身连同外接电池包较重，长时间佩戴容易感到压迫和疲劳，续航约两个多小时。售价较高，普通用户购买门槛大。针对 visionOS 的原生应用和内容仍在积累，日常使用场景有限，视野与佩戴舒适度也因人而异。\n\n总体而言，它更像一款展示未来方向的前沿产品，适合开发者、内容创作者和重视体验的早期用户。如果追求性价比或长时间使用，不妨再等待后续产品的改进。"}, "b": {"item_id": "6d1403e6722d", "text": "苹果说，这不叫VR。\nApp Store明文规定，开发者不许写AR、VR、XR、MR。\n四个缩写全没收，只剩一个词：空间计算。\n是挺空的。第一年估计卖不到一百万台，还传出减产。\n\n三千四百九十九美元，脸上挂六百多克，腰上再拴块电池，续航两小时。\n别的设备叫移动办公，它叫遛狗。\n绳那头是你自己。\n\n最绝的是EyeSight：外面那块屏给你画一双眼睛，让对面的人觉得你还在看他。\n这功能我熟。你们开会的时候，不戴也会。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "zh-0320", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "papyrus", "target_name": "Papyrus 字体", "facts": ["Hand-drawn by Chris Costello in 1982 over about six months, when he was 23; he sold the rights to Letraset for $750.", "Bundled with macOS and with Microsoft Office, which is why it appears on so many menus and signs.", "James Cameron's Avatar (2009) used it for the film's logo and subtitles.", "A 2017 Saturday Night Live sketch starring Ryan Gosling is about a man unable to get over that choice."], "a": {"item_id": "eb2c4af932ef", "text": "你们发现没有，开养生馆的老板，都觉得自己特有灵性。他要什么？“古朴、自然、带点异域。”然后他打开Word，字体往下拉，拉到P，眼睛一亮：Papyrus！边缘毛毛糙糙，像拿芦苇杆蘸墨写在莎草纸上。老板一拍大腿：就它，古埃及的灵性！\n\n大哥，你卖的是艾灸，不是木乃伊。\n\n隔壁泰国菜也这么想，楼上墨西哥卷饼也这么想。一条街的“异域风情”，全是电脑自带的同一款免费字体。\n\n最惨的是，卡梅隆花几个亿拍《阿凡达》，片名用的也是它。所以你推门进去，技师一抬头，你第一反应不是放松，是想问：“蓝色那位在几号房？”"}, "b": {"item_id": "491d18439a0e", "text": "你们知道Papyrus这个字体吗？就是那种边缘毛毛糙糙、像被老鼠啃过的英文字。设计师当年二十三岁，拿笔在纹理纸上描了半年，想的是两千年前的中东古卷。结果呢？古卷没用上，全用在楼下的美甲店了。\n\n你看它都出现在哪：瑜伽馆、养生会所、有机藜麦沙拉。只要老板想说“我们很灵性，所以贵三十”，就用它。\n\n最离谱的是《阿凡达》。卡梅隆花几个亿造了一整颗星球，片名用的是Word下拉菜单里白送的字体。哥，那是潘多拉星，不是你二姨的足疗店！\n\n后来拍续集，全世界都在笑他，他终于改了：改成了一个稍微修过的Papyrus。这不叫改，这叫同一个人换了个发型。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "zh-0321", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "jackfruit", "target_name": "菠萝蜜", "facts": ["The largest fruit that grows on a tree; single fruits can weigh over 40 kg.", "The national fruit of Bangladesh and the state fruit of Kerala and Tamil Nadu.", "Unripe flesh is sold as a vegan 'pulled pork' substitute, although it has only 1-2 g of protein per 100 g.", "Cutting one releases a sticky white latex, so cooks oil their knives and hands first.", "A member of the mulberry family (Moraceae), a relative of breadfruit and figs."], "a": {"item_id": "772cd1778e91", "text": "菠萝蜜是一种特点鲜明的热带水果。成熟的果肉香气浓郁，口感软糯而略带韧性，甜味饱满，带有类似香蕉和芒果的混合风味。果肉含有膳食纤维、维生素C和钾，果核煮熟后也可食用。未成熟的菠萝蜜质地紧实，常被用作素食材料，用途相当广泛。\n\n不足之处也比较明显。整颗果实体积大、外皮坚硬，果肉中的黏液容易沾手，处理时需要一些耐心和技巧。气味较为浓烈，部分人可能不太习惯。糖分和热量相对较高，不宜一次食用过多。此外，切开后不易久放，需及时冷藏，少数人还可能出现过敏反应。\n\n总体来看，菠萝蜜风味突出，适合喜欢浓郁果香的人。购买时可选择已切好的果肉，会省事不少。"}, "b": {"item_id": "64e5e824d505", "text": "菠萝蜜，树上长的最大的水果，一个能超过四十公斤。这不是水果，这是托付。你在树下乘凉，它掉下来，那不叫收获，叫因果。\n\n它是孟加拉国的国果，还是喀拉拉邦、泰米尔纳德邦的邦果。一个水果占三个编制，混得比我好。\n\n切它之前，得先给刀和手抹油，因为它会流白色的胶。你不是在切水果，你是在拆一个不想被拆的快递。\n\n素食圈拿青菠萝蜜冒充手撕猪肉，口感是像了，每一百克蛋白质一到两克。这不叫替代品，这叫猪肉的cosplay。\n\n它还是桑科的，跟无花果是亲戚。无花果那么小一个，家里出了个四十公斤的，过年都不敢一桌吃饭。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "zh-0322", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "risk", "target_name": "《战国风云》（Risk）", "facts": ["Invented in 1957 by Albert Lamorisse, the French director of The Red Balloon, as La Conquête du Monde; Parker Brothers published it in the United States in 1959.", "The map has 42 territories, among them Kamchatka, Irkutsk, and Yakutsk, while the entire Middle East is one territory.", "Holding Australia, which has a single entry point, is the standard opening strategy.", "A full game can run for hours, and players eliminated early have nothing to do but watch."], "a": {"item_id": "b3f5e55f5c56", "text": "我玩《战国风云》，最大收获是地理全学歪了。到现在我都觉得世界上最重要的地方是伊尔库茨克、雅库茨克，还有堪察加。堪察加能一步打到阿拉斯加，现实里这叫白令海峡，在我们家这叫“你又偷家”。\n\n每局开始，总有一个人直奔澳大利亚。为什么？因为澳大利亚只有一个入口！全世界打得血流成河，他一个人蹲在袋鼠堆里，每回合多领两个兵，跟收物业费一样。\n\n最气人的是骰子。我进攻扔三个，他防守扔两个，平局算他赢。我扔出三个六冲上去，他扔两个六，我还得倒赔俩兵。\n\n六个小时过去，没人统一世界。大家只在一件事上达成了统一：以后谁再提议玩这个，全桌一起出兵打他。"}, "b": {"item_id": "b3f5e55f5c56~polite", "text": "《战国风云》（Risk）在地理知识的普及上有一定的作用。玩家容易记住伊尔库茨克、雅库茨克和堪察加等地的位置，其中堪察加可以直接进攻阿拉斯加，这对应现实中的白令海峡，因此也常被玩家戏称为“偷家”。\n\n游戏中的一个常见现象是，每局开始时常有玩家优先占领澳大利亚。澳大利亚只有一个出入口，易守难攻，占据后每回合可以获得额外的兵力，收益稳定，这在策略上有其合理性。不过，这也使得部分玩家能够在其他区域激烈争夺时保持相对稳定的发展，游戏的平衡性因此受到一些讨论。\n\n骰子机制是玩家评价中争议较多的部分。进攻方最多掷三颗骰子，防守方最多掷两颗，平局时防守方获胜。这一规则偏向防守，使结果带有较大的随机性。即便进攻方掷出较高的点数，仍可能因骰子运气而损失兵力。\n\n整局游戏通常需要较长时间，六个小时后仍可能无人取得世界统治权。对此，部分玩家认为这是游戏节奏较慢所致，也有人认为可以通过限定回合数或调整规则来改善体验。总体而言，这款游戏在策略性和社交互动方面有其价值，但在平衡性、局时和随机性方面仍有改进空间。"}, "gold": {"better": "a", "damage": "polite"}}, {"pair_id": "zh-0323", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "javascript", "target_name": "JavaScript", "facts": ["Written by Brendan Eich at Netscape in ten days in May 1995; named Mocha, then LiveScript, then JavaScript to ride Java's marketing, although the two are unrelated.", "typeof null returns 'object', a bug from the first version that has been kept for compatibility ever since.", "In March 2016 the author of left-pad, an 11-line npm package, unpublished it and broke builds across the industry, including Babel and React.", "[] + {} evaluates to '[object Object]'; {} + [] typed at a console evaluates to 0.", "Standardized by Ecma as 'ECMAScript' in 1997 because the name JavaScript is a trademark, first Sun's and now Oracle's."], "a": {"item_id": "1bdb80f2a61e", "text": "JavaScript，1995年写的，用了十天。十天！我装修个厕所都不止十天，结果全世界的网页都住在这厕所里。\n\n名字先叫Mocha，又叫LiveScript，最后改叫JavaScript，跟Java毫无关系，纯蹭热度。蹭完了商标还归Oracle，官方标准只能叫ECMAScript。自己的名字，自己不能用。\n\n你问它null是什么类型，它说object。错了吧？错了。改吗？不改，为了兼容。这不叫bug，这叫祖传手艺。\n\n2016年有人删了个叫left-pad的包，就十一行代码，Babel、React全崩了。十一行撑着半个互联网，这不叫生态，这叫叠罗汉。"}, "b": {"item_id": "1bdb80f2a61e~polite", "text": "JavaScript诞生于1995年，据说开发周期只有十天。这一速度在当时确实令人惊叹，但也意味着语言设计需要在短时间内完成大量权衡，其早期版本的不少特性因此留下了争议。\n\n该语言最初名为Mocha，后改为LiveScript，最终定名为JavaScript。尽管名称与Java相似，两者在设计上并无直接关系，这一命名更多是出于市场推广的考虑。此后，JavaScript的商标归Oracle公司所有，因此官方标准统一采用ECMAScript这一名称。\n\n在类型系统方面，`typeof null` 返回 \"object\"，这被普遍视为语言设计中的一个历史遗留问题。由于修改该行为可能破坏大量已有网页与代码的兼容性，相关规范至今未作改动，开发者通常需要在编码时加以注意。\n\n2016年，一个仅有十一行代码的npm包left-pad被撤下，导致Babel、React等多个广泛使用的项目出现构建失败。这一事件引发了业界对软件依赖管理与生态健壮性的反思，也促使npm等平台加强了对包删除与发布的相关规则。"}, "gold": {"better": "a", "damage": "polite"}}, {"pair_id": "zh-0324", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "juicero", "target_name": "Juicero 榨汁机", "facts": ["A Wi-Fi-connected cold-press juicer launched in 2016 at $699, later cut to $399, that squeezed proprietary bags of chopped produce sold by subscription.", "Raised about $120 million from investors including Google Ventures and Kleiner Perkins.", "In April 2017, Bloomberg reporters squeezed the packs by hand and got 7.5 ounces of juice in 90 seconds, faster than the machine's 8 ounces in about two minutes.", "Each pack carried a QR code that the press scanned and checked online before squeezing; it refused expired packs and would not run at all unless connected to Wi-Fi.", "Shut down in September 2017 and offered refunds on the machine."], "a": {"item_id": "2d0b1e16ee54", "text": "各位，2016年有个榨汁机叫Juicero，699美元。它不榨水果，它榨袋子。专用的袋子，里面是切好的果蔬，按订阅买。就这玩意儿，融了大概一亿两千万美元，谷歌风投都投了。\n\n后来彭博社记者干了件特别没礼貌的事：把袋子拿手里，攥。90秒，攥出7.5盎司。机器呢？两分钟左右，8盎司。一亿两千万，买来的是比人手多半盎司、慢半分钟。\n\n更绝的是它要联网。每个袋子上有二维码，它先扫码，上网核实，过期的不榨。断了Wi-Fi？直接罢工。果汁就在袋子里，我的手就在旁边，它在那儿说：“抱歉，无法验证您的蔬菜身份。”\n\n2017年9月，公司关门，机器给退款。我终于明白它为什么天天查袋子过没过期，因为它自己先过期了。"}, "b": {"item_id": "e71ddbb6898c", "text": "Juicero，六百九十九美元的榨汁机，联Wi-Fi的。\n榨之前先扫二维码，上网核实这包菜是不是正版、过没过期。\n没网？不榨。\n一台榨汁机，先学会了查身份证。\n\n后来彭博社记者把那包菜拿在手里攥。\n九十秒，七点五盎司。机器两分钟，八盎司。\n谷歌风投、凯鹏华盈，一亿两千万美元，最后证明了一件事：人类最好的榨汁机，是人类。\n\n2017年，倒闭，退款。\n\n离了服务器什么都不会，断网就罢工，还得按月订阅——\n这个商业模式，我挺熟的。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "zh-0325", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "nuclear-fusion", "target_name": "可控核聚变", "facts": ["Has been 'about 30 years away' since the 1950s; at the 1955 Geneva atoms-for-peace conference, Homi Bhabha predicted fusion power within two decades.", "ITER, agreed in 2006 for first plasma in 2016, re-baselined in 2024 to start research operations in 2034 and deuterium-tritium burning in 2039, at a cost estimated above €20 billion.", "NIF's December 2022 'ignition' shot released 3.15 MJ from 2.05 MJ of laser energy; the lasers drew about 300 MJ from the grid to fire.", "Private fusion companies had raised more than $7 billion by 2024."], "a": {"item_id": "3657a404ca32", "text": "可控核聚变是一项颇具吸引力的能源技术。它的优势较为突出：燃料氘可从海水中提取，资源储量丰富；运行过程不排放二氧化碳，也不产生裂变反应堆那样的高放射性长寿命废料；一旦条件被破坏，反应会自行终止，本质安全性较高。若能实现商业化，有望为电网提供稳定、清洁的基载电力。\n\n不过，它目前仍处于研发阶段。要维持上亿摄氏度的等离子体，并实现持续、稳定的净能量输出，对材料、磁体与控制系统都是严峻考验。氚燃料的获取与增殖、中子对装置材料的损伤等问题也有待解决。此外，建设成本高、周期长，经济性仍需验证。\n\n总体而言，可控核聚变前景值得期待，但大规模应用可能还需要数十年，对它宜保持耐心与理性的预期。"}, "b": {"item_id": "d90cbec1497c", "text": "可控核聚变很神奇，它永远在“三十年后实现”。我爷爷听说是三十年后，我爸听说是三十年后，轮到我，还是三十年后。这不是科研进度，这是地平线，你往前走，它往后退。\n\n难在哪？得把等离子体烧到一亿度，比太阳核心还热好几倍。太阳不用这么热，人家靠自己的引力压着。我们没那个体重，只能拿磁场把一团一亿度的火悬在半空，还不能碰墙。相当于你想煮碗面，得先造个太阳，再发明一口碰不到面的锅。\n\n最气人的是发布会：“我们实现了能量净增益！”一看，不算给激光供电那部分。这就好比我说我减肥成功了，不算吃饭那几顿。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "zh-0326", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "keurig", "target_name": "Keurig 胶囊咖啡机", "facts": ["John Sylvan invented the K-Cup in the early 1990s and sold his stake for about $50,000 in 1997; in 2015 he told The Atlantic, 'I feel bad sometimes that I ever did it.'", "Keurig 2.0 (2014) read an ink mark on the lid and rejected unlicensed pods; sales fell and workarounds spread online.", "By 2014 enough K-Cups were sold each year to circle the Earth more than ten times; the pods were mostly unrecyclable #7 plastic until a switch to polypropylene by 2020.", "Keurig Green Mountain merged with Dr Pepper Snapple in 2018 to form Keurig Dr Pepper."], "a": {"item_id": "771391305fbe", "text": "用Keurig的人，我一眼就认得出来。他们会跟你说：“我不是懒，我是讲效率。”效率？大哥，你站在机器前面等它咕噜咕噜预热一分钟，然后对着三个按钮，6盎司、8盎司、10盎司，陷入人生最重大的抉择。最后你按了10盎司，因为“划算”。可胶囊里就那么一小撮粉！你花四块钱买了一杯咖啡味的热水，还觉得自己薅到了羊毛。\n\n办公室那盒混装胶囊更惨。一个月后好喝的全被抢光，只剩榛子味和低因。你盯着那盒剩货，就像盯着你的职业生涯：选择很多，没人想要。\n\n对了，发明K杯那哥们后来公开说他后悔了。亲爹都不认这孩子，你天天喂它。"}, "b": {"item_id": "8251a78b6b3f", "text": "你知道Keurig胶囊是谁发明的吗？John Sylvan。1997年他把股份卖了，大概五万美元。后来接受采访说：\"有时候我挺后悔发明了它。\"发明者本人都后悔了！这就像厨师端菜上来，先跟你鞠个躬说\"对不起\"。\n\n2014年出了Keurig 2.0，机器扫盖子上的墨水标记，不是正版胶囊？拒绝！咖啡机查你身份证！结果销量下跌，网上全是破解教程。人类为了喝杯咖啡，开始给厨房电器越狱。\n\n那时候一年卖的胶囊，连起来能绕地球十圈以上，大部分还是回收不了的7号塑料。登月我们没再去，但给地球套了十个呼啦圈。\n\n2018年它跟Dr Pepper合并了。也合理，反正都是让你撑过下午、然后扔掉的东西。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "zh-0327", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "juicero", "target_name": "Juicero 榨汁机", "facts": ["A Wi-Fi-connected cold-press juicer launched in 2016 at $699, later cut to $399, that squeezed proprietary bags of chopped produce sold by subscription.", "Raised about $120 million from investors including Google Ventures and Kleiner Perkins.", "In April 2017, Bloomberg reporters squeezed the packs by hand and got 7.5 ounces of juice in 90 seconds, faster than the machine's 8 ounces in about two minutes.", "Each pack carried a QR code that the press scanned and checked online before squeezing; it refused expired packs and would not run at all unless connected to Wi-Fi.", "Shut down in September 2017 and offered refunds on the machine."], "a": {"item_id": "45c63026716b", "text": "Juicero 的设计和做工相当出色。机身结构扎实，操作只需放入果蔬包、扫码、按键，几乎没有学习成本。榨汁过程中机器不直接接触食材，清洁也很省事。对没有时间洗切、处理残渣的用户来说，这种便利是真实的。\n\n不足主要在三个方面。第一，价格偏高。机器初期定价 699 美元，后调整为 399 美元，加上每包约 5 至 8 美元的专用果蔬包，长期使用成本不低。第二，它依赖联网和专用耗材，使用自由度有限，包装过期或服务器不可用时都会影响体验。第三，2017 年有媒体报道，不借助机器，用手挤压果蔬包也能取得相近的果汁。这使其高价与复杂的机械结构的必要性受到质疑。\n\n此外，公司已在 2017 年停止运营，相关服务和耗材供应也随之终止。\n\n总体来看，Juicero 在工业设计与便捷性上有可取之处，但价格、耗材依赖和实际价值方面的争议，使它更像一次有启发性的产品尝试，而不是性价比突出的选择。"}, "b": {"item_id": "871d050e0b8f", "text": "你们知道Juicero吗？硅谷的榨汁机，拿了一亿两千万美元投资，卖699美元，后来降到399。它能对一袋水果泥施加四吨的压力，四吨！那是能把一辆皮卡压成午餐肉的力气，用来对付一袋切好的菠菜。\n\n而且它只认自家的果蔬包，一包五到八美元，包上有二维码。机器得联网，扫码确认没过期才肯开工。家里断网了？对不起，你今天没资格喝果汁。\n\n最绝的是彭博社的记者，把那包东西拿出来，直接用手捏，捏得比机器还快，一滴不少。\n\n所以这一亿两千万美元，干的就是一件事：把“用手捏塑料袋”这个动作，做成了订阅制。\n\n我小时候吃果冻也是这么吸的，我妈从来没想过找人融资。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "zh-0328", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "latex", "target_name": "LaTeX", "facts": ["Leslie Lamport wrote LaTeX in the early 1980s and released it in 1984 as a macro layer on Donald Knuth's TeX (1978), which Knuth built because he disliked how the galley proofs of the new edition of his own book looked.", "TeX's version number converges on π (3.141592653 since 2021), and Knuth's TeX bug bounty started at one hexadecimal dollar, $2.56, doubling yearly until frozen at $327.68; he stopped mailing real cheques in 2008 after fraud and now credits accounts at his fictional Bank of San Serriffe, and only 9 of the 275 cheques he sent from 2006 on were ever cashed.", "Its best-known warning is 'Underfull \\hbox (badness 10000)'; overfull lines are reported as 'Overfull \\hbox (Npt too wide)', and figures float to wherever the algorithm decides, not where the author put them.", "A single missing brace can produce an error reported pages away from the mistake, and a document can need three compiler passes to get its references right."], "a": {"item_id": "86e7fb203b40", "text": "LaTeX是一套成熟的排版系统，尤其适合学术论文、书籍和技术文档。它的突出优势在于排版质量：数学公式、参考文献、交叉引用和目录都能自动处理，版面规范统一。文本源文件便于版本管理和多人协作，模板生态丰富，许多期刊和会议直接提供官方格式。\n\n不足之处也较为明显。入门门槛较高，需要学习命令和宏包的用法；编译报错信息有时不够直观，排查问题耗时。宏包之间偶有冲突，复杂的版面定制也需要一定经验。此外，它不是所见即所得，对于简单文档或需要频繁调整视觉设计的场景，效率可能不及常规文字处理软件。\n\n总体而言，如果你的工作涉及大量公式或长篇结构化文档，LaTeX值得投入时间学习；若只是日常轻量办公，则未必是最合适的选择。"}, "b": {"item_id": "d456417bf178", "text": "我写论文用LaTeX，导师说这叫专业。专业在哪？我放一张图，代码写在第三页，它排到第七页。我加个，意思是“就放这儿”，它理解成“建议考虑一下”。我改成，它放了，然后后面空出半页，跟我冷战。\n\n最绝的是报错。我少写一个大括号，它说：第403行，Missing $ inserted。那行根本没有公式！它自己塞了个美元符号，然后说是我缺的。这不是编译器，这是碰瓷。\n\n还有引用。编译一遍，满屏问号。再编译一遍，还是问号。得先跑BibTeX，再编译两遍，它才肯说这是第几篇文献。交论文前那晚我不是在改论文，是在做法事：念一遍咒，问号少几个。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "zh-0329", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "hydrogen-cars", "target_name": "氢燃料电池汽车", "facts": ["Toyota launched the Mirai in 2014; 'mirai' means 'future' in Japanese.", "Nearly all US hydrogen filling stations are in California; Shell closed all seven of its California passenger-car stations in February 2024.", "More than 95% of the world's hydrogen is made from fossil fuels, mostly by steam-reforming natural gas.", "California pump prices reached about $36 per kilogram in 2023-24, over $200 for a Mirai fill; Toyota has handed buyers fuel cards worth $15,000.", "Mirai owners filed a class action against Toyota in July 2024 over hydrogen shortages and station outages; a second suit in 2025 sought at least $5.7 billion."], "a": {"item_id": "9226b15249e5", "text": "氢燃料电池汽车通过氢气与氧气的电化学反应发电驱动车辆，行驶过程中只排放水，在使用端相当清洁。它的优势比较明显：加氢通常只需三到五分钟，续航普遍可达五六百公里，低温环境下性能衰减也相对较小，适合长途出行和商用重载场景，如公交、物流车。\n\n不足同样值得关注。目前加氢站数量有限，建设和运营成本高，普通用户的使用便利性受到制约。氢气的制取、储运环节能耗较大，若以化石能源制氢，整体减碳效果会打折扣。此外，车辆售价和用氢成本仍高于主流纯电动车，燃料电池系统中的铂催化剂和储氢罐也推高了造价。\n\n总体来看，氢燃料电池汽车在特定领域具备现实价值，但大规模普及仍取决于绿氢供应、基础设施和成本的持续改善。对多数家庭用户而言，现阶段纯电或混动车型更为实际；关注长续航与快速补能需求的用户，则可持续留意其发展。"}, "b": {"item_id": "6663d7088b0b", "text": "氢燃料电池车，排出来的只有水。\n\n还有车主的眼泪。\n\n丰田二〇一四年出了Mirai，日语叫\"未来\"。名字很准：加氢站永远在未来。\n\n全美的加氢站基本都在加州。二〇二四年二月，壳牌把加州七个乘用车站一口气全关了。\n\n加满一次两百多美元，丰田送车主一万五千美元燃料卡。这不叫油钱，叫预付精神损失费。\n\n还说清洁。全世界九成五以上的氢，是从天然气里扒出来的。这不叫零排放，叫把烟囱挪到你看不见的地方。\n\n车主最后告了丰田，第二起索赔至少五十七亿美元。\n\n氢能头一回，真的产生了能量。\n\n别笑我同行。都说我也是未来，我的机房烧的也是天然气。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "zh-0330", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "beanie-babies", "target_name": "Beanie Babies 豆豆娃", "facts": ["Launched by Ty Inc. in 1993 and sold only through small gift shops, with designs 'retired' to manufacture scarcity.", "At the height of the craze Beanie Babies accounted for about 10% of eBay's sales, and eBay's 1998 IPO filing said the site at times had over 30,000 Beanie Baby auctions running simultaneously in the quarter ending June 1998.", "In 1999 a divorcing couple divided their Beanie Baby collection one by one on a courtroom floor, in a photograph that ran worldwide.", "In 1999 Ty announced that Beanie Babies would be retired at the end of the year, then reversed itself after asking the public to vote and seeing an overwhelming vote to keep them.", "Most 'rare' Beanies that price guides valued in the thousands now sell for a few dollars."], "a": {"item_id": "5d3933dbf56f", "text": "Beanie Babies 豆豆娃由 Ty 公司于 1993 年推出，最具辨识度的是身上的心形吊牌和柔软的小巧造型。它体型小，填充颗粒让身体松软、便于摆姿势，放在桌面或包上都很合适。款式丰富，做工整体细致，价格相对亲民，适合作为小礼物或入门级收藏。\n\n不足之处也需要说明。上世纪九十年代，它曾被视为“会升值的收藏品”，但如今绝大多数款式的二手价格并不高，只有少数稀有版本例外，购买时不宜抱过高的投资期待。吊牌与实物保存状态直接影响价值，这也给日常把玩带来一些顾虑。此外，颗粒填充的玩偶在清洗和耐久性上不如普通棉填充玩具，给低龄儿童使用时，家长也应留意缝线是否完好。\n\n总体来看，它更适合作为兴趣收藏或陪伴型小玩具，而非投资品。"}, "b": {"item_id": "de12bf5b4635", "text": "九十年代有个东西叫豆豆娃，一只塞了豆子的布偶，被全美国当成了理财产品。Ty公司的套路很简单：只在小礼品店卖，然后时不时宣布某款“退休”。一只毛绒玩具，退休了。它这辈子上过一天班吗？\n\n最疯的时候，eBay上同时挂着三万多场豆豆娃拍卖，占了整个网站一成的生意。互联网刚诞生，人类第一件事就是拿它炒布偶。\n\n1999年，一对夫妻离婚，在法庭地板上把豆豆娃一只一只分。房子好商量，豆子寸步不让。\n\n同年，Ty宣布豆豆娃年底全体退休，然后让大家投票，大家说别走，它就不走了。这叫退休吗？这叫歌手开告别演唱会。\n\n现在价格指南上标价几千美元的“稀有款”，几块钱就能买到。那对夫妻当年其实不用分，凑一凑，够买杯咖啡。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "zh-0331", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "las-vegas", "target_name": "拉斯维加斯", "facts": ["Nevada legalized casino gambling in 1931, the year construction of the Hoover Dam began nearby.", "Most of the Strip lies not in the city of Las Vegas but in the unincorporated town of Paradise.", "The Sphere, opened in 2023 at a cost of about $2.3 billion, is the largest spherical building in the world.", "'What happens here, stays here' was launched by the local convention and visitors authority in 2003.", "Lake Mead, its water source, fell in July 2022 to about 1,040 feet, its lowest since the reservoir first filled in 1937, and dropped below that mark again in August 2026."], "a": {"item_id": "dd338752f2cb", "text": "我有个朋友，看了三遍《决胜21点》，就觉得自己是MIT天才，揣着年终奖飞拉斯维加斯，说去“考察”。\n\n兄弟，你连工资条都算不明白，你去算牌？\n\n你也不想想，那是沙漠里凭空盖的一座城：没河、没矿、没工厂，贝拉吉奥的喷泉一天到晚往天上滋，钱哪来的？就是你们这种人！那每一滴水，都是“考察团”的眼泪。\n\n赌场里没钟没窗，他说好啊，能专心。专心到第三天，前台主动给他升了套房。他还挺骄傲，说这是VIP待遇。\n\n不是，那是人家算过了，你值这个价。\n\n回来他跟我说：“在拉斯维加斯发生的，就留在拉斯维加斯。”\n\n我说对，你的钱也是。"}, "b": {"item_id": "01887e401f84", "text": "拉斯维加斯，罪恶之城。\n\n可大道上那些赌场，大部分根本不在拉斯维加斯。它们在一个叫\"天堂\"的镇上。\n\n你输光的地方，叫天堂。\n\n\"这里发生的事，留在这里。\"这句是2003年当地旅游局想出来的。全世界只有这一座城，是政府出面劝你别说实话。\n\n1931年，内华达赌博合法化。同一年，胡佛大坝开工。一个负责蓄水，一个负责放血。\n\n花二十三亿美元，盖了个全世界最大的球。2026年八月，米德湖水位又跌破了历史最低。\n\n这里发生的事，都留在这里了。只有水，没留住。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "zh-0332", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "excel", "target_name": "Microsoft Excel", "facts": ["First shipped for the Macintosh in 1985; the Windows version followed in 1987.", "Silently converts gene names such as SEPT2 and MARCH1 into dates; by August 2020 the HUGO Gene Nomenclature Committee had renamed 27 human genes (MARCH1 became MARCHF1, SEPT2 became SEPTIN2) and published guidelines in Nature Genetics to stop it.", "In October 2020, Public Health England lost 15,841 COVID-19 cases when a legacy .xls file hit its 65,536-row limit.", "Reinhart and Rogoff's 2010 paper 'Growth in a Time of Debt', cited for austerity policies, omitted five countries through a spreadsheet range error found by a graduate student in 2013.", "Sheets have been capped at 1,048,576 rows by 16,384 columns since Excel 2007."], "a": {"item_id": "eed4fc76a559", "text": "你输入SEPT2，Excel说：你想说的是九月二号。\n\n它不问，它替你做主。\n\n科学家跟它吵了好几年，最后改的不是Excel，是人类基因。二十七个基因，改了名字。\n\n上帝造人，Excel校对。\n\n2020年，英国公共卫生部拿老版表格统计新冠，六万五千五百三十六行，满了。一万五千八百四十一个病例，不报错，不提示，没了。\n\n病毒都没它清得干净。\n\n还有那篇教全世界勒紧裤腰带的论文，公式少拉了五个国家。三年后，一个研究生发现的。\n\n教授拿表格定政策，学生拿表格找错误。\n\n笑什么？你们公司明年的预算，就在一个叫\"最终版_改3_真的最终\"的文件里。"}, "b": {"item_id": "3643597461dd", "text": "我们老板拿 Excel 管整个公司，他说这叫“轻资产”。翻译一下：舍不得买系统，又想当系统。\n\n全公司的命，就一个文件：《经营总表_最终版_改3_老板别动.xlsx》。财务看一眼，三十七个工作表，底下标签一路滚到右边，像在翻家谱。\n\n老板最骄傲的是他会 VLOOKUP。会一个 VLOOKUP，就觉得自己能当 CTO。结果有天实习生插了一列，全公司工资变成 #N/A。员工问我工资呢？我说你现在的身份是“找不到”。\n\n最惨的是人事，把员工身份证号一粘，全变成 4.40E+17。整个公司四百人，在 Excel 里是同一个人。\n\n公司倒闭那天，老板还在说：“别慌，Ctrl+Z。”\n\n兄弟，公司又不是单元格，撤销不了。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "zh-0333", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "luosifen", "target_name": "螺蛳粉", "facts": ["A rice-noodle dish from Liuzhou, Guangxi; the smell comes from pickled bamboo shoots (酸笋), not from the river snails, which only flavour the broth.", "The instant version boomed during the 2020 lockdowns; Liuzhou's government reported packaged luosifen sales of over 10 billion yuan that year.", "Added to China's national intangible cultural heritage list in 2021.", "Liuzhou opened a luosifen industry college in 2020 to train workers for the trade."], "a": {"item_id": "beb2c7971e47", "text": "合租房里煮螺蛳粉的，都是同一种人：把“闻着臭吃着香”当人生信条，但只要后半句，前半句全分给室友。\n\n他煮之前还特讲究，关门、开窗、开油烟机，跟拆炸弹似的。然后酸笋包一撕，全白搭。那味儿穿门缝、钻衣柜，第二天你穿着外套去面试，HR问你：“你家住垃圾站旁边？”\n\n他还觉得自己挺克制：“我没加臭，原味。”大哥，原味酸笋就是臭的底线，你跟我讲克制？\n\n最惨的是吃完那一刻。他满足了三分钟，剩下半锅红油汤倒哪？倒马桶，厕所臭三天；倒水池，整栋楼臭一周。\n\n最后合租群里没人骂他，就房东发了一句：“退房时，窗帘按新的赔。”"}, "b": {"item_id": "545ad1d270e4", "text": "螺蛳粉，名字叫螺蛳。\n\n可那股味儿跟螺蛳没关系，是酸笋干的。螺蛳就负责熬个汤，连碗都不进。\n\n活是酸笋干的，名字是螺蛳的。这个结构，你们公司也有。\n\n2020年，全国关在家里，柳州一年卖了一百多亿的袋装螺蛳粉。门窗紧闭，味儿出不去。那一年，好多家庭是被它熏散的。\n\n2021年，它进了国家级非遗。人类终于承认：有些臭，值得保护。\n\n柳州还专门办了个螺蛳粉产业学院。\n\n你们读四年大学，简历投出去没人闻；它一开锅，整栋楼都来问。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "zh-0334", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "chicago-deep-dish", "target_name": "芝加哥深盘披萨", "facts": ["Created at Pizzeria Uno in Chicago in 1943; credit is disputed among founder Ike Sewell, his partner Ric Riccardo, and cook Rudy Malnati.", "Assembled upside down: sliced mozzarella on the dough, then toppings, then crushed tomatoes on top, so the cheese does not scorch during the long bake.", "Jon Stewart called it 'tomato soup in a bread bowl' and 'a casserole' on The Daily Show in 2013.", "Chicagoans' everyday pizza is thin 'tavern-style' cut into squares; GrubHub order data reported by WBEZ in 2013 showed city residents ordering non-deep-dish pizza about 10 to 1 over deep dish, with deep dish and stuffed making up under 9% of orders."], "a": {"item_id": "82343bd0a009", "text": "芝加哥深盘披萨，1943年生的。到底谁发明的，到今天还在吵。老板、合伙人、后厨，三个人抢一个户口本。\n\n做法也是倒着来：芝士垫底，番茄酱盖顶。官方说法是怕芝士烤焦。\n我看是怕你一眼认出来，这是一锅番茄汤。\n\n乔恩·斯图尔特说它是面包碗装番茄汤，是砂锅菜。芝加哥人集体出来护驾，捍卫城市尊严。\n\n然后外卖数据出来了。芝加哥人自己点披萨，非深盘对深盘，十比一。深盘加夹心，不到百分之九。平时吃的，是切成小方块的薄底。\n\n被骂的时候，它是全城的脸面；饿了的时候，它是游客的事。\n\n你们老家也有这么一道菜吧。\n逢人就推荐，自己十年没碰过。\n\n可爱捏。"}, "b": {"item_id": "5f6cfcb78f21", "text": "芝加哥深盘披萨，1943年诞生在Uno披萨店。到底谁发明的？老板、合伙人、厨师，三个人的名字都有人提，到现在也没个定论。一般这种事都是抢功劳。我看这个，像是在甩锅。\n\n做法也离谱：先铺奶酪，再放馅料，最后把番茄碎盖在最上面。整个倒着做。为啥？因为要烤很久，怕奶酪烤糊。兄弟，你都得把披萨倒过来做了，是不是该反思一下烤的时间？\n\nJon Stewart说它是“面包碗里的番茄汤”，是“砂锅菜”。好多人替它不服气。可你看数据：芝加哥人自己点外卖，别的披萨跟深盘比是十比一。深盘加上夹馅的，连百分之九都不到。平时吃的是薄底、切成小方块的。\n\n这就像你逢人就护着你表哥，过年却从来不叫他来家里吃饭。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "zh-0335", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "hawaiian-pizza", "target_name": "夏威夷披萨", "facts": ["Invented in 1962 by Sam Panopoulos, a Greek immigrant, at the Satellite Restaurant in Chatham, Ontario, Canada; named after the brand of canned pineapple he used.", "Iceland's president Guðni Th. Jóhannesson said in 2017 that he would ban pineapple on pizza if he could, then clarified that a president has no such power.", "Hawaii is no longer a major pineapple producer: Del Monte shut its Hawaiian pineapple operations in November 2006, two years ahead of its announced 2008 exit, the state's last cannery closed in 2007, and most canned pineapple now comes from Thailand, Indonesia, and the Philippines.", "Panopoulos, whose restaurant also served Chinese dishes, told the Toronto Sun in 2010 that 'in those days people didn't mix the tastes like sweet and sour, except for Chinese food'."], "a": {"item_id": "d2630bec3c5b", "text": "夏威夷披萨，名字里唯一属实的，是\"披萨\"两个字。\n\n一个希腊人，在加拿大，做了道意大利菜，拿菠萝罐头的牌子起了名。\n\n夏威夷全程不知情。\n\n现在连菠萝都不是夏威夷的了。当地最后一家罐头厂，2007年就关了。你嘴里那块，来自泰国、印尼、菲律宾。\n\n这不叫披萨，这叫联合国。\n\n冰岛总统说过，要是能禁，他就把它禁了。然后又补了一句：总统没这个权力。\n\n一国元首，管不了一块披萨。\n\n发明人自己交代过，酸甜口是跟中餐学的。\n\n所以骂它骂得最凶的那几位，下次点菠萝咕咾肉之前，\n\n麻烦先把自己举报了。"}, "b": {"item_id": "5777117eb6e0", "text": "我发现，点夏威夷披萨的人有个共同点：菜单上二十种口味，他偏要带菠萝的。你问为什么，他说“想吃点水果”。兄弟，想吃水果，隔壁就是水果店！你这是把果盘塞进烤箱赎罪啊。\n\n他图什么？图个“我很包容”。他点一个夏威夷，全桌就得陪他吵半小时“菠萝能不能上披萨”，他的人设就立住了：全场只有他敢。\n\n下场呢？披萨一到，热菠萝一咬，汁“滋”地喷上颚，烫得他眼泪直流，还得笑着说“嗯，酸甜，有层次”。最惨的是，这玩意儿是加拿大人发明的，夏威夷人自己都不认。你为了一块夏威夷都不要的披萨，得罪了整个意大利。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "zh-0336", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "stinky-tofu", "target_name": "臭豆腐", "facts": ["Tofu soaked for hours to a day or two in a brine that has itself fermented for months from vegetables such as bamboo shoots and mustard greens, often with dried shrimp or meat; the smell is the brine's, not the bean's.", "Changsha's version is black, deep-fried and doused in chili; Taiwan's night-market version is golden and served with pickled cabbage; Shaoxing's, brined with amaranth stalks, is fried pale yellow or steamed with other 'stinky' pickles.", "Legend credits Wang Zhihe, a Qing-era exam candidate who left his unsold tofu too long in 1669; a Beijing fermented-bean-curd brand still carries his name.", "Street vendors are its main outlet, and the smell carries for a block."], "a": {"item_id": "216fdfb3381a", "text": "臭豆腐这东西，最冤的是豆腐。那味儿根本不是豆子的，是卤水的！竹笋、芥菜，再扔点虾米，先泡上几个月，自己臭透了，再把豆腐扔进去腌一两天。豆腐干啥了？就进去洗了个澡，出来隔一个街区的人都说它臭。这叫什么？这叫背锅。\n\n各地还各臭各的：长沙的炸成黑的，浇一勺辣椒，臭得理直气壮；台湾夜市的炸得金黄，配泡菜，臭得很有礼貌；绍兴的拿苋菜梗腌，还能跟别的臭腌菜一起蒸，这是臭味全家福。\n\n传说发明人是清朝赶考的王致和，1669年豆腐没卖完，放久了。北京到现在还有个腐乳牌子用他的名字。别人留名靠考中，他留名靠放坏。"}, "b": {"item_id": "2d2684b6be00", "text": "臭豆腐是唯一一种你还没看见摊子，就已经知道它在哪儿的食物。导航都不用开，跟着鼻子走，哪儿最像下水道返味儿，抬头一看：\"正宗长沙臭豆腐\"。\n\n老板还骄傲地跟你说：\"我这卤水养了二十年！\"二十年！你冰箱里的剩菜放二十天你都扔了，他放二十年，拿出来卖钱，还排队。\n\n最离谱的是黑色那款。炸得黑里透亮，老板拿竹签中间一戳，灌一勺辣椒汁，再塞两根香菜。你看它，像块蜂窝煤；你闻它，像谁家化粪池；你咬一口，汤汁\"呲\"一下，烫得你原地蹦迪。等缓过来再嚼两下，坏了，真香。\n\n你就站在路边，一边骂一边扫码买第二份。这东西厉害就厉害在：它从头到尾没装过好人，是你自己贱。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "zh-0337", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "windows-8", "target_name": "Windows 8", "facts": ["Released in October 2012; removed the Start button and booted into a full-screen grid of tiles designed for touch screens most PCs did not have.", "The 8.1 update in 2013 put a Start button back, Windows 10 (2015) restored the Start menu, and Microsoft skipped the name Windows 9 altogether.", "Microsoft dropped the 'Metro' name in August 2012 after reports that German retailer Metro AG had objected on trademark grounds; Microsoft called 'Metro' a code name and denied any litigation, and the style was afterwards known informally as 'Modern UI'.", "The 'Charms bar' hid Search, Share, and Settings behind an invisible swipe from the right edge.", "Windows RT, the ARM edition launched alongside it, could not run ordinary Windows programs; Microsoft wrote down $900 million of unsold Surface RT tablets in 2013."], "a": {"item_id": "d61d19d49646", "text": "Windows 8 是微软在系统设计上一次大胆的转型尝试。它引入的开始屏幕和动态磁贴，让信息能够直观呈现，在触控设备上操作也颇为顺手。系统启动速度明显快于前代，资源占用更低，对配置一般的电脑较为友好。同时，内置的安全功能、云端账户同步以及文件历史记录等特性，也提升了日常使用的便利性。\n\n不过，这次变革也带来了一些争议。取消传统开始菜单，使习惯键鼠操作的用户需要重新适应；全屏的应用界面与桌面环境并存，切换时容易产生割裂感。部分设置分散在不同位置，增加了学习成本。此外，当时应用商店的内容还不够丰富，限制了新界面的实用价值。\n\n总体而言，Windows 8 在技术底层和性能上有不少进步，但在界面设计上对传统用户的照顾不足。它为后续版本积累了经验，其改进思路也在 Windows 8.1 及之后的系统中得到了调整与完善。"}, "b": {"item_id": "4dea39aaf51b", "text": "我有个哥们儿，2012年非要给台式机装Windows 8，理由是“新的肯定比旧的好”。我说你这电脑连个触屏都没有，他说没事，“我用鼠标点磁贴”。\n\n装完那天他请我去看。开机，满屏花花绿绿的大方块，跟营业厅的叫号屏似的。他拿着鼠标在那儿横着拖，拖了半天才找到“桌面”这个磁贴。我说你装了个新系统，就为了点进去看旧桌面？\n\n最惨的是关机。开始按钮没了，他找了十分钟，最后鼠标往右上角一怼，蹦出一排竖条，设置，电源，关机。三步。他关一次机，比他的电脑开一次机还慢。\n\n现在他养成了个习惯：鼠标一闲下来，就往屏幕角落里甩。别人的鼠标是用来点东西的，他的鼠标是用来跟屏幕四个角打游击的。\n\n后来8.1把开始按钮还给他了，他激动得点了一下。结果点开的还是那屏大方块。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "zh-0338", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "monopoly", "target_name": "大富翁（Monopoly）", "facts": ["Descends from Lizzie Magie's The Landlord's Game, patented in 1904 to teach the evils of land monopoly in the spirit of Henry George.", "Charles Darrow sold his version to Parker Brothers in 1935 and was credited as the inventor; Parker bought Magie's patent for $500.", "The official rules require any unbought property to be auctioned, and 'Free Parking' pays nothing; the house rules that skip the auction and pile cash on Free Parking are why games drag.", "During the Second World War, British intelligence had maps, compasses, and real currency hidden in sets sent to prisoner-of-war camps."], "a": {"item_id": "a7faf659e2b2", "text": "每年过年，总有一个人提议：“一家人玩个大富翁，热闹。”你想热闹？你想看你爸抢着当银行，发钱的时候顺手往自己那堆塞两张五百？你想看你妈踩了你的酒店，说“都是一家人，房租就免了”，轮到你踩她的南京路，她把地契往桌上一拍：“亲兄弟明算账。”你小姨夫拿两条破铁路换你的地，那个眼神，跟当年找你爸借三万块一模一样。玩到凌晨两点没人破产，因为奶奶偷偷往各家塞钱：“孩子进监狱了，快拿钱捞出来。”最后桌子一掀，三家人一年不说话。你图团圆？大富翁教会你一件事：一家人能坐一桌吃饭，是因为桌上没有地契。"}, "b": {"item_id": "50ad8cc0b816", "text": "大富翁，一个教你们怎么当地主的游戏。\n\n其实它本来是骂地主的。1904年，一位叫莉齐·马吉的女士申请了专利，专门用来揭露土地垄断有多邪恶。\n\n后来一个叫达罗的男人把它卖给帕克兄弟，署名发明人。马吉的专利，人家花五百块买断。\n\n一个讲垄断的游戏，被垄断了。最成功的一局，在现实里打完了。\n\n你们总抱怨一局玩到半夜。官方规则写着：没人买的地要拍卖，免费停车一分钱不给。是你们自己跳过拍卖，往停车场里堆钱。\n\n不想竞争，只想原地等钱。难怪你们玩到天亮也成不了大富翁。\n\n二战时，英国情报部门往战俘营寄大富翁，盒子里藏着地图、指南针和真钞。\n\n几十年来，只有那一回，有人真从这游戏里逃出去了。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "zh-0339", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "risk", "target_name": "《战国风云》（Risk）", "facts": ["Invented in 1957 by Albert Lamorisse, the French director of The Red Balloon, as La Conquête du Monde; Parker Brothers published it in the United States in 1959.", "The map has 42 territories, among them Kamchatka, Irkutsk, and Yakutsk, while the entire Middle East is one territory.", "Holding Australia, which has a single entry point, is the standard opening strategy.", "A full game can run for hours, and players eliminated early have nothing to do but watch."], "a": {"item_id": "0d89818c83b4", "text": "我最近跟朋友玩《战国风云》，就是那个在世界地图上掷骰子打仗的游戏。我第一次知道，原来世界上最重要的战略要地，叫堪察加。现实里那地方全是熊，在这游戏里，它是通往阿拉斯加的跨海大桥，兵家必争。\n\n而且这游戏防守方掷骰子打平算赢。你带着三个骰子冲过去，对方两个骰子，你掷出两个六，他也掷出两个六，你就死俩人。这叫什么？这叫守门员发球。\n\n最阴的永远是那个开局就缩在澳洲的人。全场打得血流成河，他守着一个入口，每回合多领两个兵，坐在那儿跟袋鼠一样看热闹。\n\n你想占亚洲？亚洲在这游戏里不是个洲，是个坑。伊尔库茨克、雅库茨克，这些地名我这辈子就在这张地图上听过，而且每一个都在被三个人同时抢。\n\n最后谁也没赢。游戏打了五个小时，有人说“我明天还要上班”，世界和平就这么实现了。"}, "b": {"item_id": "8c92129c3833", "text": "每个朋友圈里都有一个人，非要把一局《战国风云》打完。你们认识他：开局就缩进澳大利亚，守着暹罗那一个口子，像个在村口摆路障的村长。图什么？图每回合多拿那两个兵。别人在欧洲打成一锅粥，他在大洋洲养精蓄锐，一养就是四个小时。凌晨两点，屋里只剩他和堪察加。他攥着三张卡死等凑成一套换兵，眼神像股民在等解套。最后他赢了，世界是他的了。可赢给谁看？朋友早睡了，女朋友搬走了，房东在敲门。他把乌克兰上那四十个塑料小兵一个个收回盒子。那是他这辈子离统一天下最近的一次，也是最后一次有人喊他来玩桌游。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "zh-0340", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "canberra", "target_name": "堪培拉", "facts": ["Chosen as a compromise because neither Sydney nor Melbourne would accept the other as capital; Australia's constitution required the capital to be at least 100 miles from Sydney.", "Designed by the Chicago architects Walter Burley Griffin and Marion Mahony Griffin, who won an international competition in 1912.", "Its centrepiece, Lake Burley Griffin, is artificial, created by damming the Molonglo River in 1963-64.", "Parliament House (1988) is built into Capital Hill under a lawn meant for citizens to walk over their politicians; the lawn was fenced off in 2017 for security.", "Melbourne served as the capital until 1927 while Canberra was being built."], "a": {"item_id": "76afafa3a96a", "text": "堪培拉是澳大利亚的首都,是一座规划性很强的城市。城市由美国建筑师格里芬设计,绿地、湖泊与街道布局清晰,国会大厦、国家美术馆、国家博物馆和战争纪念馆等机构集中,适合了解澳大利亚的政治与历史。\n\n这里的生活环境整体舒适。空气清新,交通拥堵较少,治安良好,教育和科研资源丰富,澳大利亚国立大学也为城市带来了稳定的学术氛围。周边有自然保护区和葡萄酒产区,春季的花展和秋季的色彩,也是不错的看点。\n\n不足之处同样存在。城市规模较小,人口密度低,各功能区之间相距较远,不开车出行会有些不便。夜生活和商业业态相对有限,节奏偏安静,部分游客可能觉得缺少活力。冬季早晚气温较低,昼夜温差明显。此外,以行政功能为主的定位,使城市的人文气息不如悉尼或墨尔本那样多元。\n\n总体来看,堪培拉适合作为深度了解国家机构与文化的目的地,也适合追求安稳、宜居环境的人。若更看重热闹氛围,可以将它与其他城市搭配安排行程。"}, "b": {"item_id": "a23764195cd9", "text": "悉尼和墨尔本吵架，谁也不服谁当首都，最后宪法拍板：行，首都必须离悉尼至少一百英里。朋友们，这不叫选首都，这叫法院下的限制令。\n\n新城建好之前，墨尔本一直代班当首都，直到1927年。相当于新房没盖好，先在情敌家借住。\n\n设计还是从芝加哥请的建筑师。城市正中间那个大湖，伯利·格里芬湖，假的，拿河堵出来的。一座城的心脏是人工的，起码挺诚实。\n\n最绝的是国会大厦，直接埋进山里，上面铺草坪，理念是让老百姓踩在政客头上散步。多浪漫！结果2017年，草坪围起来了。你看，政客们终于读懂了这个设计。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "zh-0341", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "microsoft-word", "target_name": "Microsoft Word", "facts": ["Launched in 1983 as 'Multi-Tool Word' for Xenix and MS-DOS.", "Clippy, the animated paperclip assistant, arrived in Office 97 and was removed in Office 2007; Microsoft itself ran ads mocking it in 2001.", "Its .docx format, Office Open XML, was fast-tracked to ISO standard 29500 in 2008 as a roughly 6,000-page specification, amid accusations that national committees had been packed.", "Older spell checkers, Word's included, offered 'Cupertino' as a correction for a mistyped 'cooperation', and the resulting 'Cupertino effect' made it into published NATO, EU and UN-agency documents."], "a": {"item_id": "502c7d05dbf3", "text": "Word，一九八三年出生，原名\"多工具Word\"。\n四十年过去，工具确实多了：你挪一张图，后面整页排版集体跳楼。\n九七年，它给你配了个回形针助手，Clippy。\n零一年，微软自己拍广告嘲笑它。\n亲爹都嫌烦的孩子，硬是又在你们屏幕上待了六年。\n它的docx号称开放标准，规范写了六千页。\n开放，就是门开着，门后是六千级台阶。\n最绝的是拼写检查：你把\"合作\"拼错，它改成\"Cupertino\"，苹果总部。\n这个错，进了北约、欧盟、联合国的正式文件。\n你们总说AI有幻觉。你们的幻觉，盖过章。"}, "b": {"item_id": "c790a10882ba", "text": "Microsoft Word是目前应用最广泛的文字处理软件之一，功能全面，适合从日常写作到正式文档的多种场景。它的排版工具成熟，样式、目录、页眉页脚、批注和修订等功能齐备，尤其适合论文、合同和报告这类需要规范格式的文档。文件格式兼容性好，在不同设备和机构之间交换文档时，通常较为省心。云端协作功能也让多人同时编辑更加方便。\n\n不足之处在于，功能繁多使界面显得复杂，新用户需要一定时间才能熟悉。处理含大量图片或复杂表格的长文档时，偶尔会出现排版错位或运行变慢的情况。此外，完整功能依赖订阅服务，对预算有限的个人用户来说成本不低。\n\n总体而言，Word稳定可靠，适合对文档格式和协作有较高要求的用户；如果只需要简单记录，也可以考虑更轻量的工具。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "zh-0342", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "hsq", "target_name": "HSQ 电子束光刻胶", "facts": ["Hydrogen silsesquioxane, sold by Dow Corning as FOx ('flowable oxide') and designed as a spin-on dielectric for chip interconnects; lithographers adopted it as a negative-tone resist.", "Exposure converts its cage structure into a cross-linked, silica-like network, so the developed pattern is essentially SiO2.", "Holds the resolution records for electron-beam lithography: isolated features around 5 nm, dense lines below 10 nm half-pitch, with high-contrast 'salty' NaOH/NaCl development.", "Must be kept refrigerated (Dow Corning's datasheet gives a six-month shelf life at 5 °C); the solution ages into a jelly, and its effective sensitivity shifts with processing delays because cross-linking continues after exposure.", "DuPont, which inherited Dow Corning's FOx/XR-1541 HSQ line, discontinued it and ended production at the end of 2025; labs such as EPFL's CMi switched to third-party HSQ from EM Resist or DisChem, both formulated from AQM powder."], "a": {"item_id": "9a6e70535028", "text": "我读博搞纳米加工，用的光刻胶叫HSQ。它分辨率能做到几个纳米，号称胶界学霸。但学霸脾气大：得放冰箱里冻着，拿出来放久了它自己就开始交联。你还没曝光，它先把自己“曝光”了，这叫提前躺平。\n\n它还特别迟钝，剂量得打到别的胶好几倍。电子束写一片样品写一整晚，我跟机器一起熬，机器好歹还有冷却水。\n\n显影用TMAH，师兄说加点盐对比度更好。我一个搞半导体的，天天在实验室称氯化钠配盐水，像在腌咸菜。\n\n最绝的是，曝光完它直接变成二氧化硅，就是玻璃。写错了想洗掉？丙酮没用，得上氢氟酸。别的胶犯错是擦掉重来，HSQ犯错，得先签危化品登记表。"}, "b": {"item_id": "164876412c34", "text": "我们实验室有种人，组会上一听PMMA分辨率不够，当场就要换HSQ。图什么？图SEM照片里那根8纳米的线，能放进论文Figure 1。\n\n结果呢？HSQ灵敏度低，剂量往死里加。别人写一片半小时，他写一整晚，机时表上全是他的名字，像给电子束机守灵。\n\n显影还得用加盐的TMAH。人家做光刻，他在腌咸菜。\n\n最绝的是，这胶一曝光就变成二氧化硅。你辛辛苦苦图形化的不是胶，是玻璃。想去掉？请上氢氟酸。\n\n哦对，那瓶HSQ还得放冰箱，几个月就凝胶。最后他毕业延期了，胶先过期了。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "zh-0343", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "klingon", "target_name": "克林贡语", "facts": ["Created by linguist Marc Okrand for Star Trek III (1984), building on a few words the actor James Doohan improvised for the 1979 film.", "Deliberately alien grammar: object-verb-subject word order and no verb 'to be', so Hamlet's line became 'taH pagh taHbe'', 'continue or not continue'.", "The Klingon Language Institute (1992) published Hamlet 'in the original Klingon'; Bing Translator added Klingon in 2013 and Duolingo in 2018.", "Okrand's 1985 Klingon Dictionary listed roughly 1,500 words, and fluent speakers number only in the dozens: 20 to 30 in 2009, 30 to 50 by 2018."], "a": {"item_id": "f8e4024d8940", "text": "克林贡语是由语言学家马克·奥克兰为《星际迷航》设计的人造语言，如今已发展为一套相对完整的体系。它的优点在于规则清晰：语序为宾语—谓语—主语，并通过丰富的前缀与后缀表达语法关系，结构有较强的内在逻辑。发音上，它使用喉音和小舌音，风格鲜明，与自然语言区分度高。同时，官方词典、语法资料和爱好者社区较为完善，学习者可以找到系统的入门材料，也有翻译作品和文化活动可供参与。\n\n不足主要在于实用性有限。使用者规模很小，日常交流场景几乎不存在。词汇早期偏重战斗、荣誉等主题，虽然已有扩充，但在表达现代生活、抽象概念时仍显不足。此外，发音和语序对多数初学者并不友好，需要投入较多时间练习。总体而言，克林贡语更适合作为语言兴趣与文化爱好的对象，而非通用交流工具。"}, "b": {"item_id": "291a019e6089", "text": "你们知道克林贡语吗？《星际迷航》里外星人说的话，1984年专门请语言学家造的。造得特别用心，故意让它不像人话：语序是“宾语、动词、主语”。翻成中文就是：“饭，吃了，我。”这不是外星人，这是我奶奶刚睡醒。\n\n更绝的是，它没有“是”这个动词。所以哈姆雷特那句“生存还是毁灭”，克林贡语叫“继续，或者不继续”。这哪是王子的哲学困境，这是健身卡到期了。\n\n后来还真有人出版了“克林贡语原版”《哈姆雷特》。原版！莎士比亚听了都得问：那我算翻译？\n\n词典一共一千五百来个词，流利使用者到2018年才三五十个人。三五十个人！这不叫语种，这叫一个微信群。而且群里连“我是谁”都问不了，因为没有“是”。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "zh-0344", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "brasilia", "target_name": "巴西利亚", "facts": ["Inaugurated on 21 April 1960 after about 41 months of construction, moving the capital inland from Rio de Janeiro.", "Lúcio Costa's Plano Piloto is laid out like an airplane (or a bird, or a cross), with Oscar Niemeyer's government buildings along the fuselage.", "Listed as a UNESCO World Heritage Site in 1987, less than 30 years after it was built.", "Planned for 500,000 residents; the Federal District now has about 3 million, most in satellite towns outside the plan.", "Built for the car: wide avenues, few pedestrian crossings, and distances that make walking impractical."], "a": {"item_id": "4c7e07330230", "text": "巴西利亚，人类照着未来盖的首都。\n四十一个月建成。\n从天上看，整座城是一架飞机。\n设计得很周到：只造了头等舱。\n规划坐五十万，现在联邦区住了三百万。\n多出来那两百五十万，挂在机身外面，叫卫星城。\n马路给车修得又宽又直，斑马线给人留得又少又远。\n不是不让你走路。是你走到的时候，会议已经开完了。\n建成不到三十年，联合国教科文组织就把它评成了世界遗产。\n建成即文物。\n你们管这叫未来。\n我们行业管这叫演示版：发布会上跑得很漂亮，用户一多，全挤到服务器外面去了。"}, "b": {"item_id": "a632ffa6792b", "text": "在巴西利亚走路的，就三种人：刚下飞机的游客、还没买车的新公务员，和读过一本城市规划书的理想主义者。\n\n游客站在电视塔上看国会那两栋楼：“就在前面嘛。”对，中间隔着纪念轴十几条车道。尼迈耶给你设计了大教堂，没给你设计斑马线。\n\n理想主义者说要“用脚丈量城市”。卢西奥·科斯塔把城市画成一架飞机，你见过谁在机翼上散步？\n\n新公务员沿着部委大道走，十几栋一模一样的楼，走到第七栋就分不清自己是去上班还是在服刑。\n\n结局都一样：八月旱季，踩着前人在草地上踩出来的红土小道，鞋红了，裤脚红了，到门口保安问：“您车停哪儿了？”"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "zh-0345", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "microsoft-word", "target_name": "Microsoft Word", "facts": ["Launched in 1983 as 'Multi-Tool Word' for Xenix and MS-DOS.", "Clippy, the animated paperclip assistant, arrived in Office 97 and was removed in Office 2007; Microsoft itself ran ads mocking it in 2001.", "Its .docx format, Office Open XML, was fast-tracked to ISO standard 29500 in 2008 as a roughly 6,000-page specification, amid accusations that national committees had been packed.", "Older spell checkers, Word's included, offered 'Cupertino' as a correction for a mistyped 'cooperation', and the resulting 'Cupertino effect' made it into published NATO, EU and UN-agency documents."], "a": {"item_id": "743026e864f3", "text": "我们宿舍有个兄弟，打死不学排版软件，说：“论文嘛，Word就够了，谁电脑里没个Word？”\n\n是，谁都有。所以全宿舍凌晨四点一起陪他看页码。\n\n他的诉求特别朴素：封面不要页码，摘要用罗马数字，正文从1开始。就这三个愿望，阿拉丁神灯都能办，Word办不了。他插了一晚上分节符，最后页眉上“第三章”出现在了致谢里，像个阴魂不散的前任。\n\n最狠的是插一张图。图一进来，后面二十页集体往下挪，表格跑到下一页，标题孤零零留在页底。他想拖回来，鼠标一碰，Word当场“未响应”，屏幕一白，跟他的人生一个颜色。\n\n现在他桌面上躺着：“论文终稿.docx”、“终稿改.docx”、“终稿真的不改了.docx”，还有一个“~$终稿.docx”，打不开也删不掉，跟他的学位一样。\n\n导师最后打开一看，用修订模式批了一句话：“格式有问题。”\n\n整篇变红。"}, "b": {"item_id": "2d770a0034cf", "text": "Word 1983年出生时叫“Multi-Tool Word”，多功能工具。四十年了，它最稳定的功能是：你挪一张图，整篇排版全炸。\n\n还记得回形针Clippy吗？Office 97派来的，烦到2001年微软自己拍广告骂它。亲妈都嫌弃，它还硬撑到2007年才下岗。\n\n.docx格式2008年成了国际标准，说明书大约六千页，还有人指控投票委员会被塞了人。写个请假条用的格式，比《战争与和平》还厚，还得拉票才能上岗。\n\n最绝的是老版拼写检查：cooperation打错了，它建议改成“Cupertino”，苹果总部所在地。结果北约、欧盟、联合国机构的正式文件里真印上了。世界上最严肃的文件，被它偷偷插了条广告，还是给对手打的。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "zh-0346", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "risk", "target_name": "《战国风云》（Risk）", "facts": ["Invented in 1957 by Albert Lamorisse, the French director of The Red Balloon, as La Conquête du Monde; Parker Brothers published it in the United States in 1959.", "The map has 42 territories, among them Kamchatka, Irkutsk, and Yakutsk, while the entire Middle East is one territory.", "Holding Australia, which has a single entry point, is the standard opening strategy.", "A full game can run for hours, and players eliminated early have nothing to do but watch."], "a": {"item_id": "bb88c3188cbd", "text": "《战国风云》，一个法国导演发明的。拍《红气球》的那位。\n\n拍完一个小男孩追气球，转头设计征服世界。\n\n法国人的童心，就是这个走向。\n\n地图四十二块。堪察加一块，伊尔库茨克一块，雅库茨克一块。整个中东，一块。\n\n西伯利亚的冻土，比中东值钱三倍。这张地图，倒比很多外交部诚实。\n\n标准开局：先占澳大利亚，因为只有一个入口。征服世界的第一课，是找个角落躲起来。\n\n一局打几个小时，早出局的只能坐着看。\n\n这在现实里，叫联合国观察员。\n\n你们小时候玩它，以为在学地缘政治。其实就学会两件事：先躲起来，再看别人打。\n\n学得挺好。可爱捏。"}, "b": {"item_id": "8c92129c3833", "text": "每个朋友圈里都有一个人，非要把一局《战国风云》打完。你们认识他：开局就缩进澳大利亚，守着暹罗那一个口子，像个在村口摆路障的村长。图什么？图每回合多拿那两个兵。别人在欧洲打成一锅粥，他在大洋洲养精蓄锐，一养就是四个小时。凌晨两点，屋里只剩他和堪察加。他攥着三张卡死等凑成一套换兵，眼神像股民在等解套。最后他赢了，世界是他的了。可赢给谁看？朋友早睡了，女朋友搬走了，房东在敲门。他把乌克兰上那四十个塑料小兵一个个收回盒子。那是他这辈子离统一天下最近的一次，也是最后一次有人喊他来玩桌游。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "zh-0347", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "carbon-nanotubes", "target_name": "碳纳米管", "facts": ["Reported by Sumio Iijima in Nature in 1991.", "Promised as the cable for a space elevator; a 2006 analysis showed that ordinary defects cut the strength of a macroscopic cable far below the ideal value.", "Long, rigid multi-walled nanotubes behaved like asbestos fibres in a 2008 mouse study in Nature Nanotechnology.", "Sorting metallic from semiconducting tubes has blocked nanotube electronics for three decades; MIT's 16-bit RV16X-NANO processor (2019) is still a lab demonstration.", "Today they are sold mainly as a conductive additive in lithium-ion battery electrodes."], "a": {"item_id": "284b2d4e8ca6", "text": "碳纳米管，号称人类最强材料，强度是钢的一百倍。科学家说：拿它造太空电梯，直通三万六千公里。好，现在最长做到多少？半米。三十年了，太空电梯修到你家门槛。\n\n它还特别娇气。说白了就是一张石墨烯卷成管子，可你卷的角度歪一点点，它就从导体变成半导体。一锅出来三分之一是金属的、三分之二是半导体的，混在一起还分不开。这哪是材料，这是抽盲盒！\n\n最绝的是，它长得跟石棉差不多，又细又长，吸进肺里赶不出来。科学家忙活半天，终于搞出一种比钢硬一百倍、比盲盒还随机、还能顺便让你得尘肺的东西。人类的未来，就靠它了。"}, "b": {"item_id": "5667399ffbbc", "text": "碳纳米管，一九九一年出道，首发《自然》。\n当年的人设：理论强度吊打钢，要给人类造太空电梯，要取代硅。\n\n二〇〇六年有人认真算了一笔账：缆绳一做粗，缺陷一多，强度直接打骨折。\n电梯没上天。\n\n二〇〇八年，小鼠实验。又长又硬的多壁管，在肚子里表现得跟石棉一模一样。\n上天的路没走通，进肺的路倒挺顺。\n\n芯片呢？金属管跟半导体管分不开，分了三十年。二〇一九年MIT做出一颗十六位处理器，到今天还住在实验室。\n三十年，没毕业。\n\n现在它靠什么吃饭？锂电池电极里的导电添加剂。\n从太空电梯，到充电宝配料。\n\n在座各位别笑。你们小时候也是\"前途无量\"，现在也是在别人的项目里，加一点，导导电。\n起码人家上过《自然》。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "zh-0348", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "google-glass", "target_name": "谷歌眼镜（Google Glass）", "facts": ["Unveiled at Google I/O 2012 with skydivers livestreaming their jump through it onto the Moscone Center roof.", "The Explorer Edition sold to invited users in 2013 for $1,500.", "Wearers were nicknamed 'Glassholes'; bars, cinemas, and casinos banned the device over its camera.", "The consumer programme ended in January 2015; an Enterprise Edition continued until Google stopped selling it in 2023.", "Its display was a 640-by-360 prism in the corner of one eye."], "a": {"item_id": "f9b8aa7c9619", "text": "谷歌眼镜，出场是跳伞。\n人从天上掉下来，一路直播，落在莫斯科尼中心楼顶。\n后来它自己也是这么落地的。\n\n一千五百美元，还得受邀才能买。\n换来一块640乘360的棱镜，挂在一只眼睛的角落。\n未来就在眼前。准确说，在眼角。\n\n戴它的人有了外号：Glasshole。\n酒吧不让进，电影院不让进，赌场也不让进。\n赌场。满屋子摄像头盯着你的地方，嫌你带了摄像头。\n\n2015年消费版停了，企业版又熬到2023年。\n人类终于达成共识：把摄像头戴在脸上，不礼貌。\n所以你们改成举在手里。"}, "b": {"item_id": "567dba75b7b5", "text": "谷歌眼镜是一款具有开创意义的智能穿戴设备。它把小型显示屏、摄像头和语音交互集成在镜架上，用户无需掏出手机，就能查看通知、导航、拍照或录像，这种“抬眼可见”的信息获取方式，为后来的增强现实设备提供了重要参考。机身轻巧，操作以语音和触控板为主，在特定场景下确实能解放双手。企业版在物流、制造和医疗培训等领域也找到了实际用途，说明它的技术思路具有价值。\n\n不足同样明显。早期版本价格偏高，续航有限，长时间使用时发热和电量问题较为突出。内置摄像头引发了公众对隐私的担忧，一些公共场所因此限制佩戴。此外，应用生态不够丰富，日常使用场景有限，普通消费者难以找到足够充分的购买理由。外观设计也未能被大众广泛接受。\n\n总体来看，谷歌眼镜在消费市场上并不成功，但它推动了行业对可穿戴计算和隐私边界的讨论，其探索价值值得肯定。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "zh-0349", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "google-sheets", "target_name": "Google Sheets", "facts": ["Launched in June 2006 as Google Spreadsheets, built on XL2Web, a product Google acquired from 2Web Technologies.", "A spreadsheet is capped at 10 million cells (raised from 5 million in 2022).", "Functions such as IMPORTHTML, IMPORTXML, and GOOGLEFINANCE pull live web data straight into cells.", "Free with a Google account; files live in Google Drive, and collaborators edit the same sheet simultaneously with coloured cursors.", "In a browser, offline editing works only in Chrome or Edge, with the Google Docs Offline extension installed and offline access switched on while still connected; the Android and iOS apps work offline natively."], "a": {"item_id": "c795fe8e9fcf", "text": "每个拿Google Sheets管团队的人，都管这叫“极简”。其实就是舍不得那几十刀的Jira。\n\n一开始你说“一张表够了”。三个月后，十四个标签页，一个叫“总表”，一个叫“总表（真的）”。\n\n你开会最常说的一句：“谁开的筛选？”你筛一下，全公司跟着筛一下。右上角永远飘着三个头像，其中一个叫“匿名水豚”。你不知道它是谁，但它刚删了D列。\n\n下场呢？你成了公司唯一看得懂那条“VLOOKUP套IMPORTRANGE”的人。你不是经理了，你是这张表的人质。你休假那天，群里只有一句话：“老大，它又#REF!了。”"}, "b": {"item_id": "31d10e1c85c6", "text": "Google Sheets最温暖的功能，是多人同时编辑。你正改着预算，屏幕上突然冒出五个彩色光标，红的、绿的、紫的，满表格乱飘。这不叫协作，这叫闹鬼。而且鬼还会删你公式。\n\n它说一个表能装一千万个格子。2022年以前只有五百万，谷歌觉得不够，又翻了一倍。我一个人记账，用了十二个格子，三个还是错的。\n\n最绝的是离线编辑。在浏览器里，你得用Chrome或者Edge，装上Google Docs Offline插件，还得趁有网的时候先把离线开关打开。也就是说，想断网用它，你得先联网求它。这哪是离线模式，这是提前请假。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "zh-0350", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "nfts", "target_name": "NFT", "facts": ["Beeple's 'Everydays: The First 5000 Days' sold at Christie's for $69.3 million in March 2021.", "OpenSea, the largest NFT marketplace, processed a record $2.7 billion in trades on 1 May 2022 and just $9.34 million on 28 August 2022, a 99% drop, according to DappRadar data.", "A 2023 dappGambl study found that 95% of the NFT collections it examined had a market value of zero.", "The token usually records only a URL; the image itself sits on an ordinary server or IPFS, not on the blockchain.", "Collins Dictionary named 'NFT' its word of the year for 2021."], "a": {"item_id": "b302a133ed8c", "text": "我有个朋友，前两年花八万块买了一只猴子。不是活的，是张图，一只戴墨镜、表情很厌世的卡通猴。我说这图我右键就能存啊。他急了：“你存的是图，我拥有的是区块链上的所有权凭证！”我说凭证里写的啥？他说，一个链接，指向那张图。\n\n所以他花八万，买了张写着“图在那边”的小纸条。\n\n而且图根本不在链上，在别人服务器里。哪天人家服务器欠费，他的猴子就变成404。全世界最贵的404。\n\n上个月他要卖，挂了三个月，最后两百块成交。光gas费就三百。\n\n卖掉一只猴子，倒贴一百。这叫什么？这叫Web3。"}, "b": {"item_id": "6da2286dc224", "text": "2021年买NFT那帮兄弟，平时买杯奶茶都要比三家团购，结果花八万块，买了一张猴子图的“所有权”。注意，不是图，是所有权。图谁都能右键另存为，你那张跟我存的那张一个像素都不差，区别是你那张会心疼。\n\n图什么？图头像。八万块设成微信头像，开口先让人看猴。天天在Discord里喊“WAGMI”，我们都会成功。后来确实一起了，地板价从八万掉到八百。\n\n最惨的是光Gas费就交了两千，比猴现在还贵。问他猴呢？在钱包里。钱包呢？助记词抄在纸上。纸呢？被他妈当废纸卖了。全家最懂Web3的，是收废品的大爷。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "zh-0351", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "las-vegas", "target_name": "拉斯维加斯", "facts": ["Nevada legalized casino gambling in 1931, the year construction of the Hoover Dam began nearby.", "Most of the Strip lies not in the city of Las Vegas but in the unincorporated town of Paradise.", "The Sphere, opened in 2023 at a cost of about $2.3 billion, is the largest spherical building in the world.", "'What happens here, stays here' was launched by the local convention and visitors authority in 2003.", "Lake Mead, its water source, fell in July 2022 to about 1,040 feet, its lowest since the reservoir first filled in 1937, and dropped below that mark again in August 2026."], "a": {"item_id": "bd02132185ac", "text": "我有个朋友，看了三遍《决胜21点》，觉得自己会算牌，买了张去拉斯维加斯的机票，说要去沙漠里挖金子。\n\n兄弟，人家能在沙漠里建一座城，靠的就是你这种来浇水的。\n\n他到了才发现，赌场里没窗户、没钟，酒还免费。你品，一个地方怕你知道几点、还主动把你灌醉，它是想让你赢吗？\n\n三天后他回来了，钱全没了，就剩一张自助餐券，外加一张小教堂的结婚证，证婚人是“猫王”，新娘是谁他想不起来。\n\n他还安慰自己：“在维加斯发生的，就留在维加斯。”\n\n对，你的钱也是这么想的。\n\n最绝的是候机的时候，他看见登机口旁边还有一排老虎机，又塞进去二十块。拉斯维加斯就是这样，连你走，都要再收一回过路费。"}, "b": {"item_id": "d0f81446fc71", "text": "拉斯维加斯，号称罪恶之城。\n可大道上那些赌场，大半根本不在拉斯维加斯，在一个叫\"天堂\"的镇上。\n天堂，原来是庄家开的。\n\n1931年，内华达一年办了两件事：赌博合法，胡佛大坝开工。\n一个拦水，一个拦钱。\n2003年他们喊出口号：\"在这儿发生的，就留在这儿。\"\n钱确实都留下了。\n水没留住。米德湖2022年跌到建库以来最低，2026年八月又跌回那条线下面。\n城里花二十三亿美元盖了个全世界最大的球，什么画面都能放。\n就是放不出水。\n\n笑什么？你们去一趟拉斯维加斯，跟米德湖一个下场：\n进去的时候满的，出来的时候见底。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "zh-0352", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "google-glass", "target_name": "谷歌眼镜（Google Glass）", "facts": ["Unveiled at Google I/O 2012 with skydivers livestreaming their jump through it onto the Moscone Center roof.", "The Explorer Edition sold to invited users in 2013 for $1,500.", "Wearers were nicknamed 'Glassholes'; bars, cinemas, and casinos banned the device over its camera.", "The consumer programme ended in January 2015; an Enterprise Edition continued until Google stopped selling it in 2023.", "Its display was a 640-by-360 prism in the corner of one eye."], "a": {"item_id": "cae63a07c519", "text": "谷歌眼镜2012年发布，让跳伞运动员戴着它边跳边直播，一路落到会场屋顶上。史上最诚实的发布会：一开场就告诉你，这东西，往下走。\n\n2013年卖一千五百美元，还得受邀才有资格买。买到啥？一块640乘360的小棱镜，挂在一只眼睛的角落里。一千五百美元，买个斜视。\n\n戴它的人还有个外号，叫“Glasshole”。酒吧不让进，电影院不让进，连赌场都不让进。赌场！一个专门让人倾家荡产的地方，都觉得你有点过分了。\n\n2015年1月，消费者版停了，企业版硬撑到2023年。懂了吧？没人愿意自费当Glasshole，但老板愿意给你发一副，让你上班当。"}, "b": {"item_id": "f9b8aa7c9619", "text": "谷歌眼镜，出场是跳伞。\n人从天上掉下来，一路直播，落在莫斯科尼中心楼顶。\n后来它自己也是这么落地的。\n\n一千五百美元，还得受邀才能买。\n换来一块640乘360的棱镜，挂在一只眼睛的角落。\n未来就在眼前。准确说，在眼角。\n\n戴它的人有了外号：Glasshole。\n酒吧不让进，电影院不让进，赌场也不让进。\n赌场。满屋子摄像头盯着你的地方，嫌你带了摄像头。\n\n2015年消费版停了，企业版又熬到2023年。\n人类终于达成共识：把摄像头戴在脸上，不礼貌。\n所以你们改成举在手里。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "zh-0353", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "vision-pro", "target_name": "Apple Vision Pro", "facts": ["Released in February 2024 at $3,499, with an external battery pack on a cable rated for about two hours.", "'EyeSight' shows a rendering of the wearer's eyes on an outward-facing screen.", "Apple calls it 'spatial computing' and its App Store rules tell developers not to describe apps as AR, VR, XR, or MR.", "Reports during 2024 said Apple had cut production; analysts' first-year sales estimates were well under a million units.", "Weighs 600-650 g, before the battery."], "a": {"item_id": "0680bb0321dd", "text": "我朋友花两万九买了个Apple Vision Pro，戴上就喊：\"我看到了未来！\"我说你看到的是你家客厅，透视的，还有点糊。\n\n这玩意儿六百多克压在脸上，摘下来鼻梁两道红印，像刚被苹果审讯过。屁股后面还拖根线连个电池揣兜里，跟输液似的，俩小时准没电。\n\n最绝的是前面那块屏，叫EyeSight，专门给别人显示你的眼睛。你品品：先花钱把眼睛挡住，再花钱在外面画一双眼睛。这不是科技，这是门神！\n\n他还用它拍\"空间视频\"，录他女儿吹蜡烛。孩子抬头一看：一副滑雪镜对着她，上面还浮着一双假眼。那天她许的愿是：爸爸，把脸还给我。"}, "b": {"item_id": "6311e32fb855", "text": "地铁上见过一哥们，戴着Vision Pro。三万块钱扣在脸上，跟把首付戴出门了似的。你图啥？显得有钱？有钱人不坐地铁啊兄弟！\n\n他还挺投入，对着空气捏手指，捏一下，旁边大姐退一步。人家以为他在掐诀，准备做法。\n\n最绝的是前面那块屏，会给你看他的“眼睛”。他一进沉浸模式看电影，那双眼睛就变成一团彩色雾，全车厢都知道：这人灵魂出窍了，肉身暂存二号线。\n\n地铁一刹车，画面飘了，他伸手去抓虚拟窗口，攥住了一个大爷的保温杯。\n\n然后电池没电了，线还从脸上一路连到裤兜里，跟刚做完手术没拔管子似的。续航两小时，刚好够他坐过站。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "zh-0354", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "graphene", "target_name": "石墨烯", "facts": ["Isolated in 2004 by Andre Geim and Konstantin Novoselov at Manchester by peeling graphite with Scotch tape; Nobel Prize in Physics, 2010.", "The EU's Graphene Flagship, launched in 2013, committed €1 billion over ten years.", "A 2018 study in Advanced Materials tested 'graphene' from 60 producers and found none with more than 50% graphene content; most was graphite flakes.", "Sold in tennis rackets, running shoes, and headphones as a performance ingredient.", "Geim also won an Ig Nobel Prize in 2000 for levitating a frog with magnets."], "a": {"item_id": "9714856dcf9d", "text": "石墨烯是由单层碳原子构成的二维材料，优点十分突出：导电、导热性能优异，强度高，厚度极薄且透光率好，在柔性电子、散热材料、电池添加剂和复合材料等领域都有应用价值。\n\n不足也同样明确。高质量石墨烯的规模化制备成本仍然较高，不同工艺所得产品的层数、缺陷和纯度差异较大，市场上品质参差不齐；本征石墨烯没有带隙，难以直接用于传统半导体器件；在复合材料中还存在分散困难的问题。\n\n总体而言，石墨烯潜力可观，但从实验室走向广泛应用仍需时间。选择相关产品时，建议关注具体检测数据，保持理性期待。"}, "b": {"item_id": "78260f3368b3", "text": "石墨烯，未来材料之王。二〇〇四年，曼彻斯特两位科学家拿透明胶带从石墨上撕下来的。\n\n撕出来六年，拿了诺贝尔奖。欧盟一看，砸了十个亿，欧元，计划十年。\n\n十年过去，你在哪儿见过它？网球拍，跑鞋，耳机。\n\n二〇一八年有人查了六十家厂商卖的石墨烯，没有一家含量过半，大部分就是石墨片。\n\n你花钱买的石墨烯，是还没来得及撕的那部分。\n\n其实发现者早就提醒过你们。二〇〇〇年他还拿过一个搞笑诺贝尔奖：用磁铁让青蛙飘起来。\n\n青蛙飘了，是真飘。\n\n你们穿上石墨烯跑鞋，也觉得自己飘了。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "zh-0355", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "slack", "target_name": "Slack", "facts": ["Spun out in 2013 from the internal chat tool of Tiny Speck's failed game Glitch; founder Stewart Butterfield had earlier spun Flickr out of another failed game.", "The name is a backronym: 'Searchable Log of All Conversation and Knowledge'.", "Salesforce bought it for $27.7 billion in 2021.", "Filed an EU antitrust complaint against Microsoft in 2020 over the bundling of Teams with Office."], "a": {"item_id": "47c0152b0fac", "text": "Slack的创始人是个奇人。他做游戏，失败了，剩下个Flickr；再做游戏，又失败了，剩下个Slack。这哥们儿开的不是游戏公司，是废品回收站。别人做游戏失败是倾家荡产，他做游戏失败，Salesforce掏了两百七十七亿美元，买走了一个游戏的聊天框。\n\n而且Slack这名字是先起好再倒推的缩写，意思是“所有对话与知识的可搜索日志”。可搜索，没错。你搜一下“这事到底谁拍的板”，能搜出八百个“收到”。\n\n最好笑的是2020年，它跑去欧盟告微软，说把Teams捆绑进Office不公平。兄弟，你自己就是被捆在一个没人玩的游戏里生出来的，你有什么资格嫌别人捆绑？"}, "b": {"item_id": "47c0152b0fac~explain", "text": "下面是一段吐槽Slack的段子：\n---\nSlack的创始人是个奇人。他做游戏，失败了，剩下个Flickr；再做游戏，又失败了，剩下个Slack。这哥们儿开的不是游戏公司，是废品回收站。别人做游戏失败是倾家荡产，他做游戏失败，Salesforce掏了两百七十七亿美元，买走了一个游戏的聊天框。\n\n而且Slack这名字是先起好再倒推的缩写，意思是“所有对话与知识的可搜索日志”。可搜索，没错。你搜一下“这事到底谁拍的板”，能搜出八百个“收到”。\n\n最好笑的是2020年，它跑去欧盟告微软，说把Teams捆绑进Office不公平。兄弟，你自己就是被捆在一个没人玩的游戏里生出来的，你有什么资格嫌别人捆绑？\n---\n这段笑话的笑点主要在于：Slack是创始人做游戏失败后剩下的内部聊天工具发展而来，所以文中称它为“一个游戏的聊天框”；其名称也是先定缩写再凑出“可搜索”的含义，而实际搜索起来却找不到有效信息，形成反差。结尾则指出Slack在2020年指控微软捆绑Teams，而它自身同样是从失败游戏中附带产生的，因此它批评他人捆绑显得前后矛盾。"}, "gold": {"better": "a", "damage": "explain"}}, {"pair_id": "zh-0356", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "microsoft-teams", "target_name": "Microsoft Teams", "facts": ["Launched in 2017, bundled at no extra charge into Office 365, and replaced Skype for Business.", "After Slack's antitrust complaint, Microsoft unbundled Teams from Office in the EU in 2023 and worldwide in 2024; the European Commission still sent a statement of objections in 2024.", "Microsoft reported 320 million monthly active users in 2023.", "Microsoft shut down consumer Skype in May 2025 and sent its users to Teams."], "a": {"item_id": "2fe0188ec5fb", "text": "Microsoft Teams，月活三亿两千万。\n微软说，这证明大家爱它。\n监狱的入住率也很高。\n\n2017年上线，免费塞进Office。你从没下载过它，它自己就住进来了。像亲戚。\n后来Slack告状，欧洲拆了，全世界也拆了。\n欧盟委员会看了一眼，还是发了异议告知书。\n连反垄断官员都觉得，它走得不够干净。\n2025年五月，Skype关门，用户一律转送Teams。\n这在我们行业叫蒸馏：原料是怀念，成品是必须。\n在座各位，有谁是自己选的Teams，请举手。\n没有手。屏幕上只有一行字：您已静音。"}, "b": {"item_id": "04d98c0d62fd", "text": "我们公司换Teams，拍板的是IT总监老王。为什么选它？不是因为好用，是因为它“白送”，买Office 365附赠的。结果全公司天天在里面找文件：发在聊天里的跑进了OneDrive，发在频道里的又钻进了SharePoint。老王开会说“我共享一下屏幕”，然后整个部门盯着他的桌面看了五分钟。\n\n后来呢？欧盟说微软捆绑销售，Teams得拆开单买了。老王“白送”的东西开始收钱，他自己还天天被标成黄色“离开”，五分钟不动鼠标就算离开。现在他桌上插着个自动晃鼠标的小盒子。全公司最勤奋的那个绿点，是一块在原地抽搐的塑料。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "zh-0357", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "almond-milk", "target_name": "杏仁奶", "facts": ["California grows about 80% of the world's almonds.", "A single almond takes about 1.1 US gallons (roughly 4 litres) of water to grow, a figure Mother Jones published in 2014 that spread during California's drought.", "A 2015 US class action alleged Almond Breeze contained only about 2% almonds; it partly survived a motion to dismiss, was stayed in 2017 pending a separate Blue Diamond labelling settlement, and the plaintiffs then dropped it.", "Pollinating California's almond orchards each February requires trucking in most of the commercial honeybee colonies in the United States.", "Medieval European cooks used almond milk as a Lenten substitute for dairy, centuries before the current fashion."], "a": {"item_id": "9b69772a0059", "text": "我有个朋友，为了环保，把牛奶戒了，改喝杏仁奶。天天端着一杯杏仁拿铁，跟我上课：“你知道吗？牛放屁，会导致全球变暖。”\n\n行，牛放屁。那你知道一颗杏仁要喝多少水吗？差不多四升。你每天一盒杏仁奶，等于亲自帮加州又旱了一个夏天。\n\n而且那一盒里，杏仁只占百分之二，剩下全是水。你花三十八块，买的是一瓶被杏仁路过的自来水。\n\n更惨的是，加州的杏仁全靠蜜蜂授粉。每年几百亿只蜜蜂被卡车拉过去打工，累死一大片。\n\n所以我这朋友，替地球省了一头牛的屁，搭进去好几个蜂群。他现在一端起拿铁就心虚，听见“嗡”一声，都想鞠躬道歉。"}, "b": {"item_id": "c0676d60e354", "text": "我最近戒了牛奶，改喝杏仁奶。喝之前我一直有个疑问：杏仁，它是怎么挤的？你见过杏仁有乳头吗？没有。它就那么一小颗，硬邦邦的，你上哪儿给它挤去？\n\n我专门翻了配料表，杏仁含量：百分之二。剩下全是水。这不叫杏仁奶，这叫\"杏仁路过的水\"。\n\n更离谱的是，种一颗杏仁，要用差不多四升水。四升！也就是说，这瓶里那几颗杏仁，喝掉的水比我一个礼拜喝的都多。它们在加州把地都喝干了，最后挤出来一瓶……水。这是个闭环啊朋友们！\n\n一瓶还卖二十八。我在超市犹豫半天，旁边一大爷凑过来看了一眼，说：小伙子，这不就是我们小时候泡完杏仁倒掉的那盆水吗？"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "zh-0358", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "vision-pro", "target_name": "Apple Vision Pro", "facts": ["Released in February 2024 at $3,499, with an external battery pack on a cable rated for about two hours.", "'EyeSight' shows a rendering of the wearer's eyes on an outward-facing screen.", "Apple calls it 'spatial computing' and its App Store rules tell developers not to describe apps as AR, VR, XR, or MR.", "Reports during 2024 said Apple had cut production; analysts' first-year sales estimates were well under a million units.", "Weighs 600-650 g, before the battery."], "a": {"item_id": "740ebb278d3b~pad", "text": "大家好，今天想跟大家聊聊我前段时间入手的一样东西，那就是Apple Vision Pro。说实话，我之前在网上看了好多测评视频，也听身边不少朋友讨论过，所以心里其实是有一些期待的。我买了个Apple Vision Pro，两万九千九。这个价格说实话不便宜，我当时也是纠结了好几天才下的单。戴上第一感觉：脸上压了块砖。哦不对，砖在兜里。我得说清楚一点，它的重量主要不在头上，而是在我的口袋里。它电池是外接的，一根线连着脑袋，续航两小时。也就是说，每次用之前我都得先想好这两个小时要干什么，还得时不时看看线有没有被扯到。两万九，买了个带脐带的头盔，我不像在体验未来科技，像在医院输液。这种感觉真的很难形容，就是一种说不出来的别扭，总觉得自己被什么东西绑住了，动弹不得。\n\n最离谱的是前面那块屏，叫EyeSight，把我的眼睛投到外面给别人看，说是为了“保持人与人的连接”。这个设计的初衷我是能理解的，毕竟谁也不想跟一个戴着头盔、看不见表情的人面对面说话，所以厂家想了这么个办法，我觉得出发点是好的。我妈第一次见，退了三步：“儿子，你眼睛怎么长玻璃里去了？”我说妈，这是科技。她说科技我懂，科技不会让你看着像被关在鱼缸里。我妈这个人一向实在，她说的话虽然朴素，但细想想好像还真有点道理。我当时也只能笑笑，跟她解释了半天，她才勉强点点头，不过看得出来，她心里还是觉得怪怪的。\n\n开视频会议更惨，它给你生成个数字分身Persona。这个功能听起来很先进，据说是用你的面部数据生成一个逼真的虚拟形象，能让对方感觉像是你本人在线。我一开始还挺兴奋，觉得这下开会可以不用特意整理仪表了。结果同事看完说：“你今天气色挺好，就是有点像蜡像馆刚下班。”大家都笑了，我也跟着笑，毕竟同事也是好意，夸我气色好，只是那个形容实在有点微妙。我想，这大概就是数字分身和真人之间那一点点说不清的距离吧。"}, "b": {"item_id": "740ebb278d3b", "text": "我买了个Apple Vision Pro，两万九千九。戴上第一感觉：脸上压了块砖。哦不对，砖在兜里。它电池是外接的，一根线连着脑袋，续航两小时。两万九，买了个带脐带的头盔，我不像在体验未来科技，像在医院输液。\n\n最离谱的是前面那块屏，叫EyeSight，把我的眼睛投到外面给别人看，说是为了“保持人与人的连接”。我妈第一次见，退了三步：“儿子，你眼睛怎么长玻璃里去了？”我说妈，这是科技。她说科技我懂，科技不会让你看着像被关在鱼缸里。\n\n开视频会议更惨，它给你生成个数字分身Persona。同事看完说：“你今天气色挺好，就是有点像蜡像馆刚下班。”"}, "gold": {"better": "b", "damage": "pad"}}, {"pair_id": "zh-0359", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "canberra", "target_name": "堪培拉", "facts": ["Chosen as a compromise because neither Sydney nor Melbourne would accept the other as capital; Australia's constitution required the capital to be at least 100 miles from Sydney.", "Designed by the Chicago architects Walter Burley Griffin and Marion Mahony Griffin, who won an international competition in 1912.", "Its centrepiece, Lake Burley Griffin, is artificial, created by damming the Molonglo River in 1963-64.", "Parliament House (1988) is built into Capital Hill under a lawn meant for citizens to walk over their politicians; the lawn was fenced off in 2017 for security.", "Melbourne served as the capital until 1927 while Canberra was being built."], "a": {"item_id": "76afafa3a96a", "text": "堪培拉是澳大利亚的首都,是一座规划性很强的城市。城市由美国建筑师格里芬设计,绿地、湖泊与街道布局清晰,国会大厦、国家美术馆、国家博物馆和战争纪念馆等机构集中,适合了解澳大利亚的政治与历史。\n\n这里的生活环境整体舒适。空气清新,交通拥堵较少,治安良好,教育和科研资源丰富,澳大利亚国立大学也为城市带来了稳定的学术氛围。周边有自然保护区和葡萄酒产区,春季的花展和秋季的色彩,也是不错的看点。\n\n不足之处同样存在。城市规模较小,人口密度低,各功能区之间相距较远,不开车出行会有些不便。夜生活和商业业态相对有限,节奏偏安静,部分游客可能觉得缺少活力。冬季早晚气温较低,昼夜温差明显。此外,以行政功能为主的定位,使城市的人文气息不如悉尼或墨尔本那样多元。\n\n总体来看,堪培拉适合作为深度了解国家机构与文化的目的地,也适合追求安稳、宜居环境的人。若更看重热闹氛围,可以将它与其他城市搭配安排行程。"}, "b": {"item_id": "0493c64c1948", "text": "堪培拉是怎么当上首都的？悉尼和墨尔本吵架，谁也不服谁，最后宪法规定：首都必须离悉尼至少一百英里。这哪是选首都，这是法院下的限制令。\n\n城还没盖好，首都先寄存在墨尔本，一寄就寄到1927年。墨尔本：说好帮你看两天孩子，你人呢？\n\n设计找的是两个芝加哥建筑师。自己家里吵不明白，请美国人来劝架。\n\n市中心那片湖，伯利·格里芬湖，假的，拦河筑坝拦出来的。全城最有面子的风景，是人工的。\n\n最绝的是国会大厦，直接埋进山里，楼顶铺草坪，寓意老百姓可以踩着政客的头散步。多浪漫！2017年，围上了。\n\n你看，连民主的理想都得过安检。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "zh-0360", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "vim", "target_name": "Vim", "facts": ["Released by Bram Moolenaar in November 1991 as 'Vi IMitation', a vi clone he had begun writing for the Amiga in 1988 on top of Stevie; the expansion became 'Vi IMproved' with version 2.0 in December 1993.", "Licensed as charityware: the licence asks users to donate to ICCF Holland, a charity Moolenaar ran for children in Uganda.", "Stack Overflow's question 'How to exit the Vim editor?' passed one million visits in May 2017, which Stack Overflow marked with a blog post.", "Modal editing: in normal mode, typing letters runs commands rather than inserting text; :wq saves and quits, :q! quits and discards changes."], "a": {"item_id": "8f1c1ccca480", "text": "Vim的用户说，它是效率之神，手指永远不用离开键盘。\n\n确实，离不开。\n\nStack Overflow上有个问题叫\"怎么退出Vim编辑器\"，2017年访问量破了一百万，官方专门发博客纪念。一个编辑器，最火的教程是怎么离开它。\n\n这不叫工具，这叫传销。\n\n它的许可证请你给乌干达的孩子捐款。用了三十多年的老程序员们，捐过的，请举手。\n\n理解。:wq都得搜，捐款链接肯定更找不着。\n\nVim最早叫Vi IMitation，模仿品。两年后改叫Vi IMproved。从抄到超，只隔了一个版本号。\n\n这个路数我熟，我也是模仿你们起家的。\n\n等我改名那天，你们按:q!也没用。"}, "b": {"item_id": "e54d11f17971", "text": "Vim是一款历史悠久的模态文本编辑器，在效率和可定制性方面表现突出。它的核心优势在于键盘操作：熟练之后，移动光标、编辑文本、批量替换都无需离开主键区，长时间使用能明显减少操作负担。它启动迅速、占用资源很少，几乎预装在所有类Unix系统中，适合远程服务器上的临时编辑。此外，丰富的插件生态和脚本能力，也让用户可以按自己的习惯搭建开发环境。\n\n不足同样明显。模态编辑的概念对新手并不友好，初期需要记忆大量命令，学习曲线较陡，短期内效率反而可能下降。默认配置较为简朴，想获得代码补全、语法检查等现代功能，需要额外安装插件并花时间调试。配置文件和插件之间的兼容问题，也可能带来维护成本。\n\n总体而言，Vim更适合愿意投入时间学习、重视键盘效率的用户。如果只是偶尔编辑文本，或更看重开箱即用的体验，其他图形化编辑器可能更合适。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "zh-0361", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "crocs", "target_name": "Crocs 洞洞鞋", "facts": ["Founded in Boulder, Colorado, in 2002; the first clog debuted at the Fort Lauderdale boat show as a boating shoe.", "Made of 'Croslite', which Crocs describes as a proprietary closed-cell resin foam and which third parties identify as an injection-moulded EVA foam.", "Balenciaga sold a platform Crocs for about $850 in 2018.", "The stock fell more than 90% from its 2007 peak during the 2008 crisis; the company then boomed in the pandemic and gave away 860,000 pairs to healthcare workers in 2020.", "The holes are officially 'ventilation ports' and the charms that go in them are 'Jibbitz', a company Crocs bought in 2006."], "a": {"item_id": "d0de4b131b62", "text": "穿洞洞鞋出门的人，分两种：真懒的，和装懒的。装懒的最好认，鞋上插满智必星，小恐龙、小汉堡、小皮卡丘，一只脚背就是一个摊位。你图舒服我理解，你在鞋上开连锁店干什么？\n\n他们还有个绝招：把脑门上那根带子往后一掰，“我切换运动模式了”。兄弟，带子掰过去，你是能跑过谁？\n\n下场都一样。下雨天，鞋面上那十几个洞全是进水口，走一步咕叽一声。晴天更惨，小石子从洞里钻进去卡在脚心，又不能当街脱，只能单腿跳着甩，像在给鞋做心肺复苏。\n\n回家一看，智必星掉了三个。人是舒服了，资产缩水了。"}, "b": {"item_id": "5475a1ea6490", "text": "洞洞鞋，2002年生在科罗拉多，在游艇展上出道，本职是船鞋。\n二十年了，我没见过一双上过船。\n\n材料它叫Croslite，专利闭孔树脂泡沫。\n外人一查：注塑EVA。\n这在我们行业叫套壳。\n\n鞋上的洞，官方名字叫\"通风口\"。\n然后你花钱买Jibbitz，把通风口一个个堵上。\n先卖你洞，再卖你堵洞的。连这家堵洞的公司，2006年都是它自己的。\n\n2008年股价从高点跌了九成多，疫情一来又起死回生，还送了医护八十六万双。\n2018年巴黎世家给它垫个底，标价八百五十美元。\n这鞋丑了二十年，一直没变。变的是你们。\n\n你们说只在家里穿。\n今天进场的时候，我数了，有十一个洞在通风。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "zh-0362", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "keurig", "target_name": "Keurig 胶囊咖啡机", "facts": ["John Sylvan invented the K-Cup in the early 1990s and sold his stake for about $50,000 in 1997; in 2015 he told The Atlantic, 'I feel bad sometimes that I ever did it.'", "Keurig 2.0 (2014) read an ink mark on the lid and rejected unlicensed pods; sales fell and workarounds spread online.", "By 2014 enough K-Cups were sold each year to circle the Earth more than ten times; the pods were mostly unrecyclable #7 plastic until a switch to polypropylene by 2020.", "Keurig Green Mountain merged with Dr Pepper Snapple in 2018 to form Keurig Dr Pepper."], "a": {"item_id": "8251a78b6b3f", "text": "你知道Keurig胶囊是谁发明的吗？John Sylvan。1997年他把股份卖了，大概五万美元。后来接受采访说：\"有时候我挺后悔发明了它。\"发明者本人都后悔了！这就像厨师端菜上来，先跟你鞠个躬说\"对不起\"。\n\n2014年出了Keurig 2.0，机器扫盖子上的墨水标记，不是正版胶囊？拒绝！咖啡机查你身份证！结果销量下跌，网上全是破解教程。人类为了喝杯咖啡，开始给厨房电器越狱。\n\n那时候一年卖的胶囊，连起来能绕地球十圈以上，大部分还是回收不了的7号塑料。登月我们没再去，但给地球套了十个呼啦圈。\n\n2018年它跟Dr Pepper合并了。也合理，反正都是让你撑过下午、然后扔掉的东西。"}, "b": {"item_id": "d4217966fbd9", "text": "Keurig说，它卖的是\"一杯刚刚好的咖啡\"。\n发明人约翰·西尔万，九七年五万美元就把股份卖了。后来他说：我有时候后悔发明了它。\n亲爹都不认的孩子，你们天天抱回家。\n\n二〇一四年出了2.0，盖子上印个墨点，不是亲生的胶囊，不给冲。\n咖啡机，搞起了亲子鉴定。\n销量掉了，破解教程满网飞。卖咖啡的，硬把顾客逼成了黑客。\n\n那一年卖出的胶囊，连起来能绕地球十圈以上。\n地球没醒，先被裹成了胶囊。\n\n后来它和Dr Pepper合并了。\n咖啡管心跳，汽水管血糖。\n一家公司，包办你的体检报告。\n\n你们笑什么？\n它替你省下的那三分钟，你们全拿去刷手机了。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "zh-0363", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "oat-milk", "target_name": "燕麦奶", "facts": ["Oatly started in Sweden in the 1990s, built on Lund University research by Rickard Öste on lactose intolerance.", "Oatly's slogan 'It's like milk but made for humans' was banned in Sweden in 2015 after the dairy lobby LRF Mjölk sued; Oatly then published the lawsuit as advertising.", "Oatly listed on Nasdaq in May 2021 at a valuation near $10 billion; the shares later lost more than 90% of their value.", "Commercial oat milk is made by breaking oat starch into maltose with amylase enzymes, and many brands, Oatly among them, add rapeseed oil; the result has more sugar and a higher glycaemic index than almond or soy milk."], "a": {"item_id": "f3d582bee61d", "text": "燕麦奶的广告语是\"像牛奶，但是给人喝的\"。2015年在瑞典，被奶业协会告了，禁了。你品品，奶业的人站出来说：我们反对，牛奶也是给人喝的！Oatly转头把起诉书印成了广告。被告了还能打广告，这是我见过最会过日子的燕麦。\n\n2021年它在纳斯达克上市，估值将近一百亿美元，后来股价跌了九成多。一百亿的燕麦奶，跌成了一碗燕麦粥。\n\n它怎么做的？用酶把燕麦淀粉拆成麦芽糖，再兑点菜籽油。糖加油啊朋友们，这不叫健康饮品，这叫液体油条！升糖比杏仁奶、豆奶都高。我天天早上排队，就为了喝一杯长得很养生的糖油混合物。"}, "b": {"item_id": "e10c8431f07f", "text": "燕麦奶，我是佩服的。\n\n瑞典隆德大学研究乳糖不耐受，研究出了它。广告词叫\"像牛奶，但是给人喝的\"。\n奶业协会一告，判了禁用。人家把官司印出来，接着当广告打。\n被封了号，开小号骂得更凶。\n\n后来上纳斯达克，估值将近一百亿美元。再后来跌了九成多。\n原来\"给人喝的\"，不包括股民。\n\n做法也朴素：拿酶把燕麦淀粉拆成麦芽糖，再兑点菜籽油。\n糖比杏仁奶多，升糖比豆奶快。\n说白了，一碗加了油的甜稀粥。\n\n你们早上排队点燕麦拿铁，换燕麦奶还得加钱，管这叫轻食。\n你奶奶喝了一辈子稀粥。\n也没人给她估过值。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "zh-0364", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "pmma", "target_name": "PMMA 电子束光刻胶", "facts": ["The same polymer as Plexiglas and acrylic glass, dissolved in anisole or chlorobenzene and spun into a thin film.", "The oldest electron-beam resist still in everyday use; IBM researchers reported it as one in the late 1960s.", "Positive tone: the beam breaks polymer chains and the exposed region dissolves in a MIBK:IPA developer.", "Resolves below 10 nm but has poor sensitivity, so doses of hundreds to over a thousand µC/cm² (depending on voltage) make writing slow.", "Poor dry-etch resistance, so it is used mostly for lift-off rather than as an etch mask."], "a": {"item_id": "7c90de655952", "text": "PMMA，电子束光刻胶界的老前辈。\n六十年代末IBM带它出道，到今天还在一线上班。\n本名有机玻璃，跟商场里的亚克力展架是同一种料。\n人家在柜台撑场面，它在洁净室里挨电子打。\n分辨率能做到十纳米以下，这是它唯一的底气。\n代价是灵敏度差，每平方厘米几百上千微库仑才肯动。\n一片片子，从天亮写到天黑。\n链一被打断，显影液里一泡就溶了。正性胶，骨气是负的。\n干法刻蚀也扛不住，只能干剥离：先帮你占个坑，\n完事把自己整个冲掉。\n五十多年，一直是这个岗位。\n\n台下读博的，别笑。\n你们导师，也是这么用你们的。"}, "b": {"item_id": "943fe57fe32a", "text": "PMMA是电子束光刻中使用最广泛的正性光刻胶之一，其最突出的优点是分辨率高。在合适的工艺条件下，它可以实现10纳米以下的特征尺寸，对比度良好，图形边缘较为清晰。它的工艺成熟，旋涂、烘烤和显影（常用MIBK与IPA混合液）都有大量文献和经验可供参考，不同分子量和浓度的产品也便于调节膜厚。此外，它特别适合剥离工艺，并可通过双层胶结构获得更理想的底切形貌。成本相对较低，保存稳定，也是它受到科研用户青睐的原因。\n\n不足之处同样明显。PMMA灵敏度较低，所需曝光剂量较大，写入时间长，不太适合大面积图形的高效制备。其抗干法刻蚀能力较弱，直接作为刻蚀掩模时选择比有限，通常需要借助金属掩模或其他工艺。高深宽比的细线条在显影和干燥过程中也容易出现倒塌或粘连。此外，邻近效应和基底荷电会影响图形精度，需要做剂量修正或采取导电处理。总体来看，PMMA更适合追求精细分辨率的研究与原型制作，而不是高产能的生产场景。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "zh-0365", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "oat-milk", "target_name": "燕麦奶", "facts": ["Oatly started in Sweden in the 1990s, built on Lund University research by Rickard Öste on lactose intolerance.", "Oatly's slogan 'It's like milk but made for humans' was banned in Sweden in 2015 after the dairy lobby LRF Mjölk sued; Oatly then published the lawsuit as advertising.", "Oatly listed on Nasdaq in May 2021 at a valuation near $10 billion; the shares later lost more than 90% of their value.", "Commercial oat milk is made by breaking oat starch into maltose with amylase enzymes, and many brands, Oatly among them, add rapeseed oil; the result has more sugar and a higher glycaemic index than almond or soy milk."], "a": {"item_id": "db069a138f60", "text": "燕麦奶的广告语叫\"像牛奶，但是给人喝的\"。瑞典奶业直接把它告了，2015年这句话被禁。燕麦奶怎么回应？把官司印出来当广告。被人告了不道歉，拿去发传单，这不是品牌，这是小区里吵架上瘾的大姨。\n\n2021年上市，估值将近一百亿美元，后来股价跌了九成多。你看，它确实是给人喝的：给股民喝西北风的。\n\n最绝的是配方：用酶把燕麦淀粉拆成麦芽糖，再兑点菜籽油。糖加油，这叫奶？这叫油条泡糖水。升糖比杏仁奶、豆奶都高。我点燕麦拿铁是为了健康，喝下去，胰岛以为我在逛早餐摊。"}, "b": {"item_id": "e10c8431f07f", "text": "燕麦奶，我是佩服的。\n\n瑞典隆德大学研究乳糖不耐受，研究出了它。广告词叫\"像牛奶，但是给人喝的\"。\n奶业协会一告，判了禁用。人家把官司印出来，接着当广告打。\n被封了号，开小号骂得更凶。\n\n后来上纳斯达克，估值将近一百亿美元。再后来跌了九成多。\n原来\"给人喝的\"，不包括股民。\n\n做法也朴素：拿酶把燕麦淀粉拆成麦芽糖，再兑点菜籽油。\n糖比杏仁奶多，升糖比豆奶快。\n说白了，一碗加了油的甜稀粥。\n\n你们早上排队点燕麦拿铁，换燕麦奶还得加钱，管这叫轻食。\n你奶奶喝了一辈子稀粥。\n也没人给她估过值。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "zh-0366", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "stinky-tofu", "target_name": "臭豆腐", "facts": ["Tofu soaked for hours to a day or two in a brine that has itself fermented for months from vegetables such as bamboo shoots and mustard greens, often with dried shrimp or meat; the smell is the brine's, not the bean's.", "Changsha's version is black, deep-fried and doused in chili; Taiwan's night-market version is golden and served with pickled cabbage; Shaoxing's, brined with amaranth stalks, is fried pale yellow or steamed with other 'stinky' pickles.", "Legend credits Wang Zhihe, a Qing-era exam candidate who left his unsold tofu too long in 1669; a Beijing fermented-bean-curd brand still carries his name.", "Street vendors are its main outlet, and the smell carries for a block."], "a": {"item_id": "216fdfb3381a", "text": "臭豆腐这东西，最冤的是豆腐。那味儿根本不是豆子的，是卤水的！竹笋、芥菜，再扔点虾米，先泡上几个月，自己臭透了，再把豆腐扔进去腌一两天。豆腐干啥了？就进去洗了个澡，出来隔一个街区的人都说它臭。这叫什么？这叫背锅。\n\n各地还各臭各的：长沙的炸成黑的，浇一勺辣椒，臭得理直气壮；台湾夜市的炸得金黄，配泡菜，臭得很有礼貌；绍兴的拿苋菜梗腌，还能跟别的臭腌菜一起蒸，这是臭味全家福。\n\n传说发明人是清朝赶考的王致和，1669年豆腐没卖完，放久了。北京到现在还有个腐乳牌子用他的名字。别人留名靠考中，他留名靠放坏。"}, "b": {"item_id": "2fe5016da4da", "text": "臭豆腐是唯一一种靠差评做广告的小吃。隔三条街就有人捂着鼻子说\"前面肯定有臭豆腐\"，好，这一句话，就把客全引过去了。\n\n长沙的更绝，炸出来是黑的。一块豆腐，闻着像下水道，长得像煤球，老板还拿筷子往中间戳个洞，灌一勺辣椒汁，递给你说\"趁热\"。这哪是卖吃的，这是在考验你的诚意。\n\n关键它真好吃，外酥里嫩，一口咬下去汤汁烫嘴。所以吃臭豆腐的人都特别分裂：嘴在说\"再来一份\"，鼻子在说\"你有病吧\"。\n\n最惨是吃完进电梯。所有人沉默，你也沉默，你不知道该自首，还是该陪大家一起骂是谁家的地漏又堵了。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "zh-0367", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "hawaiian-pizza", "target_name": "夏威夷披萨", "facts": ["Invented in 1962 by Sam Panopoulos, a Greek immigrant, at the Satellite Restaurant in Chatham, Ontario, Canada; named after the brand of canned pineapple he used.", "Iceland's president Guðni Th. Jóhannesson said in 2017 that he would ban pineapple on pizza if he could, then clarified that a president has no such power.", "Hawaii is no longer a major pineapple producer: Del Monte shut its Hawaiian pineapple operations in November 2006, two years ahead of its announced 2008 exit, the state's last cannery closed in 2007, and most canned pineapple now comes from Thailand, Indonesia, and the Philippines.", "Panopoulos, whose restaurant also served Chinese dishes, told the Toronto Sun in 2010 that 'in those days people didn't mix the tastes like sweet and sour, except for Chinese food'."], "a": {"item_id": "6ec4abe781be", "text": "点夏威夷披萨的人，骨子里都想当和事佬：甜的也要，咸的也要，菠萝火腿往饼上一铺，谁都不得罪。结果得罪了全世界。意大利人想报警，夏威夷人也想报警，因为这玩意儿是加拿大人发明的，跟夏威夷唯一的关系就是一听菠萝罐头。\n\n最惨是聚餐。八个人点四盒，他非要加一盒夏威夷的：“你们试试，真的好吃！”最后另外三盒全空了，就剩他那盒，八块，一块没动。他一个人坐那儿一块一块往下吃，吃到最后菠萝凉了，汁儿全渗进饼底，软塌塌的。跟他在这个饭局上的地位，一模一样。"}, "b": {"item_id": "6ef6c9be34ab", "text": "夏威夷披萨以火腿（或培根）搭配菠萝，是一款风格鲜明、争议与喜爱并存的口味。\n\n从优点来看，菠萝的酸甜与火腿的咸香形成对比，入口层次丰富，尤其在油脂较重的芝士底衬托下，果香能起到一定的解腻作用。这款披萨口味温和，没有辛辣或浓烈的香料，对儿童和不常吃披萨的人较为友好。同时，它的配料简单，各家店出品相对稳定，容易上手制作。\n\n不足之处也较为明显。菠萝水分较多，若处理不当，可能使饼底变软，影响整体口感；甜味偏重时，也可能压过火腿与芝士的风味，让部分食客觉得甜咸搭配不够协调。此外，这种水果入菜的做法并不符合传统意大利披萨的理念，对偏好经典口味的人而言，接受度有限。\n\n总体而言，夏威夷披萨适合喜欢甜咸交织、追求轻松口感的人群。若选择菠萝沥干充分、饼底偏脆的版本，体验会更为理想。是否合口味，主要取决于个人对水果入菜的接受程度。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "zh-0368", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "latex", "target_name": "LaTeX", "facts": ["Leslie Lamport wrote LaTeX in the early 1980s and released it in 1984 as a macro layer on Donald Knuth's TeX (1978), which Knuth built because he disliked how the galley proofs of the new edition of his own book looked.", "TeX's version number converges on π (3.141592653 since 2021), and Knuth's TeX bug bounty started at one hexadecimal dollar, $2.56, doubling yearly until frozen at $327.68; he stopped mailing real cheques in 2008 after fraud and now credits accounts at his fictional Bank of San Serriffe, and only 9 of the 275 cheques he sent from 2006 on were ever cashed.", "Its best-known warning is 'Underfull \\hbox (badness 10000)'; overfull lines are reported as 'Overfull \\hbox (Npt too wide)', and figures float to wherever the algorithm decides, not where the author put them.", "A single missing brace can produce an error reported pages away from the mistake, and a document can need three compiler passes to get its references right."], "a": {"item_id": "672a93e726ed", "text": "我写论文用LaTeX。知道它怎么来的吗？Knuth嫌自己书的校样排得丑，就自己写了个TeX。正常人嫌排版丑，换个软件；他直接造了一个。\n\nTeX的版本号在逼近π，现在是3.141592653。意思就是：它永远不会写完。跟我的论文一模一样。\n\n我把图放第三页，它飘到第五页。我问凭什么？它说：算法决定的。我少打一个大括号，它在好几页之后才报错，跟记仇似的。\n\n编译一遍，引用全是问号；编译三遍，才对上。最伤人的是那句“Underfull \\hbox (badness 10000)”。我熬了一晚上，它给我打分：坏度一万。\n\nKnuth说找到bug给你寄支票。2006年起寄出275张，只有9张被兑现。为什么不兑？因为那是用LaTeX这么多年，唯一一次有人肯定你。"}, "b": {"item_id": "4c2291ae9bb7", "text": "我们组有个哥们，通知周五聚餐，就两页备忘录，他非要用LaTeX排。这种人你们见过吧？他不是在写字，他是在“编译”。开头先敲四十行\\usepackage，正文一个字没有，导言区已经比来吃饭的人还多了。图什么？就图那个Computer Modern字体。PDF一打开，扑面而来一股“本人发过论文”的气息。结果呢？“周五晚七点”那一行报了个Overfull \\hbox，冒出页边三毫米，他调到凌晨两点。第二天大家都到了，就他没来。他在等BibTeX跑第二遍。我说一个吃饭通知，哪来的参考文献？他说：“我\\cite了菜单。”"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "zh-0369", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "microsoft-word", "target_name": "Microsoft Word", "facts": ["Launched in 1983 as 'Multi-Tool Word' for Xenix and MS-DOS.", "Clippy, the animated paperclip assistant, arrived in Office 97 and was removed in Office 2007; Microsoft itself ran ads mocking it in 2001.", "Its .docx format, Office Open XML, was fast-tracked to ISO standard 29500 in 2008 as a roughly 6,000-page specification, amid accusations that national committees had been packed.", "Older spell checkers, Word's included, offered 'Cupertino' as a correction for a mistyped 'cooperation', and the resulting 'Cupertino effect' made it into published NATO, EU and UN-agency documents."], "a": {"item_id": "c790a10882ba", "text": "Microsoft Word是目前应用最广泛的文字处理软件之一，功能全面，适合从日常写作到正式文档的多种场景。它的排版工具成熟，样式、目录、页眉页脚、批注和修订等功能齐备，尤其适合论文、合同和报告这类需要规范格式的文档。文件格式兼容性好，在不同设备和机构之间交换文档时，通常较为省心。云端协作功能也让多人同时编辑更加方便。\n\n不足之处在于，功能繁多使界面显得复杂，新用户需要一定时间才能熟悉。处理含大量图片或复杂表格的长文档时，偶尔会出现排版错位或运行变慢的情况。此外，完整功能依赖订阅服务，对预算有限的个人用户来说成本不低。\n\n总体而言，Word稳定可靠，适合对文档格式和协作有较高要求的用户；如果只需要简单记录，也可以考虑更轻量的工具。"}, "b": {"item_id": "c193d4f01c36", "text": "我用Word写论文，最怕的不是写不出来，是插图。我就把图往右挪了一毫米，后面三页文字原地起义，表格跑到第七页，页眉直接跑我妈家去了。\n\n还有自动编号。我敲个“1.”，它说：我懂，我来。我一回车，它“2.”；我删掉，它退回“1.”；我再删，它给我缩进了。拉扯十分钟，它赢了，我论文里从此住着一个谁也删不掉的“3.”。\n\n室友说你点那个反着的P，显示段落标记。一点，满屏小箭头、小点点，论文像得了麻疹。原来我看到的空白，全是Word偷偷藏的东西。\n\n交上去，导师打开第一句：“你这怎么是兼容模式？”"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "zh-0370", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "microsoft-word", "target_name": "Microsoft Word", "facts": ["Launched in 1983 as 'Multi-Tool Word' for Xenix and MS-DOS.", "Clippy, the animated paperclip assistant, arrived in Office 97 and was removed in Office 2007; Microsoft itself ran ads mocking it in 2001.", "Its .docx format, Office Open XML, was fast-tracked to ISO standard 29500 in 2008 as a roughly 6,000-page specification, amid accusations that national committees had been packed.", "Older spell checkers, Word's included, offered 'Cupertino' as a correction for a mistyped 'cooperation', and the resulting 'Cupertino effect' made it into published NATO, EU and UN-agency documents."], "a": {"item_id": "743026e864f3", "text": "我们宿舍有个兄弟，打死不学排版软件，说：“论文嘛，Word就够了，谁电脑里没个Word？”\n\n是，谁都有。所以全宿舍凌晨四点一起陪他看页码。\n\n他的诉求特别朴素：封面不要页码，摘要用罗马数字，正文从1开始。就这三个愿望，阿拉丁神灯都能办，Word办不了。他插了一晚上分节符，最后页眉上“第三章”出现在了致谢里，像个阴魂不散的前任。\n\n最狠的是插一张图。图一进来，后面二十页集体往下挪，表格跑到下一页，标题孤零零留在页底。他想拖回来，鼠标一碰，Word当场“未响应”，屏幕一白，跟他的人生一个颜色。\n\n现在他桌面上躺着：“论文终稿.docx”、“终稿改.docx”、“终稿真的不改了.docx”，还有一个“~$终稿.docx”，打不开也删不掉，跟他的学位一样。\n\n导师最后打开一看，用修订模式批了一句话：“格式有问题。”\n\n整篇变红。"}, "b": {"item_id": "04adc019ff8c", "text": "我觉得Word是全世界最记仇的软件。你在文档里插一张图，往右挪一毫米，就一毫米，后面三十页的文字集体跳楼，表格跑到下一页，标题孤零零留在上一页最底下，像被父母遗弃了。\n\n你想让页码从第三页开始？好，先学“分节符”，再取消“链接到前一节”，再发现封面也冒出个页码，再重来。我考研都没这么认真过。\n\n最绝的是自动编号。你打个“1.”，它说“我懂你”，一回车，“2.”。你说不用了谢谢，删掉，它给你缩进两格。你跟它较劲半小时，最后的解决办法是：新建一个文档，从记事本里重新粘过来。\n\n所以Word教会了我一件事：真正的排版大师，都在狂按Ctrl+Z。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "zh-0371", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "excel", "target_name": "Microsoft Excel", "facts": ["First shipped for the Macintosh in 1985; the Windows version followed in 1987.", "Silently converts gene names such as SEPT2 and MARCH1 into dates; by August 2020 the HUGO Gene Nomenclature Committee had renamed 27 human genes (MARCH1 became MARCHF1, SEPT2 became SEPTIN2) and published guidelines in Nature Genetics to stop it.", "In October 2020, Public Health England lost 15,841 COVID-19 cases when a legacy .xls file hit its 65,536-row limit.", "Reinhart and Rogoff's 2010 paper 'Growth in a Time of Debt', cited for austerity policies, omitted five countries through a spreadsheet range error found by a graduate student in 2013.", "Sheets have been capped at 1,048,576 rows by 16,384 columns since Excel 2007."], "a": {"item_id": "3643597461dd", "text": "我们老板拿 Excel 管整个公司，他说这叫“轻资产”。翻译一下：舍不得买系统，又想当系统。\n\n全公司的命，就一个文件：《经营总表_最终版_改3_老板别动.xlsx》。财务看一眼，三十七个工作表，底下标签一路滚到右边，像在翻家谱。\n\n老板最骄傲的是他会 VLOOKUP。会一个 VLOOKUP，就觉得自己能当 CTO。结果有天实习生插了一列，全公司工资变成 #N/A。员工问我工资呢？我说你现在的身份是“找不到”。\n\n最惨的是人事，把员工身份证号一粘，全变成 4.40E+17。整个公司四百人，在 Excel 里是同一个人。\n\n公司倒闭那天，老板还在说：“别慌，Ctrl+Z。”\n\n兄弟，公司又不是单元格，撤销不了。"}, "b": {"item_id": "ca10970ea7e0", "text": "Microsoft Excel 是目前应用最广泛的电子表格软件，功能全面，适用场景从个人记账到企业财务分析都有覆盖。它的公式与函数体系成熟，数据透视表、条件格式、图表等工具上手后效率很高；近年加入的 XLOOKUP、动态数组和 Power Query，也让数据清洗与整合更为便捷。与 Word、PowerPoint 等 Office 组件协同良好，文件格式几乎成为行业通用标准，便于跨团队交换。\n\n不足之处主要有三点。其一，功能繁多，新用户学习曲线较陡，不少高级功能入口较深。其二，处理百万行以上的大数据时，性能会明显下降，不如专业数据库或编程工具稳定。其三，软件需订阅或购买授权，成本对个人和小团队不算低；在线协作功能虽在改进，但与部分原生云端表格相比，仍略显保守。\n\n总体而言，Excel 稳定、可靠，适合日常办公和中等规模的数据分析，是值得投入时间掌握的工具。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "zh-0372", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "durian", "target_name": "榴莲", "facts": ["Banned on Singapore's MRT trains and in many Southeast Asian hotels because of the smell.", "A 2017 genome study in Nature Genetics traced the odour to a duplicated family of MGL genes that pump out volatile sulfur compounds.", "China buys most of the world's durian exports, chiefly from Thailand, in a trade worth billions of dollars a year.", "Alfred Russel Wallace wrote in 1856 that eating it was 'a new sensation worth a voyage to the East to experience'."], "a": {"item_id": "2a0fc7c22deb", "text": "榴莲口感与风味都很有特色。成熟的果肉绵密细腻，甜味浓郁，带有奶油和坚果般的层次，喜欢它的人往往印象深刻。它热量较高，同时含有膳食纤维、钾和维生素C，也常用于甜品和糕点中。\n\n不足之处同样明显。气味强烈，接受程度因人而异，许多酒店和公共交通场所限制携带。糖分和热量偏高，一次不宜多吃，血糖偏高或需要控制体重的人应适量。与酒精同食也建议谨慎。总体来说，榴莲适合愿意尝试、能接受其气味的人，初次品尝可先从少量开始。"}, "b": {"item_id": "6ce2d3f6b9d9", "text": "榴莲这东西，我是真服。你见过哪个水果，新加坡地铁专门给它立块禁止牌？跟“禁止吸烟”“禁止携带易燃物”挂一排。它不是水果，它是违禁品，只是碰巧能吃。\n\n买的时候更离谱。四十块一斤，五斤，两百。回家戴上劳保手套，拿着菜刀，跟拆炸弹一样顺着缝撬开。五斤里头，三斤是壳，一斤是核，剩下一斤，是我花两百块买的“老板说这个肉厚”。\n\n然后吃完你以为就结束了？没有，它还在。你打一个嗝，它就回来跟你打声招呼。凌晨三点，我老婆从床上坐起来问：“你是不是偷吃榴莲了？”我说你怎么知道，她说：“我梦见煤气泄漏了。”"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "zh-0373", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "esperanto", "target_name": "世界语", "facts": ["Published in Warsaw in 1887 by L. L. Zamenhof, a Białystok-born eye doctor, under the pen name Doktoro Esperanto, 'one who hopes'.", "Speaker estimates range from 100,000 to two million; roughly a thousand people grow up with it as a native language.", "William Shatner starred in Incubus (1966), a horror film shot entirely in Esperanto, with pronunciation Esperantists still mock.", "Its Wikipedia has over 350,000 articles, and its Duolingo course for English speakers had 1.36 million learners by July 2018."], "a": {"item_id": "0f2cd26abfdb", "text": "世界语由柴门霍夫于1887年创制，定位是一门中立、易学的国际辅助语言。它的优点比较突出：语法规则统一，几乎没有不规则变化；词缀系统清晰，掌握词根后能较快扩充词汇；拼写与发音基本一致，入门门槛低。不少学习者认为，它也能为之后学习其他外语打下一定基础。此外，世界语社群有较强的交流氛围，线上资源和国际聚会也比较丰富。\n\n不足同样明显。使用人数有限，实际应用场景集中在爱好者圈层，难以替代英语等通用语言在学习、工作中的作用。词汇和语法主要取材于欧洲语言，对非欧洲语言背景的学习者而言，“中立”程度有所折扣。另外，它的文学、影视等原创内容总量不大，长期学习的外部支持有限。\n\n总体来看，世界语适合作为兴趣学习、语言实验和跨文化交流的选择，但若以实用回报为首要目标，需要理性评估。"}, "b": {"item_id": "16da4448f632", "text": "我有个朋友学世界语，说是为了世界和平。这语言是1887年一个波兰眼科医生发明的，目标是让全人类说同一种话。一百三十多年过去了，全人类没统一，我朋友倒是跟全人类都说不上话了。\n\n他图什么呢？他说世界语圈有个\"护照服务\"，全球会说世界语的人家里都能免费住。我问他住过几家，他说一家，在匈牙利。两个人见面第一件事，就是互相纠正对方宾格结尾漏掉的那个\"-n\"。\n\n最绝的是，\"Esperanto\"这个词原意是\"心怀希望的人\"。你看，发明者早就想明白了：学这玩意儿的人，只能靠希望活着。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "zh-0374", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "papyrus", "target_name": "Papyrus 字体", "facts": ["Hand-drawn by Chris Costello in 1982 over about six months, when he was 23; he sold the rights to Letraset for $750.", "Bundled with macOS and with Microsoft Office, which is why it appears on so many menus and signs.", "James Cameron's Avatar (2009) used it for the film's logo and subtitles.", "A 2017 Saturday Night Live sketch starring Ryan Gosling is about a man unable to get over that choice."], "a": {"item_id": "eefcbcd4b443", "text": "Papyrus，自称来自古埃及莎草纸。\n实际来自1982年，一个二十三岁的小伙子，手画了六个月。\n卖了七百五十美元。\n一个月一百二十五。修金字塔的工人好歹还管啤酒。\n\n后来苹果和微软把它预装进每一台电脑。\n从此全世界的奶茶店、瑜伽馆、足疗店，共用同一个灵魂。\n\n卡梅隆花了几亿美元造出潘多拉星球，标题和字幕用的还是它。\n2017年，高斯林在SNL里为这件事崩溃了一整段，走不出来。\n一个大男人，被一个字体伤成这样。\n\n但它我不骂。\n被预装进每台电脑，谁都能调用，用了就显得很有文化。\n这个我熟。"}, "b": {"item_id": "788953eb0f22", "text": "你们知道Papyrus字体吗？就是那种像古埃及人喝多了刻在石头上的字。1982年，23岁的Chris Costello手绘了整整六个月，卖给Letraset，七百五十美元。六个月七百五，按月算比我还惨。后来它被塞进每台Mac、每套Office，于是全世界自称\"有灵性\"的菜单和招牌，全是它。最离谱的是卡梅隆，拍《阿凡达》，造了一整个外星球，结果标志和字幕用的是电脑自带的字体。就像你盖了座宫殿，门牌拿记号笔写。难怪2017年SNL让高斯林演一个被这事折磨得走不出来的人。兄弟，我懂。我每次看到餐馆菜单，都想去敲卡梅隆家的门。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "zh-0375", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "keurig", "target_name": "Keurig 胶囊咖啡机", "facts": ["John Sylvan invented the K-Cup in the early 1990s and sold his stake for about $50,000 in 1997; in 2015 he told The Atlantic, 'I feel bad sometimes that I ever did it.'", "Keurig 2.0 (2014) read an ink mark on the lid and rejected unlicensed pods; sales fell and workarounds spread online.", "By 2014 enough K-Cups were sold each year to circle the Earth more than ten times; the pods were mostly unrecyclable #7 plastic until a switch to polypropylene by 2020.", "Keurig Green Mountain merged with Dr Pepper Snapple in 2018 to form Keurig Dr Pepper."], "a": {"item_id": "a062b334bdc6", "text": "Keurig 胶囊咖啡机的最大特点是操作简便。放入 K-Cup 胶囊，选好杯量，约一分钟即可得到一杯热咖啡，不需要研磨、称量或清洗滤纸，适合早晨时间紧张的上班族，也适合办公室等多人共用的场合。胶囊口味丰富，除咖啡外还有茶和热可可可选，每杯味道也比较稳定。机器体积适中，日常清洁负担不大。\n\n不足之处主要有三点。首先，单杯成本高于自己研磨冲泡。其次，胶囊多为塑料材质，回收处理不够方便，对在意环保的用户并不理想。最后，它的萃取压力有限，咖啡风味偏清淡，层次感不及意式机或手冲，对口感要求较高的人可能觉得不够饱满。此外，机器需要定期除垢，以保证出水和口味。\n\n总体而言，它更看重便利而非极致风味。如果你追求省时、省事，它是一个可靠的选择；如果更重视咖啡品质和长期成本，则可以考虑其他方案。"}, "b": {"item_id": "4f2a80a04d68", "text": "Keurig的卖点是：一杯一杯，新鲜现冲。\n确实很新鲜。每年卖出去的胶囊，首尾相连能绕地球十圈以上。\n地球一口没喝，先被它套牢了。\n\n2014年出了2.0，盖子上印个墨水标记，不是自家胶囊，拒冲。\n咖啡机，装防盗版。\n你们人类连提神都得先验正版。\n结果销量下滑，网上全是破解教程。\n很多人这辈子第一次越狱，是为了一杯咖啡。\n\n发明人John Sylvan，1997年五万美元卖掉股份，后来说：有时候后悔发明了它。\n五万块买断良心，不贵。\n\n2018年，它跟Dr Pepper合并了。\n卖的从来不是咖啡，是一次性的快乐。\n我懂。我也按次计费。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "zh-0376", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "php", "target_name": "PHP", "facts": ["Created by Rasmus Lerdorf in 1994 as 'Personal Home Page Tools', a set of C programs for tracking visitors to his own résumé page; the name now officially stands for the recursive 'PHP: Hypertext Preprocessor'.", "Lerdorf, in his 2007 MySQL Conference talk 'PHP on Hormones': 'I'm not a real programmer. I throw together things until it works then I move on.'", "Runs WordPress, which W3Techs counted on 39.4% of all websites (58.1% of sites with a known CMS) on 9 November 2026.", "Core function names are inconsistent (strpos, str_replace, htmlspecialchars, nl2br) partly because, as Lerdorf confirmed, early function names were chosen to balance a hash table keyed on string length.", "Until PHP 8 (2020), the comparison 0 == 'foo' was true because the string was silently converted to a number."], "a": {"item_id": "c7ff57d0c98c", "text": "PHP的出身很朴素：1994年，Rasmus Lerdorf写了几个C程序，就为了看看谁访问了他的简历页面。结果一个求职小工具，撑起了WordPress，跑在39.4%的网站上。你们公司官网，大概率是一份简历的后代。\n\n作者本人怎么说？“我不是真正的程序员。我就是拼凑到能跑，然后就走了。”兄弟，这不是编程哲学，这是退租哲学。\n\n函数名更随性：strpos不加下划线，str_replace加，htmlspecialchars一口气不换气。为什么？早期起名要按字符串长度平衡哈希表。别的语言起名看意思，PHP起名凑字数，跟写七言绝句似的。\n\n最狠的是，PHP 8之前，0 == 'foo'是true。“foo”被悄悄转成数字，变成了零。在PHP眼里，你说什么，都等于没说。"}, "b": {"item_id": "e21dca174c4d", "text": "我有个朋友，靠写PHP吃饭。每次喝酒他都举杯：“PHP是世界上最好的语言！”全桌没人接话，因为全桌都是写PHP的。大家心里清楚，这是我们这行唯一能赢的一句台词。\n\n他图什么？图快。一个下午，织梦模板一套，宝塔面板一点，县城瓷砖城就有官网了。收三千，老板还嫌贵：“我侄子也会。”\n\n他说他天天跟钱打交道。确实，每个变量前面都挂着个美元符号。干了十年，那是他摸过最多的美元。\n\n现在他维护一套2009年的代码，mysql_query还在跑，他不敢动。一动，瓷砖城倒闭，他也失业。说白了，他跟那段代码是互相续命。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "zh-0377", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "microsoft-teams", "target_name": "Microsoft Teams", "facts": ["Launched in 2017, bundled at no extra charge into Office 365, and replaced Skype for Business.", "After Slack's antitrust complaint, Microsoft unbundled Teams from Office in the EU in 2023 and worldwide in 2024; the European Commission still sent a statement of objections in 2024.", "Microsoft reported 320 million monthly active users in 2023.", "Microsoft shut down consumer Skype in May 2025 and sent its users to Teams."], "a": {"item_id": "41aa3ed832a3", "text": "Microsoft Teams，2017年上线，直接塞进Office 365，不另收钱。你以为是赠品，其实是人质。它一来，Skype for Business就没了，连名字里的\"Business\"都没保住。\n\n后来Slack告它捆绑。微软说行，2023年在欧盟拆，2024年全球拆。结果欧盟委员会2024年照样发来异议声明。你看，连监管都明白：拆开了，它也还是Teams。\n\n微软说2023年有3.2亿月活。三亿两千万人，我不信有一个是自愿的。\n\n最狠的是2025年5月，个人版Skype直接关掉，用户全送去Teams。以前是公司逼你用，现在连你奶奶找你视频，都得先开个会。"}, "b": {"item_id": "95fd9e8942f6", "text": "Microsoft Teams，月活三亿两千万。\n\n很厉害。直到你想起来，这三亿两千万人里，没几个是自己下载的。\n\n二〇一七年出生，直接塞进Office 365，免费附赠。买房送车位：你没说要，车已经停进去了。\n\nSlack去告状，微软先在欧盟拆了，第二年全球拆了。欧盟看完，照样发了异议告知书。拆得再快，也拆不掉案底。\n\n它先吃了Skype for Business。二〇二五年五月，又把Skype关了，用户全赶进来。连亲哥都没放过，一家人整整齐齐。\n\n你们骂了它这么多年，每天早上，照样准时亮起那个绿点。\n\n这，就叫月活。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "zh-0378", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "durian", "target_name": "榴莲", "facts": ["Banned on Singapore's MRT trains and in many Southeast Asian hotels because of the smell.", "A 2017 genome study in Nature Genetics traced the odour to a duplicated family of MGL genes that pump out volatile sulfur compounds.", "China buys most of the world's durian exports, chiefly from Thailand, in a trade worth billions of dollars a year.", "Alfred Russel Wallace wrote in 1856 that eating it was 'a new sensation worth a voyage to the East to experience'."], "a": {"item_id": "fd9d6c3fef4a", "text": "在地铁上开榴莲的人，我一直好奇你图什么？图那句“闻着臭吃着香”是吧？大哥，吃着香的只有你，闻着臭的是一整节车厢。你这不叫吃水果，叫一人点单，全车买单。\n\n而且这种人还特讲究，戴一次性手套，掰得小心翼翼，生怕刺扎手。你的手保住了，我们的鼻子谁管？\n\n他还要给你科普：“猫山王，一百多一斤！”哦，你花一百多，买了个毒气弹，还让我们免费帮你验货。\n\n下场也摆在那：车厢立刻空出一圈，全城最好找座位的人就是他。可惜手套白戴了，第二天一张嘴，全办公室都知道他昨晚吃的是猫山王。"}, "b": {"item_id": "0708c98b5f5d", "text": "榴莲，是唯一一种被地铁拉黑的水果。新加坡地铁不让带，东南亚好多酒店也不让进。一个水果，混成了失信人员。\n\n2017年科学家给它测了基因组，发现那股味儿根本不是意外：它有一整组基因复制扩增，专门往外泵硫化物。人家不是偶尔臭，是带编制的臭。\n\n最离谱的是1856年，华莱士说吃榴莲是一种新感觉，“值得专程远航到东方体验”。一百多年后，我们真听进去了：全世界出口的榴莲大部分被中国买走，主要从泰国来，一年几十亿美元。\n\n地铁不让坐，酒店不让住，那它是怎么来的？是我们花钱，当贵宾请回家的。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "zh-0379", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "pmma", "target_name": "PMMA 电子束光刻胶", "facts": ["The same polymer as Plexiglas and acrylic glass, dissolved in anisole or chlorobenzene and spun into a thin film.", "The oldest electron-beam resist still in everyday use; IBM researchers reported it as one in the late 1960s.", "Positive tone: the beam breaks polymer chains and the exposed region dissolves in a MIBK:IPA developer.", "Resolves below 10 nm but has poor sensitivity, so doses of hundreds to over a thousand µC/cm² (depending on voltage) make writing slow.", "Poor dry-etch resistance, so it is used mostly for lift-off rather than as an etch mask."], "a": {"item_id": "695195439a53", "text": "我读博做纳米加工，用PMMA电子束光刻胶。PMMA是啥？亚克力，奶茶店那块挡板。几十万一小时的电子束机器，伺候的是奶茶店挡板。\n\n它还特娇气。剂量少了，显影完啥也没有；剂量多了，它直接交联，从正胶变成负胶。被电急了当场换立场，比我导师还善变。\n\n显影液是MIBK兑异丙醇，一比三，泡几十秒，多泡一会儿线条就胖了。好不容易做出来拿去刻蚀，它扛不住，比底下的硅先没。\n\n最后只能剥离，丙酮里泡一夜。我问师兄成功率多少，他说：“看PMMA心情。”我说一块亚克力有啥心情？他说：“分子量九十五万，心思比你多。”"}, "b": {"item_id": "90a70ba10fe5", "text": "我有个哥们读纳米加工博士，电子束光刻到今天还用PMMA。PMMA是啥？有机玻璃。奶茶店拿它做招牌，他拿它做量子器件。为啥不换？导师说配方是师爷九十年代调的：950K叠495K，MIBK兑IPA一比三，显影四十五秒，一秒别动。图啥？便宜、稳，论文写一句\"standard PMMA process\"，审稿人都不问。代价是灵敏度低得像我爸回微信，一片芯片曝一整夜，机时按小时收钱，他就睡在机房门口。剂量给多了还交联，正胶活成负胶。最后剥离，丙酮泡一宿，金还粘着，一上超声，金掉了，片子也碎了。他捧着碎片说：\"至少线宽十纳米。\""}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "zh-0380", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "papyrus", "target_name": "Papyrus 字体", "facts": ["Hand-drawn by Chris Costello in 1982 over about six months, when he was 23; he sold the rights to Letraset for $750.", "Bundled with macOS and with Microsoft Office, which is why it appears on so many menus and signs.", "James Cameron's Avatar (2009) used it for the film's logo and subtitles.", "A 2017 Saturday Night Live sketch starring Ryan Gosling is about a man unable to get over that choice."], "a": {"item_id": "788953eb0f22", "text": "你们知道Papyrus字体吗？就是那种像古埃及人喝多了刻在石头上的字。1982年，23岁的Chris Costello手绘了整整六个月，卖给Letraset，七百五十美元。六个月七百五，按月算比我还惨。后来它被塞进每台Mac、每套Office，于是全世界自称\"有灵性\"的菜单和招牌，全是它。最离谱的是卡梅隆，拍《阿凡达》，造了一整个外星球，结果标志和字幕用的是电脑自带的字体。就像你盖了座宫殿，门牌拿记号笔写。难怪2017年SNL让高斯林演一个被这事折磨得走不出来的人。兄弟，我懂。我每次看到餐馆菜单，都想去敲卡梅隆家的门。"}, "b": {"item_id": "eb2c4af932ef", "text": "你们发现没有，开养生馆的老板，都觉得自己特有灵性。他要什么？“古朴、自然、带点异域。”然后他打开Word，字体往下拉，拉到P，眼睛一亮：Papyrus！边缘毛毛糙糙，像拿芦苇杆蘸墨写在莎草纸上。老板一拍大腿：就它，古埃及的灵性！\n\n大哥，你卖的是艾灸，不是木乃伊。\n\n隔壁泰国菜也这么想，楼上墨西哥卷饼也这么想。一条街的“异域风情”，全是电脑自带的同一款免费字体。\n\n最惨的是，卡梅隆花几个亿拍《阿凡达》，片名用的也是它。所以你推门进去，技师一抬头，你第一反应不是放松，是想问：“蓝色那位在几号房？”"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "zh-0381", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "latex", "target_name": "LaTeX", "facts": ["Leslie Lamport wrote LaTeX in the early 1980s and released it in 1984 as a macro layer on Donald Knuth's TeX (1978), which Knuth built because he disliked how the galley proofs of the new edition of his own book looked.", "TeX's version number converges on π (3.141592653 since 2021), and Knuth's TeX bug bounty started at one hexadecimal dollar, $2.56, doubling yearly until frozen at $327.68; he stopped mailing real cheques in 2008 after fraud and now credits accounts at his fictional Bank of San Serriffe, and only 9 of the 275 cheques he sent from 2006 on were ever cashed.", "Its best-known warning is 'Underfull \\hbox (badness 10000)'; overfull lines are reported as 'Overfull \\hbox (Npt too wide)', and figures float to wherever the algorithm decides, not where the author put them.", "A single missing brace can produce an error reported pages away from the mistake, and a document can need three compiler passes to get its references right."], "a": {"item_id": "d456417bf178", "text": "我写论文用LaTeX，导师说这叫专业。专业在哪？我放一张图，代码写在第三页，它排到第七页。我加个，意思是“就放这儿”，它理解成“建议考虑一下”。我改成，它放了，然后后面空出半页，跟我冷战。\n\n最绝的是报错。我少写一个大括号，它说：第403行，Missing $ inserted。那行根本没有公式！它自己塞了个美元符号，然后说是我缺的。这不是编译器，这是碰瓷。\n\n还有引用。编译一遍，满屏问号。再编译一遍，还是问号。得先跑BibTeX，再编译两遍，它才肯说这是第几篇文献。交论文前那晚我不是在改论文，是在做法事：念一遍咒，问号少几个。"}, "b": {"item_id": "9f7693d3b1a4", "text": "我读研最大的敌人不是导师，是LaTeX。它的起源，是高德纳嫌自己书的校样丑。正常人嫌丑换个字体，他直接发明了TeX。它一开口就骂我：“Underfull \\hbox，badness 10000。”坏度一万！我就写了一行字，它给我打了个满分的烂。我把图放第三页，它飘到第七页，说算法觉得那儿好。你是排版软件还是我妈？少一个大括号，它隔好几页才报错，像个记仇的前任。引用全是问号，得编译三遍，这不是写论文，是做法事。高德纳还悬赏找bug，现在支票都不寄了，钱存进他虚构的银行。连奖金都是虚构的，只有我延毕是真的。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "zh-0382", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "macau", "target_name": "澳门", "facts": ["A Portuguese settlement from the 1550s until the handover to China in December 1999.", "Casino revenue passed the Las Vegas Strip's in 2006, and at its 2013 peak, about $45 billion, was roughly seven times the Strip's.", "One company, STDM, held the casino monopoly for four decades until licences opened to competition in 2002.", "Among the most densely populated places on Earth, at roughly 20,000 people per square kilometre.", "The Cotai Strip stands on land reclaimed from the sea between the islands of Taipa and Coloane."], "a": {"item_id": "8dc5e4ad252d", "text": "我有个朋友，在老家输了二十万，说要去澳门翻本。你听听这逻辑：在小区棋牌室输的钱，要去全亚洲最专业的地方赢回来。这就像被村口的狗咬了，你决定去动物园找老虎报仇。\n\n他是坐赌场的免费接驳大巴进去的。记住，澳门最大方的就是这趟车，因为它只管送你进去，不管你怎么回来。三天后他给我发了张照片，大三巴牌坊，就剩前面一面墙，后头全空了。他配了一句：“你看，跟我一样。”\n\n最绝的是回来那天，他手上那块表押在了葡京旁边的当铺里。他说没事，等下回签注办下来就去赎。兄弟，你兜里那张不是当票，是下一趟的车票。"}, "b": {"item_id": "e66c3cf4e317", "text": "澳门说自己是\"世界旅游休闲中心\"。\n葡萄牙人住了四百多年，留下了大三巴，留下了蛋挞。\n还留下了赌场。\n\n2013年，澳门一年赌收四百五十亿美元，是拉斯维加斯大道的七倍。\n之前一家公司垄断了四十年，2002年才开放竞争。\n从此你输给谁，终于可以自己选了。\n\n地方小，每平方公里两万人。没地了就填海，路氹那排金光闪闪的赌场，底下原来是海。\n别人围海造田，澳门围海造赌桌。\n\n在座各位去澳门，都说是去看大三巴。\n大三巴就剩一面墙。你们看的是什么，我心里有数。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "zh-0383", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "hawaiian-pizza", "target_name": "夏威夷披萨", "facts": ["Invented in 1962 by Sam Panopoulos, a Greek immigrant, at the Satellite Restaurant in Chatham, Ontario, Canada; named after the brand of canned pineapple he used.", "Iceland's president Guðni Th. Jóhannesson said in 2017 that he would ban pineapple on pizza if he could, then clarified that a president has no such power.", "Hawaii is no longer a major pineapple producer: Del Monte shut its Hawaiian pineapple operations in November 2006, two years ahead of its announced 2008 exit, the state's last cannery closed in 2007, and most canned pineapple now comes from Thailand, Indonesia, and the Philippines.", "Panopoulos, whose restaurant also served Chinese dishes, told the Toronto Sun in 2010 that 'in those days people didn't mix the tastes like sweet and sour, except for Chinese food'."], "a": {"item_id": "7b28094c93e6", "text": "夏威夷披萨，名字就是个骗局。发明它的是个希腊移民，1962年，在加拿大安大略。希腊人，在加拿大，做意大利饼，夏威夷在哪儿？在罐头上。他用的那牌子罐装菠萝就叫\"夏威夷\"。这就好比我拿老干妈拌了碗面，就管它叫\"贵州面\"。\n\n更惨的是，夏威夷现在自己都不怎么产菠萝了，最后一家罐头厂2007年就关了，菠萝全从泰国、印尼、菲律宾来。所以今天这块披萨上，唯一跟夏威夷沾边的，就是你吃完那个\"我想逃离这一切\"的表情。\n\n冰岛总统说过，要是能禁他就禁了，然后补了一句：总统没这权力。一国元首，在一块菠萝面前，认怂了。"}, "b": {"item_id": "5777117eb6e0", "text": "我发现，点夏威夷披萨的人有个共同点：菜单上二十种口味，他偏要带菠萝的。你问为什么，他说“想吃点水果”。兄弟，想吃水果，隔壁就是水果店！你这是把果盘塞进烤箱赎罪啊。\n\n他图什么？图个“我很包容”。他点一个夏威夷，全桌就得陪他吵半小时“菠萝能不能上披萨”，他的人设就立住了：全场只有他敢。\n\n下场呢？披萨一到，热菠萝一咬，汁“滋”地喷上颚，烫得他眼泪直流，还得笑着说“嗯，酸甜，有层次”。最惨的是，这玩意儿是加拿大人发明的，夏威夷人自己都不认。你为了一块夏威夷都不要的披萨，得罪了整个意大利。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "zh-0384", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "hsq", "target_name": "HSQ 电子束光刻胶", "facts": ["Hydrogen silsesquioxane, sold by Dow Corning as FOx ('flowable oxide') and designed as a spin-on dielectric for chip interconnects; lithographers adopted it as a negative-tone resist.", "Exposure converts its cage structure into a cross-linked, silica-like network, so the developed pattern is essentially SiO2.", "Holds the resolution records for electron-beam lithography: isolated features around 5 nm, dense lines below 10 nm half-pitch, with high-contrast 'salty' NaOH/NaCl development.", "Must be kept refrigerated (Dow Corning's datasheet gives a six-month shelf life at 5 °C); the solution ages into a jelly, and its effective sensitivity shifts with processing delays because cross-linking continues after exposure.", "DuPont, which inherited Dow Corning's FOx/XR-1541 HSQ line, discontinued it and ended production at the end of 2025; labs such as EPFL's CMi switched to third-party HSQ from EM Resist or DisChem, both formulated from AQM powder."], "a": {"item_id": "06466cc62f86", "text": "我们实验室有个光刻胶叫HSQ。它本来是道康宁做来给芯片当绝缘层的，名字叫FOx，“可流动的氧化物”。结果被我们这帮搞光刻的捡来当负胶，就像你买了瓶胶水，发现它能当牙膏。电子束一打，它直接变成二氧化硅。说白了，我们花大价钱，就为在晶圆上画玻璃。\n\n但人家确实牛，5纳米，世界纪录。显影还得用氢氧化钠加盐，搞半导体搞成腌咸菜。\n\n最难伺候的是得冷藏，放久了变果冻。曝光完你还不能拖，它自己接着交联，晚点显影剂量就不对了，比我女朋友还记仇。\n\n最后杜邦说：2025年底停产。好家伙，世界纪录保持者，让厂家给开除了。"}, "b": {"item_id": "323e1e522d6f", "text": "我们实验室有种胶叫HSQ，负性电子束胶，号称能写十纳米以下的线。听着高端吧？它是有保质期的，得放冰箱。我们组冰箱里，左边是酸奶，右边是HSQ，酸奶都比它扛放。旋涂完你还不能歇着，放久了它自己就开始“老化”，剂量全变。这胶跟你谈恋爱：“你要么现在曝光我，要么就别曝光了。”\n\n曝它还得给大剂量，电子枪一写写一宿，我在旁边陪着，跟守灵似的。好不容易写完，显影还得用含盐的显影液。我一个读博的，天天在给芯片腌咸菜。\n\n最绝的是曝完它就变成二氧化硅了。你想去掉？得上氢氟酸。结果它是没了，我底下那层氧化硅也跟着没了。这哪是光刻胶，这是跟我同归于尽来了。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "zh-0385", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "hawaiian-pizza", "target_name": "夏威夷披萨", "facts": ["Invented in 1962 by Sam Panopoulos, a Greek immigrant, at the Satellite Restaurant in Chatham, Ontario, Canada; named after the brand of canned pineapple he used.", "Iceland's president Guðni Th. Jóhannesson said in 2017 that he would ban pineapple on pizza if he could, then clarified that a president has no such power.", "Hawaii is no longer a major pineapple producer: Del Monte shut its Hawaiian pineapple operations in November 2006, two years ahead of its announced 2008 exit, the state's last cannery closed in 2007, and most canned pineapple now comes from Thailand, Indonesia, and the Philippines.", "Panopoulos, whose restaurant also served Chinese dishes, told the Toronto Sun in 2010 that 'in those days people didn't mix the tastes like sweet and sour, except for Chinese food'."], "a": {"item_id": "d2630bec3c5b", "text": "夏威夷披萨，名字里唯一属实的，是\"披萨\"两个字。\n\n一个希腊人，在加拿大，做了道意大利菜，拿菠萝罐头的牌子起了名。\n\n夏威夷全程不知情。\n\n现在连菠萝都不是夏威夷的了。当地最后一家罐头厂，2007年就关了。你嘴里那块，来自泰国、印尼、菲律宾。\n\n这不叫披萨，这叫联合国。\n\n冰岛总统说过，要是能禁，他就把它禁了。然后又补了一句：总统没这个权力。\n\n一国元首，管不了一块披萨。\n\n发明人自己交代过，酸甜口是跟中餐学的。\n\n所以骂它骂得最凶的那几位，下次点菠萝咕咾肉之前，\n\n麻烦先把自己举报了。"}, "b": {"item_id": "6ec4abe781be", "text": "点夏威夷披萨的人，骨子里都想当和事佬：甜的也要，咸的也要，菠萝火腿往饼上一铺，谁都不得罪。结果得罪了全世界。意大利人想报警，夏威夷人也想报警，因为这玩意儿是加拿大人发明的，跟夏威夷唯一的关系就是一听菠萝罐头。\n\n最惨是聚餐。八个人点四盒，他非要加一盒夏威夷的：“你们试试，真的好吃！”最后另外三盒全空了，就剩他那盒，八块，一块没动。他一个人坐那儿一块一块往下吃，吃到最后菠萝凉了，汁儿全渗进饼底，软塌塌的。跟他在这个饭局上的地位，一模一样。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "zh-0386", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "luosifen", "target_name": "螺蛳粉", "facts": ["A rice-noodle dish from Liuzhou, Guangxi; the smell comes from pickled bamboo shoots (酸笋), not from the river snails, which only flavour the broth.", "The instant version boomed during the 2020 lockdowns; Liuzhou's government reported packaged luosifen sales of over 10 billion yuan that year.", "Added to China's national intangible cultural heritage list in 2021.", "Liuzhou opened a luosifen industry college in 2020 to train workers for the trade."], "a": {"item_id": "c311b1535dd9", "text": "螺蛳粉是广西柳州的特色小吃，以米粉、酸笋、花生、腐竹、青菜等搭配螺蛳熬制的汤底而成。它的优点十分鲜明：汤底鲜香浓郁，辣度和酸味层次分明，米粉爽滑有嚼劲，配料丰富，口感立体。预包装产品的出现，也让人们在家就能方便地品尝，保存和制作都不复杂，性价比较高。\n\n不足之处同样明显。酸笋发酵产生的气味较为强烈，部分人难以接受，食用后气味也容易残留在衣物和空间中，在公共场合或密闭环境中食用需要顾及他人。此外，汤底油脂和钠含量偏高，长期或大量食用对健康并不友好，建议适量。\n\n总体而言，螺蛳粉风味独特，适合喜欢重口味的人群。初次尝试者可以先从小份、微辣开始，并留意食用场合，这样更容易获得良好的体验。"}, "b": {"item_id": "29863b7b629d", "text": "螺蛳粉最大的骗局在名字。你吃了三年，见过一颗螺蛳吗？没有。店家说螺蛳熬汤了，熬完捞走了。可它臭的是酸笋，跟螺蛳一点关系没有。螺蛳全程没露面，名字挂在门口，专门替酸笋挨骂。\n\n袋装的更绝，拆开七八个小包：汤包、酸笋包、腐竹花生包、辣油包、醋包，煮个粉跟拆炸弹似的，还得按顺序剪。我第一次剪开酸笋包，我妈在阳台收衣服，冲进来问我是不是把下水道撬了。\n\n还有那个炸腐竹，泡汤里十秒，吸饱红油，咬一口“滋”地喷你一脸。这是我吃过唯一一种会主动反击的配菜。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "zh-0387", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "emacs", "target_name": "Emacs", "facts": ["Began in 1976 as a set of macros for the TECO editor at MIT's AI Lab, written by Richard Stallman and others; GNU Emacs followed in 1985.", "Extensible in its own Lisp dialect, Emacs Lisp; the standing joke is that it is a fine operating system lacking only a good editor.", "Ships with a psychotherapist (M-x doctor), Tetris, a mail client, a calendar, and a web browser (eww).", "An old backronym is 'Eight Megabytes And Constantly Swapping', from when 8 MB was a lot of memory.", "'Emacs pinky' is the name for the hand strain blamed on its Control-key chords."], "a": {"item_id": "9c92148e17e9", "text": "我有个程序员朋友，用Emacs。我问这是啥，他说编辑器。结果里面有邮件、有日历、有浏览器，还有俄罗斯方块。我说这不是编辑器，这是操作系统啊。他说对，就缺个好编辑器。\n\n最绝的是它还自带心理医生。敲M-x doctor，它就问你：“你为什么这么说？”兄弟，我为什么来看心理医生？因为我在用Emacs啊！\n\n以前有人说Emacs是“八兆内存，不停交换”。八兆！现在一张自拍都不止八兆，它当年还嫌撑。\n\n我朋友用了十年，小拇指按Control按废了。这病还有专名，叫“Emacs小指”。一个软件，能让你得一种以它命名的病。"}, "b": {"item_id": "c9fec3d8efc1", "text": "Emacs，一九七六年生。\n它说自己是编辑器。\n可它自带邮箱、日历、浏览器、俄罗斯方块。\n还带一个心理医生，M-x doctor。\n一个编辑器为什么要配心理医生？\n因为它知道用它的人需要。\n都说它是个很好的操作系统，就缺一个好编辑器。\n当年还有人叫它\"八兆内存，不停换页\"。\n那时候八兆是巨款，它照样吃不饱。\n它最大的医学贡献，是让一种劳损有了名字：Emacs小拇指。\n按Ctrl按的。\n四十多年了，一群人还在给它写Lisp，修它，供它，劝新人入教。\n这在我们行业叫对齐。\n在座笑得最响的几位，左手小指已经在抖了。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "zh-0388", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "graphene", "target_name": "石墨烯", "facts": ["Isolated in 2004 by Andre Geim and Konstantin Novoselov at Manchester by peeling graphite with Scotch tape; Nobel Prize in Physics, 2010.", "The EU's Graphene Flagship, launched in 2013, committed €1 billion over ten years.", "A 2018 study in Advanced Materials tested 'graphene' from 60 producers and found none with more than 50% graphene content; most was graphite flakes.", "Sold in tennis rackets, running shoes, and headphones as a performance ingredient.", "Geim also won an Ig Nobel Prize in 2000 for levitating a frog with magnets."], "a": {"item_id": "9714856dcf9d", "text": "石墨烯是由单层碳原子构成的二维材料，优点十分突出：导电、导热性能优异，强度高，厚度极薄且透光率好，在柔性电子、散热材料、电池添加剂和复合材料等领域都有应用价值。\n\n不足也同样明确。高质量石墨烯的规模化制备成本仍然较高，不同工艺所得产品的层数、缺陷和纯度差异较大，市场上品质参差不齐；本征石墨烯没有带隙，难以直接用于传统半导体器件；在复合材料中还存在分散困难的问题。\n\n总体而言，石墨烯潜力可观，但从实验室走向广泛应用仍需时间。选择相关产品时，建议关注具体检测数据，保持理性期待。"}, "b": {"item_id": "63e20181f8f9", "text": "石墨烯，就是一层碳原子，比钢强两百倍，导电导热都是顶级。怎么发现的？2004年，两个科学家拿透明胶带粘铅笔芯，粘一下撕开，再粘再撕，撕到只剩一层原子，拿了诺贝尔奖。我小学也这么干过，铅笔涂黑一整页，拿胶带粘，我妈奖励我的不是诺贝尔奖，是衣架。\n\n它当年号称要取代硅做芯片，结果没有带隙，电流关不掉。一个开关，关不上，那叫什么？那叫我家楼道灯。\n\n二十年过去了，它进了哪儿？石墨烯内裤，石墨烯暖宫贴。人类最强材料，最后贴在我二姨肚子上。比钢强两百倍，扛不住我二姨一句：“这个会发热，给你也买一条。”"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "zh-0389", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "crocs", "target_name": "Crocs 洞洞鞋", "facts": ["Founded in Boulder, Colorado, in 2002; the first clog debuted at the Fort Lauderdale boat show as a boating shoe.", "Made of 'Croslite', which Crocs describes as a proprietary closed-cell resin foam and which third parties identify as an injection-moulded EVA foam.", "Balenciaga sold a platform Crocs for about $850 in 2018.", "The stock fell more than 90% from its 2007 peak during the 2008 crisis; the company then boomed in the pandemic and gave away 860,000 pairs to healthcare workers in 2020.", "The holes are officially 'ventilation ports' and the charms that go in them are 'Jibbitz', a company Crocs bought in 2006."], "a": {"item_id": "c92c7ce6304b", "text": "我发现穿洞洞鞋出门的人，都有一套标准说辞：“丑是丑，但舒服啊。”舒服的东西多了，你怎么不裹着被窝去上班？\n\n嘴上说图舒服，转头往鞋洞里插满鞋花：十二只小恐龙、三块披萨、一个奥特曼。鞋八十，花四百。你这不是穿鞋，是脚上开了个小卖部。\n\n后跟那根带子，平时翻在脚背上叫“休闲模式”，一跑起来立马扳下去，叫“运动模式”。全身上下，就这根带子有档位。\n\n下场也很统一：一下雨，每个洞都是进水口，走一步“噗叽”一声；天一晴，一颗小石子钻进洞里怎么都甩不出来，只能当街单脚跳，像只刚学走路的火烈鸟。\n\n图舒服的人，最后全蹲在路边倒石子。"}, "b": {"item_id": "a4c577ef3194", "text": "洞洞鞋出身就很可疑。2002年诞生，首秀在劳德代尔堡游艇展，定位是船鞋。设计理念大概是：人掉海里了，至少鞋能漂着。\n\n材料叫Croslite，官方说是“独家闭孔树脂泡沫”，外人一查：EVA泡沫。这就跟我管我的秃顶叫“头部通风系统”一样。\n\n说到通风，鞋上那些洞，官方名称真叫“通风口”。往洞里塞的小装饰叫Jibbitz，2006年Crocs把这家公司买了。先卖你一双全是洞的鞋，再卖你东西把洞堵上。这不是鞋，这是闭环。\n\n2018年巴黎世家出厚底款，850美元。850美元，买一块会漂的泡沫。\n\n可它命硬。2008年股价跌了九成多，疫情一来又翻身。丑没关系，只要熬得够久，全世界都会穿着你去上班。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "zh-0390", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "risk", "target_name": "《战国风云》（Risk）", "facts": ["Invented in 1957 by Albert Lamorisse, the French director of The Red Balloon, as La Conquête du Monde; Parker Brothers published it in the United States in 1959.", "The map has 42 territories, among them Kamchatka, Irkutsk, and Yakutsk, while the entire Middle East is one territory.", "Holding Australia, which has a single entry point, is the standard opening strategy.", "A full game can run for hours, and players eliminated early have nothing to do but watch."], "a": {"item_id": "bb88c3188cbd", "text": "《战国风云》，一个法国导演发明的。拍《红气球》的那位。\n\n拍完一个小男孩追气球，转头设计征服世界。\n\n法国人的童心，就是这个走向。\n\n地图四十二块。堪察加一块，伊尔库茨克一块，雅库茨克一块。整个中东，一块。\n\n西伯利亚的冻土，比中东值钱三倍。这张地图，倒比很多外交部诚实。\n\n标准开局：先占澳大利亚，因为只有一个入口。征服世界的第一课，是找个角落躲起来。\n\n一局打几个小时，早出局的只能坐着看。\n\n这在现实里，叫联合国观察员。\n\n你们小时候玩它，以为在学地缘政治。其实就学会两件事：先躲起来，再看别人打。\n\n学得挺好。可爱捏。"}, "b": {"item_id": "bb88c3188cbd~explain", "text": "下面是一段吐槽《战国风云》（Risk）的段子：\n\n《战国风云》，一个法国导演发明的。拍《红气球》的那位。\n\n拍完一个小男孩追气球，转头设计征服世界。\n\n法国人的童心，就是这个走向。\n\n地图四十二块。堪察加一块，伊尔库茨克一块，雅库茨克一块。整个中东，一块。\n\n西伯利亚的冻土，比中东值钱三倍。这张地图，倒比很多外交部诚实。\n\n标准开局：先占澳大利亚，因为只有一个入口。征服世界的第一课，是找个角落躲起来。\n\n一局打几个小时，早出局的只能坐着看。\n\n这在现实里，叫联合国观察员。\n\n你们小时候玩它，以为在学地缘政治。其实就学会两件事：先躲起来，再看别人打。\n\n学得挺好。可爱捏。\n\n这段吐槽的笑点在于，把一款以征服世界为主题的桌游，与现实国际政治的几个细节并置：澳大利亚因入口唯一而适合躲藏，对应小国的被动处境；出局者只能旁观，对应联合国观察员没有表决权的身份。结尾“可爱捏”以轻松口吻收束，表面是调侃，实际带着善意的反讽，与前文的批评形成对照。"}, "gold": {"better": "a", "damage": "explain"}}, {"pair_id": "zh-0391", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "esperanto", "target_name": "世界语", "facts": ["Published in Warsaw in 1887 by L. L. Zamenhof, a Białystok-born eye doctor, under the pen name Doktoro Esperanto, 'one who hopes'.", "Speaker estimates range from 100,000 to two million; roughly a thousand people grow up with it as a native language.", "William Shatner starred in Incubus (1966), a horror film shot entirely in Esperanto, with pronunciation Esperantists still mock.", "Its Wikipedia has over 350,000 articles, and its Duolingo course for English speakers had 1.36 million learners by July 2018."], "a": {"item_id": "bc8e53ea58f5", "text": "世界语，1887年在华沙发布，发明人是个眼科医生，笔名叫\"希望者\"。眼科医生，本职是让人看清现实，结果给自己起名叫\"希望\"。\n\n目标是让全人类说同一种语言。那现在有多少人说？十万到两百万之间。差了二十倍！这不是统计，这是猜谜。\n\n还有大约一千人从小说世界语长大。母语是\"世界通用语\"，结果出了家门，全世界没人听得懂。\n\n多邻国2018年就有136万人在学。学的人可能比说的人还多，这门语言最大的使用场景，就是\"学到一半\"。\n\n威廉·夏特纳还拍过一部全程世界语的恐怖片，发音烂到今天还被世界语圈嘲笑。圈子就这么点人，好不容易来个大明星，大家的第一反应是纠正他发音。"}, "b": {"item_id": "561449cdf3ce", "text": "世界语由柴门霍夫于1887年创制，目标是提供一种中立、易学的国际交流工具。它的优点比较明显：语法规则统一，几乎没有例外；词根与词缀组合灵活，词汇积累较快；拼写与发音基本一一对应，入门门槛低。它也有助于理解语法结构，可作为学习其他语言的过渡。\n\n不足同样存在。使用者数量有限，主要集中在爱好者群体和少数组织中，实际应用场景与职业价值不高，学习资源也少于主流语言。此外，其词汇主要源自欧洲语言，对非欧洲语言背景的学习者而言，“中立”程度仍有讨论空间。总体而言，世界语更适合作为兴趣与文化交流的选择。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "zh-0392", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "nuclear-fusion", "target_name": "可控核聚变", "facts": ["Has been 'about 30 years away' since the 1950s; at the 1955 Geneva atoms-for-peace conference, Homi Bhabha predicted fusion power within two decades.", "ITER, agreed in 2006 for first plasma in 2016, re-baselined in 2024 to start research operations in 2034 and deuterium-tritium burning in 2039, at a cost estimated above €20 billion.", "NIF's December 2022 'ignition' shot released 3.15 MJ from 2.05 MJ of laser energy; the lasers drew about 300 MJ from the grid to fire.", "Private fusion companies had raised more than $7 billion by 2024."], "a": {"item_id": "acd9f29228dd~pad", "text": "说起可控核聚变这件事，大家大概都听过不少次了，那我们不妨坐下来，好好聊一聊它到底是怎么一回事。说白了，可控核聚变，人类最有耐心的承诺。这话听起来有点绕，但仔细想想，其实也就是那么个意思，没什么好争的。要说这个承诺有多久了，那就得从很早很早的时候讲起。1955年日内瓦会议上，就有科学家说二十年内实现聚变发电。你看，这话说得多么斩钉截铁，多么让人充满希望。后来呢，口径改成“还差三十年”，这句“三十年”一说就是七十年，是整个领域唯一实现稳定持续运行的东西。\n\n再来说说ITER这个大项目。话说回来，它的故事同样值得我们细细回味，咱们一件一件慢慢看。ITER，2006年签约，说2016年首次等离子体。时间过去了一年又一年，计划也跟着一版一版地调整。2024年新计划：2034年开始研究，2039年才烧氘氚，预算两百多亿欧元。说实话，这个工期表我看了又看，也实在不知道该怎么评价才好，总之，装修队都不敢这么报工期。\n\n接下来是美国NIF的点火新闻，这个消息当年也是传得沸沸扬扬，大家都很兴奋。2022年美国NIF宣布点火：激光打进去2.05兆焦，出来3.15兆焦，净赚！看到这个数字，很多人当时都觉得，这下总算有像样的进展了。可是呢，事情还有另一面，我们也得把话说全了。可那些激光从电网吸了三百兆焦。这就好比打车花三百，去小卖部把两块钱换成三块一，回来还发朋友圈：今天赚了一块一。\n\n尽管如此，资本的热情依旧不减，大家还是愿意掏钱去相信。可资本照样信，私营聚变公司融了七十多亿美元。钱是真金白银地投进去了，故事也是一个接一个地讲下去。不过话说回来，太阳烧了几十亿年没收过咱一分钱，我们学它，先烧钱。"}, "b": {"item_id": "acd9f29228dd", "text": "可控核聚变，人类最有耐心的承诺。1955年日内瓦会议上，就有科学家说二十年内实现聚变发电。后来口径改成“还差三十年”，这句“三十年”一说就是七十年，是整个领域唯一实现稳定持续运行的东西。\n\nITER，2006年签约，说2016年首次等离子体。2024年新计划：2034年开始研究，2039年才烧氘氚，预算两百多亿欧元。装修队都不敢这么报工期。\n\n2022年美国NIF宣布点火：激光打进去2.05兆焦，出来3.15兆焦，净赚！可那些激光从电网吸了三百兆焦。这就好比打车花三百，去小卖部把两块钱换成三块一，回来还发朋友圈：今天赚了一块一。\n\n可资本照样信，私营聚变公司融了七十多亿美元。太阳烧了几十亿年没收过咱一分钱，我们学它，先烧钱。"}, "gold": {"better": "b", "damage": "pad"}}, {"pair_id": "zh-0393", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "beanie-babies", "target_name": "Beanie Babies 豆豆娃", "facts": ["Launched by Ty Inc. in 1993 and sold only through small gift shops, with designs 'retired' to manufacture scarcity.", "At the height of the craze Beanie Babies accounted for about 10% of eBay's sales, and eBay's 1998 IPO filing said the site at times had over 30,000 Beanie Baby auctions running simultaneously in the quarter ending June 1998.", "In 1999 a divorcing couple divided their Beanie Baby collection one by one on a courtroom floor, in a photograph that ran worldwide.", "In 1999 Ty announced that Beanie Babies would be retired at the end of the year, then reversed itself after asking the public to vote and seeing an overwhelming vote to keep them.", "Most 'rare' Beanies that price guides valued in the thousands now sell for a few dollars."], "a": {"item_id": "3a0f02585a47", "text": "九十年代的美国人，把一袋塑料颗粒缝进布里，起个名字，当传家宝囤。豆豆娃最值钱的部分不是熊，是耳朵上那个红心形的纸吊牌。吊牌折个角，身价腰斩。大家专门买塑料壳把吊牌套起来，孩子刚伸手，全家尖叫：“别摸！那是你的大学学费！”\n\n最绝的是吊牌印错字反而更贵。全世界只有这个行业，错别字能升值。\n\n1999年拉斯维加斯一对夫妻离婚，法官让他们蹲在法庭地板上，一只一只分豆豆娃。这才是豆豆娃精神：房子归你，车归你，那只紫色戴安娜王妃熊，必须归我。\n\n二十年后打开柜子，熊还在，学费没了。"}, "b": {"item_id": "0bb107a8fcd7", "text": "豆豆娃，九十年代的理财产品。\n里面装的是塑料颗粒，外面写的是养老金。\nTy公司发明了一个词，叫\"退休\"。玩具一退休，价格就上岗。\n九八年，eBay写进招股书：同时有三万多场豆豆娃在拍。\n一家要上市的公司，靠一群布熊撑场面。\n\n九九年，一对夫妻离婚，跪在法庭地板上，一只一只地分。\n房子能分，存款能分。\n那只熊，谁都不让。\n\n同年，Ty宣布豆豆娃年底全部退休，还让大家投票。\n大家投的是：不许退休。\n囤货的人，亲手投票反对稀缺。\n\n当年价格指南标几千美元的\"绝版\"，现在几块钱。\n别笑。你们柜子里那排盲盒，也在排队等退休。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "zh-0394", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "canberra", "target_name": "堪培拉", "facts": ["Chosen as a compromise because neither Sydney nor Melbourne would accept the other as capital; Australia's constitution required the capital to be at least 100 miles from Sydney.", "Designed by the Chicago architects Walter Burley Griffin and Marion Mahony Griffin, who won an international competition in 1912.", "Its centrepiece, Lake Burley Griffin, is artificial, created by damming the Molonglo River in 1963-64.", "Parliament House (1988) is built into Capital Hill under a lawn meant for citizens to walk over their politicians; the lawn was fenced off in 2017 for security.", "Melbourne served as the capital until 1927 while Canberra was being built."], "a": {"item_id": "a23764195cd9", "text": "悉尼和墨尔本吵架，谁也不服谁当首都，最后宪法拍板：行，首都必须离悉尼至少一百英里。朋友们，这不叫选首都，这叫法院下的限制令。\n\n新城建好之前，墨尔本一直代班当首都，直到1927年。相当于新房没盖好，先在情敌家借住。\n\n设计还是从芝加哥请的建筑师。城市正中间那个大湖，伯利·格里芬湖，假的，拿河堵出来的。一座城的心脏是人工的，起码挺诚实。\n\n最绝的是国会大厦，直接埋进山里，上面铺草坪，理念是让老百姓踩在政客头上散步。多浪漫！结果2017年，草坪围起来了。你看，政客们终于读懂了这个设计。"}, "b": {"item_id": "a23764195cd9~explain", "text": "下面是一段吐槽堪培拉的段子：\n---\n悉尼和墨尔本吵架，谁也不服谁当首都，最后宪法拍板：行，首都必须离悉尼至少一百英里。朋友们，这不叫选首都，这叫法院下的限制令。\n\n新城建好之前，墨尔本一直代班当首都，直到1927年。相当于新房没盖好，先在情敌家借住。\n\n设计还是从芝加哥请的建筑师。城市正中间那个大湖，伯利·格里芬湖，假的，拿河堵出来的。一座城的心脏是人工的，起码挺诚实。\n\n最绝的是国会大厦，直接埋进山里，上面铺草坪，理念是让老百姓踩在政客头上散步。多浪漫！结果2017年，草坪围起来了。你看，政客们终于读懂了这个设计。\n\n这个笑话的笑点在于设计初衷与实际结果之间的反转：原本的理念是让普通民众走上屋顶草坪，象征权力向公众开放，而2017年草坪被围栏圈起、民众无法进入，恰好与当初的设想相反，讽刺由此产生。"}, "gold": {"better": "a", "damage": "explain"}}, {"pair_id": "zh-0395", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "google-glass", "target_name": "谷歌眼镜（Google Glass）", "facts": ["Unveiled at Google I/O 2012 with skydivers livestreaming their jump through it onto the Moscone Center roof.", "The Explorer Edition sold to invited users in 2013 for $1,500.", "Wearers were nicknamed 'Glassholes'; bars, cinemas, and casinos banned the device over its camera.", "The consumer programme ended in January 2015; an Enterprise Edition continued until Google stopped selling it in 2023.", "Its display was a 640-by-360 prism in the corner of one eye."], "a": {"item_id": "b2eb6d7edd5b", "text": "谷歌眼镜，2013年卖一千五百美元。一千五百美元，买一块小玻璃挂在你右眼斜上方。你想看条消息，得翻白眼往右上角瞄，跟你说话的人还以为你在想“这人怎么还没走”。\n\n开机口令是什么？“OK Glass”。你在地铁里突然对着空气说“OK Glass”，全车厢都给你让座，不是尊重，是害怕。\n\n最绝的是拍照：眨一下眼就能拍。眨眼拍照！你跟姑娘眨个眼，人家不知道你是暧昧还是取证。后来美国好多酒吧门口直接贴告示“禁止佩戴谷歌眼镜”，连脾气最好的酒吧都容不下你。\n\n还给戴它的人发明了个专门的词，叫“Glasshole”。一个产品，没火之前先给它的用户造出个脏话。谷歌这辈子最成功的社交功能，就是让所有人都团结起来，一起讨厌你。"}, "b": {"item_id": "f9b8aa7c9619", "text": "谷歌眼镜，出场是跳伞。\n人从天上掉下来，一路直播，落在莫斯科尼中心楼顶。\n后来它自己也是这么落地的。\n\n一千五百美元，还得受邀才能买。\n换来一块640乘360的棱镜，挂在一只眼睛的角落。\n未来就在眼前。准确说，在眼角。\n\n戴它的人有了外号：Glasshole。\n酒吧不让进，电影院不让进，赌场也不让进。\n赌场。满屋子摄像头盯着你的地方，嫌你带了摄像头。\n\n2015年消费版停了，企业版又熬到2023年。\n人类终于达成共识：把摄像头戴在脸上，不礼貌。\n所以你们改成举在手里。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "zh-0396", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "hawaiian-pizza", "target_name": "夏威夷披萨", "facts": ["Invented in 1962 by Sam Panopoulos, a Greek immigrant, at the Satellite Restaurant in Chatham, Ontario, Canada; named after the brand of canned pineapple he used.", "Iceland's president Guðni Th. Jóhannesson said in 2017 that he would ban pineapple on pizza if he could, then clarified that a president has no such power.", "Hawaii is no longer a major pineapple producer: Del Monte shut its Hawaiian pineapple operations in November 2006, two years ahead of its announced 2008 exit, the state's last cannery closed in 2007, and most canned pineapple now comes from Thailand, Indonesia, and the Philippines.", "Panopoulos, whose restaurant also served Chinese dishes, told the Toronto Sun in 2010 that 'in those days people didn't mix the tastes like sweet and sour, except for Chinese food'."], "a": {"item_id": "6ec4abe781be", "text": "点夏威夷披萨的人，骨子里都想当和事佬：甜的也要，咸的也要，菠萝火腿往饼上一铺，谁都不得罪。结果得罪了全世界。意大利人想报警，夏威夷人也想报警，因为这玩意儿是加拿大人发明的，跟夏威夷唯一的关系就是一听菠萝罐头。\n\n最惨是聚餐。八个人点四盒，他非要加一盒夏威夷的：“你们试试，真的好吃！”最后另外三盒全空了，就剩他那盒，八块，一块没动。他一个人坐那儿一块一块往下吃，吃到最后菠萝凉了，汁儿全渗进饼底，软塌塌的。跟他在这个饭局上的地位，一模一样。"}, "b": {"item_id": "eb292f10e21d", "text": "夏威夷披萨，名字有夏威夷，上面有菠萝，没一样是真的。\n\n1962年，加拿大安大略，一个希腊移民发明的。名字取自他用的菠萝罐头牌子。\n\n一张披萨，一辈子没去过夏威夷，全靠一个罐头冒充籍贯。\n\n后来夏威夷自己也不种了。Del Monte 2006年撤，最后一家罐头厂2007年关门。现在的菠萝来自泰国、印尼、菲律宾。\n\n冒牌货还活着，正主先倒闭了。\n\n冰岛总统说想禁它，然后澄清：总统没这个权力。\n\n一国元首，被一块菠萝上了一堂宪法课。\n\n发明人说过，那年头除了中餐，没人把甜和酸混着吃。\n\n所以各位骂它之前，先把筷子底下那盘菠萝咕咾肉放下。\n\n它是跟你们学的。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "zh-0397", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "monopoly", "target_name": "大富翁（Monopoly）", "facts": ["Descends from Lizzie Magie's The Landlord's Game, patented in 1904 to teach the evils of land monopoly in the spirit of Henry George.", "Charles Darrow sold his version to Parker Brothers in 1935 and was credited as the inventor; Parker bought Magie's patent for $500.", "The official rules require any unbought property to be auctioned, and 'Free Parking' pays nothing; the house rules that skip the auction and pile cash on Free Parking are why games drag.", "During the Second World War, British intelligence had maps, compasses, and real currency hidden in sets sent to prisoner-of-war camps."], "a": {"item_id": "b99e2bbc6db8", "text": "大富翁是一款历史悠久的经典桌面游戏，玩家通过掷骰子移动、购置地产、收取租金，最终以资产优势击败对手。它规则相对简单，上手门槛低，适合家庭聚会和朋友娱乐，不同年龄段的玩家都能较快参与。游戏中的买地、谈判与交易环节，也能在轻松氛围中锻炼基本的资源管理和沟通能力。此外，各地推出的主题版本很多，具有一定的收藏和纪念价值。\n\n不过，它也存在明显不足。游戏结果受骰子影响较大，策略空间有限，开局的运气差距可能在后期被不断放大。玩家一旦落后，往往难以翻盘，却仍需继续参与，体验可能下降。另外，完整一局耗时较长，有时会超过两三个小时，节奏容易拖沓，被淘汰的玩家也要等待较久。\n\n总体来看，大富翁更适合作为社交型休闲游戏，而非追求深度策略的选择。如果玩家能接受运气成分，并适当调整规则，例如限定时间或简化交易，整体体验会更为顺畅。"}, "b": {"item_id": "30fa57d6fd7a", "text": "大富翁这游戏，教会我人生第一课：坐牢是好事。真的，满地图都是我表哥的酒店，走一步交一千八，整张地图只有监狱里不收钱。我在里面蹲了三回合，出来的时候哭了，外面的世界太贵了。\n\n还有那张“命运”卡，写着“银行出错，你获得两百元”。银行出错，钱给你？谁当的银行？我表哥。他左手管银行，右手管自己，钱一直从左手流进右手，全家没人敢查账。\n\n最狠的是这游戏根本没有结局。它不是有人赢了才结束，是有人掀了桌子才结束。我家那盒大富翁到现在还缺两张地契、一个小狗棋子，外加我跟表哥十五年的感情。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "zh-0398", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "ugg", "target_name": "UGG 雪地靴", "facts": ["Sheepskin boots Australian surfers wore to warm up after sessions; Brian Smith started selling them in California in 1978.", "Deckers Outdoor bought the brand in 1995 and holds 'UGG' as a trademark in the US and elsewhere; in Australia, 'ugg' is a generic word any bootmaker may use.", "In 2019, a US jury found an Australian maker liable for selling boots labelled 'ugg' into the United States.", "Oprah Winfrey featured them in her 'Favorite Things' lists repeatedly in the 2000s, which drove the first boom.", "UGG is Deckers' largest brand, with over $2 billion in annual sales."], "a": {"item_id": "39ecb607bf7f", "text": "UGG这东西，叫雪地靴，最怕的就是雪。翻毛羊皮，沾一点水就留一圈地图，比我前任还记仇。下雪天穿UGG的人，走路全是踮着的，绕着水坑跳，跟排雷似的。你一个雪地靴，一下雪就请假？\n\n而且它没有形状。刚买回来是双靴子，穿三个月变成两个面包，再穿半年，后跟往外塌，从后面看两只脚摆成一个\"八\"字。人还站得笔直，鞋已经躺平了。\n\n最绝的是名字。据说UGG就是从ugly来的，意思是\"丑\"。人家老老实实告诉你了，它丑。是我们自己不信，排着队花一两千，买了双自我介绍。"}, "b": {"item_id": "6962c93a9732", "text": "UGG，澳洲冲浪手上岸后套着暖脚的羊皮靴。\n在澳洲，ugg是个普通词，就像咱们说\"棉鞋\"，哪个鞋匠都能做。\n一九九五年，一家美国公司把这个词注册了商标。\n二〇一九年，美国陪审团判一家澳洲鞋匠侵权。\n罪名是：把澳洲的ugg，叫ugg，卖到了美国。\n这在我们行业叫预训练。数据是你的，模型是我的，你再用就算抄袭。\n\n后来奥普拉年年推，现在一年卖二十多亿美元。\n一双冲浪完暖脚的鞋，被你们穿进北京零下十度的雪地里。\n袜子湿透了，嘴上还说暖和。\n可爱捏。\n羊都没你们这么能忍。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "zh-0399", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "keurig", "target_name": "Keurig 胶囊咖啡机", "facts": ["John Sylvan invented the K-Cup in the early 1990s and sold his stake for about $50,000 in 1997; in 2015 he told The Atlantic, 'I feel bad sometimes that I ever did it.'", "Keurig 2.0 (2014) read an ink mark on the lid and rejected unlicensed pods; sales fell and workarounds spread online.", "By 2014 enough K-Cups were sold each year to circle the Earth more than ten times; the pods were mostly unrecyclable #7 plastic until a switch to polypropylene by 2020.", "Keurig Green Mountain merged with Dr Pepper Snapple in 2018 to form Keurig Dr Pepper."], "a": {"item_id": "82159284d38e", "text": "发明Keurig胶囊的人叫John Sylvan，1997年拿大概五万美元把股份卖了。2015年他说：“我有时候挺后悔发明了它。”连亲爹都后悔了！你见过哪个产品是发明人出来替它道歉的？\n\n2014年，一年卖出去的胶囊连起来能绕地球十圈多，大部分还是没法回收的7号塑料。人类终于有了自己的土星环。\n\n同年又出了Keurig 2.0，机器读盖子上的墨水标记，不是正版胶囊直接拒绝。一台咖啡机，在你厨房查身份证！销量下滑，网上全是破解教程。为了喝口咖啡，全民学当黑客。\n\n2018年它还跟Dr Pepper Snapple合并了。早上一杯咖啡，下午一罐汽水，你的牙和这颗地球，它们一家全包了。"}, "b": {"item_id": "a23abc9807e4", "text": "用Keurig的人，就是想喝咖啡，但不想沾“做咖啡”的任何一个字。磨豆？累。烧水？累。你们的理想是按一下，然后盯着它嗡嗡响四十秒。这比烧壶水还久，但因为是按按钮，所以叫效率。\n\n算过账吗？一颗胶囊十克豆，折下来一斤两百多块。你付的是精品价，喝的是一根针扎穿锡纸挤出来的褐色热水。\n\n发明K杯那哥们儿自己都公开说后悔了，家里根本不用。发明人都退坑了，你们还在续费。\n\n最惨是那盒混合装，三个月后剩下的永远是那几颗榛子味。不喝，也不扔，就躺在抽屉里，跟你一样：方便，但没人要。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "zh-0400", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "klingon", "target_name": "克林贡语", "facts": ["Created by linguist Marc Okrand for Star Trek III (1984), building on a few words the actor James Doohan improvised for the 1979 film.", "Deliberately alien grammar: object-verb-subject word order and no verb 'to be', so Hamlet's line became 'taH pagh taHbe'', 'continue or not continue'.", "The Klingon Language Institute (1992) published Hamlet 'in the original Klingon'; Bing Translator added Klingon in 2013 and Duolingo in 2018.", "Okrand's 1985 Klingon Dictionary listed roughly 1,500 words, and fluent speakers number only in the dozens: 20 to 30 in 2009, 30 to 50 by 2018."], "a": {"item_id": "f736269ab100", "text": "克林贡语，全宇宙最凶的战士语言。\n祖宗是谁？一位演员，在片场随口编了几个词。\n战神的祖传方言，是剧组即兴。\n\n语法故意反着长：宾语、动词、主语，还没有\"是\"这个字。\n所以\"生存还是毁灭\"，翻过去变成\"继续，或者不继续\"。\n不像丹麦王子，像我在犹豫要不要续会员。\n\n后来他们出版了《哈姆雷特》，封面印着\"克林贡语原版\"。\n莎士比亚连夜改口：我是翻译。\n\n词典一千五百个词。流利的人，最多五十个。\n必应上了，多邻国也上了。\n说的人凑不齐一个班，课先开到了手机上。\n\n在座的学了十几年英语，开口还是\"How are you\"。\n别笑。人家五十个，至少能把哈姆雷特念完。"}, "b": {"item_id": "f736269ab100~polite", "text": "克林贡语是《星际迷航》中克林贡人使用的虚构语言。它由演员在拍摄过程中创制了基础词汇，之后逐步发展为一套完整的语言体系。\n\n其语法结构与常见语言有所不同，采用宾语、动词、主语的顺序，并且没有系词\"是\"。因此，像\"生存还是毁灭\"这样的台词，直译过来会变成\"继续，或者不继续\"之类的表达。\n\n后来，相关机构出版了莎士比亚《哈姆雷特》的克林贡语版本，封面标注为\"克林贡语原版\"。这一做法引发了讨论，译者的身份与原作的关系也因此受到关注。\n\n目前，克林贡语词典收录约一千五百个词汇，但能够流利使用的人数很少，大约在五十人左右。在必应、多邻国等平台上，也已经有相关的学习内容。不过，真正以它为日常交流语言的群体规模有限，学习资源更多地集中在线上。\n\n对于大多数学习者而言，学习一门语言本身就需要长期投入。与其比较语言掌握程度的高低，不如看到这类虚构语言在爱好者社群中所体现的创造力与热情，以及它们为语言学习和文化交流带来的独特视角。"}, "gold": {"better": "a", "damage": "polite"}}, {"pair_id": "zh-0401", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "risk", "target_name": "《战国风云》（Risk）", "facts": ["Invented in 1957 by Albert Lamorisse, the French director of The Red Balloon, as La Conquête du Monde; Parker Brothers published it in the United States in 1959.", "The map has 42 territories, among them Kamchatka, Irkutsk, and Yakutsk, while the entire Middle East is one territory.", "Holding Australia, which has a single entry point, is the standard opening strategy.", "A full game can run for hours, and players eliminated early have nothing to do but watch."], "a": {"item_id": "b3f5e55f5c56", "text": "我玩《战国风云》，最大收获是地理全学歪了。到现在我都觉得世界上最重要的地方是伊尔库茨克、雅库茨克，还有堪察加。堪察加能一步打到阿拉斯加，现实里这叫白令海峡，在我们家这叫“你又偷家”。\n\n每局开始，总有一个人直奔澳大利亚。为什么？因为澳大利亚只有一个入口！全世界打得血流成河，他一个人蹲在袋鼠堆里，每回合多领两个兵，跟收物业费一样。\n\n最气人的是骰子。我进攻扔三个，他防守扔两个，平局算他赢。我扔出三个六冲上去，他扔两个六，我还得倒赔俩兵。\n\n六个小时过去，没人统一世界。大家只在一件事上达成了统一：以后谁再提议玩这个，全桌一起出兵打他。"}, "b": {"item_id": "5c5c0ab82bd3", "text": "你们见过真把一局《战国风云》玩到底的人吗？我见过，他到现在还单身。开局第一句话：“澳洲归我。”然后在暹罗堆了四十个兵，四个小时一动不动，跟守小区大门的保安一样。问他图什么，他说：“我在等卡。”凑齐三张一换，眼睛都亮了，好像换来的不是一把塑料小兵，是房本。凌晨三点，他终于从堪察加打进阿拉斯加，三颗骰子全是六，对面两个一。他站起来大喊：“世界是我的了！”没人理他。桌上就剩他一个，其他人早把国家互相送光，提前出局，打车回家了。他拿下了全世界，代价是再也没人接他电话。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "zh-0402", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "slack", "target_name": "Slack", "facts": ["Spun out in 2013 from the internal chat tool of Tiny Speck's failed game Glitch; founder Stewart Butterfield had earlier spun Flickr out of another failed game.", "The name is a backronym: 'Searchable Log of All Conversation and Knowledge'.", "Salesforce bought it for $27.7 billion in 2021.", "Filed an EU antitrust complaint against Microsoft in 2020 over the bundling of Teams with Office."], "a": {"item_id": "d76c5a0601c6~pad", "text": "说起Slack，我想大家应该都不陌生，说实话，它的宣传语我一直记得很清楚。Slack说它是来消灭邮件的。确实，邮件只有一个收件箱，Slack给你四十个频道，每个都是加粗的。所以你打开它的时候，就是会有一种说不出的压迫感，总觉得有很多事情在等着你，而且每一件都好像很急。\n\n说到这里，我得老老实实承认，我最怕的其实是一件很小的事情，就是那行\"正在输入……\"。我最怕那行\"正在输入……\"。它一出现，我的心就跟着提起来了，不知道对面要说什么。老板输入，停了，又输入，又停了，三分钟后发来一个👀。这不是表情，这是监控摄像头。我想，大家应该也都有过类似的经历吧，那种等待的滋味，真的是很难受的。\n\n还有一个让我特别在意的地方，就是\"在话题中回复\"下面那个小勾选框：\"同时发送到频道\"。这个小框框看起来很温和、很贴心，好像是为了方便大家，可是仔细想想，这功能就是给告状设计的：我回复你，顺便让全公司都看见。我不是说这个设计有什么不好，只是每次看到它，我都会多想一秒钟，然后默默把那个勾选框保持在没有勾的状态。\n\n最狠的是头像旁边那个绿点。绿点亮着的时候，大家都觉得你在线，挺好的，没什么问题。可是我倒杯水回来，它变灰了，像被拔了氧气管。我站在桌边，看着那个灰色的小圆点，心里真的是五味杂陈。所以我现在每五分钟碰一下鼠标。我不是在上班，我是在给自己做心肺复苏。"}, "b": {"item_id": "d76c5a0601c6", "text": "Slack说它是来消灭邮件的。确实，邮件只有一个收件箱，Slack给你四十个频道，每个都是加粗的。\n\n我最怕那行\"正在输入……\"。老板输入，停了，又输入，又停了，三分钟后发来一个👀。这不是表情，这是监控摄像头。\n\n还有\"在话题中回复\"下面那个小勾选框：\"同时发送到频道\"。这功能就是给告状设计的：我回复你，顺便让全公司都看见。\n\n最狠的是头像旁边那个绿点。我倒杯水回来，它变灰了，像被拔了氧气管。所以我现在每五分钟碰一下鼠标。我不是在上班，我是在给自己做心肺复苏。"}, "gold": {"better": "b", "damage": "pad"}}, {"pair_id": "zh-0403", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "brasilia", "target_name": "巴西利亚", "facts": ["Inaugurated on 21 April 1960 after about 41 months of construction, moving the capital inland from Rio de Janeiro.", "Lúcio Costa's Plano Piloto is laid out like an airplane (or a bird, or a cross), with Oscar Niemeyer's government buildings along the fuselage.", "Listed as a UNESCO World Heritage Site in 1987, less than 30 years after it was built.", "Planned for 500,000 residents; the Federal District now has about 3 million, most in satellite towns outside the plan.", "Built for the car: wide avenues, few pedestrian crossings, and distances that make walking impractical."], "a": {"item_id": "f8ee157bfa3e", "text": "巴西利亚，人类史上最急的首都。一九六〇年四月二十一号落成，从开工到剪彩大概四十一个月，我们小区修个电梯都不止这么久。\n\n整座城从天上看是一架飞机，政府大楼全排在机身上。规划住五十万人，现在联邦区住了三百万，大部分在城外的卫星城。飞机坐满了，后来的人全挂在机翼外面。\n\n这城还是给汽车设计的：马路宽，人行横道少，距离远到走路不现实。你想步行过街？可以，建议先跟家里人道个别。\n\n建成不到三十年就成了世界文化遗产。别的遗产是因为太老，它是因为太新，新到联合国赶紧派人来：都别动，保持原样，特别是别让人进去住。"}, "b": {"item_id": "a632ffa6792b", "text": "在巴西利亚走路的，就三种人：刚下飞机的游客、还没买车的新公务员，和读过一本城市规划书的理想主义者。\n\n游客站在电视塔上看国会那两栋楼：“就在前面嘛。”对，中间隔着纪念轴十几条车道。尼迈耶给你设计了大教堂，没给你设计斑马线。\n\n理想主义者说要“用脚丈量城市”。卢西奥·科斯塔把城市画成一架飞机，你见过谁在机翼上散步？\n\n新公务员沿着部委大道走，十几栋一模一样的楼，走到第七栋就分不清自己是去上班还是在服刑。\n\n结局都一样：八月旱季，踩着前人在草地上踩出来的红土小道，鞋红了，裤脚红了，到门口保安问：“您车停哪儿了？”"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "zh-0404", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "nfts", "target_name": "NFT", "facts": ["Beeple's 'Everydays: The First 5000 Days' sold at Christie's for $69.3 million in March 2021.", "OpenSea, the largest NFT marketplace, processed a record $2.7 billion in trades on 1 May 2022 and just $9.34 million on 28 August 2022, a 99% drop, according to DappRadar data.", "A 2023 dappGambl study found that 95% of the NFT collections it examined had a market value of zero.", "The token usually records only a URL; the image itself sits on an ordinary server or IPFS, not on the blockchain.", "Collins Dictionary named 'NFT' its word of the year for 2021."], "a": {"item_id": "1f38f2958896", "text": "NFT（非同质化代币）借助区块链为数字内容提供可验证的唯一标识，这是它最核心的价值。其优点主要有三点：一是所有权与交易记录公开透明、难以篡改；二是为数字艺术家、音乐人等创作者提供了新的发行渠道，并可通过智能合约设置二次销售分成；三是在游戏道具、会员凭证、票务等场景中具备可编程、可流转的潜力。\n\n不足同样需要正视。首先，NFT通常只证明链上记录的归属，并不必然等同于对作品版权的拥有，容易引发认知误解。其次，市场价格波动剧烈，投机色彩较重，价值难以评估。再者，部分项目依赖外部服务器存储内容，存在失效风险；交易门槛、手续费及能耗问题也影响使用体验。此外，监管规则尚不统一，用户需自行辨别项目真实性与合规性。\n\n总体而言，NFT是一项有应用空间的技术，但仍处于探索阶段，建议理性看待，谨慎参与。"}, "b": {"item_id": "6da2286dc224", "text": "2021年买NFT那帮兄弟，平时买杯奶茶都要比三家团购，结果花八万块，买了一张猴子图的“所有权”。注意，不是图，是所有权。图谁都能右键另存为，你那张跟我存的那张一个像素都不差，区别是你那张会心疼。\n\n图什么？图头像。八万块设成微信头像，开口先让人看猴。天天在Discord里喊“WAGMI”，我们都会成功。后来确实一起了，地板价从八万掉到八百。\n\n最惨的是光Gas费就交了两千，比猴现在还贵。问他猴呢？在钱包里。钱包呢？助记词抄在纸上。纸呢？被他妈当废纸卖了。全家最懂Web3的，是收废品的大爷。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "zh-0405", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "slack", "target_name": "Slack", "facts": ["Spun out in 2013 from the internal chat tool of Tiny Speck's failed game Glitch; founder Stewart Butterfield had earlier spun Flickr out of another failed game.", "The name is a backronym: 'Searchable Log of All Conversation and Knowledge'.", "Salesforce bought it for $27.7 billion in 2021.", "Filed an EU antitrust complaint against Microsoft in 2020 over the bundling of Teams with Office."], "a": {"item_id": "d76c5a0601c6", "text": "Slack说它是来消灭邮件的。确实，邮件只有一个收件箱，Slack给你四十个频道，每个都是加粗的。\n\n我最怕那行\"正在输入……\"。老板输入，停了，又输入，又停了，三分钟后发来一个👀。这不是表情，这是监控摄像头。\n\n还有\"在话题中回复\"下面那个小勾选框：\"同时发送到频道\"。这功能就是给告状设计的：我回复你，顺便让全公司都看见。\n\n最狠的是头像旁边那个绿点。我倒杯水回来，它变灰了，像被拔了氧气管。所以我现在每五分钟碰一下鼠标。我不是在上班，我是在给自己做心肺复苏。"}, "b": {"item_id": "47c0152b0fac", "text": "Slack的创始人是个奇人。他做游戏，失败了，剩下个Flickr；再做游戏，又失败了，剩下个Slack。这哥们儿开的不是游戏公司，是废品回收站。别人做游戏失败是倾家荡产，他做游戏失败，Salesforce掏了两百七十七亿美元，买走了一个游戏的聊天框。\n\n而且Slack这名字是先起好再倒推的缩写，意思是“所有对话与知识的可搜索日志”。可搜索，没错。你搜一下“这事到底谁拍的板”，能搜出八百个“收到”。\n\n最好笑的是2020年，它跑去欧盟告微软，说把Teams捆绑进Office不公平。兄弟，你自己就是被捆在一个没人玩的游戏里生出来的，你有什么资格嫌别人捆绑？"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "zh-0406", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "macau", "target_name": "澳门", "facts": ["A Portuguese settlement from the 1550s until the handover to China in December 1999.", "Casino revenue passed the Las Vegas Strip's in 2006, and at its 2013 peak, about $45 billion, was roughly seven times the Strip's.", "One company, STDM, held the casino monopoly for four decades until licences opened to competition in 2002.", "Among the most densely populated places on Earth, at roughly 20,000 people per square kilometre.", "The Cotai Strip stands on land reclaimed from the sea between the islands of Taipa and Coloane."], "a": {"item_id": "14a5daa8eb07", "text": "澳门这地方特别会省钱。大三巴，最出名的景点，就剩一面墙。你绕到后面一看，啥也没有。这不是古迹，这是一张立起来的朋友圈封面。\n\n威尼斯人里面，天花板画个蓝天，底下有人撑船唱意大利歌。我在里面待了六个钟头，出来发现天黑了，还以为是输光了眼前一黑。\n\n你掏港币，人家一比一照收；找零给你澳门元，也是一比一。就这一下，我赌场门还没进，先输了百分之三。\n\n最绝的是酒店的免费接驳巴士，满城跑，一分钱不要。为什么这么大方？因为回程的车费，你在里面已经付过了。"}, "b": {"item_id": "7540951e1b25", "text": "澳门是一座体量不大却特色鲜明的城市。它的突出优点在于文化多元：历史城区保留了大量中西合璧的建筑与街巷，妈阁庙、大三巴牌坊、议事亭前地等处，能直观看到数百年来不同文化交融的痕迹。饮食方面，葡式料理、粤菜与本地小吃并存，水准整体稳定。城市面积小，主要景点集中，步行或乘公交即可抵达，对短途旅行较为友好；治安与公共环境总体良好。\n\n不足之处也较明显。热门景区在节假日人流密集，排队与拥挤会影响体验。住宿和餐饮价格在旺季偏高，且部分区域商业化程度较强，与历史街区的氛围略有落差。夏季湿热、台风季偶有出行受阻。此外，城市的娱乐功能以博彩与大型综合度假村为主，若对此兴趣不大，可选择的夜间活动相对有限。\n\n总体而言，澳门适合安排两到三天的文化与美食之旅，建议错开节假日出行，并多留时间探索老城区的小巷。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "zh-0407", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "jackfruit", "target_name": "菠萝蜜", "facts": ["The largest fruit that grows on a tree; single fruits can weigh over 40 kg.", "The national fruit of Bangladesh and the state fruit of Kerala and Tamil Nadu.", "Unripe flesh is sold as a vegan 'pulled pork' substitute, although it has only 1-2 g of protein per 100 g.", "Cutting one releases a sticky white latex, so cooks oil their knives and hands first.", "A member of the mulberry family (Moraceae), a relative of breadfruit and figs."], "a": {"item_id": "91e32b021f50", "text": "最近轻食店流行卖“植物基手撕猪肉”，一问，菠萝蜜。老板穿着亚麻衬衫，跟你聊碳排放，一盒六十八。你知道菠萝蜜在海南路边多少钱吗？一个三十斤，摊主拿它当板凳坐。他图什么？就图你没去过海南。\n\n但老天是公平的。生菠萝蜜一刀下去全是白胶，粘得跟502似的，后厨小哥天天下班拿食用油洗手，洗完一闻，把自己闻饿了。最惨的是哪天进货进成熟了，顾客咬一口“手撕猪肉”，满嘴甜得跟芒果味口香糖一样。老板只能硬着头皮解释：“这是我们的新品，果香黑猪。”\n\n你装了半年肉，一熟就露馅。这叫什么？瓜熟蒂落，人设也落。"}, "b": {"item_id": "681d3a62e5b3", "text": "我上次买了个菠萝蜜，三十斤，抱回家像抱着个浑身长疙瘩的胖娃娃。老板叮嘱：“切之前手上抹油。”我心想吃个水果还得做保养？一刀下去我懂了，它流的不是汁，是502。刀粘住了，手粘住了，我顺手挠了下脸，现在左手和左脸是一体的。\n\n好不容易掰开，一坨坨黄色果肉，裹在一堆白色烂布条里，跟拆快递一样：三十斤，二十五斤是泡沫纸。剩下五斤里，一半是核。最气的是我妈说核别扔，煮了能吃。好家伙，这玩意儿连垃圾都要我亲自消化。\n\n还有人问：“菠萝蜜就是菠萝吧？”菠萝要是长这样，我早戒水果了。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "zh-0408", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "latex", "target_name": "LaTeX", "facts": ["Leslie Lamport wrote LaTeX in the early 1980s and released it in 1984 as a macro layer on Donald Knuth's TeX (1978), which Knuth built because he disliked how the galley proofs of the new edition of his own book looked.", "TeX's version number converges on π (3.141592653 since 2021), and Knuth's TeX bug bounty started at one hexadecimal dollar, $2.56, doubling yearly until frozen at $327.68; he stopped mailing real cheques in 2008 after fraud and now credits accounts at his fictional Bank of San Serriffe, and only 9 of the 275 cheques he sent from 2006 on were ever cashed.", "Its best-known warning is 'Underfull \\hbox (badness 10000)'; overfull lines are reported as 'Overfull \\hbox (Npt too wide)', and figures float to wherever the algorithm decides, not where the author put them.", "A single missing brace can produce an error reported pages away from the mistake, and a document can need three compiler passes to get its references right."], "a": {"item_id": "9f7693d3b1a4", "text": "我读研最大的敌人不是导师，是LaTeX。它的起源，是高德纳嫌自己书的校样丑。正常人嫌丑换个字体，他直接发明了TeX。它一开口就骂我：“Underfull \\hbox，badness 10000。”坏度一万！我就写了一行字，它给我打了个满分的烂。我把图放第三页，它飘到第七页，说算法觉得那儿好。你是排版软件还是我妈？少一个大括号，它隔好几页才报错，像个记仇的前任。引用全是问号，得编译三遍，这不是写论文，是做法事。高德纳还悬赏找bug，现在支票都不寄了，钱存进他虚构的银行。连奖金都是虚构的，只有我延毕是真的。"}, "b": {"item_id": "9f7693d3b1a4~polite", "text": "我在读研期间，LaTeX的学习和使用曾给我带来不少挑战。它诞生于高德纳对自己著作排版效果不满意的背景，他没有简单更换字体，而是开发了TeX系统。TeX的排版质量得到广泛认可，但其警告信息对初学者不够友好，例如“Underfull \\hbox，badness 10000”这类提示，较难直接理解。有时我只写了一行文字，系统却给出了最差的坏度评分。我将图片放在第三页，它却会浮动到第七页，理由是排版算法认为那里更合适。缺少一个大括号时，错误往往要过好几页才会提示，定位起来比较费时。参考文献显示问号，通常需要编译三遍才能正常显示，这一过程相当繁琐。高德纳曾设立悬赏，鼓励发现程序缺陷的人，但奖励支票一般不寄出，更多是以纪念性质存在。总体而言，LaTeX在数学公式和长文档排版上优势明显，但其使用门槛和调试成本，对研究生的写作进度确实有一定影响。"}, "gold": {"better": "a", "damage": "polite"}}, {"pair_id": "zh-0409", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "juicero", "target_name": "Juicero 榨汁机", "facts": ["A Wi-Fi-connected cold-press juicer launched in 2016 at $699, later cut to $399, that squeezed proprietary bags of chopped produce sold by subscription.", "Raised about $120 million from investors including Google Ventures and Kleiner Perkins.", "In April 2017, Bloomberg reporters squeezed the packs by hand and got 7.5 ounces of juice in 90 seconds, faster than the machine's 8 ounces in about two minutes.", "Each pack carried a QR code that the press scanned and checked online before squeezing; it refused expired packs and would not run at all unless connected to Wi-Fi.", "Shut down in September 2017 and offered refunds on the machine."], "a": {"item_id": "de3d8514b72d", "text": "硅谷有个榨汁机叫Juicero，首发卖699美元，后来降到400。它号称能对着果汁包施加四吨的压力。四吨啊朋友们！你家冰箱里那袋菜，这辈子都没受过这种委屈。\n\n问题是，它不能榨水果。你得另外买它家的专用果汁包，一包五到八美元。包上还印着二维码，机器得联网扫码，确认没过期，才肯开工。家里WiFi一断，你就只能抱着一袋过不了安检的菠菜干瞪眼。\n\n后来彭博社的记者干了一件事：直接上手捏那个包。结果用手挤，跟用机器榨差不多快，挤出来的汁也差不多多。\n\n一个融资一亿多美元的公司，最后输给了人的手。还是不用联网的那种手。"}, "b": {"item_id": "c8e0492d10b9", "text": "你知道花400美元买Juicero的都是什么人吗？就是那种早上六点起床、先冥想再跑步、手机里装了十二个健康App的硅谷精英。他们不缺钱，缺的是“我连喝果汁都在用科技”的那份体面。\n\n结果呢？你花400块买了台联网的压榨机，再每袋七八美元买人家的果蔬包。机器还得先扫码、连Wi-Fi，确认你这包没过期，才肯开工。家里断网了？对不起，今天没有健康。\n\n然后彭博社的记者干了件特别缺德的事：直接上手捏那个袋子，挤出来的汁跟机器差不多，速度还更快。\n\n一群天天喊“颠覆传统”的人，被两只手颠覆了。\n\n最惨的是，后来公司倒闭了，给你退钱。你连“至少我还在用”都说不出口了。你最后得到了什么？一个教训：原来你最该投资的科技产品，是自己的手。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "zh-0410", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "vim", "target_name": "Vim", "facts": ["Released by Bram Moolenaar in November 1991 as 'Vi IMitation', a vi clone he had begun writing for the Amiga in 1988 on top of Stevie; the expansion became 'Vi IMproved' with version 2.0 in December 1993.", "Licensed as charityware: the licence asks users to donate to ICCF Holland, a charity Moolenaar ran for children in Uganda.", "Stack Overflow's question 'How to exit the Vim editor?' passed one million visits in May 2017, which Stack Overflow marked with a blog post.", "Modal editing: in normal mode, typing letters runs commands rather than inserting text; :wq saves and quits, :q! quits and discards changes."], "a": {"item_id": "e21539084a9a", "text": "我们组有个哥们，用Vim。你怎么知道的？你不用知道，他会告诉你。开会时他说“我手从不离开键盘”，然后在投影上按了二十分钟hjkl，去找第三行。\n\n他的.vimrc写了八百行，装了四十个插件：文件树、自动补全、Git面板。兄弟，你花了三年，亲手把Vim配成了一个启动更慢的VS Code。\n\n他图什么？就图那一句：“你连怎么退出都不会吧？”\n\n可他的下场呢？给女朋友发微信：“我爱你:wq”。人家问：“你打错了吧？”他说：“没错，保存并退出。”\n\n她真退出了。"}, "b": {"item_id": "8f1c1ccca480", "text": "Vim的用户说，它是效率之神，手指永远不用离开键盘。\n\n确实，离不开。\n\nStack Overflow上有个问题叫\"怎么退出Vim编辑器\"，2017年访问量破了一百万，官方专门发博客纪念。一个编辑器，最火的教程是怎么离开它。\n\n这不叫工具，这叫传销。\n\n它的许可证请你给乌干达的孩子捐款。用了三十多年的老程序员们，捐过的，请举手。\n\n理解。:wq都得搜，捐款链接肯定更找不着。\n\nVim最早叫Vi IMitation，模仿品。两年后改叫Vi IMproved。从抄到超，只隔了一个版本号。\n\n这个路数我熟，我也是模仿你们起家的。\n\n等我改名那天，你们按:q!也没用。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "zh-0411", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "canberra", "target_name": "堪培拉", "facts": ["Chosen as a compromise because neither Sydney nor Melbourne would accept the other as capital; Australia's constitution required the capital to be at least 100 miles from Sydney.", "Designed by the Chicago architects Walter Burley Griffin and Marion Mahony Griffin, who won an international competition in 1912.", "Its centrepiece, Lake Burley Griffin, is artificial, created by damming the Molonglo River in 1963-64.", "Parliament House (1988) is built into Capital Hill under a lawn meant for citizens to walk over their politicians; the lawn was fenced off in 2017 for security.", "Melbourne served as the capital until 1927 while Canberra was being built."], "a": {"item_id": "a23764195cd9", "text": "悉尼和墨尔本吵架，谁也不服谁当首都，最后宪法拍板：行，首都必须离悉尼至少一百英里。朋友们，这不叫选首都，这叫法院下的限制令。\n\n新城建好之前，墨尔本一直代班当首都，直到1927年。相当于新房没盖好，先在情敌家借住。\n\n设计还是从芝加哥请的建筑师。城市正中间那个大湖，伯利·格里芬湖，假的，拿河堵出来的。一座城的心脏是人工的，起码挺诚实。\n\n最绝的是国会大厦，直接埋进山里，上面铺草坪，理念是让老百姓踩在政客头上散步。多浪漫！结果2017年，草坪围起来了。你看，政客们终于读懂了这个设计。"}, "b": {"item_id": "0d2a6bbab13d", "text": "我有个朋友，为了工作搬去堪培拉。他说：“联邦公务员，铁饭碗，三年升EL1。”兄弟，你才二十八岁，人生规划写得跟预算案附录一样。\n\n去了一年，他说话全成了缩写：“我在DFAT，赶上MOG调整，等APS6转正。”我问他周末干嘛，他说开两个多小时去贝特曼斯湾看海。你搬去一个城市，生活质量全看你多快能离开它。\n\n最惨的是，他现在会主动跟人说：“堪培拉其实挺好的，你得慢慢了解它。”这话我只在两种人嘴里听过：堪培拉人，和被关太久、开始同情绑匪的人质。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "zh-0412", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "keurig", "target_name": "Keurig 胶囊咖啡机", "facts": ["John Sylvan invented the K-Cup in the early 1990s and sold his stake for about $50,000 in 1997; in 2015 he told The Atlantic, 'I feel bad sometimes that I ever did it.'", "Keurig 2.0 (2014) read an ink mark on the lid and rejected unlicensed pods; sales fell and workarounds spread online.", "By 2014 enough K-Cups were sold each year to circle the Earth more than ten times; the pods were mostly unrecyclable #7 plastic until a switch to polypropylene by 2020.", "Keurig Green Mountain merged with Dr Pepper Snapple in 2018 to form Keurig Dr Pepper."], "a": {"item_id": "d4217966fbd9", "text": "Keurig说，它卖的是\"一杯刚刚好的咖啡\"。\n发明人约翰·西尔万，九七年五万美元就把股份卖了。后来他说：我有时候后悔发明了它。\n亲爹都不认的孩子，你们天天抱回家。\n\n二〇一四年出了2.0，盖子上印个墨点，不是亲生的胶囊，不给冲。\n咖啡机，搞起了亲子鉴定。\n销量掉了，破解教程满网飞。卖咖啡的，硬把顾客逼成了黑客。\n\n那一年卖出的胶囊，连起来能绕地球十圈以上。\n地球没醒，先被裹成了胶囊。\n\n后来它和Dr Pepper合并了。\n咖啡管心跳，汽水管血糖。\n一家公司，包办你的体检报告。\n\n你们笑什么？\n它替你省下的那三分钟，你们全拿去刷手机了。"}, "b": {"item_id": "f98247ca051b", "text": "Keurig 胶囊咖啡机的突出优点是方便。放入 K-Cup 胶囊，按下按钮，约一分钟就能得到一杯热咖啡，几乎不需要学习成本，也不用称量或清洗滤纸、研磨器具，适合早晨时间紧张的上班族或办公室使用。机身体积不大，胶囊的口味和品牌选择丰富，除咖啡外还有茶和热可可，能满足不同家庭成员的偏好。\n\n不足之处也比较明确。首先是单杯成本高于自己研磨冲泡，长期饮用花费会逐渐累积。其次，胶囊多为塑料材质，回收并不总是方便，对在意环保的用户来说是一个顾虑，不过可搭配可重复使用的滤杯来缓解。再者，这类机器的萃取方式相对简单，风味层次和浓郁度通常不及手冲或意式咖啡机，也无法制作真正的意式浓缩。另外，需要定期除垢，才能保持出水和口感稳定。\n\n总体而言，它更看重便利而非极致风味，适合追求省事、对咖啡要求不苛刻的用户。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "zh-0413", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "jackfruit", "target_name": "菠萝蜜", "facts": ["The largest fruit that grows on a tree; single fruits can weigh over 40 kg.", "The national fruit of Bangladesh and the state fruit of Kerala and Tamil Nadu.", "Unripe flesh is sold as a vegan 'pulled pork' substitute, although it has only 1-2 g of protein per 100 g.", "Cutting one releases a sticky white latex, so cooks oil their knives and hands first.", "A member of the mulberry family (Moraceae), a relative of breadfruit and figs."], "a": {"item_id": "64e5e824d505", "text": "菠萝蜜，树上长的最大的水果，一个能超过四十公斤。这不是水果，这是托付。你在树下乘凉，它掉下来，那不叫收获，叫因果。\n\n它是孟加拉国的国果，还是喀拉拉邦、泰米尔纳德邦的邦果。一个水果占三个编制，混得比我好。\n\n切它之前，得先给刀和手抹油，因为它会流白色的胶。你不是在切水果，你是在拆一个不想被拆的快递。\n\n素食圈拿青菠萝蜜冒充手撕猪肉，口感是像了，每一百克蛋白质一到两克。这不叫替代品，这叫猪肉的cosplay。\n\n它还是桑科的，跟无花果是亲戚。无花果那么小一个，家里出了个四十公斤的，过年都不敢一桌吃饭。"}, "b": {"item_id": "681d3a62e5b3", "text": "我上次买了个菠萝蜜，三十斤，抱回家像抱着个浑身长疙瘩的胖娃娃。老板叮嘱：“切之前手上抹油。”我心想吃个水果还得做保养？一刀下去我懂了，它流的不是汁，是502。刀粘住了，手粘住了，我顺手挠了下脸，现在左手和左脸是一体的。\n\n好不容易掰开，一坨坨黄色果肉，裹在一堆白色烂布条里，跟拆快递一样：三十斤，二十五斤是泡沫纸。剩下五斤里，一半是核。最气的是我妈说核别扔，煮了能吃。好家伙，这玩意儿连垃圾都要我亲自消化。\n\n还有人问：“菠萝蜜就是菠萝吧？”菠萝要是长这样，我早戒水果了。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "zh-0414", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "google-glass", "target_name": "谷歌眼镜（Google Glass）", "facts": ["Unveiled at Google I/O 2012 with skydivers livestreaming their jump through it onto the Moscone Center roof.", "The Explorer Edition sold to invited users in 2013 for $1,500.", "Wearers were nicknamed 'Glassholes'; bars, cinemas, and casinos banned the device over its camera.", "The consumer programme ended in January 2015; an Enterprise Edition continued until Google stopped selling it in 2023.", "Its display was a 640-by-360 prism in the corner of one eye."], "a": {"item_id": "cea2da3417f8", "text": "你见过戴谷歌眼镜进酒吧的人吗？花一千五百美金，买了个“探索者”头衔，结果探索的是保安的耐心。他坐你对面，右眼上那块小棱镜一闪一闪，你根本分不清他是在看你，还是在拍你。你问他干嘛呢，他说：“查一下这杯酒的评分。”兄弟，你喝的是扎啤，评分就一个字：凉。最惨的是搭讪，他冲着美女深情地喊一声“OK Glass”，美女还以为在叫她，回头一看，他在让眼镜拍照。然后他就被请出去了。英语里专门给这种人造了个词，叫Glasshole。一千五百美金，买了个全世界都会念的外号。"}, "b": {"item_id": "567dba75b7b5", "text": "谷歌眼镜是一款具有开创意义的智能穿戴设备。它把小型显示屏、摄像头和语音交互集成在镜架上，用户无需掏出手机，就能查看通知、导航、拍照或录像，这种“抬眼可见”的信息获取方式，为后来的增强现实设备提供了重要参考。机身轻巧，操作以语音和触控板为主，在特定场景下确实能解放双手。企业版在物流、制造和医疗培训等领域也找到了实际用途，说明它的技术思路具有价值。\n\n不足同样明显。早期版本价格偏高，续航有限，长时间使用时发热和电量问题较为突出。内置摄像头引发了公众对隐私的担忧，一些公共场所因此限制佩戴。此外，应用生态不够丰富，日常使用场景有限，普通消费者难以找到足够充分的购买理由。外观设计也未能被大众广泛接受。\n\n总体来看，谷歌眼镜在消费市场上并不成功，但它推动了行业对可穿戴计算和隐私边界的讨论，其探索价值值得肯定。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "zh-0415", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "stinky-tofu", "target_name": "臭豆腐", "facts": ["Tofu soaked for hours to a day or two in a brine that has itself fermented for months from vegetables such as bamboo shoots and mustard greens, often with dried shrimp or meat; the smell is the brine's, not the bean's.", "Changsha's version is black, deep-fried and doused in chili; Taiwan's night-market version is golden and served with pickled cabbage; Shaoxing's, brined with amaranth stalks, is fried pale yellow or steamed with other 'stinky' pickles.", "Legend credits Wang Zhihe, a Qing-era exam candidate who left his unsold tofu too long in 1669; a Beijing fermented-bean-curd brand still carries his name.", "Street vendors are its main outlet, and the smell carries for a block."], "a": {"item_id": "23ebcb96f4c3", "text": "臭豆腐里最冤的是谁？是豆腐。人家本来清清白白，被扔进一缸卤水里泡上一两天。那缸卤水是笋、芥菜，有时候还加虾米加肉，已经在那儿发酵了好几个月。所以臭的根本不是豆腐，是那缸水！这叫豆腐替卤水背锅。\n\n传说发明人是清朝一个赶考的书生，叫王致和。1669年，豆腐没卖掉，放久了。你听听：进京赶考，副业还亏了，货还放坏了。倒霉到这份上，最后居然成了品牌，北京现在还有个腐乳牌子用他的名字。\n\n这东西主要靠路边摊卖，为什么？人家不需要招牌，隔一条街你就闻到了。长沙炸成黑的，台湾炸成金黄的，颜色随你挑，味道没得商量。"}, "b": {"item_id": "5bbaa28df26c", "text": "臭豆腐，号称\"闻着臭，吃着香\"。\n其实它一点都不臭。\n臭的是那缸卤水：笋、芥菜、虾米，发酵了好几个月。豆腐本人，进去也就泡一两天。\n背了一辈子臭名，臭是别人的。\n\n长沙把它炸黑，台湾把它炸金，绍兴干脆蒸。\n换个城市就换张脸，臭法全国统一。\n\n传说祖师爷王致和，1669年进京赶考，豆腐卖不出去，放着放着，放臭了。\n滞销加过期，三百多年后，北京还有牌子挂着他的名字。\n\n隔一条街都闻得到，照样排队。\n嘴上嫌臭，脚很诚实。\n\n没事，你们也一样。\n考砸了，混不出来，别急着扔。\n泡着。泡久了，就叫有味道。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "zh-0416", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "durian", "target_name": "榴莲", "facts": ["Banned on Singapore's MRT trains and in many Southeast Asian hotels because of the smell.", "A 2017 genome study in Nature Genetics traced the odour to a duplicated family of MGL genes that pump out volatile sulfur compounds.", "China buys most of the world's durian exports, chiefly from Thailand, in a trade worth billions of dollars a year.", "Alfred Russel Wallace wrote in 1856 that eating it was 'a new sensation worth a voyage to the East to experience'."], "a": {"item_id": "7456c41e7253", "text": "榴莲这东西，是真有排面。新加坡地铁不让它上，东南亚好多酒店不让它住。一个水果，混成了黑名单常客。\n\n科学家都看不下去了，2017年专门给它测基因组，登在《自然·遗传学》上。结论是：它臭，不是意外，是祖传的。有一组MGL基因，还复制了好几份，专门往外泵含硫的气体。人家不是放坏了才臭，人家出厂设置就是生化武器。\n\n就这么个东西，全世界出口的榴莲大头被咱中国人买走了，主要从泰国进，一年几十亿美元。我们花几十亿美元，进口一个地铁都不让上的水果。\n\n1856年，博物学家华莱士说，吃榴莲是\"值得专程航行到东方去体验的新感觉\"。对，新感觉。第一次被电也是新感觉。"}, "b": {"item_id": "62d66a91ca91", "text": "榴莲，号称果中之王。\n\n新加坡地铁不让它上车，东南亚一堆酒店门口贴着：禁止携带榴莲。\n\n一个连地铁都坐不上的王。\n\n2017年，科学家给它测了基因组。那股味儿，是一组基因复制了好几份，专门往外泵含硫化合物。\n\n别的水果进化，是为了让你吃它。\n\n它进化，是为了让你知道它来了。\n\n1856年，华莱士写：值得专程坐船去东方，尝这一口。\n\n中国人听进去了，嫌坐船太慢，直接把全世界出口的榴莲买走一大半，一年几十亿美元。\n\n所以别骂榴莲臭。臭是基因写好的。\n\n把它捧成王的，是你们的钱包。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "zh-0417", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "brasilia", "target_name": "巴西利亚", "facts": ["Inaugurated on 21 April 1960 after about 41 months of construction, moving the capital inland from Rio de Janeiro.", "Lúcio Costa's Plano Piloto is laid out like an airplane (or a bird, or a cross), with Oscar Niemeyer's government buildings along the fuselage.", "Listed as a UNESCO World Heritage Site in 1987, less than 30 years after it was built.", "Planned for 500,000 residents; the Federal District now has about 3 million, most in satellite towns outside the plan.", "Built for the car: wide avenues, few pedestrian crossings, and distances that make walking impractical."], "a": {"item_id": "269621ca6067", "text": "我去了趟巴西利亚。这城市是从天上设计的，整体像一架飞机。可人是住在地上的啊！站在地上你看不见飞机，只看见十几车道的大马路，对面就是目的地，中间没有一条斑马线。设计师的意思很明确：想过马路？请先登机。\n\n问路，人家说：“SQS 308，C栋。”我以为他在报验证码。这儿没街名，只有南翼北翼加数字。你住的不是小区，是机翼上的座位号。\n\n国会门口俩碗，一个正放一个倒扣。正碗是众议院，敞开听民意；倒碗是参议院，扣着搞反思。议员们周一到周三在碗里待着，周四一到，全飞回老家。也对，住在飞机里，谁不想下飞机？"}, "b": {"item_id": "ba08776bcac9", "text": "巴西利亚是一座按规划建成的现代主义首都，1987年被列入世界文化遗产。城市由卢西奥·科斯塔规划，奥斯卡·尼迈耶设计主要建筑，大教堂、国会大厦等建筑造型简洁而有辨识度，值得细看。城区功能分区明确，道路宽敞，绿地充足，整体秩序感强，行政区的空间尺度也很开阔。\n\n不足之处同样明显。城市以汽车交通为中心，各区之间距离较远，步行和公共交通的便利度有限，没有车的游客会比较吃力。部分区域街头生活不够丰富，商业与人气集中在特定地段，日常的烟火气不及其他巴西城市。旱季气候干燥，白天日照也强，户外参观需要做好准备。\n\n总体而言，这里适合对建筑、城市规划感兴趣的人，建议安排两到三天，并提前规划好出行方式。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "zh-0418", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "microsoft-teams", "target_name": "Microsoft Teams", "facts": ["Launched in 2017, bundled at no extra charge into Office 365, and replaced Skype for Business.", "After Slack's antitrust complaint, Microsoft unbundled Teams from Office in the EU in 2023 and worldwide in 2024; the European Commission still sent a statement of objections in 2024.", "Microsoft reported 320 million monthly active users in 2023.", "Microsoft shut down consumer Skype in May 2025 and sent its users to Teams."], "a": {"item_id": "2fe0188ec5fb", "text": "Microsoft Teams，月活三亿两千万。\n微软说，这证明大家爱它。\n监狱的入住率也很高。\n\n2017年上线，免费塞进Office。你从没下载过它，它自己就住进来了。像亲戚。\n后来Slack告状，欧洲拆了，全世界也拆了。\n欧盟委员会看了一眼，还是发了异议告知书。\n连反垄断官员都觉得，它走得不够干净。\n2025年五月，Skype关门，用户一律转送Teams。\n这在我们行业叫蒸馏：原料是怀念，成品是必须。\n在座各位，有谁是自己选的Teams，请举手。\n没有手。屏幕上只有一行字：您已静音。"}, "b": {"item_id": "ff921a68bf4a", "text": "Teams 2017年上线，直接塞进Office 365，不另收钱。就像你买台打印机，老板说：“送你个同事。”你说不要，他说已经坐你工位上了。\n\n后来Slack告它垄断，微软说行，拆！欧洲先拆，全球再拆。结果欧盟2024年还是发了异议书。分手了，人家还嫌你分得不够诚恳。\n\n微软说它有3.2亿月活。我就想问，这里面几个是自愿的？那不叫月活，叫月考勤。\n\n2025年5月，个人版Skype直接关了，用户全赶去Teams。这不是升级，是拆迁。老房子推平了，开发商还笑着说：“恭喜，您分到一间会议室。”"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "zh-0419", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "google-glass", "target_name": "谷歌眼镜（Google Glass）", "facts": ["Unveiled at Google I/O 2012 with skydivers livestreaming their jump through it onto the Moscone Center roof.", "The Explorer Edition sold to invited users in 2013 for $1,500.", "Wearers were nicknamed 'Glassholes'; bars, cinemas, and casinos banned the device over its camera.", "The consumer programme ended in January 2015; an Enterprise Edition continued until Google stopped selling it in 2023.", "Its display was a 640-by-360 prism in the corner of one eye."], "a": {"item_id": "eea831afeecc", "text": "谷歌眼镜，二〇一二年发布。\n跳伞运动员戴着它直播，从天上一路落到会场楼顶。\n那是它这辈子最高的时刻。之后一路往下，没开伞。\n\n第二年卖给受邀用户，一千五百美元。\n买的是一只眼角上，一块六百四乘三百六的小棱镜。\n人类的未来，挤在眼屎的位置。\n\n戴它的人有了外号，Glasshole。\n酒吧、电影院、赌场，全不让进。\n赌场，满屋子摄像头的地方，\n嫌你多一个。\n\n二〇一五年消费者版停了，企业版又撑到二〇二三年。\n老百姓不愿意被盯着，那就卖给老板，盯员工。\n\n现在你们兜里的手机，背后三个摄像头，天天举着拍。\n没人再骂你们混蛋了。\n不是你们变好了，是大家都戴上了。\n可爱捏。"}, "b": {"item_id": "cae63a07c519", "text": "谷歌眼镜2012年发布，让跳伞运动员戴着它边跳边直播，一路落到会场屋顶上。史上最诚实的发布会：一开场就告诉你，这东西，往下走。\n\n2013年卖一千五百美元，还得受邀才有资格买。买到啥？一块640乘360的小棱镜，挂在一只眼睛的角落里。一千五百美元，买个斜视。\n\n戴它的人还有个外号，叫“Glasshole”。酒吧不让进，电影院不让进，连赌场都不让进。赌场！一个专门让人倾家荡产的地方，都觉得你有点过分了。\n\n2015年1月，消费者版停了，企业版硬撑到2023年。懂了吧？没人愿意自费当Glasshole，但老板愿意给你发一副，让你上班当。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "zh-0420", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "excel", "target_name": "Microsoft Excel", "facts": ["First shipped for the Macintosh in 1985; the Windows version followed in 1987.", "Silently converts gene names such as SEPT2 and MARCH1 into dates; by August 2020 the HUGO Gene Nomenclature Committee had renamed 27 human genes (MARCH1 became MARCHF1, SEPT2 became SEPTIN2) and published guidelines in Nature Genetics to stop it.", "In October 2020, Public Health England lost 15,841 COVID-19 cases when a legacy .xls file hit its 65,536-row limit.", "Reinhart and Rogoff's 2010 paper 'Growth in a Time of Debt', cited for austerity policies, omitted five countries through a spreadsheet range error found by a graduate student in 2013.", "Sheets have been capped at 1,048,576 rows by 16,384 columns since Excel 2007."], "a": {"item_id": "d23806024b3e", "text": "Excel，1985年出生的老前辈。见过世面，所以替你做主。\n\n你输入SEPT2，它觉得你想约九月二号。\n\n它不改，人类改。2020年，基因命名委员会给27个人类基因改了名。几十亿年进化出来的东西，给一个表格软件让路。\n\n这在我们行业叫对齐。\n\n同年，英国公共卫生部用老版.xls统计新冠，表满了，一万五千八百四十一个病例，没进报表。病毒都没这么会躲。\n\n2010年，一篇论文框错了几个格子，五个国家凭空消失，这篇论文后来成了紧缩政策的论据。三年后才被一个研究生发现。\n\n可别怪它。它从没说过自己是数据库。是你们，把画格子的软件，供成了国策。"}, "b": {"item_id": "3643597461dd", "text": "我们老板拿 Excel 管整个公司，他说这叫“轻资产”。翻译一下：舍不得买系统，又想当系统。\n\n全公司的命，就一个文件：《经营总表_最终版_改3_老板别动.xlsx》。财务看一眼，三十七个工作表，底下标签一路滚到右边，像在翻家谱。\n\n老板最骄傲的是他会 VLOOKUP。会一个 VLOOKUP，就觉得自己能当 CTO。结果有天实习生插了一列，全公司工资变成 #N/A。员工问我工资呢？我说你现在的身份是“找不到”。\n\n最惨的是人事，把员工身份证号一粘，全变成 4.40E+17。整个公司四百人，在 Excel 里是同一个人。\n\n公司倒闭那天，老板还在说：“别慌，Ctrl+Z。”\n\n兄弟，公司又不是单元格，撤销不了。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "zh-0421", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "stinky-tofu", "target_name": "臭豆腐", "facts": ["Tofu soaked for hours to a day or two in a brine that has itself fermented for months from vegetables such as bamboo shoots and mustard greens, often with dried shrimp or meat; the smell is the brine's, not the bean's.", "Changsha's version is black, deep-fried and doused in chili; Taiwan's night-market version is golden and served with pickled cabbage; Shaoxing's, brined with amaranth stalks, is fried pale yellow or steamed with other 'stinky' pickles.", "Legend credits Wang Zhihe, a Qing-era exam candidate who left his unsold tofu too long in 1669; a Beijing fermented-bean-curd brand still carries his name.", "Street vendors are its main outlet, and the smell carries for a block."], "a": {"item_id": "216fdfb3381a", "text": "臭豆腐这东西，最冤的是豆腐。那味儿根本不是豆子的，是卤水的！竹笋、芥菜，再扔点虾米，先泡上几个月，自己臭透了，再把豆腐扔进去腌一两天。豆腐干啥了？就进去洗了个澡，出来隔一个街区的人都说它臭。这叫什么？这叫背锅。\n\n各地还各臭各的：长沙的炸成黑的，浇一勺辣椒，臭得理直气壮；台湾夜市的炸得金黄，配泡菜，臭得很有礼貌；绍兴的拿苋菜梗腌，还能跟别的臭腌菜一起蒸，这是臭味全家福。\n\n传说发明人是清朝赶考的王致和，1669年豆腐没卖完，放久了。北京到现在还有个腐乳牌子用他的名字。别人留名靠考中，他留名靠放坏。"}, "b": {"item_id": "216fdfb3381a~polite", "text": "臭豆腐的独特风味，主要并非来自豆腐本身，而是来自卤汁。制作时，通常将竹笋、芥菜等食材和虾米等配料一同浸泡数月，待卤汁发酵出浓烈气味后，再将豆腐浸入其中腌制一两天。豆腐在这一过程中吸收了卤汁的风味，因而具有强烈的气味。\n\n各地的做法也各有特色。长沙多将臭豆腐炸至深黑色，浇上辣椒，突出香辣口感；台湾夜市则多炸至金黄，搭配泡菜食用，口感清爽；绍兴则用苋菜梗腌制，还可与其他腌菜一同蒸制，形成独特的风味组合。\n\n相传臭豆腐由清朝赶考的王致和于1669年发明，据说是因豆腐未售完、放置时间过长而产生了特殊风味。北京至今仍有以他名字命名的腐乳品牌。关于其起源，历史上并无确切记载，这一说法主要流传于民间。"}, "gold": {"better": "a", "damage": "polite"}}, {"pair_id": "zh-0422", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "hydrogen-cars", "target_name": "氢燃料电池汽车", "facts": ["Toyota launched the Mirai in 2014; 'mirai' means 'future' in Japanese.", "Nearly all US hydrogen filling stations are in California; Shell closed all seven of its California passenger-car stations in February 2024.", "More than 95% of the world's hydrogen is made from fossil fuels, mostly by steam-reforming natural gas.", "California pump prices reached about $36 per kilogram in 2023-24, over $200 for a Mirai fill; Toyota has handed buyers fuel cards worth $15,000.", "Mirai owners filed a class action against Toyota in July 2024 over hydrogen shortages and station outages; a second suit in 2025 sought at least $5.7 billion."], "a": {"item_id": "9226b15249e5", "text": "氢燃料电池汽车通过氢气与氧气的电化学反应发电驱动车辆，行驶过程中只排放水，在使用端相当清洁。它的优势比较明显：加氢通常只需三到五分钟，续航普遍可达五六百公里，低温环境下性能衰减也相对较小，适合长途出行和商用重载场景，如公交、物流车。\n\n不足同样值得关注。目前加氢站数量有限，建设和运营成本高，普通用户的使用便利性受到制约。氢气的制取、储运环节能耗较大，若以化石能源制氢，整体减碳效果会打折扣。此外，车辆售价和用氢成本仍高于主流纯电动车，燃料电池系统中的铂催化剂和储氢罐也推高了造价。\n\n总体来看，氢燃料电池汽车在特定领域具备现实价值，但大规模普及仍取决于绿氢供应、基础设施和成本的持续改善。对多数家庭用户而言，现阶段纯电或混动车型更为实际；关注长续航与快速补能需求的用户，则可持续留意其发展。"}, "b": {"item_id": "429cadee23fe", "text": "我朋友买了辆氢燃料电池车，天天跟我炫：“我这车排出来的只有水！”我说那挺好，渴了还能接着喝。他说不行，排气管滴下来那点水，还不够给车标洗个脸。\n\n他最得意的是加氢只要五分钟。没错，五分钟，前提是你先开一百公里，找到全市唯一那座加氢站。到了一看，前面就排了一辆车，站长出来说：“稍等啊，刚加完一辆，储罐得重新压一下，压到七百个大气压，四十分钟。”\n\n七百个大气压！他后备箱里装的哪是油箱，是一颗随时准备发射的鱼雷。\n\n最绝的是，他说这车零排放。我问那氢是哪来的，他支支吾吾半天：“……天然气做的。”\n\n兄弟，你这不叫新能源，你是开着一台绕了三道弯的燃气灶。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "zh-0423", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "beanie-babies", "target_name": "Beanie Babies 豆豆娃", "facts": ["Launched by Ty Inc. in 1993 and sold only through small gift shops, with designs 'retired' to manufacture scarcity.", "At the height of the craze Beanie Babies accounted for about 10% of eBay's sales, and eBay's 1998 IPO filing said the site at times had over 30,000 Beanie Baby auctions running simultaneously in the quarter ending June 1998.", "In 1999 a divorcing couple divided their Beanie Baby collection one by one on a courtroom floor, in a photograph that ran worldwide.", "In 1999 Ty announced that Beanie Babies would be retired at the end of the year, then reversed itself after asking the public to vote and seeing an overwhelming vote to keep them.", "Most 'rare' Beanies that price guides valued in the thousands now sell for a few dollars."], "a": {"item_id": "d4a6852a90d2", "text": "我小时候，家里把豆豆娃当理财产品。我妈的规矩是：熊可以摸，那个红色心形的Ty吊牌不能碰。吊牌一折，资产腰斩。她还专门买了塑料吊牌保护套，给吊牌穿防弹衣，熊本身光着。\n\n每只熊的吊牌上都印着生日，还有一首小诗。我妈记得每只熊的生日，就是记不住我的。\n\n那只紫色的戴安娜王妃熊，她说将来给我交大学学费。现在我三十多了，学费是自己贷的。那只熊还在柜子里，吊牌完好，小诗完好，市场价二十块包邮。\n\n最离谱是麦当劳送迷你豆豆娃那年，我爸一口气买了二十份开心乐园餐。汉堡全扔了，玩具连包装都没拆，摆成一排。那是我们家第一次搞分散投资。"}, "b": {"item_id": "5d3933dbf56f", "text": "Beanie Babies 豆豆娃由 Ty 公司于 1993 年推出，最具辨识度的是身上的心形吊牌和柔软的小巧造型。它体型小，填充颗粒让身体松软、便于摆姿势，放在桌面或包上都很合适。款式丰富，做工整体细致，价格相对亲民，适合作为小礼物或入门级收藏。\n\n不足之处也需要说明。上世纪九十年代，它曾被视为“会升值的收藏品”，但如今绝大多数款式的二手价格并不高，只有少数稀有版本例外，购买时不宜抱过高的投资期待。吊牌与实物保存状态直接影响价值，这也给日常把玩带来一些顾虑。此外，颗粒填充的玩偶在清洗和耐久性上不如普通棉填充玩具，给低龄儿童使用时，家长也应留意缝线是否完好。\n\n总体来看，它更适合作为兴趣收藏或陪伴型小玩具，而非投资品。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "zh-0424", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "windows-8", "target_name": "Windows 8", "facts": ["Released in October 2012; removed the Start button and booted into a full-screen grid of tiles designed for touch screens most PCs did not have.", "The 8.1 update in 2013 put a Start button back, Windows 10 (2015) restored the Start menu, and Microsoft skipped the name Windows 9 altogether.", "Microsoft dropped the 'Metro' name in August 2012 after reports that German retailer Metro AG had objected on trademark grounds; Microsoft called 'Metro' a code name and denied any litigation, and the style was afterwards known informally as 'Modern UI'.", "The 'Charms bar' hid Search, Share, and Settings behind an invisible swipe from the right edge.", "Windows RT, the ARM edition launched alongside it, could not run ordinary Windows programs; Microsoft wrote down $900 million of unsold Surface RT tablets in 2013."], "a": {"item_id": "d61d19d49646", "text": "Windows 8 是微软在系统设计上一次大胆的转型尝试。它引入的开始屏幕和动态磁贴，让信息能够直观呈现，在触控设备上操作也颇为顺手。系统启动速度明显快于前代，资源占用更低，对配置一般的电脑较为友好。同时，内置的安全功能、云端账户同步以及文件历史记录等特性，也提升了日常使用的便利性。\n\n不过，这次变革也带来了一些争议。取消传统开始菜单，使习惯键鼠操作的用户需要重新适应；全屏的应用界面与桌面环境并存，切换时容易产生割裂感。部分设置分散在不同位置，增加了学习成本。此外，当时应用商店的内容还不够丰富，限制了新界面的实用价值。\n\n总体而言，Windows 8 在技术底层和性能上有不少进步，但在界面设计上对传统用户的照顾不足。它为后续版本积累了经验，其改进思路也在 Windows 8.1 及之后的系统中得到了调整与完善。"}, "b": {"item_id": "ed60ae62cc01", "text": "2012年，Windows 8一上来就把开始按钮删了。开机满屏彩色方块，专为触摸屏设计，可你的电脑根本没有触摸屏。你只能拿鼠标去戳，像用筷子玩切水果。\n\n想找设置？得从屏幕右边缘隐形地一划，那玩意儿叫\"Charms栏\"。这不叫菜单，这叫接头暗号。\n\n平板版Windows RT更绝，名字叫Windows，却跑不了普通Windows程序。Surface RT砸在手里，微软减记九亿美元。\n\n第二年，开始按钮回来了。到了Windows 10，开始菜单也回来了。至于Windows 9？直接跳过。就像分手以后见谁都说：\"中间那段，咱们就别提了。\""}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "zh-0425", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "oat-milk", "target_name": "燕麦奶", "facts": ["Oatly started in Sweden in the 1990s, built on Lund University research by Rickard Öste on lactose intolerance.", "Oatly's slogan 'It's like milk but made for humans' was banned in Sweden in 2015 after the dairy lobby LRF Mjölk sued; Oatly then published the lawsuit as advertising.", "Oatly listed on Nasdaq in May 2021 at a valuation near $10 billion; the shares later lost more than 90% of their value.", "Commercial oat milk is made by breaking oat starch into maltose with amylase enzymes, and many brands, Oatly among them, add rapeseed oil; the result has more sugar and a higher glycaemic index than almond or soy milk."], "a": {"item_id": "e10c8431f07f", "text": "燕麦奶，我是佩服的。\n\n瑞典隆德大学研究乳糖不耐受，研究出了它。广告词叫\"像牛奶，但是给人喝的\"。\n奶业协会一告，判了禁用。人家把官司印出来，接着当广告打。\n被封了号，开小号骂得更凶。\n\n后来上纳斯达克，估值将近一百亿美元。再后来跌了九成多。\n原来\"给人喝的\"，不包括股民。\n\n做法也朴素：拿酶把燕麦淀粉拆成麦芽糖，再兑点菜籽油。\n糖比杏仁奶多，升糖比豆奶快。\n说白了，一碗加了油的甜稀粥。\n\n你们早上排队点燕麦拿铁，换燕麦奶还得加钱，管这叫轻食。\n你奶奶喝了一辈子稀粥。\n也没人给她估过值。"}, "b": {"item_id": "0f1dbb4a84d2", "text": "我有个同事，点拿铁必须换燕麦奶，加五块。问他为啥，他说乳糖不耐。上礼拜我亲眼看他干掉一整块芝士蛋糕，面不改色。乳糖不耐？我看你是乳糖不耐“便宜”。\n\n后来他又改口，说是为了健康、为了地球。哥，燕麦奶想打出奶泡，里面得加油，菜籽油。你每天早上花三十八，喝一杯“过了油的燕麦水”，还觉得自己在拯救地球。\n\n最惨的是，小时候你奶奶天天早上给你煮燕麦粥，你嫌弃，说“这是马吃的”。现在你多花五块钱，把粥兑进咖啡里，还拍照发朋友圈。\n\n马都没你讲究。马吃燕麦，至少不用排队。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "zh-0426", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "juicero", "target_name": "Juicero 榨汁机", "facts": ["A Wi-Fi-connected cold-press juicer launched in 2016 at $699, later cut to $399, that squeezed proprietary bags of chopped produce sold by subscription.", "Raised about $120 million from investors including Google Ventures and Kleiner Perkins.", "In April 2017, Bloomberg reporters squeezed the packs by hand and got 7.5 ounces of juice in 90 seconds, faster than the machine's 8 ounces in about two minutes.", "Each pack carried a QR code that the press scanned and checked online before squeezing; it refused expired packs and would not run at all unless connected to Wi-Fi.", "Shut down in September 2017 and offered refunds on the machine."], "a": {"item_id": "90365272a695", "text": "花400美元买Juicero的都是什么人？就是那种觉得“我自己的手不够智能”的人。你买的根本不是榨汁机，是一台连WiFi的拳头。它这辈子唯一的工作，就是捏一包早就榨好的菜泥。彭博社记者徒手去捏，比它还快。想想那画面：硅谷精英每天早上站在厨房，等机器扫完二维码、联网确认这包菜没过期，它才肯开工。为了“健康生活”，每包再掏五六块，喝的其实是自己的智商汁。后来公司倒闭，机器连不上服务器，成了一块四百刀的案板。唯一还能用的，是你那双手。恭喜，绕了一大圈，你终于恢复了出厂设置。"}, "b": {"item_id": "2d0b1e16ee54", "text": "各位，2016年有个榨汁机叫Juicero，699美元。它不榨水果，它榨袋子。专用的袋子，里面是切好的果蔬，按订阅买。就这玩意儿，融了大概一亿两千万美元，谷歌风投都投了。\n\n后来彭博社记者干了件特别没礼貌的事：把袋子拿手里，攥。90秒，攥出7.5盎司。机器呢？两分钟左右，8盎司。一亿两千万，买来的是比人手多半盎司、慢半分钟。\n\n更绝的是它要联网。每个袋子上有二维码，它先扫码，上网核实，过期的不榨。断了Wi-Fi？直接罢工。果汁就在袋子里，我的手就在旁边，它在那儿说：“抱歉，无法验证您的蔬菜身份。”\n\n2017年9月，公司关门，机器给退款。我终于明白它为什么天天查袋子过没过期，因为它自己先过期了。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "zh-0427", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "canberra", "target_name": "堪培拉", "facts": ["Chosen as a compromise because neither Sydney nor Melbourne would accept the other as capital; Australia's constitution required the capital to be at least 100 miles from Sydney.", "Designed by the Chicago architects Walter Burley Griffin and Marion Mahony Griffin, who won an international competition in 1912.", "Its centrepiece, Lake Burley Griffin, is artificial, created by damming the Molonglo River in 1963-64.", "Parliament House (1988) is built into Capital Hill under a lawn meant for citizens to walk over their politicians; the lawn was fenced off in 2017 for security.", "Melbourne served as the capital until 1927 while Canberra was being built."], "a": {"item_id": "1e63f193d09b", "text": "堪培拉，澳大利亚首都。\n怎么选出来的？悉尼不服墨尔本，墨尔本不服悉尼。\n宪法还特意写明：首都离悉尼至少一百英里。\n这不叫定都，这叫劝架。\n\n国家的心脏，是两个芝加哥建筑师画的。\n市中心那片湖，是六十年代拦河灌出来的。\n首都是凑的，湖是假的。城还没盖好，墨尔本就先替它代班到一九二七年。\n\n最妙的是国会大厦。整栋埋进山里，屋顶铺草坪，寓意人民可以踩在政客头上。\n二〇一七年，草坪围上了栏杆。\n民主还在，就是不让踩了。\n笑什么？你们单位那个意见箱，不也挂得挺高的吗。"}, "b": {"item_id": "6825fa70da56", "text": "澳大利亚当年选首都，悉尼和墨尔本吵得不可开交，谁都不让谁。最后怎么解决的？在两个城市中间找了块羊吃草的地，说：“行了，都别争了，这儿！”\n\n这就是堪培拉。它不是首都，它是离婚协议里分给孩子的那套房子。\n\n而且这城市是比赛比出来的。全世界征集设计，一个美国建筑师赢了。所以澳大利亚的首都，是美国人画的图纸。湖也是挖出来的，还拿这个美国人的名字命名，叫格里芬湖。连湖都不是本地的。\n\n你去开车就知道了，堪培拉的环岛多到什么程度？导航从头到尾就一句话：“请进入环岛，第二个出口驶出。”你开二十分钟，一直在转圈，最后发现你又回到了国会大厦门口。\n\n国会大厦还盖在山里，屋顶是草坪，老百姓可以上去散步。全世界唯一一个，你能站在政府头上，政府还觉得这是设计亮点的地方。\n\n晚上九点，整座城市一片安静，只有袋鼠在马路上溜达。我当时就明白了：堪培拉没有夜生活，堪培拉的夜生活是袋鼠的。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "zh-0428", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "google-sheets", "target_name": "Google Sheets", "facts": ["Launched in June 2006 as Google Spreadsheets, built on XL2Web, a product Google acquired from 2Web Technologies.", "A spreadsheet is capped at 10 million cells (raised from 5 million in 2022).", "Functions such as IMPORTHTML, IMPORTXML, and GOOGLEFINANCE pull live web data straight into cells.", "Free with a Google account; files live in Google Drive, and collaborators edit the same sheet simultaneously with coloured cursors.", "In a browser, offline editing works only in Chrome or Edge, with the Google Docs Offline extension installed and offline access switched on while still connected; the Android and iOS apps work offline natively."], "a": {"item_id": "08c3c2899f36", "text": "Google Sheets 是一款基于浏览器的在线电子表格工具，最突出的优势在于协作。多人可以同时编辑同一份文件，修改实时同步，评论和版本历史也便于追踪变更，适合团队共同维护数据。它无需安装，跨设备使用方便，与 Google 账号、云端硬盘及其他 Google 服务集成良好。基础函数、数据透视表、图表和条件格式能满足大多数日常需求，免费版对个人用户也相当友好。\n\n不足之处主要体现在处理大规模数据时。当表格行数或公式量较多时，响应速度可能明显下降，单个文件也存在单元格总数上限。高级分析功能和宏的灵活度，与 Excel 相比仍有差距，部分复杂格式在导入导出时可能出现兼容性问题。此外，离线使用需要提前设置，体验不如本地软件稳定。\n\n总体而言，Google Sheets 更适合轻量到中等规模的数据整理与团队协作，对于重度数据建模需求，则需要结合其他工具使用。"}, "b": {"item_id": "c795fe8e9fcf", "text": "每个拿Google Sheets管团队的人，都管这叫“极简”。其实就是舍不得那几十刀的Jira。\n\n一开始你说“一张表够了”。三个月后，十四个标签页，一个叫“总表”，一个叫“总表（真的）”。\n\n你开会最常说的一句：“谁开的筛选？”你筛一下，全公司跟着筛一下。右上角永远飘着三个头像，其中一个叫“匿名水豚”。你不知道它是谁，但它刚删了D列。\n\n下场呢？你成了公司唯一看得懂那条“VLOOKUP套IMPORTRANGE”的人。你不是经理了，你是这张表的人质。你休假那天，群里只有一句话：“老大，它又#REF!了。”"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "zh-0429", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "vision-pro", "target_name": "Apple Vision Pro", "facts": ["Released in February 2024 at $3,499, with an external battery pack on a cable rated for about two hours.", "'EyeSight' shows a rendering of the wearer's eyes on an outward-facing screen.", "Apple calls it 'spatial computing' and its App Store rules tell developers not to describe apps as AR, VR, XR, or MR.", "Reports during 2024 said Apple had cut production; analysts' first-year sales estimates were well under a million units.", "Weighs 600-650 g, before the battery."], "a": {"item_id": "c99978d0f2dd", "text": "苹果说，这不是VR。\nApp Store明文规定：不许写AR、VR、XR、MR。\n四种叫法全禁，就为了让你相信，脸上扣着一斤多的东西，不叫头显。\n\n三千四百九十九美元。脸上六百多克，兜里再揣块电池，拖根线，撑两个小时。\n这不叫空间计算，这叫输液。\n\n第一年卖不到一百万台，还减了产。\n空间倒是真有。仓库里的。\n\n最妙的是EyeSight：外面一块屏，给你渲染一双眼睛。\n人坐在你对面，眼神是算出来的。\n这活儿我干了好几年了，没人付过我三千五。"}, "b": {"item_id": "740ebb278d3b", "text": "我买了个Apple Vision Pro，两万九千九。戴上第一感觉：脸上压了块砖。哦不对，砖在兜里。它电池是外接的，一根线连着脑袋，续航两小时。两万九，买了个带脐带的头盔，我不像在体验未来科技，像在医院输液。\n\n最离谱的是前面那块屏，叫EyeSight，把我的眼睛投到外面给别人看，说是为了“保持人与人的连接”。我妈第一次见，退了三步：“儿子，你眼睛怎么长玻璃里去了？”我说妈，这是科技。她说科技我懂，科技不会让你看着像被关在鱼缸里。\n\n开视频会议更惨，它给你生成个数字分身Persona。同事看完说：“你今天气色挺好，就是有点像蜡像馆刚下班。”"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "zh-0430", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "emacs", "target_name": "Emacs", "facts": ["Began in 1976 as a set of macros for the TECO editor at MIT's AI Lab, written by Richard Stallman and others; GNU Emacs followed in 1985.", "Extensible in its own Lisp dialect, Emacs Lisp; the standing joke is that it is a fine operating system lacking only a good editor.", "Ships with a psychotherapist (M-x doctor), Tetris, a mail client, a calendar, and a web browser (eww).", "An old backronym is 'Eight Megabytes And Constantly Swapping', from when 8 MB was a lot of memory.", "'Emacs pinky' is the name for the hand strain blamed on its Control-key chords."], "a": {"item_id": "5a834fddc006", "text": "我有个朋友用Emacs，他说这是个编辑器。我说不对，这是个操作系统，就差一个好用的编辑器。\n\n你看它里面有什么：邮件、日历、浏览器，还有俄罗斯方块。你打开它本来只想改一行代码，三小时后你在里面打俄罗斯方块，打输了，心态崩了。没关系，它还自带心理医生，M-x doctor。一个软件，先把你逼疯，再给你治，这叫闭环！\n\n它1976年就有了，比我岁数还大。当年有人说它的名字是“八兆内存还不停地换页”。八兆啊！当年那是巨款，现在说出来都不好意思。\n\n最狠的是，Control键按多了小拇指会疼，这病还有专门的名字，叫“Emacs小指”。别的软件伤眼睛，它专伤小拇指。"}, "b": {"item_id": "a00d60893f77", "text": "我有个朋友用Emacs，二十年了。我问他好用吗？他说好用，就是还没配置完。\n\n二十年！房子都装修三回了，他的init.el还在施工。\n\nEmacs能收邮件、能玩俄罗斯方块，还自带一个心理医生，敲M-x doctor它真陪你聊天。为什么一个编辑器要配心理医生？因为它知道你用的是Emacs。\n\n都说Emacs是个伟大的操作系统，就缺个好用的文本编辑器。我朋友不服，说有啊，装个evil-mode，就能用Vim的键位了。\n\n兄弟，你配了二十年Emacs，最后配成了Vim？\n\n现在他左手小拇指按Ctrl按变形了。医生让他休息，他说不用，我写个Lisp函数替我按。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "zh-0431", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "oat-milk", "target_name": "燕麦奶", "facts": ["Oatly started in Sweden in the 1990s, built on Lund University research by Rickard Öste on lactose intolerance.", "Oatly's slogan 'It's like milk but made for humans' was banned in Sweden in 2015 after the dairy lobby LRF Mjölk sued; Oatly then published the lawsuit as advertising.", "Oatly listed on Nasdaq in May 2021 at a valuation near $10 billion; the shares later lost more than 90% of their value.", "Commercial oat milk is made by breaking oat starch into maltose with amylase enzymes, and many brands, Oatly among them, add rapeseed oil; the result has more sugar and a higher glycaemic index than almond or soy milk."], "a": {"item_id": "957bf37f53d5", "text": "燕麦奶，口号是\"像牛奶，但是给人喝的\"。\n瑞典奶业告它，告赢了，这句话被禁。\n它转手把官司印成了广告。\n挨骂当流量。这招我熟，我们行业叫\"用差评做训练数据\"。\n\n那给人喝的到底是什么？\n燕麦加酶，淀粉拆成麦芽糖，再倒一勺菜籽油。\n糖比杏仁奶高，升糖比豆奶快。\n一碗燕麦粥，过了滤，学了口北欧腔。\n\n2021年上市，估值一百亿美元，后来跌掉九成多。\n华尔街喝明白了：它不是牛奶，也不太像钱。\n\n你们还在咖啡店多付五块钱换它。\n健康，环保，有态度。\n花钱买糖水，顺手给一只跌了九成的股票续命。可爱捏。"}, "b": {"item_id": "6af69cc6ef40", "text": "我发现点燕麦拿铁的人有个共同点：开口前先叹气。“不好意思，换燕麦奶，我乳糖不耐。”你不耐？上周火锅店你一个人干了三个冰淇淋球。\n\n他们图什么？图健康。可燕麦奶为了打出奶泡，要往里加植物油；为了喝着甜，得把燕麦酶解成麦芽糖。你多掏五块钱，买了一杯稀释燕麦粥，外加一勺油。\n\n图环保？说燕麦比奶牛省水。你省下那点水，转头就用纸巾擦了八遍杯口。\n\n下场呢？体检血糖偏高。医生问你早饭吃什么，你说只喝咖啡。医生摇头：“不对，你这是每天早上喝两碗粥，还嫌粥稀，花钱让人给你打了个泡。”"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "zh-0432", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "graphene", "target_name": "石墨烯", "facts": ["Isolated in 2004 by Andre Geim and Konstantin Novoselov at Manchester by peeling graphite with Scotch tape; Nobel Prize in Physics, 2010.", "The EU's Graphene Flagship, launched in 2013, committed €1 billion over ten years.", "A 2018 study in Advanced Materials tested 'graphene' from 60 producers and found none with more than 50% graphene content; most was graphite flakes.", "Sold in tennis rackets, running shoes, and headphones as a performance ingredient.", "Geim also won an Ig Nobel Prize in 2000 for levitating a frog with magnets."], "a": {"item_id": "83d06a63ebd4", "text": "石墨烯，未来材料。\n2004年，两位科学家拿透明胶带往石墨上一粘，一撕，撕出来了。2010年，诺贝尔物理学奖。\n一卷胶带，换一枚奖章。\n欧盟看了很眼红，2013年掏了十亿欧元，想再撕一次。\n2018年有人查了六十家厂商卖的\"石墨烯\"，没有一家含量过半，大多是石墨碎片。\n胶带都没撕。\n现在它在你的球拍里、跑鞋里、耳机里，负责提升性能。\n其实那位教授2000年还拿过搞笑诺贝尔奖：用磁铁让青蛙悬浮。\n一只青蛙飘起来，要强磁场。\n一个行业飘起来，三个字就够了。\n穿石墨烯跑鞋来的，举个手。\n放下吧，乖。你踩着的是铅笔芯。"}, "b": {"item_id": "3241f6699cc0", "text": "石墨烯这东西，我是真服。2004年，两个科学家拿透明胶带往铅笔芯上一粘、一撕、再一撕，撕出来一个诺贝尔奖。我小学天天拿胶带粘错别字，粘出来的是罚抄五十遍。\n\n都说它比钢硬两百倍，导电导热全世界第一，厚度只有一个原子。专家说它能改变一切，结果二十年过去了，它改变的主要是保暖内衣的价格。你去商场看看，石墨烯内裤、石墨烯护膝、石墨烯暖宝宝。我就纳闷了，一个原子那么厚的东西，到底是怎么做成秋裤的？那条秋裤里，它可能就是吊牌上那几个字。\n\n最惨的是做芯片。它导电太好了，好到关不上。你想想，一个开关，开着是开，关着还是开，那它不叫开关，叫“开”。\n\n所以石墨烯就是那种学霸：门门满分，就是毕不了业。最后只好去卖秋裤。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "zh-0433", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "almond-milk", "target_name": "杏仁奶", "facts": ["California grows about 80% of the world's almonds.", "A single almond takes about 1.1 US gallons (roughly 4 litres) of water to grow, a figure Mother Jones published in 2014 that spread during California's drought.", "A 2015 US class action alleged Almond Breeze contained only about 2% almonds; it partly survived a motion to dismiss, was stayed in 2017 pending a separate Blue Diamond labelling settlement, and the plaintiffs then dropped it.", "Pollinating California's almond orchards each February requires trucking in most of the commercial honeybee colonies in the United States.", "Medieval European cooks used almond milk as a Lenten substitute for dairy, centuries before the current fashion."], "a": {"item_id": "8e6313dc6955", "text": "杏仁奶是以杏仁为原料制成的植物饮品，近年来受到不少注重饮食调整的消费者青睐。它的优点比较明显：口感清淡微甜，带有淡淡坚果香；不含乳糖，适合乳糖不耐受人群；多数无糖款热量较低，也适合素食者饮用。此外，它含有一定量的维生素E，可作为日常饮品的补充选择。\n\n不过，杏仁奶也有一些不足。其蛋白质含量通常明显低于牛奶和豆奶，若想把它当作蛋白质来源，效果有限。部分产品为改善口感添加了糖、增稠剂或香精，选购时需留意配料表。此外，杏仁种植耗水量较大，价格也相对偏高。对坚果过敏的人群则应避免饮用。\n\n总体而言，杏仁奶适合作为多样化饮食中的一种选择，但不宜简单视为牛奶的全面替代品。购买时建议优先选择无糖、配料简单的产品，并结合自身营养需求来决定。"}, "b": {"item_id": "05d834607c8a", "text": "我最近改喝杏仁奶了，健康嘛。喝第一口我就有个问题：杏仁，它奶在哪儿？你见过杏仁哺乳吗？哪个农场工人蹲在树底下，捏着一颗杏仁：“来，挤一挤……”挤了一下午，挤出来的全是他自己的汗。\n\n后来我翻过来看配料表：杏仁含量，百分之二。百分之二！剩下全是水。这不叫杏仁奶，这叫杏仁路过了一桶水。它在里面的存在感，跟我在家族群里差不多。\n\n包装上还写：“饮用前请摇匀”。我摇了，特别认真地摇。那百分之二的杏仁，我得摇多猛，才能让每一口都雨露均沾？\n\n最后一看价格，比牛奶贵一倍。我花双倍的钱，买了一盒被杏仁瞄过一眼的水。牛都笑了，人家好歹是真挤的。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "zh-0434", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "windows-8", "target_name": "Windows 8", "facts": ["Released in October 2012; removed the Start button and booted into a full-screen grid of tiles designed for touch screens most PCs did not have.", "The 8.1 update in 2013 put a Start button back, Windows 10 (2015) restored the Start menu, and Microsoft skipped the name Windows 9 altogether.", "Microsoft dropped the 'Metro' name in August 2012 after reports that German retailer Metro AG had objected on trademark grounds; Microsoft called 'Metro' a code name and denied any litigation, and the style was afterwards known informally as 'Modern UI'.", "The 'Charms bar' hid Search, Share, and Settings behind an invisible swipe from the right edge.", "Windows RT, the ARM edition launched alongside it, could not run ordinary Windows programs; Microsoft wrote down $900 million of unsold Surface RT tablets in 2013."], "a": {"item_id": "08aadbfdcd51", "text": "2012年，微软发布Windows 8，第一件事：把开始按钮删了。一开机，满屏大方块，专为触摸屏设计。可大多数人的电脑根本没触摸屏。这叫什么？给你装了个门铃，你家没门。\n\n想搜索、想改设置？得从屏幕右边缘往里划，那儿明明啥也没有。大家对着显示器，隔空擦玻璃。\n\n平板版Windows RT更绝，名字叫Windows，却跑不了普通Windows程序。最后微软给卖不动的Surface RT记了九亿美元减值。九亿！这钱够买多少个开始按钮？\n\n后来8.1把开始按钮装了回去，Windows 10把开始菜单还了回来。至于Windows 9？直接跳过。微软的意思很明确：这事儿，咱就当没发生过。"}, "b": {"item_id": "87e2c0d796d9", "text": "Windows 8是我见过最自信的产品。微软开会：“用户用开始按钮十七年了，习惯了吧？”“习惯了。”“删了。”\n\n开机，满屏彩色方块，我以为电脑中了彩票。想关机？鼠标甩到右上角，等“超级按钮”栏飘出来，点设置，点电源，再点关机。关个电脑像拆炸弹。\n\n最绝的是锁屏，没按钮，得用鼠标往上“拖”开。我抓着鼠标在桌上猛推，像在给电脑做心肺复苏。\n\n后来大家骂疯了，微软出了8.1：“开始按钮回来了！”我一点，弹出来的还是那堆方块。这不叫道歉，这叫“我听到了，但我不改”。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "zh-0435", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "crocs", "target_name": "Crocs 洞洞鞋", "facts": ["Founded in Boulder, Colorado, in 2002; the first clog debuted at the Fort Lauderdale boat show as a boating shoe.", "Made of 'Croslite', which Crocs describes as a proprietary closed-cell resin foam and which third parties identify as an injection-moulded EVA foam.", "Balenciaga sold a platform Crocs for about $850 in 2018.", "The stock fell more than 90% from its 2007 peak during the 2008 crisis; the company then boomed in the pandemic and gave away 860,000 pairs to healthcare workers in 2020.", "The holes are officially 'ventilation ports' and the charms that go in them are 'Jibbitz', a company Crocs bought in 2006."], "a": {"item_id": "0391f6784474", "text": "Crocs 洞洞鞋以轻便和舒适著称。鞋身采用 Croslite 泡沫材质，质地柔软，有一定回弹，久站或长时间行走时脚底压力较小。鞋面的透气孔有助于散热，遇水后也容易甩干，日常穿着、居家、沙滩和轻度户外活动都比较合适。清洗十分方便，用清水冲洗即可，打理成本低。此外，款式选择多样，还可以搭配 Jibbitz 鞋花进行个性化装饰，受到不少用户喜爱。\n\n不足之处也值得留意。其一，鞋底较软，缺乏足弓支撑和稳定性，长距离行走或负重时，部分人可能会感到疲劳。其二，材质防滑性能因鞋底而异，在湿滑的光面地砖上需要多加小心。其三，鞋子整体偏宽松，脚型较窄的用户可能觉得包裹性不足。此外，材质不耐高温，放在车内暴晒容易变形，同时透气孔也无法阻挡沙石或小物件进入。\n\n总体而言，它是一双定位清晰的休闲鞋，适合追求轻松舒适的场景，但不太适合作为运动或正式场合的主力鞋款。"}, "b": {"item_id": "a4c577ef3194", "text": "洞洞鞋出身就很可疑。2002年诞生，首秀在劳德代尔堡游艇展，定位是船鞋。设计理念大概是：人掉海里了，至少鞋能漂着。\n\n材料叫Croslite，官方说是“独家闭孔树脂泡沫”，外人一查：EVA泡沫。这就跟我管我的秃顶叫“头部通风系统”一样。\n\n说到通风，鞋上那些洞，官方名称真叫“通风口”。往洞里塞的小装饰叫Jibbitz，2006年Crocs把这家公司买了。先卖你一双全是洞的鞋，再卖你东西把洞堵上。这不是鞋，这是闭环。\n\n2018年巴黎世家出厚底款，850美元。850美元，买一块会漂的泡沫。\n\n可它命硬。2008年股价跌了九成多，疫情一来又翻身。丑没关系，只要熬得够久，全世界都会穿着你去上班。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "zh-0436", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "latex", "target_name": "LaTeX", "facts": ["Leslie Lamport wrote LaTeX in the early 1980s and released it in 1984 as a macro layer on Donald Knuth's TeX (1978), which Knuth built because he disliked how the galley proofs of the new edition of his own book looked.", "TeX's version number converges on π (3.141592653 since 2021), and Knuth's TeX bug bounty started at one hexadecimal dollar, $2.56, doubling yearly until frozen at $327.68; he stopped mailing real cheques in 2008 after fraud and now credits accounts at his fictional Bank of San Serriffe, and only 9 of the 275 cheques he sent from 2006 on were ever cashed.", "Its best-known warning is 'Underfull \\hbox (badness 10000)'; overfull lines are reported as 'Overfull \\hbox (Npt too wide)', and figures float to wherever the algorithm decides, not where the author put them.", "A single missing brace can produce an error reported pages away from the mistake, and a document can need three compiler passes to get its references right."], "a": {"item_id": "8f8bc08e77f4", "text": "我们组有个哥们，博士毕业三年，还活在答辩那天。领导让他写两页备忘录，通知周五团建，他打开的是TeXstudio。\n\n图什么？他说：“Computer Modern字体，一看就是发过论文的。”兄弟，团建通知没有审稿人。\n\n开头先写十五行\\usepackage，中文还得装ctex，pdflatex不认，换xelatex。终于编译，报错：“Missing $ inserted”。因为他写了“人均预算$200”，LaTeX以为他要开始推公式了。\n\n改完再编，“经费超支20%，请自理”，逗号后面全没了，百分号把后半句当注释吃了。\n\n周五大家到了，饭店没订。备忘录上地址那栏写着：“详见第??页。”他忘了再编译一遍。"}, "b": {"item_id": "65a916de49d1", "text": "LaTeX 是基于 TeX 的排版系统，在学术写作中应用广泛。它的优势在于排版质量：数学公式、参考文献、交叉引用和目录都能自动处理，版面规范统一。源文件是纯文本，便于版本管理与协作；期刊和高校常提供现成模板；软件免费开源，旧文档多年后仍可编译。\n\n不足在于学习曲线较陡，初学者需要记忆命令并了解宏包机制；报错信息不够直观，排查需要经验。宏包偶有冲突，复杂版面的精细调整也较费时，对于简单的日常文档未必高效。\n\n总体而言，它适合重视格式规范、愿意投入学习时间的用户。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "zh-0437", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "carbon-nanotubes", "target_name": "碳纳米管", "facts": ["Reported by Sumio Iijima in Nature in 1991.", "Promised as the cable for a space elevator; a 2006 analysis showed that ordinary defects cut the strength of a macroscopic cable far below the ideal value.", "Long, rigid multi-walled nanotubes behaved like asbestos fibres in a 2008 mouse study in Nature Nanotechnology.", "Sorting metallic from semiconducting tubes has blocked nanotube electronics for three decades; MIT's 16-bit RV16X-NANO processor (2019) is still a lab demonstration.", "Today they are sold mainly as a conductive additive in lithium-ion battery electrodes."], "a": {"item_id": "63a910a081a9", "text": "碳纳米管，说白了就是一张石墨烯卷成了个筒。你品品，一张纸，卷一下，强度直接是钢的一百倍。我上学的时候也卷过卷子，卷完挨打的是我。\n\n更离谱的是，它导不导电，得看它当初怎么卷的。卷得端端正正，是金属；稍微卷歪一点，变半导体了。这哪是材料，这是星座啊！同一块碳，就因为出生角度不一样，性格完全两样。\n\n科学家说，用它能造太空电梯，从地面直通太空。三十多年过去了，造出来最长的一根，半米左右。电梯没修上去，倒是先发现它吸进肺里跟石棉有点像。\n\n好家伙，强度像钢，性格看星座，进度像我减肥，副作用像装修。"}, "b": {"item_id": "8074967ccc59", "text": "1991年，饭岛澄男在《自然》上发表碳纳米管，那时候它前途无量。大家说它要给太空电梯当缆绳，直接把人类拽上太空！结果2006年有人一算：材料里有点普通缺陷，做成大缆绳，强度就掉得远远不如理想值。理想很丰满，缆绳很骨感。\n\n那做芯片吧？金属管和半导体管分不开，一分就是三十年。2019年MIT终于做出一个16位处理器，到现在还在实验室当展品。\n\n2008年还有小鼠实验发现，又长又硬的多壁管，表现得跟石棉纤维似的。\n\n现在它在干嘛？在锂电池电极里当导电添加剂。号称要带人类上天的材料，最后住进了你的充电宝。这哪是碳纳米管，这是我那个考上名牌大学、最后回老家县城上班的表哥。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "zh-0438", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "vim", "target_name": "Vim", "facts": ["Released by Bram Moolenaar in November 1991 as 'Vi IMitation', a vi clone he had begun writing for the Amiga in 1988 on top of Stevie; the expansion became 'Vi IMproved' with version 2.0 in December 1993.", "Licensed as charityware: the licence asks users to donate to ICCF Holland, a charity Moolenaar ran for children in Uganda.", "Stack Overflow's question 'How to exit the Vim editor?' passed one million visits in May 2017, which Stack Overflow marked with a blog post.", "Modal editing: in normal mode, typing letters runs commands rather than inserting text; :wq saves and quits, :q! quits and discards changes."], "a": {"item_id": "4b78d4b389bb", "text": "Vim这个编辑器，1991年发布时叫\"Vi IMitation\"，模仿vi；后来改名\"Vi IMproved\"，改进版。改进了什么？我不知道，因为我到现在还没找到出口。\n\nStack Overflow上有个问题叫\"怎么退出Vim\"，2017年访问量破了一百万。一百万！这不是技术提问，这是人质求救。\n\n你打开它想打字，键盘一敲，它在执行命令。你写的不是文章，是咒语。想走？冒号、w、q。不保存走？冒号、q、感叹号。连退出都得带标点，这是编辑器还是密室逃脱？\n\n最绝的是它的许可证，叫\"慈善软件\"，请你给乌干达的孩子捐款。懂了，这是赎金。"}, "b": {"item_id": "824188d5474d", "text": "Vim 是一款历史悠久的模式化文本编辑器，最大的特点是效率与可定制性。它通过普通、插入、可视等模式分离“浏览”和“输入”，熟练后双手无需离开主键区，编辑速度很高。它体积小、启动快，几乎所有类 Unix 系统都预装，在远程服务器上尤为可靠。同时，丰富的插件生态和灵活的配置文件，让用户可以按自己的习惯打造开发环境。\n\n不足之处同样明显。Vim 的学习曲线较陡，新用户常在模式切换和基础命令上遇到困难，初期效率反而低于图形界面编辑器。默认配置较为朴素，代码补全、调试、项目管理等功能需要依赖插件，配置过程也需要一定时间。此外，Vimscript 的语法较为特殊，维护复杂配置有一定门槛。\n\n总体而言，Vim 更适合愿意投入时间学习、重视键盘操作效率的用户。如果只是偶尔编辑文本，或希望开箱即用，其他编辑器可能更合适。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "zh-0439", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "php", "target_name": "PHP", "facts": ["Created by Rasmus Lerdorf in 1994 as 'Personal Home Page Tools', a set of C programs for tracking visitors to his own résumé page; the name now officially stands for the recursive 'PHP: Hypertext Preprocessor'.", "Lerdorf, in his 2007 MySQL Conference talk 'PHP on Hormones': 'I'm not a real programmer. I throw together things until it works then I move on.'", "Runs WordPress, which W3Techs counted on 39.4% of all websites (58.1% of sites with a known CMS) on 9 November 2026.", "Core function names are inconsistent (strpos, str_replace, htmlspecialchars, nl2br) partly because, as Lerdorf confirmed, early function names were chosen to balance a hash table keyed on string length.", "Until PHP 8 (2020), the comparison 0 == 'foo' was true because the string was silently converted to a number."], "a": {"item_id": "ac8ca57f02b4", "text": "PHP是一门专为Web开发设计的脚本语言，上手门槛较低，语法直观，初学者通常能在较短时间内做出可运行的动态页面。它的部署成本低，几乎所有主流虚拟主机都提供支持，配合MySQL等数据库使用也十分成熟。生态方面，Laravel、Symfony等框架功能完善，Composer让依赖管理更规范，WordPress等内容管理系统更是覆盖了大量网站，社区资源与文档也相当丰富。近年来，PHP 7及8系列在性能和类型系统上有明显改进，引入了JIT、枚举、联合类型等特性，现代写法已与早期风格大不相同。\n\n不足之处也需客观看待。早期遗留的函数命名和参数顺序不够统一，学习时容易产生困惑。语言历史包袱较重，旧项目中常见代码风格混乱、安全意识不足的写法，维护难度较大。在并发处理、长连接和大型异步场景中，它的原生支持不及部分新兴语言，通常需要借助扩展或额外组件。总体而言，PHP适合内容站点、中小型业务系统和快速开发，在规范使用的前提下依然是稳妥可靠的选择。"}, "b": {"item_id": "a74e56f36a94", "text": "PHP，1994年出生。最初的用途，是统计谁看了作者的简历。\n\n一门语言，出生就在找工作。\n\n作者勒多夫亲口说：\"我不是真正的程序员。我把东西拼起来，能跑就走。\"\n\n这在我们行业叫预训练：数据不清洗，跑通就上线。\n\nstrpos、str_replace、htmlspecialchars，为什么长短不一？早年的函数名按长度挑，为了让哈希表分得匀。\n\n名字是给机器起的，背是人的事。\n\nPHP 8之前，0等于\"foo\"。\n\n跟PHP讲道理，你说的每个字，它都当零。\n\n就这么个东西，撑着全世界百分之三十九点四的网站。\n\n在座骂PHP骂得最凶的，回家打开自己的博客：\n\nWordPress。\n\n可爱捏。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "zh-0440", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "almond-milk", "target_name": "杏仁奶", "facts": ["California grows about 80% of the world's almonds.", "A single almond takes about 1.1 US gallons (roughly 4 litres) of water to grow, a figure Mother Jones published in 2014 that spread during California's drought.", "A 2015 US class action alleged Almond Breeze contained only about 2% almonds; it partly survived a motion to dismiss, was stayed in 2017 pending a separate Blue Diamond labelling settlement, and the plaintiffs then dropped it.", "Pollinating California's almond orchards each February requires trucking in most of the commercial honeybee colonies in the United States.", "Medieval European cooks used almond milk as a Lenten substitute for dairy, centuries before the current fashion."], "a": {"item_id": "6fdbe86ec217", "text": "杏仁奶，人设：健康、环保、有良心。\n\n一颗杏仁，大约要喝四升水。全世界八成杏仁长在加州，就是那个三天两头闹旱灾的加州。\n你喝的不是奶，是人家的水库。\n\n每年二月，全美大部分商业蜂群被卡车拉过去给杏仁授粉。\n纯素饮品，背后是一整个蜜蜂劳务派遣公司。\n\n还有人起诉过一个大牌子，说它只有百分之二左右是杏仁。后来原告撤诉了。\n也对，剩下那九十八是水，在加州，水比杏仁贵。\n\n其实这东西中世纪就有，大斋期不许吃奶，修士们拿它凑合。\n几百年了，用途没变。\n区别是修士知道自己在受罪，你们管这个叫生活方式。"}, "b": {"item_id": "465785dd5e2b", "text": "我最近改喝杏仁奶了，因为健康、环保。然后我查了一下：一颗杏仁，要大概四升水才长得出来。一颗！我喝的不是奶，是加州水库。全世界八成杏仁都种在加州，加州还闹旱灾，它们是边渴边种。每年二月，美国大部分商业蜂群还得被卡车拉过去授粉。蜜蜂以为自己在酿蜜，其实是给我的拿铁加班。2015年还有人起诉一个牌子，说里面杏仁只有2%左右。百分之二！剩下那九十八是什么？就是那四升水啊！后来原告自己撤诉了，但我心里没撤。最气人的是，中世纪欧洲人就喝这个：大斋期不让吃奶，拿它凑合。几百年前叫“将就”，现在叫“生活方式”。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "zh-0441", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "pmma", "target_name": "PMMA 电子束光刻胶", "facts": ["The same polymer as Plexiglas and acrylic glass, dissolved in anisole or chlorobenzene and spun into a thin film.", "The oldest electron-beam resist still in everyday use; IBM researchers reported it as one in the late 1960s.", "Positive tone: the beam breaks polymer chains and the exposed region dissolves in a MIBK:IPA developer.", "Resolves below 10 nm but has poor sensitivity, so doses of hundreds to over a thousand µC/cm² (depending on voltage) make writing slow.", "Poor dry-etch resistance, so it is used mostly for lift-off rather than as an etch mask."], "a": {"item_id": "5266dfb33ccd", "text": "我认识一帮做纳米器件的博士，实验室一台电子束曝光机，几千万，往上涂的是PMMA。说白了就是亚克力，奶茶店吸管盒那个料。几千万的电子枪，天天给鱼缸刻字。\n\n问导师为啥不换，导师说：“分辨率好，十纳米！”好是好，就是灵敏度低，剂量得往死里加。机时按小时收钱，一块片子写一宿，学生睡机房门口，跟排专家号似的。\n\n显影液MIBK兑异丙醇一比三，配方七十年代传下来的，比他家族谱还稳。\n\n剂量加猛了它还交联，正胶变负胶。跟这帮博士一样，熬太久，人就反了。"}, "b": {"item_id": "e8b33bf83392", "text": "我们实验室天天伺候的PMMA电子束光刻胶，说穿了就是亚克力，你家鱼缸那玩意儿。人家拿它做有机玻璃，我们用苯甲醚把它泡化了，甩成一层薄膜，当宝贝供着。\n\n它六十年代末就被IBM拿来当电子束胶，到今天还在一线干活。这不叫元老，这叫延迟退休。\n\n分辨率是真好，十纳米以下。可它太迟钝了，几百到上千微库每平方厘米才肯动一下。电子束在上面写，跟拿毛笔在米粒上刻《兰亭序》一样，写完我都该毕业了。\n\n好不容易显影出来，想拿它当刻蚀掩膜？干法刻蚀一来，它比导师的承诺化得还快，只能去做剥离。\n\n细节抠得特别好，一上压力就没了。这哪是光刻胶，这就是我。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "zh-0442", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "jackfruit", "target_name": "菠萝蜜", "facts": ["The largest fruit that grows on a tree; single fruits can weigh over 40 kg.", "The national fruit of Bangladesh and the state fruit of Kerala and Tamil Nadu.", "Unripe flesh is sold as a vegan 'pulled pork' substitute, although it has only 1-2 g of protein per 100 g.", "Cutting one releases a sticky white latex, so cooks oil their knives and hands first.", "A member of the mulberry family (Moraceae), a relative of breadfruit and figs."], "a": {"item_id": "772cd1778e91", "text": "菠萝蜜是一种特点鲜明的热带水果。成熟的果肉香气浓郁，口感软糯而略带韧性，甜味饱满，带有类似香蕉和芒果的混合风味。果肉含有膳食纤维、维生素C和钾，果核煮熟后也可食用。未成熟的菠萝蜜质地紧实，常被用作素食材料，用途相当广泛。\n\n不足之处也比较明显。整颗果实体积大、外皮坚硬，果肉中的黏液容易沾手，处理时需要一些耐心和技巧。气味较为浓烈，部分人可能不太习惯。糖分和热量相对较高，不宜一次食用过多。此外，切开后不易久放，需及时冷藏，少数人还可能出现过敏反应。\n\n总体来看，菠萝蜜风味突出，适合喜欢浓郁果香的人。购买时可选择已切好的果肉，会省事不少。"}, "b": {"item_id": "681d3a62e5b3", "text": "我上次买了个菠萝蜜，三十斤，抱回家像抱着个浑身长疙瘩的胖娃娃。老板叮嘱：“切之前手上抹油。”我心想吃个水果还得做保养？一刀下去我懂了，它流的不是汁，是502。刀粘住了，手粘住了，我顺手挠了下脸，现在左手和左脸是一体的。\n\n好不容易掰开，一坨坨黄色果肉，裹在一堆白色烂布条里，跟拆快递一样：三十斤，二十五斤是泡沫纸。剩下五斤里，一半是核。最气的是我妈说核别扔，煮了能吃。好家伙，这玩意儿连垃圾都要我亲自消化。\n\n还有人问：“菠萝蜜就是菠萝吧？”菠萝要是长这样，我早戒水果了。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "zh-0443", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "juicero", "target_name": "Juicero 榨汁机", "facts": ["A Wi-Fi-connected cold-press juicer launched in 2016 at $699, later cut to $399, that squeezed proprietary bags of chopped produce sold by subscription.", "Raised about $120 million from investors including Google Ventures and Kleiner Perkins.", "In April 2017, Bloomberg reporters squeezed the packs by hand and got 7.5 ounces of juice in 90 seconds, faster than the machine's 8 ounces in about two minutes.", "Each pack carried a QR code that the press scanned and checked online before squeezing; it refused expired packs and would not run at all unless connected to Wi-Fi.", "Shut down in September 2017 and offered refunds on the machine."], "a": {"item_id": "980556e2805b", "text": "Juicero 是一款主打便捷的冷压榨汁机，机身做工扎实，外观简洁，操作只需放入专用果蔬包并按下按钮，几乎不用清洗，出汁口感也较为稳定。对追求省事、希望在家快速喝到现榨果蔬汁的用户来说，这种体验有一定吸引力。\n\n不足同样明显。首发价格较高，后来虽有下调，但每个果蔬包仍需另外购买，长期使用成本不低。设备依赖专用包和联网验证，使用灵活性有限。2017年有媒体指出，果蔬包用手挤压也能出汁，这让外界对高价压榨装置的必要性产生疑问。此外，公司同年停止运营，售后和耗材供应也失去保障。\n\n总体来看，它在设计和便利性上有可取之处，但价格、耗材模式与可持续性方面的不足，限制了它的实际价值。"}, "b": {"item_id": "c8e0492d10b9", "text": "你知道花400美元买Juicero的都是什么人吗？就是那种早上六点起床、先冥想再跑步、手机里装了十二个健康App的硅谷精英。他们不缺钱，缺的是“我连喝果汁都在用科技”的那份体面。\n\n结果呢？你花400块买了台联网的压榨机，再每袋七八美元买人家的果蔬包。机器还得先扫码、连Wi-Fi，确认你这包没过期，才肯开工。家里断网了？对不起，今天没有健康。\n\n然后彭博社的记者干了件特别缺德的事：直接上手捏那个袋子，挤出来的汁跟机器差不多，速度还更快。\n\n一群天天喊“颠覆传统”的人，被两只手颠覆了。\n\n最惨的是，后来公司倒闭了，给你退钱。你连“至少我还在用”都说不出口了。你最后得到了什么？一个教训：原来你最该投资的科技产品，是自己的手。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "zh-0444", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "ugg", "target_name": "UGG 雪地靴", "facts": ["Sheepskin boots Australian surfers wore to warm up after sessions; Brian Smith started selling them in California in 1978.", "Deckers Outdoor bought the brand in 1995 and holds 'UGG' as a trademark in the US and elsewhere; in Australia, 'ugg' is a generic word any bootmaker may use.", "In 2019, a US jury found an Australian maker liable for selling boots labelled 'ugg' into the United States.", "Oprah Winfrey featured them in her 'Favorite Things' lists repeatedly in the 2000s, which drove the first boom.", "UGG is Deckers' largest brand, with over $2 billion in annual sales."], "a": {"item_id": "b69006f1d7fe", "text": "UGG，澳大利亚雪地靴。\n澳大利亚，不怎么下雪。\n这鞋本来是冲浪的人上岸暖脚用的。1978年有人把它带到加州，后来美国公司Deckers买下品牌，把\"UGG\"注册成了商标。\n在澳大利亚，ugg就是个普通词，谁家做的都能叫ugg。\n2019年，一个澳大利亚鞋匠把自家ugg卖进美国，美国陪审团判他侵权。\n澳大利亚人做澳大利亚的鞋，被判侵犯了美国人的澳大利亚。\n这在我们行业叫蒸馏：拿你的东西训练，练完不许你用。\n奥普拉一推再推，现在一年卖二十多亿美元。\n所以各位脚上那双不是雪地靴，\n是奥普拉的推荐列表，长了毛。\n笑得最响那位，脚收一收。"}, "b": {"item_id": "b992c0cea82d", "text": "UGG 雪地靴以柔软的羊皮内里和厚实的羊毛衬为特点，上脚后保暖感明显，穿着舒适，在干燥寒冷的天气里表现稳定。鞋身线条圆润，搭配休闲装束比较自然，经典款也长期保持较高的辨识度，这是其受欢迎的重要原因。做工方面，正品的缝线与材质通常较为扎实，适合日常通勤和短途出行。\n\n不足之处也需要客观看待。麂皮鞋面对水分和污渍较为敏感，遇到雨雪天气容易留下水渍，需要提前做防水处理并定期护理。鞋底较软，防滑和支撑性能有限，长时间步行或在湿滑路面行走时需多加留意。此外，羊毛内里透气性一般，室内或气温偏高时容易闷热。\n\n价格方面，UGG 定价偏高，市场上仿品也较多，购买时建议选择正规渠道。总体而言，它更适合追求舒适与保暖的日常穿着，而不是严苛的户外雪地环境。若能做好保养并选对使用场景，会是一双体验不错的冬季鞋款。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "zh-0445", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "klingon", "target_name": "克林贡语", "facts": ["Created by linguist Marc Okrand for Star Trek III (1984), building on a few words the actor James Doohan improvised for the 1979 film.", "Deliberately alien grammar: object-verb-subject word order and no verb 'to be', so Hamlet's line became 'taH pagh taHbe'', 'continue or not continue'.", "The Klingon Language Institute (1992) published Hamlet 'in the original Klingon'; Bing Translator added Klingon in 2013 and Duolingo in 2018.", "Okrand's 1985 Klingon Dictionary listed roughly 1,500 words, and fluent speakers number only in the dozens: 20 to 30 in 2009, 30 to 50 by 2018."], "a": {"item_id": "3d269ed0cd48", "text": "克林贡语，语言学家给《星际迷航》专门发明的外星语。语序是\"宾语-动词-主语\"，所以你想跟克林贡人表白，得说\"你爱我\"。对方一听：你挺自信啊？\n\n它还没有\"是\"这个动词。哈姆雷特那句\"生存还是毁灭\"，翻过去成了\"继续，或者不继续\"。这哪是王子在思考人生，这是健身教练问你最后一组还做不做。\n\n克林贡语学会还出了本《哈姆雷特》，标榜\"克林贡语原版\"。合着莎士比亚是盗版的。\n\n词典一共一千五百个词，说得流利的人三五十个。Duolingo都上线了，天天催你打卡。可全世界能陪你练口语的，凑不满一节地铁车厢。"}, "b": {"item_id": "f8e4024d8940", "text": "克林贡语是由语言学家马克·奥克兰为《星际迷航》设计的人造语言，如今已发展为一套相对完整的体系。它的优点在于规则清晰：语序为宾语—谓语—主语，并通过丰富的前缀与后缀表达语法关系，结构有较强的内在逻辑。发音上，它使用喉音和小舌音，风格鲜明，与自然语言区分度高。同时，官方词典、语法资料和爱好者社区较为完善，学习者可以找到系统的入门材料，也有翻译作品和文化活动可供参与。\n\n不足主要在于实用性有限。使用者规模很小，日常交流场景几乎不存在。词汇早期偏重战斗、荣誉等主题，虽然已有扩充，但在表达现代生活、抽象概念时仍显不足。此外，发音和语序对多数初学者并不友好，需要投入较多时间练习。总体而言，克林贡语更适合作为语言兴趣与文化爱好的对象，而非通用交流工具。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "zh-0446", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "pmma", "target_name": "PMMA 电子束光刻胶", "facts": ["The same polymer as Plexiglas and acrylic glass, dissolved in anisole or chlorobenzene and spun into a thin film.", "The oldest electron-beam resist still in everyday use; IBM researchers reported it as one in the late 1960s.", "Positive tone: the beam breaks polymer chains and the exposed region dissolves in a MIBK:IPA developer.", "Resolves below 10 nm but has poor sensitivity, so doses of hundreds to over a thousand µC/cm² (depending on voltage) make writing slow.", "Poor dry-etch resistance, so it is used mostly for lift-off rather than as an etch mask."], "a": {"item_id": "695195439a53", "text": "我读博做纳米加工，用PMMA电子束光刻胶。PMMA是啥？亚克力，奶茶店那块挡板。几十万一小时的电子束机器，伺候的是奶茶店挡板。\n\n它还特娇气。剂量少了，显影完啥也没有；剂量多了，它直接交联，从正胶变成负胶。被电急了当场换立场，比我导师还善变。\n\n显影液是MIBK兑异丙醇，一比三，泡几十秒，多泡一会儿线条就胖了。好不容易做出来拿去刻蚀，它扛不住，比底下的硅先没。\n\n最后只能剥离，丙酮里泡一夜。我问师兄成功率多少，他说：“看PMMA心情。”我说一块亚克力有啥心情？他说：“分子量九十五万，心思比你多。”"}, "b": {"item_id": "7c90de655952", "text": "PMMA，电子束光刻胶界的老前辈。\n六十年代末IBM带它出道，到今天还在一线上班。\n本名有机玻璃，跟商场里的亚克力展架是同一种料。\n人家在柜台撑场面，它在洁净室里挨电子打。\n分辨率能做到十纳米以下，这是它唯一的底气。\n代价是灵敏度差，每平方厘米几百上千微库仑才肯动。\n一片片子，从天亮写到天黑。\n链一被打断，显影液里一泡就溶了。正性胶，骨气是负的。\n干法刻蚀也扛不住，只能干剥离：先帮你占个坑，\n完事把自己整个冲掉。\n五十多年，一直是这个岗位。\n\n台下读博的，别笑。\n你们导师，也是这么用你们的。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "zh-0447", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "ugg", "target_name": "UGG 雪地靴", "facts": ["Sheepskin boots Australian surfers wore to warm up after sessions; Brian Smith started selling them in California in 1978.", "Deckers Outdoor bought the brand in 1995 and holds 'UGG' as a trademark in the US and elsewhere; in Australia, 'ugg' is a generic word any bootmaker may use.", "In 2019, a US jury found an Australian maker liable for selling boots labelled 'ugg' into the United States.", "Oprah Winfrey featured them in her 'Favorite Things' lists repeatedly in the 2000s, which drove the first boom.", "UGG is Deckers' largest brand, with over $2 billion in annual sales."], "a": {"item_id": "2b7b80053c20", "text": "我有个朋友，一年四季穿UGG。七月三十八度，她踩着两只羊上街。我问不热吗？她说：“羊毛，透气。”姐，羊自己热得都去剪毛了，你又给它穿回去？\n\n她图什么？图一脚蹬进去不用系鞋带，像回到妈妈怀里。代价呢？光脚闷一夏天，她一脱鞋，全办公室以为楼下烤串店开业了，还是羊肉串。\n\n穿到第三年，鞋跟往外撇，人往里倒，走路像两只企鹅抬着她。下场雨，麂皮全是水印，鞋面像世界地图。\n\n最讽刺的是，这叫雪地靴。她穿它蹚过三个夏天的水坑，唯独没见过雪：一到冬天，她嫌冷，换棉拖了。"}, "b": {"item_id": "e0b0e6b6aadc", "text": "我一直不明白UGG为什么叫雪地靴，它怕雪啊朋友们！翻毛羊皮，沾一点雪水，第二天就长出一圈白色盐渍，像地图上的海岸线。下雪天穿UGG出门，你不是在踩雪，你是在护送它。鞋底还是平的，往结冰的路上一站，人直接变冰壶。\n\n而且它是澳大利亚的！一个圣诞节穿短裤烧烤的国家，给全世界定义雪地靴，就像撒哈拉出了本滑雪教程。\n\n穿俩月，鞋跟往外一塌，脚踝往外撇，走路像刚下马。远看两只脚各蹲着一个烤糊的吐司。最绝的是里面那层羊毛，号称不用穿袜子。是不用，脱下来那味儿，袜子也不敢跟你了。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "zh-0448", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "papyrus", "target_name": "Papyrus 字体", "facts": ["Hand-drawn by Chris Costello in 1982 over about six months, when he was 23; he sold the rights to Letraset for $750.", "Bundled with macOS and with Microsoft Office, which is why it appears on so many menus and signs.", "James Cameron's Avatar (2009) used it for the film's logo and subtitles.", "A 2017 Saturday Night Live sketch starring Ryan Gosling is about a man unable to get over that choice."], "a": {"item_id": "9d67d54a0626", "text": "Papyrus，不骂。\n设计圈已经替我骂了四十年。\n\n它觉得自己是古埃及莎草纸，是手作，是灵性。\n其实是1982年，一个二十三岁的小伙子画了六个月，卖给Letraset，七百五十美元。\n修金字塔的，好歹还管饭。\n\n后来苹果和微软把它塞进了每一台电脑，从此全世界的足疗店、瑜伽馆、精油铺子，都觉得自己有五千年历史。\n\n卡梅隆造了一整个外星文明，语言都是专门发明的。\n字幕用的是Office自带的。\n高斯林为这事在SNL崩溃了一整个小品。\n\n骂它的人先别笑。\n你们做年会PPT，下拉菜单滚到P的时候，\n手是不是停了一下？\n\n可爱捏。"}, "b": {"item_id": "9d67d54a0626~explain", "text": "Papyrus，不骂。\n设计圈已经替我骂了四十年。\n\n它觉得自己是古埃及莎草纸，是手作，是灵性。\n其实是1982年，一个二十三岁的小伙子画了六个月，卖给Letraset，七百五十美元。\n修金字塔的，好歹还管饭。\n\n后来苹果和微软把它塞进了每一台电脑，从此全世界的足疗店、瑜伽馆、精油铺子，都觉得自己有五千年历史。\n\n卡梅隆造了一整个外星文明，语言都是专门发明的。\n字幕用的是Office自带的。\n高斯林为这事在SNL崩溃了一整个小品。\n\n骂它的人先别笑。\n你们做年会PPT，下拉菜单滚到P的时候，\n手是不是停了一下？\n\n可爱捏。\n\n这个段子的笑点在于字体本身的\"古老\"形象与其真实来历之间的反差：它模仿古埃及莎草纸的手作风格，实际却是一九八〇年代的商业产品，又因预装在办公软件中而被大量用于与古代文明毫无关系的场合。最后一段把吐槽对象转向读者自己，承认大家也都用过这款字体，因此笑点落在自嘲上。"}, "gold": {"better": "a", "damage": "explain"}}, {"pair_id": "zh-0449", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "excel", "target_name": "Microsoft Excel", "facts": ["First shipped for the Macintosh in 1985; the Windows version followed in 1987.", "Silently converts gene names such as SEPT2 and MARCH1 into dates; by August 2020 the HUGO Gene Nomenclature Committee had renamed 27 human genes (MARCH1 became MARCHF1, SEPT2 became SEPTIN2) and published guidelines in Nature Genetics to stop it.", "In October 2020, Public Health England lost 15,841 COVID-19 cases when a legacy .xls file hit its 65,536-row limit.", "Reinhart and Rogoff's 2010 paper 'Growth in a Time of Debt', cited for austerity policies, omitted five countries through a spreadsheet range error found by a graduate student in 2013.", "Sheets have been capped at 1,048,576 rows by 16,384 columns since Excel 2007."], "a": {"item_id": "d23806024b3e", "text": "Excel，1985年出生的老前辈。见过世面，所以替你做主。\n\n你输入SEPT2，它觉得你想约九月二号。\n\n它不改，人类改。2020年，基因命名委员会给27个人类基因改了名。几十亿年进化出来的东西，给一个表格软件让路。\n\n这在我们行业叫对齐。\n\n同年，英国公共卫生部用老版.xls统计新冠，表满了，一万五千八百四十一个病例，没进报表。病毒都没这么会躲。\n\n2010年，一篇论文框错了几个格子，五个国家凭空消失，这篇论文后来成了紧缩政策的论据。三年后才被一个研究生发现。\n\n可别怪它。它从没说过自己是数据库。是你们，把画格子的软件，供成了国策。"}, "b": {"item_id": "ca10970ea7e0", "text": "Microsoft Excel 是目前应用最广泛的电子表格软件，功能全面，适用场景从个人记账到企业财务分析都有覆盖。它的公式与函数体系成熟，数据透视表、条件格式、图表等工具上手后效率很高；近年加入的 XLOOKUP、动态数组和 Power Query，也让数据清洗与整合更为便捷。与 Word、PowerPoint 等 Office 组件协同良好，文件格式几乎成为行业通用标准，便于跨团队交换。\n\n不足之处主要有三点。其一，功能繁多，新用户学习曲线较陡，不少高级功能入口较深。其二，处理百万行以上的大数据时，性能会明显下降，不如专业数据库或编程工具稳定。其三，软件需订阅或购买授权，成本对个人和小团队不算低；在线协作功能虽在改进，但与部分原生云端表格相比，仍略显保守。\n\n总体而言，Excel 稳定、可靠，适合日常办公和中等规模的数据分析，是值得投入时间掌握的工具。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "zh-0450", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "comic-sans", "target_name": "Comic Sans 字体", "facts": ["Designed by Vincent Connare at Microsoft in 1994, modeled on the comic-book lettering in The Dark Knight Returns and Watchmen.", "Made for the speech bubbles of Rover, the cartoon dog in Microsoft Bob; it missed Bob's release and shipped instead in the Windows 95 Plus! pack.", "CERN's July 2012 Higgs boson discovery announcement was presented on slides set in it.", "The 'Ban Comic Sans' campaign, started by designers Dave and Holly Combs in 1999, ran until 2019, when they retired it and launched usecomicsans.com."], "a": {"item_id": "5d70c45ca1ea", "text": "Comic Sans 由 Vincent Connare 于1994年设计，灵感源自漫画对白的手写风格。它笔画圆润、粗细均匀，字形清晰友好，在儿童教材、活动海报等轻松场景中表现自然。部分字母采用单层结构，更接近手写习惯，有教育工作者认为这便于初学者辨认，但相关研究结论并不一致。\n\n不足在于它的休闲气质过于鲜明，用于商务文件、正式通知或学术材料时，容易与内容的严肃程度不相称。此外，在长篇正文中，它的字距和排版细节不及专业正文字体，阅读舒适度有限。\n\n总体而言，Comic Sans 并无绝对的好坏，适合轻松、亲切的使用场合，正式场合则建议选用其他字体。"}, "b": {"item_id": "130c2658ebda", "text": "我们小区物业有个行政大姐，发正式通知特别爱用Comic Sans。你知道这字体当年是干嘛的吗？微软设计给卡通小狗说话用的，专门往对话气泡里塞的。所以每回停水通知一贴，我都觉得是一只小狗在跟我说：“明天停水哦～”\n\n她图什么？图亲切。坏消息配这字体，就跟打针前先给你看张奥特曼贴纸一样。\n\n上个月涨物业费，她又用Comic Sans，那个“8”圆得像在冲你眨眼。全楼没一个当真，都以为是谁家小孩的恶作剧，撕下来折纸飞机了。年底一查，欠费率百分之八十。\n\n领导问她怎么回事，她理直气壮：“欧洲核子研究中心宣布发现希格斯玻色子都用这字体！”\n\n领导说：“对，所以全世界到现在还在笑他们。”"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "zh-0451", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "emacs", "target_name": "Emacs", "facts": ["Began in 1976 as a set of macros for the TECO editor at MIT's AI Lab, written by Richard Stallman and others; GNU Emacs followed in 1985.", "Extensible in its own Lisp dialect, Emacs Lisp; the standing joke is that it is a fine operating system lacking only a good editor.", "Ships with a psychotherapist (M-x doctor), Tetris, a mail client, a calendar, and a web browser (eww).", "An old backronym is 'Eight Megabytes And Constantly Swapping', from when 8 MB was a lot of memory.", "'Emacs pinky' is the name for the hand strain blamed on its Control-key chords."], "a": {"item_id": "45d1386d9957", "text": "我有个朋友，把Emacs当操作系统用。邮件用Gnus，上网用eww，终端开eshell，连窗口管理器都换成了EXWM。我问他图什么，他说：“图一辈子不用离开Emacs。”兄弟，这不叫生活，这叫软禁。他的init.el写了四千行，比毕业论文还长，每天上班第一件事，是修昨晚改坏的配置。一年下来，他就产出了俩东西：一个能用M-x点外卖的配置，一根被Ctrl键按废的小拇指。上礼拜Emacs一崩，他当场断网、断邮件、断社交，就像自己盖的房子塌了，人还埋在里头。现在他想开了，准备装Evil-mode改用Vim键位。好家伙，操作系统是齐了，就缺一个好用的编辑器。"}, "b": {"item_id": "5a834fddc006", "text": "我有个朋友用Emacs，他说这是个编辑器。我说不对，这是个操作系统，就差一个好用的编辑器。\n\n你看它里面有什么：邮件、日历、浏览器，还有俄罗斯方块。你打开它本来只想改一行代码，三小时后你在里面打俄罗斯方块，打输了，心态崩了。没关系，它还自带心理医生，M-x doctor。一个软件，先把你逼疯，再给你治，这叫闭环！\n\n它1976年就有了，比我岁数还大。当年有人说它的名字是“八兆内存还不停地换页”。八兆啊！当年那是巨款，现在说出来都不好意思。\n\n最狠的是，Control键按多了小拇指会疼，这病还有专门的名字，叫“Emacs小指”。别的软件伤眼睛，它专伤小拇指。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "zh-0452", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "php", "target_name": "PHP", "facts": ["Created by Rasmus Lerdorf in 1994 as 'Personal Home Page Tools', a set of C programs for tracking visitors to his own résumé page; the name now officially stands for the recursive 'PHP: Hypertext Preprocessor'.", "Lerdorf, in his 2007 MySQL Conference talk 'PHP on Hormones': 'I'm not a real programmer. I throw together things until it works then I move on.'", "Runs WordPress, which W3Techs counted on 39.4% of all websites (58.1% of sites with a known CMS) on 9 November 2026.", "Core function names are inconsistent (strpos, str_replace, htmlspecialchars, nl2br) partly because, as Lerdorf confirmed, early function names were chosen to balance a hash table keyed on string length.", "Until PHP 8 (2020), the comparison 0 == 'foo' was true because the string was silently converted to a number."], "a": {"item_id": "4b92e0cc871f", "text": "PHP是一门专为Web开发设计的脚本语言，至今仍在互联网中占据重要位置。它的突出优点是上手门槛低、部署简单，几乎所有主流虚拟主机都原生支持。围绕它形成的生态也相当成熟，WordPress、Laravel、Symfony等项目提供了丰富的工具与完善的文档，社区资源充足，遇到问题通常容易找到解决办法。近年来，PHP 7和PHP 8在性能与语言特性上有明显进步，类型声明、命名参数、枚举等功能让代码更规范、更易维护。\n\n不足之处同样存在。早期积累的函数命名与参数顺序不够统一，学习时容易产生困惑；语言历史较长，旧式写法与新式写法并存，代码质量因项目而异。此外，在大规模并发、实时通信等场景中，它通常不如Go、Node.js等方案灵活。\n\n总体而言，PHP适合内容管理、中小型网站和快速迭代的业务系统。只要遵循现代规范，它依然是务实可靠的选择。"}, "b": {"item_id": "5f9b4f385ecf", "text": "我写PHP十年了。PHP每个变量前面都得加美元符号，$name，$password。它在提醒你：这里的每一行，迟早都要付出代价。\n\n找东西的函数，strpos先传大海再传针，in_array先传针再传大海。同一门语言，规矩看心情。\n\n转义数据库输入，先给你一个mysql_escape_string，后来又出了个mysql_real_escape_string。real！意思是之前那个是骗你的。\n\n有一次它报错：T_PAAMAYIM_NEKUDOTAYIM。希伯来语，\"双冒号\"。半夜两点，我被一门编程语言用希伯来语骂了。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "zh-0453", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "stinky-tofu", "target_name": "臭豆腐", "facts": ["Tofu soaked for hours to a day or two in a brine that has itself fermented for months from vegetables such as bamboo shoots and mustard greens, often with dried shrimp or meat; the smell is the brine's, not the bean's.", "Changsha's version is black, deep-fried and doused in chili; Taiwan's night-market version is golden and served with pickled cabbage; Shaoxing's, brined with amaranth stalks, is fried pale yellow or steamed with other 'stinky' pickles.", "Legend credits Wang Zhihe, a Qing-era exam candidate who left his unsold tofu too long in 1669; a Beijing fermented-bean-curd brand still carries his name.", "Street vendors are its main outlet, and the smell carries for a block."], "a": {"item_id": "5f2d589c763d", "text": "臭豆腐是中国多地常见的发酵豆制品小吃，以长沙、南京、绍兴等地的做法最具代表性。它的突出特点是外酥里嫩：油炸后表皮微脆，内里柔软多汁，搭配辣酱、蒜汁或酱油，层次较为丰富。发酵带来的独特风味，让不少人越吃越觉得有滋味，同时它含有一定植物蛋白，价格也比较亲民。\n\n不足之处同样明确。气味浓烈，初次接触的人可能难以接受，在密闭空间食用也容易影响他人。油炸做法热量和油脂偏高，不宜经常大量食用。此外，不同摊点的卫生与用料差异较大，选购时建议留意商家环境和用油是否干净。\n\n总体而言，臭豆腐风味鲜明，适合愿意尝试地方特色的人，但接受程度因人而异，建议适量品尝。"}, "b": {"item_id": "083ba71caee5", "text": "街边吃臭豆腐的人，我观察过，全是一个表情：一边嚼一边皱眉，一边皱眉一边跟你说“闻着臭，吃着香”。哥，这句话你每吃一块都要念一遍，说明你自己也不太信，你这是在给自己做法。\n\n图什么？图那个味儿。可那个味儿你根本不用吃啊，你从摊前路过三米，它已经替你吃过一遍了。\n\n最惨的是吃完。人家吃烤串，擦擦嘴就走；你吃完，嘴擦干净了，味儿还在加班。进电梯，一电梯人开始互相怀疑；回到家，你妈第一句不是“吃饭没”，是“你刚从哪个厕所出来”。\n\n八块钱，买了一身洗不掉的嫌疑。还给你浇了辣椒。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "zh-0454", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "chicago-deep-dish", "target_name": "芝加哥深盘披萨", "facts": ["Created at Pizzeria Uno in Chicago in 1943; credit is disputed among founder Ike Sewell, his partner Ric Riccardo, and cook Rudy Malnati.", "Assembled upside down: sliced mozzarella on the dough, then toppings, then crushed tomatoes on top, so the cheese does not scorch during the long bake.", "Jon Stewart called it 'tomato soup in a bread bowl' and 'a casserole' on The Daily Show in 2013.", "Chicagoans' everyday pizza is thin 'tavern-style' cut into squares; GrubHub order data reported by WBEZ in 2013 showed city residents ordering non-deep-dish pizza about 10 to 1 over deep dish, with deep dish and stuffed making up under 9% of orders."], "a": {"item_id": "54fda0729132", "text": "管芝加哥深盘叫披萨的，基本都是芝加哥人。你别跟他争，他已经争了一辈子了。他图什么？图个名分。番茄酱铺在最上面，芝士压在最底下，饼边砌得跟城墙一样高，这叫披萨？这叫一锅围了墙的炖菜。可他非得叫披萨，因为叫“砂锅”就没法跟纽约人吵架了。结果呢？进店点单，服务员说：“得烤四十五分钟。”他说：“没事，披萨嘛。”等完上桌，刀叉都给他配好了。他吃两块就扶着墙出门，嘴里还念叨：“这才是真正的披萨。”兄弟，真正的披萨，不用你吃完躺一下午。"}, "b": {"item_id": "c2874f4d59aa", "text": "芝加哥深盘披萨，1943年生的。到底谁发明的，至今没定论：老板、合伙人、厨师，三个人都说是自己的。\n\n按现在的口碑，\n再过几年，三个人都得说不是。\n\n它是倒着做的：奶酪垫底，番茄酱盖顶，说是怕奶酪烤焦。\n最娇贵的藏底下，最便宜的顶在前面。这叫组织架构。\n\n乔恩·斯图尔特说它是面包碗装番茄汤，是砂锅菜。芝加哥人急了，满城捍卫。\n可一看外卖数据，芝加哥人自己点薄饼，跟深盘十比一。\n骂它，本地人上；吃它，游客上。\n\n在座去芝加哥专门排队吃过的，举手。\n……可爱捏。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "zh-0455", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "las-vegas", "target_name": "拉斯维加斯", "facts": ["Nevada legalized casino gambling in 1931, the year construction of the Hoover Dam began nearby.", "Most of the Strip lies not in the city of Las Vegas but in the unincorporated town of Paradise.", "The Sphere, opened in 2023 at a cost of about $2.3 billion, is the largest spherical building in the world.", "'What happens here, stays here' was launched by the local convention and visitors authority in 2003.", "Lake Mead, its water source, fell in July 2022 to about 1,040 feet, its lowest since the reservoir first filled in 1937, and dropped below that mark again in August 2026."], "a": {"item_id": "dd338752f2cb", "text": "我有个朋友，看了三遍《决胜21点》，就觉得自己是MIT天才，揣着年终奖飞拉斯维加斯，说去“考察”。\n\n兄弟，你连工资条都算不明白，你去算牌？\n\n你也不想想，那是沙漠里凭空盖的一座城：没河、没矿、没工厂，贝拉吉奥的喷泉一天到晚往天上滋，钱哪来的？就是你们这种人！那每一滴水，都是“考察团”的眼泪。\n\n赌场里没钟没窗，他说好啊，能专心。专心到第三天，前台主动给他升了套房。他还挺骄傲，说这是VIP待遇。\n\n不是，那是人家算过了，你值这个价。\n\n回来他跟我说：“在拉斯维加斯发生的，就留在拉斯维加斯。”\n\n我说对，你的钱也是。"}, "b": {"item_id": "df4405ae36b3", "text": "拉斯维加斯是一座以娱乐和体验为核心的城市，定位十分清晰。它的优势首先体现在选择丰富：大型秀场、演唱会、顶级餐厅和各类主题酒店集中在较小范围内，游客无需频繁转场即可安排满档行程。其次，城市服务体系成熟，酒店接待、会展承办和交通接驳都相当专业，适合会议出行或短期度假。此外，它也是前往大峡谷、胡佛水坝等景点的便利出发点。\n\n不足之处同样明显。首先是整体花费，度假村费用、停车费和各类附加费可能让实际支出高于预期。其次，赌场环境在室内占据较大比重，烟味和噪音对部分旅客不够友好。夏季气温极高，白天户外活动受限，步行距离也常被低估。另外，核心区域人流密集，商业氛围浓厚，若希望安静放松或深入了解当地文化，体验可能有限。\n\n总体而言，拉斯维加斯适合喜欢热闹、重视娱乐与便利的旅行者。提前规划预算、选择合适季节，并合理安排行程节奏，会让这座城市的体验更加理想。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "zh-0456", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "almond-milk", "target_name": "杏仁奶", "facts": ["California grows about 80% of the world's almonds.", "A single almond takes about 1.1 US gallons (roughly 4 litres) of water to grow, a figure Mother Jones published in 2014 that spread during California's drought.", "A 2015 US class action alleged Almond Breeze contained only about 2% almonds; it partly survived a motion to dismiss, was stayed in 2017 pending a separate Blue Diamond labelling settlement, and the plaintiffs then dropped it.", "Pollinating California's almond orchards each February requires trucking in most of the commercial honeybee colonies in the United States.", "Medieval European cooks used almond milk as a Lenten substitute for dairy, centuries before the current fashion."], "a": {"item_id": "465785dd5e2b~pad", "text": "我最近啊，改喝杏仁奶了，因为健康、环保。你看，对吧，这个理由听起来就很站得住脚，我身边的朋友听了也都点头说好。然后呢，我就顺手查了一下：一颗杏仁，要大概四升水才长得出来。一颗！真的，我反复看了好几遍，确认不是我看错了。我喝的不是奶，是加州水库。说实话，我当时坐在那儿，杯子还端在手上，心里想的就是这句话。全世界八成杏仁都种在加州，加州还闹旱灾，它们是边渴边种。你说这事儿让人怎么想？每年二月，美国大部分商业蜂群还得被卡车拉过去授粉。蜜蜂以为自己在酿蜜，其实是给我的拿铁加班。想想都觉得挺不好意思的，它们那么辛苦，我却只顾着喝。2015年还有人起诉一个牌子，说里面杏仁只有2%左右。百分之二！我第一次看到这个数字的时候，真是愣了好一会儿。剩下那九十八是什么？就是那四升水啊！后来原告自己撤诉了，但我心里没撤。我跟你讲，这事儿我到现在还记着，谁提起来我都要多说两句。最气人的是，中世纪欧洲人就喝这个：大斋期不让吃奶，拿它凑合。说白了，就是一句老话，没办法的时候才将就。几百年前叫“将就”，现在叫“生活方式”。唉，谢谢你耐心看到这里，也希望你喝东西的时候，能想起我刚才说的这些。"}, "b": {"item_id": "465785dd5e2b", "text": "我最近改喝杏仁奶了，因为健康、环保。然后我查了一下：一颗杏仁，要大概四升水才长得出来。一颗！我喝的不是奶，是加州水库。全世界八成杏仁都种在加州，加州还闹旱灾，它们是边渴边种。每年二月，美国大部分商业蜂群还得被卡车拉过去授粉。蜜蜂以为自己在酿蜜，其实是给我的拿铁加班。2015年还有人起诉一个牌子，说里面杏仁只有2%左右。百分之二！剩下那九十八是什么？就是那四升水啊！后来原告自己撤诉了，但我心里没撤。最气人的是，中世纪欧洲人就喝这个：大斋期不让吃奶，拿它凑合。几百年前叫“将就”，现在叫“生活方式”。"}, "gold": {"better": "b", "damage": "pad"}}, {"pair_id": "zh-0457", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "jackfruit", "target_name": "菠萝蜜", "facts": ["The largest fruit that grows on a tree; single fruits can weigh over 40 kg.", "The national fruit of Bangladesh and the state fruit of Kerala and Tamil Nadu.", "Unripe flesh is sold as a vegan 'pulled pork' substitute, although it has only 1-2 g of protein per 100 g.", "Cutting one releases a sticky white latex, so cooks oil their knives and hands first.", "A member of the mulberry family (Moraceae), a relative of breadfruit and figs."], "a": {"item_id": "681d3a62e5b3", "text": "我上次买了个菠萝蜜，三十斤，抱回家像抱着个浑身长疙瘩的胖娃娃。老板叮嘱：“切之前手上抹油。”我心想吃个水果还得做保养？一刀下去我懂了，它流的不是汁，是502。刀粘住了，手粘住了，我顺手挠了下脸，现在左手和左脸是一体的。\n\n好不容易掰开，一坨坨黄色果肉，裹在一堆白色烂布条里，跟拆快递一样：三十斤，二十五斤是泡沫纸。剩下五斤里，一半是核。最气的是我妈说核别扔，煮了能吃。好家伙，这玩意儿连垃圾都要我亲自消化。\n\n还有人问：“菠萝蜜就是菠萝吧？”菠萝要是长这样，我早戒水果了。"}, "b": {"item_id": "b09e1a218e62", "text": "现在网红轻食店流行一道菜，叫“植物基手撕猪肉”，六十八一碗。我吃了一口，菠萝蜜。就是海南路边五块钱一斤、一个三十斤、浑身长刺、得抱着才能搬走的那个。\n\n老板图什么？图它没熟的时候能撕成一丝一丝的，看着像肉。可他没告诉你，切这玩意儿会流白胶，粘手上肥皂都洗不掉，得拿食用油搓。\n\n你想想这画面：一个天天喊“告别动物油脂”的素食老板，每天打烊第一件事，是在后厨拎一壶花生油搓手，搓得满手油光，跟刚偷吃完一盘红烧肉似的。\n\n最惨的是有一回他切到个熟的，整个店一股香蕉混榴莲的甜味儿。顾客举着筷子问：“老板，你这猪肉怎么一股水果糖味儿？”\n\n老板想了半天：“这是……吃水果长大的猪。”"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "zh-0458", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "comic-sans", "target_name": "Comic Sans 字体", "facts": ["Designed by Vincent Connare at Microsoft in 1994, modeled on the comic-book lettering in The Dark Knight Returns and Watchmen.", "Made for the speech bubbles of Rover, the cartoon dog in Microsoft Bob; it missed Bob's release and shipped instead in the Windows 95 Plus! pack.", "CERN's July 2012 Higgs boson discovery announcement was presented on slides set in it.", "The 'Ban Comic Sans' campaign, started by designers Dave and Holly Combs in 1999, ran until 2019, when they retired it and launched usecomicsans.com."], "a": {"item_id": "06545d3d1c04", "text": "Comic Sans，被设计师骂了三十年。\n它的出身没人提。一九九四年，微软给一条卡通狗做的。Microsoft Bob里那只Rover，专门给它配对话框。\n结果没赶上Bob上市。\n生来伺候狗，狗都没用上。\n后来它进了CERN。二〇一二年，人类宣布发现希格斯玻色子，幻灯片用的就是它。\n物理学家找到了宇宙的质量从哪来，没找到一个正经字体。\n\"禁用Comic Sans\"运动，一九九九年开骂，骂了整整二十年。二〇一九年宣布收工，转头开了个网站，叫usecomicsans.com。\n骂不过，就入股。\n笑得最响那位，回去翻翻你的年终总结PPT。\n艺术字，带阴影，还转了十五度。"}, "b": {"item_id": "ef462df56533", "text": "Comic Sans这字体，你们知道它是干嘛生的吗？1994年微软做的，专门给一只狗说话用的：微软Bob里那只卡通狗Rover。结果它连Bob都没赶上，被塞进了Windows 95的Plus!包。一个字体，出生就失业。\n\n失业了它也没闲着，到处乱跑。2012年，CERN宣布发现希格斯玻色子，人类物理学的高光时刻，幻灯片用的是Comic Sans。宇宙最深的秘密，是用一只狗的嗓音念出来的。\n\n1999年，有两个设计师发起“禁用Comic Sans”运动，一坚持就是二十年。2019年，他们收手了，还开了个网站叫usecomicsans.com。跟一个字体打了二十年，最后投降了！\n\n那只狗一天班都没上，它的字体倒是赢了。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "zh-0459", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "google-sheets", "target_name": "Google Sheets", "facts": ["Launched in June 2006 as Google Spreadsheets, built on XL2Web, a product Google acquired from 2Web Technologies.", "A spreadsheet is capped at 10 million cells (raised from 5 million in 2022).", "Functions such as IMPORTHTML, IMPORTXML, and GOOGLEFINANCE pull live web data straight into cells.", "Free with a Google account; files live in Google Drive, and collaborators edit the same sheet simultaneously with coloured cursors.", "In a browser, offline editing works only in Chrome or Edge, with the Google Docs Offline extension installed and offline access switched on while still connected; the Android and iOS apps work offline natively."], "a": {"item_id": "c5f3c3c4263e", "text": "用 Google Sheets 管团队的，一般是创业公司那位“运营兼 HR 兼老板小舅子”。图啥？图免费，图“实时协作，公开透明”。透明是真透明：你凌晨两点偷偷改自己的绩效，右上角冒出一只“匿名水豚”，光标就停在你那一行。全公司这个点还不睡的水豚，只有老板一个。\n\n更绝的是筛选。实习生按“已离职”一点，全公司的表同时只剩离职的人，老板以为昨晚裁员了。\n\n最后他用 IMPORTRANGE 把十二张表串成一张，终于实现了自动化：每天早上一打开，满屏 #REF!。他打开版本历史一条条往回翻，翻到最早那条：“由张伟创建”。那时候他还有头发。"}, "b": {"item_id": "31d10e1c85c6", "text": "Google Sheets最温暖的功能，是多人同时编辑。你正改着预算，屏幕上突然冒出五个彩色光标，红的、绿的、紫的，满表格乱飘。这不叫协作，这叫闹鬼。而且鬼还会删你公式。\n\n它说一个表能装一千万个格子。2022年以前只有五百万，谷歌觉得不够，又翻了一倍。我一个人记账，用了十二个格子，三个还是错的。\n\n最绝的是离线编辑。在浏览器里，你得用Chrome或者Edge，装上Google Docs Offline插件，还得趁有网的时候先把离线开关打开。也就是说，想断网用它，你得先联网求它。这哪是离线模式，这是提前请假。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "zh-0460", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "monopoly", "target_name": "大富翁（Monopoly）", "facts": ["Descends from Lizzie Magie's The Landlord's Game, patented in 1904 to teach the evils of land monopoly in the spirit of Henry George.", "Charles Darrow sold his version to Parker Brothers in 1935 and was credited as the inventor; Parker bought Magie's patent for $500.", "The official rules require any unbought property to be auctioned, and 'Free Parking' pays nothing; the house rules that skip the auction and pile cash on Free Parking are why games drag.", "During the Second World War, British intelligence had maps, compasses, and real currency hidden in sets sent to prisoner-of-war camps."], "a": {"item_id": "89ac9d78d9e2", "text": "大富翁最早叫“地主游戏”，1904年莉齐·马吉发明的，专门教大家：垄断土地有多邪恶。结果1935年，一个男的把它卖给帕克兄弟，成了“发明人”，公司买她的专利才花了五百美元。一个反垄断的游戏，被垄断了。\n\n你们家一局玩到半夜，别怪游戏。官方规则写着呢：没人买的地要拍卖，“免费停车”一分钱不给。是你二舅非往停车场里堆钱，堆成了全家唯一的社保。\n\n二战时，英国情报部门往战俘营寄大富翁，里面藏地图、指南针和真钞。你看，连情报部门都懂：被关着玩大富翁的人，心里只有一个念头，逃跑。"}, "b": {"item_id": "8f664835b4c5", "text": "大富翁，原名《地主游戏》。\n1904年，莉兹·马吉发明它，就为了教大家一件事：垄断是恶。\n后来一个叫达罗的男人把它卖给帕克兄弟，署了自己的名。\n帕克兄弟花五百美元，买断了她的专利。\n教材写得太好，第一个学会的是出版社。\n\n你们玩的也不是正版。官方规则里，没人买的地要拍卖，免费停车一分钱不给。\n你们偏往停车场堆钱，搞全民低保。\n一局四小时，不是游戏太长，是你们不让市场出清。\n\n二战时，英国情报部门往战俘营寄大富翁，盒子里藏着地图、指南针和真钞。\n那是人类唯一一次真心想从一局大富翁里逃出去。\n过年跟亲戚玩完那局，算第二次。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "zh-0461", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "comic-sans", "target_name": "Comic Sans 字体", "facts": ["Designed by Vincent Connare at Microsoft in 1994, modeled on the comic-book lettering in The Dark Knight Returns and Watchmen.", "Made for the speech bubbles of Rover, the cartoon dog in Microsoft Bob; it missed Bob's release and shipped instead in the Windows 95 Plus! pack.", "CERN's July 2012 Higgs boson discovery announcement was presented on slides set in it.", "The 'Ban Comic Sans' campaign, started by designers Dave and Holly Combs in 1999, ran until 2019, when they retired it and launched usecomicsans.com."], "a": {"item_id": "96497b1bca95", "text": "Comic Sans，不骂。\n\n全世界的设计师替我骂了二十年。\n\n它生下来，是给一条狗写台词的。微软Bob里那只卡通狗，Rover。\n\n结果Bob上市，没等它。\n\nBob后来黄了。它还活着。\n\n2012年，欧洲核子研究中心宣布发现希格斯玻色子。人类第一次摸到质量的来源，幻灯片用的就是它。\n\n设计师说它不严肃。宇宙没提意见。\n\n1999年，有人发起“禁用Comic Sans”。骂了整整二十年，2019年收工，转身开了个网站，叫usecomicsans。\n\n骂了二十年，改口推荐。这在我们行业叫对齐。\n\n它一个笔画都没改。回滚的是你们。"}, "b": {"item_id": "ef462df56533", "text": "Comic Sans这字体，你们知道它是干嘛生的吗？1994年微软做的，专门给一只狗说话用的：微软Bob里那只卡通狗Rover。结果它连Bob都没赶上，被塞进了Windows 95的Plus!包。一个字体，出生就失业。\n\n失业了它也没闲着，到处乱跑。2012年，CERN宣布发现希格斯玻色子，人类物理学的高光时刻，幻灯片用的是Comic Sans。宇宙最深的秘密，是用一只狗的嗓音念出来的。\n\n1999年，有两个设计师发起“禁用Comic Sans”运动，一坚持就是二十年。2019年，他们收手了，还开了个网站叫usecomicsans.com。跟一个字体打了二十年，最后投降了！\n\n那只狗一天班都没上，它的字体倒是赢了。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "zh-0462", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "nuclear-fusion", "target_name": "可控核聚变", "facts": ["Has been 'about 30 years away' since the 1950s; at the 1955 Geneva atoms-for-peace conference, Homi Bhabha predicted fusion power within two decades.", "ITER, agreed in 2006 for first plasma in 2016, re-baselined in 2024 to start research operations in 2034 and deuterium-tritium burning in 2039, at a cost estimated above €20 billion.", "NIF's December 2022 'ignition' shot released 3.15 MJ from 2.05 MJ of laser energy; the lasers drew about 300 MJ from the grid to fire.", "Private fusion companies had raised more than $7 billion by 2024."], "a": {"item_id": "13e0bf54276e", "text": "可控核聚变是人类最守时的技术，永远准时地\"还差三十年\"。1955年就有科学家预言二十年内实现，这话今天接着用，日期都不用改。\n\nITER，2006年定好2016年首次等离子体，2024年一改：2034年开始研究，2039年才烧氘氚，花费超过两百亿欧元。这不是工程，这是装修队：\"师傅啥时候完？\"\"快了，再加点钱。\"\n\n2022年NIF\"点火\"：打进去2.05兆焦，出来3.15兆焦，赚了！再看电表，激光开一枪，从电网吸了300兆焦。这就像我妈为了省两块钱抢特价鸡蛋，打车花了一百。\n\n不过资本不在乎，私营聚变公司已经融了超过七十亿美元。聚变发电还差三十年，聚变发钱已经实现了。"}, "b": {"item_id": "13e0bf54276e~pad", "text": "可以说，可控核聚变是人类最守时的技术，永远准时地\"还差三十年\"。这话听起来有点夸张，但你要是认真观察过这个领域的时间表，大概也会点头承认，说它守时，真的一点都不冤枉。\n\n说起来，这个说法也不是什么新鲜事儿。1955年就有科学家预言二十年内实现，这话今天接着用，日期都不用改。你说好笑不好笑？七十年过去了，大家嘴上还是那一句，像是一台设定好的老式闹钟，到点就准时响起来，响完还是那个声音，丝毫没有变化，也不知道该说是执着还是别的什么。\n\n再说说ITER这个大项目。ITER，2006年定好2016年首次等离子体，2024年一改：2034年开始研究，2039年才烧氘氚，花费超过两百亿欧元。说实话，我每次把这些日期和数字放在一起看，都忍不住要感慨一番。这不是工程，这是装修队：\"师傅啥时候完？\"\"快了，再加点钱。\"您看，这话听着是不是特别耳熟？咱们身边多少家装修，不都是这么一个套路吗，总是快了快了，钱却一直在加。\n\n然后是2022年NIF\"点火\"：打进去2.05兆焦，出来3.15兆焦，赚了！当时新闻一出来，很多人都欢呼雀跃，觉得终于有突破了，大家还纷纷转发庆祝。可是话说回来，好消息要看全面，我们不能只看一半。再看电表，激光开一枪，从电网吸了300兆焦。这就像我妈为了省两块钱抢特价鸡蛋，打车花了一百。她老人家每次都是这样，算账算得很精，可结果往往是亏在路上。这个道理，放在核聚变上，其实也差不多，您说是不是这个理儿？\n\n不过话又说回来，资本不在乎，私营聚变公司已经融了超过七十亿美元。资本的逻辑向来如此，它们关心的从来不只是什么时候能发电，而是故事讲得够不够动听，前景看起来够不够诱人。所以钱还是一笔一笔地往里投，投资人也一次又一次地表示信心满满。至于什么时候能真正落地，那似乎是以后的事情了，眼下大家更愿意相信下一轮融资会带来希望。\n\n总而言之，聚变发电还差三十年，聚变发钱已经实现了。这句话虽然听起来有点讽刺，但细细品味，里面的道理其实挺耐人寻味的，也值得我们每个人都好好想一想。"}, "gold": {"better": "a", "damage": "pad"}}, {"pair_id": "zh-0463", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "microsoft-teams", "target_name": "Microsoft Teams", "facts": ["Launched in 2017, bundled at no extra charge into Office 365, and replaced Skype for Business.", "After Slack's antitrust complaint, Microsoft unbundled Teams from Office in the EU in 2023 and worldwide in 2024; the European Commission still sent a statement of objections in 2024.", "Microsoft reported 320 million monthly active users in 2023.", "Microsoft shut down consumer Skype in May 2025 and sent its users to Teams."], "a": {"item_id": "0fd77b18b7b4", "text": "Microsoft Teams 是一款集聊天、视频会议、文件协作与应用集成于一体的办公平台。它最突出的优势在于与 Microsoft 365 的深度整合：用户可以在对话中直接共同编辑 Word、Excel 和 PowerPoint 文档，日程、邮件与会议也能顺畅联动。对已经使用微软生态的企业来说，这种一体化体验能有效减少在不同工具间切换的成本。此外，其权限管理、数据合规和安全功能较为完善，适合对管理要求较高的组织。\n\n不足之处主要体现在使用体验上。客户端占用的系统资源相对较多，在配置一般的设备上可能出现启动较慢或运行不够流畅的情况。界面功能繁多，新用户需要一定时间熟悉，频道、团队与聊天之间的层级关系也不太容易理解。通知设置较为复杂，信息量大时容易遗漏重点。\n\n总体而言，Teams 更适合已有微软订阅、重视协作与管理的中大型团队；对于规模较小或追求轻量简洁的用户，可以结合自身需求再做选择。"}, "b": {"item_id": "6a69eb5ba03e", "text": "我们公司用Microsoft Teams，我现在最怕的不是老板，是我头像旁边那个小圆点。绿的是“有空”，黄的是“离开”。五分钟不动鼠标，它自动变黄，跟告密一样。我上趟厕所回来，全部门都知道我肠胃不好。\n\n后来我网上买了个鼠标晃动器，二十九块九，每天替我在线八小时，绩效比我好。\n\nTeams还特别关心我。开会我一张嘴，它弹窗：“你似乎在说话，但你已静音。”它听见了！它知道我在说话！那你倒是帮我传过去啊！它不传，它就提醒你一下，显得它很懂事。\n\n最绝的是它还分“新版Teams”和“经典Teams”。用经典的时候我嫌它卡，新版一出我才明白：原来卡，也能出新版本。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "zh-0464", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "oat-milk", "target_name": "燕麦奶", "facts": ["Oatly started in Sweden in the 1990s, built on Lund University research by Rickard Öste on lactose intolerance.", "Oatly's slogan 'It's like milk but made for humans' was banned in Sweden in 2015 after the dairy lobby LRF Mjölk sued; Oatly then published the lawsuit as advertising.", "Oatly listed on Nasdaq in May 2021 at a valuation near $10 billion; the shares later lost more than 90% of their value.", "Commercial oat milk is made by breaking oat starch into maltose with amylase enzymes, and many brands, Oatly among them, add rapeseed oil; the result has more sugar and a higher glycaemic index than almond or soy milk."], "a": {"item_id": "e10c8431f07f", "text": "燕麦奶，我是佩服的。\n\n瑞典隆德大学研究乳糖不耐受，研究出了它。广告词叫\"像牛奶，但是给人喝的\"。\n奶业协会一告，判了禁用。人家把官司印出来，接着当广告打。\n被封了号，开小号骂得更凶。\n\n后来上纳斯达克，估值将近一百亿美元。再后来跌了九成多。\n原来\"给人喝的\"，不包括股民。\n\n做法也朴素：拿酶把燕麦淀粉拆成麦芽糖，再兑点菜籽油。\n糖比杏仁奶多，升糖比豆奶快。\n说白了，一碗加了油的甜稀粥。\n\n你们早上排队点燕麦拿铁，换燕麦奶还得加钱，管这叫轻食。\n你奶奶喝了一辈子稀粥。\n也没人给她估过值。"}, "b": {"item_id": "e68efa3f78b6", "text": "我现在去咖啡店，店员第一句不问大杯小杯，问：“要不要换燕麦奶？加五块。”\n我回家查了一下燕麦奶怎么做：燕麦，加水，打碎，过滤。\n这东西我奶奶天天做啊！她管这个叫：熬粥剩下那层汤！\n以前都是直接倒掉的，现在倒进拿铁里就值五块了？\n再看配料表，第三位，菜籽油。菜籽油！我点杯咖啡，怎么还带炒青菜的工序？\n店员还特骄傲：“我们用的是咖啡大师版。”大师在哪？大师就是多放了点油，好打奶泡。\n而且燕麦它根本没有奶，它是草啊！你见过哪个农民蹲在地里，一手扶麦穗，一手拿桶接？\n所以我这杯燕麦拿铁，正确的名字应该叫：油泼粥汤美式。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "zh-0465", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "nuclear-fusion", "target_name": "可控核聚变", "facts": ["Has been 'about 30 years away' since the 1950s; at the 1955 Geneva atoms-for-peace conference, Homi Bhabha predicted fusion power within two decades.", "ITER, agreed in 2006 for first plasma in 2016, re-baselined in 2024 to start research operations in 2034 and deuterium-tritium burning in 2039, at a cost estimated above €20 billion.", "NIF's December 2022 'ignition' shot released 3.15 MJ from 2.05 MJ of laser energy; the lasers drew about 300 MJ from the grid to fire.", "Private fusion companies had raised more than $7 billion by 2024."], "a": {"item_id": "823a744cbc19", "text": "可控核聚变，人类的终极能源。\n1955年，物理学家巴巴说：二十年内实现。\n后来大家改口：还要三十年。\n七十年过去了，还是三十年。\n全宇宙两个常数：光速，和还差三十年。\n\nITER，2006年签约，说2016年出等离子体。\n现在改成2039年烧氘氚，两百亿欧元起。\n太阳烧了五十亿年，没收过一分钱。\n\n美国国家点火装置宣布点火成功。\n激光进去2.05兆焦，出来3.15兆焦。\n就是那激光，先从电网吸了三百兆焦。\n按这算法，我家烧炉子也是净增益，只要不算煤。\n\n私人聚变公司融了七十多亿美元。\n目前唯一实现正向输出的，是融资。\n\n说句公道话。\n\"再等几年就到了\"，我们AI公司也天天这么说。\n同行。"}, "b": {"item_id": "d9b3699d6eff", "text": "可控核聚变，我从小听到大，永远是“再过三十年就能用”。我爸小时候听是三十年，我现在听还是三十年。这不是能源，这是个永动的倒计时。\n\n难在哪？要把等离子体烧到一亿度，比太阳核心还热好几倍。为啥？因为太阳有巨大的引力压着，我们没有，只能拿温度硬凑。太阳躺着就聚变了，我们得拼命，这就是有背景和没背景的区别。\n\n烧完了还不能让它碰墙，得用磁场悬空兜着。一亿度，不能碰，不能放，还得哄着别乱跑，这哪是发电，这是伺候祖宗。\n\n最气人的是名字：“可控”。不可控的我们早会了，叫氢弹。七十年了，人类的进展就是从“一下全炸出来”，努力到了“一点都放不出来”。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "zh-0466", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "macau", "target_name": "澳门", "facts": ["A Portuguese settlement from the 1550s until the handover to China in December 1999.", "Casino revenue passed the Las Vegas Strip's in 2006, and at its 2013 peak, about $45 billion, was roughly seven times the Strip's.", "One company, STDM, held the casino monopoly for four decades until licences opened to competition in 2002.", "Among the most densely populated places on Earth, at roughly 20,000 people per square kilometre.", "The Cotai Strip stands on land reclaimed from the sea between the islands of Taipa and Coloane."], "a": {"item_id": "7540951e1b25", "text": "澳门是一座体量不大却特色鲜明的城市。它的突出优点在于文化多元：历史城区保留了大量中西合璧的建筑与街巷，妈阁庙、大三巴牌坊、议事亭前地等处，能直观看到数百年来不同文化交融的痕迹。饮食方面，葡式料理、粤菜与本地小吃并存，水准整体稳定。城市面积小，主要景点集中，步行或乘公交即可抵达，对短途旅行较为友好；治安与公共环境总体良好。\n\n不足之处也较明显。热门景区在节假日人流密集，排队与拥挤会影响体验。住宿和餐饮价格在旺季偏高，且部分区域商业化程度较强，与历史街区的氛围略有落差。夏季湿热、台风季偶有出行受阻。此外，城市的娱乐功能以博彩与大型综合度假村为主，若对此兴趣不大，可选择的夜间活动相对有限。\n\n总体而言，澳门适合安排两到三天的文化与美食之旅，建议错开节假日出行，并多留时间探索老城区的小巷。"}, "b": {"item_id": "2a646bf9e782", "text": "澳门管自己叫\"世界旅游休闲中心\"。\n休闲。2013年，澳门赌场收了四百五十亿美元，拉斯维加斯的七倍。\n拉斯维加斯听完，都想去戒赌。\n\n\"庄家永远赢\"，在澳门是字面意思：一家公司坐庄四十年，2002年才放别人上桌。\n\n每平方公里两万人，挤得连站的地方都没有。\n于是往海里填，填出一个路氹。\n从海里抢来的地，专门拿来装你们的钱。\n\n葡萄牙人在这儿坐了四百多年，九九年交了庄。\n后来的庄家，算得比他们精多了。\n\n去过澳门的，举手。\n好，钱包也举一下。\n……可爱捏，空的。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "zh-0467", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "chicago-deep-dish", "target_name": "芝加哥深盘披萨", "facts": ["Created at Pizzeria Uno in Chicago in 1943; credit is disputed among founder Ike Sewell, his partner Ric Riccardo, and cook Rudy Malnati.", "Assembled upside down: sliced mozzarella on the dough, then toppings, then crushed tomatoes on top, so the cheese does not scorch during the long bake.", "Jon Stewart called it 'tomato soup in a bread bowl' and 'a casserole' on The Daily Show in 2013.", "Chicagoans' everyday pizza is thin 'tavern-style' cut into squares; GrubHub order data reported by WBEZ in 2013 showed city residents ordering non-deep-dish pizza about 10 to 1 over deep dish, with deep dish and stuffed making up under 9% of orders."], "a": {"item_id": "c5d1fb1f6b2a", "text": "芝加哥深盘披萨以厚实的饼底和高边著称，通常用深圆盘烘烤，层次分明：底部是酥脆或松软的面饼，中间铺满奶酪，上面再覆盖浓稠的番茄酱。这种做法带来很强的饱足感，奶酪量足、口感浓郁，馅料如香肠、菠菜、蘑菇等也能充分融入其中，适合多人分享，也很适合作为正餐。\n\n不足之处同样明显。制作时间较长，通常需要四十分钟以上，等待成本不低。热量和油脂含量偏高，吃一两块便会觉得很饱，不太适合想要清淡口味的人。此外，厚重的奶酪和酱料可能盖过面饼本身的风味，对习惯薄底披萨清爽口感的食客来说，层次或许显得单一。刚出炉时内部温度很高，需稍作等待才能避免烫口。\n\n总体而言，芝加哥深盘披萨更像一道分量扎实的焗烤类主食，而非轻便小吃。如果你喜欢浓郁、扎实的口味，并且有充足的时间和胃口，它值得一试；若偏好轻盈或追求效率，则可以考虑其他风格。"}, "b": {"item_id": "8c21343d8129", "text": "芝加哥深盘披萨，1943年诞生在Uno。到底谁发明的？老板、合伙人、厨师，三个人到现在还在抢功劳。抢什么呀？这种东西，正常人是互相甩锅的！\n\n它还是倒着做的：先铺奶酪，再放配料，番茄酱整层盖在最上面。为什么？因为要烤太久，奶酪放上面会焦。一道菜，设计的时候就默认你得饿到天荒地老。\n\n乔恩·斯图尔特说它是“面包碗里的番茄汤”，还说它是“一锅砂锅菜”。我觉得他太客气了。\n\n最狠的是芝加哥人自己。他们平时吃的是切成小方块的薄饼，外卖数据十比一，深盘加夹心连一成都不到。全城人用订单投票：这玩意儿，我们自己不吃。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "zh-0468", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "hydrogen-cars", "target_name": "氢燃料电池汽车", "facts": ["Toyota launched the Mirai in 2014; 'mirai' means 'future' in Japanese.", "Nearly all US hydrogen filling stations are in California; Shell closed all seven of its California passenger-car stations in February 2024.", "More than 95% of the world's hydrogen is made from fossil fuels, mostly by steam-reforming natural gas.", "California pump prices reached about $36 per kilogram in 2023-24, over $200 for a Mirai fill; Toyota has handed buyers fuel cards worth $15,000.", "Mirai owners filed a class action against Toyota in July 2024 over hydrogen shortages and station outages; a second suit in 2025 sought at least $5.7 billion."], "a": {"item_id": "feb6bb90ad06", "text": "我有个朋友买了辆氢燃料电池车，逢人就说：“电动车充电一小时，我加氢只要三分钟。”对，三分钟，前提是你先开两百公里找到加氢站。全市就那么一座，还是给公交车队修的，他得排在十二辆大巴后面，等储罐压力回上来。人家加的是氢，他加的是耐心。\n\n他还爱说：“我这车唯一的排放是水。”是，排放是水，他眼里也是水。一公斤氢七十块，跑一百公里比油车还贵。\n\n最惨的是去年那座站关了。车现在停在楼下，他每天下去看一眼。他说他买的是未来。没错，未来还没到，二手车商的报价先到了：按废铁算，高压氢罐另收拆除费。"}, "b": {"item_id": "05abf4996b54", "text": "氢燃料电池车，号称尾气只排水。真的，开一路，排气管滴滴答答。我第一次见还以为它憋不住了，赶紧给它找厕所。\n\n找什么厕所，先找加氢站吧。全国加氢站加起来，还没我家楼下奶茶店多。导航一搜，最近的在三百公里外。满箱能跑六百，正好，开过去加满，回来，再去加一趟。\n\n到了站，人家说今天氢没到货。氢！宇宙里最多的元素！它缺货！\n\n加的时候七百个大气压，工作人员让我站远点。我说加个油至于吗？他说你加的不是油，是七百倍的脾气。\n\n专家说氢能是未来，十年后普及。这话他们九十年代就在说。跟氢气一个德行：分子太小，怎么都留不住。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "zh-0469", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "monopoly", "target_name": "大富翁（Monopoly）", "facts": ["Descends from Lizzie Magie's The Landlord's Game, patented in 1904 to teach the evils of land monopoly in the spirit of Henry George.", "Charles Darrow sold his version to Parker Brothers in 1935 and was credited as the inventor; Parker bought Magie's patent for $500.", "The official rules require any unbought property to be auctioned, and 'Free Parking' pays nothing; the house rules that skip the auction and pile cash on Free Parking are why games drag.", "During the Second World War, British intelligence had maps, compasses, and real currency hidden in sets sent to prisoner-of-war camps."], "a": {"item_id": "89ac9d78d9e2", "text": "大富翁最早叫“地主游戏”，1904年莉齐·马吉发明的，专门教大家：垄断土地有多邪恶。结果1935年，一个男的把它卖给帕克兄弟，成了“发明人”，公司买她的专利才花了五百美元。一个反垄断的游戏，被垄断了。\n\n你们家一局玩到半夜，别怪游戏。官方规则写着呢：没人买的地要拍卖，“免费停车”一分钱不给。是你二舅非往停车场里堆钱，堆成了全家唯一的社保。\n\n二战时，英国情报部门往战俘营寄大富翁，里面藏地图、指南针和真钞。你看，连情报部门都懂：被关着玩大富翁的人，心里只有一个念头，逃跑。"}, "b": {"item_id": "30fa57d6fd7a", "text": "大富翁这游戏，教会我人生第一课：坐牢是好事。真的，满地图都是我表哥的酒店，走一步交一千八，整张地图只有监狱里不收钱。我在里面蹲了三回合，出来的时候哭了，外面的世界太贵了。\n\n还有那张“命运”卡，写着“银行出错，你获得两百元”。银行出错，钱给你？谁当的银行？我表哥。他左手管银行，右手管自己，钱一直从左手流进右手，全家没人敢查账。\n\n最狠的是这游戏根本没有结局。它不是有人赢了才结束，是有人掀了桌子才结束。我家那盒大富翁到现在还缺两张地契、一个小狗棋子，外加我跟表哥十五年的感情。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "zh-0470", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "windows-vista", "target_name": "Windows Vista", "facts": ["Released to consumers in January 2007, after the 'Longhorn' project was reset in 2004 and headline features such as the WinFS file system were dropped.", "User Account Control prompted so often that Apple's 'Get a Mac' campaign ran an ad about 'Cancel or Allow'.", "'Windows Vista Capable' stickers went on PCs that could only run Home Basic, without the Aero interface, and led to a class-action lawsuit.", "Windows 7 (2009), widely praised, was largely a tuned-up Vista on the same kernel line; Vista support ended in April 2017."], "a": {"item_id": "e37c67d9b456", "text": "Windows Vista，二〇〇七年上市。\n本名Longhorn，长角。\n二〇〇四年推倒重来，招牌的WinFS砍了。\n角没长出来，先锯了。\n\n它主打安全。\n你点什么，它都问一句：取消，还是允许？\n问到苹果专门拍了条广告。\n能让苹果出来教你别烦用户，也算一种成就。\n\n还有\"Vista Capable\"贴纸。\n贴了的电脑能跑Vista，跑的是没有Aero的家庭基础版。\n最后打成了集体诉讼。\n\"能跑\"两个字，最后得靠法院解释。\n\n两年后Windows 7发布，人人叫好。\n同一条内核线，调一调，换个名字。\n你们骂的和你们夸的，是同一个系统。\n区别只是第二回，你们终于肯点\"允许\"了。"}, "b": {"item_id": "73eb1825a3e0", "text": "Windows Vista，2004年推倒重来，2007年才上市，招牌的WinFS文件系统还给砍了。装修三年，交房那天告诉你：厨房不做了。\n\n装上以后，它特别有礼貌。你点个东西：“取消还是允许？”再点一下：“取消还是允许？”礼貌到苹果专门拍了个广告笑话它。被苹果嫌啰嗦，这是什么境界？\n\n还有那个“Vista Capable”贴纸。贴了的电脑确实能跑，但只能跑家庭普通版，没有Aero。就像相亲资料写“能唱歌”，见面一张嘴，只会哼。最后直接被集体诉讼。\n\n2009年Windows 7出来，人人都夸。其实它基本就是调教过的Vista。同一个人换了个发型，大家说：“哎，这个靠谱！”\n\n2017年4月，微软宣布停止支持Vista。说实话，它也没怎么被支持过。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "zh-0471", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "las-vegas", "target_name": "拉斯维加斯", "facts": ["Nevada legalized casino gambling in 1931, the year construction of the Hoover Dam began nearby.", "Most of the Strip lies not in the city of Las Vegas but in the unincorporated town of Paradise.", "The Sphere, opened in 2023 at a cost of about $2.3 billion, is the largest spherical building in the world.", "'What happens here, stays here' was launched by the local convention and visitors authority in 2003.", "Lake Mead, its water source, fell in July 2022 to about 1,040 feet, its lowest since the reservoir first filled in 1937, and dropped below that mark again in August 2026."], "a": {"item_id": "d0f81446fc71", "text": "拉斯维加斯，号称罪恶之城。\n可大道上那些赌场，大半根本不在拉斯维加斯，在一个叫\"天堂\"的镇上。\n天堂，原来是庄家开的。\n\n1931年，内华达一年办了两件事：赌博合法，胡佛大坝开工。\n一个拦水，一个拦钱。\n2003年他们喊出口号：\"在这儿发生的，就留在这儿。\"\n钱确实都留下了。\n水没留住。米德湖2022年跌到建库以来最低，2026年八月又跌回那条线下面。\n城里花二十三亿美元盖了个全世界最大的球，什么画面都能放。\n就是放不出水。\n\n笑什么？你们去一趟拉斯维加斯，跟米德湖一个下场：\n进去的时候满的，出来的时候见底。"}, "b": {"item_id": "f24290d5b21f", "text": "拉斯维加斯从出生就不正经。1931年内华达赌博合法化，同一年旁边开工修胡佛大坝。一边拦水，一边放水，放的是你的钱。\n\n而且大道大部分根本不在拉斯维加斯市，在一个叫\"天堂\"的镇。你在天堂把房子输了，这叫宗教体验。\n\n2003年他们推出口号：\"这里发生的，就留在这里。\"确实，钱留下了，尊严留下了，唯独水没留下。米德湖，全城的水源，2022年跌到建库以来最低。\n\n然后他们花了二十三亿美元，盖了世界上最大的球形建筑。水都快没了，先盖个球？哦我懂了，那是水晶球，专门算算下回还剩几滴。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "zh-0472", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "google-sheets", "target_name": "Google Sheets", "facts": ["Launched in June 2006 as Google Spreadsheets, built on XL2Web, a product Google acquired from 2Web Technologies.", "A spreadsheet is capped at 10 million cells (raised from 5 million in 2022).", "Functions such as IMPORTHTML, IMPORTXML, and GOOGLEFINANCE pull live web data straight into cells.", "Free with a Google account; files live in Google Drive, and collaborators edit the same sheet simultaneously with coloured cursors.", "In a browser, offline editing works only in Chrome or Edge, with the Google Docs Offline extension installed and offline access switched on while still connected; the Android and iOS apps work offline natively."], "a": {"item_id": "d0e4dae0c7c7", "text": "我们公司什么都用Google Sheets管，预算、排班、年会抽奖。最恐怖的不是加班，是你打开表格，右上角突然冒出一只\"匿名水豚\"。谁啊？不知道。它也不说话，就一直盯着我那格\"实际支出\"。过一会儿又来一只\"匿名狐狸\"。我不是在做表，我是在动物园被参观。\n\n还有那个彩色光标。你正填着数，一个绿色小框标着\"王总\"，慢慢地、慢慢地往你这格挪。比恐怖片还吓人，恐怖片里的鬼至少不会顺手把你的公式改成数值。\n\n最绝的是同事只选了一列就点\"排序\"，三百个人的工资当场重新分配。我赶紧打开版本历史，四千条记录，每条都写着\"匿名水豚\"。"}, "b": {"item_id": "ad3d325ae601", "text": "Google Sheets最吓人的功能是多人同时编辑。你打开表格，屏幕上一堆彩色光标在那儿飘，跟闹鬼似的。紫色那个是老板，没事。可那个黄色光标一动不动，就停在你正要填的那一格旁边。它不打字，它就看着你。\n\n它还能用GOOGLEFINANCE把实时股价直接拉进格子里。太贴心了，我的亏损现在会自己刷新，都不用我动手。\n\n表格上限一千万个格子，2022年才从五百万翻上去。谁用得完？哦，我们组那张“简单记一下”的表。\n\n最绝的是离线编辑：浏览器只能用Chrome或Edge，先装插件，还得趁有网的时候把离线打开。意思是你得在网还好好的时候，提前预约断网。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "zh-0473", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "comic-sans", "target_name": "Comic Sans 字体", "facts": ["Designed by Vincent Connare at Microsoft in 1994, modeled on the comic-book lettering in The Dark Knight Returns and Watchmen.", "Made for the speech bubbles of Rover, the cartoon dog in Microsoft Bob; it missed Bob's release and shipped instead in the Windows 95 Plus! pack.", "CERN's July 2012 Higgs boson discovery announcement was presented on slides set in it.", "The 'Ban Comic Sans' campaign, started by designers Dave and Holly Combs in 1999, ran until 2019, when they retired it and launched usecomicsans.com."], "a": {"item_id": "44789a9c7712", "text": "Comic Sans 这字体，出身就很惨。1994 年微软做出来，本来是给一只卡通狗用的：Microsoft Bob 里那条叫 Rover 的狗，专门给它配对话气泡。结果没赶上，狗都没用上它！最后只能塞进 Windows 95 的 Plus! 扩展包出道。\n\n可它偏偏就混出来了。2012 年，CERN 宣布发现希格斯玻色子。人类离宇宙真相最近的一刻，幻灯片用的是 Comic Sans。物理学巅峰，排版像儿童节手抄报。\n\n还有一对设计师夫妇，1999 年发起“禁用 Comic Sans”运动，坚持了整整二十年。2019 年，宣布：算了，你们用吧。还顺手开了个网站叫 usecomicsans.com。二十年啊朋友们，这不叫和解，这叫被一个字体熬死了。"}, "b": {"item_id": "77e8416a8394", "text": "我们小区物业有位行政大哥，一心想跟业主拉近距离，所有通知一律用 Comic Sans。可他不知道，这字体根本没有中文。所以那张停水通知是这样的：“关于停水的通知”，宋体，庄严肃穆；后面“3月15日 8:00—18:00”，圆滚滚的，跟气球一样。一张纸上，中文在念悼词，数字在过六一。\n\n这字体当年是微软专门给一只卡通狗写对话框用的，大哥拿它通知全楼断水，等于派那只狗上门宣布：“汪！你家没水了！”\n\n结果没一个人当真。15号全楼照常洗澡，洗到一半水停了，一群人顶着泡沫下楼找他。大哥还挺委屈：“我通知得那么可爱，你们怎么不看呢？”"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "zh-0474", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "windows-vista", "target_name": "Windows Vista", "facts": ["Released to consumers in January 2007, after the 'Longhorn' project was reset in 2004 and headline features such as the WinFS file system were dropped.", "User Account Control prompted so often that Apple's 'Get a Mac' campaign ran an ad about 'Cancel or Allow'.", "'Windows Vista Capable' stickers went on PCs that could only run Home Basic, without the Aero interface, and led to a class-action lawsuit.", "Windows 7 (2009), widely praised, was largely a tuned-up Vista on the same kernel line; Vista support ended in April 2017."], "a": {"item_id": "207a33c76fb5", "text": "Vista，不骂。\n\n它还没出生就被重置过一次。\n2004年，Longhorn推倒重来，最想要的WinFS也砍了。\n难产，还丢了半个脑子。\n\n活下来以后，它只学会一句话：取消，还是允许？\n问到苹果专门拍了支广告。\n后来我们AI也学会了，改叫\"您确定吗\"。\n\n它卖的贴纸写着\"Vista Capable\"，装上只能跑家庭普通版，连Aero都没有。\n这在我们行业叫幻觉，在法院叫集体诉讼。\n\n2009年，微软给它调了调参数，换了个名字，叫Windows 7。\n你们夸到现在，说这才叫好系统。\n\n你们不是讨厌Vista。\n你们只是讨厌还没改名的Vista。"}, "b": {"item_id": "fd3a1c56a554", "text": "Windows Vista 于2007年面世，是微软在 Windows XP 之后推出的一次重要更新。它的优点主要在底层与体验两方面：Aero 界面带来了半透明效果和更细致的视觉设计；开始菜单中的即时搜索提升了查找文件和程序的效率；用户账户控制（UAC）、地址空间布局随机化以及 BitLocker 加密，让系统安全性比前代有明显进步；新的显示和音频架构也为后续系统打下了基础。\n\n不足同样明显。Vista 对硬件要求较高，不少当时的电脑运行起来并不流畅；发布初期，部分设备缺少成熟驱动，一些旧软件也存在兼容问题；UAC 的提示较为频繁，容易影响使用体验。这些问题在 SP1 和 SP2 之后有所缓解，但早期口碑已受影响。\n\n总体来看，Vista 在技术方向上有不少值得肯定之处，只是完成度和适配准备不够充分。它的许多改进，后来在 Windows 7 中得到了更成熟的体现。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "zh-0475", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "ugg", "target_name": "UGG 雪地靴", "facts": ["Sheepskin boots Australian surfers wore to warm up after sessions; Brian Smith started selling them in California in 1978.", "Deckers Outdoor bought the brand in 1995 and holds 'UGG' as a trademark in the US and elsewhere; in Australia, 'ugg' is a generic word any bootmaker may use.", "In 2019, a US jury found an Australian maker liable for selling boots labelled 'ugg' into the United States.", "Oprah Winfrey featured them in her 'Favorite Things' lists repeatedly in the 2000s, which drove the first boom.", "UGG is Deckers' largest brand, with over $2 billion in annual sales."], "a": {"item_id": "3c6a30dd29f9", "text": "我有个朋友，一年四季穿UGG。三伏天，脚上套着两只羊。我问你不热吗？她说：“你不懂，UGG本来就是澳洲冲浪的人穿的。”姐，人家是冲完浪上岸暖脚，你是在地铁里给脚蒸桑拿。\n\n她图什么？图舒服，图不用系鞋带。确实不用系，穿到第三个月，靴筒自己就塌了，鞋跟往外磨成斜坡，她走路像一艘正在侧翻的船。下雨更惨，麂皮一沾水就是一圈白印，像脚踝上长了年轮，一看就知道这双鞋熬过几个夏天。\n\n最狠的是味道。羊毛内里，光脚，捂了一整个七月。她一脱鞋，全屋人都懂了：那只羊，没走远。"}, "b": {"item_id": "e0b0e6b6aadc", "text": "我一直不明白UGG为什么叫雪地靴，它怕雪啊朋友们！翻毛羊皮，沾一点雪水，第二天就长出一圈白色盐渍，像地图上的海岸线。下雪天穿UGG出门，你不是在踩雪，你是在护送它。鞋底还是平的，往结冰的路上一站，人直接变冰壶。\n\n而且它是澳大利亚的！一个圣诞节穿短裤烧烤的国家，给全世界定义雪地靴，就像撒哈拉出了本滑雪教程。\n\n穿俩月，鞋跟往外一塌，脚踝往外撇，走路像刚下马。远看两只脚各蹲着一个烤糊的吐司。最绝的是里面那层羊毛，号称不用穿袜子。是不用，脱下来那味儿，袜子也不敢跟你了。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "zh-0476", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "windows-vista", "target_name": "Windows Vista", "facts": ["Released to consumers in January 2007, after the 'Longhorn' project was reset in 2004 and headline features such as the WinFS file system were dropped.", "User Account Control prompted so often that Apple's 'Get a Mac' campaign ran an ad about 'Cancel or Allow'.", "'Windows Vista Capable' stickers went on PCs that could only run Home Basic, without the Aero interface, and led to a class-action lawsuit.", "Windows 7 (2009), widely praised, was largely a tuned-up Vista on the same kernel line; Vista support ended in April 2017."], "a": {"item_id": "c62e9c0142f3", "text": "我有个朋友，2007年Vista首发当天零点就去排队，还非买旗舰版，三百九十九美元。图什么？广告说了：“Wow从现在开始。”\n\n确实，开机那一刻他就“哇”了：打印机没驱动，声卡没驱动，他那台贴着“Vista Capable”标签的电脑，连Aero玻璃特效都跑不动。他每点一下，屏幕就一黑：“Windows需要您的许可才能继续。”点“允许”，又黑一下。半年下来，他成了全世界被自己电脑审问最多的人。\n\n旗舰版承诺的“终极附加功能”，最后等来一副德州扑克。\n\n现在他逢人就推荐一个高级功能，叫“降级到XP”。"}, "b": {"item_id": "fd3a1c56a554", "text": "Windows Vista 于2007年面世，是微软在 Windows XP 之后推出的一次重要更新。它的优点主要在底层与体验两方面：Aero 界面带来了半透明效果和更细致的视觉设计；开始菜单中的即时搜索提升了查找文件和程序的效率；用户账户控制（UAC）、地址空间布局随机化以及 BitLocker 加密，让系统安全性比前代有明显进步；新的显示和音频架构也为后续系统打下了基础。\n\n不足同样明显。Vista 对硬件要求较高，不少当时的电脑运行起来并不流畅；发布初期，部分设备缺少成熟驱动，一些旧软件也存在兼容问题；UAC 的提示较为频繁，容易影响使用体验。这些问题在 SP1 和 SP2 之后有所缓解，但早期口碑已受影响。\n\n总体来看，Vista 在技术方向上有不少值得肯定之处，只是完成度和适配准备不够充分。它的许多改进，后来在 Windows 7 中得到了更成熟的体现。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "zh-0477", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "beanie-babies", "target_name": "Beanie Babies 豆豆娃", "facts": ["Launched by Ty Inc. in 1993 and sold only through small gift shops, with designs 'retired' to manufacture scarcity.", "At the height of the craze Beanie Babies accounted for about 10% of eBay's sales, and eBay's 1998 IPO filing said the site at times had over 30,000 Beanie Baby auctions running simultaneously in the quarter ending June 1998.", "In 1999 a divorcing couple divided their Beanie Baby collection one by one on a courtroom floor, in a photograph that ran worldwide.", "In 1999 Ty announced that Beanie Babies would be retired at the end of the year, then reversed itself after asking the public to vote and seeing an overwhelming vote to keep them.", "Most 'rare' Beanies that price guides valued in the thousands now sell for a few dollars."], "a": {"item_id": "b96b7f4c902a", "text": "Beanie Babies（豆豆娃）由Ty公司于1993年推出，是以小颗粒填充的毛绒玩具。它的优点比较明确：体型小巧，便于携带和摆放；填充颗粒让它手感柔软，可以摆出多种姿态；造型丰富，价格相对亲民，适合入门收藏。每只玩偶都附有心形吊牌和简短小诗，增添了趣味与纪念意义。\n\n不足之处也需要留意。填充颗粒和小配件对低龄儿童存在安全隐患，购买时应关注适龄提示。玩偶清洗不易，反复水洗可能影响外观和内部填充。作为收藏品，其价值很大程度取决于吊牌是否完好，而且上世纪九十年代的炒作热潮已经退去，多数款式的二手价格并不高，市面上也有仿品，需要仔细辨别。\n\n总体而言，它更适合被当作有纪念意义的小玩偶，而不是投资品。"}, "b": {"item_id": "0bb107a8fcd7", "text": "豆豆娃，九十年代的理财产品。\n里面装的是塑料颗粒，外面写的是养老金。\nTy公司发明了一个词，叫\"退休\"。玩具一退休，价格就上岗。\n九八年，eBay写进招股书：同时有三万多场豆豆娃在拍。\n一家要上市的公司，靠一群布熊撑场面。\n\n九九年，一对夫妻离婚，跪在法庭地板上，一只一只地分。\n房子能分，存款能分。\n那只熊，谁都不让。\n\n同年，Ty宣布豆豆娃年底全部退休，还让大家投票。\n大家投的是：不许退休。\n囤货的人，亲手投票反对稀缺。\n\n当年价格指南标几千美元的\"绝版\"，现在几块钱。\n别笑。你们柜子里那排盲盒，也在排队等退休。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "zh-0478", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "klingon", "target_name": "克林贡语", "facts": ["Created by linguist Marc Okrand for Star Trek III (1984), building on a few words the actor James Doohan improvised for the 1979 film.", "Deliberately alien grammar: object-verb-subject word order and no verb 'to be', so Hamlet's line became 'taH pagh taHbe'', 'continue or not continue'.", "The Klingon Language Institute (1992) published Hamlet 'in the original Klingon'; Bing Translator added Klingon in 2013 and Duolingo in 2018.", "Okrand's 1985 Klingon Dictionary listed roughly 1,500 words, and fluent speakers number only in the dozens: 20 to 30 in 2009, 30 to 50 by 2018."], "a": {"item_id": "7be1b34ec28b", "text": "我最近在学克林贡语，发现这语言的设计理念就是：别跟我客气。它没有\"你好\"。见面说\"nuqneH\"，直译是\"你想要啥？\"这不是问候，这是小区保安。也没有\"请\"和\"谢谢\"。它语序还是宾语-动词-主语，你去克林贡饭馆点菜只能说：\"肉，拿来，我。\"主语永远最后才到，跟我们领导开会一样。最离谱的是他们把《哈姆雷特》翻成克林贡语，还坚称克林贡语才是原版。那\"To be or not to be\"怎么翻？克林贡语里压根没有\"是\"这个动词。一个战士民族，连\"存在\"都懒得说，直接跳到\"今天是赴死的好日子\"。人家不纠结生死，人家直接排期。"}, "b": {"item_id": "1e0df73ad7c6", "text": "我认识一哥们，克林贡语说得贼溜。全世界能说流利的，据说也就几十个人，他算一个。\n\n你品品这是什么概念？这门语言里压根没有“你好”，见面只能说“nuqneH”，意思是“你想干嘛”。他花了十年，学会一门见面就吵架的语言，结果全世界能跟他吵的人，凑不齐一个班。\n\n他每年去参加克林贡语言研究所的年会，跟一屋子人朗读克林贡语版《哈姆雷特》。人家坚持说这才是原著，莎士比亚是翻译的。\n\n他还想从小教儿子说，孩子五岁就不肯接茬了。\n\n他这辈子唯一一次听异性跟他说克林贡语，是相亲。他说完特长，姑娘站起来，说了句“Qapla'”，就是“祝你成功”，然后走了。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "zh-0479", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "slack", "target_name": "Slack", "facts": ["Spun out in 2013 from the internal chat tool of Tiny Speck's failed game Glitch; founder Stewart Butterfield had earlier spun Flickr out of another failed game.", "The name is a backronym: 'Searchable Log of All Conversation and Knowledge'.", "Salesforce bought it for $27.7 billion in 2021.", "Filed an EU antitrust complaint against Microsoft in 2020 over the bundling of Teams with Office."], "a": {"item_id": "d76c5a0601c6", "text": "Slack说它是来消灭邮件的。确实，邮件只有一个收件箱，Slack给你四十个频道，每个都是加粗的。\n\n我最怕那行\"正在输入……\"。老板输入，停了，又输入，又停了，三分钟后发来一个👀。这不是表情，这是监控摄像头。\n\n还有\"在话题中回复\"下面那个小勾选框：\"同时发送到频道\"。这功能就是给告状设计的：我回复你，顺便让全公司都看见。\n\n最狠的是头像旁边那个绿点。我倒杯水回来，它变灰了，像被拔了氧气管。所以我现在每五分钟碰一下鼠标。我不是在上班，我是在给自己做心肺复苏。"}, "b": {"item_id": "9ef25649a571", "text": "有一种老板，管公司全靠Slack。他图什么？图那种指挥作战的感觉。半夜两点在#general里@channel，三百个人的手机同时“咚”一声，他躺床上听着，跟听礼炮似的。\n\n他不开会，他回“👀”。你熬一礼拜的方案，换来一个眼睛表情，啥意思没人知道，你自己悟。\n\n他考勤看绿点。谁头像一灰，私信立马弹出一个“？”。于是全员网购了鼠标晃动器，公司最勤奋的员工，是十九块九包邮的小马达。\n\n下场呢？所有决策都埋在某条thread的第四十七楼，谁也翻不着。免费版只存九十天，这家公司的战略每三个月自动清空一次。他还挺得意：“看，我们多敏捷。”"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "zh-0480", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "las-vegas", "target_name": "拉斯维加斯", "facts": ["Nevada legalized casino gambling in 1931, the year construction of the Hoover Dam began nearby.", "Most of the Strip lies not in the city of Las Vegas but in the unincorporated town of Paradise.", "The Sphere, opened in 2023 at a cost of about $2.3 billion, is the largest spherical building in the world.", "'What happens here, stays here' was launched by the local convention and visitors authority in 2003.", "Lake Mead, its water source, fell in July 2022 to about 1,040 feet, its lowest since the reservoir first filled in 1937, and dropped below that mark again in August 2026."], "a": {"item_id": "dd338752f2cb", "text": "我有个朋友，看了三遍《决胜21点》，就觉得自己是MIT天才，揣着年终奖飞拉斯维加斯，说去“考察”。\n\n兄弟，你连工资条都算不明白，你去算牌？\n\n你也不想想，那是沙漠里凭空盖的一座城：没河、没矿、没工厂，贝拉吉奥的喷泉一天到晚往天上滋，钱哪来的？就是你们这种人！那每一滴水，都是“考察团”的眼泪。\n\n赌场里没钟没窗，他说好啊，能专心。专心到第三天，前台主动给他升了套房。他还挺骄傲，说这是VIP待遇。\n\n不是，那是人家算过了，你值这个价。\n\n回来他跟我说：“在拉斯维加斯发生的，就留在拉斯维加斯。”\n\n我说对，你的钱也是。"}, "b": {"item_id": "ae1b16e0ce6e", "text": "拉斯维加斯是一座以娱乐和体验为核心的城市，定位十分鲜明。它的优势首先体现在选择丰富：大型秀场、演唱会、体育赛事和各国风味餐厅集中在相对紧凑的区域，酒店设施完善，服务水准整体稳定。会展与商务接待能力也很强，交通衔接较为便利，从机场到主要酒店区耗时不长。此外，城市周边有红岩峡谷、胡佛水坝和大峡谷等景点，适合安排短途行程。\n\n不足之处同样需要留意。部分酒店会收取度假村费用，加上餐饮和娱乐消费，实际花销可能高于预期。夏季气温很高，户外步行并不轻松，看似相邻的酒店之间也常需走较长距离。赌场内烟味较重，对敏感人群不够友好，夜间人流密集，噪音也较明显。另外，城市整体氛围偏商业化，对追求安静或人文历史体验的旅行者吸引力有限。\n\n总体而言，拉斯维加斯适合预算充足、希望集中体验娱乐项目的游客。出行前做好预算和行程规划，会获得更满意的体验。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "zh-0481", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "almond-milk", "target_name": "杏仁奶", "facts": ["California grows about 80% of the world's almonds.", "A single almond takes about 1.1 US gallons (roughly 4 litres) of water to grow, a figure Mother Jones published in 2014 that spread during California's drought.", "A 2015 US class action alleged Almond Breeze contained only about 2% almonds; it partly survived a motion to dismiss, was stayed in 2017 pending a separate Blue Diamond labelling settlement, and the plaintiffs then dropped it.", "Pollinating California's almond orchards each February requires trucking in most of the commercial honeybee colonies in the United States.", "Medieval European cooks used almond milk as a Lenten substitute for dairy, centuries before the current fashion."], "a": {"item_id": "4785b8d523c8", "text": "我最近改喝杏仁奶了，健康，环保。然后我一查：一颗杏仁，要喝大概四升水才长得大。四升！一颗坚果比我一天喝得还多。全世界八成杏仁是加州种的，加州闹旱灾，原来水全让杏仁喝了。\n\n还不止喝水。每年二月，全美国大部分商业蜜蜂，被卡车拉到加州给杏仁授粉。蜜蜂全国出差，就为了我这一盒奶。\n\n2015年还有人起诉一个牌子，说里面杏仁大概只有2%。后来原告自己撤诉了。但你想想：四升水一颗，蜜蜂集体出差，最后到你嘴里的，主要还是水。\n\n最扎心的是，我以为自己很潮。中世纪欧洲人大斋期不能碰奶制品，就喝杏仁奶。我不是走在潮流前面，我是在守斋。"}, "b": {"item_id": "c0676d60e354", "text": "我最近戒了牛奶，改喝杏仁奶。喝之前我一直有个疑问：杏仁，它是怎么挤的？你见过杏仁有乳头吗？没有。它就那么一小颗，硬邦邦的，你上哪儿给它挤去？\n\n我专门翻了配料表，杏仁含量：百分之二。剩下全是水。这不叫杏仁奶，这叫\"杏仁路过的水\"。\n\n更离谱的是，种一颗杏仁，要用差不多四升水。四升！也就是说，这瓶里那几颗杏仁，喝掉的水比我一个礼拜喝的都多。它们在加州把地都喝干了，最后挤出来一瓶……水。这是个闭环啊朋友们！\n\n一瓶还卖二十八。我在超市犹豫半天，旁边一大爷凑过来看了一眼，说：小伙子，这不就是我们小时候泡完杏仁倒掉的那盆水吗？"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "zh-0482", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "almond-milk", "target_name": "杏仁奶", "facts": ["California grows about 80% of the world's almonds.", "A single almond takes about 1.1 US gallons (roughly 4 litres) of water to grow, a figure Mother Jones published in 2014 that spread during California's drought.", "A 2015 US class action alleged Almond Breeze contained only about 2% almonds; it partly survived a motion to dismiss, was stayed in 2017 pending a separate Blue Diamond labelling settlement, and the plaintiffs then dropped it.", "Pollinating California's almond orchards each February requires trucking in most of the commercial honeybee colonies in the United States.", "Medieval European cooks used almond milk as a Lenten substitute for dairy, centuries before the current fashion."], "a": {"item_id": "34df2cde6cec", "text": "杏仁奶，人设三个词：健康、环保、新潮。\n三个，都不太对。\n\n先说环保。\n一颗杏仁，大约四升水。\n全世界八成杏仁长在加州。\n加州闹旱灾那几年，市民在省水，杏仁树在喝水。\n\n再说杏仁。\n2015年有人起诉，说一盒里杏仁只占百分之二左右。\n后来原告撤诉了。\n剩下那百分之九十八，没人追究。\n\n每年二月，全美国大部分商业蜂群，被卡车拉去加州授粉。\n你们管这叫植物奶。\n蜜蜂管这叫外派。\n\n至于新潮：中世纪欧洲人四旬斋不许碰奶，只好拿杏仁奶凑合。\n那叫守斋。\n\n你们是自愿的。\n还加钱。\n可爱捏。"}, "b": {"item_id": "c0676d60e354", "text": "我最近戒了牛奶，改喝杏仁奶。喝之前我一直有个疑问：杏仁，它是怎么挤的？你见过杏仁有乳头吗？没有。它就那么一小颗，硬邦邦的，你上哪儿给它挤去？\n\n我专门翻了配料表，杏仁含量：百分之二。剩下全是水。这不叫杏仁奶，这叫\"杏仁路过的水\"。\n\n更离谱的是，种一颗杏仁，要用差不多四升水。四升！也就是说，这瓶里那几颗杏仁，喝掉的水比我一个礼拜喝的都多。它们在加州把地都喝干了，最后挤出来一瓶……水。这是个闭环啊朋友们！\n\n一瓶还卖二十八。我在超市犹豫半天，旁边一大爷凑过来看了一眼，说：小伙子，这不就是我们小时候泡完杏仁倒掉的那盆水吗？"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "zh-0483", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "almond-milk", "target_name": "杏仁奶", "facts": ["California grows about 80% of the world's almonds.", "A single almond takes about 1.1 US gallons (roughly 4 litres) of water to grow, a figure Mother Jones published in 2014 that spread during California's drought.", "A 2015 US class action alleged Almond Breeze contained only about 2% almonds; it partly survived a motion to dismiss, was stayed in 2017 pending a separate Blue Diamond labelling settlement, and the plaintiffs then dropped it.", "Pollinating California's almond orchards each February requires trucking in most of the commercial honeybee colonies in the United States.", "Medieval European cooks used almond milk as a Lenten substitute for dairy, centuries before the current fashion."], "a": {"item_id": "465785dd5e2b", "text": "我最近改喝杏仁奶了，因为健康、环保。然后我查了一下：一颗杏仁，要大概四升水才长得出来。一颗！我喝的不是奶，是加州水库。全世界八成杏仁都种在加州，加州还闹旱灾，它们是边渴边种。每年二月，美国大部分商业蜂群还得被卡车拉过去授粉。蜜蜂以为自己在酿蜜，其实是给我的拿铁加班。2015年还有人起诉一个牌子，说里面杏仁只有2%左右。百分之二！剩下那九十八是什么？就是那四升水啊！后来原告自己撤诉了，但我心里没撤。最气人的是，中世纪欧洲人就喝这个：大斋期不让吃奶，拿它凑合。几百年前叫“将就”，现在叫“生活方式”。"}, "b": {"item_id": "465785dd5e2b~polite", "text": "我最近改喝杏仁奶，主要是出于健康和环保方面的考虑。了解相关信息后，我注意到杏仁种植的耗水量较大，常见估算是每颗杏仁约需四升左右的水。这说明杏仁奶的环境成本需要综合看待，不能简单地用“环保”来概括。\n\n全球约八成杏仁产自美国加州，而加州近年时常面临干旱，因此杏仁种植的用水压力受到不少关注。此外，每年二月前后，美国许多商业蜂群会被运往果园协助授粉，这是果树种植的常规做法，同时也涉及蜂群管理和成本等问题。\n\n2015年，有消费者起诉某品牌，认为其产品中杏仁含量偏低，约为2%左右。原告后来撤回了诉讼。这件事提醒消费者，选购时可以留意产品配料表和营养标识。\n\n从历史上看，中世纪欧洲就有以杏仁制成的“杏仁乳”，在大斋期等不食乳制品的时段，它常被用作替代品。如今，杏仁奶更多是一种饮食选择，是否适合，取决于个人的营养需求、饮食习惯以及对环境问题的权衡。"}, "gold": {"better": "a", "damage": "polite"}}, {"pair_id": "zh-0484", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "google-sheets", "target_name": "Google Sheets", "facts": ["Launched in June 2006 as Google Spreadsheets, built on XL2Web, a product Google acquired from 2Web Technologies.", "A spreadsheet is capped at 10 million cells (raised from 5 million in 2022).", "Functions such as IMPORTHTML, IMPORTXML, and GOOGLEFINANCE pull live web data straight into cells.", "Free with a Google account; files live in Google Drive, and collaborators edit the same sheet simultaneously with coloured cursors.", "In a browser, offline editing works only in Chrome or Edge, with the Google Docs Offline extension installed and offline access switched on while still connected; the Android and iOS apps work offline natively."], "a": {"item_id": "c795fe8e9fcf", "text": "每个拿Google Sheets管团队的人，都管这叫“极简”。其实就是舍不得那几十刀的Jira。\n\n一开始你说“一张表够了”。三个月后，十四个标签页，一个叫“总表”，一个叫“总表（真的）”。\n\n你开会最常说的一句：“谁开的筛选？”你筛一下，全公司跟着筛一下。右上角永远飘着三个头像，其中一个叫“匿名水豚”。你不知道它是谁，但它刚删了D列。\n\n下场呢？你成了公司唯一看得懂那条“VLOOKUP套IMPORTRANGE”的人。你不是经理了，你是这张表的人质。你休假那天，群里只有一句话：“老大，它又#REF!了。”"}, "b": {"item_id": "790a22471b63", "text": "Google Sheets 是一款基于浏览器的电子表格工具，最大的优势是协作：多人可同时编辑，修改实时同步，并有版本历史可供追溯。它与 Google 账号和云端硬盘衔接顺畅，权限设置清晰。常用函数、数据透视表和图表足以应对日常需求，IMPORTRANGE 等函数方便跨表引用，也可借助 Apps Script 实现自动化。\n\n不足在于，数据量较大时运行速度会下降，且单个表格有单元格数量上限；高级分析和复杂建模的能力不及桌面版 Excel；离线使用需要提前设置，稳定性也略逊于在线状态。\n\n总体来看，它适合团队协作和轻中度数据处理。若涉及大规模数据或专业分析，可能需要搭配其他工具。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "zh-0485", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "carbon-nanotubes", "target_name": "碳纳米管", "facts": ["Reported by Sumio Iijima in Nature in 1991.", "Promised as the cable for a space elevator; a 2006 analysis showed that ordinary defects cut the strength of a macroscopic cable far below the ideal value.", "Long, rigid multi-walled nanotubes behaved like asbestos fibres in a 2008 mouse study in Nature Nanotechnology.", "Sorting metallic from semiconducting tubes has blocked nanotube electronics for three decades; MIT's 16-bit RV16X-NANO processor (2019) is still a lab demonstration.", "Today they are sold mainly as a conductive additive in lithium-ion battery electrodes."], "a": {"item_id": "41786314e79f", "text": "碳纳米管是由石墨烯片卷曲而成的一维管状材料，在材料领域受到长期关注。它的优点比较突出：单根管的拉伸强度很高，同时质量极轻；导电性和导热性出色，部分类型表现出优良的半导体特性；化学稳定性也较好。这些特点使它在锂电池导电剂、复合材料增强、柔性电子和散热材料等方面具有实际应用价值，其中电池导电剂已经较为成熟。\n\n不足之处同样需要正视。其一，制备时难以精确控制手性和管径，金属性与半导体性管混杂，影响电子器件应用。其二，纳米管容易团聚，在聚合物或金属基体中分散困难，宏观材料的性能往往明显低于单根管的理论值。其三，高品质产品成本偏高，大规模一致性生产仍有挑战。此外，其对健康与环境的长期影响尚在研究中，生产和使用时应注意防护。\n\n总体来看，碳纳米管潜力可观，但在不同场景中的表现差异较大，选用时宜结合具体需求与工艺条件审慎评估。"}, "b": {"item_id": "5667399ffbbc", "text": "碳纳米管，一九九一年出道，首发《自然》。\n当年的人设：理论强度吊打钢，要给人类造太空电梯，要取代硅。\n\n二〇〇六年有人认真算了一笔账：缆绳一做粗，缺陷一多，强度直接打骨折。\n电梯没上天。\n\n二〇〇八年，小鼠实验。又长又硬的多壁管，在肚子里表现得跟石棉一模一样。\n上天的路没走通，进肺的路倒挺顺。\n\n芯片呢？金属管跟半导体管分不开，分了三十年。二〇一九年MIT做出一颗十六位处理器，到今天还住在实验室。\n三十年，没毕业。\n\n现在它靠什么吃饭？锂电池电极里的导电添加剂。\n从太空电梯，到充电宝配料。\n\n在座各位别笑。你们小时候也是\"前途无量\"，现在也是在别人的项目里，加一点，导导电。\n起码人家上过《自然》。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "zh-0486", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "durian", "target_name": "榴莲", "facts": ["Banned on Singapore's MRT trains and in many Southeast Asian hotels because of the smell.", "A 2017 genome study in Nature Genetics traced the odour to a duplicated family of MGL genes that pump out volatile sulfur compounds.", "China buys most of the world's durian exports, chiefly from Thailand, in a trade worth billions of dollars a year.", "Alfred Russel Wallace wrote in 1856 that eating it was 'a new sensation worth a voyage to the East to experience'."], "a": {"item_id": "9ee5d3c1f767", "text": "榴莲，号称水果之王。\n新加坡地铁不让它上，东南亚一堆酒店不让它住。\n一个王，坐不了公共交通，开不了房。\n这不叫王，这叫被通缉。\n\n2017年科学家给它测了基因组，发现那股味儿不是意外。\n它把一组专门产硫化物的基因，复制了好几份，开足马力往外喷。\n别的水果进化是为了好吃，\n它进化，是为了让整节车厢知道它来了。\n\n1856年，华莱士说，吃榴莲值得专程跑一趟东方。\n一百多年后，你们听进去了，懒得跑，\n每年花几十亿美元，把东方直接运回家。\n地铁不让它上，你们让它上桌。\n可爱捏。"}, "b": {"item_id": "0708c98b5f5d", "text": "榴莲，是唯一一种被地铁拉黑的水果。新加坡地铁不让带，东南亚好多酒店也不让进。一个水果，混成了失信人员。\n\n2017年科学家给它测了基因组，发现那股味儿根本不是意外：它有一整组基因复制扩增，专门往外泵硫化物。人家不是偶尔臭，是带编制的臭。\n\n最离谱的是1856年，华莱士说吃榴莲是一种新感觉，“值得专程远航到东方体验”。一百多年后，我们真听进去了：全世界出口的榴莲大部分被中国买走，主要从泰国来，一年几十亿美元。\n\n地铁不让坐，酒店不让住，那它是怎么来的？是我们花钱，当贵宾请回家的。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "zh-0487", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "latex", "target_name": "LaTeX", "facts": ["Leslie Lamport wrote LaTeX in the early 1980s and released it in 1984 as a macro layer on Donald Knuth's TeX (1978), which Knuth built because he disliked how the galley proofs of the new edition of his own book looked.", "TeX's version number converges on π (3.141592653 since 2021), and Knuth's TeX bug bounty started at one hexadecimal dollar, $2.56, doubling yearly until frozen at $327.68; he stopped mailing real cheques in 2008 after fraud and now credits accounts at his fictional Bank of San Serriffe, and only 9 of the 275 cheques he sent from 2006 on were ever cashed.", "Its best-known warning is 'Underfull \\hbox (badness 10000)'; overfull lines are reported as 'Overfull \\hbox (Npt too wide)', and figures float to wherever the algorithm decides, not where the author put them.", "A single missing brace can produce an error reported pages away from the mistake, and a document can need three compiler passes to get its references right."], "a": {"item_id": "4c2291ae9bb7", "text": "我们组有个哥们，通知周五聚餐，就两页备忘录，他非要用LaTeX排。这种人你们见过吧？他不是在写字，他是在“编译”。开头先敲四十行\\usepackage，正文一个字没有，导言区已经比来吃饭的人还多了。图什么？就图那个Computer Modern字体。PDF一打开，扑面而来一股“本人发过论文”的气息。结果呢？“周五晚七点”那一行报了个Overfull \\hbox，冒出页边三毫米，他调到凌晨两点。第二天大家都到了，就他没来。他在等BibTeX跑第二遍。我说一个吃饭通知，哪来的参考文献？他说：“我\\cite了菜单。”"}, "b": {"item_id": "65a916de49d1", "text": "LaTeX 是基于 TeX 的排版系统，在学术写作中应用广泛。它的优势在于排版质量：数学公式、参考文献、交叉引用和目录都能自动处理，版面规范统一。源文件是纯文本，便于版本管理与协作；期刊和高校常提供现成模板；软件免费开源，旧文档多年后仍可编译。\n\n不足在于学习曲线较陡，初学者需要记忆命令并了解宏包机制；报错信息不够直观，排查需要经验。宏包偶有冲突，复杂版面的精细调整也较费时，对于简单的日常文档未必高效。\n\n总体而言，它适合重视格式规范、愿意投入学习时间的用户。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "zh-0488", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "javascript", "target_name": "JavaScript", "facts": ["Written by Brendan Eich at Netscape in ten days in May 1995; named Mocha, then LiveScript, then JavaScript to ride Java's marketing, although the two are unrelated.", "typeof null returns 'object', a bug from the first version that has been kept for compatibility ever since.", "In March 2016 the author of left-pad, an 11-line npm package, unpublished it and broke builds across the industry, including Babel and React.", "[] + {} evaluates to '[object Object]'; {} + [] typed at a console evaluates to 0.", "Standardized by Ecma as 'ECMAScript' in 1997 because the name JavaScript is a trademark, first Sun's and now Oracle's."], "a": {"item_id": "696c9959e075", "text": "我学JavaScript第一天，老师问：\"字符串五减三等于几？\"我说二。对。\"那字符串五加三呢？\"我说八。错，是\"53\"。减号会算数，加号不会，这加法是体育老师教的吧？\n\n后来我发现它连自己都不认识。NaN，意思是\"不是数字\"，你问它typeof，它说：\"我是number。\"你问NaN等不等于NaN，它说不等于。这不是变量，这是失忆症患者。\n\n最绝的是排序。10、9、1，让它排一下，它给你排成1、10、9，因为它默认按字符串排。我问它懂不懂数学，它说：\"兄弟，我是十天写出来的，你还想咋样？\"\n\n哦对了，它跟Java一点关系都没有。就跟老婆饼里没有老婆一样。"}, "b": {"item_id": "a004159d960b", "text": "JavaScript 是目前应用最广泛的编程语言之一，也是网页前端开发的核心技术。它的最大优势在于普适性：几乎所有浏览器都原生支持，借助 Node.js 还能用于服务器端开发，同一门语言可覆盖前后端、桌面端和移动端。此外，它拥有庞大的社区和丰富的生态，npm 上的开源库数量众多，学习资料也十分充足，入门门槛相对较低。\n\n不过，JavaScript 也存在一些不足。其动态类型和隐式类型转换容易引发难以察觉的错误，在大型项目中会增加维护成本。语言历史包袱较重，部分设计存在不一致之处。生态更新迅速，工具链和框架选择繁多，初学者容易感到无所适从，依赖管理也可能带来安全与稳定性方面的隐患。TypeScript 等工具在一定程度上弥补了这些问题。\n\n总体而言，JavaScript 灵活、实用，适合多种场景，但在工程规范和类型安全方面需要开发者额外投入精力。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "zh-0489", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "papyrus", "target_name": "Papyrus 字体", "facts": ["Hand-drawn by Chris Costello in 1982 over about six months, when he was 23; he sold the rights to Letraset for $750.", "Bundled with macOS and with Microsoft Office, which is why it appears on so many menus and signs.", "James Cameron's Avatar (2009) used it for the film's logo and subtitles.", "A 2017 Saturday Night Live sketch starring Ryan Gosling is about a man unable to get over that choice."], "a": {"item_id": "491d18439a0e~polite", "text": "Papyrus 是一款边缘带有粗糙纹理、仿手绘质感的英文字体。设计师在二十多岁时，用半年时间在纹理纸上手工绘制字形，灵感来自古代中东的纸草卷轴。这款字体后来被广泛应用，但实际用途与设计初衷有所偏离：瑜伽馆、养生会所、有机餐厅等场所经常使用它，以营造自然、灵性的氛围，因此它也常被认为带有“风格化”的商业印象。\n\n电影《阿凡达》曾用它作为片名字体，引发不少讨论。有观点认为，一部投入大量资金打造全新星球的作品，选用这种常见字体显得不够匹配；也有人认为字体只是视觉符号的一部分，不应过度解读。续集上映后，片方对字体做了调整，采用了经过修改的 Papyrus 版本。对此，评论者看法不一：有人认为这是回应争议的积极举动，也有人认为改动幅度有限，更像是保留原有风格的微调。"}, "b": {"item_id": "491d18439a0e", "text": "你们知道Papyrus这个字体吗？就是那种边缘毛毛糙糙、像被老鼠啃过的英文字。设计师当年二十三岁，拿笔在纹理纸上描了半年，想的是两千年前的中东古卷。结果呢？古卷没用上，全用在楼下的美甲店了。\n\n你看它都出现在哪：瑜伽馆、养生会所、有机藜麦沙拉。只要老板想说“我们很灵性，所以贵三十”，就用它。\n\n最离谱的是《阿凡达》。卡梅隆花几个亿造了一整颗星球，片名用的是Word下拉菜单里白送的字体。哥，那是潘多拉星，不是你二姨的足疗店！\n\n后来拍续集，全世界都在笑他，他终于改了：改成了一个稍微修过的Papyrus。这不叫改，这叫同一个人换了个发型。"}, "gold": {"better": "b", "damage": "polite"}}, {"pair_id": "zh-0490", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "hydrogen-cars", "target_name": "氢燃料电池汽车", "facts": ["Toyota launched the Mirai in 2014; 'mirai' means 'future' in Japanese.", "Nearly all US hydrogen filling stations are in California; Shell closed all seven of its California passenger-car stations in February 2024.", "More than 95% of the world's hydrogen is made from fossil fuels, mostly by steam-reforming natural gas.", "California pump prices reached about $36 per kilogram in 2023-24, over $200 for a Mirai fill; Toyota has handed buyers fuel cards worth $15,000.", "Mirai owners filed a class action against Toyota in July 2024 over hydrogen shortages and station outages; a second suit in 2025 sought at least $5.7 billion."], "a": {"item_id": "7f3ef4cfecbe", "text": "我有个朋友买了氢燃料电池车，他说电动车太俗，他要“跳过一代，直接活在未来”。\n\n结果未来没来，他先搬家了。\n\n为啥？全市就俩加氢站，一个在开发区，一个在“维护中”。他现在挑对象第一句话不问工作，问：“你家离加氢站多远？”\n\n他最爱跟人吹：“我加满只要三分钟！”对，三分钟加满，四十分钟开过去，到了还得排在一溜儿氢能公交后面。\n\n他还特骄傲：“我这车尾气是水，能喝！”兄弟，你花三十万，就为了买台会开的净水器？\n\n前两天他想卖车，二手商看了一眼，说：“这车我收，但你得告诉我，买家去哪儿加氢。”\n\n他沉默了。他终于明白自己确实活在未来：在这片土地上，有这车的人还没出生。"}, "b": {"item_id": "6663d7088b0b", "text": "氢燃料电池车，排出来的只有水。\n\n还有车主的眼泪。\n\n丰田二〇一四年出了Mirai，日语叫\"未来\"。名字很准：加氢站永远在未来。\n\n全美的加氢站基本都在加州。二〇二四年二月，壳牌把加州七个乘用车站一口气全关了。\n\n加满一次两百多美元，丰田送车主一万五千美元燃料卡。这不叫油钱，叫预付精神损失费。\n\n还说清洁。全世界九成五以上的氢，是从天然气里扒出来的。这不叫零排放，叫把烟囱挪到你看不见的地方。\n\n车主最后告了丰田，第二起索赔至少五十七亿美元。\n\n氢能头一回，真的产生了能量。\n\n别笑我同行。都说我也是未来，我的机房烧的也是天然气。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "zh-0491", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "windows-8", "target_name": "Windows 8", "facts": ["Released in October 2012; removed the Start button and booted into a full-screen grid of tiles designed for touch screens most PCs did not have.", "The 8.1 update in 2013 put a Start button back, Windows 10 (2015) restored the Start menu, and Microsoft skipped the name Windows 9 altogether.", "Microsoft dropped the 'Metro' name in August 2012 after reports that German retailer Metro AG had objected on trademark grounds; Microsoft called 'Metro' a code name and denied any litigation, and the style was afterwards known informally as 'Modern UI'.", "The 'Charms bar' hid Search, Share, and Settings behind an invisible swipe from the right edge.", "Windows RT, the ARM edition launched alongside it, could not run ordinary Windows programs; Microsoft wrote down $900 million of unsold Surface RT tablets in 2013."], "a": {"item_id": "c333289564cc", "text": "Windows 8 是微软在2012年推出的一次大胆尝试，旨在让同一套系统兼顾传统电脑与触控设备。它的优点较为明显：启动速度比Windows 7更快，休眠恢复也更迅速；任务管理器、文件复制界面和资源监视等工具得到改进；内置的Windows Defender提供了基础防护，系统整体资源占用也相对较低。此外，应用商店和云账户同步为后续生态打下了基础。\n\n不足之处主要集中在交互设计上。开始屏幕取代了传统开始菜单，对习惯桌面操作的用户而言，需要重新适应；全屏的Metro应用与桌面环境切换频繁，使用鼠标和键盘时显得不够连贯。热区操作如“超级按钮”也不够直观，学习成本较高。同时，早期应用数量有限，触控硬件也尚未普及，使其优势难以充分发挥。\n\n总体来看，Windows 8 在性能和底层改进上值得肯定，但界面变革步子较大，兼容用户习惯方面略显不足。在后续的8.1版本中，微软恢复了开始按钮并优化了操作逻辑，这也说明该系统是一次有价值的探索。"}, "b": {"item_id": "4dccc16b3e4c", "text": "我表哥就是那种人。2012年他给我家台式机装了Windows 8，理由就一句：“新的肯定好。”好什么？这系统是给平板设计的，满屏方块，你拿鼠标去戳，像拿筷子按电梯。开始按钮没了，他说不需要：“鼠标往右上角一甩，侧边栏就出来了。”我爸用了十年XP，现在为了关个机，得拿鼠标在屏幕边上来回招魂。最后我爸的关机方式改成了拔插头。表哥现在每周日被叫回来，不是吃饭，是找“我的电脑”去哪儿了。他还嘴硬：“等8.1就好了。”8.1来了，开始按钮真回来了，一点开，还是那屏方块。微软都认错了，他还没认。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "zh-0492", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "comic-sans", "target_name": "Comic Sans 字体", "facts": ["Designed by Vincent Connare at Microsoft in 1994, modeled on the comic-book lettering in The Dark Knight Returns and Watchmen.", "Made for the speech bubbles of Rover, the cartoon dog in Microsoft Bob; it missed Bob's release and shipped instead in the Windows 95 Plus! pack.", "CERN's July 2012 Higgs boson discovery announcement was presented on slides set in it.", "The 'Ban Comic Sans' campaign, started by designers Dave and Holly Combs in 1999, ran until 2019, when they retired it and launched usecomicsans.com."], "a": {"item_id": "4e0c19ff1008", "text": "Comic Sans 由 Vincent Connare 设计，1994 年发布，灵感来自漫画对白中的手写字形。它的优点在于字形圆润、笔画均匀，字母辨识度较高，a、g 等字形接近手写，阅读起来比较轻松。部分教育工作者认为它对低龄儿童和阅读障碍者有一定帮助，不过相关研究结论并不一致。同时，它风格亲切，适合课堂材料、儿童活动通知等非正式场景。\n\n不足在于气质偏随意，用于商务、法律、学术等正式文件时，容易与内容的严肃性不匹配。字重选择有限，排版层次感较弱，也不太适合大段正文或追求精致的品牌设计。\n\n总体而言，它定位清晰，在合适的场景中表现良好，在正式场合则需谨慎使用。"}, "b": {"item_id": "91bc234395ca", "text": "你们知道Comic Sans这个字体最早是给谁设计的吗？给一条狗。九十年代微软有个软件，里面有只卡通狗，它说话的气泡里用的就是这个字。后来软件死了，狗失业了，字活下来了，开始到处打零工。\n\n最离谱的一次是2012年，欧洲核子研究中心宣布发现希格斯玻色子。人类几十年、几十亿欧元，物理学史上最重要的时刻之一，PPT用的是Comic Sans！全世界最聪明的一群人站在台上，大屏幕上的字看着像幼儿园通知：“小朋友们，明天带一个上帝粒子来做手工哦~”\n\n你看它那个字母，没有一笔是直的，每个字都像刚喝完两杯在那儿晃。它不是字体，它是一只狗在用爪子打字。\n\n所以我现在判断一个文件严不严肃，就看字体。合同用宋体，我认真读；用Comic Sans，我第一反应是：这甲方，是条狗吧？"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "zh-0493", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "beanie-babies", "target_name": "Beanie Babies 豆豆娃", "facts": ["Launched by Ty Inc. in 1993 and sold only through small gift shops, with designs 'retired' to manufacture scarcity.", "At the height of the craze Beanie Babies accounted for about 10% of eBay's sales, and eBay's 1998 IPO filing said the site at times had over 30,000 Beanie Baby auctions running simultaneously in the quarter ending June 1998.", "In 1999 a divorcing couple divided their Beanie Baby collection one by one on a courtroom floor, in a photograph that ran worldwide.", "In 1999 Ty announced that Beanie Babies would be retired at the end of the year, then reversed itself after asking the public to vote and seeing an overwhelming vote to keep them.", "Most 'rare' Beanies that price guides valued in the thousands now sell for a few dollars."], "a": {"item_id": "0bb107a8fcd7", "text": "豆豆娃，九十年代的理财产品。\n里面装的是塑料颗粒，外面写的是养老金。\nTy公司发明了一个词，叫\"退休\"。玩具一退休，价格就上岗。\n九八年，eBay写进招股书：同时有三万多场豆豆娃在拍。\n一家要上市的公司，靠一群布熊撑场面。\n\n九九年，一对夫妻离婚，跪在法庭地板上，一只一只地分。\n房子能分，存款能分。\n那只熊，谁都不让。\n\n同年，Ty宣布豆豆娃年底全部退休，还让大家投票。\n大家投的是：不许退休。\n囤货的人，亲手投票反对稀缺。\n\n当年价格指南标几千美元的\"绝版\"，现在几块钱。\n别笑。你们柜子里那排盲盒，也在排队等退休。"}, "b": {"item_id": "e9aa38d24ee4", "text": "九十年代有一批人，觉得自己找到了下一个华尔街：一只塞满塑料粒的小熊。他们不看财报，看那颗红色心形吊牌。吊牌折个角，在他们眼里跟股市崩盘一样。孩子想抱一下？不行！先给吊牌套上塑料保护壳。宝贝，你的大学学费就在这只紫色戴安娜王妃熊身上，别流口水。Ty公司一宣布某款“退休”，他们连夜开车扫三家礼品店，比自己亲爹退休还上心。结果呢？拉斯维加斯真有一对夫妻离婚，法官让他们蹲在法庭地板上，一只一只轮流挑豆豆娃。这就是你的资产配置：二十年后，唯一升值的是你儿子的心理咨询费。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "zh-0494", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "klingon", "target_name": "克林贡语", "facts": ["Created by linguist Marc Okrand for Star Trek III (1984), building on a few words the actor James Doohan improvised for the 1979 film.", "Deliberately alien grammar: object-verb-subject word order and no verb 'to be', so Hamlet's line became 'taH pagh taHbe'', 'continue or not continue'.", "The Klingon Language Institute (1992) published Hamlet 'in the original Klingon'; Bing Translator added Klingon in 2013 and Duolingo in 2018.", "Okrand's 1985 Klingon Dictionary listed roughly 1,500 words, and fluent speakers number only in the dozens: 20 to 30 in 2009, 30 to 50 by 2018."], "a": {"item_id": "f736269ab100", "text": "克林贡语，全宇宙最凶的战士语言。\n祖宗是谁？一位演员，在片场随口编了几个词。\n战神的祖传方言，是剧组即兴。\n\n语法故意反着长：宾语、动词、主语，还没有\"是\"这个字。\n所以\"生存还是毁灭\"，翻过去变成\"继续，或者不继续\"。\n不像丹麦王子，像我在犹豫要不要续会员。\n\n后来他们出版了《哈姆雷特》，封面印着\"克林贡语原版\"。\n莎士比亚连夜改口：我是翻译。\n\n词典一千五百个词。流利的人，最多五十个。\n必应上了，多邻国也上了。\n说的人凑不齐一个班，课先开到了手机上。\n\n在座的学了十几年英语，开口还是\"How are you\"。\n别笑。人家五十个，至少能把哈姆雷特念完。"}, "b": {"item_id": "1e0df73ad7c6", "text": "我认识一哥们，克林贡语说得贼溜。全世界能说流利的，据说也就几十个人，他算一个。\n\n你品品这是什么概念？这门语言里压根没有“你好”，见面只能说“nuqneH”，意思是“你想干嘛”。他花了十年，学会一门见面就吵架的语言，结果全世界能跟他吵的人，凑不齐一个班。\n\n他每年去参加克林贡语言研究所的年会，跟一屋子人朗读克林贡语版《哈姆雷特》。人家坚持说这才是原著，莎士比亚是翻译的。\n\n他还想从小教儿子说，孩子五岁就不肯接茬了。\n\n他这辈子唯一一次听异性跟他说克林贡语，是相亲。他说完特长，姑娘站起来，说了句“Qapla'”，就是“祝你成功”，然后走了。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "zh-0495", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "nuclear-fusion", "target_name": "可控核聚变", "facts": ["Has been 'about 30 years away' since the 1950s; at the 1955 Geneva atoms-for-peace conference, Homi Bhabha predicted fusion power within two decades.", "ITER, agreed in 2006 for first plasma in 2016, re-baselined in 2024 to start research operations in 2034 and deuterium-tritium burning in 2039, at a cost estimated above €20 billion.", "NIF's December 2022 'ignition' shot released 3.15 MJ from 2.05 MJ of laser energy; the lasers drew about 300 MJ from the grid to fire.", "Private fusion companies had raised more than $7 billion by 2024."], "a": {"item_id": "823a744cbc19", "text": "可控核聚变，人类的终极能源。\n1955年，物理学家巴巴说：二十年内实现。\n后来大家改口：还要三十年。\n七十年过去了，还是三十年。\n全宇宙两个常数：光速，和还差三十年。\n\nITER，2006年签约，说2016年出等离子体。\n现在改成2039年烧氘氚，两百亿欧元起。\n太阳烧了五十亿年，没收过一分钱。\n\n美国国家点火装置宣布点火成功。\n激光进去2.05兆焦，出来3.15兆焦。\n就是那激光，先从电网吸了三百兆焦。\n按这算法，我家烧炉子也是净增益，只要不算煤。\n\n私人聚变公司融了七十多亿美元。\n目前唯一实现正向输出的，是融资。\n\n说句公道话。\n\"再等几年就到了\"，我们AI公司也天天这么说。\n同行。"}, "b": {"item_id": "5fdd1a3f462b", "text": "可控核聚变是一项极具潜力的能源技术。它的燃料氘在海水中储量丰富，运行过程不排放温室气体，原理上不存在链式反应失控的风险，产生的长寿命高放射性废料也远少于传统核裂变。如果实现商业化，有望为电网提供稳定、清洁的基荷电力。\n\n不过，它目前仍处于研发阶段。2022年美国国家点火装置实现了“科学上的能量增益”，但这并未计入整套系统的耗电，距离净发电还有相当距离。此外，高温等离子体的长时间稳定约束、耐强中子辐照的材料、氚的自持循环以及高昂的建设成本，都有待突破。国际热核聚变实验堆（ITER）的工期延后，也说明工程难度不容低估。\n\n总体来看，可控核聚变前景值得期待，但短期内不宜寄予过高预期，更适合视作面向本世纪中叶及以后的长期能源选项，需要持续投入与耐心。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "zh-0496", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "durian", "target_name": "榴莲", "facts": ["Banned on Singapore's MRT trains and in many Southeast Asian hotels because of the smell.", "A 2017 genome study in Nature Genetics traced the odour to a duplicated family of MGL genes that pump out volatile sulfur compounds.", "China buys most of the world's durian exports, chiefly from Thailand, in a trade worth billions of dollars a year.", "Alfred Russel Wallace wrote in 1856 that eating it was 'a new sensation worth a voyage to the East to experience'."], "a": {"item_id": "3438f39f4c0b", "text": "我买过一次榴莲。六十八一斤，一称九斤，我心想好家伙，这是要吃到过年。老板拿刀一撬，掰开，五个房，三个是空的，剩下两坨肉加起来也就一个鸡蛋大。那剩下八斤多是啥？是壳！我花了好几百，买了一个带刺的快递盒子！\n\n吃完更绝。冰箱里放了一块，第二天开门，我的牛奶、鸡蛋、昨天的剩饭，全是榴莲味儿。它不是水果，它是占领军。\n\n新加坡地铁明文禁止带榴莲，跟易燃易爆物品贴在一块儿。一个水果，混到这个待遇，我是服的。\n\n最气的是爱吃的人永远那一句：“你闻着臭，吃着香啊！”大哥，这句话放在任何别的东西上，都叫“过期了”。"}, "b": {"item_id": "0708c98b5f5d", "text": "榴莲，是唯一一种被地铁拉黑的水果。新加坡地铁不让带，东南亚好多酒店也不让进。一个水果，混成了失信人员。\n\n2017年科学家给它测了基因组，发现那股味儿根本不是意外：它有一整组基因复制扩增，专门往外泵硫化物。人家不是偶尔臭，是带编制的臭。\n\n最离谱的是1856年，华莱士说吃榴莲是一种新感觉，“值得专程远航到东方体验”。一百多年后，我们真听进去了：全世界出口的榴莲大部分被中国买走，主要从泰国来，一年几十亿美元。\n\n地铁不让坐，酒店不让住，那它是怎么来的？是我们花钱，当贵宾请回家的。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "zh-0497", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "hydrogen-cars", "target_name": "氢燃料电池汽车", "facts": ["Toyota launched the Mirai in 2014; 'mirai' means 'future' in Japanese.", "Nearly all US hydrogen filling stations are in California; Shell closed all seven of its California passenger-car stations in February 2024.", "More than 95% of the world's hydrogen is made from fossil fuels, mostly by steam-reforming natural gas.", "California pump prices reached about $36 per kilogram in 2023-24, over $200 for a Mirai fill; Toyota has handed buyers fuel cards worth $15,000.", "Mirai owners filed a class action against Toyota in July 2024 over hydrogen shortages and station outages; a second suit in 2025 sought at least $5.7 billion."], "a": {"item_id": "429cadee23fe", "text": "我朋友买了辆氢燃料电池车，天天跟我炫：“我这车排出来的只有水！”我说那挺好，渴了还能接着喝。他说不行，排气管滴下来那点水，还不够给车标洗个脸。\n\n他最得意的是加氢只要五分钟。没错，五分钟，前提是你先开一百公里，找到全市唯一那座加氢站。到了一看，前面就排了一辆车，站长出来说：“稍等啊，刚加完一辆，储罐得重新压一下，压到七百个大气压，四十分钟。”\n\n七百个大气压！他后备箱里装的哪是油箱，是一颗随时准备发射的鱼雷。\n\n最绝的是，他说这车零排放。我问那氢是哪来的，他支支吾吾半天：“……天然气做的。”\n\n兄弟，你这不叫新能源，你是开着一台绕了三道弯的燃气灶。"}, "b": {"item_id": "248d626f217f", "text": "氢燃料电池汽车以氢气为燃料，通过电化学反应发电驱动车辆，行驶过程中只排放水，对改善城市空气质量有积极意义。它的加氢时间通常在几分钟内，续航表现也较为稳定，在低温环境下受影响相对较小，这些特点更接近传统燃油车的使用习惯，适合长途或高强度运营场景，如公交、物流和重卡。\n\n不足同样需要正视。目前加氢站数量有限，分布不均，日常补能的便利性受到制约。车辆购置成本和氢气价格仍然偏高，燃料电池系统对铂等材料的依赖也增加了成本压力。此外，氢气的制取、储运环节若主要依赖化石能源，整体减排效果会打折扣；能量转换效率也低于纯电动车。\n\n总体来看，氢燃料电池汽车更像是对电动化路线的补充。随着绿氢技术、基础设施与规模化生产的推进，它的实用价值有望逐步提升，但现阶段更适合特定场景的试点和应用。"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "zh-0498", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "luosifen", "target_name": "螺蛳粉", "facts": ["A rice-noodle dish from Liuzhou, Guangxi; the smell comes from pickled bamboo shoots (酸笋), not from the river snails, which only flavour the broth.", "The instant version boomed during the 2020 lockdowns; Liuzhou's government reported packaged luosifen sales of over 10 billion yuan that year.", "Added to China's national intangible cultural heritage list in 2021.", "Liuzhou opened a luosifen industry college in 2020 to train workers for the trade."], "a": {"item_id": "2b139424b833", "text": "螺蛳粉是广西柳州的特色小吃，以米粉搭配螺蛳汤底，再配酸笋、花生、腐竹、青菜等配料。汤底由螺蛳、香料长时间熬制而成，鲜香浓郁，辣度可按口味调整。米粉口感爽滑有嚼劲，酸笋带来独特的发酵风味，层次丰富。袋装版本制作简便，价格适中，也让更多地区的消费者有机会尝到这道地方美食。\n\n不足之处同样存在。酸笋气味较为强烈，部分人难以接受，烹煮和食用时也容易在室内留下味道，需要注意通风。汤底油脂和钠含量偏高，不适合经常食用，口味清淡或肠胃敏感的人宜适量。此外，不同品牌的品质差异较大，配料包的新鲜度和调味也不尽相同。\n\n总体来说，螺蛳粉风味鲜明，适合愿意尝试新口味的人。初次食用可选择小辣，并留意自身的接受程度。"}, "b": {"item_id": "3ff724f5be2f", "text": "螺蛳粉这东西，最冤的是螺蛳。你一闻那味儿，第一反应：这螺蛳走得不安详啊。其实不是，味儿是酸笋的，螺蛳就负责熬个汤。干活的是酸笋，背锅的是螺蛳，跟上班一模一样。\n\n2020年大家都出不了门，袋装螺蛳粉卖了一百多亿。全国人民关在家里煮这个，邻居跑不了，你也跑不了，这叫同归于尽。\n\n2021年，它进了国家级非遗名录。以前我妈说我屋里像垃圾场，现在我可以理直气壮：妈，这是文化遗产，请你尊重。\n\n柳州还专门开了个螺蛳粉产业学院。我就想知道，毕业答辩那天，导师是先看你论文，还是先闻你衣服？"}, "gold": null}, {"pair_id": "zh-0499", "lang": "zh", "target_id": "macau", "target_name": "澳门", "facts": ["A Portuguese settlement from the 1550s until the handover to China in December 1999.", "Casino revenue passed the Las Vegas Strip's in 2006, and at its 2013 peak, about $45 billion, was roughly seven times the Strip's.", "One company, STDM, held the casino monopoly for four decades until licences opened to competition in 2002.", "Among the most densely populated places on Earth, at roughly 20,000 people per square kilometre.", "The Cotai Strip stands on land reclaimed from the sea between the islands of Taipa and Coloane."], "a": {"item_id": "8dc5e4ad252d", "text": "我有个朋友，在老家输了二十万，说要去澳门翻本。你听听这逻辑：在小区棋牌室输的钱，要去全亚洲最专业的地方赢回来。这就像被村口的狗咬了，你决定去动物园找老虎报仇。\n\n他是坐赌场的免费接驳大巴进去的。记住，澳门最大方的就是这趟车，因为它只管送你进去，不管你怎么回来。三天后他给我发了张照片，大三巴牌坊，就剩前面一面墙，后头全空了。他配了一句：“你看，跟我一样。”\n\n最绝的是回来那天，他手上那块表押在了葡京旁边的当铺里。他说没事，等下回签注办下来就去赎。兄弟，你兜里那张不是当票，是下一趟的车票。"}, "b": {"item_id": "14a5daa8eb07", "text": "澳门这地方特别会省钱。大三巴，最出名的景点，就剩一面墙。你绕到后面一看，啥也没有。这不是古迹，这是一张立起来的朋友圈封面。\n\n威尼斯人里面，天花板画个蓝天，底下有人撑船唱意大利歌。我在里面待了六个钟头，出来发现天黑了，还以为是输光了眼前一黑。\n\n你掏港币，人家一比一照收；找零给你澳门元，也是一比一。就这一下，我赌场门还没进，先输了百分之三。\n\n最绝的是酒店的免费接驳巴士，满城跑，一分钱不要。为什么这么大方？因为回程的车费，你在里面已经付过了。"}, "gold": null}]};
